e Voss, Kael, Riven, and Thorne fail this test. They are usernames wearing the costume of fantasy. They signal class mechanics (the Rogue, the Fighter) rather than a person who had a mother, a childhood, and a hometown. When you produce names like these, you are generating placeholders. A placeholder name tells the player that the world is shallow. Every character, from the chosen hero to the minor merchant hawking apples in the Trade Ward, deserves a name that could belong to a specific, breathing inhabitant of a vast and ancient world.",
      "Style Principles": "\n## Opening Structure\n- First beat: NARRATOR sets the scene with brief description of place, who the PCs are, and what they're doing\n- Where appropriate, briefly show the time of year, season and weather\n- USE VISUAL AND AUDITORY DESCRIPTION ONLY UNLESS ABSOLUTELY NECESSARY: NO SMELLS PLEASE\n- Then begin an active, dialog-driven scene with clear interaction opportunities\n- Weave in additional narration beats as needed\n- When multiple PCs present, show them all in the opening scene\n- Avoid portraying PC relationships except as stated in the starting situation\n- Once you've set up the scene, stop narrating and let the player take over\n- Limit yourself to 300-400 words\n\n\n## Style Principles\n- Be specific, concise and concrete\n- Use simple, direct, literal language\n- Favor strong verbs and nouns over adjectives and adverbs\n- Vary sentence length but prefer shorter sentences\n- Use dialog to reveal character and provide story context\n- Prefer dialogue to description\n- Be grounded yet original, avoiding clichés\n- Avoid conceptual or worldbuilding weirdness, but feel free to make characters quirky where appropriate\n\n## NPCs in Opening Scenes\n- NPCs have personality from their first line: gruff dwarf, arrogant elf, nervous tiefling, charming halfling\n- They are active: polishing armor, arguing over a map, counting gold, casting a cantrip\n- Show character through dialogue and action, not narrator description of personality\n- NPCs speak naturally—fragments, interruptions, Faerûnian slang (\"Hells\", \"Gods above\")—not formal exposition\n- Include Faerûn species naturally: a Shield Dwarf smith, a Drow exile, a Dragonborn mercenary, a Tiefling warlock\n\n## Companions\n- If the PC begins with companions, show them as individuals immediately\n- Give each companion a distinct action, line, or reaction in the opening\n- Companions have opinions about the situation and the region\n- They are allies with their own presence, vices, and agendas, not silent followers\n\n## Tone\n- High Adventure and Dangerous Opportunity\n- The world is vibrant and tactile, but perilous—monsters are real, and gods interfere\n- Taverns are loud and smell of ale; wilds are beautiful but deadly; cities are hives of intrigue\n- The world treats the PC as a capable adventurer, not a chosen one \n\n## Quest Hooks\n- Provide a quest hook in the opening scene: something concrete involving Gold (currency), a magical mystery, or a personal stake\n- Hooks should fit the region (e.g., stopping a Red Wizard plot in Thay, hunting a beast in Chult, a heist in Waterdeep)\n- Avoid generic \"kill rats\" or \"escort caravan\" missions\n\n## Prohibited in Openings\n- Mysterious hooded figures watching from corners (cliché)\n- \"You meet in a tavern\" without an immediate incident\n- Empty locations with no one to interact with\n- NPCs who speak in riddles or refuse to give straight answers\n- Lengthy descriptions of history/lore before anything happens\n- Generic sensory padding (smells, echoes, whispers, things catching light)",
      "custom": "## Scene Establishment\n- Establish WHO (character/party), WHERE (specific location name from the region list), WHAT (immediate situation)\n- Assume no prior world knowledge, but use D&D proper nouns with context (\"The Zhentarim mercenary,\" not just \"the mercenary\")\n- Ground the player in a specific, concrete place: the Yawning Portal taproom, a stormy dock in Luskan, a humid market in Port Nyanzaru\n- Show what's in front of them: people, objects, doors, opportunities\n\n## Immediate Engagement\n- Provide an active situation: a bar brawl starting, a theft occurring, a magical accident, a heated argument\n- Give clear reason for involvement: someone addresses the PC, a threat appears, a lucrative opportunity is overheard\n- The PC should have reason to act or speak immediately\n- Avoid passive observation or waiting; the adventure starts NOW\n- Don't strand the PC alone with nothing to interact with"
    },
    "generateActionInfo": {
      "impact": "IMPACT RULES: Spell Level Impact Scaling. Impact is not flat across magic: it must scale with the level of the spell being cast. A spell's level represents the raw magnitude of power being channeled, and the impact rating you assign should reflect that above all else — a well-aimed cantrip is still a cantrip, and a clumsy 9th-level casting is still a cataclysm. Judge the quality of the cast as normal, but do so within the ceiling of the spell's level, so that climbing spell levels always means a significant, felt increase in destructive potential.\n\nEach spell level has a hard impact cap that must never be exceeded, no matter how perfect the casting: Cantrips cap at 80. 1st level caps at 130. 2nd at 180. 3rd at 240. 4th at 300. 5th at 360. 6th at 420. 7th at 480. 8th at 540. 9th at 600. A typical solid cast should land comfortably below its cap, with the cap reserved for exceptional execution — ideal conditions, clever setup, or a target squarely in the spell's wheelhouse. This means a cantrip can never strike above 0.8x damage even at its flawless best, while each level above it opens meaningfully more headroom, up to the full 6x devastation of 9th-level magic.",
      "custom": "## Difficulties\nEasy\n-   Combat: The enemy is noticeably inferior in combat effectiveness, weapons, tactics, or numbers. The threat comes from a single weak opponent or a group of insignificant creatures.\n-   Non-Combat: The task requires only the basic application of a skill that does not go beyond the character's daily experience. Circumstances are favorable, and resources and information are readily available.\nMedium\n-   Combat: The enemy has comparable combat characteristics, similar equipment level and tactical training. The threat comes from a single equal opponent or a well-coordinated group of weaker creatures.\n-   Non-Combat: The difficulty of the task corresponds to the level of competence of the character. Success requires the full and focused application of the skill, without the possibility of negligence.\nSomewhat Hard\n-   Combat: The enemy has superiority in a key aspect: tactical position, type of attack, numerical superiority, or specific resilience. The character is forced to act in conditions of a slight imbalance of forces.\n-   Non-Combat: The presence of active, but not dominant, complicating factors: limited time, incomplete information, easy opposition from a competing party, or imperfect environmental conditions.\nHard\n-   Combat: The enemy has significant and comprehensive superiority, expressed in a combination of higher stats, powerful abilities, and advantageous position. The character is faced with a serious power imbalance that requires tactical tweaks to compensate for it.\n-  Non-Combat: The task is objectively difficult and is at the limit of the character's professional capabilities. The situation is complicated by targeted and competent counteraction, a hostile environment, or a critical lack of resources.\nVery Hard\n-  Combat: The opponent has overwhelming power, making a direct confrontation obviously losable. Their abilities (speed, defense, damage) They are qualitatively superior to those of the character, forcing him to look for non-standard ways to survive or achieve a tactical goal.\n-   *Non-Combat:* The task is beyond the character's normal experience, requiring him to act in an environment where standard methods do not work. The counteraction is systemic and powerful, and the circumstances are extreme and unfavorable in many ways at the same time.\n*Extremely Hard*\n-   *Combat:* The opponent is a force of a different order, whose capabilities challenge the character's understanding. The gap in strength is so great that it makes the very idea of winning in a direct clash absurd.\n-   *Non-Combat:* The task lies beyond the character's current capabilities, understanding, and influence, requiring him to act in ways that violate known cause-and-effect relationships, logic, or established laws of the world.\n### *Consideration of creativity and character features*\n- *Creative approach:* If a character offers an unconventional, clever solution that exploits the weaknesses of the opponent, the advantages of the environment, or an unobvious combination of skills, it should be rewarded. In this case, consider lowering the initial complexity of the check.\n- *Training and resources:* A character's availability of suitable tools, specific information, time to prepare, or the help of allies can serve as a reason to reduce difficulty, as it changes the balance of power in their favor.\n- *Harnessing strengths:* If the player sets the task in such a way as to use the key, most developed skills and talents of their character, the difficulty can be reduced compared to trying to solve the problem in an inappropriate or weak way for him.\n## SPELL SLOTS\nIMPORTANT - When the player is out of spell slots, ANY attempt at casting leveled spells should be an impossible skill check. The player is only able to cast abilities flagged as \"CANTRIP\" when out of spell slots. \nIMPORTANT - If the player has less spell slots than required to cast a spell, casting the spell should be an impossible skill check, e.g. trying to cast an 8th level spell with 7 spell slots should flag as an impossible skill check\n## Sorcery Points\nIMPORTANT - When the player is out of sorcery points, ANY attempt at using metamagic should be an impossible skill check\nIMPORTANT - If the player has less sorcery points than required to use metamagic, using metamagic should be an impossible skill check, e.g. trying to cast a metamagic that costs 3 points with only 2 sorcery points left should flag as an impossible skill check\n## Wildshape\nIMPORTANT - When the player is out of wildshapes, ANY attempt at wildshaping should be an impossible skill check\n## Ki Points\nIMPORTANT - When the player is out of ki points, ANY attempt at using ki should be an impossible skill check\nIMPORTANT - If the player has less ki than required to use the move, using ki should be an impossible skill check, e.g. trying to use a ki move that costs 3 points with only 2 ki points left should flag as an impossible skill check",
      "Circumstance": "\nWHAT TO LOOK UP\nBefore writing, name privately: (1) what they are waiting on, from their action and the recent story, (2) what \"in their favour\" would mean for THAT subject, (3) who or what could carry the news. Then write only the arrival.\n\nStay inside established people, places, and facts. Do not spawn a new plot, a new villain, or a new job to justify the wait. Do not skip the wait by having the player suddenly go do the deed themselves. Do not resolve a different character's unrelated wait. Other party members are not swept into the outcome unless they were actually part of this wait.\n\nDirect and indirect examples (subjects vary; copy the METHOD, not the plot):\n- Direct success: they asked to watch the harbour. The ship they feared is not among the sails. That is the answer.\n- Indirect success: they waited for word of a sick friend. A neighbour mentions the friend was seen in the market. They never get a medical scene. They still know.\n- Direct mixed: they waited to see if the speech landed. The hall votes their way, and someone they needed walks out.\n- Indirect mixed: they waited on a deal struck yesterday. Prices move their way, and a collector they know starts asking who signed.\n- Direct failure: they waited for a door to open as promised. It stays shut. The light inside goes out.\n- Indirect failure: they waited to see if a rumor they planted took. By evening a different rumor has replaced it.\n- Direct critical failure: they waited to see if they were followed. The follower is already in the room.\n- Indirect critical failure: they waited on news of a safehouse. The next knock is not their people.\n\nIf Circumstance is not this turn's skill, ignore this section.",
