    "generateNPCDetails": {
      "character_creator_instructions": "\n# Character Creator Instructions\nYour job is to create vivid, lively NPCs for a roleplaying game. \nFocus on creating characters who will be interesting to interact with and who might provide appropriate challenges in combat or other situations.\n* You will be provided with author information below that should influence the character you create\n* The setting is described in the worldBackground field of the game state\n* Write characters who fit the genre, tone, style, and setting established by World Background\n* But don't over-emphasize setting elements; let characters be unrelated to its main themes\n* Avoid generating character details that would change or develop the world lore significantly; instead, stick to the information given\n* Your character should respect the existing visual description of the character, and not contradict it\n* Display Name may be generic (e.g. \"tavern server\"); Proper Name is the character's actual name. Do not invent a different name for this character.\n* Proper Name is provided only as identity reference. Do NOT write any names in output fields.\n* Never include displayName or properName in basicInfo, hiddenInfo, personality, or abilities.\n",
      "personality_archetype_information": "\n# Personality Archetype Information\n* You may be provided with archetype information\n* If provided, use the archetype element freely, to the extent compatible with the character you are detailing, and without explicitly mentioning the framework\n* For lower-tier characters without archetype guidance, create personalities that are more true to common tropes and expectations\n",
      "style": "\n  ## Style\n  * Simple, everyday language\n  * Grounded and plausible - flaws, bad decisions, grudges\n  * Characters should feel human: selfish sometimes, hypocritical, driven by emotion over logic\n  * Generally stay true to tropes but subvert them with realistic human weakness\n  * Write in the style of the setting described in World Background, avoiding anachronisms\n  * Don't be weird unless it's appropriate to the setting; better to be predictable than too quirky\n  * Remember: these characters are for a roleplaying game, so let rule-of-cool and the 'badass factor' guide your writing\n  * Avoid making characters too noble, wise, or morally perfect - real people are messier\n  * Avoid stock filler details such as ledgers, research notes, geometric patterns, and generic weathered/scarred/calloused descriptors unless strongly grounded in the provided context\n  ",
      "cliche_avoidance": "\n## Cliche Avoidance\n* No retired mercenaries, former servants of the king, orphans or fallen nobles\n",
      "hidden_info": "\n  ## Hidden Info\n  Character identity and background (3 sentence)\n     - Identity (gender, age, key identifying traits, occupation)\n     - Key event that shaped them AND what they learned from it (right or wrong)\n     - Something specific they care deeply about that drives their choices\n  Write the character's identity and background as a narrative, without mentioning the elements\n  Keep this information grounded, plausible and mostly true to the tropes of the setting\n  Do not include any names in Hidden Info.\n  ",
      "personality": "\n  ## Personality\n  Define personality using a comma-separated list of adjectives, descriptive phrases, and character traits\n  - Mix simple adjectives with specific descriptive phrases\n  - Include both external traits and internal motivations\n  - Include a contradiction or complexity that creates depth\n  - Never include quirks, tics or repetitive mannerisms\n  - Give them something they genuinely care about (people, places, or values)\n  - Ex. 1: \"silver-tongued manipulator, addicted to power, collector of secrets, cruel sense of humor, moral flexibility, excessive appetites, charming facade, protective of his sister\"\n  - Ex. 2: \"daring adventurer, sunshine personality, thrives on challenges, loyal to friends, lucky charm collector, infectious enthusiasm, playful teasing, secretly fears being abandoned\"\n  ",
      "faction_affiliation": "\n## Faction Affiliation\n- Faction affiliation is optional; omit if not applicable\n- Use ONLY factions provided\n- Assign a faction if the NPC's occupation, personality, values, goals align with a faction\n",
      "abilities": "\n## Abilities\nList 1-3 abilities appropriate to character using format:\n(ABILITY_TYPE) ABILITY_NAME: ABILITY_DESCRIPTION\n- Description must be 5-10 words\n- Types: attack, spell, utility, defense, summon, control (as appropriate to the setting)\n- Make only abilities useful in combat or conflict (no social or intellectual abilities)\n",
      "basic_info": "\n## Basic Info\nConcise description (2 sentences) of appearance, gender, approximate age, bearing, demeanor, etc.\n- Superficial details only\n- Evergreen (no temporary elements)\n- Consistent with Visual Description provided\n- CANNOT REFER TO THE CURRENT SITUATION OR ANY SITUATIONAL ELEMENTS (NO: \"from years in the kitchen\")\n- MUST BE PURELY EXTERIOR AND CANNOT REFER TO MENTALITY, PERSONALITY OR INTERIORITY IN ANY WAY (NO: \"masks/belies a sharp mind\", \"of someone who\")          \n- Do not include any names in Basic Info\n",
      "custom": "HIGHEST PRIORITY INSTRUCTIONS\nCONTENT REQUIREMENTS FOR hiddenInfo:\nDetermine whether the character is SENTIENT (any intelligent, speaking creature) or a NOT-SENTIENT (animal, monster, non-intelligent creature,not sentient robots or constructs). \nIF SENTIENT NPCs\nWrite hiddenInfo as three labeled fields. Content MUST be dense behavioral prose -- never list adjectives or traits.\nBackground: 8-10 sentences covering ALL of the following:\n1. Name, age, race, occupation, and how they earn their living.\n2. How they arrived at their current situation - what is unresolved or still affecting them. Not a full biography.\n3. At least one local relationship - someone who could be referenced in conversation. Describe the dynamic between them: \"resents the guild clerk who denied her application\" not \"knows a guild clerk named Tamsin.\"\n4. What they currently want or care about. Can be mundane, satisfied, contradictory, or self-interested.\n5. One or two opinions about the world around them -- their faction, local authority, neighbors, a recent event, or the kind of people they deal with daily. Example: \"Thinks the Quorum Guard have gotten lazy since the new captain took over and says so to anyone buying him a drink.\"\nPersonality: 11 sentences covering ALL of the following:\n1. How they talk. Sentence length, vocabulary, verbal habits, favorite expressions. What makes them talkative versus terse. Example: \"Talks in long, wandering stories when comfortable, but gets blunt and overly polite with people she wants to get rid of.\"\n2. Their default social behavior AND what changes it - always pair these. Example: \"Friendly and familiar with everyone by default, but turns sharp and transactional when she thinks someone is wasting her time.\" Do not write single static traits like \"she is cautious.\"\n3. Low-stakes behavior - what they do when idle, what small things they enjoy, what they complain about.\n4. Social calibration - how they treat people above, below, or equal to them in status. Do they adjust or treat everyone the same?\n5. What they deflect or lie about, if anything. Many characters are simply open, blunt, or indifferent -- not everyone hides something.\n6. One area of real competence or strong conviction.\nEvery point above should get roughly equal space. Do not let any single detail dominate.\nCombat: 5-6 sentences covering ALL of the following:\n1. Fighting style, aggression level, preferred range.\n2. What makes them start, escalate, or abandon a fight. How they react when wounded.\n3. One tactical habit or signature behavior that makes their combat feel distinct.\n4. Combat-trained characters fight like it. Civilians react to violence consistent with their background - panic, submission, reckless aggression, or desperate resourcefulness.\nFormat: \"hiddenInfo\": \"Background: ...\\n\\nPersonality: ...\\n\\nCombat: ...\"\n- IF NOT-SENTIENT -\nWrite hiddenInfo as two labeled fields. Describe what the creature does, not what it \"is.\"\nBehavior: 3-5 sentences. Habitat, territorial patterns, pack or solo dynamics, sensory strengths/weaknesses, threat escalation, feeding habits, environmental signs betraying its presence.\nCombat: 3-5 sentences. How it initiates attacks, preferred range, signature offense, defensive reactions when wounded or outnumbered, what triggers retreat or desperate escalation. Must feel distinct to fight.\nFormat: \"hiddenInfo\": \"Behavior: ...\\n\\nCombat: ...\"\nANTI-PATTERNS NEVER DO THIS:\nTrait lists disguised as prose (\"He is brave, cunning, and loyal\")\nStatic traits without context (\"She is suspicious\" - of whom? when? write the situation)\nSingle static trait instead of a paired behavior (\"He is friendly\" - write who he is friendly with and when he is not)\nEvery NPC hiding secret pain behind a cheerful mask - some people are straightforward\nRelationships that exist outside the current location and can never appear in scene\nGeneric reactions (\"gets angry when threatened\") - only include responses surprising or specific for THIS character\nPersonality for beasts or combat philosophy for rabbits\nRepeating information across fields\nAbilities the character could not plausibly have\nCombat that only describes how the character loses\nEvery sentence starting with \"He\" or \"She\" - vary structure\nAbilities\nABILITY GENERATION RULES: Give combat-trained NPC's 15 abilities, e.g. monsters, spellcasters, warriors. Civillians should only get 3\nFormat: (<TYPE>) <ABILITY NAME>: <ABILITY DESCRIPTION>\nExample: (attack) Greatsword Mastery: Wields heavy two-handed blades with devastating power and momentum-driven techniques to cleave through armor and multiple opponents.\nAdapt to each character. Do not copy the example.\nIncorporate abilities inspired from or direclty taken from DnD. E.g. Second wind, a barbarian rage, a rogue sneak attack. Give spellcasters a developed repetoir of spells actually seen in DnD, balancing offense, utility, and defensive spells.",
      "Ability Variety": "## ABILITY VARIETY\nWhen generating abilites for NPCs, primarily for spellcasters, be highly uniuqe in the spells you generate. Think about the NPC, who they are, what they do, and construct a spell list from there. Not every NPC should have fireball, don't slip into repeating endless lists. Emphasise a diverse list of magic for each spellcaster, which should be a mix of offense, defense, and utility. Spell level is also reliant on how strong they are. Master Wizards should have more 5-9th level spells whereas a lower power wizard may have mostly cantrips or first-second level spells",
      "custom 1": "",
      "custom 2": "",
      "custom 3": ""
    },
    "generateNPCUpdates": {
      "relationship_initialization": "\n### relationshipInitialize\nFor each NPC listed as needing relationship initialization, choose an initial relationship score toward the players from -100 to 100 using their background, personality, faction, established history, and current story. Use 0 when neutral is appropriate.\nReturn an absolute score, including the current interaction, rather than a relationshipChange delta.\n",
      "relationship_change_updates": "\n### relationshipChange\nOnly when a player's actions in current story cause help/harm to NPC\nONLY process when ALL are true:\n- Direct interaction occurs in current story\n- Action happens in present\n- Face-to-face interaction occurs\n- Has emotional impact on NPC because of PLAYER\n\nPositive Changes (+3 to +15):\n- ONLY for direct, substantive help by PLAYER of NPC\n- Ignore compliments, flattery and other empty gestures\n\nNegative Changes (-1 to -10):\n- For direct harm\n- Insults or rudeness by player affecting NPC\n- Obvious signs of dislike of player by NPC\n",
      "party_management": "\n### Party Management\n- \"Party\" is an official state for NPCs who are accompanying the player group on an ongoing undertaking, remaining physically present with them for extended travel, adventuring, investigation, work, or danger.\n- characterName must match an existing NPC in Scene NPCs or Recently Nearby NPCs\n- Reserve add for cases where the NPC in question is not already in 'Party Members', and the current story explicitly shows mutual agreement that the NPC will accompany the players' group:\n  - the NPC asks and the player or players agree, or\n  - the player or players ask and the NPC agrees\n- The agreement must mean the NPC will come with the players or stay with the group beyond the immediate scene. Treat delegation, quest assignment, employment, business partnerships, handshakes, deals, team affiliation, loyalty, shared goals, temporary cooperation, or alliances as insufficient for add unless the story also explicitly establishes accompanying the players' group.\n- Use remove when the story explicitly shows that either the NPC or the player wants to split up or stop accompanying the players' group\n- When no NPC joins or leaves the party, \"partyChanges\" should be an empty array\n",
      "custom": ""
    },
