  "realms": {
    "Faerûn": {
      "name": "Faerûn",
      "basicInfo": "A vast and diverse continent on the planet Toril, Faerûn is the heart of the Forgotten Realms. This land of high fantasy spans from the frozen wastes of Icewind Dale in the north to the steaming jungles of Chult in the south, and from the Sword Coast's bustling ports in the west to the mysterious lands of Kara-Tur in the east. Magic permeates every aspect of existence here, flowing through ley lines and wielded by wizards, clerics, and sorcerers. Ancient civilizations have left their mark in countless ruins, while great city-states like Waterdeep, Baldur's Gate, and Neverwinter serve as bastions of civilization. The continent is home to diverse races and cultures, from humans and elves to dragonborn and tieflings. Powerful deities actively shape events through their followers, while ancient evils lurk in the Underdark below and dragons rule from mountain strongholds. Political intrigue, guild conflicts, and the secret machinations of organizations like the Zhentarim and Harpers constantly reshape the political landscape. It is a world where adventure awaits around every corner and heroes can rise to challenge the gods themselves.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Underdark": {
      "name": "Underdark",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, interconnected network of subterranean tunnels and caverns spanning the world beneath the surface of Faerûn. It is a realm of eternal darkness and ancient, terrifiying evils where bioluminescent fungi provide the only light. The environment is deadly, filled with dangerous predators and isolated drow city-states.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Nine Hells": {
      "name": "Nine Hells",
      "basicInfo": "A plane of absolute law and evil, composed of nine descending layers of torment ruled by powerful archdevils. It is a place of infernal contracts, political machinations, and soul-harvesting, serving as the ultimate destination for the wicked. The realm is a stark contrast to the mortal world, defined by cold, fire, and the rigid hierarchy of its devilish inhabitants.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Astral Plane": {
      "name": "Astral Plane",
      "basicInfo": "The boundless Silver Void that stretches between the planes of existence. The Astral Plane is a realm of timeless, star-speckled emptiness, where gravity holds no sway and thought propels movement. Colossal, petrified husks of dead gods drift in eternal silence, their bodies carved into cities by the githyanki, who rule this realm from their capital of Tu'narath. Colour pools swirl like liquid windows, offering passage to the Outer Planes and beyond. Here, time is suspended; creatures do not age, hunger, or thirst, but their minds can be lost in the infinite silence. It is a place of profound isolation and strange beauty, where the echoes of forgotten deities linger, and the githyanki patrol on red dragon back, hunting illithid stragglers and raiding worlds across the multiverse. The Astral Plane is a crossroads for planar travelers, a graveyard of gods, and a frontier of psionic mystery, where the very fabric of reality is thin enough for a single thought to reshape one's surroundings.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Fugue Plane": {
      "name": "Fugue Plane",
      "basicInfo": "A grey, featureless plane of waiting, suspended between the Material Plane and the Outer Planes. Here, the souls of the dead gather after passing from the world, drawn by the call of Kelemvor, the Lord of the Dead. The Fugue Plane is the first stop on the soul's journey, where it is judged and sent to its appropriate afterlife in the realms of the gods. At its heart stands the Crystal Spire, where Kelemvor sits in judgment, and the City of Judgment, a sprawling necropolis where souls await their fate. The Faithless, those who rejected all gods, are entombed in the Wall of the Faithless, a terrible edifice of fused souls, fading into nothingness. The plane is a place of profound stillness and order, where time seems suspended and only the will of Kelemvor holds sway. The air is cold and dry, and a perpetual twilight sky offers neither sun nor stars. Psychopomps, the servants of Kelemvor, guide the dead, while demons and devils sometimes attempt to steal souls for their own purposes.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Mount Celestia": {
      "name": "Mount Celestia",
      "basicInfo": "The Seven Heavens, a single colossal mountain of pure law and good rising from a silver sea into radiant, unreachable heights. Each of its seven layers is a higher terrace of the ascent, and each is holier, brighter, and harder to reach than the last. Souls of the virtuous climb the mountain across ages, growing in perfection as they rise, until the worthiest are subsumed into the blinding light of the summit. Mount Celestia is the celestial home of the Faerûnian gods of justice, mercy, duty, and protection: Tyr the Even-Handed, Torm the True, Ilmater the Crying God, and the platinum dragon Bahamut, along with the archon host that guards every switchback of the sacred road. The mountain is defended not by hatred of evil but by an overwhelming, patient goodness; fiends who set foot upon its lower slopes are burned by the very holiness of the ground. Crystal rivers pour down its flanks, healing all who drink, and the higher one climbs, the more one's own flaws are gently stripped away. It is a plane of second chances, of redemption earned through effort, and of a peace so profound that even the memory of suffering fades. Yet the mountain is not passive: the archons wage an eternal, disciplined vigil against the incursions of the Lower Planes, and the paladins of the living world look to Celestia as the source of their oaths.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Bytopia": {
      "name": "Bytopia",
      "basicInfo": "The Twin Paradises, a plane of two facing worlds joined at their heights, where honest labor and untamed wilderness meet in perfect balance. Bytopia is unique among the Upper Planes: it consists of two great layers, Dothion and Shurrock, that hang above one another like the two halves of a hollow sphere, their skies meeting in a shared band of light at the center where the gravity of each gives way to the other. To travel from one to the other, a soul climbs the highest peaks until 'up' becomes 'down' and descends into the opposite world. Dothion is the gentle, cultivated half: a paradise of tidy farms, snug villages, skilled craftsfolk, and industrious gnomes and halflings who find deep joy in good work honestly done. Shurrock is the wild, rugged half: a paradise of untamed forests, soaring mountains, and free-roaming beasts, where the goodness of nature runs unchecked and the strong protect the weak beneath open skies. Together they express Bytopia's central truth: that the good life balances diligent labor with the freedom of the wild, that civilization and nature are not enemies but partners. It is the celestial home of the gnomish and halfling gods, of Garl Glittergold and Yondalla and their kin, and a haven for every soul that found its virtue in honest work, simple pleasures, and the protection of home and hearth. Bytopia is a plane without grand cosmic drama, and that is precisely its glory: it is the heaven of the ordinary good, of the well-tilled field and the well-made tool and the neighbor helped in need.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Elysium": {
      "name": "Elysium",
      "basicInfo": "The plane of pure, restful, unconditional good, where kindness needs no reason and peace is not the absence of struggle but its reward. Elysium is the heaven of the compassionate heart — the destination of souls who loved freely, gave generously, forgave truly, and sought no recompense for their goodness. Where Mount Celestia's good is disciplined, ordered, and ever-vigilant, and Arborea's is wild and passionate, the good of Elysium is gentle, warm, and complete: a plane of golden meadows, sun-dappled forests, still lakes, meandering rivers, and rolling green country beneath a sky of soft, forgiving light, where every living thing radiates and receives benevolence. It is the celestial home of the guardinals — the noble beast-souled celestials (avoral, leonal, ursinal, equinal, cervidal, lupinal, musteval) who embody good without law or chaos, protectors and comforters who defend the plane out of pure compassion rather than duty. Elysium consists of four great layers or realms, each a river-fed country of its own: Amoria, the golden entry-land of the River Oceanus; Eronia, the wilder middle country of forests and hills; Belierin, the storm-wracked coastal layer where the good stands against wilder forces; and Thalasia, the deep, still, contemplative layer of the great sea and the passage beyond. The River Oceanus, the shining silver-blue river of good that flows through all the Upper Planes, has its headwaters here and threads all four layers together, carrying souls and celestials gently from one to the next. Elysium has no fortresses of ambition and few grand institutions; its glory is the quiet, total goodness of its every field and grove and shore. Yet it is not naive: the guardinals know that a plane defended by compassion alone must be defended fiercely, for the Lower Planes forever probe the borders of a paradise that keeps no walls, and the yugoloths — the mercenary fiends who serve no cause but their own — hold a special hatred for a plane whose selfless good is the antithesis of everything they are. Elysium is rest earned, love without condition, and peace that must, paradoxically, be guarded to endure.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Beastlands": {
      "name": "The Beastlands",
      "basicInfo": "The plane of untamed nature and the wild goodness of the animal soul, a boundless, cityless wilderness where the beast and the wild are sacred and where civilization simply does not exist. The Beastlands are the heaven of pure nature: not the benevolent-but-settled wild of Bytopia's Shurrock nor the river-threaded pastoral of Elysium, but wilderness absolute — trackless forests older than any world, endless plains, deep jungles, high mountains, and vast night-woods, all of it alive with beasts of every kind, from the smallest insect to the mightiest primal predator, each perfect and sacred in its own nature. Here there are no roads, no buildings, no farms, no permanent works of any hand, for the Beastlands reject all of that; the plane belongs to the animals and to the souls who loved the wild and the beast in life. Its most striking feature is that the boundary between person and animal dissolves here: petitioners take the forms of beasts, celestials are themselves great animal-spirits, and even visitors slowly find themselves growing more bestial the longer they stay, sprouting fur or feather, gaining fangs and instincts, until the unwary may forget they were ever anything but a beast of the wild. The plane consists of three layers that follow the cycle of a single day: Krigala, the layer of eternal noon and bright open wilderness; Brux, the layer of eternal twilight, dusky and transitional; and Karasuthra, the layer of eternal night, deep and predatory and primal. It is home to the animal lords — the great archetypal spirit-beasts who embody each kind of animal — and to the nature gods and their beast-servants, and it is defended not by armies but by the fierce, instinctive protectiveness of the wild itself. The Beastlands' goodness is the goodness of nature living true to itself: the predator and the prey, the hunt and the flight, the fierce mother and the loyal pack, all held in a sacred balance where cruelty and corruption have no place, but where the wild is genuinely wild, and the soft-hearted visitor who mistakes it for a petting-garden may be swiftly, instructively humbled.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Arborea": {
      "name": "Arborea",
      "basicInfo": "The plane of wild passion, fierce beauty, and untamed goodness, where every emotion runs deep and every joy and sorrow is felt to its fullest — the chaotic-good heaven of the elven gods, the Olympians, and every soul who lived and loved with a full and burning heart. Arborea (also called the Olympian Glades) is goodness set aflame with passion: not the disciplined ascent of Mount Celestia nor the restful compassion of Elysium, but a realm of towering mountains, endless forests, wine-dark seas, and blazing emotion, where beauty is worshipped, art is sacred, love and rage and grief and joy burn without restraint, and the good is expressed through passionate creation and fierce, spontaneous heroism rather than through order or gentle rest. It is a plane of glory and excess, where the wine flows freely and the songs never end, where heroes feast and quarrel and love and weep on a scale grander than any mortal world, and where the raw creative and emotional force of goodness runs at full flood. Arborea consists of three great layers: Arvandor, the mighty first layer of soaring mountains and deep forests, home of the elven gods (the Seldarine) and the Greek Olympians, the beating heart of the plane's wild beauty and passion; Aquallor, the second layer of the great infinite ocean, wine-dark and beautiful, where the sea-gods dwell and the deep passions run; and Mithardir, the third layer of white desert and dust, the fading, melancholy realm of dead and forgotten gods, beautiful in its desolation. Arborea is defended not by disciplined legions but by the spontaneous fury of its passionate inhabitants — elven warriors, Olympian heroes, and wild celestial spirits who need no orders to rush to the defense of beauty and goodness. Its emotions are its glory and its danger: the same passion that makes Arborea the most vividly alive of all the Upper Planes can tip, in its extremes, toward obsession, jealousy, and destructive excess, and the plane walks forever the fierce, exhilarating edge between passionate good and passion's own undoing.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Ysgard": {
      "name": "Ysgard",
      "basicInfo": "The plane of eternal glorious struggle, heroic battle, and the deeds that echo forever, where the brave die gloriously only to rise again at dawn and fight once more — the chaotic heaven of the Norse gods, the great warrior-souls, and every spirit who lived boldly and sought greatness through struggle. Ysgard (also called Gladsheim or Asgard) is goodness expressed through courage, striving, and the reckless embrace of a hard, magnificent existence: not the peace of Elysium nor the ordered ascent of Celestia, but a wild, roaring realm of endless battle-glory, roaring mead-halls, towering deeds, and the fierce joy of a life lived at the edge of the blade. Here the fallen heroes of a thousand worlds feast and fight in the great halls, dying each day in glorious combat and rising whole each dawn to fight again, for in Ysgard death in battle is not an ending but the price and proof of a life lived boldly. The plane's most striking feature is its geography of vast earthbergs — enormous floating slabs of earth, some the size of continents, drifting through an endless sky, their undersides ablaze with fire, so that the ground itself is impermanent and adventure waits over every edge. Ysgard consists of three great layers: Ysgard itself, the first layer of the endless earthbergs, the mead-halls of the Norse gods, and the eternal glorious battle; Muspelheim, the second layer of fire, the burning realm of the fire giants and the forge of the fierce; and Nidavellir, the third layer of the deep dark, the subterranean realm of the dwarves and their endless mines and forges. It is home to the Aesir and Vanir — Odin, Thor, Frigga, and the Norse pantheon — and to the warrior-souls who fill the halls of the slain. Ysgard's goodness is fierce and free rather than gentle, defended by the reckless valor of heroes who need no orders to charge into glorious battle, and its danger is the danger of all its passion: that the love of struggle can tip into mere violence, that glory can curdle into pride, and that a plane built on endless war walks forever the knife-edge between heroic greatness and destructive chaos.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Limbo": {
      "name": "Limbo",
      "basicInfo": "The Ever-Changing Chaos, a plane of raw, untamed creation where matter, energy, and thought swirl in an endless storm of possibility. Limbo is a realm without fixed form or geography; its landscape shifts constantly, shaped only by the will of those who impose their own order upon the chaos. The githzerai, masters of mental discipline, maintain stable monastic sanctuaries amidst the turbulence, while the slaadi, embodiments of chaos, spawn from the Spawning Stone at the plane's heart. Elemental pockets of fire, water, earth, and air boil and vanish, and the very air crackles with unstructured magic. Here, a single focused thought can shape reality for a time, but distraction or weakness means dissolution back into the primal soup. It is a realm of infinite potential and endless danger, where only the strong-willed survive and the mad are truly at home.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Pandemonium": {
      "name": "Pandemonium",
      "basicInfo": "The Windswept Depths of absolute chaos and madness, Pandemonium is a plane of endless, screaming tunnels bored through solid darkness by winds that never cease. There is no natural light, no stable ground, and no silence—only the howling gale that drives all who hear it to insanity. The plane is divided into four layers, each more oppressive than the last, connected by shafts and passages that channel the wind into ever-more-deafening shrieks. Here, exiled powers, mad gods, and the deranged are imprisoned or choose to dwell, their minds already broken. The air is frigid, the darkness absolute, and the only landmarks are the bones of those who lost their way. It is a realm where the strong-willed survive only by embracing madness, and where even the gods tread carefully.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Abyss": {
      "name": "The Abyss",
      "basicInfo": "The Infinite Layers, the bottomless pit of the multiverse, a realm of absolute, unending evil. The Abyss is not a single plane but an ever-proliferating collection of layers, each a unique hell of demonic design. Countless layers exist, each ruled by a demon lord or left to the whim of its own madness. The River Styx winds through many layers, carrying souls and filth, while the Blood War between demons and devils rages eternally. Here, the tanar'ri spawn from the raw chaos of the Abyss, and the landscape is a shifting nightmare of fire, ice, acid, and flesh. The air is thick with the stench of decay and the howls of the damned. There is no order, only strength, and the strong rule until they are devoured by the stronger. The Abyss is not merely a place of punishment; it is the source of all that is chaotic, destructive, and irredeemable in creation.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Carceri": {
      "name": "Carceri",
      "basicInfo": "The Tarterian Depths, the prison plane of the multiverse, a realm of exile and punishment for traitors, backstabbers, and those who betrayed the trust of the gods. Carceri is composed of six nested spheres, each layer enclosing the next like the layers of an onion, and each more terrible than the last. The River Styx winds through the layers, carrying the damned to their final cells. The landscape is harsh and unforgiving: swamps of black water, jungles of acid, deserts of dust, mountains of jagged rock, frozen seas, and finally a sphere of absolute cold and darkness. The plane is guarded by the demodands, fiendish wardens who take pleasure in tormenting the prisoners. Escape is nearly impossible, for the spheres are self-contained and the only ways out are heavily warded. Carceri is a place of despair, where hope itself is a punishment.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Hades": {
      "name": "Hades",
      "basicInfo": "The Gray Waste of Hades, a plane of absolute despair, where all color drains from existence and hope itself decays. Known also as the Three Glooms, it is divided into three layers: Oinos, the battleground of the Blood War; Niflheim, a frozen realm of mists and forgotten memories; and Pluton, the silent depths where dead gods lie. The River Styx winds through the plane, its black waters carrying souls to their final dissolution. Here, the yugoloths, the fiends of pure evil, make their home, and night hags trade in misery. The air is gray, the ground is gray, and even light seems to dim as if suffocated by apathy. It is a realm where emotion is the ultimate sacrifice, and those who linger too long find themselves fading into nothingness, their memories and identities eroded into the eternal gloom.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Gehenna": {
      "name": "Gehenna",
      "basicInfo": "The Bleak Eternity, a plane of four immense volcanic mountains drifting in an endless void, each layer a separate peak of black rock, ash, and frozen fire. The slopes are impossibly steep, and the craters belch smoke and embers into the starless sky. Here, the yugoloths, fiends of pure evil, make their mercenary camps and forge their weapons of war. The plane is a place of grinding ambition, endless labor, and cold calculation. Souls sent here are worked to the bone in mines and forges, their lives a monotony of pain and toil. The only law is the contract, and the only pleasure is the suffering of others. Gehenna is a realm of fear and oppression, where even the strong live in constant dread of betrayal.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Acheron": {
      "name": "Acheron",
      "basicInfo": "The Infernal Battlefield, a plane of endless, meaningless war waged across colossal iron cubes that drift in an infinite void. The sky is a black emptiness, and the ground is made of rusted metal, splintered bone, and the detritus of a thousand forgotten battles. Four layers comprise Acheron: Avalas, where the great armies clash and the legions of Gruumsh and Maglubiyet hold sway; Thuldanin, a scrapyard of broken weapons and war machines; Tintibulus, a realm of silent, floating cubes where magic itself is twisted; and Ocanthus, a frozen sea of razor-sharp ice shards whipped into a perpetual storm. Souls consigned here are pressed into military service, their identities ground away by the endless carnage. The plane is a monument to the futility of war, where even victory is hollow and the only certainty is death.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Mechanus": {
      "name": "Mechanus",
      "basicInfo": "The Clockwork Nirvana, a plane of absolute law and order, composed of an infinite field of interlocking gears, cogs, and pistons that turn in perfect synchrony. The sky is a grid of mathematical precision, and the light comes from a source of pure white radiance that never wavers. Here, the modrons, the geometric embodiments of law, march in their endless processions, performing their assigned tasks with flawless efficiency. Primus, the One and the Prime, rules from the city of Regulus, directing the modron hierarchy and the operations of the plane. The air hums with the sound of machinery, and the ground moves beneath one's feet as the gears turn. It is a place of utter predictability, where even a single unexpected variable is an aberration to be corrected. Souls consigned here find peace in absolute routine, their individualities subsumed into the great machine of existence.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Arcadia": {
      "name": "Arcadia",
      "basicInfo": "The Peaceable Kingdoms, a plane of harmonious order and gentle goodness, where law and good are perfectly balanced. Arcadia is composed of three layers, each a realm of ordered beauty: Abellio, a land of perfect farmland and well-tended orchards under an eternal golden afternoon; Buxenus, a carefully managed wilderness of symmetrical forests and placid rivers; and Nemausus, the corrupted third layer, once a realm of pure light now overgrown with a poisonous, tangled jungle after a failed attempt to impose rigid order. The plane is home to deities of community, protection, and order, such as Clangeddin Silverbeard, Cyrrollalee, and formerly the Harmonium. Souls here find peace in honest labor, craftsmanship, and the quiet satisfaction of contributing to the greater good. The air is fresh and clear, the light warm and steady, and the inhabitants are welcoming but expect all to follow the rules. Arcadia is a place of quiet contentment, where even the wilds are tamed and the harvest is always bountiful.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Outlands": {
      "name": "Outlands",
      "basicInfo": "The Concordant Domain of the Outlands, the plane of true neutrality and the fulcrum of the Great Wheel. At its center rises the Spire, an impossibly tall needle of stone that pierces the sky, and around it, a ring of sixteen gate-towns, each attuned to a different outer plane. Beyond the gate-towns stretch the Hinterlands, a wild and varied landscape of plains, forests, deserts, and mountains, where the laws of alignment are weakest and magic behaves unpredictably. The Outlands is not a place of judgment or reward, but of balance and transition. It is home to the rilmani, the metallic-skinned exemplars of neutrality, and to the Lady of Pain's city of Sigil, which floats above the Spire. Here, all paths cross, and all alignments mingle in an uneasy truce. The plane is a crossroads for planar travelers, a refuge for the unaligned, and a reminder that the multiverse is held together by the delicate equilibrium between extremes.",
      "known": true
    }
  },
  "regions": {
    "Evermeet": {
      "name": "Evermeet",
      "basicInfo": "The Green Isle, the final refuge of the Elves. It is a paradise of towering spires, magical forests, and eternal peace, located thousands of miles to the west (conceptually placed here for the map). Non-elves are forbidden from setting foot here on pain of death. It is the center of elven high magic and culture, a piece of Arvandor on the Material Plane.",
