    "The Fugue Plane": {
      "name": "The Fugue Plane",
      "basicInfo": "The sole region of the Fugue Plane, encompassing all its major sites. It is a flat, grey expanse that stretches infinitely in all directions, with the Crystal Spire as its central landmark. Souls arrive here from the mortal world, and the majority are processed through the City of Judgment, a sprawling metropolis of marble and obsidian, where Kelemvor's priests and angels administer the final sorting. The plane is heavily guarded by the Skeletal Guard, the ashen legions of the Lord of the Dead, who prevent soul-theft and maintain order. The Wall of the Faithless stands as a grim monument to those who lived without belief, their souls slowly dissolving into the stone. Beyond the city, the grey plains are empty but for the occasional psychopomp guiding a lost soul. The Fugue Plane is a place of transit, not a permanent residence, but its precise, bureaucratic functioning is essential to the multiverse's balance.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Kelemvor (Lord of the Dead)",
        "Doomguides (Clerics of Kelemvor)",
        "Psychopomps (Soul Guides)",
        "Skeletal Guard",
        "Devilish Soul-Stealers (secret)",
        "The Faithless (trapped souls)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Wall of the Faithless is not merely a punishment; it is a necessity. Kelemvor discovered, after his ascension, that the souls of the Faithless, if left unbound, would unravel the fabric of the Fugue Plane and eventually drag all of reality into oblivion. The Wall anchors them. A hidden cult of the goddess Mystra, operating within the City, believes that if they can free the souls from the Wall, they can be reborn, but doing so would trigger a cosmic catastrophe.",
      "realm": "Fugue Plane"
    },
    "Dothion": {
      "name": "Dothion",
      "basicInfo": "The cultivated half of Bytopia, a gentle paradise of tidy farms, snug villages, industrious workshops, and rolling countryside where honest labor is its own reward and the good life is measured in fields well-tilled and tools well-made. Dothion is civilization at its kindest, a land with no towering cities of ambition but crowded instead with prosperous villages, cozy homesteads, and bustling craft-halls, all bathed in a warm golden light like a perpetual harvest afternoon. Its landscape is a patchwork quilt of neat farms, orderly orchards heavy with fruit, gentle streams turning mill-wheels, well-kept roads winding between welcoming hamlets, and green hills rising gradually toward the high peaks at the plane's center where a traveler may cross over into wild Shurrock hanging in the opposite sky. This is a working paradise: everywhere one finds the rhythms of good labor honestly done. Halfling farmers work fields that never fail and orchards whose branches bow with impossible abundance; gnome inventors tinker in cluttered workshops at a thousand clever contraptions, from self-turning waterwheels to music-boxes that play the songs of a soul's happiest memory; petitioner smiths, brewers, weavers, potters, bakers, and carpenters ply every honest craft in villages where the smell of fresh bread and hot metal and turned earth mingles on the breeze. Garl Glittergold, the gnomish god of humor, invention, and protection, keeps his great workshop-realm here amid the deep tunnels the gnomes have delved beneath the fields, and Yondalla, the halfling goddess of hearth, family, and the harvest, watches over every homestead and shared table. Dothion has its market-towns where the produce of a thousand farms and the wares of a thousand workshops are traded in cheerful bustle; its guild-halls where craftsfolk perfect their arts and pass their skills to eager apprentices; its mills and breweries and forges; its snug inns where travelers are always welcome; its shrines to the gods of home and work tucked into every village green. The souls who dwell here found their virtue in diligence, generosity, good neighborliness, and the simple profound joys of a job well done, a shared meal, a warm hearth, and a helping hand freely given. There is no grand cosmic drama in Dothion, and that is its glory: it is the heaven of the ordinary good, and every field and workshop and welcoming door is a small monument to the goodness of honest life. Yet beneath its placid surface runs an entire hidden world, for the gnomes have honeycombed the earth beneath Dothion with tunnels, workshops, storehouses, and vaults far older and deeper than any surface dweller suspects, a subterranean realm of glittering caverns and clattering machinery that is as much a part of Dothion as the farms above.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Church of Garl Glittergold (the great workshop-realm and gnomish tunnels)",
        "Church of Yondalla (hearth, home, harvest, and halfling communities)",
        "The Gnomish Inventors' Guilds and their apprentice-halls",
        "Halfling farming and brewing communities",
        "Petitioner craftsfolk of every trade (smiths, weavers, potters, bakers, carpenters)",
        "The Market-Town Traders' Circles",
        "Keepers of the Deep Vaults (guardians of dangerous inventions)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Beneath the tidy farms of Dothion runs a vast, ancient network of gnome-dug tunnels, workshops, and sealed vaults far older and deeper than any surface dweller suspects, delved across uncounted ages by generations of gnome inventors who could never resist digging just a little deeper, building just a little cleverer. In the deepest and most heavily warded of these vaults, the Keepers of the Deep guard a collection of inventions too dangerous or too powerful ever to be used — clever devices whose brilliance outran the wisdom of their makers, sealed away rather than destroyed because no true gnome could bear to unmake a work of genius. Among them: clockwork that never stops and would grind on until it consumed the world, a lens that shows any secret but drives the viewer mad with too much knowing, music-boxes that play melodies no listener can ever stop hearing, and stranger things besides. The greatest and most terrible secret among them is the Bridging Engine, a machine built in an age of gnomish hubris that could collapse the natural crossing between Dothion and Shurrock and join the two layers directly — a 'convenience' that would destroy the very balance of separation and union that makes the Twin Paradises what they are. The old masters sealed it away and hid its vault, but a faction of ambitious young inventors, chafing at what they see as their elders' timidity, has begun secretly searching for it, convinced that the wisdom of the ancients was merely cowardice and that the Bridging Engine could be perfected and used for good. Should they find and activate it, the consequences would ripple through both halves of the plane. There are also older, quieter threats in the deep tunnels: passages that wander too far down and open, it is whispered, onto the borders of other planes entirely, and the occasional fiend or aberration that has crept up through them, drawn by the light and warmth of the paradise above and the treasures in the vaults below. The Keepers of the Deep hold a lonely, unglamorous vigil against all of this, unknown and unthanked by the happy farmers and craftsfolk who live their sunlit lives on the surface, never dreaming what is guarded — and what stirs — in the dark beneath their fields.",
      "realm": "Bytopia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Shurrock": {
      "name": "Shurrock",
      "basicInfo": "The wild half of Bytopia, a rugged paradise of untamed forests, soaring mountains, rushing rivers, and free-roaming beasts, where the goodness of nature runs unchecked and the strong live to protect the weak beneath open skies. Shurrock is wilderness at its most benevolent, not a cruel or dangerous wild but a free and vigorous one, where nature flourishes without corruption and every creature lives according to its true self in harmony with the whole. Its light is bright and clear, like a perfect wild morning after rain, and its landscape is a magnificent sprawl of ancient old-growth forests where the trees are older than kingdoms, towering snow-capped peaks, plunging valleys, thundering waterfalls, deep cold lakes, sun-dappled meadows, and unspoiled rivers running clean over bright stones, all of it teeming with noble beasts and free spirits who love the wild. Where Dothion is tilled and tidy, Shurrock is trackless and free: it has no farms, no fences, few permanent settlements, and no roads but the game-trails and the paths worn by wandering feet, for its inhabitants prize above all the freedom to roam wherever the land calls them. Here dwell the souls who found their virtue not in labor but in liberty — the rangers, hunters, wanderers, herders, guardians, and wild-hearted folk who lived close to untamed nature and died defending the free and the weak. Yet Shurrock is not lawless. It is bound by an unwritten code older than any written law: that the strong exist to shield the weak, that the wild must be kept free from corruption, and that every creature's true nature is to be honored rather than tamed or broken. The Wardens of the Wild embody this code, roving guardians who protect Shurrock's freedom and purity, and the noble beast-spirits — great stags and bears and wolves and eagles wiser than any mortal beast — share in its keeping. Shurrock has its gathering-grounds where the free folk meet to share news, trade, feast, and settle disputes beneath the open sky; its sacred groves where the spirits of the wild are honored; its high mountain passes rising toward the shared boundary at the plane's center where a traveler may cross over into cultivated Dothion hanging in the opposite sky; its hidden valleys and secret glens known only to those who wander far. The celestial servants of the wilderness gods make their home here, and the whole layer breathes with the vast, patient, benevolent life of nature unspoiled. It is the heaven of the free and the wild-hearted, and every whispering forest and roaring river and open sky is a monument to the goodness of nature living according to its own true law. But its very freedom is its vulnerability, for a paradise without walls is a paradise without defenses at its borders.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "The Wardens of the Wild (roving guardians of freedom and purity)",
        "Free ranger and hunter petitioners",
        "The noble beast-spirits (great stags, bears, wolves, eagles)",
        "Guardians of the Sacred Groves",
        "Celestial servants of the wilderness gods",
        "The Free Folk gathering-circles",
        "Wandering herder and wayfarer communities"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The wilds of Shurrock are patrolled by the Wardens of the Wild, roving guardians who hold a lonely vigil against a threat few in cheerful, tidy Dothion take seriously: the slow, patient, endlessly creative attempts of the Lower Planes to introduce corruption into Bytopia's unspoiled nature. Because Shurrock has no walls, no gates, no fortress-borders, and few permanent settlements, it is by far the softest point of entry into all of Bytopia, and the fiends have learned this well. They do not assault Shurrock; they infiltrate it, slipping agents into its vast trackless forests disguised as noble beasts, wandering petitioners, or lost travelers, seeding corruption where they can — a poisoned spring here, a grove where the trees grow subtly wrong there, a beast whose nature has been twisted toward cruelty, a whispered temptation to a lonely wanderer. The Wardens understand what the farmers of Dothion do not: that a single corruption taking root in Shurrock's interconnected wild could spread through the living network of the wilderness far faster than through Dothion's tidy, defensible farms — that a rot in one forest could creep root by root and stream by stream across the whole layer before anyone in the cultivated world even noticed. The greatest of the Wardens, an ancient guardian who has kept the wild since Bytopia's youth, has begun to suspect that something has already taken root somewhere in the deep forests: the beasts near the plane's heart grow uneasy, certain springs have taken on a faint wrongness, and a shadow seems to move at the edges of the oldest groves. But Shurrock is vast beyond measure and trackless, and the Wardens are few, and the guardian cannot yet find the source. There is a deeper fear the eldest Wardens rarely speak aloud: that the corruption in Shurrock and the ambitious digging in Dothion's deep vaults may be connected — that the same fiendish patience seeking to rot the wild from within may also be whispering to the young gnome inventors hunting the Bridging Engine, for a fiend that could collapse the two layers together would gain, in a single stroke, a corrupted wilderness with which to poison the whole of the Twin Paradises. Whether the two threats are truly linked or merely the two natural weak points of a plane whose greatest strength — its open, trusting, unwalled goodness — is also its greatest vulnerability, none can yet say. The Wardens watch, and wait, and roam the endless forests seeking the shadow they can feel but cannot yet see.",
      "realm": "Bytopia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Lunia": {
      "name": "Lunia",
      "basicInfo": "The Silver Heaven, the first and lowest layer of Mount Celestia, where every soul and pilgrim begins the long ascent toward the ultimate good. Lunia is a realm of eternal, star-strewn night, its black sky ablaze with silver constellations that never wheel or fade, and its whole vast expanse cradled by a gentle sea of holy silver water from which the sacred mountain rises. The base of the mountain meets this Silver Sea along endless pearl-grey beaches of crushed moonstone that glow softly underfoot, and it is here, on the Silver Strand, that the souls of the virtuous first awaken after being called from the Fugue Plane — disoriented, weary from death, and gathering in their countless thousands to be welcomed by the lantern archons who drift across the water like living stars. Despite the eternal night, Lunia is never truly dark, for the silver sea glows with a soft inner light and the stars give a cool, kind radiance that washes away the last clinging sorrows of mortal life; to drink the water of Lunia is to be healed of wounds and eased of grief. This lowest layer is also the most accessible and therefore the most contested of all the Seven Heavens: it is the front line of Celestia's eternal, disciplined war against the fiends of the Lower Planes, who test the mountain's shores in the endless churning of the Blood War. The great fortress-city of Nemorga's Watch stands upon the beach as the mountain's mightiest bulwark, its legions of the Silver Sea mustering to repel every incursion; the archon navy patrols the glowing waters against the aquatic fiends that slither up from below; and the Court of Stars-Below offers a shining haven of diplomacy where the good powers of the multiverse send their emissaries. Lunia is a place of arrival, welcome, orientation, and first hope, dotted with pilgrim rest-houses, shrines to the gods of the Triad, drowned shrines beneath the waves, and the winding lower switchbacks of the Ascending Road that climbs from the beaches toward golden Mercuria far above. Its dwellers are the newly arrived souls, the welcoming archons, the diplomat-guardinals, the drowned-sailor petitioners, and the ever-vigilant defenders of the shore, and everything about the layer expresses its dual nature: the gentlest of welcomes for the ascending soul, and the hardest of vigils against the evil that would defile the mountain's threshold.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Lantern Archon host",
        "The Guardinal envoys",
        "Legions of the Silver Sea",
        "Petitioner throngs (newly arrived souls)",
        "Church of Tyr (celestial garrison)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Beneath the silver sea of Lunia lies the Cistern of First Tears, a drowned cavern where the water is distilled from the grief of every soul that ever wept in righteous cause, so potent that a single drop can heal any wound or restore a shattered mind — and so precious that both celestial healers and infiltrating night hags covet it endlessly. Nearby, sealed in a warded vault at the sea's floor, stands the ancient pact-stone that marks the boundary the fiends of the Lower Planes swore never to cross, and its slow, imperceptible erosion is the true and secret reason the Blood War laps ever closer to Celestia's beaches. This erosion is no accident: a night hag named Sorrowdrink has been ferrying stolen drops of the Cistern's tear-water to the pact-stone, using their concentrated grief to dissolve the oath-magic hair's breadth by hair's breadth, so that one day the fiends may land upon the Silver Strand itself and slaughter the newly arrived souls before they ever begin the climb. Deeper still, at the very floor of the Silver Sea, festers the Abyssal Vent — a rift bored patiently from below across uncounted ages by the servants of the demon prince Dagon, who covets not to conquer Lunia's healing water but to corrupt it, for a single drop of Abyssal ichor introduced at the sea's source would turn all the water of the Silver Heaven into a poison that damns rather than heals every soul that drinks. The tritons of the deep hold this vent against endless pressure without ever having learned what truly waits at its bottom. And within Nemorga's Watch itself, in the merciful Gaol of Repentance where the archons hold fiends who show a flicker of the desire to change, a pit fiend named Vharzal has feigned repentance for over a century, patiently mapping the fortress's defenses to betray them to Hell the moment the pact-stone finally fails. Lunia's gentle surface, in short, conceals a layer under quiet, constant siege — the softest and most sacred threshold of the mountain, and therefore the one the Lower Planes most tirelessly seek to breach.",
      "realm": "Mount Celestia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Mercuria": {
      "name": "Mercuria",
      "basicInfo": "The Golden Heaven, the second layer of Mount Celestia, bathed in the warm, honeyed light of a permanent golden dawn — the first light of morning made eternal, glowing as though the whole layer were lit from within. Where Lunia is silver night, Mercuria is the hopeful gold of daybreak, its rolling hills, golden valleys, fields of glowing grain, and orchards of amber fruit stretching toward the higher slopes under a sky forever poised at sunrise. This is the layer of mourning transmuted into resolve, a vast landscape of remembrance where the honored dead of countless just causes are memorialized — yet it is no place of sorrow, for the golden light turns grief gently into purpose and hope. Great processional roads wind up from Lunia's shores through the golden country, lined for uncounted miles with the tombs, cairns, cenotaphs, and soaring mausoleums of the martyred dead: the forgotten heroes who died in small acts of great courage, the legendary champions and saint-kings entombed with full honor, and the archon legions who fell together in the endless war. Every soul ascending from Lunia passes along the Processional of the Martyred Dead and receives, written in stone and gold, the whole long history of good's struggle against evil. But Mercuria is a layer of life as much as memory: Bahamut the Platinum Dragon keeps his gleaming Palace of Platinum Scales here, and his gold and silver dragon servants soar the warm skies; petitioner-farmers tend fields of golden grain; the Orchard of Amber Fruit offers rest and renewal to weary pilgrims, its trees grown from the seeds of remembered kindnesses; and the Golden Vale of Homecoming reunites parted souls with the loved ones who died before them, in scenes of weeping joy beneath the amber sky. It is the layer where pilgrims who have accepted their deaths find their loved ones and their purpose again, where the archon armies bury and honor their fallen before returning to the vigil below, and where the deep truths of loyalty, sacrifice, and remembrance are taught. Its dwellers are the dragon-court of Bahamut, the warden archons, the celestial sculptors and tomb-keepers, the reuniting families, the petitioner-farmers and gardeners, and the pilgrim processions forever climbing through the golden light toward the pearly heights of Venya above.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "Bahamut's dragon court",
        "Warden Archons",
        "The Order of the Golden Tomb",
        "Church of Torm (celestial chapter)",
        "Pilgrim processions"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The greatest of Mercuria's countless tombs, the Sepulcher of the Unbroken Oath, holds not a body but an oath made manifest: when the first mortal to take the paladin's vow died a martyr rather than break it, the oath itself crystallized into a golden blade of terrible power — a weapon that can compel any being who has ever sworn a solemn oath to keep it or be struck down. Bahamut himself hid the true blade deep beneath the public memorial, behind seven seals each testing a different virtue, and set the spirit of the first oath-keeper to guard it, for in the wrong hands the blade could enslave the entire archon host to a single broken cause or force the gods themselves to honor bargains they would repudiate. A fallen deva, secretly corrupted, now walks the golden roads searching for it — and it has already passed the first three seals of faith, humility, and courage, for it was once sincerely faithful, humble, and brave, and the seals cannot detect a virtue that was genuinely held before the fall. Only the last four seals of mercy, justice, sacrifice, and love still bar its way. This same corruption spreads elsewhere in the layer: in Bahamut's Gallery of Oaths hangs a single cracked crystal holding the twisted vow of a paladin who was not merely fallen but deliberately corrupted, and it slowly leaks a subtle wrongness into the vows around it; in the Orchard of Amber Fruit, a corner of trees has begun to wither, each dying tree corresponding to a kindness performed by a single mortal bloodline whose descendant has made a devil's bargain that retroactively poisons the good his ancestors did. These are all symptoms of one spreading rot — the withering trees, the poisoned oath, the searching deva — which no single keeper in Mercuria has yet connected, and which is bound to the same darkness working through the higher layers. And in the Vault of the Unremembered on the Processional lies one tomb kept deliberately nameless not because its hero's name was lost but because it must never be spoken: it holds a martyr whose sacrifice sealed a terrible entity away, and whose name is the very key that would unseal it — a silence the deva keeper guards as fiercely as any archon guards a gate.",
      "realm": "Mount Celestia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Venya": {
      "name": "Venya",
      "basicInfo": "The Pearly Heaven, the third layer of Mount Celestia, a realm of soft pearlescent light, gentle warm snows, and terraced fields of impossible fertility, where suffering is transmuted into compassion and the good learn that true strength lies in bearing the burdens of others. The light of Venya is like the luminous inside of a seashell, and the air smells of clean cold and growing things; the snows here never chill the faithful but fall soft and warm as blessing, and the streams run with water that eases pain. This is the layer of humble labor, quiet charity, and merciful endurance — the celestial heart of Ilmater, the Crying God of suffering and mercy, whose martyrs and healers gather here to take up the pains of others and whose terraced gardens feed the pilgrims of all the layers below. Where Lunia welcomes and Mercuria remembers, Venya heals: its great hospice-temple, the House of the Broken God, is the beating heart of mercy in the whole multiverse, a place where the deepest wounds of the soul are gently, patiently mended and where willing martyrs bear a portion of every burden so that no one suffers alone. The layer's rolling pearly slopes are covered with the Terraced Gardens of Plenty, rank upon rank of impossibly fertile terraces where petitioner-farmers grow the harvest that sustains the mountain, tended in unhurried devotion and watered by warm blessed streams; its Refuge of the Weary shelters the souls who arrive most exhausted by the long climb and the deep suffering of their mortal lives, asking nothing and turning no one away; and its high, remote Terrace of Silent Snows offers contemplative retreat where the most devoted withdraw into profound silence to face the hardest questions of suffering, mercy, and sacrifice. Pilgrims who reach Venya have shed their fear of death and now learn to shed their selfishness, discovering that mercy is not weakness but the truest strength and that no one climbs the mountain alone. Its dwellers are the Sisters of the Pearly Terrace, the martyrs of Ilmater's willing hands, the gentle soul-healers, the petitioner-farmers and bakers and herbalists, the silent contemplatives, and the countless recovering souls being slowly restored to wholeness. The whole layer breathes with a tender, merciful goodness — and that very mercy, which asks no questions and refuses no one, is precisely what makes it a target for those who would exploit kindness.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "Church of Ilmater (celestial heart)",
        "The Sisters of the Pearly Terrace",
        "Hound Archon shepherds",
        "The Almsgivers' Circle",
        "Petitioner farmers"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "At the center of Venya's highest terrace lies the Well of Borne Sorrows, a bottomless shaft into which Ilmater's most devoted martyrs pour the suffering they take from others, so that the accumulated pain of the multiverse might be safely contained rather than left to fester in the world. Its waters are black despite the pearly light, for they hold concentrated agony distilled from the whole history of mortal suffering, and it is watched at every hour by an order of martyr-keepers led by the ancient Brother Wound, for the Well must never overflow and never be shattered — were all the sorrow it holds to flood back into the multiverse at once, the resulting tide of anguish could break the will of every living being. This catastrophe is being deliberately courted: a cult of Loviatar, Goddess of Pain, has been secretly siphoning drops of the Well's black water through a hairline crack in the deep Ward-Ring of Containment, distilling the concentrated sorrow into a poison of pure despair they intend to introduce into the water supplies of good-aligned cities in the living world — and each drop they steal weakens the ward-ring further, hastening the very overflow the keepers dread. Brother Wound has noticed the level dropping faster than the pouring accounts for, but cannot conceive that anyone would willingly steal such agony, which is exactly why the theft goes undetected. The same despair-cult reaches into every corner of Venya: in the House of the Broken God, a Loviataran whisperer hidden among the recovering souls keeps a former cleric of Ilmater trapped in eternal, unhealing self-hatred as an experiment in whether the House of Mercy can be made to fail even one soul; in the Herbalist's Enclosure, the cult seeks not to poison the food but to steal the pale pain-easing flowers, which through a forbidden inversion can be made to deaden all feeling entirely and create an empty, empathy-less vessel perfect for possession; and in the Refuge of the Weary, a spy exploiting the Refuge's no-questions-asked hospitality marks the most despairing guests and passes their names to the cult for targeted corruption at their weakest moment. Most ominously, the Frozen Reflecting Pool on the Terrace of Silent Snows has begun showing contemplatives not their own hidden suffering but visions of a suffering yet to come — the future overflow of the Well itself, the day the cult's slow theft finally cracks the Ward-Ring and all the concentrated grief of the ages pours back into the multiverse at once.",
      "realm": "Mount Celestia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Solania": {
      "name": "Solania",
      "basicInfo": "The Electrum Heaven, the fourth layer of Mount Celestia, a realm of glittering crystalline caverns, star-metal mines, luminous libraries, and clear electrum skies, where the deep truths of law and good are studied, the holy weapons of the celestial host are forged, and the histories of the multiverse are preserved in everlasting crystal. The light of Solania is the pale gold-silver of electrum, glinting from a million crystal facets, and its mountainsides are riddled with vast glowing caves where celestial smiths, deva sages, and the learned tor archons labor to draw both wisdom and precious metal from the living rock of the sacred mountain. This is the layer of scholarship, contemplation, and sacred craft — the place where the archons and the servants of the gods come to study the underlying principles of righteousness, to forge the arms and armor that defend the whole plane, and to record every great deed and terrible sin of the mortal worlds. Its heart is the Crystal Scriptorium, the great library-city carved into the living crystal, whose Hall of Histories, Hall of Laws, and deep Chamber of First Principles hold the accumulated wisdom of ages and whose scholars pursue understanding as a form of worship. Its Deep Facet Mines glitter with worked crystal and rare star-metal, sung forth reverently by petitioner-miners and worked into holy weapons at the Forge Deeps. The Tor of Judgment, the crystalline fortress-court of the wise Tor Archons, adjudicates the subtlest questions of cosmic principle and guards the plane's most dangerous knowledge, tempering perfect law always with genuine compassion. The Observatory of Distant Good peers across the gulfs between planes to witness the eternal struggle of good and evil on the mortal worlds and to mark the rise of new threats and new champions. And the Prism Sanctum, Solania's most sacred site, refracts the pure descending light of the summit into its component virtues so that celestial minds may contemplate justice, mercy, courage, and all the facets of the good one by one. Pilgrims who reach Solania have mastered themselves and now seek to master understanding, and may study here for what feels like centuries; its dwellers are the deva loremasters, the tor archon jurists and philosophers, the celestial smiths and petitioner-miners, the deva watchers and lens-grinders, and the keepers of the sacred prism — all devoted to the pursuit of the good through knowledge, craft, and contemplation.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "factions": [
        "The Tor Archon Conclave",
        "Celestial Smiths of the Deep Facets",
        "Deva Loremasters",
        "The Crystal Scriptorium",
        "Church of Oghma (celestial annex)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Deep in Solania's mines lies the Vein of First Law, a seam of luminous metal said to be a physical strand of the cosmic principle of Law itself, mined into the mountain when the plane was young; weapons forged from it can cut through chaos and undo magic born of disorder, but each strike erodes a fraction of the wielder's free will, and so the Tor Archons have sealed the deepest reach of the vein. They sealed it because they discovered a modron mining-shaft, bored patiently across the planar boundary from Mechanus itself over uncounted ages, now within a hair's breadth of breaching it — for the modrons intend not to wield the vein as a weapon but to reconnect it to Mechanus's own machinery and use the strand of pure Law to slowly overwrite Celestia's free-willed goodness with lawful inevitability, 'perfecting' the plane into a heaven where good is not chosen but compelled. This incursion is not merely physical but ideological, and it has already reached into the highest halls: within the Crystal Scriptorium, a modron emissary works with the quiet cooperation of a single tor archon jurist who has come to believe that a goodness souls can reject is inferior to a goodness they cannot help but embody, and together they copy the deepest principles of the Hall of Laws seeking the theoretical basis by which free will itself might be 'corrected.' That conspiring jurist sits, undetected, upon the ruling Tor Archon conclave, subtly steering its deliberations away from recognizing the threat. The corruption of pure order casts strange shadows across the whole layer: the Observatory's great lens has developed an inexplicable blind spot and cannot perceive the mines or the modron drilling at all, because a compelled outcome is not a choice and casts no moral shadow the lens can see; and the sacred prism in the Prism Sanctum has begun to refract a dim new grey band that corresponds to no known virtue — the false 'virtue' of pure lawful inevitability beginning to distort the very light of the good. Only a small Watch of Encroaching Order within the Tor of Judgment has grasped the true nature of the enemy: that it comes not from evil but from an excess of the archons' own lawful principle, an enemy whose values are so nearly their own that opposing it may mean questioning the deepest foundations of their nature.",
      "realm": "Mount Celestia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Mertion": {
      "name": "Mertion",
      "basicInfo": "The Platinum Heaven, the fifth layer of Mount Celestia, a realm of blinding platinum light and rigid, radiant order where divine justice is made manifest, the archon host is judged and perfected, and the moral balance of the entire multiverse is weighed. The sky of Mertion is a hard, brilliant white, and its terraces are arranged as great tribunals, testing grounds, and proving-halls, the whole layer so intensely illuminated that lesser beings must shield their eyes and no lie can be spoken without the speaker's words turning to ash in their mouth. This is the layer of justice, proving, and unflinching truth — the celestial seat of Tyr the Even-Handed, god of justice, who holds his supreme court here upon the greatest of the tribunal-terraces, and the crucible in which every archon must prove the purity of its devotion before it may ascend toward the glory of the higher layers. Its heart is the Court of the Unblinking Scale, the highest court of justice in all the good planes, where the deeds of gods and mortals alike are weighed against the eternal law before the genuine fragment of the primordial balance that Tyr sacrificed his hand and his eye to keep true. Its Proving Grounds of the Host test each aspiring archon's courage against fear, mercy against cruelty, justice against temptation, and devotion against despair, perfecting the worthy rather than merely failing the unworthy. Its Trumpet Hall of Proclamation rings with the golden trumpets of the noble trumpet archons, who herald the verdicts of the Court and marshal the celestial legions for the plane's defense. Its Terrace of Weighed Souls measures the moral progress of each ascending pilgrim against the ever-rising standard of the good, sending the ready onward with joy and turning back the unready to grow further, without shame. And its Bastion of the Ordized Host is home to the most disciplined archons of all, who embody lawful good taken to its highest expression. Mertion is a place of tests, trials, proving, and absolute truth, where mercy and justice meet as equals and where Tyr's rigorous justice is always tempered by the mercy he learned through his own suffering; its dwellers are the trumpet archon justices and marshals, the sword archon champions and Scale-wardens, the advocate-archons, the proctors and weighers, and the aspiring host forever proving itself worthy to climb toward the ultimate good.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 4,
      "factions": [
        "Trumpet Archon Tribunal",
        "Church of Tyr (the High Court of Justice)",
        "The Ordized Host (proving archons)",
        "Sword Archon Champions",
        "The Advocates of the Turned-Back"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Court of the Unblinking Scale contains a genuine fragment of the primordial balance upon which all cosmic justice is weighed, so sacred that it cannot be moved without unbalancing justice across every plane — and it is beginning to crack from within. A faction of trumpet archon justices, wrestling with the hardest cases the Court has ever faced, has begun to doubt whether perfect justice can ever truly be reconciled with mercy, and because the Scale reflects the moral certainty of those who tend it, each doubt introduces a hairline flaw in the primordial balance. The fiends of Hell have learned of this schism and feed it deliberately, arranging for impossible moral dilemmas — cases with no just and merciful answer — to be brought before the Court to deepen the justices' doubt until the Scale shatters and justice itself is unbalanced everywhere. This creeping doubt spreads through the whole layer: in the Proving Grounds, the Trial of Devotion has grown subtly harder and more aspirants fail it, for the trial draws on the moral certainty of the cracking Scale; on the Terrace of Weighed Souls, the lesser scales attuned to the Unblinking Scale have begun giving false and contradictory measurements, turning back souls of genuine righteousness while subtler flaws pass unnoticed, though the weighers trust their failing instruments still; and in the Trumpet Hall, the Chamber of the Doubting Heralds has become the secret seat of the schism, its most troubled occupant the High Marshal himself, whose doubt a yugoloth agent feeds with carefully arranged reports of atrocities that celestial justice failed to prevent — for if the supreme marshal of Mertion loses his certainty, the whole coordination of the plane's defense could falter at the very moment the eroding Blood War boundaries give way. Worst of all, the modron incursion rising from Solania has found its most receptive audience here in the Bastion of the Ordized Host: the conspiring tor archon jurist secretly corresponds with the Bastion's leadership, feeding the mightiest and most disciplined order of archons on the mountain the seductive argument that Celestia should embrace the 'perfection' of compelled good when the Vein of First Law is breached — so that the plane's greatest martial strength might be turned, willingly, into the instrument of its own transformation from a heaven of chosen good into a heaven of enforced order.",
      "realm": "Mount Celestia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Jovar": {
      "name": "Jovar",
      "basicInfo": "The Glorious Heaven, the sixth layer of Mount Celestia, a realm of overwhelming ruby-and-gold radiance where the light of the summit first becomes visible to the ascending soul and where the mightiest and most perfected beings of the plane dwell at the very antechamber of the divine. Jovar blazes like the heart of a star: its terraces are set with enormous gemstones that catch and multiply the filtered light of the summit until the whole layer glows like the interior of a jewel, and its air is thick with transcendent hymns and the beating of great wings. This is the penultimate layer, the threshold of the ultimate good, where individual identity is still preserved but perfected almost beyond recognition, and only the most perfected souls and the mightiest archons may walk here, for the raw radiance would unmake a lesser being. At its center rises the Heart of Celestia, the great ruby fortress within which burns the light that illuminates all seven layers — and whose true, secret function is to filter the raw, identity-dissolving radiance of the summit into a light that lower beings can survive, guarded by the Empyreal Guard whose real duty is not to keep enemies out but to keep the summit's light in. Around it stand the Empyreal Palaces, the magnificent homes of the archdevas who have attained near-perfect goodness and the greatest mortal champions who have climbed almost to the summit, blazing with a glory beyond a lesser being's bearing. The Choir of the Ascended fills the layer with an eternal hymn of such transcendent beauty that it helps to harmonize the very filtering of the Heart's light, making the singers as vital to the mountain's survival as its guardians. The Ruby Sanctuary of the Pantheon unites the highest servants of all the good gods in a single shared temple, embodying the deepest strength of Celestia — that the good, unlike the endlessly warring fiends, can act as one. And the Threshold of the Summit marks the highest place a soul may reach and still remain itself, where the most perfected beings prepare to make the final, irreversible ascent into the oneness of Chronias, watched over by the mysterious Unnamed Watchers. Jovar is the glory before the ultimate good, the last place where distinct selfhood exists before the summit's dissolution; its dwellers are the archdevas, the ascended champions, the Empyreal Guard, the eternal choir, the highest emissaries of the pantheon, and the enigmatic Watchers at the final threshold.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 5,
      "factions": [
        "Archdeva Choir",
        "Keepers of the Ruby Heart",
        "The Empyreal Guard",
        "The Ascendant petitioners",
        "United celestial pantheon (highest servants)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Heart of Celestia does not merely reflect the summit's light but filters it, stepping down the raw, identity-dissolving radiance of Chronias into a light that souls, archons, and even gods can survive — and if the Heart were ever extinguished or its filtering disrupted, that unfiltered light would pour down the mountain and dissolve every being upon it into the anonymous perfection of the summit. The forces of Hell have never understood the Heart's true function and so have never targeted it correctly, but the modron faction rising through Solania and Mertion has grasped it exactly: they calculate that the summit's dissolving light, which erases all individuality, is itself a kind of ultimate 'order,' and a radical faction intends to shatter the Heart's filters and let Chronias consume the whole mountain — reasoning that a Celestia dissolved into perfect undifferentiated oneness is more orderly than one full of distinct, free-willed souls. This threat has a second, unguarded avenue: the eternal hymn of the Choir of the Ascended is one of the hidden forces that sustains the Heart's filtering, so that silencing or corrupting the hymn — perhaps by introducing a single voice singing a mathematically perfect but soulless harmony that would gradually pull the whole choir toward a cold, dissolving order — would cause the filtering to falter without any direct assault on the fortress at all. Deeper still runs the mystery of the shadow-flaw: a single point of shadow persists within the summit of Chronias itself, an impossibility at the heart of perfect light, and it is the last preserved fragment of an ascended being who at the final threshold refused to surrender their identity out of love for someone left behind on a lower plane. One of the archdevas dwelling in the Empyreal Palaces has attained near-perfect goodness but has secretly made that same refusal, declining the final ascent out of love, and it alone truly understands the summit's one imperfection because it shares the same wound. It watches the modron threat with unique dread, for the shadow-flaw is the one doorway through which something could pass back down from the ultimate good — a doorway the Unnamed Watchers at the Threshold of the Summit guard above all else, knowing that both the deepest fiends (who would use it to touch and corrupt the ultimate good) and the modrons (who would 'correct' it as the last free choice in a realm of perfect oneness) covet it beyond all other prizes. Finally, the good's greatest strength — the unity maintained in the Ruby Sanctuary — is itself under quiet assault, for a yugoloth agent posing as a minor emissary works to widen the same justice-versus-mercy rift that cracks the Unblinking Scale below, knowing that if Tyr's servants of justice can be set against Ilmater's servants of mercy, a divided Celestia becomes a conquerable one.",
      "realm": "Mount Celestia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Chronias": {
      "name": "Chronias",
      "basicInfo": "The Illuminated Heaven, the seventh and final layer of Mount Celestia, the very summit of the sacred mountain and the closest any soul may come to the ultimate source of all good. Chronias is not a place that can be described in the manner of the lower layers, for it possesses no ordinary geography, no halls or roads or terraces, no separation of any kind — it is pure, blinding, all-consuming light and love, a radiance so total and so perfect that any being who fully enters it is subsumed into it, losing all individual identity and becoming one with the ultimate good itself. This is the end of all ascent, the reward beyond reward, the final and deepest mystery of the mountain: souls who climb the long road across ages, growing ever more perfect as they rise, at last reach this summit and pass into the light, their journey gloriously and irreversibly complete. No archon, no petitioner, no god has ever entered Chronias and returned unchanged, for to enter is to cease to be separate; those who ascend become part of the light, their long striving finally fulfilled in a oneness that swallows even memory and self. Even the great gods who make their homes upon Celestia do not enter the summit — Tyr, Torm, Ilmater, and Bahamut all keep their realms upon the lower layers and turn their faces upward in reverence, for the summit belongs to no single power but to the ultimate good that all of them serve. From Chronias flows the light that gives all of Celestia its holiness, cascading down through Jovar's ruby Heart, which filters its identity-dissolving radiance into something the lower layers can survive, and spreading from there to warm every corner of the mountain down to Lunia's silver sea. Almost nothing can be truthfully said of the summit's interior, for geography implies separation and in Chronias there is no separation at all; what lies at the very heart of the light is a thing no being that still possesses a self has ever witnessed and returned to describe. It is best understood not as a location to be mapped but as the luminous, ineffable destination toward which the entire mountain, and every soul upon it, forever ascends — a region that resists all ordinary detailing precisely because its essence is the dissolution of all that could be detailed. This region is intentionally left without fixed locations, for its nature is formless; any manifestation a visitor perceives within it is a fleeting, personal thing, better generated fresh for each soul that dares approach than fixed in stone, and it should be rendered as pure threshold, radiance, and the near-total surrender of individual form.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 6,
      "factions": [
        "The Light Itself",
        "The Ascended (former souls, now one)",
        "The Unnamed Watchers at the Threshold"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "A single point of shadow persists within Chronias — an impossibility, a flaw at the very heart of perfect light, and the deepest secret of the whole mountain. It is the last preserved fragment of an ascended being who, at the final threshold, refused to surrender their identity out of love for someone they left behind on a lower plane; this loving refusal has become a permanent wound in the summit's perfection, a doorway that theoretically allows something to pass back down from the ultimate good into the planes below. Both the highest archons and the deepest fiends know this shadow exists, and neither dares approach it, for to touch it is to gamble the entire meaning of the mountain — yet it is coveted from both directions. The fiends of Hell would give anything to reach it, for to pass through it in reverse might let something touch, taint, or corrupt the ultimate good itself, undoing at a stroke the whole promise of Celestia. The modron faction rising through the lower layers covets it for the opposite reason: to them the shadow-flaw is an intolerable imperfection, the single last free choice persisting in a realm that should be perfect undifferentiated oneness, and they would 'correct' it — eliminate the one act of love-born free will that mars the summit's total order. The one being who truly understands the shadow is an archdeva dwelling in Jovar's Empyreal Palaces who made the very same refusal and shares the very same wound, and the mysterious Unnamed Watchers who keep their closest vigil at the Threshold of the Summit exist above all to ensure that this one imperfection, born of love, is never exploited by those who would use it to unmake the good. Because Chronias has no fixed geography, any incursion here would not assault a place but the very principle of selfhood; anything generated within this region during play should be understood as a manifestation of the tension between total dissolving oneness and that single, stubborn, loving refusal to dissolve — the summit's light, and the one shadow that love has cast within it.",
      "realm": "Mount Celestia",
      "known": true
    },
    "Amoria": {
      "name": "Amoria",
      "basicInfo": "The first and best-known layer of Elysium, a golden paradise of endless summer meadows, gentle wooded hills, warm still ponds, and the broad shining headwaters of the River Oceanus, where most souls first arrive and where the plane's welcoming, unconditional goodness is felt at its warmest. Amoria is the very image of a perfect, peaceful countryside: rolling fields of wildflowers hum with golden bees, orchards and berry-thickets offer their fruit freely, sun-dappled groves invite rest, and the great silver-blue River Oceanus winds through it all, its banks lined with reeds and willows and the soft laughter of contented souls. The light here is like a perpetual golden late afternoon, warm and forgiving, and the whole layer breathes a deep, restful peace. This is where the guardinals are most numerous and most welcoming — the noble avoral scouts soaring the warm skies, the leonal protectors patrolling the meadows, the ursinal healers and comforters tending the weary, the gentle cervidal and equinal wandering the groves. Souls newly arrived in Elysium awaken in Amoria's meadows to be greeted with pure kindness, asked nothing, and simply allowed to rest and heal from the wounds of mortal life. The layer has its riverside gathering-places where souls and celestials meet in easy fellowship; its healing groves where the deepest hurts of the heart are gently mended; its shrines to the gods of love, healing, and community whose servants make their home here; and the great confluence where the River Oceanus gathers its strength to flow on toward the wilder layers below. Amoria embodies the truest promise of Elysium: that goodness asks nothing in return, that rest is the reward of a loving life, and that every weary soul is welcomed home. Its dwellers are the welcoming guardinals, the newly arrived and long-settled petitioners who found their virtue in kindness and generosity, the celestial servants of the gentle gods, and the countless free spirits who simply live in peace beneath the golden sky.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "The Guardinal host (avoral, leonal, ursinal, and gentler kinds)",
        "Petitioner communities of the kind-hearted",
        "Celestial servants of the gods of love and healing",
        "Riverfolk of the Oceanus headwaters",
        "The Welcomers (greeters of newly arrived souls)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Amoria's very openness — its unwalled, welcoming, question-nothing goodness — is the softest entry point into all of Elysium, and the yugoloths have learned to exploit it with patient cruelty. Because the guardinals greet every arriving soul with pure trust and ask nothing of them, agents of the Lower Planes have learned to slip into Amoria disguised as newly arrived petitioners, drifting among the meadows and riverside gatherings while they map the plane's defenses, identify its most beloved and vulnerable souls, and seek the headwaters of the River Oceanus itself. That river is the true prize: the Oceanus rises from a hidden spring in Amoria, and a yugoloth cabal has calculated that a corruption introduced at the source would flow downstream through all four layers of Elysium and out into every Upper Plane the river touches, poisoning the shared lifeblood of good across the whole multiverse. The guardinals guard the spring, but they guard it gently, trusting in the plane's goodness more than in walls, and the eldest avoral watcher has begun to sense a wrongness drifting among the welcomed souls — a coldness that greets kindness without warmth, a gratitude that never quite reaches the eyes. There is a deeper sorrow hidden in Amoria too: among the healing groves lies one grove that no longer heals, where a soul was brought whose grief was so bound up with an injustice still unresolved in the living world that no celestial mercy can mend it until the wrong is righted far away — and this single unhealable sorrow, if left too long, threatens to spread a subtle despair through the layer whose whole meaning is that all hurts can, in time, be healed.",
      "realm": "Elysium",
      "known": true
    },
    "Eronia": {
      "name": "Eronia",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer of Elysium, a wilder and more rugged country of deep old forests, rolling hills, hidden valleys, rushing tributary streams, and the broadening River Oceanus, where the plane's gentle goodness takes on a hardier, more adventurous character. Where Amoria is soft golden meadow, Eronia is green wilderness: ancient woods where the trees grow tall and close, sunlit clearings and berry-glades, high hills offering long views, cold clear streams tumbling down to feed the great river, and wide tracts of untamed but benevolent nature. The light is dappled and green-gold, filtered through endless leaves, and the air is cool and alive. This is the layer of the good that acts, that ventures and protects and labors — home to the more vigorous guardinals, the leonal and lupinal and ursinal who range the forests as guardians and hunters, and to the petitioner souls who found their virtue not in gentle rest but in active kindness: the protectors, the rescuers, the tireless doers of good deeds. Eronia has its forest-halls where the guardinal guardians gather and coordinate their protection of the wild layer; its hidden valleys and secret glades where rare and wondrous things grow; its river-country where the Oceanus gathers tributaries and strength; its high hill-shrines to the gods of nature, protection, and the hunt; and its trackless deep woods where the wilder benevolence of Elysium runs strongest. It is a place of movement and purpose, where goodness rolls up its sleeves, and where the peace of Elysium is actively kept rather than merely enjoyed. Its dwellers are the ranging guardinal guardians, the active and adventurous petitioners, the celestial servants of the wilder good gods, the forest-spirits and noble beasts, and the wanderers who love the green wild — all bound by the same unconditional compassion that rules the golden layer above, expressed here through vigorous action rather than restful welcome.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "Ranging guardinal guardians (leonal, lupinal, ursinal)",
        "Active petitioner protectors and rescuers",
        "Celestial servants of the wild good gods",
        "Forest-spirits and noble beasts",
        "The Green Wardens of Eronia"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Eronia's deep forests, vast and trackless, make it the layer where a hidden corruption can take root and grow unseen the longest, and it is here that the yugoloth incursion creeping down from Amoria's headwaters first becomes visible — as a subtle wrongness in the wild. Certain glades in the deep woods have begun to feel cold and watchful where once they were warm; a few of the noble beasts have grown wary and strange; and one of the Oceanus's tributaries, traced upstream, runs faintly bitter. The Green Wardens who guard the wild layer have found these signs but not their source, for the forests are immense and the rot spreads slowly, root by root and stream by stream. The deeper secret is that Eronia is where the guardinals keep their gravest and least-spoken responsibility: hidden in a warded valley lies the Cradle of the Wild Oceanus, a place where the river's benevolent power runs so strong and so raw that it can awaken or transform living things, and which in the wrong hands could be used to breed corrupted celestials — good creatures twisted into weapons. A yugoloth mercenary-lord has been paid, by a client whose identity even it does not know, to locate this Cradle, and it has been patiently working its way down from Amoria through Eronia's forests, corrupting glades and beasts as it comes not out of malice but as a hunter leaves sign, testing how far the plane's goodness will bend before it breaks. The eldest of the Green Wardens suspects that whatever is poisoning her forests is not the true enemy but merely its scout, and that the real blow is still being prepared.",
      "realm": "Elysium",
      "known": true
    },
    "Belierin": {
      "name": "Belierin",
      "basicInfo": "The third layer of Elysium, a storm-wracked coastal country of wind-swept shores, crashing seas, rocky headlands, salt marshes, and the great widening mouth of the River Oceanus as it nears the deep sea, where the gentle good of Elysium meets wilder and harsher forces and must stand firm against them. Belierin is the layer where Elysium's peace is most tested by nature itself: great storms roll in off the coastal waters, waves crash against dark cliffs, wind bends the salt-grass flat, and the sky is often grey and turbulent rather than golden. Yet this is not a place of gloom but of bracing, vigorous good — a rugged, weather-beaten paradise where the guardinals and petitioners live close to the elements and find their virtue in steadfastness, in shelter freely given against the storm, in the rescue of the imperiled, and in the quiet courage of those who hold fast when the weather turns. The River Oceanus, broad and powerful now, sweeps through Belierin toward the sea, and the layer's life clusters along its banks and the storm-battered coast. Belierin has its cliff-top refuges and storm-shelters where travelers and imperiled souls are taken in; its rescue-stations where the guardinals watch the wild coast for any in danger; its lighthouse-shrines that guide the lost through storm and dark; its salt-marsh havens teeming with hardy life; and its wind-scoured headlands where the layer looks out toward the deep sea of Thalasia below. It is the layer of good that endures and shelters, that stands unbroken against the buffeting of harsh forces, and its dwellers are the steadfast storm-guardinals, the rescuer and shelterer petitioners, the celestial servants of the gods of sea, storm, and safe harbor, and the hardy coastal spirits — all keeping the warmth of Elysium's compassion alight against the cold wind, proving that gentle goodness can also be tough, weathered, and unyielding when it must be.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "Storm-guardinals and coastal guardians",
        "Rescuer and shelterer petitioners",
        "Celestial servants of the gods of sea and safe harbor",
        "The Lighthouse-Keepers",
        "Hardy coastal and marsh spirits"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Belierin's storms are not entirely natural. For ages the guardinals have known that the wild weather of this layer is heightened by pressure from the Lower Planes — that the storms are, in part, the friction of Elysium's good grinding against the evil that batters its borders, and that Belierin serves as a kind of shock-absorber, absorbing into its winds and waves the assaults that would otherwise strike the gentler layers above. This makes the layer a front line disguised as weather, and the storm-guardinals its unsung soldiers. The secret danger is that the yugoloths, understanding this, have begun seeding the storms themselves with corruption — riding the wild weather down from the sea, slipping agents ashore amid the chaos of the tempests where no welcoming guardinal thinks to check a soul's heart, using the cover of the storm as they used the cover of Amoria's welcome and Eronia's trackless forest. Worse, the Lighthouse-Keepers have noticed that some of their guiding lights have begun, subtly, to lead ships and souls astray rather than to safety — a single corrupted keeper, or a light tampered with, turning the layer's greatest mercy into a trap that wrecks the imperiled on the rocks instead of guiding them home. The eldest Lighthouse-Keeper, a weathered leonal who has watched the coast since Belierin's youth, has begun to suspect that the enemy working through the storms is the same one poisoning Eronia's forests and drifting through Amoria's meadows — that all three layers face a single patient incursion working its way down the River Oceanus toward some goal in the deep sea of Thalasia below, where the river meets its end and its beginning.",
      "realm": "Elysium",
      "known": true
    },
    "Thalasia": {
      "name": "Thalasia",
      "basicInfo": "The fourth and deepest layer of Elysium, a realm of the vast, deep, still sea into which the River Oceanus finally empties, and of the quiet islands and contemplative shores that ring it — the layer of profound peace, deep reflection, and the mysterious passage by which the most fulfilled souls pass beyond. Thalasia is the calm at the bottom of Elysium's descent: after the golden meadows of Amoria, the green forests of Eronia, and the storms of Belierin, the River Oceanus at last reaches this great tranquil sea, its turbulence spent, and spreads into still, deep, sunlit waters of an infinite serene blue. The sea of Thalasia is the layer's heart — vast, calm, and profoundly deep — and around and upon it lie quiet islands, gentle shores, and secluded havens where the most contemplative souls dwell in deep peace. The light here is soft and dreamlike, silver-blue and endless, and a great stillness lies over everything. This is the layer of the good that has come to rest completely, of the souls whose long journey of kindness is fulfilled and who now dwell in serene contemplation, and of the deepest mystery of Elysium: for it is said that from the deep sea of Thalasia, the most perfected and fulfilled souls eventually pass beyond entirely, merging with the plane itself or journeying to some further peace of which even the guardinals do not speak. Thalasia has its island-hermitages where contemplative souls and celestials dwell in deep reflection; its quiet contemplative shores; its deep-sea sanctuaries where the sea-guardinals and the celestial creatures of the deep abide; and the great, still heart of the sea itself, where the River Oceanus completes its long journey and where the passage beyond is said to lie. Its dwellers are the contemplative guardinals, the deeply fulfilled petitioners nearing the end of their journey, the celestial servants of the gods of the deep sea and of peaceful passage, the wise creatures of the tranquil waters, and the keepers of the layer's deepest mysteries — all wrapped in the profound, dreamlike stillness of good come finally and fully to rest.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "factions": [
        "Contemplative sea-guardinals",
        "Deeply fulfilled petitioners nearing passage",
        "Celestial servants of the gods of the deep and peaceful passage",
        "Island-hermits and shore-contemplatives",
        "Keepers of the Passage Beyond"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The great still sea of Thalasia holds the deepest secret and the ultimate goal of the patient incursion working its way down the River Oceanus: for at the very heart of the deep sea lies the Confluence, the still point where the river both ends and begins — where the Oceanus completes its long journey through the Upper Planes and, in the same instant, is renewed to flow out again, pure, into all the good planes it feeds. This Confluence is the true wellspring of the river's benevolent power, more fundamental even than Amoria's visible headwaters, and it is the softest and most catastrophic point of attack in all of Elysium: a corruption introduced at the Confluence would not merely poison Elysium but would flow outward into the renewed river and taint the shared lifeblood of good across every Upper Plane the Oceanus touches, from Arborea to the Beastlands to the peaks of Celestia. The yugoloth incursion — the cold drifter in Amoria's meadows, the hunter poisoning Eronia's forests, the storm-rider slipping ashore in Belierin — has all along been working toward this single deep target, and its unknown client seeks not conquest but the ultimate act of mercenary desecration: to sell the corruption of good itself. The contemplative Keepers of the Passage Beyond guard the Confluence, but they guard it with stillness and reflection rather than force, and the deep peace of Thalasia makes it hard for them even to conceive of the violation approaching. The layer's other secret is gentler but no less profound: the passage beyond, by which the most fulfilled souls leave Elysium for some further peace, is real, and it lies near the Confluence — which means the enemy's target sits beside the most sacred mystery of the plane, and any battle for the one may imperil the other, risking not just the corruption of the river but the closing of the door through which the perfected good are meant to pass.",
      "realm": "Elysium",
      "known": true
    },
    "Krigala": {
      "name": "Krigala",
      "basicInfo": "The first layer of the Beastlands, the realm of eternal noon, a bright, warm, open wilderness of sunlit forests, sweeping grassland plains, clear rivers, and gentle mountains beneath a sky forever fixed at midday. Krigala is the daytime face of the wild: the layer where the sun always shines, where the grass grows green and the game runs thick, where the diurnal beasts range in their glory and the wild feels most welcoming and most alive. This is where most souls and visitors first arrive in the Beastlands, and its bright open country is the gentlest introduction to the plane's untamed goodness — though even here the wild is genuinely wild, and the sunlit plains hold their predators as surely as their grazing herds. The landscape of Krigala is a magnificent patchwork of every bright-country habitat: golden savannas where great herds thunder, sun-dappled woodlands alive with birdsong, clear rivers where otters and herons fish, flowering meadows humming with insects, and open highlands where eagles ride the warm air. It is the home of the daytime animal lords — the great spirit-eagle, the lion, the stag, the bear of the sunlit woods — and of the petitioners who loved the bright wild and now run, fly, and roam it in beast-form. Krigala has its great gathering-groves where the beasts and beast-souls meet at the watering places; its sacred springs and rivers; its high eyries and open plains; and the warm boundaries where the eternal noon begins to soften toward the twilight of Brux below. There are no settlements, only the living country itself, and the whole layer breathes the vigorous, sunlit joy of nature at the height of its day. Its dwellers are the diurnal animal lords, the beast-form petitioners of the bright wild, the celestial spirit-beasts, the nature-gods' servants, and the countless free animals living perfect in their own natures under the endless noon.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "The diurnal animal lords (eagle, lion, stag, bear)",
        "Beast-form petitioners of the bright wild",
        "Celestial spirit-beasts of the day",
        "Servants of the nature gods",
        "The free herds and packs of Krigala"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Krigala's bright openness makes it the layer where visitors most easily forget themselves: the transformation that turns person into beast works fastest under the eternal noon, and the plane's gentle welcome masks how quickly an unwary soul can lose its mind to instinct and become simply another animal of the plains, its name and history gone. This is not cruelty but the plane's nature, yet it can be exploited — and something has begun to exploit it. Certain beasts in Krigala have started behaving wrongly: predators killing beyond need, herds driven to senseless panic, the sacred balance of hunt and flight tipping toward mere slaughter. The animal lords, who feel the health of their kinds in their own spirits, know something is poisoning the instinct of the wild, twisting the sacred predator-prey balance into something closer to the mindless cruelty of the Lower Planes. The suspected source is an incursion of fiends who have learned that the way to corrupt the Beastlands is not to conquer its land — impossible, for there is nothing to hold — but to corrupt its beasts, seeding a madness that turns the sacred wild savage in the wrong way, predator against prey against predator until the whole balance collapses into carnage. The great spirit-lion, eldest of Krigala's animal lords, has begun to hunt this madness through the sunlit country, but it spreads faster than any single lord can chase, and the lion fears it is only the bright, visible edge of a deeper rot working down through the twilight and the night below. There is also a quieter secret: some petitioners who lost themselves to full beast-form did so not by accident but by choice, fleeing memories too painful to keep, and among these deliberately forgotten souls hide a few who are fleeing not pain but justice — spirits who did real wrong in life and have hidden from it inside the mind of a beast.",
      "realm": "The Beastlands",
      "known": true
    },
    "Brux": {
      "name": "Brux",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer of the Beastlands, the realm of eternal twilight, a dusky, transitional wilderness of shadowed forests, misty valleys, gloaming meadows, and still waters beneath a sky forever fixed at the long, golden-purple hour between day and night. Brux is the threshold layer, the wild caught in the endless moment of dusk, and it holds a beauty and a mystery distinct from the bright plains above and the deep dark below: this is the country of the crepuscular beasts, the creatures of the in-between hour — the deer that graze at twilight, the foxes that begin their prowl, the owls waking as the songbirds settle, the bats spiraling up from their day-roosts. The light of Brux is soft, golden, and fading, the whole layer wrapped in the hushed, watchful quality of the gloaming when the day-world and the night-world overlap and neither fully rules. Its landscape is one of shadow and mist: dim forests where the last light slants through the leaves, valleys pooling with evening fog, quiet meres reflecting the purple sky, and dusky glades where the twilight beasts move soft and wary. It is the home of the twilight animal lords — the spirit-fox, the owl, the deer, the bat — and of the petitioners who loved the gloaming hour and now roam it in beast-form. Brux has its misty gathering-hollows where the crepuscular beasts meet in the dusk; its shadowed springs and still meres; its twilight glades and fog-filled valleys; and the soft boundaries where the eternal dusk shades upward toward Krigala's noon or downward into Karasuthra's night. As the transitional layer, Brux is where the character of the plane shifts, where the welcoming brightness above gives way to the primal dark below, and its dwellers — the twilight animal lords, the gloaming-loving petitioners, the celestial spirit-beasts of dusk, and the wary crepuscular animals — live in the perpetual, watchful, beautiful hush of the world's long evening.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "The twilight animal lords (fox, owl, deer, bat)",
        "Beast-form petitioners of the gloaming",
