    "Mithardir": {
      "name": "Mithardir",
      "basicInfo": "The third and deepest layer of Arborea, a realm of endless white desert and fine pale dust beneath a bleached and colorless sky, the fading, melancholy, hauntingly beautiful land of dead and forgotten gods, where the passion of the plane above has burned down at last to ash and quiet sorrow. Mithardir is the strangest and saddest of Arborea's layers: where Arvandor blazes with passion and Aquallor runs deep with emotion, Mithardir is the place where all that fire has finally spent itself — a vast, silent desert of white dust that was once, it is said, the emotion and glory of the plane, now settled into a pale, powdery waste. It is not an evil place, nor even truly a sad one in the ordinary sense, but a place of profound, quiet melancholy and austere, ghostly beauty: the white dunes stretch to a colorless horizon, the ruins of forgotten temples rise half-buried from the sand, and the ghosts of dead and forgotten gods — deities whose last worshippers died ages ago, who have faded almost to nothing — drift across the pale waste, remembering what they were. This is the layer of endings, of forgotten things, of the beauty that lies in fading and in memory, and it holds a quiet fascination for philosophers, mystics, and those souls drawn to the contemplation of impermanence and loss. Mithardir has its half-buried ruined temples of dead gods; its drifting divine ghosts, faded almost to shadows; its rare oases where the last echoes of the layer's old life linger; its pale trackless dunes where the dust of spent passion lies deep; and its silent, colorless expanses where the plane's fire has cooled to ash. Few dwell here by choice — some contemplative petitioners, some scholars of the forgotten, the ghosts of the dead gods themselves, and the rare keeper who tends the memory of what has faded. It is a place of endings and quiet, austere beauty, the still white ash at the bottom of Arborea's great fire, where even the gods go at last to be forgotten.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "The ghosts of dead and forgotten gods",
        "Contemplative petitioners and scholars of the forgotten",
        "Keepers of faded memory",
        "The rare oasis-dwellers",
        "Mystics of impermanence"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Mithardir's dead gods are not always as dead as they seem, and its silence hides the plane's most dangerous secret: some of the forgotten deities drifting the white waste are not truly gone but merely sleeping, faded almost to nothing yet not quite extinguished, and a fading god is a reservoir of divine power lying unguarded in the dust. The enemy corrupting Arborea from above understands this perfectly: the ultimate aim of the campaign — inflaming Arvandor's passion into obsession, drowning Aquallor's emotion into despair — is to send a flood of corrupted emotional energy cascading down into Mithardir, where it could wake a dead god. A forgotten deity roused not by worship but by a tide of corrupted passion would rise mad, its faded divinity rekindled by obsession and despair rather than love, a god of ruin born from the ash of a god that was — and such a being, loosed in Arborea, could shatter the whole plane. This is the true shape of the enemy's design: not to conquer Arborea but to make it destroy itself, using its own passion as the fuel and one of its own forgotten gods as the final weapon. Among the half-buried temples lies the tomb of one particular dead god whose name and nature even the keepers have forgotten, but whose ruin is the largest and whose presence the ghosts avoid — and it is toward this greatest sleeper that the corrupted emotional tide is being steered. The scholars of the forgotten who dwell in Mithardir have begun to notice the white dust stirring where it should lie still, and the drifting divine ghosts growing agitated, remembering things they had let go; the wisest of the keepers, an ancient mystic who has tended the memory of the dead gods since the layer's beginning, has grasped that something means to wake what sleeps beneath the dunes, and that the passion burning wrong in the layers above is being deliberately funneled down toward the one grave that must never be disturbed. Whether the Seldarine and Olympians above can be roused to see the danger before the corrupted tide reaches the sleeping god is the question on which the fate of all Arborea turns.",
      "realm": "Arborea",
      "known": true
    },
    "Ysgard": {
      "name": "Ysgard",
      "basicInfo": "The first and greatest layer of the plane of Ysgard, a realm of vast floating earthbergs drifting through an endless golden sky, crowned with roaring mead-halls, rolling battle-plains, deep pine forests, and towering mountains, where the fallen heroes fight and feast in eternal glory beneath the halls of the Norse gods. This is Ysgard at its most iconic: the enormous slabs of floating earth — some small as a farmstead, some vast as a continent — drift majestically through the bright sky, their undersides ablaze with the fires of their making, so that the land itself floats and adventure waits over every edge, where a bold soul may leap from one earthberg to another or fall gloriously into the burning sky below. Upon these floating lands rise the great mead-halls of the slain, roaring with feast and song and the clash of friendly and not-so-friendly arms; the wide battle-plains where the hero-souls die each day in glorious combat and rise each dawn made whole; the deep forests where the wild hunt rides and the fierce beasts roam; and the towering mountains where the Norse gods keep their high seats. This is the layer of the Aesir and Vanir — of Odin's hall of Valhalla where the chosen slain feast, of Thor's thunderous domain, of the whole roaring Norse pantheon — and of the warrior-souls who lived boldly and now fill the endless halls of battle-glory. Ysgard has its great mead-halls of the fallen heroes; its eternal battle-plains of glorious daily war; its drifting earthberg-wilds where adventure and danger wait over every edge; its high mountain-seats of the gods; and its blazing boundaries where the earthbergs' fire shades down toward the flame-realm of Muspelheim below. There are no peaceful cities, only the roaring halls and the wild floating country, and the whole layer thunders with courage, battle, feast, song, and the fierce joy of glorious struggle. Its dwellers are the Norse gods, the valkyries who choose the slain, the hero-souls of the mead-halls, the wild spirits of the hunt and the storm, and the countless bold warriors living their afterlife at the roaring edge of the blade.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "The Aesir and Vanir (Norse gods)",
        "The Valkyries (choosers of the slain)",
        "Hero-souls of the mead-halls",
        "Wild spirits of the hunt and storm",
        "The einherjar (eternal warriors of Valhalla)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Ysgard's eternal battle is meant to be glorious struggle — death each day, rebirth each dawn, courage proven and honed without true loss — but the sacred cycle that makes the daily war a joy rather than a horror is more fragile than the roaring heroes suspect, and something has begun to corrupt it. On certain earthbergs, warriors who fall in the daily battle have failed to rise at dawn: they stay dead, truly and finally, their glorious rebirth denied, and the joy of the eternal struggle curdles into real grief and real fear. The valkyries, who choose the slain and shepherd the cycle of death and rebirth, have felt the wrongness first, for it is their sacred charge that is failing. The suspected cause is an incursion aimed not at conquering Ysgard's floating lands — impossible, for they drift and cannot be held — but at breaking the rebirth-cycle itself, so that the plane's endless glorious war becomes endless glorious slaughter, its heroes dying for real and its halls emptying toward silence. Whoever is behind it understands that Ysgard's whole goodness rests on the promise that bold death is not final here; break that promise, and the courage that is the plane's glory becomes mere reckless waste, and the roaring joy of the mead-halls turns to the ash of true and permanent death. There is a deeper thread: the corruption seems to spread from the earthbergs' burning undersides, from the fire that shades down toward Muspelheim below, and the wisest of the valkyries suspects that the breaking of the rebirth-cycle is being worked upward from the fire-realm — that something in Muspelheim, or something using Muspelheim's flame, means to turn Ysgard's sacred fire of rebirth into a fire of final death. And there is a secret the gods themselves rarely speak: the Norse pantheon lives under the shadow of Ragnarok, the prophesied final battle in which many of them are fated to die, and a corruption that could make death permanent in Ysgard threatens to turn that distant myth into an immediate catastrophe — for gods who cannot be reborn are gods who can be ended.",
      "realm": "Ysgard",
      "known": true
    },
    "Muspelheim": {
      "name": "Muspelheim",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer of Ysgard, the realm of eternal fire, a burning land of molten rivers, blazing plains, volcanic peaks, and endless flame, where the fire giants dwell, the fierce forge their glory in heat and struggle, and the raw elemental fire of the plane runs at its hottest. Muspelheim is the flame-heart of Ysgard: a magnificent, dangerous realm of fire in every form — rivers of molten rock winding through plains of black glass, volcanic mountains spewing sparks into a smoke-red sky, geysers of flame, seas of embers, and vast burning wastes where the very air shimmers with heat. It is not a place of evil but of fierce, elemental intensity: the fire here is the fire of the forge and the fire of the heart, of struggle and creation and the tempering of the strong, and those who dwell here are hardened by it into beings of tremendous power and pride. Muspelheim is home to the fire giants, the great smith-warriors of the flame who forge mighty works in their volcanic foundries and test their strength in the burning wastes; to the fire spirits and salamanders of the elemental flame; and to the fierce warrior-souls drawn to the heat of struggle and creation. The layer has its blazing fortress-forges where the fire giants labor and war; its molten rivers and fire-seas; its volcanic peaks and burning plains; its ember-wastes where the fiercest trials are met; and its scorching boundaries where the flame shades up toward Ysgard's earthbergs above or down toward the deep dark of Nidavellir below. It is a harsh, glorious, dangerous layer where only the strong endure, and its dwellers — the proud fire giants, the elemental fire spirits, the fierce warrior-souls, and the smiths who forge in the heart of the flame — live at the roaring intensity of eternal fire, tempered by heat into hardness and glory.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "The fire giants of the flame-forges",
        "Fire spirits and salamanders",
        "Fierce warrior-souls of the heat",
        "The Smiths of the Molten Forge",
        "Elemental powers of fire"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Muspelheim's fire is sacred to Ysgard in a way few remember: the flame that blazes on the undersides of the earthbergs above, the fire that makes the floating lands and warms the eternal cycle, has its deep source here in the fire-realm, and Muspelheim's flame is therefore bound up with the rebirth-magic that lets Ysgard's fallen heroes rise again each dawn. This makes the fire-realm the true battleground of the corruption breaking Ysgard's cycle, for whoever means to turn the sacred fire of rebirth into a fire of final death must work here, at the source. The signs are present: certain of the molten rivers have begun to burn cold and wrong, their flame no longer the warm fire of creation and rebirth but a consuming fire that destroys utterly and gives nothing back; fire giants who forge in these tainted flames find their works cursed, and warrior-souls who fall near them do not rise. The proud fire giants, slow to admit weakness and quick to blame their own rivalries, have been dangerously late to recognize that an outside hand is poisoning their flame. The deepest secret lies at the layer's heart: the Primordial Forge, the source-fire from which all Muspelheim's flame flows and from which the rebirth-fire of the whole plane is kindled, a blaze so ancient and powerful that it predates the Norse gods themselves. The enemy's true target is this Forge, for to corrupt the source-fire would turn every flame in Ysgard — every earthberg's burning heart, every spark of the rebirth-cycle — into a fire of final death, breaking the plane's sacred promise at its root and dooming its heroes and even its gods to permanent ends. One ancient fire giant smith, keeper of the Primordial Forge, has felt the wrongness creeping toward the source-fire and has begun to suspect that the enemy is not of the fire-realm at all but comes from below — from the deep dark of Nidavellir, or from something that has crept up through it — using Muspelheim's flame as the instrument of a design aimed at all three layers at once.",
