    "Nidavellir": {
      "name": "Nidavellir",
      "basicInfo": "The third and deepest layer of Ysgard, the realm of the deep dark, a vast subterranean world of endless caverns, deep mines, ringing forges, and rivers of molten metal, where the dwarves delve and craft their legendary works in the great dark beneath the floating lands and the burning realm above. Nidavellir is the underworld of Ysgard — not an evil underworld but a deep, dark, industrious one, a boundless realm of stone and shadow lit by the glow of forge-fire and molten metal and the gleam of veins of precious ore. It is the land of the dwarves, the master smiths and miners of the multiverse, who delve the deep stone for its treasures and forge in their great subterranean foundries the finest works ever made — legendary weapons, wondrous devices, treasures of surpassing craft, the very artifacts that arm the gods themselves. The layer is a magnificent darkness of vast echoing caverns, deep glittering mines, roaring forge-halls, underground rivers of molten metal and cold black water, crystal caves, and stone cities carved into the living rock (for unlike the upper layers, Nidavellir does have its dwarf-holds, though they are hewn from stone and hidden in the deep rather than built in the open). It is home to the dwarves and their gods, the dwarf-souls who loved the craft and the deep, the deep spirits of stone and metal, and the strange things that dwell in the oldest and darkest caverns. Nidavellir has its great dwarf-holds carved into the deep stone; its ringing forge-halls where legendary works are made; its glittering mines and ore-veins; its rivers of molten metal; its deepest dark caverns where old and secret things abide; and its shadowed boundaries where the deep dark shades up toward the fire of Muspelheim above. It is a layer of craft, industry, endurance, and deep patient strength, and its dwellers — the master-smith dwarves, the dwarf-gods, the deep spirits, and the crafters of legendary works — live in the ringing, glowing, industrious dark, forging glory from stone and fire in the deep beneath the roaring world.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "The dwarves and their gods",
        "The Master Smiths of the deep forges",
        "Dwarf-souls of craft and stone",
        "Deep spirits of metal and rock",
        "The keepers of the oldest caverns"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Nidavellir's deep dark hides the origin of the whole design against Ysgard, for the enemy breaking the rebirth-cycle above did not come from the fire or the floating lands but crept up from the deepest, oldest, most forgotten caverns of the dwarf-realm — caverns that delve so far down that they open, it is whispered, onto the roots of the plane and the borders of darker realms below. Something has come up through the deep dark: an incursion that understands Ysgard's sacred machinery of death and rebirth better than the roaring heroes above do, and that has chosen to work its corruption through fire and craft rather than open battle, precisely because Ysgard's warriors know how to answer a charge but not how to fight a poison seeping up from below. The dwarves, master crafters who understand how things are made and therefore how they may be unmade, have grasped part of the truth: some of their deepest forges have been subtly tampered with, and works of craft meant to sustain the rebirth-cycle — for the dwarves forge sacred implements for the gods and valkyries, tools bound up with the magic of death and return — have been corrupted at the anvil, so that what should preserve life now quietly ends it. The deepest secret is the Sundering Vein, a seam of black metal in the lowest caverns that the dwarves have shunned since the plane's youth, an ore that does not build but unmakes, that can sever the bonds of magic and the cords of fate and rebirth; the enemy seeks this Sundering Vein, for a weapon forged from it could cut the rebirth-cycle at its root, and worked into the Primordial Forge of Muspelheim above could turn the whole plane's sacred fire of rebirth into a fire of final death. The eldest dwarf-smith, keeper of the deep memory, knows what the Sundering Vein can do and has sealed the way to it, but the enemy has already corrupted forges and turned dwarf-souls to its purpose, and the seals may not hold. The dwarves alone among Ysgard's peoples have the patience and the craft-knowledge to understand and counter the threat — if they can be made to see that their quiet, deep, tampered forges are the true front line of a war the roaring heroes above do not yet know they are losing.",
      "realm": "Ysgard",
      "known": true
    },
    "Limbo": {
      "name": "Limbo",
      "basicInfo": "The sole region of the Ever-Changing Chaos, encompassing all its major sites. This region is a maelstrom of shifting elemental matter, psychic static, and the roiling turbulence of raw creation. Within it, islands of relative stability exist: the githzerai monasteries of Shra'kt'lor and Zerth'Ad'Lun, the chaotic nexus of the Spawning Stone, and the treacherous pathways of the Anarchic Gate. The rest is the Chaos Wastes, a boundless expanse of unpredictable transformations where slaadi hunt and elementals rage. Time and distance are unreliable here, and navigation is impossible without a focused mind or an artifact of order. The region is a crucible for the soul, rewarding discipline and punishing indecision.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Githzerai (Shra'kt'lor and Zerth'Ad'Lun)",
        "Slaadi Broods (all colors)",
        "Chaos Elementals",
        "The Anarchs (chaos-worshipping cultists)",
        "Yugoloth scouts (secret)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Spawning Stone is not a natural artifact; it is a fragment of the original Chaos that existed before the multiverse was ordered. If it were destroyed, the boundary between Limbo and the Far Realm would weaken catastrophically, allowing entities of pure madness to pour into the planes. The githzerai know this and guard it indirectly, not to protect the slaadi but to prevent a cosmic unraveling. A hidden sect of the Anarchs, called the 'Unmakers', actively seeks to shatter the Stone, believing that true chaos is the only truth.",
      "realm": "Limbo"
    },
    "Pandesmos": {
      "name": "Pandesmos",
      "basicInfo": "The first and uppermost layer of Pandemonium, a maze of howling tunnels carved by the never-ending wind. The gale here is strong enough to strip flesh from bone, and the walls are worn smooth by its constant assault. The darkness is nearly absolute, broken only by the faint, phosphorescent glow of lichen and the occasional flash of lightning from the wind itself. This layer is home to the Madhouse, a fortress of the derro god Diinkarazan, and the Gate of Winds, a nexus where the gale is born. Exiled devils, insane mortals, and the shrieking terrors that give the plane its name roam the passages, hunting those foolish enough to enter. The only safety lies in the deep side-caves where the wind cannot reach, but even these are not silent.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "factions": [
        "The Madhouse (derro and Diinkarazan's cult)",
        "Howler packs",
        "Exiled devils",
        "The Wind Dukes of Aaqa (hidden observers)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The wind of Pandesmos is not natural; it is the exhalation of a primordial entity of air and madness, imprisoned at the plane's heart. The Wind Dukes of Aaqa secretly maintain a watchpost here to ensure the entity never wakes fully. If it did, the entire plane would collapse into a single, endless scream that would echo across the multiverse.",
      "realm": "Pandemonium"
    },
    "Cocytus": {
      "name": "Cocytus",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer, a realm of freezing fog and bitter cold, where the howling wind becomes a shriek of pure agony. The tunnels are slick with black ice, and the air is thick with a mist that clings to the skin and seeps into the bones. Cocytus is a place of sorrow and despair, where the exiled and the broken come to freeze their emotions and forget their pasts. The River of Lamentation, a sluggish flow of black water that never freezes, winds through the layer, carrying the tears of the damned. Here, the derro and other mad creatures mine the ice for 'frost-shards', crystals that contain frozen screams. The layer is also home to the Screaming Cliffs, a vast chasm where the wind is amplified into a physical force that can shatter stone.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "Derro mining guilds",
        "Night hags (frost hags)",
        "The Frozen Choir (undead banshees)",
        "Cult of Auril (exiled)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The River of Lamentation is not a river of water but of concentrated grief, drawn from the tears of all who have ever mourned. Bathing in it can erase a painful memory, but it also steals the capacity for joy. A hidden spring at its source, the 'Spring of First Tears', is guarded by a night hag who trades its waters to the highest bidder.",
      "realm": "Pandemonium"
    },
    "Phlegethon": {
      "name": "Phlegethon",
      "basicInfo": "The third layer, a realm of fire and ash where the wind is a searing blast that ignites the very air. The tunnels are lined with rivers of magma and fields of smoldering embers, and the heat is so intense that even the rocks glow. Phlegethon is a place of rage and destruction, where the mad come to burn away their sanity or to unleash their fury. The Firefalls, a cascade of liquid flame, is the layer's most spectacular and dangerous feature. Here, fire giants exiled from their home, salamanders, and fire-infused howlers battle for territory. The Ash Warrens, a network of tunnels filled with choking ash, hide the lairs of those who seek to avoid the flames but not the heat.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "Fire giant exiles",
        "Salamander clans",
        "Fire howlers",
        "Cult of Kossuth (heretics)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Beneath the Firefalls lies the 'Heart of the Inferno', a shard of the Elemental Plane of Fire that was torn loose during the Dawn War. It is the source of Phlegethon's heat, and if it were removed, the layer would rapidly cool, transforming into a realm of steam and mud. A renegade fire giant chieftain seeks to claim it and forge a new realm of elemental fire within Pandemonium.",
      "realm": "Pandemonium"
    },
    "Agathion": {
      "name": "Agathion",
      "basicInfo": "The fourth and deepest layer of Pandemonium, a realm of absolute darkness and crushing silence—a paradox in a plane of endless noise. Here, the wind dies, replaced by a suffocating stillness that weighs on the mind. The tunnels are narrow and close, and the air is cold and stale. Agathion is a place of imprisonment and forgotten secrets, where the most dangerous exiles are kept. The Bottomless Pit, a vertical shaft that plunges into oblivion, is the final destination for those who have committed unforgivable sins. The Silent Dark, a region of total blackness, is home to creatures that have evolved to hunt by sound and vibration. A few ancient structures, built by long-dead civilizations, remain intact, their purposes lost. It is a realm where even madness fades into apathy, and the only escape is death—or something worse.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "The Silent Order (monks of oblivion)",
        "Undead remnants",
        "Imprisoned entities",
        "The Queen of Air and Darkness (hidden)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Queen of Air and Darkness, the dark aspect of the elven goddess Auril, has a hidden court in the Silent Dark. She is not imprisoned but chooses to dwell here, feeding on the despair of the plane. Her presence is the reason Agathion's silence is so oppressive; she has woven a spell that absorbs all sound, and if she were ever driven out, the pent-up noise would erupt in a cataclysm that would shatter the other three layers.",
      "realm": "Pandemonium"
    },
    "The Infinite Layers": {
      "name": "The Infinite Layers",
      "basicInfo": "A single region representing the most significant and accessible layers of the Abyss. Though the plane has infinite layers, travelers and scholars generally concern themselves with the domains of the greatest demon lords and the most stable (relatively speaking) locations. This region encompasses the major strongholds and iconic layers that adventurers might seek, each with its own terrible character: the Demonweb Pits, Thanatos, Azzagrat, the Gaping Maw, the Death Dells, and the Slime Pits, among others. The River Styx connects many of these layers, making travel possible but perilous. The region is a maelstrom of demonic politics, where alliances shift as quickly as the landscape, and where even the mightiest can be brought low by a moment's distraction.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Demon Lords (Lolth, Orcus, Graz'zt, Demogorgon, Yeenoghu, Juiblex, Baphomet, etc.)",
        "Tanar'ri hordes",
        "Chaos cults",
        "Blood War legions (demonic)",
        "Yugoloth mercenaries"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Abyss is not truly infinite; it is a wound in reality that is slowly growing, feeding on the chaos of the multiverse. If it is not stopped, the Abyss will eventually consume all other planes. The only way to seal it is to find the 'Shard of Pure Law' that was used to create the first layer, but that artifact is lost, and even the demon lords fear what would happen if it were found.",
      "realm": "The Abyss"
    },
    "Othrys": {
      "name": "Othrys",
      "basicInfo": "The first and outermost layer of Carceri, a realm of fetid swamps, tangled forests, and rugged mountains under a gloomy, greenish sky. The air is thick with humidity and the stench of decay. The River Styx flows sluggishly through the swamps, and its waters are black and cold. This layer is home to the exiled Titans, ancient beings of immense power who were imprisoned here for rebelling against the gods. They dwell in crumbling fortresses and forgotten temples, nursing their grudges. The layer is also infested with demodands and other fiends who patrol the swamps and prey on the lost. A few scattered villages of exiled mortals eke out a miserable existence, trading with passing fiends and avoiding the Titans' wrath.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "factions": [
        "The Exiled Titans",
        "Demodand patrols",
        "River Styx ferrymen (merrenoloths)",
        "Lost souls and exiles"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Titans are not merely prisoners; they are slowly dying, their divine essence seeping into the swamp and corrupting the land. A hidden spring, the 'Spring of the Titan's Tears', contains the last drops of their old power, and drinking from it grants temporary godlike strength but also a share of their eternal rage.",
      "realm": "Carceri"
    },
    "Cathrys": {
      "name": "Cathrys",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer, a sweltering, crimson jungle where the rain is acid and the plants are carnivorous. The sky is a dim red, and the air is thick with the smell of rot and blood. The terrain is a dense tangle of thorny vines, poisonous flowers, and towering trees that weep caustic sap. Here, the exiled and the damned are hunted by demodands and by the layer's native horrors. A few ancient ruins, half-swallowed by the jungle, provide shelter. The layer is also home to the Crimson Citadel, a fortress of red stone where a powerful demodand lord rules. The rivers are acidic, and the ground is soft with decay. Survival requires constant vigilance and a high tolerance for pain.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "Demodand lords",
        "Jungle predators",
        "Lost exiles",
        "Yugoloth mercenaries"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The acid rain of Cathrys is not natural; it is the tears of a forgotten goddess of mercy, imprisoned in the layer's core. If she were freed, the acid would turn to healing water, and the jungle would wither. A cult of her last worshippers hides in the deepest jungle, waiting for a sign.",
      "realm": "Carceri"
    },
    "Minethys": {
      "name": "Minethys",
      "basicInfo": "The third layer, a vast desert of rust-colored sand and dust under a sun that is a dull, red orb. The wind is constant and abrasive, stripping flesh from bone. The layer is home to the dust mephits, the howling spirits of those who died of thirst, and exiled fiends who have adapted to the dryness. A single, massive fortress of black iron, the 'Spire of the Oinoloth', rises from the desert, where a powerful yugoloth lord conducts his business. The desert is riddled with ancient tombs and buried cities, their treasures still waiting for those brave enough to dig. The only respite is the occasional oasis, but the water is often poisoned or guarded.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "The Oinoloth's servants",
        "Dust mephits",
        "Undead of the buried cities",
        "Nomadic exiles"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Beneath the desert lies the 'Vault of Forgotten Suns', a series of chambers that hold the essence of dead stars. The Oinoloth seeks to harness this power to create a weapon capable of destroying the upper planes. A hidden map, carved on a stone in the Spire, shows the way.",
      "realm": "Carceri"
    },
    "Colothys": {
      "name": "Colothys",
      "basicInfo": "The fourth layer, a realm of jagged mountains, deep ravines, and howling winds that never cease. The peaks are so sharp they cut the sky, and the valleys are filled with shadows and the screams of the tortured. Here, the most dangerous prisoners are chained to the rock, their flesh torn by the wind and their minds broken by the isolation. The layer is ruled by the demodand lords, who use the mountains as their personal torture grounds. The only settlements are the fortified camps of the wardens, built into the cliffs. The air is thin and cold, and the constant wind carries the scent of blood and iron. Few who enter ever leave.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Demodand lords",
        "Chained prisoners",
        "Wind howlers",
        "Escaped exiles (rare)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The mountains are not natural; they are the spines of a dead primordial, the 'Father of Mountains'. If the correct ritual is performed at the highest peak, the primordial will stir, causing a cataclysm that could shatter the entire layer and release all prisoners. The demodands know this and have sealed the peak with powerful wards.",
      "realm": "Carceri"
    },
    "Porphatys": {
      "name": "Porphatys",
      "basicInfo": "The fifth layer, a frozen sea of black ice and acidic snow, where the temperature is so low that even demons shiver. The ocean is solid except for a few channels of liquid ammonia, and the air is a constant blizzard of needle-sharp ice crystals. The layer is a prison for those who committed the coldest, most calculated betrayals. Their souls are frozen into the ice, their faces visible just below the surface. The only inhabitants are the demodand wardens, the night hags who trade in frozen souls, and a few mad exiles who have learned to survive the cold. The silence is broken only by the cracking of ice and the whispers of the damned.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -1,
      "factions": [
        "Demodand wardens",
        "Night hags (soul traders)",
        "Frozen souls",
        "White dragons (rare)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Beneath the ice, a single chamber of liquid warmth exists, the 'Heart of the Sea', where the original ocean of Porphatys still flows. This warmth is the only source of life on the layer, and the night hags use it to brew potions of cold resistance. If the ice were ever melted, the entire layer would become a temperate ocean, releasing the frozen souls and flooding the lower layers.",
      "realm": "Carceri"
    },
    "Agathys": {
      "name": "Agathys",
      "basicInfo": "The sixth and innermost layer of Carceri, a sphere of absolute cold and darkness, with no light, no air, and no sound. The pressure is immense, and the cold is so extreme that even magic freezes. This is the final prison, where the most irredeemable souls and the most dangerous entities are kept. The layer is ruled by the 'Demodand Court', a council of the oldest and most powerful demodands, who exist in a state of frozen agony. There are no landmarks, only the crushing weight of the darkness. Those sent here do not scream; they simply fade into the silence, their consciousness slowly dissolving over millennia. The only way out is through the 'Gate of Absolute Nothing', which requires a key made from a tear of a god—an impossible object.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -2,
      "factions": [
        "Demodand Court",
        "Imprisoned entities",
        "The Silent Ones (lost souls)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Gate of Absolute Nothing is not a physical door but a state of mind. To open it, one must achieve perfect emptiness—no desire, no fear, no memory. The Demodand Court has been trying for eons and has not succeeded. A single mortal, a monk who achieved enlightenment before being imprisoned, is said to have come closest, and his frozen body is hidden somewhere in the ice, a faint warmth still emanating from his heart.",
      "realm": "Carceri"
    },
    "Oinos": {
      "name": "Oinos",
      "basicInfo": "The first layer of Hades, a desolate expanse of gray fields, stagnant bogs, and the endless front lines of the Blood War. Here, the tanar'ri hordes of the Abyss and the baatezu legions of Hell clash in an eternal, grinding conflict that leaves the land scarred and littered with the husks of fiends. The River Styx meanders through the layer, its waters thick with the ashes of the fallen. The sky is a permanent, sickly gray, and the air smells of rot and sulfur. This is also the domain of the yugoloths, who dwell in the Wasting Tower of Khin-Oin and broker weapons, information, and mercenary services to both sides of the war. The only neutral ground is the town at the Crossroads, a ramshackle settlement where travelers, deserters, and soul merchants can find a precarious respite. It is a place of grinding, meaningless conflict and quiet, suffocating despair.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "Yugoloth mercenaries",
        "Tanar'ri hordes",
        "Baatezu legions",
        "Night hags",
        "Merrenoloth ferrymen"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Blood War is not merely a conflict; it is a release valve for the multiverse. If the demons and devils ever united, their combined evil could overwhelm the upper planes. The yugoloths, who secretly serve neither side, manipulate the war to keep it forever in balance, preventing either side from achieving victory. A hidden cabal of ultroloths, the 'Keepers of the Balance', meets in Khin-Oin to ensure the war never ends.",
      "realm": "Hades"
    },
    "Niflheim": {
      "name": "Niflheim",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer of Hades, a realm of freezing mists, black ice, and the slow dissolution of memory. The cold here is not physical but spiritual, seeping into the soul and numbing all feeling. The landscape is a maze of fog-shrouded valleys, frozen bogs, and dead forests of petrified trees. Souls who arrive here have lost all hope, and they wander aimlessly, their memories fading like breath on a cold mirror. Night hags and yugoloths prowl the mists, seeking to harvest the last shreds of identity from the lost. At the heart of the layer lies the Palace of Memory, a structure of ice and shadow where the memories of the forgotten are stored, traded, and sometimes stolen back. It is a place of profound emptiness, where even the gods rarely tread.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Night hags",
        "Yugoloth collectors",
        "Lost souls",
        "The Frozen Choir"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Palace of Memory is not a prison but a vault. It holds the last memories of every soul that has ever been forgotten by the living. A single, perfect memory—a moment of pure, selfless love—is kept at its heart, protected by a guardian of ice. If that memory were released, it could restore hope to all of Hades, but its release would also unravel the layer's very fabric.",
      "realm": "Hades"
    },
    "Pluton": {
      "name": "Pluton",
      "basicInfo": "The third and deepest layer of Hades, a realm of absolute silence and stillness. Here, the dead gods are buried, their colossal corpses petrified into mountains of black stone. The air is so cold and dry that even sound seems to die, and the only movement is the slow drift of ash from the decaying divine forms. The River Lethe, the river of forgetfulness, flows through this layer, and those who drink from it lose all memory of their past. The layer is home to the most ancient and powerful yugoloths, who rule from the Bone Castle of Myrkul, a fortress built from the remains of dead deities. It is a place of final endings, where even the concept of hope is a forgotten dream.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -1,
      "factions": [
        "Ancient yugoloths",
        "The Dead Gods (remnants)",
        "The Silent Watchers",
        "Souls of the forgotten"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The dead gods are not truly dead; they are dreaming. Their collective unconscious forms a psychic undercurrent that influences the entire plane. If they were ever awakened, their combined will could unmake Hades itself and rewrite the multiverse. The yugoloths keep them asleep through rituals performed in the Bone Castle, rituals that require the constant sacrifice of memories.",
      "realm": "Hades"
    },
    "Khalas": {
      "name": "Khalas",
      "basicInfo": "The first and largest layer of Gehenna, a colossal volcanic mountain whose lower slopes are covered in grim cities, military camps, and the ever-churning war machines of the yugoloths. The air is hot and thick with ash, and the ground trembles with the mountain's constant rumbling. The main settlement is the Crawling City, a mobile fortress that inches its way up the slope, leaving a trail of devastation. Here, mercenary companies are hired, slaves are traded, and the Blood War is supplied with weapons and soldiers. The yugoloth hierarchy is on full display, with mezzoloths and nycaloths enforcing the will of the ultroloths. It is a place of ruthless efficiency and casual cruelty.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "factions": [
        "Yugoloth mercenary companies",
        "Ultroloth overlords",
        "Slave traders",
        "The Crawling City's guilds"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Crawling City is not merely a fortress; it is a massive construct built around the still-beating heart of a dead god, which provides the power to move. If the heart were destroyed, the city would collapse and thousands of yugoloths would be crushed. The heart is hidden in the city's lowest vault, guarded by the most loyal mezzoloths.",
      "realm": "Gehenna"
    },
    "Chamada": {
      "name": "Chamada",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer of Gehenna, a mountain of perpetual war and grinding industry. The slopes are riddled with mine shafts, slag heaps, and the ruins of fortresses destroyed in countless yugoloth civil wars. The air is thick with the smell of brimstone and blood, and the ground is littered with the remains of failed campaigns. Here, the yugoloth clans fight for territory and resources, their battles as endless as the mountain's eruptions. The layer is also home to the Tower of the Arcanaloth, a spire of black glass where powerful arcanaloths study forbidden magic and broker deals that span the planes. It is a place of constant danger, where a single misstep can mean enslavement or death.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "Yugoloth war clans",
        "Arcanaloth scholars",
        "Mining overseers",
        "Slave laborers"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Tower of the Arcanaloth contains a portal to the Far Realm, sealed but weakening. The arcanaloths study it, hoping to harness the chaos beyond, but they risk unleashing something that could consume Gehenna. A single, silver key hidden in the tower's vault can seal the portal permanently, but no one knows its location.",
      "realm": "Gehenna"
    },
    "Mungoth": {
      "name": "Mungoth",
      "basicInfo": "The third layer of Gehenna, a frozen volcano where ice and fire coexist in a surreal landscape. The upper slopes are encased in glaciers, while rivers of lava flow beneath the surface, creating steam vents and sudden explosions. The air is cold and dry, but the ground is warm to the touch. Here, outcast yugoloths and exiled fiends eke out a living, mining the rare 'froststeel' that forms in the thermal cracks. The layer is quieter than the lower ones, but no less dangerous: white dragons, ice mephits, and the occasional frost giant make their lairs here. The main settlement is the Frozen Caldera, a fortress of black ice built into the volcano's rim, where a powerful ultroloth maintains a fragile order.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "Outcast yugoloths",
        "White dragons",
        "Frost giant clans",
        "The Frozen Caldera's guard"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Frozen Caldera is built over a vent that releases a gas capable of preserving anything in perfect stasis. The ruling ultroloth, Vhazhnar, uses it to store captives and treasures. A hidden chamber contains the petrified body of an ancient god, still alive but frozen in time. If the gas were shut off, the god would awaken and wreak havoc.",
      "realm": "Gehenna"
    },
    "Krangath": {
      "name": "Krangath",
      "basicInfo": "The fourth and highest layer of Gehenna, a realm of absolute cold and eternal darkness. The mountain's peak is shrouded in a perpetual blizzard of black ice, and the air is so thin that even fiends struggle to breathe. Here, the most dangerous and powerful yugoloths dwell in isolation, meditating on the nature of evil. The only structure is the Frozen Throne, a fortress of black ice and petrified bone where the Lord of Gehenna, an ultroloth of immense age, sits in silent contemplation. The slopes are riddled with ice caves that lead to the deepest chambers of the mountain, where the 'Chasm of Black Ice' descends into the void. It is a place of utter desolation, where even the strongest will eventually fade.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "The Lord of Gehenna",
        "Ancient ultroloths",
        "Ice fiends",
        "The Silent Ones (lost souls)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Chasm of Black Ice is not bottomless; it ends at a crystal containing the essence of the first yugoloth, a being of pure evil that spawned the entire race. If the crystal were shattered, the yugoloths would lose their connection to their fiendish nature and become mortal. The Lord of Gehenna guards it as the ultimate secret.",
      "realm": "Gehenna"
    },
    "Avalas": {
      "name": "Avalas",
      "basicInfo": "The first and largest layer of Acheron, a vast battlefield of iron cubes that collide and shatter in the void. The ground is a churned mass of rusted metal, broken weapons, and the corpses of countless soldiers. The air is filled with the clash of steel, the roar of war engines, and the screams of the dying. Here, the legions of Gruumsh and Maglubiyet fight an eternal war against each other, their souls reforged after every death to fight again. The layer is dominated by two great fortresses: Nishrek, the iron citadel of Gruumsh, and Clangor, the goblinoid stronghold of Maglubiyet. Between them lies the Grinding Plain, where the armies meet in endless battle. It is a place of relentless violence and grim discipline, where the only escape is promotion through the ranks or annihilation.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "factions": [
        "Legions of Gruumsh (orcs)",
        "Armies of Maglubiyet (goblins, hobgoblins, bugbears)",
        "The Iron Legion (souls of mortal warriors)",
        "Bladeling mercenaries",
        "Yugoloth arms dealers"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The war between Gruumsh and Maglubiyet is not merely a conflict; it is a prison. Both gods are bound to Acheron by ancient pacts, forced to fight until one is utterly destroyed. A secret faction of orcs and goblins, the 'United Horde', works in secret to break the pact and lead their peoples to a new destiny, but their efforts are constantly betrayed by spies from both sides.",
      "realm": "Acheron"
    },
    "Thuldanin": {
      "name": "Thuldanin",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer of Acheron, a graveyard of war. Here, the broken weapons, abandoned war machines, and shattered artifacts of countless battles are dumped and left to rust. The cubes of this layer are piled high with scrap, forming mountains of twisted metal and bone. The air is still and dry, and the only sound is the creak of shifting debris. Scavengers, including bladelings, mephits, and the occasional yugoloth, search the piles for usable salvage. Hidden among the junk are ancient relics of immense power, left behind by long-dead generals and forgotten empires. The layer is a treasure trove for the bold, but also a deadly maze of collapsing tunnels and traps.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "Scavenger guilds",
        "Bladeling clans",
        "Yugoloth junk dealers",
        "Guardian golems of ancient wars"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Beneath the scrap lies the 'Vault of the Broken Blade', a sealed chamber containing the weapons of a primordial war god who was slain and dismembered. Each piece of the god's arsenal is a major artifact, but they are cursed: any who claim one are doomed to fight an endless war of their own, never knowing peace. The vault's location is known only to a few ancient bladeling elders.",
      "realm": "Acheron"
    },
    "Tintibulus": {
      "name": "Tintibulus",
      "basicInfo": "The third layer of Acheron, a silent void filled with thousands of small, floating iron cubes. There is no gravity, no sound, and no light except the faint glow of arcane symbols etched into the cubes. Magic behaves erratically here: spells may fail, misfire, or have unpredictable effects. The layer is home to the most disciplined and fanatical of Acheron's soldiers, who train in absolute silence to perfect their martial skills. The cubes themselves are repositories of ancient war knowledge, and those who can decipher their runes may learn secrets of strategy and tactics lost for millennia. The only permanent structure is the Silent Citadel, a fortress of interlocking cubes that serves as a monastery for the 'Order of the Unspoken', a sect of warrior-monks who have taken vows of silence.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "Order of the Unspoken",
        "Arcane researchers (drawn by wild magic)",
        "Bladeling scouts",
        "Souls of dead strategists"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The cubes are not natural; they are the shattered fragments of a single, colossal die used by the gods to gamble for the fate of the multiverse. The die was destroyed when a mortal cheated, and its fragments retain a fraction of that reality-warping power. If the die could be reassembled, it could be rolled again to change the outcome of any event, past or future. The Order of the Unspoken knows this and has been secretly collecting the pieces for millennia.",
      "realm": "Acheron"
    },
    "Ocanthus": {
      "name": "Ocanthus",
      "basicInfo": "The fourth and final layer of Acheron, a frozen sea of black ice under a sky of eternal night. The ice is not still; it is constantly shattered and reformed by a perpetual storm of razor-sharp ice shards that fly through the air with lethal speed. The cold is so intense that even fire freezes, and the wind is a constant scream. Here, the most dishonored souls are sent to be flayed by the storm for eternity. Only the hardiest creatures, such as ice fiends and the bladeling clans, can survive. At the center of the layer stands the Citadel of the Ice Lords, a fortress of fused black ice where the rulers of Ocanthus brood over the layer's frozen wastes. It is a place of absolute desolation, where even the memory of warmth is a torment.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Ice fiends",
        "Bladeling frost clans",
        "The Ice Lords (rulers of Ocanthus)",
        "Lost souls of the storm"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The storm of ice shards is not natural; it is the manifestation of a dead god of winter, whose shattered consciousness still rages against its own demise. If someone could find the god's frozen heart, they could either calm the storm or amplify it into a weapon capable of freezing an entire plane. The Ice Lords have been searching for the heart for centuries, but its location is hidden within the storm itself.",
      "realm": "Acheron"
    },
    "Mechanus": {
      "name": "Mechanus",
      "basicInfo": "The primary region of Mechanus, encompassing the core areas where order is maintained and law is enforced. This region includes the city of Regulus, the seat of Primus; the Modron Cathedral, where modrons are created and programmed; the Great Gears, the very fabric of the plane; the Inevitable Tribunal, where the Inevitables are forged and pass judgment on those who violate cosmic law; and the Forge of Primus, where new forms of modrons are designed. The region is a vast, intricate machine, and travel between its parts is possible only by following the precise paths of the gears. The modrons maintain absolute order here, and any disruption is met with immediate, calculated response.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "Primus (the One and the Prime)",
        "The Modron Hierarchy (from monodrones to Primus)",
        "The Inevitables (law enforcers)",
        "The Formian Hive (allied law)",
        "Yugoloth observers (secret)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "Primus is not a single entity but a title held by the current leader of the modrons, who is recreated each time the previous Primus dies. The current Primus has been in power for eons, but the modron hierarchy contains a hidden flaw: a rogue modron, designated 'the Aberrant', has achieved true consciousness and seeks to disrupt the perfect order. It hides in the Great Gears, waiting for a chance to overthrow Primus and introduce chaos into the plane.",
      "realm": "Mechanus"
    },
    "Abellio": {
      "name": "Abellio",
      "basicInfo": "The first and outermost layer of Arcadia, a realm of endless, perfectly ordered farmland under a warm, golden sky. Fields of golden wheat, orchards of fruit trees, and vineyards stretch to the horizon, separated by neat stone walls and winding country lanes. The land is tended by the souls of farmers, craftsmen, and community builders, who work in harmony with the archons and the native celestial animals. Small, prosperous villages dot the landscape, each with a central square, a well, and a shrine to a deity of hearth and home. The air smells of fresh bread, ripe fruit, and clean earth. It is a place of simple, honest toil, where every soul contributes to the common good and all are cared for. The layer is the spiritual heart of Arcadia, embodying the ideal of peaceful community.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "factions": [
        "The Archons of Abellio",
        "Farming communities (souls)",
        "The Church of Cyrrollalee",
        "The Order of the Golden Field"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The golden light of Abellio is not merely sunlight; it is the distilled gratitude of all who have ever found peace in their labor. A hidden spring at the layer's center, the 'Spring of Contentment', flows with water that, when drunk, grants a vision of one's true purpose in life. The archons guard it carefully, as it also reveals any hidden selfishness, causing great pain to those who are not pure of heart.",
      "realm": "Arcadia"
    },
    "Buxenus": {
      "name": "Buxenus",
      "basicInfo": "The second layer of Arcadia, a realm of ordered wilderness: forests with perfectly spaced trees, rivers that flow in straight lines, and hills that roll in gentle, predictable patterns. The wilds here are not untamed but carefully managed, a balance between nature and law. Animals move in accordance with natural cycles but respect the boundaries set by the celestial keepers. The layer is home to the soul of those who loved nature but also sought order—foresters, game wardens, and rangers who valued stewardship. Deities of the hunt and the wild, such as Mielikki (in her lawful aspect) and the elven god Rillifane Rallathil, have small shrines here. The air is fresh with the scent of pine and wildflowers, and the light filters through the canopy in perfectly aligned beams. It is a place of quiet retreat and disciplined communion with nature.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2,
      "factions": [
        "The Keepers of the Wild",
        "Souls of rangers and foresters",
        "The Order of the Stag",
        "Celestial druids of Buxenus"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "At the heart of Buxenus stands the 'Great Tree of Order', a colossal oak whose roots bind the layer's natural laws into a single, harmonious pattern. Its leaves are inscribed with the names of every species, and its sap can heal any wound. The tree is guarded by a council of treants and celestial druids. A single, withered branch hangs from its lowest limb—the last remnant of the corruption that would eventually consume Nemausus. If that branch ever grew, the entire layer would begin to decay.",
      "realm": "Arcadia"
    },
    "Nemausus": {
      "name": "Nemausus",
      "basicInfo": "The third and innermost layer of Arcadia, once a realm of pure light and perfect order, now a corrupted, overgrown tangle of poisonous plants and twisted vines. The layer fell from grace when the Harmonium, a faction of extreme law, attempted to impose their rigid order upon it. The backlash of nature, combined with the plane's own rejection of the overreach, resulted in a slow corruption. Now, the once-pristine fields are a choking jungle of thorny brambles, toxic fungi, and predatory flora. The light is dim and greenish, filtered through a canopy of diseased leaves. The inhabitants are the souls of those who became too rigid, too controlling, and they are now trapped in a realm that reflects their inner decay. A few pockets of resistance remain, where the original order still holds, but they are shrinking. It is a cautionary tale of what happens when good intentions are twisted by fanaticism.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1,
      "factions": [
        "The Corrupted (trapped souls)",
        "The Resistance of the Light",
        "The Harmonium remnants (now twisted)",
        "Blighted treants and thorn spirits"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The corruption of Nemausus is not irreversible. At the center of the layer lies the 'Seed of Original Order', a perfect, uncorrupted seed that contains the blueprint for the layer's original form. If planted in pure soil and watered with the tears of a reformed Harmonium member, it would bloom and begin the process of healing. The Resistance protects the seed, but the Corrupted would destroy it to prevent the return of what they see as a false order.",
      "realm": "Arcadia"
    },
    "The Outlands": {
      "name": "The Outlands",
      "basicInfo": "The primary region of the Outlands, encompassing the Spire, the ring of sixteen gate-towns, the city of Sigil, and the surrounding Hinterlands. This region is the heart of the plane, where the influence of the outer planes bleeds through the gate-towns and creates unique cultural and magical fusions. The gate-towns are arranged in a circle around the Spire, each serving as a portal to its aligned plane and a microcosm of that plane's ethos. The Hinterlands are a patchwork of environments that shift and change with the winds of alignment, and they are home to the rilmani and other neutral creatures. Sigil, the City of Doors, floats above the Spire, a hub of planar trade and intrigue. The region is a place of transition, where travelers can find passage to any plane, but also a place of strict neutrality, where the forces of good, evil, law, and chaos are held in check by the enigmatic Lady of Pain.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "factions": [
        "The Rilmani (neutral exemplars)",
        "The Lady of Pain (Sigil's silent ruler)",
        "The Gate-Town Councils",
        "The Planar Trade Consortium",
        "The Dustmen (faction of death)",
        "The Athar (defiers of the gods)"
      ],
      "hiddenInfo": "The Spire is not merely a natural formation; it is the axis of the multiverse, and the further one travels from it, the stronger the influence of the outer planes becomes. At the very base of the Spire, magic and alignment are completely nullified, and even the gods cannot enter. The rilmani guard this region zealously, as it is the only place where true neutrality exists. A hidden chamber within the Spire contains the 'Balance of All', a device that could tip the entire multiverse toward any alignment if misused. The Lady of Pain is aware of its existence and has placed wards that only she can bypass.",
      "realm": "Outlands"
    }
  },
