    "Ooltul": {
      "name": "Ooltul",
      "basicInfo": "Also known as the City of Tyrants, Ooltul is a small beholder city nestled in a bell-shaped cavern in the Upperdark of the Buried Realms. Once an eastern outpost of the beholder nation of Xun'Qoroth, it became cut off when the Sharn Wall was raised. The city is secretly ruled by the Triumvirate, a trio of phaerimm sorcerers who subjugated the beholders in −350 DR. Following the fall of the Sharn Wall in 1372 DR, Ooltul opened its gates to all Underdark denizens and became a major hub for trade in information, Netherese artifacts, and slaves. The cavern is illuminated by the dim glow of beljuril gems studded into the stalactite-homes of the beholder inhabitants above, while the sand island of Zorx below hosts a bustling bazaar and slave shanties.",
      "x": 4,
      "y": -36,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Buried Realms",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Sand Lake Landing": {
          "description": "The point where visitors arrive after descending from surface shafts or Underdark tunnels. Flat-bottomed skiffs ferry newcomers across Sand Lake to the island of Zorx, propelled by a beholder's telekinesis ray for a fee of 1 gp. The mist from the Sixstreams River falls constantly from the ceiling, creating an eerie, perpetual drizzle.",
          "paths": [
            "Zorx Bazaar",
            "Slave Shanties"
          ]
        },
        "Zorx Bazaar": {
          "description": "A chaotic marketplace on the eastern edge of the sand island where beholders sell Netherese magic, rare ores, uncut gems, slaves, water, and even surface goods like produce and fine art. Prices are wildly inconsistent—either too high or too low. The Triumvirate has codified information itself as currency, using a complex formula to value secrets traded here.",
          "paths": [
            "Sand Lake Landing",
            "Zorx-ka Base"
          ]
        },
        "Slave Shanties": {
          "description": "A sprawling cluster of crude lean-tos and shanties housing the city's slave population. Slaves toil endlessly, and any visitor who loses their bronze entrance pass is immediately condemned to join them. The mood is one of simmering desperation, with cranium rats acting as the Triumvirate's spies, stealing knowledge from the unwary.",
          "paths": [
            "Sand Lake Landing"
          ]
        },
        "Zorx-ka Base": {
          "description": "The base of the massive 900-foot-diameter stone column that rises from Zorx to the cavern ceiling. Its exterior is covered in intricate glowing runes and glyphs. Hidden behind a rock facade is a two-way portal connecting to the beholder capital of Xunqaq. The lower chambers house the city's army of death tyrants—undead beholders awaiting the Triumvirate's command.",
          "paths": [
            "Zorx Bazaar",
            "Zorx-ka Interior"
          ]
        },
        "Zorx-ka Interior": {
          "description": "A bewildering honeycomb of tunnels, chambers, and vertical shafts within the great column. Gravity-defying architecture and confusing layouts disorient intruders. The Triumvirate rules from hidden chambers deep within, while beholder mages and trusted servants occupy the upper levels. The interior reeks of ozone and alien magic.",
          "paths": [
            "Zorx-ka Base",
            "Triumvirate Sanctum"
          ]
        },
        "Triumvirate Sanctum": {
          "description": "A secret chamber at the heart of Zorx-ka where the three phaerimm satraps known as the Triumvirate scheme and plot. The air hums with ancient, life-draining magic. Magical scrying pools allow them to observe events throughout the city and beyond. The walls are inscribed with spells designed to breach the Sharn Wall—rituals that have so far failed.",
          "paths": [
            "Zorx-ka Interior"
          ]
        },
        "Beholder Stalactites": {
          "description": "Hundreds of massive stalactites hang from the cavern ceiling, each hollowed out to serve as a home for one of the city's beholder inhabitants. They are studded with beljuril gems whose faint luminescence provides the cavern's only natural light. Beholders levitate between them, glaring down at the island below with paranoia and disdain.",
          "paths": [
            "Sand Lake Landing"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Triumvirate (Phaerimm rulers)",
        "Xun'Qoroth beholder nation",
        "Death Tyrant army",
        "Cranium Rat spy network"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "By 1479 DR, the Shadovar of returned Netheril annihilated the phaerimm hold over Ooltul during their campaign to reclaim the Buried Realms, leaving the city under Netherese command. Many beholders secretly plotted against the Triumvirate for centuries before this, and several of the four remaining Tyranarchs would eagerly aid any effort to overthrow their alien masters.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Xunqaq": {
      "name": "Xunqaq",
      "basicInfo": "The capital city of Xun'Qoroth, a beholder nation spanning a half-dozen cities in the western Buried Realms. Xun'Qoroth lies beneath southeastern Anauroch and is secretly controlled by the phaerimm, who conquered it after their imprisonment behind the Sharn Wall. Unlike the beholders of Ooltul, those of Xun'Qoroth conceal their allegiance to the phaerimm and consider their Ooltul cousins corrupted, attacking them on sight. The city serves as the political and trade nexus of the nation, with portals linking it to sister-cities Aixlintar, Ginsunlix, Qintaroth, and Viksanmaq.",
      "x": 38,
      "y": -21,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Buried Realms",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Portal Nexus": {
          "description": "A vast circular chamber ringed by six shimmering two-way portals, each connecting to one of Xun'Qoroth's sister cities. The air crackles with transit magic, and beholder guards scrutinize every traveler. A seventh, larger portal connects directly to Ooltul, enabling trade and communication with the phaerimm Triumvirate. The floor is polished obsidian, reflecting the glow of the portals in unsettling ways.",
          "paths": [
            "Overlord's Spire",
            "Grand Market"
          ]
        },
        "Overlord's Spire": {
          "description": "The seat of the puppet-ruler installed by the phaerimm. A towering stalagmite carved into a fortress-palace, its walls adorned with beholder-crafted art that subtly incorporates phaerimm mind-control sigils. The current overlord rules at the phaerimm's pleasure, their every decision whispered into their mind by unseen masters.",
          "paths": [
            "Portal Nexus",
            "Phaerimm Warrens"
          ]
        },
        "Phaerimm Warrens": {
          "description": "Hidden passages and chambers accessible only to those who know the secret routes. Here the true rulers of Xun'Qoroth dwell in darkness, manipulating the beholder nation through proxies. The walls are etched with life-draining wards, and the temperature is unnaturally cold. Enslaved mind flayers serve as psychic amplifiers, extending the phaerimm's mental reach across the entire nation.",
          "paths": [
            "Overlord's Spire"
          ]
        },
        "Grand Market": {
          "description": "A sprawling underground plaza where beholder merchants trade gems, scrolls, Netherese artifacts, and slaves with visitors from across the Underdark. The market operates on a strict caste system—beholders occupy the upper tiers, lesser races the lower. Goods pass between cities via the portal network in a constant stream of commerce.",
          "paths": [
            "Portal Nexus",
            "Slave Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Slave Pens": {
          "description": "Massive holding cells carved into the cavern walls where captured humanoids, aberrations, and even lesser beholders await their fate. The pens are warded against teleportation and magical escape. The phaerimm periodically select slaves for their experiments, dragging them into the Warrens from which they never return.",
          "paths": [
            "Grand Market"
          ]
        },
        "Death Tyrant Vaults": {
          "description": "Deep vaults beneath the city where Xun'Qoroth stores its most feared weapons: legions of death tyrants, animated beholder corpses capable of following simple commands. The vaults are patrolled by living beholders who consider guard duty an honorable assignment, though the constant presence of undead kin slowly erodes their sanity.",
          "paths": [
            "Overlord's Spire"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Xun'Qoroth beholder nation",
        "Phaerimm puppet-masters",
        "Enslaved mind flayer cabal",
        "Ooltul Triumvirate (via portal)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The phaerimm of Xun'Qoroth use the two-way portal to Ooltul as their primary conduit for communicating with the few phaerimm who remained outside the Sharn Wall, passing items and instructions through enslaved beholders for centuries. This secret link was instrumental in coordinating the phaerimm's campaign against Evereska after the Sharn Wall was breached.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Synod": {
      "name": "Synod",
      "basicInfo": "A fallen Netherese floating citadel buried beneath the Stonelands, now a treacherous ruin half-consumed by the earth. Synod was an ecclesiastical enclave ruled by a pious archwizard, home to some of Netheril's finest cathedrals. Unlike most enclaves destroyed in Karsus's Folly, Synod was at a relatively low altitude when magic failed and fell in upon itself rather than shattering, digging into the rough terrain where rockslides and blowing sand buried it. The enclave's damaged mythallar reconstituted itself when the Weave reformed after Karsus's Folly, creating an ongoing interaction with the Sharn Wall that causes a visible bulge and thinning in the magical barrier. The site is now also the Crypt of Augathra the Mad, the Netherese seer who came here seeking to transform herself into a sharn.",
      "x": -23,
      "y": -4,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Buried Realms",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Buried Gate": {
          "description": "The primary entrance to the fallen enclave, a half-collapsed archway of white Netherese stone protruding from the rocky hillside. The stone is unnaturally warm to the touch, and faint cathedral bells can be heard echoing from within on the hour—a lingering enchantment that has persisted for millennia. Sand and rubble make passage difficult, and the entrance is watched by phaerimm scouts drawn to the mythallar's energy.",
          "paths": [
            "Cathedral of Amaunator",
            "Mythallar Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "Cathedral of Amaunator": {
          "description": "A vast, partially collapsed worship hall dedicated to Amaunator, the Netherese sun god. The ceiling has caved in, leaving sharp stalactites of fused glass where magical fires once burned. Ancient pews are overturned and covered in crystalline growths—residue from the mythallar's raw magical leakage. Ghostly choirs can sometimes be heard singing hymns in archaic Netherese.",
          "paths": [
            "Buried Gate",
            "Crypt of Augathra"
          ]
        },
        "Crypt of Augathra": {
          "description": "The sealed tomb-laboratory of the mad Netherese seer Augathra, who came to Synod around −390 DR seeking to end the torment of her prophetic visions. The chamber is filled with half-finished scrolls of the Black Chronology, a dark alternate prophecy, and the apparatus of an incomplete ritual meant to transform its occupant into a sharn. The Book of the Black, containing many of Shar's dark secrets, was interred here until recovered by adventurers in 1375 DR. Residual wild magic makes divination spells behave erratically within the crypt.",
          "paths": [
            "Cathedral of Amaunator",
            "Ritual Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "Ritual Chamber": {
          "description": "The site of Augathra's failed transformation ritual, where conflicting energies from the Sharn Wall and the mythallar once intertwined. The stone floor is scorched with a permanent, swirling pattern, and three humanoid silhouettes are burned into one wall—the remnant of Augathra, the mage Shar Cormrael, and a spying phaerimm who were fused together into a single sharn entity when the ritual was interrupted in 684 DR. The chamber now serves as a thin point between the Material Plane and the Ethereal.",
          "paths": [
            "Crypt of Augathra"
          ]
        },
        "Mythallar Chamber": {
          "description": "The deepest and most dangerous area of Synod. The damaged mythallar pulses at the center of a web of crystallized magical energy, its cracked surface bleeding raw arcane power into the surrounding stone. Touching the mythallar destroys any living or undead creature instantly. The chamber is the source of the anomaly affecting the Sharn Wall above, and its energy signature can be detected for miles through the Underdark—drawing phaerimm, Netherese treasure-hunters, and worse. The walls are covered in glowing Netherese command words.",
          "paths": [
            "Buried Gate"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Sharn Wall anomalies",
        "Phaerimm scouts",
        "Netherese treasure seekers",
        "Sharran cultists seeking the Book of the Black"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The mythallar of Synod is one of the few still-functioning Netherese power cores in existence, its energy having reconstituted when the Weave reformed after Karsus's Folly. The Shadovar of Thultanthar were aware of Synod but never excavated it, possibly fearing what else might be drawn to its power. The fused sharn entity created in the Ritual Chamber still exists somewhere in the Ethereal Plane, composed of three minds eternally warring for dominance.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Philock": {
      "name": "Philock",
      "basicInfo": "The ruins of an ancient Netherese successor-state city located in the Netherese Caverns of the Upperdark. Philock was founded after the Fall of Netheril in −339 DR by refugees who fled beneath the Wood of Sharp Teeth. Like the surface survivor-kingdoms of Anauria, Asram, and Hlondath, Philock struggled to preserve Netherese culture and magic in the hostile Underdark. The city is built within a large rift, its structures stretching from the canyon floor up its lower walls. Centuries of cave-ins and rockslides have buried most of the settlement under tons of stone, though recent drow excavation efforts have uncovered intact stairwells—only to be halted by deadly lungrot disease released from sealed chambers.",
      "x": -32,
      "y": 31,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Buried Realms",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Rift Entrance": {
          "description": "A narrow crack in the cavern wall that opens into the great rift holding Philock. The floor here is littered with ancient Netherese coins, broken pottery, and the bones of treasure hunters who ventured too deep. Strange echoes—voices speaking in archaic Netherese—bounce off the rift walls, and the air carries the faint musty scent of lungrot spores from the depths.",
          "paths": [
            "Canyon Streets",
            "Drow Excavation"
          ]
        },
        "Canyon Streets": {
          "description": "The main thoroughfares of ancient Philock, now a jumbled maze of collapsed buildings, fallen bridges, and precariously leaning towers that scale the lower reaches of the rift walls. Many structures still contain sealed rooms that have not been opened since the city's fall—some holding treasures, others holding ancient plagues. The silence is oppressive, broken only by the occasional distant rumble of shifting stone.",
          "paths": [
            "Rift Entrance",
            "Magistrate's Tower",
            "Sealed Tombs"
          ]
        },
        "Magistrate's Tower": {
          "description": "One of the few buildings still largely intact, a five-story structure of fitted black stone that once housed the city's ruling arcanist-magistrate. The upper floors contain a library of crumbling Netherese scrolls, many describing the early years of survival after the Fall. The magistrate's personal chambers on the top floor are warded with an active protective spell that has somehow endured for over 1,700 years.",
          "paths": [
            "Canyon Streets"
          ]
        },
        "Drow Excavation": {
          "description": "A recent excavation site near the rift's deepest point, where a drow expedition equipped with slave labor uncovered an intact stone stairwell descending further into the city's underlevels. The site is now abandoned, with tools, ropes, and dead slaves scattered about—victims of the lungrot disease released when the stairwell was opened. Warning glyphs in drow script mark the area as contaminated.",
          "paths": [
            "Rift Entrance",
            "Sealed Underlevels"
          ]
        },
        "Sealed Underlevels": {
          "description": "The stairwell uncovered by the drow descends into a network of sealed chambers and corridors that have been isolated since the city's fall. The air here is thick with lungrot spores—a deadly inhaled disease that causes rapid Constitution damage and can only be cured magically. What treasures or horrors these chambers hold remains unknown, as the drow abandoned their excavation before fully exploring them.",
          "paths": [
            "Drow Excavation"
          ]
        },
        "Sealed Tombs": {
          "description": "A series of burial chambers carved into the rift walls, sealed with Netherese wards. Some tombs have been broken open by previous expeditions, their contents—mummified Netherese nobles and their grave goods—looted or scattered. Others remain intact, their wards still crackling with defensive magic that has outlived its creators by millennia.",
          "paths": [
            "Canyon Streets"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Drow expedition (abandoned)",
        "Netherese undead guardians",
        "Treasure hunters (sporadic)",
        "Lungrot contamination"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The lungrot disease released from the sealed underlevels was deliberately cultivated by Philock's last arcanist-magistrate as a biological defense to prevent the phaerimm or their thralls from accessing a hidden vault. Beneath the contaminated chambers lies a cache of Netherese spellbooks and a functioning mythallar fragment small enough to be transported—one of the rarest and most valuable artifacts in all Faerûn.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Oreme": {
      "name": "Oreme",
      "basicInfo": "The impossibly ancient capital of the sarrukh empire of Isstosseffifil, predating Netheril by tens of thousands of years. Located 200 miles east of Weathercote Wood, Oreme is buried by the sands of the Frozen Sea for decades at a time between sandstorms. On the surface, only circular rubble of collapsed great towers remains visible. But its true extent lies below: the deepest catacombs connect directly to the Buried Realms, where sixty sarrukh liches—the Crypt of the Dreaming Lizards—rule in endless rotation, each awakening to govern for a time while the others hibernate. The Nether Scrolls were created here around −30,000 DR. A barrier similar to the Sharn Wall, constructed by the liches, prevents phaerimm from entering the city. The ruins are guarded by asabi and stingtail tribes descended from the empire's original servants, who worship Merrshaulk and periodically sacrifice captured troglodytes or lizardfolk.",
      "x": 10,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Buried Realms",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Sunken Plaza": {
          "description": "The surface entrance to Oreme, a vast circular depression in the desert sands where the city's central plaza once stood. Broken obelisks jut from the sand at odd angles, covered in serpentine script so ancient that even Netherese scholars could not fully translate it. When sandstorms recede, portions of the plaza are briefly exposed before being buried again. Asabi guards patrol the perimeter, attacking any who approach without proper tribute.",
          "paths": [
            "Tower of the Nether Scrolls",
            "Descent to the Catacombs"
          ]
        },
        "Tower of the Nether Scrolls": {
          "description": "The shattered remnant of the great tower where sarrukh mages created the Nether Scrolls, the most powerful collection of magical knowledge ever assembled on Toril. Only the first three subterranean levels remain intact. The walls are covered in golden serpentine script that seems to writhe when not directly observed—fragments of the original Nether Scrolls' magic. Touching the script triggers visions of ancient sarrukh rituals and may impart fragments of lost spells.",
          "paths": [
            "Sunken Plaza",
            "Crypt of the Dreaming Lizards"
          ]
        },
        "Descent to the Catacombs": {
          "description": "A massive spiral ramp descending from the Sunken Plaza into the earth, wide enough for twenty men to walk abreast. The walls are carved with bas-reliefs depicting the history of Isstosseffifil: the creation of the asabi servant race, the war against the phaerimm, and the desperate ritual that rerouted the Narrow Sea, flooding the Phaerlin and dooming the empire. Asabi mercenary companies muster here before deploying to the surface or the depths.",
          "paths": [
            "Sunken Plaza",
            "Council of Chiefs",
            "Phae Barrier"
          ]
        },
        "Council of Chiefs": {
          "description": "A grand subterranean hall where asabi and stingtail chieftains govern their tribes under the nominal authority of King Oreme. The hall is dominated by a throne of fused serpent bones and volcanic glass. Tribal banners hang from the ceiling, each representing a different mercenary company. The council answers to the sarrukh liches below but handles day-to-day governance, including the ritual capture and sacrifice of troglodytes to Merrshaulk.",
          "paths": [
            "Descent to the Catacombs"
          ]
        },
        "Phae Barrier": {
          "description": "A shimmering, silver-colored magical barrier sealing the deepest catacombs from the Buried Realms beyond. Identical in construction to the Sharn Wall, it permits all creatures except phaerimm to pass freely. The barrier pulses with ancient magic and is inscribed with sarrukh sigils of warding. On the other side lies the contested border between Oreme's catacombs and the phaerimm-controlled depths—a no-man's-land where asabi and mind-slaves of the phaerimm have skirmished for millennia.",
          "paths": [
            "Descent to the Catacombs",
            "Buried Realms Approaches"
          ]
        },
        "Crypt of the Dreaming Lizards": {
          "description": "The innermost sanctum of Oreme, where sixty sarrukh liches lie in enchanted hibernation. Only one lich is awake at any given time, serving as the current ruler. The chamber is lined with stone sarcophagi carved in the likeness of coiled serpents, each radiating an aura of ancient, patient malice. A contingency spell ensures all sixty liches awaken simultaneously if the city faces existential danger or the current ruler is destroyed. The walls are inscribed with fragments of the original Golden Skins of the World Serpent—the sarrukh name for the Nether Scrolls.",
          "paths": [
            "Tower of the Nether Scrolls"
          ]
        },
        "Buried Realms Approaches": {
          "description": "The tunnels and caverns beyond the Phae Barrier, connecting Oreme's catacombs to the wider Buried Realms. These passages are scarred by centuries of battle between asabi guardians and phaerimm mind-slaves. Scorch marks, petrified remains, and the shattered bodies of constructs litter the ground. The air grows progressively colder and more oppressive as one moves deeper into phaerimm territory.",
          "paths": [
            "Phae Barrier"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Sixty Sarrukh Liches (Crypt of the Dreaming Lizards)",
        "Asabi mercenary tribes (Council of Chiefs)",
        "Stingtail tribes",
        "Phaerimm mind-slaves (beyond the Phae Barrier)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "By 1375 DR, a yuan-ti abomination half-fiend named Kharantes discovered the existence of the sarrukh liches and sought to supplant them as ruler of Oreme, believing the city held the key to restoring the yuan-ti empire. The Golden Skins of the World Serpent fragments in the Crypt of the Dreaming Lizards are among the last surviving original Nether Scroll texts, making Oreme one of the most magically significant sites in all Faerûn.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Tomb Tapper Tomb": {
      "name": "Tomb Tapper Tomb",
      "basicInfo": "Also known as the Tomb of the Thaluud, this is not a location within the Buried Realms proper but a demiplane accessible only through a portal deep in the Lowerdark beneath Anauroch. Created by Netherese arcanists, the tomb is a vast extradimensional vault where the constructs known as tomb tappers—fifteen-foot-tall intelligent constructs designed to hunt magic—deposit the enchanted items and artifacts they collect from across Faerûn. Over millennia, the vault has accumulated an incalculable hoard of magic items, along with entire chunks of fortresses and ruins torn from the surface. The tomb is ruled by a mad Netherese demi-lich called The Keeper of the Thaal, who tends the collection with obsessive precision. Tomb tappers can sense the location of the portal without difficulty, but for all other creatures, finding it requires navigating the deepest, most dangerous reaches of the Buried Realms.",
      "x": 13,
      "y": 34,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Buried Realms",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Portal Cavern": {
          "description": "A nondescript chamber in the Lowerdark, indistinguishable from a thousand other caves except for the faint shimmer of a permanent portal set into one wall. The portal activates only when a tomb tapper approaches—or when the correct Netherese command word is spoken. The stone around the portal is unnaturally smooth, worn away by centuries of tomb tappers passing through with their burdens. The cavern sometimes opens into completely isolated spaces in the earth with no connection to any nearby cave system.",
          "paths": [
            "Threshold of the Keeper"
          ]
        },
        "Threshold of the Keeper": {
          "description": "The entry chamber of the demiplane, a vast stone hall lined with towering shelves and pedestals displaying the Keeper's most prized artifacts. The air is still and silent, and the only light comes from the soft glow of countless enchanted objects. A massive set of iron doors bars the way deeper into the vault, inscribed with warnings in archaic Netherese promising oblivion to those who steal from the collection. The Keeper's telepathic voice echoes here, demanding to know the purpose of any who enter.",
          "paths": [
            "Portal Cavern",
            "Hall of Hoarded Magic",
            "Keeper's Sanctum"
          ]
        },
        "Hall of Hoarded Magic": {
          "description": "An endless-seeming corridor stretching beyond the iron doors, lined floor to ceiling with magic items of every description: weapons, wands, staves, rings, and stranger artifacts piled in haphazard abundance. Entire sections of fortress walls have been deposited here by tomb tappers, embedded with their own treasures. The sheer concentration of magic creates a palpable pressure in the air, and the items seem to whisper to each other in faint, unintelligible voices. The Keeper demands a price for every item removed—and the price is always another magic item of equal or greater value.",
          "paths": [
            "Threshold of the Keeper",
            "Fortress Fragments"
          ]
        },
        "Fortress Fragments": {
          "description": "A subsection of the vault where entire chunks of fortresses, towers, and temples from across Faerûn have been deposited by tomb tappers. These massive stone blocks, some still containing intact rooms with their original furnishings and guardians, jut from the demiplane floor at odd angles. Exploring them is perilous—some still have active defensive wards, and the creatures trapped within when they were torn from the surface have adapted to the eternal twilight of the tomb. Netherese tomb tappers patrol this area, demanding that any found carrying magic items drop them and flee.",
          "paths": [
            "Hall of Hoarded Magic"
          ]
        },
        "Keeper's Sanctum": {
          "description": "The innermost chamber of the tomb, where the demi-lich Keeper of the Thaal floats above a throne made of fused magic items. The Keeper is a skull wreathed in cold blue flame, its eye sockets burning with obsessive intelligence. It maintains a mental catalog of every item in the collection and can instantly detect if anything has been removed without proper exchange. The walls of the sanctum are lined with soul gems containing the trapped essences of would-be thieves, their faces frozen in silent screams. The Keeper can command every tomb tapper in existence from this chamber and will animate the entire collection to defend itself if threatened.",
          "paths": [
            "Threshold of the Keeper"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Keeper of the Thaal (Netherese demi-lich)",
        "Thaluud (tomb tapper constructs)",
        "Trapped souls of thieves"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The tomb tappers, called 'thaluud' in Netherese meaning 'faceless,' were originally created by Netherese arcanists to hunt and destroy magic items used by the phaerimm during their ancient war. When the phaerimm were imprisoned behind the Sharn Wall, the tomb tappers' purpose became obsolete, but they continue their eternal mission, accumulating one of the largest collections of magic items in existence. The Keeper is not merely a guardian but also a curator—it has been known to trade items with powerful visitors who offer something unique enough to interest it.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Dunspeirrin": {
      "name": "Dunspeirrin",
      "basicInfo": "The mightiest duergar city in the Darklands, Dunspeirrin is a fortress of endless ambition dug into a colossal cavern lit only by the orange glow of eternal forges. Ruled by the Council of Thanes, the gray dwarves here mine metal and psionic crystals, forge weapons of war, and maintain a vast network of slave-driven supply lines. Discipline is absolute, and paranoia is an art form. The city's walls are manned by duergar who can turn invisible at will, and glyphs of psychic alarm line every tunnel. Dunspeirrin stands as the primary bulwark against incursions from the Glimmersea and the mind flayer city of Oryndoll, and it secretly trades with surface slavers through intermediaries in the Dragon Coast.",
      "x": 4,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Darklands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Ironward Gate": {
          "description": "The immense armored entrance to Dunspeirrin, a hundred-foot gate of black iron etched with runes of aversion to illithid and drow. Two invisible duergar sentries are always on duty, their heavy crossbows primed. Beyond lies a long kill-tunnel with murder holes, ready to collapse on invaders. The gate opens only for recognized caravans or those who pay the blood tax: a finger bone from a creature of each visitor's race, kept as a magical ward.",
          "paths": [
            "Forge Quarter",
            "Deep Road Hub"
          ]
        },
        "Forge Quarter": {
          "description": "The industrial heart of the city, a maze of smoke-filled galleries where thousands of duergar toil at anvils the size of houses. The heat from lava-fed forges is so intense that the very air ripples. Here they craft the black-steel weapons and psionically resonant armor that make Dunspeirrin's soldiers so feared. Slaves from a hundred conquered races haul ore and stoke flames under the lash of taskmasters.",
          "paths": [
            "Ironward Gate",
            "Thane Council Spire",
            "Slave Pits"
          ]
        },
        "Thane Council Spire": {
          "description": "A tower carved from a single stalagmite, honeycombed with chambers and meeting halls. The five ruling thanes, each a master of a different craft (war, mining, psionics, trade, and secrets), govern the city from this spire. Their deliberations are bound by the Pacts of Steel, which forbid open conflict but encourage covert assassination. The upper chamber is warded against all scrying and teleportation.",
          "paths": [
            "Forge Quarter",
            "Psionic Vault"
          ]
        },
        "Slave Pits": {
          "description": "A series of tiered galleries cut into the cavern floor, jammed with captured goblins, troglodytes, svirfneblin, and surface-dwellers. The pits are overcrowded, and the duergar deliberately pit different races against each other to prevent organized revolt. The air reeks of unwashed bodies and despair. Any slave who shows psionic ability is immediately taken to the Psionic Vault for conditioning—or for study.",
          "paths": [
            "Forge Quarter",
            "Psionic Vault"
          ]
        },
        "Psionic Vault": {
          "description": "A sealed complex deep beneath the Thane Council Spire, where the duergar refine their natural psionic potential. Crystals harvested from the Underdark are cut and attuned here, empowering the city's invisible strike teams. The Vault also houses a laboratory where captured illithids are dissected—the one project that even the thanes pursue with caution. The walls resonate with a low psychic hum that causes headaches in non-duergar and suppresses telepathy.",
          "paths": [
            "Thane Council Spire",
            "Slave Pits"
          ]
        },
        "Deep Road Hub": {
          "description": "A great vaulted crossroads where the main Underdark trade routes pass through Dunspeirrin. Here, caravan masters bargain with duergar commissars for safe passage, and goblin guides hire themselves out to navigate the treacherous eastern tunnels toward Traaskl Thorog. Stone piers accommodate packs of giant lizards and domesticated underdark rothé. The Hub is neutral ground by decree of the thanes, but daggers are drawn after dark.",
          "paths": [
            "Ironward Gate",
            "Underway Portal"
          ]
        },
        "Underway Portal": {
          "description": "A permanent magical gateway connecting Dunspeirrin to the duergar port city of Mantol-Derith in the Glimmersea. Heavily guarded and taxed, the portal allows rapid movement of trade goods and troops. The duergar keep the key-phrase a closely held secret, changing it weekly. The portal's surface shimmers with the reflected waters of the Glimmersea, and a constant patrol of death tyrants is stationed on the Dunspeirrin side.",
          "paths": [
            "Deep Road Hub"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Council of Thanes (Five Masters of Dunspeirrin)",
        "Duergar Invisible Strike Squads",
        "Deep Road Caravan Guild",
        "Slave Collectives (subjugated)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The duergar of Dunspeirrin have successfully weaponized a fragment of an imprisoned illithid elder brain, using its psychic energies to power their early-warning network. The elder brain, however, is not truly dead; it subtly manipulates the thanes' paranoia to ensure the city never cooperates long enough with other races to pose a real threat to the mind flayer city of Oryndoll.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Cairnheim": {
      "name": "Cairnheim",
      "basicInfo": "The grim necropolis-realm of the Dodkong, the only known stone giant lich. Cairnheim is carved into a vast cavern of petrified trees and fossilized remains, where the boundary between life and undeath has become a mockery. The Dodkong, once a powerful stone giant chieftain, returned from death with the power to raise his kin as undying servitors. The city is a place of echoing silence, where pallid stone giants wrapped in grave shrouds toil at necromantic forges and perform rituals to extend their master's control over the Darklands. The Dodkong seeks to transform all stone giants into liches under his sway, believing it the only path to racial immortality.",
      "x": -29,
      "y": 18,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Darklands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Ossuary Gate": {
          "description": "The entrance to Cairnheim is a massive archway made of fused giant bones, through which a cold, biting wind perpetually blows. The gate is guarded by two colossal stone giant zombies, their eyes burning with pale blue light. All who pass must speak the Dodkong's name and present an offering of a rune-carried stone from the surface. Those who fail are crushed and their bones added to the arch.",
          "paths": [
            "Court of the Undying",
            "Petrified Forest"
          ]
        },
        "Court of the Undying": {
          "description": "The Dodkong's throne room, a cyclopean hall where the lich-giant sits on a throne of fused skulls. The walls depict the history of the stone giant race, ending with the Dodkong's vision of a world ruled by undying colossi. Stone giant wraiths, insubstantial but deadly, drift through the hall, whispering ancient secrets. The Dodkong speaks in a voice like grinding boulders, his skeletal form clad in obsidian armor.",
          "paths": [
            "Ossuary Gate",
            "Rune Forge",
            "Greystone Bridge"
          ]
        },
        "Rune Forge": {
          "description": "A cavernous forge where captured stone giants and enslaved drow smiths carve necromantic runes onto massive slabs of obsidian. These runes, powered by the life force drained from sacrificed creatures, are used to animate the stone giant dead and bind them to the Dodkong's will. The heat of the forge is dead and cold, yet the metal somehow softens under the touch of wraith-tools.",
          "paths": [
            "Court of the Undying",
            "Pit of the Failed"
          ]
        },
        "Pit of the Failed": {
          "description": "A deep chasm where the Dodkong discards his failed experiments: stone giants who resisted the lichdom ritual and were instead transformed into gibbering, half-undead horrors. Their twisted forms still retain a terrible strength, and they claw at the walls in eternal agony. The Dodkong sometimes unleashes them as shock troops against enemies, though they are nearly impossible to control.",
          "paths": [
            "Rune Forge"
          ]
        },
        "Greystone Bridge": {
          "description": "A massive natural stone bridge that spans an underground chasm, connecting the throne room to the Petrified Forest beyond. The bridge is carved with reliefs of stone giant heroes, their eyes sockets missing—the Dodkong wore them as adornments. Under the bridge, a river of liquid shadow flows silently, its touch draining life.",
          "paths": [
            "Court of the Undying",
            "Petrified Forest"
          ]
        },
        "Petrified Forest": {
          "description": "A eerie forest of stone trees frozen in time, their trunks gleaming like polished marble. Here, the Dodkong's warbands train, smashing through petrified trunks and practicing formations against animated stone bears and boars. The forest is patrolled by stone giant skeletons, who watch for intruders with unblinking eye sockets. Veins of necromantic energy pulse through the roots, feeding the Dodkong's magic.",
          "paths": [
            "Greystone Bridge",
            "Ossuary Gate"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Dodkong (Stone Giant Lich)",
        "Stone Giant Wraiths and Zombies",
        "Undying Warbands",
        "Enslaved drow smiths"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Dodkong did not achieve lichdom alone; he was taught the ritual by a phaerimm survivor who escaped the Sharn Wall and took refuge in the Darklands. The phaerimm, still bound to the Dodkong by a secret pact, uses the lich-giant's armies to harry the duergar of Dunspeirrin and the mind flayers of Oryndoll, while it plots the final destruction of the phaerimm's ancient enemies.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Traaskl Thorog": {
      "name": "Traaskl Thorog",
      "basicInfo": "The legendary Goblin Warrens of the Darklands, Traaskl Thorog is an immense, labyrinthine city of interconnected tunnels, sinkholes, and cavern-cities ruled by a council of goblinoid warlords. Built over centuries of expansion, the warrens stretch for miles, populated by a teeming multitude of goblins, hobgoblins, and bugbears who maintain a fragile peace through mutual fear and the worship of Maglubiyet. The city is a hub for Underdark goblin mercenaries, slave trade, and scavenged goods, sitting astride one of the main trade routes from Dunspeirrin into the eastern Darklands. Those who navigate Traaskl Thorog without a goblin guide seldom emerge; the warrens are riddled with pit traps, flame vents, and ambush points where whole companies can be swallowed by the hungry earth.",
      "x": 36,
      "y": 15,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Darklands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Warren Gate": {
          "description": "The chaotic entrance to Traaskl Thorog, a wide natural cave mouth bristling with barricades made of scrap metal, bones, and broken carts. Hobgoblin toll-keepers demand a 'tribute of iron'—anything from a rusted sword to a gilded helmet will do, but refusal is met with a hail of goblin arrows. Beyond the gate, a stench of unwashed goblinkind and cooking rat meat assaults the senses.",
          "paths": [
            "Great Bazaar",
            "Upper Warrens"
          ]
        },
        "Great Bazaar": {
          "description": "A sprawling, cacophonous market lit by phosphorescent fungus and flickering tallow lamps. Stalls made of hide and bone sell everything from Underdark mushrooms to captured surface weapons and (poorly) cured monster parts. Goblin merchants haggle in shrieking voices, while bugbear enforcers squelch disputes with iron-shod clubs. The bazaar is the dumping ground of loot from a hundred surface raids, and occasionally a genuine magic item surfaces amid the trash.",
          "paths": [
            "Warren Gate",
            "Temple of Maglubiyet",
            "Warlord's Spire"
          ]
        },
        "Temple of Maglubiyet": {
          "description": "A crude but massive cavern-temple dedicated to the goblin god of war. At the center stands a ten-foot iron statue of Maglubiyet brandishing a bloodied axe, its eyes sockets glowing yellow. Goblin shamans in blood-stained robes chant ceaselessly, and cages of captured enemies await sacrifice on the altar. The walls are decorated with scalps, and the floor is perpetually slick with sacrificial gore.",
          "paths": [
            "Great Bazaar",
            "Deep Warrens"
          ]
        },
        "Warlord's Spire": {
          "description": "A towering stalagmite crudely turned into a fortress, home to the five ruling warlords of Traaskl Thorog: a hobgoblin general, a bugbear chieftain, a goblin assassin kingpin, a hobgoblin battle-priest, and a half-mad goblin alchemist. Each rules a sector of the city and meets here only under the threat of mutual destruction. The spire's interior is a maze of traps and hidden passages, and the warlords' true chambers shift constantly to avoid assassination.",
          "paths": [
            "Great Bazaar"
          ]
        },
        "Upper Warrens": {
          "description": "The safer, upper tunnels where goblin families dwell in relative stability. Here, goblins practice crafts—tanning, bone carving, simple smithing—and raise their young in crowded but well-defended niches. The tunnels are narrow and easily collapsed by defenders, making the warrens a nightmare for invading drow or duergar. Still, the constant threat of the 'Deep Horrors' (see Deep Warrens) keeps the population constantly vigilant.",
          "paths": [
            "Warren Gate",
            "Deep Warrens"
          ]
        },
        "Deep Warrens": {
          "description": "The terrifying lower levels of Traaskl Thorog, where the tunnels descend into the true Underdark. Here, the goblinoid city gives way to older, more dangerous caverns plagued by oozes, carrion crawlers, and worse. The goblins dare not settle these depths but use them as a hunting ground and a place to exile criminals. The Temple of Maglubiyet draws power from the blood sacrifices fed to the shadows here, and some say a slumbering aboleth waits in a deep pool, whispering madness into the dreams of goblin shamans.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple of Maglubiyet",
            "Upper Warrens"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Five Warlords (Council of the Spire)",
        "Goblin Merchant Guilds",
        "Temple of Maglubiyet (shamans)",
        "Bugbear Enforcer Clans"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The half-mad goblin alchemist warlord has discovered how to refine a potent alchemical explosive from fungal spores found in the Deep Warrens. He sells this weapon secretly to both the duergar and the drow of Eryndlyn, playing all sides. A large cache is stored beneath the Temple of Maglubiyet, and if ignited, it could collapse half the upper warrens.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Oryndoll": {
      "name": "Oryndoll",
      "basicInfo": "An ancient and powerful mind flayer city beneath the Orsraun Mountains, Oryndoll is a crown jewel of illithid civilization. The city is an ominous spiral of towering stalactites and black stone spires, linked by thrall-driven lift cages and psionic bridges. At its center lies the Elder Concord, where a massive elder brain coordinates the city's consciousness. Oryndoll's illithids view all other races as cattle, though they trade with outsiders when it serves their inscrutable purposes. The city maintains a cold, efficient order, where every mind is catalogued and every thought surveilled. Thrills are permitted only within the Creche and the Extraction Pools, where brains are harvested to sustain the elder brain.",
      "x": 26,
      "y": -28,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Darklands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Outer Precincts": {
          "description": "The approach to Oryndoll is a maze of psionically warded tunnels that disorient even determined intruders. Mind-wiped thralls shuttle goods in silent obedience, and illithid patrols drift silently, tasting the psychic impressions of all who pass. A permanent psionic field here causes mild headaches in non-illithids and makes telepathy difficult. Trespassers are paralyzed and taken for the Extraction Pools.",
          "paths": [
            "Spiral Gate",
            "Thrall Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Spiral Gate": {
          "description": "The main entrance to the city proper, a massive set of iris-like stone doors that open only to a specific psionic command. Beyond it, the city spirals downward in a great helix of platforms and bridges, illuminated by a sickly purple phosphorescence. The gate is guarded by a pair of elder brains in clear domes, whose combined mental weight can crush the psyche of any who try to force entry.",
          "paths": [
            "Outer Precincts",
            "Elder Concord Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "Elder Concord Chamber": {
          "description": "The heart of Oryndoll, a vaulted hall where the city's ruling elder brain floats in a pool of briny fluid, its tentacles pulsing with arcane light. Lesser brains, its direct offspring, orbit it in smaller pools. The elder brain speaks telepathically to all illithids, issuing commands and gathering sensory input. The chamber is sacred ground; non-illithids who enter without permission are instantly dominated and drowned in the pool to be consumed.",
          "paths": [
            "Spiral Gate",
            "Creche of Newborn",
            "Psionic Spire"
          ]
        },
        "Creche of Newborn": {
          "description": "Where new illithids are created through ceremorphosis, the process of implanting a tadpole into a humanoid host. The Creche contains rows of living hosts suspended in nutrient fluid, their bodies slowly transforming. Illithid overseers monitor the process, discarding failed attempts. The chamber is deathly silent except for the wet squelching of transformation and the occasional scream of a thrall being prepared.",
          "paths": [
            "Elder Concord Chamber",
            "Extraction Pools"
          ]
        },
        "Extraction Pools": {
          "description": "A grim chamber where the brains of sentient beings are surgically removed for the elder brain's direct consumption. Thralls are brought here when their usefulness ends, or when a mind flayer desires a delicacy. The pools are lined with stone basins filled with preservative fluid, and the air smells of brine and coppery blood. The shelves hold jars of pickled brains, each labeled with the victim's identity and the taste notes recorded by its consumer.",
          "paths": [
            "Creche of Newborn",
            "Thrall Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Thrall Pens": {
          "description": "A vast network of chambers where thousands of thralls—taken from all Underdark races—live out their hollow existence, serving the illithids without question. Their minds are conditioned through psionic re-education, leaving them obedient and devoid of will. The pens are clean and orderly, a mockery of a city. Thralls breed here, producing fresh generations that know no other life.",
          "paths": [
            "Outer Precincts",
            "Extraction Pools"
          ]
        },
        "Psionic Spire": {
          "description": "The tallest spire in Oryndoll, rising from the spiral city to pierce the cavern ceiling. It functions as a psionic amplifier and a research station where the illithids study arcane and psionic phenomena. The spire's peak houses a psionic cannon capable of projecting the elder brain's will up to the surface, influencing the dreams of rulers in Turmish and the Dragon Coast. The mere presence of the spire gives sensitive creatures nightmares even miles away.",
          "paths": [
            "Elder Concord Chamber"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Elder Brain of Oryndoll (supreme ruler)",
        "Illithid Illuminati (research cadre)",
        "Thrall Overseers",
        "Psionic Cannon Operators"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The mind flayers of Oryndoll have secretly bred a strain of illithid capable of withstanding sunlight by splicing enslaved surface-elf DNA. These 'daywalker illithids' are kept in stasis beneath the Psionic Spire, to be released during an eventual coordinated uprising against the surface.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Eryndlyn": {
      "name": "Eryndlyn",
      "basicInfo": "A drow city built beneath the Dragon Coast, Eryndlyn is infamous for its worship of Selvetarm, the Spider That Waits—Lolth's champion—and for the uneasy presence of a strong Vhaeraunite minority. The city is carved into a cavern of black crystal, its spires and bridges resembling a twisted spiderweb. Eryndlyn maintains a tense neutrality with the duergar and a brutal rivalry with other drow cities. Its military consists of Selvetargtlin fanatics and Vhaeraunite infiltrators who eye each other with simmering hatred. The city has no contact with the surface, prefering to prey on caravans heading to Westgate or the Sea of Fallen Stars.",
      "x": -29,
      "y": -30,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Darklands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Web Gate": {
          "description": "The monumental entrance to Eryndlyn, a cavern mouth filled with an illusion of woven spider silk that parts only for those who speak the correct prayer to Selvetarm. Any who pass without permission are entangled by real sticky strands and hoisted into the upper darkness to be devoured by choldrith spiders. The gate is guarded by a pair of giant driders, their eyes burning with fanaticism.",
          "paths": [
            "Webway Market",
            "Temple of the Spider"
          ]
        },
        "Webway Market": {
          "description": "A bustling trade hub where drow merchants deal in poisons, silks, and Underdark delicacies. The market is strung with enchanted spider-web bridges that serve as roads for the nimble-footed drow, while non-drow are forced to navigate the lower, dirtier paths. Duergar and goblin traders are common, but surface-dwellers are viewed with suspicion and often 'disappear' as sacrifice to Selvetarm.",
          "paths": [
            "Web Gate",
            "Noble Estates",
            "Temple of the Masked Lord"
          ]
        },
        "Noble Estates": {
          "description": "The upper reaches of the city, where the ruling Matron Mothers dwell in elegant spires carved from black crystal. The houses are in constant, bloody competition, but the Matron of House T'sarran, the dominant family, keeps the peace through a combination of assassination and alliance with the Selvetargtlin priesthood. Illuminated by permanent faerie fire, the estates glow in shades of violet and deep blue.",
          "paths": [
            "Webway Market",
            "Temple of the Spider"
          ]
        },
        "Temple of the Spider": {
          "description": "A grand temple dedicated to Selvetarm, the drow god of warriors and Lolth's favored servant. The temple is built in the shape of a monstrous spider, its legs forming the outer walls and its abdomen serving as the main altar. Priests in spiked armor chant war hymns, and the floor is stained with the blood of captured enemies. The altar holds a living heart of a purple worm, still beating, the focus of Selvetarm's power.",
          "paths": [
            "Web Gate",
            "Noble Estates",
            "Underways Hub"
          ]
        },
        "Temple of the Masked Lord": {
          "description": "A hidden shrine in the lower city, accessed through a secret door in a crumbling warehouse. Here, followers of Vhaeraun—the drow god of thievery and surface conquest—gather in secret, masked and cloaked. They plot against the Selvetargtlin dominance and dream of establishing drow colonies on the surface. The temple is modest but fortified with traps, and its inner sanctum holds a portal to a neutral hub in the Underdark, used for smuggling operatives.",
          "paths": [
            "Webway Market"
          ]
        },
        "Underways Hub": {
          "description": "A nexus of magical portals and natural tunnels that connect Eryndlyn to the wider Darklands and beyond. Key portals lead to a network of abandoned drow outposts along the Dragon Coast and to the Goblin Warrens. The hub is controlled by House T'sarran, which extracts a toll in information from all who use it. Stone columns are carved with drow spider motifs, and the air hums with the distant thrum of portal magic.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple of the Spider"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "House T'sarran (Ruling Matron Mother)",
        "Selvetargtlin fanatic warriors",
        "Vhaeraunite secret society (The Masked)",
        "Merchant caravans"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Matron Mother of House T'sarran is actually a secret devotee of Vhaeraun, playing both sides to ensure her house's supremacy. She has placed a potent magical bomb beneath the Temple of the Spider, ready to destroy Selvetarm's priesthood and install the Masked Lord as the city's primary god if the political winds shift.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Sschindylryn": {
      "name": "Sschindylryn",
      "basicInfo": "The drow City of Portals, Sschindylryn is a marvel of arcane engineering and ruthless ambition. Built within a vast cavern of jagged stalactites and lava-lit chasms, the city's power comes from its mastery of portal magic. Hundreds of permanent gates connect to strategic points across the Underdark and even to the surface, enabling lightning raids, rapid trade, and the swift relocation of entire noble houses during feuds. The city is ruled by the Matron Mother of House Vrael'thael, whose mages have unlocked secrets of planar travel gleaned from ancient Netherese lore stolen from the Buried Realms. The constant hum of portal energy saturates the air, and the city is perpetually on edge—each house maintains its own private gate network, making betrayal and invasion a constant threat.",
      "x": 16,
      "y": -13,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Deep Wastes",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Portal Nexus": {
          "description": "A vast, domed chamber at the city's center where the largest and most heavily guarded public portals converge. Stone archways ring the chamber, each marked with a glowing drow rune indicating its destination. At the center, a massive obsidian dais supports the 'Eye of Passage'—a scrying artifact that monitors all active portals. Drow soldiers and merchants throng here, while portal-keepers exact tolls in coin or secrets. The air crackles with multicolored energies, and the constant opening and closing of portals creates an eerie, rhythmic whooshing sound.",
          "paths": [
            "Matron's Spire",
            "Trade Arcanum",
            "Gate of Whispers"
          ]
        },
        "Matron's Spire": {
          "description": "A towering stalactite citadel that serves as the seat of House Vrael'thael, the ruling family. The spire is honeycombed with chambers, each containing a private portal to a different Underdark city or safe house. The Matron Mother's throne room is at the highest point, where a permanent scry-wall shows live images of her enemies. The spire's base is guarded by drider sentinels and enchanted spider-golems that phase in and out of the Ethereal Plane.",
          "paths": [
            "Portal Nexus",
            "House Portals Annex"
          ]
        },
        "House Portals Annex": {
          "description": "A heavily warded district where each noble house maintains its own sealed portal enclave. The annex is a maze of narrow streets and fortified towers, each house jealously guarding its gate codes. Duels for portal routes are common, and the area is littered with the petrified remains of defeated rivals. The air here is thick with defensive magic, and unauthorized teleportation triggers lethal traps.",
          "paths": [
            "Matron's Spire",
            "Black Web Alley"
          ]
        },
        "Trade Arcanum": {
          "description": "The commercial heart of Sschindylryn, where portal-linked warehouses receive goods from as far as Menzoberranzan and the surface. The arcanum is a series of interconnected platforms connected by spider-silk bridges. Wealthy drow merchants sell exotic spell components, living monsters, and surface-world curiosities. A special market, the 'Glimmer of Distant Stars,' deals exclusively in items stolen via portal raids on Waterdeep and Silverymoon. Duergar and goblin traders rub shoulders with drow, all under the watch of the city's portal-guards.",
          "paths": [
            "Portal Nexus",
            "Glimmer Exchange",
            "Slave Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Glimmer Exchange": {
          "description": "A secret sub-section of the Trade Arcanum where only the wealthiest and most trusted drow may enter. Here, surface goods of immense value—uncut gems, first-edition spellbooks, and captured parrots—are auctioned. The exchange is warded against divination, and its location shifts randomly among three identical rooms via a complex portal loop. A single misplaced step can send a burglar tumbling into the plane of Fire.",
          "paths": [
            "Trade Arcanum"
          ]
        },
        "Slave Pens": {
          "description": "A grim holding area attached to the Trade Arcanum where captured surface-dwellers, rival drow, and various Underdark creatures wait to be sold. The pens are divided by species, with magical dampeners preventing rebellion. The drow take pains to keep the most useful slaves—smiths, mages, and warriors—alive for export, while the rest are condemned to the city's spider pits.",
          "paths": [
            "Trade Arcanum",
            "Spider Pits"
          ]
        },
        "Spider Pits": {
          "description": "Deep, dark shafts descending into the lower Underdark, where the drow breed and train monstrous spiders for mounts, guardians, and sacrifices to Lolth. The walls team with web-festooned niches, and the low thrum of chitinous bodies echoes constantly. Prisoners hurled into the pits serve as live prey for the hatchlings. At the very bottom, a nightmare-sized 'Matron Spider' is tended by arachnomancers, her venom potent enough to kill a demon lord.",
          "paths": [
            "Slave Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Gate of Whispers": {
          "description": "A hidden portal located off the main Nexus, accessible only by a whispered passphrase. It connects to a network of secret routes used by House Vrael'thael's spies and assassins. The gate chamber is lined with sound-dampening fungi, and the portal itself is a rippling curtain of shadow. Those who step through emerge in a random location within a mile of their intended target—perfect for deniable operations.",
          "paths": [
            "Portal Nexus"
          ]
        },
        "Black Web Alley": {
          "description": "The city's underbelly, a warren of twisting passages where lesser drow, half-breeds, and outcasts eke out a living. The alley is lit only by faintly glowing cobwebs, and the air smells of fungal rot and desperation. Illegal portal-runes are carved into the walls, linking to unsanctioned destinations. The city's ruling house tolerates its existence as a pressure valve, sending patrols through periodically to cull the population.",
          "paths": [
            "House Portals Annex"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "House Vrael'thael (Ruling Matron Mother)",
        "Portal Masters' Guild",
        "Surface raiders (portal strike teams)",
        "Black Web Alley cartels"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "House Vrael'thael's matron mother secretly possesses a portal key that can link directly to the private sanctum of the Shadovar High Prince in the returned city of Thultanthar—a dangerous gift from a captured Netherese arcanist. She has never dared to use it, knowing that opening that door could bring the full force of the Shadow Enclave upon her city.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Maerimydra": {
      "name": "Maerimydra",
      "basicInfo": "Once one of the most powerful drow cities in the Underdark, Maerimydra is now a smoking ruin, a testament to the catastrophic folly of its last Matron Mother, Irae T'sarran. The city was laid waste in the Year of the Third Circle (1374 DR) when Irae's desperate ritual to summon a demon army backfired, unleashing a horde of uncontrolled fiends and undead that slaughtered the populace. Today, Maerimydra is a vast, haunted necropolis—an open wound in the Deep Wastes. Demonic remnants still prowl its shattered spires, and Irae's undying spirit, a wrathful spectral lich, rules from the Desecrated Temple of Lolth. Treasure hunters brave the dangers for the fabled wealth of the noble houses still buried in the rubble, but few return sane.",
      "x": 36,
      "y": -36,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Deep Wastes",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Shattered Gates of Ceremonial Entry": {
          "description": "The grand entrance to Maerimydra, a colossal arched portal flanked by statues of spider-queens now toppled and shattered. The gate's outer plaza is littered with the charred bones of defenders and the rusting remains of war machines used in the city's final battle. A permanent magical darkness hangs over the threshold, and the howls of wandering demons echo from within. The stonework still pulses with residual abjuration magic that triggers alarms when living beings pass.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ruined Atrium",
            "Demon Scorched Bazaar"
          ]
        },
        "The Ruined Atrium": {
          "description": "The city's former heart, a sprawling open cavern now collapsed in many places. Twisted spires of broken obsidian jut from piles of rubble, and the air is thick with ash and the sting of brimstone. Scavengers—hobgoblins, duergar, and the occasional surface adventurer—pick through the debris, always listening for the scrape of demon claws. The Atrium is effectively no-man's-land; different demon packs claim different sections, warring endlessly over territory.",
          "paths": [
            "Shattered Gates",
            "Desecrated Temple of Lolth",
            "Noble Catacombs"
          ]
        },
        "Desecrated Temple of Lolth": {
          "description": "The tallest intact structure in Maerimydra, a black spire in the shape of a spider whose legs form buttresses. Inside, the shrine to Lolth has been defiled and rededicated to Irae T'sarran's own blasphemous vanity. Her spectral form hovers above a pool of liquid shadow, attended by hollow-eyed priestesses turned undead. The temple radiates a powerful necromantic aura, and any drow who enter feel the pull of the fallen Matron Mother's will, compelling them to kneel.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ruined Atrium",
            "Spire of the Accursed Matron"
          ]
        },
        "Spire of the Accursed Matron": {
          "description": "Irae's personal sanctum, located at the top of the temple. The spire's walls are inscribed with the names of every drow she blamed for her failure, and those names now writhe and moan. A permanent portal to the Abyss, sealed but imperfect, flickers on the ceiling, occasionally vomiting forth a lesser demon. Irae's phylactery, a black pearl, is hidden somewhere within the spire, guarded by a shadow dragon bound to her service.",
          "paths": [
            "Desecrated Temple of Lolth"
          ]
        },
        "Demon Scorched Bazaar": {
          "description": "Once a thriving market, now a charred landscape of fused glass where wild demons and fire-infused undead hunt in packs. The bazaar's stalls are melted into surreal shapes, and the ground gives off stifling heat. A powerful group of armored scorch-maddened vrock rules the area from a makeshift nest of stolen loot, and they demand tribute from any scavengers who wish to pass.",
          "paths": [
            "Shattered Gates",
            "The Summoning Rift"
          ]
        },
        "The Summoning Rift": {
          "description": "The source of the catastrophe: a deep chasm in the cavern floor where Irae T'sarran attempted to summon a demon army. The rift glows with a sickly green light and occasionally belches plumes of noxious gas. Demons spontaneously manifest here, drawn from the Abyss by the lingering corruption. A circle of broken summoning stones still holds a flicker of power—any mage who can activate it might call forth something from the other side, but the result is always catastrophic.",
          "paths": [
            "Demon Scorched Bazaar",
            "Noble Catacombs"
          ]
        },
        "Noble Catacombs": {
          "description": "The sealed burial chambers of Maerimydra's former noble houses, accessed via collapsing staircases from the Ruined Atrium. Many tombs remain untouched, their wards still active, holding the material wealth of the city's elite. However, the catacombs are infested with wraiths driven mad by the demonic presence, and the spirits of the unavenged drow dead gather in a silent, hungry court around a massive crypt of obsidian—the Tomb of the First Matron, which no one has ever entered and returned.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ruined Atrium",
            "The Summoning Rift",
            "Tomb of the First Matron"
          ]
        },
        "Tomb of the First Matron": {
          "description": "The deepest chamber of the Noble Catacombs, a massive pyramid of black stone inscribed with the oldest drow script. The door is sealed with a riddle of blood and shadow. Inside, according to legend, the founder of Maerimydra lies in state, surrounded by the phylacteries of all subsequent Matron Mothers—a vault of unspeakable necromantic power. The door has never been opened, and those who have tried are found outside, their souls torn from their bodies.",
          "paths": [
            "Noble Catacombs"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Irae T'sarran, Spectral Matron Lich",
        "Demon packs (various)",
        "Scavenger lords (hobgoblins, duergar)",
        "Wraith court of the Catacombs"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Irae T'sarran's defeat in 1374 DR was incomplete. Her true phylactery—the black pearl—is hidden not in the Spire but in a pocket dimension attached to the Tomb of the First Matron, accessible only when the riddle is solved. She is slowly rebuilding her power and plans to reopen the Abyssal portal fully, sacrificing the remaining scavengers to summon a demon lord permanently into the Underdark.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Interstice": {
      "name": "The Interstice",
      "basicInfo": "A bizarre and hazardous planar anomaly in the Deep Wastes, the Interstice is a sprawling cavern complex where natural portals flicker into and out of existence at random intervals. Some are barely visible shimmerings, others are roaring vortices. The portals connect to random locations across Toril, the Feywild, the Shadowfell, and even the Elemental Planes. The area is a magnet for daring travelers, planar refugees, and deadly creatures that have stumbled through the rifts and set up lairs. Navigating the Interstice is a test of luck and mental fortitude, for the portals emit a low psychic hum that slowly erodes sanity. A small, hardened community of portal guides, called the Void-Treaders, has claimed the safest central cavern, offering to guide caravans—for exorbitant fees—through the ever-shifting paths.",
      "x": -10,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Deep Wastes",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Void-Treader Camp": {
          "description": "The only permanent settlement in the Interstice, a cluster of sturdy fungal-leather tents and stone lean-tos built around a large, stable portal that leads back to Sschindylryn. The camp is bustling with traders, exiles, and mercenaries. The Void-Treader guides, a mix of drow outcasts, duergar, and even a friendly mind flayer, hire out their services here. They maintain a crude map of the current portal layout using colored pebbles on a stone table, updated hourly as rifts open and close.",
          "paths": [
            "Portal Field Alpha",
            "The Lunatic's Pool"
          ]
        },
        "Portal Field Alpha": {
          "description": "The largest concentration of active portals, a vast cavern with walls that shimmer like oil on water. At any time, a dozen portals may be open, each displaying a glimpse of its destination—a sunlit forest, a lava field, a frozen waste. The portal field is patrolled by displaced monsters that have wandered through and set up hunting grounds. Experienced guides know safe paths, but even they sometimes vanish when a portal opens beneath their feet.",
          "paths": [
            "The Void-Treader Camp",
            "The Whispering Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Whispering Gallery": {
          "description": "A long, narrow tunnel where the portals are replaced by permanent, stationary 'windows' to other planes. The walls are covered with translucent veils of force that show scenes of distant worlds and alien skies. Unearthly whispers emanate from the veils, speaking prophecies, secrets, and maddening half-truths in languages long dead. Those who linger too long begin to lose their sense of self, mirroring the voices they hear.",
          "paths": [
            "Portal Field Alpha",
            "The Feywild Rift"
          ]
        },
        "The Feywild Rift": {
          "description": "A shimmering, arch-sized portal that remains stable for weeks at a time, opening directly into the Feywild. The archway is wreathed in glowing vines and flowers that sing in high, ethereal tones. During its active periods, eladrin, satyrs, and other fey creatures pass through, either seeking adventure or fleeing the unseelie. The Void-Treaders charge double to guide people here, as the Feywild's time distortion can age a traveler years in mere minutes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Whispering Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Lunatic's Pool": {
          "description": "A still, black underground lake fed by a waterfall that seems to fall upward—a portal in the ceiling that opens into the Elemental Plane of Water. The pool reflects not images but thoughts, showing eerie visions of desires and fears. Drinking the water induces vivid nightmares and sometimes grants cryptic oracular insights. The Void-Treaders have built a small shrine to Savras here, where they perform scrying rituals for paying clients.",
          "paths": [
            "The Void-Treader Camp",
            "The Shadow Gate"
          ]
        },
        "The Shadow Gate": {
          "description": "A dark, permanently active rift in a side cavern, leading to the Shadowfell. The gate is a gaping, lightless crack in the stone, and the air around it is frigid and lifeless. Gloom-stalkers, shadow mastiffs, and shade-kin sometimes emerge to raid the camp. The Void-Treaders keep this area under constant watch, but the gate is increasingly unstable and has begun to expand, threatening to swallow the entire camp.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lunatic's Pool"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Void-Treader Portal Guides",
        "Displaced planar creatures (various)",
        "Fey refugees from the Feywild Rift",
        "Shadowfell incursion forces"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The expansion of the Shadow Gate is not natural—a powerful shadow dragon named Vizirak has established a lair on the other side and is deliberately widening the rift to establish a permanent invasion beachhead into the Deep Wastes, aiming to conquer Sschindylryn and turn its portal network into a Shadowfell dominion.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Darkmirror Lake": {
      "name": "The Darkmirror Lake",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, perfectly still underground sea of black, reflective water in the eastern Deep Wastes. The lake's surface acts as a scrying mirror, showing visions of possible futures, alternate timelines, and sometimes the deepest fears of those who gaze upon it. The water is cold as death and rumored to be an extension of the Shadowfell itself. An ancient aboleth known as the Dream Reflected dwells in the depths, using the lake's properties to project illusions and manipulate the surface world through psychic influence. The shores are dotted with the ruins of a long-vanished pre-drow civilization, now home to kuo-toa cultists who worship the aboleth as a god.",
      "x": 19,
      "y": 16,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Deep Wastes",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Shore of Broken Monoliths": {
          "description": "The main access point to the lake, a pebble beach littered with massive broken stone pillars carved with serpentine runes. The pillars emit a faint cyan glow, and their shadows move in ways that contradict the light. Kuo-toa patrol boats made of stitched hide paddle back and forth, offering to ferry visitors to the Island of the Eye for a 'tribute of memories'—a strand of hair or a drop of blood.",
          "paths": [
            "Kuo-toa Fishing Hamlet",
            "Island of the Eye"
          ]
        },
        "Kuo-toa Fishing Hamlet": {
          "description": "A squalid village of stilted huts built over the shallows, home to a tribe of fish-like humanoids who worship the aboleth, Deep Dagon. The kuo-toa craft crude effigies of their god from polished obsidian and fish bones. They are suspicious but can be bargained with for safe passage—for a price. The village shaman performs daily rituals to 'feed the mirror,' tossing live sacrifices into the lake to appease the visions.",
          "paths": [
            "Shore of Broken Monoliths",
            "Sunken Temple of the Mirror"
          ]
        },
        "Island of the Eye": {
          "description": "The center of the lake, a black volcanic island that protrudes from the glassy water like an unblinking eye. The island is entirely barren except for a single, massive crystalline lens set into the rock. The lens focuses the lake's scrying energy, allowing those who touch it to view specific futures with startling clarity—but at the cost of a fragment of their sanity. The aboleth uses the island as its primary contact point with the surface, appearing as reflections in the lens.",
          "paths": [
            "Shore of Broken Monoliths",
            "The Abyssal Trench"
          ]
        },
        "The Abyssal Trench": {
          "description": "The deepest part of the lake, a seemingly bottomless gash where the water turns icy and the light from the crystalline island does not reach. The Dream Reflected aboleth lairs here in a palace of frozen time, a structure that appears as a halted moment of a collapsing temple. The aboleth's charm is so powerful that those who dive here risk becoming its permanent thralls, joining its collection of drugged surface-dwellers who dance eternally in a hallucinatory ballroom.",
          "paths": [
            "Island of the Eye"
          ]
        },
        "Sunken Temple of the Mirror": {
          "description": "A pre-drow ziggurat half-submerged near the southern shore, its upper chambers accessible by a stone causeway. The temple was dedicated to a forgotten god of fate, and its walls are covered in bas-reliefs of branching timelines. Inside, an altar holds a quicksilver pool that acts as a localized scrying mirror. The kuo-toa use the temple for their high sacraments, but the upper chamber is guarded by a pair of crystal golems left by the original builders.",
          "paths": [
            "Kuo-toa Fishing Hamlet"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Dream Reflected (Aboleth)",
        "Kuo-toa cult of the Deep Dagon",
        "Crystal golems (ancient guardians)",
        "Thrall-ballroom victims"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The aboleth's true name is Zz'Pzora, and it is one of the few surviving members of the Abolethic Sovereignty. It uses the Darkmirror Lake to project visions that guide surface-world leaders toward decisions that further the aboleths' long-term goal of enslaving all thinking creatures. The quicksilver pool in the Sunken Temple is a key component of a ritual that could permanently breach the Wall of the Faithless and allow the Sovereignty to invade the Fugue Plane.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Labyrinth of the Lost": {
      "name": "The Labyrinth of the Lost",
      "basicInfo": "A massive, unnatural maze of carved stone tunnels winding through the Deep Wastes, created millennia ago by a mad beholder architect as a living experiment in spatial distortion. The labyrinth's corridors shift positions when unobserved, and many are dead ends that loop back on themselves. The place is heavily populated by mimics, trappers, and other ambush predators who thrive on disoriented prey. A central chamber, the Atrium of False Eternity, contains a portal to a pocket dimension of rooms that endlessly replicate—the home of a lich who lost her sanity to the maze and now considers herself its goddess. Adventurers enter seeking the rumored treasure hoards of swallowed caravans, but many become permanent residents, their bodies assimilated into the walls.",
      "x": -31,
      "y": -21,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Deep Wastes",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Entry Echo Chamber": {
          "description": "The first room of the labyrinth, a circular stone chamber covered in ancient, swirling runes that absorb sound. Any noise made here is echoed after a delay, making it almost impossible to pinpoint the source. The walls are sticky with a residue that deadens magic, and three identical archways lead to different sections—all of which will eventually bring a traveler back here unless they carry a magical compass attuned to the maze.",
          "paths": [
            "Hall of Shifting Walls",
            "Mimic Bazaar",
            "Atrium of False Eternity"
          ]
        },
        "Hall of Shifting Walls": {
          "description": "A long, straight corridor that appears to stretch for miles, but after two hundred feet, walkers find themselves back at the start. The hall is faced with polished obsidian that reflects distorted versions of those who pass, and the reflections occasionally step out of the mirrors as hostile duplicates. Several side passages open into the Mimic Bazaar, but only on every third pass through the hall.",
          "paths": [
            "Entry Echo Chamber",
            "Mimic Bazaar"
          ]
        },
        "Mimic Bazaar": {
          "description": "A grotesque marketplace where mimics of all sizes—from small purses to entire buildings—hunt the labyrinth's lost prey. The area is a chaotic jumble of broken carts, scattered coins, and discarded weapons, all of which may be alive. A particularly large mimic called Old Grandfather, big as a house, acts as the 'mayor,' offering safe passage in exchange for any magical trinket. It knows many secrets of the labyrinth but speaks only in riddles.",
          "paths": [
            "Hall of Shifting Walls",
            "The Lich's Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Lich's Gallery": {
          "description": "A long, narrow room filled with statues of terrified adventurers frozen in mid-scream—the lich's collection. The statues are not stone but petrified victims, their souls still aware. The lich, the Self-Styled Queen of the Mad, drifts through the gallery, arranging her 'subjects' into artistic tableaus and monologuing to them. She is powerful but deeply insane, and can be reasoned with if one compliments her 'art.'",
          "paths": [
            "Mimic Bazaar",
            "Atrium of False Eternity"
          ]
        },
        "Atrium of False Eternity": {
          "description": "The center of the lich's pocket dimension, a towering room whose ceiling is lost in a golden mist. Around the walls, hundreds of identical doors open into replicas of the same room, each slightly different, creating an infinite regression. The 'real' lich's phylactery—a single, perfect pearl—is hidden in one of the infinite rooms, but finding it requires solving a puzzle that changes each time the party enters. The mist occasionally coalesces into ghostly figures who offer cryptic hints.",
          "paths": [
            "Entry Echo Chamber",
            "The Lich's Gallery"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Self-Styled Queen (Lich)",
        "Old Grandfather (Elder Mimic)",
        "Mimic houses",
        "Petrified adventurer souls"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The beholder architect, Xanathar of the Deep (no relation to the Waterdeep crime lord), did not die; it transformed itself into the labyrinth's core—a beating heart of earth and magic buried beneath the Atrium. If the heart is destroyed, the entire maze collapses, but the lich and the mimics will fight to the death to prevent it, as they would lose their home and its endless supply of victims.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Deep Imaskar": {
      "name": "Deep Imaskar",
      "basicInfo": "The last bastion of the ancient Imaskari Empire, Deep Imaskar is a hidden city of arcane splendor buried beneath the frozen bedrock of the Earthroot. For millennia, its human inhabitants have preserved the magical arts of their fallen empire behind potent illusion wards that make the city appear as an empty cavern to outsiders. The city is built in a massive geode of sapphire and crystal, lit by perpetual magical light. Its people are xenophobic arcanists ruled by a council of the most powerful wizards, each a master of a forbidden school. They harness the raw magic of the Underdark through devices known as the Imaskarcana, and they vigilantly guard against the drow of Undrek'Thoz, the duergar, and the prying eyes of Thayan Red Wizards. Entry is forbidden to all non-Imaskari on pain of annihilation, and the city's veiling spells ensure that even those who wander into the region forget they ever saw it.",
      "x": -36,
      "y": -38,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Earthroot",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Ward of Nightfall": {
          "description": "The deceptive outer boundary of the city, a vast cavern of black basalt that appears entirely empty. Illusory magic makes the city invisible to non-Imaskari; only those carrying a Seal of Admission—a sapphire wafer—can perceive the true entrance. The air here is unnaturally still, and scrying spells fail. Intruders feel an overwhelming urge to turn back, and those who press on often find themselves wandering in circles until they perish of exhaustion.",
          "paths": [
            "Spire of the Confluence"
          ]
        },
        "Spire of the Confluence": {
          "description": "The heart of Deep Imaskar's government, a towering crystal needle that rises from the center of the geode city. Here, the High Confluence—a council of seven archwizards representing the lost schools of Imaskari magic—debates the city's isolationist policy. The spire's walls are inscribed with living spells that drift across the surface, and council chambers are suspended in midair on discs of force. Access is restricted to Confluence members and their trusted aides.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ward of Nightfall",
            "The Hollow Library",
            "Quarantine Vaults"
          ]
        },
        "The Hollow Library": {
          "description": "A vast repository of Imaskari knowledge carved into a hollowed-out stalactite. The library contains millions of scrolls, tablets, and crystal memory-cores, many predating the fall of the original empire. Luminous servitors, constructs of pure magic, retrieve information for authorized scholars. The library's lower depths contain sealed sections that house forbidden texts on the creation of the Imaskarcana and the original sin that brought the Mulhorandi gods to Toril.",
          "paths": [
            "Spire of the Confluence",
            "The Imaskarcana Vault"
          ]
        },
        "The Imaskarcana Vault": {
          "description": "The most secure location in the city, a vault at the bottom of the geode where the seven legendary Imaskarcana—sentient artifacts of immense power—are stored. Each artifact is kept in its own warded sub-chamber, and only the High Confluence can grant access. The vault is patrolled by invisible stalkers and guarded by a massive golem of pure sapphire. The mere proximity of the Imaskarcana causes wild magic effects and auditory hallucinations.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hollow Library"
          ]
        },
        "Quarantine Vaults": {
          "description": "A series of isolation chambers where the Imaskari study rare planar diseases, corrupted magic items, and captured aberrations. The walls are lined with lead and warded against planar travel. One vault, sealed for over two thousand years, contains a hibernating phaerimm that was captured before the Sharn Wall was raised. The Imaskari keep it alive for study, draining its magic-eating properties to power the city's wards.",
          "paths": [
            "Spire of the Confluence",
            "Phaerimm Prison"
          ]
        },
        "Phaerimm Prison": {
          "description": "The deepest cell in the Quarantine Vaults, a spherical room suspended in a null-magic field. Inside, the emaciated but very much alive phaerimm floats in stasis, its tail wrapped around a black crystal that suppresses its magic. The walls are etched with the recorded screams of the creature, which have been translated into a dark prophecy predicting the fall of Deep Imaskar within the next century. The Confluence regards this prophecy as a riddle to be solved.",
          "paths": [
            "Quarantine Vaults"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "High Confluence of Seven",
        "Librarians of the Hollow Library",
        "Imaskarcana sentient artifacts",
        "Phaerimm Prison watch"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The High Confluence is not united. Three of the seven archwizards believe that isolation is doomed and seek to open a permanent portal to Thay to negotiate an alliance with Szass Tam. They have secretly begun constructing the portal in an abandoned section of the city, unaware that the lich has already scried their location and plans to invade and seize the Imaskarcana.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Nethra'Vayn": {
      "name": "Nethra'Vayn",
      "basicInfo": "The capital city of the Ten Cities of Undrek'Thoz, Nethra'Vayn is a frozen drow metropolis linked by portals to its nine sister-cities. The city is built inside a massive ice cavern, where stalagmites and stalactites serve as the foundations for spires of dark stone and magically reinforced ice. The ruling Council of Ten—the Matron Mothers of each city—meets here in the Spire of the Spider Moon to coordinate policy and manage the delicate balance of power. Nethra'Vayn is a hub of portal travel and espionage, with a cold, disciplined drow society that values arcane mastery and survival in the bitter cold above the chaotic worship of Lolth elsewhere. The city maintains a standing army of frost-adapted soldiers and a network of spies who monitor the duergar of Fraaszummdin and the Thayans above.",
      "x": 28,
      "y": 15,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Earthroot",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Gate of the Ten Portals": {
          "description": "A massive circular chamber ringed by ten shimmering archways of ice and shadow, each leading to one of the sister cities of Undrek'Thoz. The portals are constantly active, and a stream of drow soldiers, merchants, and spies passes between them. The gate is guarded by elite drow warriors known as the Frozen Fangs, who can make their bodies as cold as ice. The portal codes change daily, and unauthorized use triggers a teleport to a deadly ice chasm.",
          "paths": [
            "Spire of the Spider Moon",
            "Frostglass Bazaar"
          ]
        },
        "Spire of the Spider Moon": {
          "description": "The tallest structure in Nethra'Vayn, a needle-thin tower of black ice that houses the Council of Ten chamber. The spire's apex is enchanted to appear as a full moon made of spiderweb, an illusion visible across the city. Below the council chamber, the spire contains offices, audience halls, and the personal quarters of the Matron Mother of Nethra'Vayn. The spire is protected by a permanent anti-magic field that can be activated only when the council is in session.",
          "paths": [
            "Gate of the Ten Portals",
            "The Winter Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "Frostglass Bazaar": {
          "description": "A sprawling market carved into the frozen cavern floor, where drow merchants sell goods from all ten cities. The bazaar is lit by glowing ice sculptures that refreeze every morning. Stalls offer frost-spider silk, enchanted ice weapons, captured duergar artifacts, and surface-world luxuries stolen in raids. The air is filled with the clipped, cold cadences of drow merchant-haggling, and a silent auction for information is held daily in a back room under the icy gaze of House Vah'krii's matrons.",
          "paths": [
            "Gate of the Ten Portals",
            "Slave Pens of the Frost"
          ]
        },
        "Slave Pens of the Frost": {
          "description": "A grim holding area where captured humanoids—duergar, goblins, deep gnomes, and even surface elves—are penned in freezing cages of magically formed ice. Slaves are fitted with frost-collars that cause intense pain if they attempt to escape. The pens are managed by a cruel half-drow overseer who sells them in bulk to the matron houses for use in mines, sacrifices, or the arena.",
          "paths": [
            "Frostglass Bazaar",
            "The Shar'Vos Arena"
          ]
        },
        "The Shar'Vos Arena": {
          "description": "A coliseum carved from a single ice block, where drow warriors and captive monsters fight to the death for the entertainment of the nobles. The floor is enchanted to create random ice hazards, and the stands are filled with coldly appreciative drow who bet heavily on the outcomes. Prisoners who win five matches are granted freedom and enrollment in the Frozen Fangs, a reward that ensures desperate loyalty.",
          "paths": [
            "Slave Pens of the Frost",
            "The Winter Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Winter Gallery": {
          "description": "A long, elegant hall of ice where the matron houses display their bloodlines through frozen sculptures of ancestors. Each house has a niche, and the sculptures are animated with minor magic to reenact great victories. The gallery doubles as a neutral meeting ground where houses conduct negotiations, plot assassinations, or seal alliances in whispers. The walls are lined with listening spells controlled by the Council of Ten, making every secret a potential weapon.",
          "paths": [
            "Spire of the Spider Moon",
            "The Shar'Vos Arena"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Council of Ten Matron Mothers",
        "Frozen Fangs elite guard",
        "House Vah'krii (dominant house)",
        "Slave Overseers Guild"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Council of Ten is secretly fractured. Five of the cities, led by Sshanntynlan, wish to open diplomatic relations with Thay to gain arcane power and rid themselves of Lolth's yoke. The other five, led by Nethra'Vayn, remain loyal to the Spider Queen. The Council meetings are increasingly tense, and an assassination of the Nethra'Vayn Matron Mother could tip the balance into civil war.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Fraaszummdin": {
      "name": "Fraaszummdin",
      "basicInfo": "The Free Deepkingdom of Fraaszummdin, the duergar fortress-city that anchors the northern Earthroot. Built into a cluster of volcanic vents that provide thermal warmth in the freezing underground, the city is a bastion of duergar industry and grim determination. Unlike the surface-hated duergar of Dunspeirrin, Fraaszummdin maintains a grudging trade relationship with the drow of Undrek'Thoz, selling ironwork and psionic crystals in exchange for surface slaves and luxuries. The deepking rules from the Iron Caldera Hall, surrounded by his Black Hammer priests. The city's forges never cool, and its soldiers, the Ashguard, wield weapons of molten iron that sizzle in the cold air.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 34,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Earthroot",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Outer Charred Ring": {
          "description": "The defensive perimeter of Fraaszummdin, a series of stone and iron walls built atop volcanic vents that release superheated steam. Duergar crossbowmen patrol invisible, and the ground is covered with soot and ash. Intruders must walk through the steam clouds, which are imbued with a low-level psionic field that disrupts concentration. The only clear path is a narrow tunnel known as the Coal Crawl, which is guarded by an iron golem.",
          "paths": [
            "Coal Crawl Approach",
            "Iron Caldera Hall"
          ]
        },
        "Coal Crawl Approach": {
          "description": "The primary entrance tunnel, a winding passage lined with glowing ember-stones that heat the air. The walls are rough-hewn and covered in duergar pictograms telling the history of Fraaszummdin's founding. At the halfway point, a massive portcullis of black iron drops in an instant if the guards sound the alarm. The tunnel emerges into the Forge Ward of the city.",
          "paths": [
            "Outer Charred Ring",
            "Forge Ward"
          ]
        },
        "Forge Ward": {
          "description": "The industrial core of the city, a cavern filled with enormous anvils, smelting pits, and water-powered trip hammers that pound molten metal day and night. The noise is deafening, and the heat is stifling despite the surrounding cold. Here, duergar smiths craft weapons, armor, and the distinctive ash-colored psionic crystals used by the Ashguard. Slaves work the bellows, and overseers crack whips that channel psionic pain.",
          "paths": [
            "Coal Crawl Approach",
            "Iron Caldera Hall",
            "Deepkings' Vault"
          ]
        },
        "Iron Caldera Hall": {
          "description": "The throne room of the deepking, a vast circular chamber built around a bubbling pit of molten iron that provides constant orange light. The deepking's throne is a slab of obsidian floating on an anti-gravity field above the heat. The walls are decorated with the molten metal casts of dead enemies. The Black Hammer priesthood holds rituals here to Grimley, the duergar god of toil and tyranny. Diplomats from Undrek'Thoz are received in this hall under a flag of truce, though the duergar keep their weapons close.",
          "paths": [
            "Outer Charred Ring",
            "Forge Ward",
            "Psionic Foundry"
          ]
        },
        "Psionic Foundry": {
          "description": "A secret annex where the duergar refine psionic crystals for use in their invisibility and mind-assault abilities. The foundry is staffed by duergar psions who shape raw crystal from the Underdark into resonant shards. The atmosphere is thick with psychic static, and unprotected visitors suffer hallucinations. The foundry also houses a large crystal that acts as a communication device to Dunspeirrin, though the deepking keeps its existence hidden from the drow.",
          "paths": [
            "Iron Caldera Hall",
            "Ashguard Barracks"
          ]
        },
        "Ashguard Barracks": {
          "description": "The garrison of Fraaszummdin's elite soldiers, the Ashguard, located in a side cavern off the Forge Ward. The barracks are spartan but comfortable, with thermal vents keeping the soldiers warm. The Ashguard train here in psionic combat and maintain their signature molten-iron weapons in small personal forges. A trophy hall displays captured drow skulls, each carved with a tally of the soldier's kills.",
          "paths": [
            "Psionic Foundry",
            "Deepkings' Vault"
          ]
        },
        "Deepkings' Vault": {
          "description": "A heavily warded treasure chamber beneath the Iron Caldera, where the deepking stores ingots of precious metals, captured drow house regalia, and a sealed reliquary containing the Bloodaxe of Varash—a legendary weapon said to have been used to slay a demon lord. The vault is guarded by a pair of elder stone giants turned to duergar thralls via psionic reconditioning. The deepking visits only once a year to add new treasures.",
          "paths": [
            "Forge Ward",
            "Ashguard Barracks"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Deepking of Fraaszummdin",
        "Black Hammer Priests (Clerics of Grimley)",
        "Ashguard (elite duergar warriors)",
        "Psionic Foundry Master"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The deepking has secretly forged a pact with a Thayan zulkir: duergar psionic crystals in exchange for scrolls of mass domination magic. The deepking plans to launch a simultaneous psionic assault on all ten cities of Undrek'Thoz, enslaving the drow in a single catastrophic strike.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Eldritch Frost": {
      "name": "The Eldritch Frost",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, unnaturally cold glacier-filled cavern at the eastern edge of the Earthroot, the Eldritch Frost is the domain of an ancient white dragon named Frostmourn. The dragon, a matriarch of immense magical power, has laired here for over a millennium, freezing the cavern with her breath until its walls are thirty feet of eternal ice. The area is a labyrinth of ice tunnels, crevasses, and frozen lakes. The dragon is not a beast of mere instinct; she is a sorceress of ice magic and has collected a hoard of frozen artifacts from the lost Imaskari empire. The Eldritch Frost is avoided by the drow and duergar alike, as Frostmourn's hunting flights have been known to freeze entire caravans solid. A tribe of arctic kobolds, the White Talons, serves the dragon, maintaining her lair and offering sacrifices.",
      "x": -23,
      "y": 4,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Earthroot",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Glacial Maw": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Eldritch Frost, a yawning fissure in the earth from which a constant blast of freezing wind howls. The walls are mirror-smooth ice that reflects distorted images of those who approach. Kobold sentries hide in ice niches and demand a loud reply to the riddle carved above the gate in Draconic: 'What freezes still but never dies?' The answer is 'Memory,' and failure to answer correctly brings an avalanche of sharpened ice shards.",
          "paths": [
            "Hoarfrost Labyrinth"
          ]
        },
        "Hoarfrost Labyrinth": {
          "description": "A maze of twisting ice corridors, constantly reshaped by Frostmourn's magic. The walls are filled with frozen bodies of past victims, their expressions of terror preserved perfectly. The air temperature is so low that metal becomes brittle and breathing unprotected causes lung damage. White Talon kobolds guide visitors who pay tribute; those who do not are left to freeze or are herded into the Predator's Fields.",
          "paths": [
            "Glacial Maw",
            "Dragon's Roost",
            "Frozen Menagerie"
          ]
        },
        "Dragon's Roost": {
          "description": "The heart of the dragon's lair, a massive ice chamber with a cathedral-like ceiling. Frostmourn coils on a mound of frozen treasure: coins encased in ice, petrified spellbooks, and the frozen forms of would-be heroes. The chamber radiates a palpable dread, and the dragon's cold presence makes even the boldest warriors' hearts hammer. Frostmourn speaks in a low, rumbling voice and values flattery and arcane riddles. She will bargain for powerful magic items but demands a frozen trophy from any who seek to leave with her hoard.",
          "paths": [
            "Hoarfrost Labyrinth",
            "Frozen Artifact Vault"
          ]
        },
        "Frozen Menagerie": {
          "description": "A series of side chambers where Frostmourn stores living prey frozen solid in ice blocks. The menagerie includes creatures from across the Underdark—illithids, drow warriors, giant spiders, and even a captured young red dragon—all perfectly preserved. The dragon uses them as decorative trophies and occasionally thaws one as a meal. The White Talons perform rituals here to keep the ice from cracking.",
          "paths": [
            "Hoarfrost Labyrinth"
          ]
        },
        "Frozen Artifact Vault": {
          "description": "Deeper than the Roost, a sub-chamber where Frostmourn stores items she considers too valuable or dangerous to scatter in her hoard. The vault contains Imaskari spell-crystals, a staff of frost, and a mysterious black ice sphere that hums with necromantic energy—the frozen heart of a dracolich that attempted to usurp her lair centuries ago. The vault is warded with a symbol of insanity that affects all non-dragons.",
          "paths": [
            "Dragon's Roost",
            "The Crystal Veins"
          ]
        },
        "The Crystal Veins": {
          "description": "Deepest tunnels beyond the vault, natural ice caverns lined with gems of pure frozen magic that glow blue. These veins are the source of Frostmourn's arcane strength. The gems, if harvested, can be used to craft powerful ice-enchanted items, but doing so enrages the dragon. The White Talons consider the Veins sacred and will die to protect them. A permanent portal to the Elemental Chaos (Plane of Ice) flickers at the very bottom, occasionally spewing elemental creatures.",
          "paths": [
            "Frozen Artifact Vault"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Frostmourn (Ancient White Dragon Sorceress)",
        "White Talon Kobold Tribe",
        "Imaskari spirit guardians (in the Vault)",
        "Elemental creatures (from the portal)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Frostmourn's true name is Vix'thralneth, and she was once a consort of a Netherese blue dragon lord. She fled to the Earthroot during the Fall of Netheril, carrying the Imaskarcana of Fate—a seventh artifact that the Deep Imaskari believe was destroyed. The black ice sphere is not a dracolich heart but a shard of the Crenshinibon, the Crystal Shard, its evil silently corrupting the dragon's dreams.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Red Wizard Excavation": {
      "name": "The Red Wizard Excavation",
      "basicInfo": "A heavily fortified Thayan military outpost and archaeological dig established by the Zulkir of Divination to plunder the secrets of the Earthroot. Located in a series of large, cold caverns directly beneath the Thayan plateau, the excavation is a major forward operating base for Red Wizard agents seeking ancient Imaskari artifacts and portals into Deep Imaskar. The site is surrounded by slave pens, barracks, and magical laboratories, all guarded by undead legions and Thayan knights. The dig has uncovered a buried Imaskari temple, but the lower levels are sealed by traps and a guardians that have already killed several wizards. The camp bristles with tension: the zulkir demands results, while the local commander fears attracting the attention of the drow or the dragon Frostmourn.",
      "x": 38,
      "y": -14,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Earthroot",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Command Block": {
          "description": "A series of connected pavilions of red silk and bone-white stone, housing the expedition's leadership. The lead wizard, an ambitious thayan mage named Tharchioness Velmaris, rules from here with an iron will. The block is protected by a permanent anti-scrying field and a ring of animated bone golems. Maps of the dig litter the walls, pinned with glowing pins indicating discovered chambers.",
          "paths": [
            "Slave Line",
            "Dig Alpha",
            "Portal Circle"
          ]
        },
        "Slave Line": {
          "description": "A long, grim corridor lined with iron cages where hundreds of slaves—captured from surface villages, goblin tribes, and even captured duergar—toil. They are fed minimal rations and work under the lash in the excavation tunnels. A slave revolt is brewing, led by a disguised Harper agent who has been forced into the pens. The line connects to the cargo lift that brings in supplies from Thay above.",
          "paths": [
            "Command Block",
            "Supply Lift"
          ]
        },
        "Supply Lift": {
          "description": "A massive mechanical lift powered by a bound earth elemental, raising and lowering cages of supplies, slaves, and looted artifacts between the base and a secret tower on the surface. The lift is heavily guarded by undead archers, and the elemental must be fed a daily offering of precious stones. The shaft is a vertical tunnel that opens directly into the Thayan Heartlands, allowing rapid reinforcement.",
          "paths": [
            "Slave Line"
          ]
        },
        "Dig Alpha": {
          "description": "The primary excavation site, a vast open cavern where an Imaskari temple has been partially uncovered. Its black stone walls are carved with serpentine script that glows faintly. The air is thick with ancient dust and the hum of latent enchantments. Red Wizard apprentices supervise slave crews as they carefully chip away at rubble. Several side chambers have been breached, yielding scrolls and minor artifacts, but the inner sanctum remains sealed with a complex arcane lock.",
          "paths": [
            "Command Block",
            "Inner Sanctum Entrance",
            "Artifact Sorting Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "Inner Sanctum Entrance": {
          "description": "The sealed doorway to the temple's heart, a massive slab of obsidian covered in glowing wards. The door's riddle is written in ancient Imaskari, asking for the 'price of immortality.' Several wizards have died trying to force it open. The excavation's chief arcanist has determined that the answer involves a willing sacrifice of a bodily organ, but no one has yet volunteered.",
          "paths": [
            "Dig Alpha"
          ]
        },
        "Artifact Sorting Chamber": {
          "description": "A laboratory where recovered items are catalogued, studied, and prepared for shipment to Thay. The chamber is cluttered with crates, scrying pools, and a containment circle for dangerous items. A young red wizard, an apprentice secretly opposed to Szass Tam, has been hiding certain powerful artifacts and smuggling them to a hidden Harper cache in the slave pens, hoping to undermine the expedition.",
          "paths": [
            "Dig Alpha",
            "Portal Circle"
          ]
        },
        "Portal Circle": {
          "description": "A permanent teleportation circle etched in ruby dust on a raised stone dais, connecting the base directly to the Zulkir's palace in Eltabbar. The circle is guarded by two death knights who obey only the zulkir's voice. Reinforcements, supplies, and orders flow through here daily. The circle is unstable; a miscast could send travelers to a random location in the Underdark, a fact the base commander keeps secret.",
          "paths": [
            "Command Block",
            "Artifact Sorting Chamber"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Tharchioness Velmaris (Expedition Commander)",
        "Red Wizard Arcanists",
        "Thayan Knights and Undead Guard",
        "Harper infiltrator among slaves",
        "Secret apprentice dissenter"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The inner sanctum does not contain treasure but a portal to Deep Imaskar's Ward of Nightfall, deliberately hidden by the ancient Imaskari. If opened, it would allow the Thayans to bypass the city's illusions. The Harpers have learned this and are desperately trying to destroy the dig before the portal is breached, fearing a war between Thay and the reclusive Imaskari.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Shivering Pools": {
      "name": "The Shivering Pools",
      "basicInfo": "A geothermal anomaly in the southern Earthroot, the Shivering Pools are a series of bubbling hot springs surrounded by strange, humming crystal formations. The water is richly mineralized and has mild healing properties, but the true value lies in the psionically resonant crystals that grow at the pools' edges. These crystals amplify telepathy and can store memories, making them highly sought after by duergar, mind flayers, and the Deep Imaskari. A hidden illithid outpost, the Pool of Echoing Thoughts, lurks in the deeper tunnels, where an ulitharid and its thralls conduct experiments to weaponize the crystals. The area is also inhabited by a peaceful but reclusive group of svirfneblin exiles who mine the lesser crystals and trade with the drow under false identities. The pools are beautiful but dangerous: the steam can induce vivid hallucinations, and the crystals sometimes project the stored memories of past victims.",
      "x": -18,
      "y": -27,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Earthroot",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Steam-Wreathed Shore": {
          "description": "The main access point, a broad shelf of rock covered in mineral deposits and steaming pools. The air is warm and thick with the scent of sulfur and ozone. Svirfneblin miners, cloaked and masked, chip at small crystals along the shore, silently watching newcomers. The steam here causes auditory hallucinations—whispers of past conversations absorbed by the crystals—often revealing secrets of previous visitors.",
          "paths": [
            "Crystal Exchange"
          ]
        },
        "Crystal Exchange": {
          "description": "A hidden alcove where the svirfneblin have set up a makeshift market. They sell raw psionic crystals, healing salts, and potent mushroom-based remedies. The exchange is warded against scrying by a permanent illusion that makes it appear as a blank rock wall. The svirfneblin leader, an old gnome named Veldir, is suspicious but can be bribed with surface goods or protection from the illithids.",
          "paths": [
            "Steam-Wreathed Shore",
            "Deep Gnome Warrens"
          ]
        },
        "Deep Gnome Warrens": {
          "description": "A network of tunnels behind the exchange where the svirfneblin live in modest but comfortable niches. The warrens are filled with soft, bioluminescent moss and the sound of tinkering. The gnomes have constructed a psionic alarm system using the crystals, and they maintain a hidden escape route to the Northdark. They know much about the illithid outpost but fear retaliation if they speak openly.",
          "paths": [
            "Crystal Exchange",
            "Pool of Echoing Thoughts"
          ]
        },
        "Pool of Echoing Thoughts": {
          "description": "A large, perfectly circular pool of crystal-clear water at the deepest part of the caves. The pool's surface reflects not images but the thoughts of those who gaze into it, making it a potent scrying tool. The ulitharid Vorg'zill uses this pool to probe the minds of visitors and implant psychic suggestions. The illithid's thralls—duergar miners, goblins, and a dominated drow mage—patrol the pool's edge, appearing as lost wanderers in need.",
          "paths": [
            "Deep Gnome Warrens",
            "Illithid Research Spire"
          ]
        },
        "Illithid Research Spire": {
          "description": "A spire of fused crystal extruded from the cavern ceiling, invisible to normal sight due to a psionic camouflage. Inside, Vorg'zill and its illithid assistants experiment on captured svirfneblin and duergar, implanting crystals into their brains to create living psychic transmitters. The spire is connected to the Pool by a psionic bridge that allows rapid thought projection. The air hums with the collective agony of the victims.",
          "paths": [
            "Pool of Echoing Thoughts",
            "Crystal Spires"
          ]
        },
        "Crystal Spires": {
          "description": "A forest of naturally grown, giant psionic crystals that hum with stored memories. These spires are the source of the pool's power and the largest concentration of psionically active mineral in the Earthroot. Touching a spire triggers a rush of alien memories—often the last moments of creatures drained by illithids centuries ago. The spires are dangerous, but a skilled psion could extract valuable information or lost spells from them.",
          "paths": [
            "Illithid Research Spire"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Vorg'zill, Ulitharid (Illithid Scientist)",
        "Svirfneblin exiles (Crystal Exchange)",
        "Thrall patrols (duergar, goblin, drow)",
        "Crystal memory-spirits"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Vorg'zill is not alone; it is a renegade from Oryndoll, conducting unsanctioned experiments to create a new elder brain from the psionic crystals. Its success would birth a crystal-based hive mind that could challenge the existing illithid cities. The svirfneblin Veldir knows this and desperately seeks adventurers to destroy the spire before it is completed.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Underhome - Great Bhaerynden": {
      "name": "Underhome - Great Bhaerynden",
      "basicInfo": "Once the magnificent capital of the gold dwarves' Deep Realm, Underhome was a fortress-city of three massive caverns sealed behind colossal golden gates seventy feet tall. Founded in the Age of the Proud People, it served as the center of gold dwarven society for millennia until the Spellplague of 1385 DR, when cataclysmic rifts beneath the Sea of Fallen Stars caused vast sections of the Great Rift to collapse. The city was devastated, its population fleeing through elemental portals that fed the Riftlake or escaping east to Eartheart. Now Underhome is a haunted ruin, its shattered golden gates half-buried under tons of fallen stone. Drow squatters briefly claimed the ruins before being driven out by forces from Eartheart, and the dwarves established the fortress of Underwatch to seal the approach. Treasure-seekers still whisper of the untouched wealth of the Deep Lords' vaults somewhere in the rubble, but the ruins are prowled by terrible beasts drawn up from the Underdark below.",
      "x": 39,
      "y": 17,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Great Bhaerynden",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Shattered Gate": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Underhome from the Riftlake shore, where the three colossal golden gates once stood. Now one gate lies twisted and half-melted against the cavern wall, another is buried under a rockslide, and the third hangs from a single hinge, creaking mournfully in the cold draft from the depths. The stone around the gates is scorched and cracked, and ancient dwarven runes of warding flicker with residual magic. A squad from Underwatch maintains a watch post here, but they venture no further into the ruins.",
          "paths": [
            "Underwatch Post",
            "The Grand Concourse",
            "The Riftlake Shore"
          ]
        },
        "Underwatch Post": {
          "description": "A fortified dwarven outpost established after the drow squatters were driven out. The post is manned by Steel Shield veterans who monitor the ruins and aid surface-dwellers traveling into the exposed Underdark. The walls are lined with crossbow slits and a permanent alarm horn carved from a giant ram's horn. A map room charts known hazards within the ruins, though large sections remain marked 'unexplored.' The commander, a grim dwarf named Thordrek Firebrow, interrogates all who seek to enter the deeper ruins.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Gate"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Concourse": {
          "description": "Once the main thoroughfare of Underhome, a vaulted avenue wide enough for forty dwarves to march abreast. The ceiling is a marvel of dwarven engineering, carved with scenes from the founding of Bhaerynden, though sections have collapsed. Spired buildings line the concourse, many still intact but eerily silent. Floating bridges that once connected upper walkways hang broken, and the mechanical elevators that moved goods between levels are frozen with rust. The whispers of echoed dwarven voices seem to linger in the air, though whether they are ghosts or tricks of acoustics is unknown.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Gate",
            "The Deep Lords' Vaults",
            "Garment-Makers' Hall",
            "The Elemental Portal Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The Deep Lords' Vaults": {
          "description": "The legendary treasury of Underhome's ruling council, protected by a vault door of black adamantine inscribed with the seals of all the great clans. The door has never been breached, though scorch marks and deep gouges show where drow, duergar, and worse have tried. Rumors claim the vaults hold the accumulated wealth of millennia: uncut gems, mithral ingots, ancestral weapons of power, and the Crown of the Deep Realm. A complex mechanical lock requires seven keys, all lost when the Deep Lords fled the cataclysm. The vault door radiates a palpable aura of ancient dwarvish power.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Concourse",
            "Clan Crypts"
          ]
        },
        "Garment-Makers' Hall": {
          "description": "A partially collapsed district where the clothiers of Underhome once set the trends for dwarven fashion across all Faerûn. Mannequins still display rotting finery, and bolts of moldering silk spill from broken crates. The hall's upper levels are crushed, but the lower workshops remain accessible. A colony of cloakers has laired here, using the discarded fabrics to camouflage themselves among the debris.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Concourse"
          ]
        },
        "Clan Crypts": {
          "description": "A network of burial chambers carved into the walls of the Great Rift adjacent to the Deep Lords' Vaults. The crypts house the honored dead of Underhome's greatest clans, their sarcophagi carved from single blocks of obsidian. Many crypts have been desecrated by grave robbers or drow, but some remain sealed with ancestral wards. The spirits of the unavenged dead are said to whisper warnings to worthy dwarves who approach.",
          "paths": [
            "The Deep Lords' Vaults"
          ]
        },
        "The Elemental Portal Chamber": {
          "description": "The cavern where elemental portals that fed the Riftlake were located, through which many gold dwarves escaped the Spellplague's destruction. The portals are now dormant, their stone arches cracked and leaking faint planar energy. The chamber floor is submerged in icy water that seeps from the portals' remnants. Strange elemental creatures occasionally wander through the weakened barriers between planes. A permanent mist hangs in the air, and the temperature fluctuates wildly.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Concourse",
            "The Sunken Depths"
          ]
        },
        "The Sunken Depths": {
          "description": "The lowest levels of Underhome, now flooded by the Riftlake's waters and connected to the Underdark below. The water is black and cold, and the submerged streets are patrolled by aquatic predators drawn up from the Glimmersea. The drow who briefly occupied the ruins after the Spellplague used these depths as an escape route, and some of their web-bridges still span the flooded chambers. Treasure-seekers brave enough to dive here may find artifacts washed down from the upper city.",
          "paths": [
            "The Elemental Portal Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The Riftlake Shore": {
          "description": "The edge of the great, icy-cold Riftlake that splits the floor of the Great Rift. The shore is a pebble beach sloping into crystal-clear water that is often shrouded in morning mist. The lake is so wide that the far wall of the Rift cannot be seen. The River Shaar flows from the lake, disappearing underground in a spray-filled gorge to the north. Small boats from Eartheart occasionally dock here to check on the Underwatch post.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Gate"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Underwatch garrison (Steel Shields)",
        "Cloaker colony",
        "Residual elemental spirits",
        "Treasure hunter expeditions"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Deep Lords' Vaults are not merely a treasure room; according to fragmentary records in the Hollow Library of Deep Imaskar, the vaults contain a portal to a demiplane where the first king of Bhaerynden stored the original charter of the dwarven empire—a document that, if recovered, could legally unite all dwarven clans under a single ruler. Several powerful factions, including the Zhentarim and the duergar of Dunspeirrin, have agents seeking this artifact.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Eartheart": {
      "name": "Eartheart",
      "basicInfo": "The primary settlement of the gold dwarves and their cultural and spiritual heart since the fall of Underhome. Built on the edge of the Great Rift where sunlight still reaches, Eartheart is a city of soaring stone towers, flying bridges, minarets, and ramp-linked multilevel streets. The city is divided into two sections: Eartheart proper, a walled fortress-city open only to dwarves, and the tent-city of Hammer and Anvil outside the walls, where foreign traders and non-dwarves conduct business. The Deep Lords rule from behind masked identities, using intermediaries to thwart assassins and drow infiltrators. A standing militia of over 14,000 dwarven fighters, the Steel Shields, monitors all movement in and out of the city. Griffon-riding skyguards patrol the Rift from great aviaries high in the towers. The city houses the Temple of the Morndinsamman, the most sacred site in gold dwarf religion, where pilgrims journey from across Faerûn.",
      "x": 5,
      "y": -28,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Great Bhaerynden",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Hammer and Anvil": {
          "description": "The sprawling tent-city outside Eartheart's walls, where foreign merchants, surface traders, and non-dwarven visitors conduct business. The market is a riot of color and noise, with stalls selling surface grain, Underdark fungi, dwarven crafts, and exotic goods from as far as Kara-Tur. Plains tribes bring fresh meat from their hunts to feed the city's griffons. The Hammer and Anvil operates under strict rules enforced by Steel Shield patrols: weapons must be peace-bonded, and violence is met with immediate expulsion. The tent-city is also a hub for information, as travelers from across Faerûn swap tales and rumors.",
          "paths": [
            "The Riftedge Gate",
            "Aviaries of the Peacehammers"
          ]
        },
        "The Riftedge Gate": {
          "description": "The massive main entrance to Eartheart proper, a curtain wall interspersed with great towers known as the Riftedge Towers. Each tower houses a garrison of Steel Shield soldiers who watch for incursions from the Underdark. The gate is forged from adamantine and inscribed with runes of detection that glow when drow, duergar, or aberrations approach. Non-dwarves are turned away here unless they carry special dispensation from a Deep Lord.",
          "paths": [
            "Hammer and Anvil",
            "Temple of the Morndinsamman",
            "Steel Shield Headquarters"
          ]
        },
        "Temple of the Morndinsamman": {
          "description": "The most sacred site in gold dwarf civilization, a collection of individual temples gathered at the city's center. Each building is dedicated to a different deity of the dwarven pantheon, with the Temple of Moradin at the center and the others forming a circle around it. The temples appear as simple shrines on the surface, but their true stature is revealed below: spiral descents through extravagant halls where the Mysteries are explored deep within the earth. Pilgrims travel from across Faerûn to worship here, and the lowest levels house the most revered servants of the dwarven gods.",
          "paths": [
            "The Riftedge Gate",
            "The Deep Lords' Council Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The Deep Lords' Council Chamber": {
          "description": "A secure chamber beneath the Temple of Moradin where the masked Deep Lords convene to govern the city and the remnants of the Deep Realm. The chamber is warded against all scrying and teleportation, and the identities of the council members are known only to each other and to Moradin's high priest. The walls are carved with the laws of the gold dwarves in runes of gold. Decisions are reached through lengthy debate, and the Lord Scepter, Mariochar Bladebeard, executes their will. The chamber is accessible only through a series of trapped passages.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple of the Morndinsamman"
          ]
        },
        "Steel Shield Headquarters": {
          "description": "An immense walled compound near the Riftedge Gate that serves as the barracks, training facility, and command center for the 14,000-strong Steel Shield militia. The headquarters is a city within a city, with its own mess halls, armories, and drill yards. The clang of practice weapons and the bark of drill sergeants echo constantly. A war room contains detailed maps of all known Underdark approaches to the Great Rift, and a trophy hall displays the skulls of notable enemies slain by the Shields over the centuries.",
          "paths": [
            "The Riftedge Gate",
            "Watch Stations"
          ]
        },
        "Watch Stations": {
          "description": "A network of fortified observation posts ringing the perimeter of Eartheart's section of the Great Rift. The watch stations cannot cover the entire breach, so regular patrols sweep the area for signs of new assaults. Each station is manned by a squad of Steel Shields and equipped with signal flares that can alert the entire city within minutes. The stations are grim places, their walls scarred by countless skirmishes with drow raiders and Underdark monsters.",
          "paths": [
            "Steel Shield Headquarters"
          ]
        },
        "Aviaries of the Peacehammers": {
          "description": "The great roosts high in Eartheart's towers where the Peacehammers—the Rift's aerial defense force—house their hippogriff and griffon mounts. The aviaries are staffed by grooms, trainers, and even druids who care for the creatures. The beasts require an incredible amount of fresh meat daily, most obtained from the hunters of the plains tribes who trade at Hammer and Anvil. The skyguards take their mounts on regular patrols, their silhouettes wheeling against the canyon walls a common sight.",
          "paths": [
            "Hammer and Anvil"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Deep Lords (masked ruling council)",
        "Steel Shields (14,000 dwarven militia)",
        "Peacehammers (griffon-riding skyguards)",
        "Priesthood of the Morndinsamman"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "One of the Deep Lords has been replaced by a doppelganger agent of the drow city of T'lindhet, who is systematically feeding information about Eartheart's defenses to the drow. The doppelganger has been in place for nearly a decade and has risen to a position of significant influence. The true Deep Lord is imprisoned in a hidden cell beneath T'lindhet, kept alive for continued interrogation.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Llurth Dreir": {
      "name": "Llurth Dreir",
      "basicInfo": "Known as the Accursed City and the City of Ooze, Llurth Dreir is one of the most populous drow cities in Faerûn, housing nearly 60,000 drow and over 100,000 slaves across three vast, shallow caverns deep beneath the Shaar, west of the Great Rift. The city is unique among drow settlements: Lolth has completely forsaken it for an unknown transgression, and the dominant deity is now Ghaunadaur, the Elder Eye—god of oozes, slimes, and abominations. The city is a sprawling, decaying squalor ruled nominally by drow noble houses but truly controlled by aboleth psions and clerics of Ghaunadaur who wield immense psionic power. Countless jellies, oozes, and slimes are revered and cultivated throughout the city, oozing through the streets and serving as both defense and worship. After the Spellplague, the city was destroyed and became known as the Oozing Ruin, though its legacy of psionic corruption persists.",
      "x": -24,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Great Bhaerynden",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The First Cavern - Matron's District": {
          "description": "The largest of Llurth Dreir's three caverns, housing the decaying palaces of the ruling noble houses. The ceiling rises only forty feet, creating a claustrophobic atmosphere. The streets are coated with a thin layer of slime from the countless oozes that slither freely. The houses fight constantly, not over political power as in other drow cities, but over goods to sacrifice to Ghaunadaur—slaves, food, and captured magic. The district is lit by phosphorescent fungi that cast a sickly green glow, and the constant drip of water from the low ceiling creates an oppressive ambiance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Second Cavern - Market District",
            "The Third Cavern - Temple District"
          ]
        },
        "The Second Cavern - Market District": {
          "description": "A chaotic bazaar where drow merchants trade in adamantine, iron, electrum, slaves, and fungus from the city's extensive mines. The market is a cacophony of shouted haggling, the clink of coins, and the wet squelch of oozes moving through the crowd. Slave auctions occur daily on raised stone blocks stained dark with old blood. The ruling houses compete to control the best stalls, and assassinations over prime locations are common. Aboleth psions drift through the market invisible, monitoring all transactions through their mental probes.",
          "paths": [
            "The First Cavern - Matron's District",
            "The Third Cavern - Temple District",
            "Slave Pits"
          ]
        },
        "The Third Cavern - Temple District": {
          "description": "The religious heart of the city, dominated by a grand temple to Ghaunadaur built from fused stone and glistening slime. The temple is a ziggurat of black obsidian, its steps slick with consecrated ooze. At the summit burns an ever-burning green flame that consumes daily sacrifices. An avatar of Ghaunadaur is said to lurk somewhere in the surrounding caverns, a monstrous entity of shifting protoplasm that the drow both worship and fear. Lesser shrines to Eilistraee, Kiaransalee, and Vhaeraun can be found hidden in the shadows, their worshippers persecuted by Ghaunadaur's clergy.",
          "paths": [
            "The First Cavern - Matron's District",
            "The Second Cavern - Market District",
            "The Ooze Pits"
          ]
        },
        "Slave Pits": {
          "description": "A network of holding pens carved into the walls of all three caverns, housing the city's massive slave population. The pits are divided by race and purpose: deep gnomes work the mines, captured humans and half-elves toil in the fungus farms, and goblins serve as menial labor. The conditions are brutal, and escape attempts are punished by being fed alive to the city's sacred oozes. The pits are overseen by House Elprenna, whose matron mother has perfected the art of slave breeding to maintain the population.",
          "paths": [
            "The Second Cavern - Market District"
          ]
        },
        "The Ooze Pits": {
          "description": "Sacred breeding pools where the city's countless jellies, oozes, and slimes are cultivated. The pits are tended by specially trained drow ooze-handlers who wear protective gear of treated leather and coat themselves in alchemical repellents. The largest pit contains a gargantuan black pudding used for executions and major sacrifices. The oozes are not merely religious symbols but also serve as the city's most effective defense; invaders who breach the gates are funneled into tunnels that can be flooded with oozes at a moment's notice.",
          "paths": [
            "The Third Cavern - Temple District"
          ]
        },
        "The Aboleth Spire": {
          "description": "A hidden tower of smooth, organic stone in the Matron's District that serves as the true seat of power in Llurth Dreir. Here, aboleth psions and their cleric allies conduct their long-term psychic dominance over the city. The spire is warded against detection and filled with psionically charged water in which the aboleths float. The walls are inscribed with protean symbols that shift when observed, and the air hums with constant telepathic chatter. The aboleths' prolonged psychic influence has begun to awaken latent psionic abilities in the drow population, a development the aboleths monitor with scientific interest.",
          "paths": [
            "The First Cavern - Matron's District"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "House Elprenna (slave-breeding rulers)",
        "Clerics of Ghaunadaur (true power behind the houses)",
        "Aboleth psions (secret masters)",
        "Hidden cults of Eilistraee, Kiaransalee, and Vhaeraun"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The aboleths of Llurth Dreir are not working alone. They are in psionic contact with the Abolethic Sovereignty in the Glimmersea and are using the city's drow as a long-term experiment in awakening psionic potential in humanoids. The avatar of Ghaunadaur is actually a manifestation created by the aboleths to keep the drow docile and controllable. The true reason Lolth abandoned the city was the aboleths' successful infiltration of her temple hierarchy.",
      "known": true
    },
    "T'lindhet": {
      "name": "T'lindhet",
      "basicInfo": "The largest drow enclave in Great Bhaerynden, T'lindhet is built on an immense plateau within an elongated cavern beneath the Gnollwatch Mountains, some four and a half kilometers below the surface. The plateau is surrounded on both sides by a subterranean river, forming a natural island fortress. The city maintains sixteen major noble houses that compete for power through intrigue and bloodshed. Unlike other drow cities, T'lindhet developed a unique relationship with the surface: for centuries, it ruled the nation of Dambrath through its half-drow Crinti proxies, controlling trade routes and extracting wealth. The Dambrathan uprising and loss of the surface realm dealt a severe blow to the city, which took decades to recover. A trade village at the foot of the plateau serves as the starting point for the sealed surface road. T'lindhet absorbed many refugees from Llurth Dreir after that city's destruction.",
      "x": 3,
      "y": -5,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Great Bhaerynden",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Trade Village": {
          "description": "A settlement at the foot of the main plateau that serves as the interface between T'lindhet and the wider Underdark. Most transactions with non-drow are conducted here, as the drow are reluctant to allow outsiders into the city proper. The village is a cluster of stone buildings and hide tents surrounding a large caravanserai. Merchants hawk surface goods from Dambrath, Underdark fungus, and drow-crafted poisons. The village is watched by drow snipers from the plateau above, and violence is dealt with swiftly.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ascent Bridges",
            "The Sealed Surface Road"
          ]
        },
        "The Ascent Bridges": {
          "description": "A series of narrow stone bridges and switchback staircases that climb from the Trade Village to the plateau city above. The bridges span the subterranean river that encircles the plateau, offering no cover for attackers. Magical wards detect invisible intruders, and the bridges can be collapsed by destroying key support stones. At the top, a fortified gatehouse checks all who enter the city for weapons, contraband, and surface allegiance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Trade Village",
            "The Noble Plateau",
            "The River Docks"
          ]
        },
        "The Noble Plateau": {
          "description": "The heart of T'lindhet, a sprawl of elegant drow architecture built across the flat top of the plateau. Sixteen noble house compounds cluster around the city center, their spires and domes illuminated by permanent faerie fire in shades of violet, blue, and sickly green. The streets are paved with black stone and lined with statues of past matron mothers. The city is orderly compared to Llurth Dreir, its drow accustomed to the comforts of surface trade. The plateau offers commanding views of the surrounding cavern, making surprise attacks nearly impossible.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ascent Bridges",
            "The Council of Sixteen",
            "The Temple of Lolth",
            "The Crinti Quarter"
          ]
        },
        "The Council of Sixteen": {
          "description": "The ruling chamber where representatives of the sixteen noble houses meet to govern the city. The chamber is a circular amphitheater of black marble, with each house's representative seated on a throne carved with their house sigil. Debate is controlled by the most powerful matron mother currently holding the title of First Speaker. Duels to the death are permitted as a form of dispute resolution, and the floor is permanently stained with blood. The walls are inscribed with binding oaths that prevent house warfare within the city limits—oaths that are frequently bent but rarely broken openly.",
          "paths": [
            "The Noble Plateau"
          ]
        },
        "The Temple of Lolth": {
          "description": "A grand temple dedicated to the Spider Queen, built in the shape of a colossal spider whose legs form the outer buttresses. The temple's high priestess, a fanatical ancient drow named Matron Zessril, maintains strict orthodoxy and brutally suppresses any hint of Ghaunadaur worship that might have crept in from Llurth Dreir refugees. The inner sanctum contains a living altar—a giant spider continually fed on the blood of sacrifices. Webs thick as rope drape the ceiling, and the constant chittering of thousands of spiders fills the air.",
          "paths": [
            "The Noble Plateau",
            "The Spider Pits"
          ]
        },
        "The Crinti Quarter": {
          "description": "A district reserved for the half-drow Crinti who once governed Dambrath on T'lindhet's behalf. Since the uprising that lost Dambrath, the Crinti have been relegated to second-class status, their power diminished. The quarter is a mix of fading elegance and simmering resentment, its buildings decorated with surface-world trophies from the glory days. The Crinti still maintain military training grounds and dream of reclaiming their lost domain, though they lack the resources to act alone.",
          "paths": [
            "The Noble Plateau"
          ]
        },
        "The Spider Pits": {
          "description": "Deep breeding pits beneath the Temple of Lolth where monstrous spiders are raised for mounts, guardians, and sacrifices. The pits descend through multiple levels, each housing spiders of increasing size and lethality. The lower pits are home to a brood of phase spiders that can shift into the Ethereal Plane. The drow arachnomancers who tend the pits are a secretive order, their faces hidden behind spider-shaped masks, their bodies scarred by ritual venom injections.",
          "paths": [
            "The Temple of Lolth"
          ]
        },
        "The Sealed Surface Road": {
          "description": "A trade route tunnel that once connected T'lindhet directly to Dambrath on the surface. The road was sealed after the Dambrathan uprising, blocked by a massive stone plug reinforced with magical wards. The walls are carved with drow spider motifs and Dambrathan horse-lord symbols intermingled—a testament to centuries of cultural exchange. Smugglers and secret drow agents still know hidden bypasses through smaller side tunnels, though using them risks the wrath of the city's matron mothers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Trade Village"
          ]
        },
        "The River Docks": {
          "description": "A series of stone piers on the subterranean river that encircles the plateau. Flat-bottomed boats transport goods and passengers around the city's natural moat. The docks are a secondary trade hub, where goods are transferred from the Trade Village to the city via water. Kuo-toa mercenaries are sometimes hired to pull barges through the swiftest currents. The river flows from the cavern walls into darkness, its ultimate destination unknown.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ascent Bridges"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Council of Sixteen (Noble Houses)",
        "Matron Zessril's Spider Priestesses",
        "Crinti half-drow warriors",
        "Arachnomancers of the Spider Pits"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "One of the sixteen noble houses, House Vraz'dalth, is secretly an agent of the Eldreth Veluuthra—surface elf supremacists. The house matron is a surface elf who was magically disguised and implanted decades ago as part of a long-term mission to destabilize drow power in the southern Underdark. The Crinti know her secret but have chosen not to reveal it, hoping to use her as a bargaining chip to regain their lost status in Dambrath.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Fluvenilstra": {
      "name": "Fluvenilstra",
      "basicInfo": "Known as the Garden City of the Underdark, Fluvenilstra is a breathtaking oasis of magical plant life in the otherwise inhospitable Lowerdark, located eleven miles beneath the Shaar Desolation. The city is the homeland of the slyths—a mysterious, reclusive race of plant-like humanoids—and the only known place where more than a few dozen of them gather. Fluvenilstra is a jungle of glowing fungi, flowering lichens, and magically enhanced plants that have been shaped into living buildings. Water trickles from cracks in the cavern ceiling, collecting in a scummy moat around the circular cavern's edge. The city is governed by the Circle of the Depths, a druid circle dedicated to Silvanus, led by the powerful druid Flowrider Plyp Shurlush. A population of about 370 myconids, led by their sovereign Meln, controls the city's defense force of shambling mounds, phantom fungi, assassin vines, and other plant monsters. Fluvenilstra maintains secret portal networks connecting to the Emerald Enclave, Gulthandor, and the surface world, making it a hidden nexus of druidic power.",
      "x": -35,
      "y": 36,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Great Bhaerynden",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Scummy Moat": {
          "description": "The perimeter of Fluvenilstra's circular cavern, where water trickling from the ceiling collects in a shallow, algae-choked moat. The water is lukewarm and rich in minerals, supporting a diverse ecosystem of bioluminescent algae and tiny aquatic creatures. Wooden bridges cross the moat at regular intervals, leading into the jungle of the inner city. The moat serves as a natural defense, and shambling mounds lurk beneath its surface, ready to drag intruders under. The air is humid and thick with the scent of wet earth and blooming fungi.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fungal Jungle",
            "The Portal Grove"
          ]
        },
        "The Fungal Jungle": {
          "description": "The heart of Fluvenilstra, a dense forest of gigantic mushrooms, phosphorescent lichens, and flowering plants that grow in a riot of colors. Green luminescent fungi on the ceiling provide enough light for low-light vision. Buildings are formed from magically shaped plants—homes of woven vines, towers of hardened fungus stalks, and communal halls grown from living trees. Slyths move silently through the undergrowth, while myconids tend the fungal gardens. The air is thick with spores that induce calm emotions in visitors, a natural defense mechanism cultivated by the druids.",
          "paths": [
            "The Scummy Moat",
            "The Circle of the Depths",
            "The Myconid Warrens",
            "The Living Buildings"
          ]
        },
        "The Circle of the Depths": {
          "description": "The governing center of Fluvenilstra, a natural amphitheater formed by a ring of ancient, intertwined mushroom stalks as thick as tree trunks. The druids of the Circle meet here to discuss city matters, resolve disputes, and perform rituals to Silvanus. The central altar is a flat stone covered in living moss that glows faintly. Flowrider Plyp Shurlush, the chief druid, speaks from a throne of woven flowering vines. Novices from distant slyth outposts and allied druidic circles study here under the calm supervision of the senior druids.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fungal Jungle",
            "The Emerald Portal"
          ]
        },
        "The Myconid Warrens": {
          "description": "The district where the city's myconid population dwells, a series of moist, warm caves dug into the cavern wall and lined with soft fungal carpets. The myconids, led by their sovereign Meln, maintain a quiet, orderly community that communicates through rapport spores. They control the city's defense creatures—shambling mounds, assassin vines, and phantom fungi—through a complex system of chemical signals. The warrens are peaceful but alien to non-fungus creatures, the air filled with spores that carry fragments of the myconids' communal dreams.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fungal Jungle"
          ]
        },
        "The Living Buildings": {
          "description": "A cluster of structures grown from magically enhanced plants rather than built. Slyth artisans have shaped living trees into multi-story homes, bridges of woven vines, and communal halls with walls of flowering petals. The buildings continue to grow and change over time, requiring careful tending. Each structure is unique, reflecting the personality of its inhabitant. The area smells of nectar and fresh growth, and the gentle hum of plant life is a constant background.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fungal Jungle"
          ]
        },
        "The Portal Grove": {
          "description": "A hidden clearing in the Fungal Jungle where the city's portal network is maintained. Stone archways entwined with living vines connect to the Emerald Enclave's headquarters, the forest of Gulthandor, the Amtar Forest, and other druidic strongholds. The portals are camouflaged by illusion magic, appearing as natural stone formations to the untrained eye. The druids use these portals to transport goods, students, and messages across Faerûn, maintaining Fluvenilstra's secret connections to the surface world.",
          "paths": [
            "The Scummy Moat"
          ]
        },
        "The Emerald Portal": {
          "description": "The largest and most stable portal in the city, a permanent, shimmering green archway that connects directly to a hidden grove in the High Forest where the Emerald Enclave maintains a chapterhouse. The portal is used for the exchange of students and the trade of rare Underdark flora for surface-world seeds and knowledge. Access is restricted to Circle members and their authorized guests. The archway is flanked by two colossal phantom fungi that serve as silent guardians.",
          "paths": [
            "The Circle of the Depths"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Circle of the Depths (Slyth druids of Silvanus)",
        "Myconid sovereign Meln and the fungal collective",
        "Shambling mound guardians",
        "Emerald Enclave (allied surface druids)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Flowrider Plyp Shurlush is not a native slyth. He is a human druid who was transformed into a slyth by Silvanus after sacrificing his original body to save a dying slyth elder. The elder's memories merged with his own, giving him centuries of knowledge about the Lowerdark. The secret is known only to the three eldest Circle members. If revealed, it could shatter the slyths' trust in their leadership, as they believe only a true slyth can understand the deep connection between their race and the Underdark's plant life.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Wyrmcaves": {
      "name": "The Wyrmcaves",
      "basicInfo": "A dangerous series of interconnected dragon lairs linked by tunnels beneath the Shaar, the Wyrmcaves are the most feared region of the Deepwild. The caves are uncomfortably large, their ceilings soaring high enough to accommodate dragons of considerable size in flight. The main passage connecting the Wyrmcaves to the Deep Realm is defended by the Wyrmtrap Portal—a masterwork of dwarven engineering that teleports encroaching dragons into a played-out mine section thirty miles from any occupied area. Despite this defense, several dragons have made the Wyrmcaves their home over the millennia. The most notorious current resident is Zellenesterex, an adult blue dragon who is the only creature ever to escape the Dragon's Noose. The caves are littered with the bones of past dragon kills and the remnants of failed adventuring expeditions drawn by rumors of draconic hoards.",
      "x": -21,
      "y": -25,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Great Bhaerynden",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Wyrmtrap Portal": {
          "description": "The main passage connecting the Wyrmcaves to the gold dwarf Deep Realm, a tunnel over fifty feet high and forty feet wide. The portal encompasses the entire area of one slice of the tunnel, from ceiling to floor, wall to wall. A seam of runes carved in a line around the circumference marks the portal's location, cleverly positioned past a second row of carved glyphs to appear as a single band. When activated by a dwarven commander speaking the key phrase 'Steady, soldiers,' the portal teleports anything that passes through it into a trap tunnel thirty miles away. The trap tunnel looks identical but abruptly halts around a corner, where dwarven spellcasters and archers wait to finish any trapped dragon.",
          "paths": [
            "The Great Carrion Hall",
            "The Trap Tunnel"
          ]
        },
        "The Great Carrion Hall": {
          "description": "The largest cavern in the Wyrmcaves, a vast chamber littered with the bones of creatures slain by dragons over centuries. The ceiling soars beyond the reach of torchlight, and the floor is a jumble of massive ribs, skulls, and spines from purple worms, umber hulks, and even other dragons. A faint draconic musk hangs in the air, warning all who enter that they are in a predator's territory. Multiple smaller tunnels branch off to individual lairs, and the acoustics of the hall amplify even the smallest sound into an echoing roar.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wyrmtrap Portal",
            "Zellenesterex's Lair",
            "The Abandoned Hoard"
          ]
        },
        "Zellenesterex's Lair": {
          "description": "The current lair of Zellenesterex, an adult blue dragon who escaped the Wyrmtrap Portal and holds a bitter grudge against the gold dwarves of the Great Rift. The lair is a side cavern that opens from the Great Carrion Hall, its walls scorched by lightning breath and embedded with blue dragon scales. The floor is covered with a carpet of sand the dragon brought from the surface, and the air crackles with static electricity. Zellenesterex's hoard—a collection of sapphires, dwarven-crafted armor, and the petrified remains of an adventuring party—is piled at the center. The dragon is cunning and patient, having learned to avoid the Wyrmtrap through painful experience.",
          "paths": [
            "The Great Carrion Hall",
            "The Lightning Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Lightning Gallery": {
          "description": "A long, narrow tunnel that Zellenesterex has trapped with blue dragon lightning magic. The walls are encrusted with crystals that hold electrical charge, and stepping on the wrong stone triggers a chain reaction of arcing electricity. The gallery serves as an early warning system for the dragon's lair. The burned remains of several duergar raiders are fused to the walls, their forms frozen in their final moments of agony.",
          "paths": [
            "Zellenesterex's Lair"
          ]
        },
        "The Abandoned Hoard": {
          "description": "A side cavern that once housed a now-dead red dragon, its hoard partially looted but still substantial. Mounds of tarnished copper coins, rusted weapons, and broken statues lie scattered across the floor. A skeletal red dragon skull the size of a wagon dominates the center of the chamber, its jaw still locked around the body of a dwarven hero whose adamantine axe remains embedded in the roof of the skull's mouth. Treasure hunters have picked through the hoard, but the deeper piles may still contain overlooked valuables—and possibly the spirits of the dragon's victims.",
          "paths": [
            "The Great Carrion Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Trap Tunnel": {
          "description": "The receiving end of the Wyrmtrap Portal, located thirty miles from any dwarven settlement in a played-out mine section. The tunnel is carved to look identical to the real passage for the first thirty feet before curving right and abruptly ending. A narrow three-foot crawlspace at the tunnel's end allows Medium-size creatures to escape while trapped dragons remain confused. Dwarven workers regularly clean the tunnel of remains, repair damage, and reset the illusion. The tunnel floor shows deep gouges from the claws of past trapped dragons.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wyrmtrap Portal"
          ]
        },
        "The Deepwall": {
          "description": "A massive underground waterfall at the far end of the Wyrmcaves system, plunging hundreds of feet into a mist-filled abyss. The thunder of falling water is deafening, and the spray creates permanent mist that nourishes a unique ecosystem of blind cave fish and phosphorescent moss. The Deepwall marks the boundary between the Wyrmcaves and the deeper, uncharted sections of the Deepwild. Rope bridges once spanned the chasm, but most have rotted away. According to slyth legend, the Deepwall flows all the way down to Fluvenilstra, feeding the city's moat.",
          "paths": [
            "The Great Carrion Hall"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Zellenesterex (adult blue dragon)",
        "Gold dwarf Wyrmtrap defenders (Steel Shields detachment)",
        "Scavengers and treasure hunters"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Zellenesterex is not the only dragon to have escaped the Wyrmtrap. A second, older dragon—a shadow dragon named Murgauroth—entered the Wyrmcaves from the Shadowfell through a rift near the Deepwall. Murgauroth lairs in a pocket of shadow-stuff beyond the waterfall and has been subtly influencing Zellenesterex's dreams, feeding the blue dragon's hatred of the dwarves to create a distraction while Murgauroth searches for a way to permanently open the Shadowfell rift.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Golothaer": {
      "name": "Golothaer",
      "basicInfo": "The lost drow city of Golothaer was one of the earliest drow settlements in the Underdark, founded over five thousand years ago as part of the first drow realm of Telantiwar. The city was destroyed by a cataclysmic civil war between worshippers of Lolth and worshippers of Ghaunadaur—a conflict so devastating that it tore the city apart with magical explosions, opening fissures that swallowed entire fortresses. It was from Golothaer's destruction that the priestess Menzoberra the Kinless fled, leading seven loyal Lolthite families on an epic exodus through the Underdark to found the city of Menzoberranzan in the Northdark. Today, Golothaer is a sprawling, haunted ruin of shattered obsidian spires and collapsed caverns. The ghosts of both Lolthite and Ghaunadauran drow still battle in the silent streets, their spectral war replaying endlessly. The ruins are a magnet for historians of drow civilization, treasure hunters seeking pre-Schism artifacts, and agents of both Menzoberranzan and the Temple of Ghaunadaur seeking lost relics.",
      "x": -1,
      "y": 22,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Great Bhaerynden",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Rift Entrance": {
          "description": "The main approach to Golothaer, where the destruction of the city tore open a permanent fissure in the Underdark. The entrance is a jagged gap in the cavern wall, its edges still sharp from the magical explosions that created it. A cold wind blows from within, carrying faint whispers of ancient battle cries. The ground is littered with fragments of obsidian and the petrified remains of drow warriors caught in the final cataclysm. A permanent magical darkness hangs in the entrance, and crossing it causes the sounds of the ancient civil war to grow louder.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sundered Plaza",
            "The Lolthite Quarter"
          ]
        },
        "The Sundered Plaza": {
          "description": "The central plaza of Golothaer, now split by a deep chasm that opened during the city's destruction. The two halves of the plaza are connected only by the petrified body of a colossal spider that was caught in the cataclysm and now serves as an unintended bridge. The plaza is dominated by the toppled statue of Menzoberra, its head smashed by Ghaunadaurans during their final assault. Ghostly echoes of the city's last moments replay here: spectral drow fighting, screaming, and dying in an endless loop. The plaza is a place of intense psychic resonance, and sensitive individuals may experience flashbacks of the ancient battle.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rift Entrance",
            "The Temple of the Spider Queen",
            "The Ghaunadauran Catacombs",
            "The Exodus Gate"
          ]
        },
        "The Temple of the Spider Queen": {
          "description": "The ruined temple of Lolth, once the heart of Golothaer's Lolthite faction. The temple is a collapsed ziggurat of black stone, its spider-shaped architecture still recognizable despite the destruction. The main altar is cracked but intact, still oozing a faint divine energy that causes spiders in the area to grow to unnatural size. The ghost of the temple's last high priestess, a spectral banshee, haunts the inner sanctum, her wails calling down curses on any worshipper of Ghaunadaur who enters. Hidden safe rooms beneath the altar may still contain relics from the time before the city's fall.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sundered Plaza",
            "The High Priestess's Sanctum"
          ]
        },
        "The High Priestess's Sanctum": {
          "description": "A hidden chamber beneath the Temple of the Spider Queen, sealed by Menzoberra before her flight and never breached. The sanctum contains the personal possessions of the last Lolthite high priestess: ceremonial vestments, correspondence with other drow cities, and a crystal memory-sphere containing a recording of Menzoberra's speech to her followers before the exodus. The chamber is protected by a guardian drider—the high priestess's former bodyguard, transformed by Lolth's blessing into an undying sentinel.",
          "paths": [
            "The Temple of the Spider Queen"
          ]
        },
        "The Ghaunadauran Catacombs": {
          "description": "The subterranean stronghold of Golothaer's Ghaunadauran faction, a network of slime-coated tunnels beneath the Sundered Plaza. The catacombs are still inhabited by the descendants of the Ghaunadauran victors—now devolved into feral, slime-worshipping savages who have forgotten their own history. The walls are covered in ancient glyphs praising the Elder Eye. A pool of sentient black ooze in the deepest chamber is believed to be a fragment of Ghaunadaur's avatar, and it still communicates telepathically with those who approach.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sundered Plaza",
            "The Ooze Pool"
          ]
        },
        "The Ooze Pool": {
          "description": "The deepest chamber of the Ghaunadauran Catacombs, filled with a pool of black, sentient ooze that is a fragment of Ghaunadaur's avatar manifested during the city's destruction. The ooze communicates telepathically, offering power in exchange for service. It has absorbed the memories of thousands of drow who were consumed by it during the civil war and can share fragments of ancient lore with those who please it. The pool is guarded by ooze mephits and a particularly intelligent black pudding that serves as the pool's high priest.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ghaunadauran Catacombs"
          ]
        },
        "The Exodus Gate": {
          "description": "The tunnel through which Menzoberra led her seven families out of Golothaer at the height of the city's destruction. The gate is marked by a monument carved by the fleeing drow—a spider glyph that still glows faintly. The tunnel descends steeply into the Underdark, its walls scarred by the passage of thousands of desperate refugees. The route to Menzoberranzan is long and dangerous, but the first few miles are still marked with ancient way-finding runes. Ghosts of drow who died during the exodus are said to walk this path, still trying to reach the promised city.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sundered Plaza"
          ]
        },
        "The Lolthite Quarter": {
          "description": "The ruined residential district of the Lolthite faction, a maze of collapsed noble house compounds and shattered spires. The quarter is haunted by the spirits of drow who chose to fight rather than flee, their ghostly forms still barricaded in defensive positions. Treasure hunters have picked through the surface rubble, but many houses have intact cellars and vaults that remain unexplored. A particularly wealthy house, House Vrazden, is rumored to have sealed its vault with a spell that can only be broken by a direct descendant—a bloodline that may still exist in Menzoberranzan.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rift Entrance"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Spectral Lolthite defenders (ghosts)",
        "Feral Ghaunadauran descendants",
        "The Ooze Pool consciousness (fragment of Ghaunadaur's avatar)",
        "Menzoberranzan historical expeditions"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The crystal memory-sphere in the High Priestess's Sanctum contains not just Menzoberra's speech but also the names and locations of seven other caches of Lolthite artifacts hidden across the Underdark during the exodus. One of these caches is said to contain the original charter of Menzoberranzan, a document that legally defines the city's founding houses. If recovered by a rival house, it could be used to challenge the dominance of House Baenre in Menzoberranzan and trigger a succession crisis.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Iltkazar": {
      "name": "Iltkazar",
      "basicInfo": "The sole surviving kingdom of the eight sub-kingdoms of Deep Shanatar, Iltkazar is a reclusive but magnificently defended city beneath the Omlarandin Mountains of Tethyr. Known as the Mithral Kingdom, it is home to over 7,500 shield dwarves and ruled by King Mith Barak the Clanless—a silver dragon in dwarven form who spends seventy-five years of every century as a solid mithral statue. During his stasis, the Regency Council governs. Three approach tunnels, each protected by nine gates and the Wyrmtrap Portal, make it the best-defended city in the Underdark. Its seven major caverns contain palaces, forges, and the legendary Deep Lords' Vaults.",
      "x": 33,
      "y": -11,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Old Shanatar",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Drakkalor Gate": {
          "description": "The northernmost of Iltkazar's three gate-tunnels, a passage of nine successive adamantine gates each inscribed with dwarven runes of warding, detection, and destruction. The gates are spaced so that any force breaching one finds itself trapped between two. The walls between gates are lined with murder holes manned by elite crossbowdwarves. At the outermost gate, a great carved statue of a dragon looms, warning all who approach that Iltkazar is guarded by more than stone and steel.",
          "paths": [
            "The City Circle",
            "Holorar Approach"
          ]
        },
        "Barakuir Gate": {
          "description": "The eastern approach, a tunnel that once led to the now-ruined Iron Kingdom of Barakuir. The nine gates here are reinforced against psionic assault, a legacy of the Mindstalker Wars against Oryndoll's illithids. The walls are carved with reliefs depicting dwarven victories. Beyond the outermost gate lies a hall of statues of the eight kings of Shanatar, their stone eyes said to watch for treachery.",
          "paths": [
            "The City Circle",
            "The Arnvaults"
          ]
        },
        "Ultoksamrin Gate": {
          "description": "The southwestern entrance, leading toward the ruined kingdom of Ultoksamrin. These nine gates are enchanted to nullify drow magic, a necessary precaution given the proximity of Guallidurth. The walls are scorched by ancient battles. A permanent garrison of Steel Shield veterans stands ready to collapse the tunnel should the city ever face overwhelming assault.",
          "paths": [
            "The City Circle"
          ]
        },
        "The City Circle": {
          "description": "The main cavern of Iltkazar, a vast ring-shaped open space where buildings are constructed in the surface-world style—stone towers, halls, and forges clustered around a central plaza. The ceiling soars high enough for griffon flight, and the entire cavern is lit by enchanted crystal orbs that mimic daylight. The City Circle is the commercial and social heart, with taverns, guildhalls, and the homes of the city's artisans. The air rings with the sound of hammers and the smell of forge-smoke.",
          "paths": [
            "Drakkalor Gate",
            "Barakuir Gate",
            "Ultoksamrin Gate",
            "The Throne Chamber",
            "Forge Ward",
            "The Arnvaults"
          ]
        },
        "The Throne Chamber": {
          "description": "A grand hall at the center of the City Circle where King Mith Barak sits upon his throne—a mithral slab carved with the history of Shanatar. The king appears as a perfect statue, unmoving for decades at a time, yet his astral form travels the planes and his consciousness watches over the city. The walls of the chamber are lined with the shields and arms of defeated enemies. The Regency Council convenes in an attached chamber, their decisions guided by the king's telepathic communications.",
          "paths": [
            "The City Circle",
            "Regency Council Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "Regency Council Chamber": {
          "description": "A round room adjoining the Throne Chamber where the Regency Council—six elders representing the city's major clans—governs during King Mith Barak's stasis. The walls are hung with maps of the Underdark, and a great table of obsidian holds a scrying pool that shows threats approaching the city. The Council is efficient but conservative, viewing any change with deep suspicion.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "Forge Ward": {
          "description": "A secondary cavern branching from the City Circle, filled with the constant thunder of dwarven forges. Mithral from the city's mines is worked here into weapons, armor, and the intricate automatons that supplement the city's defenses. The heat is intense, and the walls glow faintly from centuries of forge-fire. Master smiths pass down secrets in a language of hammer-strikes, and the quality of Iltkazar's mithral work is renowned across the Underdark.",
          "paths": [
            "The City Circle",
            "Deep Lords' Vaults"
          ]
        },
        "The Arnvaults": {
          "description": "A network of chambers between the Barakuir Gate and the City Circle where the city's stores of grain, fungus, and preserved meat are kept. The vaults are enchanted to keep food fresh for centuries—a necessity for a city that rarely opens its gates. The Arnvaults also house the city's treasury of coins and trade bars.",
          "paths": [
            "Barakuir Gate",
            "The City Circle"
          ]
        },
        "Holorar Approach": {
          "description": "The tunnel beyond the Drakkalor Gate that once connected to the fallen dwarven kingdom of Holorar. Now it is a carefully maintained defensive kill-zone lined with pit traps, crystal mines, and a final desperate fallback position known as the Last Redoubt. The tunnel eventually opens into the wild Underdark, patrolled regularly by griffon-riding scouts.",
          "paths": [
            "Drakkalor Gate"
          ]
        },
        "Deep Lords' Vaults": {
          "description": "The most heavily guarded location in Iltkazar, a series of chambers beneath the Forge Ward sealed by doors of pure mithral. The vaults hold the accumulated wealth of surviving Shanatar: crowns, scepters, ancestral weapons, and the recovered treasures of the fallen sub-kingdoms. Among the items is a portal to the dwarven city of Morndivver, kept secret from all but the Regency Council.",
          "paths": [
            "Forge Ward"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "King Mith Barak the Clanless (silver dragon in dwarven form)",
        "Regency Council of Iltkazar",
        "Steel Shield dwarven militia",
        "Griffon riders of the sky patrol"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "In 1367 DR, King Mith Barak's astral form was captured by Tiamat while he traveled the planes, and he was not released until 1477 DR. During his century-long absence, the Regency Council was secretly infiltrated by a duergar agent who subtly redirected trade routes to favor Dunspeirrin. The king's return in 1477 DR revealed the treachery, but the duergar had already mapped every defensive tunnel in the city and shared that intelligence with Gracklstugh.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Guallidurth": {
      "name": "Guallidurth",
      "basicInfo": "The Temple City of Lolth, Guallidurth is the oldest drow settlement in the southern Underdark, located beneath the Calim Desert in the Middledark. With over 80,000 inhabitants, it is a sprawling metropolis ruled by a council of twenty-one noble houses, though Mistress Mother Fizzri Khaven-Ghell has systematically reduced the number of rival houses to twenty-four. The city is distinguished by the countless temples and shrines to Lolth built by each noble house interpreting the Spider Queen's faith differently. Vhaeraunites, once a powerful minority, have been largely driven out to found their own settlements. The city is at constant war with Iltkazar and views Sloopdilmonpolop as its rightful possession.",
      "x": -15,
      "y": -15,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Old Shanatar",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Stacks": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Guallidurth, a vertical descent through a series of guard posts and checkpoints built into the natural chimney that leads from the Upperdark into the city's main cavern. Each level of the Stacks is controlled by a different noble house, creating a bewildering bureaucracy of bribes, threats, and inspections. The walls are carved with spider motifs and warnings in drow script. At the bottom, the entrance opens into the Temple Row promenade.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple Row",
            "The Bazaar of a Thousand Webs"
          ]
        },
        "Temple Row": {
          "description": "The central thoroughfare of Guallidurth, a wide avenue lined with the temples of the twenty-one ruling houses. Each temple is a unique architectural statement of its house's interpretation of Lolth's will. Some are soaring ziggurats, others are domed basilicas, and a few are carved into the shapes of monstrous spiders. The constant chanting of priestesses and the smell of incense and blood saturate the air. At the center stands the Grand Web, the city's ruling council chamber.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stacks",
            "The Grand Web",
            "The Bazaar of a Thousand Webs"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Web": {
          "description": "The meeting place of the twenty-one ruling houses, a structure of interlocking obsidian platforms suspended over a deep chasm by cables of spider-silk. The council convenes here to debate war, trade, and the endless feuds between houses. Assassinations during council sessions are technically forbidden, but the chasm below has swallowed many a careless matron mother. The acoustics of the chamber amplify whispers, ensuring that every secret is heard by someone.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple Row",
            "House Khaven-Ghell Compound"
          ]
        },
        "House Khaven-Ghell Compound": {
          "description": "The heavily fortified estate of the city's dominant house, ruled by Mistress Mother Fizzri Khaven-Ghell. The compound sprawls across three stalagmite towers connected by web-bridges, defended by drider sentinels and phase spider guardians. The Mistress Mother's private quarters occupy the highest spire, where she keeps a scrying pool fed by the blood of captured Iltkazar dwarves. The house's temple is shaped like a crouching black widow spider.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Web"
          ]
        },
        "The Bazaar of a Thousand Webs": {
          "description": "A chaotic marketplace in a side cavern off Temple Row, where drow merchants sell poisons, silks, slaves, and Underdark war-gear. The bazaar is perpetually shrouded in a fine mist of water droplets that catch the faerie fire lighting and create a rainbow sheen. Transactions are conducted in a rapid, whispered cant that non-drow find almost impossible to follow. The slave pens at the bazaar's edge are divided by race and condition, with surface elves commanding the highest prices.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stacks",
            "Temple Row",
            "Slave Pits of the Thousand Webs"
          ]
        },
        "Slave Pits of the Thousand Webs": {
          "description": "A grim series of holding cells carved into the cavern floor, where thousands of captives—humans, dwarves, goblins, svirfneblin, and even lesser drow—await sale or sacrifice. The pits are overseen by a caste of half-drow overseers who take grim pleasure in their work. Escape is virtually impossible, as the pits are warded against teleportation and patrolled by spider swarms.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bazaar of a Thousand Webs"
          ]
        },
        "The Spider Pits": {
          "description": "Deep breeding caverns beneath the city where the priestesses of Lolth raise monstrous spiders of all sizes for mounts, guardians, and sacrifices. The largest pit contains the Broodmother, a spider of colossal proportions fed on a diet of living sacrifices. The arachnomancers who tend the pits are scarred with ritual venom injections and speak in a clicking, chittering language they believe is the voice of Lolth herself.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple Row"
          ]
        },
        "Dallnothax Passage": {
          "description": "A hidden tunnel on the city's western edge, once used by Vhaeraunite worshippers to flee persecution. The passage leads to a network of escape routes connecting to the three Vhaeraunite settlements—Dallnothax, Holldaybim, and Iskasshyoll—hidden in the Forest of Mir on the surface, and ultimately to the Vault of Cloaked Midnight in Ultoksamrin. Guallidurth's matron mothers have sealed the main passage, but smaller bypasses remain, known only to the faithful.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple Row"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Mistress Mother Fizzri Khaven-Ghell (dominant matron)",
        "Council of Twenty-One Ruling Houses",
        "Priestesses of Lolth (many sects)",
        "Hidden Vhaeraunite sympathizers",
        "Arachnomancers of the Spider Pits"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Despite Mistress Mother Fizzri Khaven-Ghell's systematic purges, a Vhaeraunite cell still operates within the city, hidden among the lower-ranking members of House Mizz'rynturl. This cell has been feeding intelligence about Guallidurth's troop movements to the dwarves of Iltkazar, hoping to weaken the Lolthite matriarchy and eventually seize control of the city for the Masked Lord.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Sshamath": {
      "name": "Sshamath",
      "basicInfo": "The City of Dark Weavings, Sshamath is unique among drow cities: it is ruled not by the priestesses of Lolth, but by a Conclave of wizards representing the eight schools of arcane magic. Located in the Gauth Grottoes beneath the Far Hills, the city is a thriving center of magical trade, its Dark Weavings Bazaar the largest market for magic items in the Underdark. The civil war that overthrew the matron mothers occurred nearly three millennia ago, and since then, arcane mastery has been the only path to power. The city maintains trading contacts with the Zhentarim citadel of Darkhold above and plunders ruins of lost civilizations for magical lore. The massive central pillar Z'orr'bauth dominates the city, its spiral ramp winding past thousands of bridges to the cavern roof.",
      "x": 36,
      "y": 38,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Old Shanatar",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Z'orr'bauth": {
          "description": "The great central pillar of Sshamath, a colossal natural stone column stretching from floor to ceiling of the main cavern. A wide, steep ramp spirals around the outside of this rock tower, winding past thousands of slim stone bridges that extend to and from the surrounding forest of stalactites and stalagmites. At the pillar's base is the entrance to the Dark Weavings Bazaar. At its top, a guarded chamber houses the city's emergency portal network, deployable only in times of existential threat. Z'orr'bauth is the heart of the city, and its spiral is crowded at all hours with drow climbing, descending, and traversing bridges.",
          "paths": [
            "Dark Weavings Bazaar",
            "The Stonestave",
            "The Quillspires"
          ]
        },
        "Dark Weavings Bazaar": {
          "description": "The marketplace at the bottom of the great cavern, a sprawling maze of hundreds of tents and small shops carved into stalagmites. Visitors can buy almost anything here, but the true specialty is magic—scrolls, wands, potions, and stranger items plundered from Netherese ruins. At the center of the bazaar stands a single building housing the slave market, where captives are magically shrunk into fist-sized clearstone cubes and stored on shelves. The air crackles with residual magic and the polyglot chatter of traders from across the Underdark.",
          "paths": [
            "Z'orr'bauth"
          ]
        },
        "The Stonestave": {
          "description": "The meeting place of the Conclave of wizards, a stalactite-stalagmite column shaped like a wizard's staff. The Stonestave stretches from floor to ceiling, its interior honeycombed with chambers and corridors. The Conclave consists of eight archmages, each a master of one school of magic. The Stonestave is heavily warded against scrying and assassination, and the magical defenses are refreshed daily. At its base, a hall of records contains the city's arcane contracts and history.",
          "paths": [
            "Z'orr'bauth"
          ]
        },
        "The Quillspires": {
          "description": "A cluster of hollowed stalactites hanging from the roof northwest of Z'orr'bauth, where the city's scribes, low-level wizards, and arcane researchers create scrolls for sale in the bazaar and conduct minor magical research. The Quillspires glow with the light of thousands of candles and ever-burning lamps. The sound of scratching quills is a constant backdrop. Senior scribes occupy the highest spires, while apprentices work in the lower levels.",
          "paths": [
            "Z'orr'bauth",
            "Tower of the Masked Mage"
          ]
        },
        "Tower of the Masked Mage": {
          "description": "A stalactite temple dedicated to Vhaeraun, suspended over the Darkwoods district. The tower is a secret place of worship for the city's Vhaeraunites, who operate with guarded tolerance from the wizard-rulers. The temple's exterior is carved with masked faces that seem to shift expression when not directly observed. Inside, worshippers gather in cloaked anonymity to plot the eventual return of male drow dominance—though they tread carefully, as the wizards are not without their own spies.",
          "paths": [
            "The Quillspires",
            "Darkwoods"
          ]
        },
        "Darkwoods": {
          "description": "A petrified forest in the southeast corner of the city, where the stone trunks of ancient trees create a shadowy maze. This district is the city's slum, home to the poorest drow, outcasts, and a small population of monsters. The wizards of the Conclave mostly ignore the Darkwoods, making it a haven for those who prefer obscurity. A black market operates here, dealing in items too dangerous or illegal for the main bazaar.",
          "paths": [
            "Tower of the Masked Mage"
          ]
        },
        "Web of the Spider Queen": {
          "description": "A cluster of stalactites and stalagmites that serves as the city's token temple to Lolth. Only a nominal deference is given to the Spider Queen here; the temple is small and underfunded, its priestesses bitter and marginalized. The walls are covered in dusty webs, and the altar sees only a handful of sacrifices each year. The priestesses dream of a counter-revolution but lack the power to challenge the wizards.",
          "paths": [
            "Z'orr'bauth"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Conclave of Eight Archmages (ruling wizard council)",
        "Sshamath House (founding Lolth-worshipers, now marginalized)",
        "Vhaeraunite worshippers (Tower of the Masked Mage)",
        "Dark Weavings Bazaar merchant guilds"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Conclave's Master of Divination, Seldszar Elpragh, has secretly discovered that the faerzress of the Gauth Grottoes is not natural but was deliberately weakened by an ancient Netherese weapon test. He has located the weapon's power source—a still-functioning mythallar fragment buried beneath the Darkwoods—and is quietly excavating it, planning to use its power to elevate himself above the other seven masters.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Oaxaptupa": {
      "name": "Oaxaptupa",
      "basicInfo": "The World Beneath the World, Oaxaptupa is a hive-city of tlincallis (scorpion-folk) built within the ruins of the dwarven sub-kingdom of Xothaerin beneath the Small Teeth Mountains of Amn. The tlincallis arrived here in 1365 DR through a mass gate ritual from Maztica and immediately set about remaking the abandoned dwarven city in their own image. Dwarven buildings were gutted—stairs removed, doors widened, and every other floor torn out to accommodate the stingers' height. The city's architecture follows a precise mystical grid to maximize divinatory magic, governed by Lord Diviner Tluipacal. About thirty clans operate as raiders and scouts, while the Diviner caste performs daily sacrifices and reads omens in everything. The old dwarven coliseum now serves as a cleric workshop producing healing potions.",
      "x": 9,
      "y": 26,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Old Shanatar",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Plaza of Visions": {
          "description": "The center of Oaxaptupa, a massive stone plaza anchored by a wondrous hot spring fountain that once powered dwarven water wheels. The stingers have gutted the machinery and replaced it with a brilliant tile mosaic radiating one hundred feet in every direction. The mosaic is divided into eleven sections corresponding to the limbs of a scorpion, the tlincalli zodiac, and their gods' avatar animals. When the sulfurous spring water bubbles up and splashes onto the mosaic, individual stingers spend days staring at the fall of droplets, drinking only the fountain's water and waiting for the interplay of symbols to reveal their destinies. This is the most public form of divination in the city.",
          "paths": [
            "Corantllil Workshop",
            "Tluipacal's Observatory",
            "The Raider Camps"
          ]
        },
        "Corantllil Workshop": {
          "description": "Housed in the former coliseum of Xothaerin, this massive workshop is manned by clerics dedicated to Corantllil, the tlincalli deity of unceasing labor. Here, healing potions are brewed almost daily using recipes brought from Maztica and adapted to Underdark ingredients. The clerics work in rhythmic, hypnotic unison, chanting prayers as they prepare their concoctions. Potions are stored in hollowed stalagmite vials and traded to other Underdark denizens through intermediaries—the stingers themselves have little use for surface gold.",
          "paths": [
            "Plaza of Visions",
            "Sacrificial Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Tluipacal's Observatory": {
          "description": "The seat of Lord Diviner Tluipacal, the ruler of Oaxaptupa, located in a converted dwarven tower that was the only structure the stingers found adequate. The observatory is filled with scrying bowls, astrological charts, and cages of captive creatures used for augury. Tluipacal conducts daily divinations to determine which threats to respond to, which enemies to attack, and whether visitors should be received or sacrificed. The walls are covered in the carapaces of previous Lord Diviners, each inscribed with their greatest portents.",
          "paths": [
            "Plaza of Visions",
            "Gateway to the Elemental Plane of Fire"
          ]
        },
        "Sacrificial Pens": {
          "description": "A network of holding cells where prisoners—captured salamanders, azers, duergar, and the occasional surface adventurer—await sacrifice. The stingers do not keep slaves, finding the effort of motivating others bothersome. Instead, prisoners are sacrificed on a regular schedule to feed the city's divinatory magic. The pens stink of fear and sulfur, and the walls are scratched with the desperate messages of past captives.",
          "paths": [
            "Corantllil Workshop"
          ]
        },
        "The Raider Camps": {
          "description": "A cluster of crude, rebuilt dwarven structures on the city's perimeter where about half of the tlincalli population—the raiders and scouts—live in a state of constant readiness. Each camp belongs to one of the thirty clans, flying a standard made from the silk of Underdark spiders dyed in clan colors. The air is filled with the clash of weapons practice and the chittering war-songs of stingers preparing for expeditions. Trophies—skulls of salamanders, efreeti, and fomorians—decorate the camp walls.",
          "paths": [
            "Plaza of Visions"
          ]
        },
        "Gateway to the Elemental Plane of Fire": {
          "description": "Deep within the southern caverns of ruined Xothaerin, beneath the volcanic Kossuth's Eyes, a natural gate to the Elemental Plane of Fire burns eternally. The tlincallis discovered this gate when they arrived and immediately launched a furious assault on the azers, efreeti, and salamanders who lived nearby. Though they drove most away, the gate still occasionally disgorges fire elementals, which the stingers either destroy or capture for sacrifice. The cavern walls glow with heat, and the air shimmers with distortion.",
          "paths": [
            "Tluipacal's Observatory"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Lord Diviner Tluipacal (ruler of Oaxaptupa)",
        "Corantllil cleric workshops (healing potions)",
        "Thirty raider clans (Huitzolputl, War Chief)",
        "Nest Keeper Ahxalli (maintains the city's mystical grid)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The tlincallis did not arrive by accident. The mass gate ritual was actually orchestrated by a rogue group of Amnian merchants who sought to destabilize the Underdark beneath their rivals in Calimshan. The merchants provided the stingers with detailed maps of Xothaerin in exchange for a promise of future tribute—a promise the stingers have no intention of keeping. The merchants' agent, a half-drow translator, still lives in Oaxaptupa, and the knowledge of this surface connection would enrage the drow of Guallidurth if it were revealed.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Stommheim": {
      "name": "Stommheim",
      "basicInfo": "The Everlasting Kingdom of Stommheim is a nation of trolls occupying the ruins of Korolnor, one of the eight sub-kingdoms of Shanatar. Located beneath the Troll Mountains of Amn in a network of intricate tunnels and caverns, Stommheim is ruled by Ursummnogh the Unkillable, a prodigiously ancient and massive troll who has survived centuries of assassination attempts. Five of the original dwarven cities have been occupied and crudely adapted by the trolls, while a small number of derro led by the sorcerer Kursh Brokenhorn live as uneasy allies, assisting in defense against mind flayers in exchange for troll protection. The kingdom's culture is heavily influenced by giant customs, and the trolls speak a pidgin of Giant and dwarven. The realm claims both the subterranean territories and the mountain peaks above.",
      "x": -28,
      "y": 34,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Old Shanatar",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gnawed Gate": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Stommheim, a once-magnificent dwarven archway now defaced by troll claw marks and festooned with the bones of fallen enemies. The gate is 'guarded' by a particularly large and perpetually hungry troll who demands food from all who pass—typically a live animal or a significant chunk of meat. The walls are slick with troll saliva and bear the scratched warnings of past visitors. Beyond the gate lies the main descent into the Everlasting Kingdom.",
          "paths": [
            "Ursummnogh's Pit",
            "The Splintered City"
          ]
        },
        "Ursummnogh's Pit": {
          "description": "The lair of Ursummnogh the Unkillable, a vast circular chamber carved from the heart of the mountain. The floor is littered with the still-regenerating body parts of trolls who displeased the king—arms, legs, and heads that twitch and writhe. Ursummnogh sits on a throne of fused dwarf skulls, his body scarred by millennia of battle. The walls are hung with the preserved heads of his most notable victims, each labeled in crude Giant script. A deep pool of stagnant water at the chamber's edge serves as a regeneration bath.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gnawed Gate",
            "The Splintered City",
            "Derro Enclave"
          ]
        },
        "The Splintered City": {
          "description": "The largest of Stommheim's five occupied dwarven cities, a sprawling ruin of exquisite stonework now inhabited by trolls. While the dwarven architecture remains largely intact—the stonework of Korolnor was so excellent that even millennia of troll occupation have not destroyed it—the interiors have been gutted and adapted. Buildings serve as sleeping dens and food storage, and the streets are patrolled by packs of trolls who settle disputes through violent regeneration contests.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gnawed Gate",
            "Ursummnogh's Pit",
            "The Forge of Broken Horns"
          ]
        },
        "Derro Enclave": {
          "description": "A fortified quarter within the Splintered City where Kursh Brokenhorn and the derro live, protected by an uneasy alliance with the trolls. The enclave is clean and orderly compared to the troll-occupied ruins. Derro sorcerers and alchemists work here, producing the hallucinogenic fungi and psionic items they trade to the trolls in exchange for protection. A shrine to Diinkarazan, the derro demigod, occupies the center of the enclave, its walls covered in gibbering script.",
          "paths": [
            "Ursummnogh's Pit"
          ]
        },
        "The Forge of Broken Horns": {
          "description": "An ancient dwarven forge that the derro and trolls have adapted for crude smithwork. The trolls, while not natural craftsmen, have learned to hammer out simple weapons and armor under the derro's instruction. The forge is powered by a bound fire elemental that the original dwarves summoned millennia ago, still laboring in its binding circle. The heat is intense, and the noise of crude hammering echoes through the nearby tunnels.",
          "paths": [
            "The Splintered City"
          ]
        },
        "The Star Bridge": {
          "description": "A magnificent dwarven bridge spanning a deep chasm at the edge of Stommheim's territory, one of the finest surviving examples of Korolnor's stonework. The bridge is inscribed with constellations in mithral inlay that still gleam in the dark. It connects the kingdom to the deeper tunnels of Old Shanatar and is one of the few structures the trolls actively maintain, as it serves as a strategic choke point. The derro have warded the bridge against illithid approach.",
          "paths": [
            "The Splintered City"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "King Ursummnogh the Unkillable (troll ruler)",
        "Kursh Brokenhorn (derro sorcerer)",
        "Troll clans of the Splintered City",
        "Derro alchemists and psions"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Ursummnogh is not merely a large troll; he is a rare exemplar of trollkind who has absorbed the lingering divine magic of the Korolnor Scepter, one of ten Ruling Scepters of Shanatar. The scepter was shattered millennia ago but a fragment remains embedded in Ursummnogh's skull, granting him rapid regeneration and immunity to fire—the one weakness of his kind. If the scepter fragment were removed, he would become vulnerable.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Sloopdilmonpolop": {
      "name": "Sloopdilmonpolop",
      "basicInfo": "The greatest kuo-toa city in the Underdark, Sloopdilmonpolop lies beneath Firedrake Bay off the coast of Tethyr, approximately eight miles below the seafloor. Known as the City of Pools, it is built around the Sacred Pool of the Sea Mother—a square, subterranean lake occupying a cavern nearly 1,000 feet long and wide. The city is a theocracy ruled by Va-Poolgol Dagagoorg, who serves as both king and high priest of Blibdoolpoolp. Founded by the prophet Sloopdilmonpol around −3000 DR, the city survived centuries of war against the dwarves of Xothaerin and Sondarr during the Axe and Fin Wars. Though never conquered, the long conflict ushered in a period of decline from which the city has never fully recovered, and it has since emerged as a surprisingly welcoming mercantile hub for non-illithid traders.",
      "x": 3,
      "y": -35,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Old Shanatar",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Sacred Pool of the Sea Mother": {
          "description": "The heart of Sloopdilmonpolop, a square subterranean lake occupying a cavern nearly 1,000 feet long and wide. The water is crystal-clear and faintly bioluminescent, casting shifting patterns of light on the cavern ceiling. At the center of the pool stands a colossal statue of Blibdoolpoolp carved from a single massive stalactite, its lobster-clawed arms extended in blessing. Kuo-toa pilgrims from across Faerûn come here to worship, wading into the pool's edges to receive the Sea Mother's touch. The water is sacred and healing, but non-believers who enter without permission are dragged under by the pool's guardian spirits.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple of the Sea Mother",
            "The Coral Market",
            "The Prophet's Spire"
          ]
        },
        "Temple of the Sea Mother": {
          "description": "The grand temple of Blibdoolpoolp, built on the northern shore of the Sacred Pool. The temple is a massive structure of fused coral and salvaged stone, its entrance carved in the shape of a gaping lobster maw. Inside, the walls are draped in nets filled with the bones of sacrificed enemies, and a constant chorus of gurgling hymns echoes through the halls. The high altar is a living giant clam that opens only for the king-priest Va-Poolgol Dagagoorg, revealing a pool of water that allows direct communion with Blibdoolpoolp.",
          "paths": [
            "Sacred Pool of the Sea Mother",
            "King-Priest's Throne"
          ]
        },
        "King-Priest's Throne": {
          "description": "The seat of Va-Poolgol Dagagoorg, located in a chamber adjoining the temple. The throne is carved from the shell of a colossal crab and encrusted with pearls. From here, the king-priest issues decrees and judges disputes. The chamber walls are covered in murals depicting the history of Sloopdilmonpolop—the founding by the prophet, the Axe and Fin Wars, and the city's eventual emergence as a mercantile hub. Petitioners approach on their bellies, and the king-priest speaks in a bubbling voice that sounds like words spoken underwater.",
          "paths": [
            "Temple of the Sea Mother"
          ]
        },
        "The Prophet's Spire": {
          "description": "A tall stalagmite on the Sacred Pool's eastern shore, hollowed out and converted into a shrine to the prophet Sloopdilmonpol. The spire's walls are covered in the prophet's original writings, carved into the stone in flowing kuo-toan script. Pilgrims ascend the spiral stair to meditate on the prophet's wisdom. At the top, a small pool of water shows visions to those who fast and pray for three days.",
          "paths": [
            "Sacred Pool of the Sea Mother"
          ]
        },
        "The Coral Market": {
          "description": "A bustling bazaar on the southern shore where kuo-toa merchants sell their wares to Underdark travelers. The market is built from living coral that continues to grow, requiring constant pruning. Goods on offer include preserved deep-sea fish, luminous pearls, enchanted shells, and the distinctive coral jewelry for which Sloopdilmonpolop is famous. The market is surprisingly open to outsiders—illithids excepted—and surface-world goods are traded for kuo-toan crafts at haggled rates.",
          "paths": [
            "Sacred Pool of the Sea Mother",
            "Traveler's Landing"
          ]
        },
        "Traveler's Landing": {
          "description": "The designated area for non-kuo-toa visitors, a series of stone guesthouses and a tavern built on the western shore. The buildings are crude but functional, furnished with the best the kuo-toa can manage—mats of woven seaweed, stone basins of fresh (if slightly fishy) water, and walls covered in carvings of the Sea Mother. The tavern, the Splashing Fin, serves fermented algae beer and raw fish. Illithids are unwelcome and are attacked on sight.",
          "paths": [
            "The Coral Market"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "King-Priest Va-Poolgol Dagagoorg (ruler and high priest)",
        "Priesthood of Blibdoolpoolp",
        "Coral Market merchant guilds",
        "Pilgrim guardians of the Sacred Pool"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Sacred Pool is not natural; it is a planar gateway to the Elemental Plane of Water that the prophet Sloopdilmonpol stabilized millennia ago. The pool's bottom connects directly to the deep trenches of the Glimmersea, and during certain lunar alignments, creatures from the abyssal depths enter the pool. The king-priest has been secretly feeding surface-world captives to these creatures in exchange for prophetic visions, a practice that would horrify the pilgrim faithful if exposed.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Ultoksamrin": {
      "name": "Ultoksamrin",
      "basicInfo": "Once the richest and most important sub-kingdom of Deep Shanatar, Ultoksamrin is now a contested realm beneath Calimshan whose caverns harbor the Vault of Cloaked Midnight—a cathedral city of Vhaeraunite drow. The Vault is so named because its grand temple covers nearly half of the ruling cavern, a breathtaking structure of shadow-wreathed stone that serves as the primary temple to Vhaeraun in all the Underdark. The drow here are escapees from Guallidurth who fled religious persecution and now live in the former clanholds of the dwarves for protection. Led by Vokkrzyr Rualfren, the community survives through stealth, faith, and an uneasy network of alliances with surface elves who sympathize with the Masked Lord's vision of drow reclamation. The rest of Ultoksamrin is a patchwork of duergar claimants, fomorian raiders, and treasure seekers searching for lost dwarven wealth.",
      "x": -40,
      "y": -11,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Old Shanatar",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Cathedral of Cloaked Midnight": {
          "description": "The great temple of Vhaeraun that dominates half the ruling cavern of Ultoksamrin, a structure of fused shadow-stuff and dark stone that seems to drink light. The cathedral's ceiling rises to the cavern roof, and its walls are carved with masked faces that whisper secrets to the faithful. The main sanctuary seats thousands, and the central altar is a block of obsidian upon which a perpetual flame of silver fire burns—a gift from the surface elf allies of the faith. The high priestess conducts nightly masses under the cover of supernatural darkness, and the faithful gather in cloaked anonymity.",
          "paths": [
            "The Masked Halls",
            "The Pilgrims' Passage",
            "The Deep Vaults"
          ]
        },
        "The Masked Halls": {
          "description": "The governing chambers of the Vault of Cloaked Midnight, where Vokkrzyr Rualfren and his council of Shadowhands direct the Vhaeraunite resistance. The halls are filled with maps of Guallidurth, intelligence reports from agents in the surface world, and a detailed plan for the eventual liberation of the drow from Lolth's tyranny. The walls are hung with masks—each representing a clan of faithful—and the air vibrates with quiet, fervent discussion. Initiates undergo ritual branding here to mark their dedication.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cathedral of Cloaked Midnight"
          ]
        },
        "The Pilgrims' Passage": {
          "description": "A hidden tunnel connecting Ultoksamrin to the surface, guarded at both ends by Vhaeraunite sentries. The passage is used to smuggle Vhaeraunite faithful from Guallidurth and to bring in supplies and allies from the surface. It emerges in a cave in the Forest of Mir, near the three Vhaeraunite surface settlements. The passage is warded against scrying by Lolthite clergy and is lined with shrines to Vhaeraun.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cathedral of Cloaked Midnight"
          ]
        },
        "The Deep Vaults": {
          "description": "The ancient dwarven clanholds beneath the cathedral, repurposed as living quarters, armories, and storage. The original dwarven stonework is still visible—exquisite carvings of Moradin and the Morndinsamman—but the drow have draped them with Vhaeraunite tapestries and masked iconography. The vaults house the three legendary Tomes of Nyx, ancient books cared for by the High Old Ones. The tunnels are a maze, designed by dwarves to confuse invaders, and the drow have added their own traps and illusions.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cathedral of Cloaked Midnight"
          ]
        },
        "The Ruined Forum": {
          "description": "A large, collapsed dwarven plaza on the outskirts of the Vhaeraunite territory that serves as a contested no-man's-land. Duergar prospectors, treasure hunters, and fomorian raiders skirmish here over access to untapped dwarven ruins. The Forum's original purpose—a marketplace—is still evident in the toppled stone stalls and the scattered coins of a long-dead civilization. Control of the Forum changes frequently, and the Vhaeraunite drow keep it under constant observation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cathedral of Cloaked Midnight"
          ]
        },
        "Tomes of Nyx Repository": {
          "description": "A hidden chamber within the Deep Vaults where the three legendary Tomes of Nyx are kept, cared for by the High Old Ones. These ancient books contain secrets of shadow magic, prophecy, and the history of the Vhaeraunite faith. The chamber is warded against fire, theft, and divination, and only the most senior clergy are permitted entry. The air within is cool and still, and the books seem to shift slightly when not directly observed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cathedral of Cloaked Midnight"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Vokkrzyr Rualfren (leader of the Vhaeraunite community)",
        "Shadowhand Council (governing body of Vhaeraunites)",
        "High Old Ones (keepers of the Tomes of Nyx)",
        "Duergar scavengers (competing for ruins)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The three Tomes of Nyx are not merely repositories of knowledge—they are linked to a trapped shard of the Shadowfell, and collectively, they can open a portal to the demiplane where the Masked Lord himself walks. Vokkrzyr Rualfren has been seeking a ritually pure descendant of the first Vhaeraunite exiles to complete a ritual described in the third tome that would allow him to physically summon Vhaeraun into the Underdark—an act that would instantly trigger a holy war with every Lolthite city in Faerûn.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Menzoberranzan": {
      "name": "Menzoberranzan",
      "basicInfo": "The City of Spiders, the most infamous and powerful drow city in Faerûn. Located in the enormous arrowhead-shaped cavern of Araulcaurak, two miles wide and a thousand feet high, the city is illuminated by perpetual faerie fire in shades of blue, violet, and green. Founded by Menzoberra the Kinless after fleeing the destruction of Golothaer, the city is home to over 20,000 drow and countless slaves. It is a rigid matriarchy ruled by the Council of Eight — the Matron Mothers of the eight most powerful noble houses — though House Baenre has held effective control for centuries through soft power and calculated brutality. The city is the primary seat of Lolth's worship in the Underdark, and its priestesses wield absolute authority. Menzoberranzan is connected by tunnels to Mithral Hall, Blingdenstone, and the surface world of the Silver Marches, making it both a trade nexus and a constant military threat to its neighbors.",
      "x": 5,
      "y": -37,
      "radius": 8,
      "region": "Northdark",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Tier Breche": {
          "description": "The Academy of Menzoberranzan, a high ledge overlooking the city from the northern wall of the cavern. Three schools train the drow elite here: Arach-Tinilith, the seminary where priestesses of Lolth are forged through torture and indoctrination; Sorcere, the wizard's school housed in a stalagmite tower carved with ever-shifting protective runes; and Melee-Magthere, the warrior academy where male drow learn the art of killing. The ledge is perpetually wreathed in incense smoke and the screams of initiates. Access is forbidden to all but students and faculty, and trespassers are sacrificed to Lolth.",
          "paths": [
            "Qu'ellarz'orl",
            "Narbondellyn"
          ]
        },
        "Qu'ellarz'orl": {
          "description": "The Palace of Nobles, a high plateau in the southwestern corner of the cavern separated from the rest of the city by a forest of giant mushrooms called Kyorbblivvin. Here, the four most powerful noble houses — Baenre, Barrison Del'Armgo, Oblodra, and Faen Tlabbar — maintain their fortress-compounds. No businesses or crowds intrude on the aristocratic silence. The Baenre compound alone is a city within a city, its spider-shaped chapel visible from across the cavern. Nine tunnels connect the plateau to Lake Donigarten, and on the other side of the mushroom forest lies the wealthy district of Narbondellyn. The plateau is watched by drider sentinels and protected by wards that incinerate unauthorized teleporters.",
          "paths": [
            "Tier Breche",
            "Narbondellyn",
            "Donigarten"
          ]
        },
        "Narbondellyn": {
          "description": "The Broad Streets, named after the great pillar-clock Narbondel that dominates the district. Every morning, the Archmage of Menzoberranzan heats the pillar's base with magic, and the warmth rises through the day — the drow tell time by tracking the heat with their infravision. Narbondellyn is the district of wealthy houses on the rise, home to seven powerful noble families and the city's finest businesses: gem cutters, perfumeries, moneylenders, chic cafes, and theaters. Gromph Baenre maintains a private palace here, a spire of black stalactites with shifting runes. Goblin slaves serve as personal attendants, dwarven slaves repair fine metalwork, and minotaur slaves stand guard at noble gates. The streets are broad and elegant, yet the threat of assassination lurks behind every silk curtain.",
          "paths": [
            "Tier Breche",
            "Qu'ellarz'orl",
            "The Bazaar",
            "West Wall"
          ]
        },
        "The Bazaar": {
          "description": "The commercial heart of Menzoberranzan, a chaotic marketplace where drow merchants hawk goods beneath the glow of faerie fire. Stalls sell poisons, spell components, Underdark delicacies, surface-world curiosities, and slaves of every race. The air is thick with the clink of copper fangs and silver strands — the city's coinage, each stamped with the Baenre emblem. Street performers, pickpockets, and food vendors selling spiced rothe sausage crowd the thoroughfares. The Bazaar is the one place where non-drow traders are tolerated, though they are watched constantly. The Council of Spiders maintains a hidden safehouse here, their phase spider guardian assessing the intentions of potential recruits.",
          "paths": [
            "Narbondellyn",
            "Eastmyr",
            "Duthcloim",
            "The Braeryn"
          ]
        },
        "Eastmyr": {
          "description": "The district of commoners and lesser merchants, home to poor drow and middle-class non-drow residents. Minor noble houses like Hunzrin and Kenafin maintain compounds here, but the streets are mostly populated by laborers, artisans, and outcasts who cannot afford the rents of Narbondellyn. The Shadow of Narbondel inn, run by a scarred halfling named Dalfred Noakes, caters to travelers seeking anonymity. A finer establishment, Symeera's, was founded by a Neveren mage stranded in the Underdark, offering excellent accommodations at moderate prices. House Hunzrin controls most of the city's agriculture from its Eastmyr headquarters, its members arrogant despite their lowly status.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bazaar",
            "The Braeryn",
            "Donigarten"
          ]
        },
        "The Braeryn": {
          "description": "The Stenchstreets, the most crowded and desperate district of Menzoberranzan. Sick and outlaw drow crowd into decaying tenements alongside the non-drow 'undercreatures' — goblins, kobolds, half-drow, and escaped slaves. Twenty to thirty beings cram into single rooms, and the air reeks of unwashed bodies and despair. A popular sport among nobles is the Hunt, during which they descend on the Braeryn to slaughter as many undercreatures as possible and collect trophies — ears, teeth, and heads. Jenbaraan's Pit, a high-class slave-fighting establishment, draws nobles seeking blood sport. In 1372 DR, the Braeryn was the ignition point of Syrzan's slave revolt, and the Council of Spiders now operates from a hidden townhouse here, using a phase spider to test new recruits.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bazaar",
            "Eastmyr",
            "Clawrift"
          ]
        },
        "Duthcloim": {
          "description": "Also known as Manyfolk, this district is home to wealthy non-drow merchants and well-to-do non-noble drow who have earned their fortunes through trade. The streets are cleaner than the Braeryn but lack the prestige of Narbondellyn. Imported goods from the surface — wines, fabrics, and weapons — are traded here in quiet deals brokered by intermediaries. The district is a microcosm of the wider Underdark, with duergar craftsmen, svirfneblin gem-cutters, and even the occasional surface agent conducting business under the ever-watchful eyes of the drow nobility.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bazaar",
            "West Wall"
          ]
        },
        "West Wall": {
          "description": "The western district of Araulcaurak, stretching from Tier Breche to Narbondellyn and scarred by the deep chasm of Westrift. Many noble houses maintain compounds here, including the former home of Drizzt Do'Urden — House Do'Urden, now a burned-out ruin since its destruction by House Baenre. The district houses the Fane of Quarvelsharess, a public cathedral to Lolth, and the Barracks of House Barrison Del'Armgo. The Six Black Rings tavern serves poisoned drinks with a smile, and the Xeva's Den gambling hall sees fortunes won and lives lost nightly. Warmfires, a rentable mansion, offers luxury accommodations for visiting dignitaries who prefer not to lodge with the noble houses.",
          "paths": [
            "Narbondellyn",
            "Duthcloim",
            "Tier Breche"
          ]
        },
        "Donigarten": {
          "description": "The easternmost district of the city, centered on Lake Donigarten — a dark, still body of water where the city's food supply is cultivated. An island in the lake holds the city's rothe herds, raised by House Hunzrin for meat and cheese. The shores are lined with moss fields and fungus farms tended by orc and goblin slaves under the lash of drow overseers. The lake harbors pyrimo, an aggressive schooling fish prized for its bitter flavor, though it is poisonous if prepared incorrectly. The district is quiet compared to the city's heart, its rhythms dictated by the cycles of planting and slaughter.",
          "paths": [
            "Qu'ellarz'orl",
            "Eastmyr",
            "Clawrift"
          ]
        },
        "Clawrift": {
          "description": "A great chasm at the southeastern edge of the cavern that serves as both a garbage disposal and an execution ground. The rift descends into the unknown lower Underdark, and the bodies of sacrificed slaves, failed assassination victims, and political enemies are hurled into its depths. Strange creatures occasionally crawl up from the darkness, and the rift is said to connect to the Demonweb Pits of Lolth herself. A perpetual cold wind blows from Clawrift, carrying the faint scent of decay. The drow avoid the edge, but the desperate of the Braeryn sometimes make pilgrimages here to toss offerings to the Spider Queen.",
          "paths": [
            "The Braeryn",
            "Donigarten"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Council of Eight (Ruling Matron Mothers)",
        "House Baenre (dominant house, soft power)",
        "Priestesses of Lolth (Arach-Tinilith graduates)",
        "Bregan D'aerthe (Jarlaxle's mercenary band)",
        "Council of Spiders (Vhaeraunite underground)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The great pillar Narbondel is not merely a clock — it is the tomb of Menzoberra the Kinless, the city's founder. Her body was interred within the pillar, and only the Archmage of Menzoberranzan knows the truth. The heating ritual performed each morning is not merely timekeeping but a binding spell that keeps Menzoberra's spirit trapped. If the ritual were to cease, legend holds that her vengeful ghost would rise to reclaim the city she built from the hands of House Baenre.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Gracklstugh": {
      "name": "Gracklstugh",
      "basicInfo": "The City of Blades, the duergar deepkingdom on the shores of the Darklake. Founded in −3717 DR by duergar escaping the fall of Deep Shanatar, Gracklstugh is a cavern city that slopes down to meet the Darklake on its eastern side, split in half by the great chasm of Laduguer's Furrow. The city is an industrial nightmare of smelters, foundries, and forges whose firelight mingles among stalagmites, filling the air with acrid smoke that causes a persistent cough known as grackle-lung. Ruled by the Deepking and the Council of Lairds, the duergar here are master smiths and psionic warriors, exporting fine weapons and armor across the Underdark. A sizable minority of derro — mad, dwarven-kin psions — live in uneasy alliance with the gray dwarves, their Council of Savants wielding considerable influence. The city maintains a standing army of 5,000, one of the strongest in the Underdark, and patrols the Darklake with ironclad warships. Foreigners are permitted only in the Darklake District near the docks; venturing beyond without a writ of passage means death or enslavement.",
      "x": 3,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Northdark",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Darklake District": {
          "description": "The only section of Gracklstugh open to outsiders, located on the eastern shore where the cavern slopes into the Darklake. The district is the poorest and most dangerous part of the city despite its commercial importance. The Blade Bazaar, the main marketplace, is surrounded by low buildings and weaponsmiths hawking duergar steel. Foreign merchants — drow, kuo-toa, and the occasional surface agent — haggle under the watch of Stone Guard patrols. The Darklake Docks at the northern end are a forest of ironclad warship masts and kuo-toa skiffs, the water lapping against stone piers stained with industrial runoff. The air is thick with smog and the smell of fish, and the persistent cough of grackle-lung echoes from every alley.",
          "paths": [
            "Blade Bazaar",
            "Laduguer's Furrow",
            "Northfurrow District"
          ]
        },
        "Blade Bazaar": {
          "description": "The central marketplace of the Darklake District, a town square surrounded by low stone buildings that house the finest weaponsmiths in the Underdark. Duergar smiths display their wares on iron racks: serrated short swords, black plate mail, and the distinctive King's Blades carried by the Stone Guard. Weapons are tested on captured slaves in demonstration pits, and the clang of steel accompanies all transactions. The bazaar is also a hub for information, with merchants trading gossip alongside goods. The Deepking's agents monitor the square for sedition, their psionic probes invisible but ever-present.",
          "paths": [
            "Darklake District",
            "Deepking's Hall"
          ]
        },
        "Deepking's Hall": {
          "description": "The austere palace of the Deepking of Gracklstugh, located in the Southfurrow District across Laduguer's Furrow. The hall features a massive iron gate completely devoid of decoration — the duergar consider ornamentation frivolous. Inside, the Stone Guard in their black plate mail stand motionless at attention, their King's Blades gleaming with gold filigree denoting rank. The throne room is a cavernous chamber lit only by forge-fire, where the Deepking receives lairds, foreign emissaries, and the occasional condemned prisoner. The hall also houses the treasury and the war room, where maps of the Darklake and surrounding Underdark are etched into stone tables.",
          "paths": [
            "Blade Bazaar",
            "Southfurrow District"
          ]
        },
        "Laduguer's Furrow": {
          "description": "Also called the Cleft, this great chasm splits Gracklstugh in half from north to south, named for the duergar god of labor and tyranny. Bridges of stone and iron span the Furrow at intervals, each guarded by toll-collectors who demand writs of passage from those crossing from the foreigner's district to the duergar homelands. The Furrow's depths glow with the orange light of lava, and the clang of hammers rises from the smithies built into its walls. The bridges are choke points in the city's defense, each capable of being collapsed in seconds by destroying key support stones.",
          "paths": [
            "Darklake District",
            "Southfurrow District",
            "Northfurrow District"
          ]
        },
        "Southfurrow District": {
          "description": "The southern half of the duergar homelands, home to the city's most powerful clans and the Deepking's Hall. The district is a maze of stone halls and clan compounds, each marked with a clan sigil carved into the doorpost. Slaves — goblins, svirfneblin, and captured surface-dwellers — toil in the alleys under the watch of duergar overseers. The Hall of the Council of Lairds, where clan leaders debate policy, is a circular stone chamber with concentric benches carved into the floor. The air is slightly cleaner here than in the Darklake District, but the ever-present smog still clings to every surface.",
          "paths": [
            "Deepking's Hall",
            "Laduguer's Furrow",
            "Derro Enclave"
          ]
        },
        "Northfurrow District": {
          "description": "The northern half of the duergar homelands, encompassing the residences of clans specializing in crafts, brewing, and other commercial goods. The district is the industrial quarter, filled with smelters and workshops. Clan Cahaldrho brews the city's ale, Clan Dunnhaer practices traditional crafts, and Clan Ekhlond experiments with dangerous new inventions. The derro maintain their enclave here, a ward of twisted architecture where the Council of Savants conducts psionic experiments. The district slopes upward from the Darklake, its highest reaches relatively free of smog.",
          "paths": [
            "Darklake District",
            "Laduguer's Furrow",
            "Derro Enclave"
          ]
        },
        "Derro Enclave": {
          "description": "A walled quarter within the Northfurrow District where the derro population lives under the watch of the Council of Savants. The enclave is a place of twisted, illogical architecture — stairs that lead to ceilings, doors that open onto walls — reflecting the derro's mad psionic minds. Alchemical laboratories produce hallucinogenic fungi and potions of dubious stability. The derro sorcerers maintain an uneasy alliance with the duergar, their psionic abilities too valuable to discard but too dangerous to trust. The enclave's central temple to Diinkarazan is a chamber of gibbering stone faces that seem to shift expression when not directly observed.",
          "paths": [
            "Northfurrow District",
            "Southfurrow District"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Deepking Horgar Steelshadow V (ruler)",
        "Council of Lairds (clan leaders)",
        "Council of Savants (derro psions)",
        "Stone Guard (royal guards and secret police)",
        "Clan Muzgardt (black market merchants)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The derro Council of Savants is not truly loyal to the Deepking. They are secretly breeding a new generation of psionically enhanced duergar-derro hybrids in their enclave's depths, intending to eventually replace the Deepking with a puppet ruler of their own creation. The Deepking suspects but lacks proof, and the Savants' psionic defenses make scrying their activities nearly impossible.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Blingdenstone": {
      "name": "Blingdenstone",
      "basicInfo": "The City of the Speaking Stones, the greatest svirfneblin settlement in the Northdark. Founded in −690 DR by deep gnomes fleeing the phaerimm, Blingdenstone is a city of cunning traps, stone illusions, and earth magic carved into the limestone beneath the Rauvin Vale. The drow of Menzoberranzan destroyed the city in 1370 DR by summoning bebelith demons inside its walls, forcing the gnomes to flee to Mithral Hall and Silverymoon. In recent years, the svirfneblin have begun reclaiming their ancestral home, clearing rubble, resetting traps, and driving out the monsters that claimed the ruins. The city is a labyrinth of narrow corridors, hidden chambers, and cleverly disguised defenses that make it nearly impossible for larger races to navigate without a guide. The deep gnomes are masters of elemental magic, and the city's walls whisper with the bound spirits of earth elementals.",
      "x": -7,
      "y": -21,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Northdark",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Trapped Approach": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Blingdenstone, a natural cave mouth that appears unguarded to the untrained eye. In reality, the approach is a death trap of invisible pit falls, collapsing ceilings, and walls enchanted to appear as open corridors while hiding solid stone. Glyphs of warding trigger elemental explosions for those who step on the wrong cobblestone. The svirfneblin guides know the safe path through muscle memory alone, and they change the trap patterns weekly. The bones of drow raiders are still embedded in some of the walls, left as a warning.",
          "paths": [
            "The Reclaimed Warrens",
            "Stoneheart Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Reclaimed Warrens": {
          "description": "The resettled heart of Blingdenstone, where the returned svirfneblin have repaired the damage of the drow attack. Homes are carved into the living rock, their entrances hidden behind sliding stone panels and illusionary walls. The warrens are warm and cozy, lit by softly glowing crystals and the smell of baking mushroom bread. Families work together to carve new chambers, and the sound of tinkering — hammers, chisels, earth elementals grumbling — fills the air. Murals depicting the city's destruction and the long exile decorate the main corridors, ensuring no gnome forgets what was lost.",
          "paths": [
            "The Trapped Approach",
            "The Great Gem Cavern",
            "Temple of Callarduran Smoothhands"
          ]
        },
        "The Great Gem Cavern": {
          "description": "A spectacular natural geode at the city's center, its walls encrusted with uncut gems that glitter in the light of permanent magical lanterns. This was the city's original treasure vault and remains its most sacred site. Before the destruction, the cavern held the accumulated wealth of centuries; much was looted by drow, but the svirfneblin believe many gems were sealed behind magical collapses triggered during the attack. Mining teams work daily to recover the lost treasures, guided by earth elementals that sense the gems through the stone.",
          "paths": [
            "The Reclaimed Warrens",
            "Elemental Binding Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "Elemental Binding Chamber": {
          "description": "A chamber of smooth, polished obsidian where the city's elementalists bind earth elementals into service. Summoning circles are etched into the floor with powdered gemstone, and the air hums with the low resonance of elemental speech. The bound elementals serve as guardians, miners, and construction laborers, their strength enabling the svirfneblin to reshape stone with ease. The current Master Elementalist, an ancient gnome named Therlin Gemfinder, maintains the bindings with daily rituals. The chamber also houses a large, sealed portal to the Elemental Plane of Earth used only in emergencies.",
          "paths": [
            "The Great Gem Cavern"
          ]
        },
        "Stoneheart Market": {
          "description": "A trading post carved into the outer ring of the city, where svirfneblin merchants barter with surface-world traders from Mithral Hall and Silverymoon. The market is a series of interconnected chambers, each specializing in a different good: uncut gems, fine stonework, alchemical earth-salts, and captured Underdark curiosities. Prices are fair but nonnegotiable — the svirfneblin have little patience for haggling. The market is protected by a permanent anti-scrying ward and a squad of earth elemental guardians that rest as statues until danger approaches.",
          "paths": [
            "The Trapped Approach",
            "The Reclaimed Warrens"
          ]
        },
        "Temple of Callarduran Smoothhands": {
          "description": "The primary temple of the svirfneblin god, located in a domed chamber of polished hematite. The walls are covered in bas-reliefs depicting the gnomes' journey through the Underdark, their founding of Blingdenstone, and their eventual return. The altar is a single massive ruby that pulses with a faint inner light during religious ceremonies. The high priest, Belwar Dissengulp — the Most Honored Burrow Warden and a living legend among his people — leads the congregation in prayers of gratitude for the city's reclamation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Reclaimed Warrens"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Belwar Dissengulp (Most Honored Burrow Warden)",
        "Elementalist Guild (earth elemental binders)",
        "Gemfinder mining teams",
        "Stonewarden defenders"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Not all of Blingdenstone's original chambers have been reclaimed. Deep in the city's lowest levels, sealed behind collapses, the bebelith demons that destroyed the city still lurk in hibernation. The svirfneblin know they are there but lack the military strength to clear them. Worse, one of the bebeliths has begun spawning — and the young demons are slowly digging their way toward the resettled warrens.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Ched Nasad": {
      "name": "Ched Nasad",
      "basicInfo": "Once the second-greatest drow city in the Underdark, the City of Shimmering Webs now lies in utter ruin. Built in a massive vertical cavern beneath the Graypeak Mountains, Ched Nasad was a marvel of drow engineering — its buildings and noble compounds suspended above the floor by calcified webs of magical spider silk. The city was destroyed on Uktar 3, 1372 DR during the Silence of Lolth, when duergar agents used alchemical stonefire bombs to ignite the calcified webs. The entire city fell from its moorings, crashing to the cavern floor in a cascade of shattered stone, burning silk, and screaming drow. Today, Ched Nasad is a haunted graveyard of collapsed towers, dangling web-fragments, and the vengeful spirits of the thousands who died in the cataclysm. Scavengers from across the Underdark pick through the rubble, but the dead do not rest easily here.",
      "x": -33,
      "y": 15,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Northdark",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Fallen Gate": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Ched Nasad, once a magnificent archway of fused obsidian and spider silk now shattered and half-buried in rubble. The gate is watched by a group of duergar scavengers from Gracklstugh who charge a toll for entry — payable in loot extracted from the ruins. The ground is littered with calcified web fragments that crunch underfoot like broken glass. Faint, ghostly luminescence — the remnants of the city's faerie fire enchantments — still flickers in the depths, casting eerie shadows across the rubble.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shards",
            "The Web Wastes"
          ]
        },
        "The Shards": {
          "description": "The main impact zone where the city's noble compounds struck the cavern floor. Shattered towers jut from the rubble at impossible angles, their interiors exposed like dissected organs. The calcified webs that once suspended them hang from the ceiling in broken strands, some still strong enough to support a climber, others crumbling at the slightest touch. The spirits of drow nobles who died in the fall haunt the area, their translucent forms reenacting the final moments of the cataclysm. Scavengers have picked the surface clean, but sealed vaults still wait in the deeper rubble.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fallen Gate",
            "The Temple of Lolth's Silence",
            "The Calcified Web Bridges"
          ]
        },
        "The Temple of Lolth's Silence": {
          "description": "The ruined temple of Lolth, once the largest of its kind outside Menzoberranzan. When the city fell, the temple's priestesses were trapped inside as their goddess remained silent — their prayers unanswered, their magic useless. The temple now lies on its side, its spider-shaped architecture still recognizable despite the collapse. The main altar is intact but desecrated, covered in the scrawled blasphemies of duergar looters. A palpable aura of despair permeates the chamber, and clerics of Lolth who enter experience intense vertigo and nausea — the Spider Queen's lingering displeasure.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shards",
            "The Silent Crypt"
          ]
        },
        "The Silent Crypt": {
          "description": "The burial chamber of Ched Nasad's fallen matron mothers, located beneath the collapsed temple. Unlike other areas, the crypt was largely protected from the destruction by powerful wards that still flicker with fading magic. The sarcophagi of ancient matrons line the walls, their lids carved with scenes of Lolth's glory — scenes that now seem mocking. A ghostly chorus of lamentation fills the chamber, the combined voices of the unavenged dead. Treasure seekers whisper of a still-sealed vault containing the regalia of the last ruling council, including the Scepter of Ched Nasad.",
          "paths": [
            "The Temple of Lolth's Silence"
          ]
        },
        "The Calcified Web Bridges": {
          "description": "The remnants of the web network that once connected the suspended city. A few calcified strands still stretch across the chasm, their surfaces rough and crystalline. Crossing them is a test of nerve — the webs creak ominously and shed fragments into the darkness below. The bridges offer the only access to the few structures still partially suspended near the ceiling: a wizards' tower that hangs upside down, and a guard post where the petrified bodies of drow soldiers still stand at attention.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shards",
            "The Hanging Tower"
          ]
        },
        "The Hanging Tower": {
          "description": "A wizards' tower that was torn free during the destruction but snagged on an outcropping, now suspended upside down hundreds of feet above the cavern floor. The interior is a disorienting maze where floors are ceilings and staircases defy gravity. The wizard who once ruled here, an archmage named Kelnozz, perished in the fall but his spirit remains — a maddened specter that still conducts arcane experiments on any who enter. His spellbooks, scattered across the inverted rooms, contain unique magic developed during the Silence of Lolth.",
          "paths": [
            "The Calcified Web Bridges"
          ]
        },
        "The Web Wastes": {
          "description": "The outskirts of the ruin where the destruction was less absolute — a maze of collapsed warehouses, slave pens, and lesser merchant compounds. The area is infested by ettercaps and giant spiders that have spun new webs among the old, creating a deadly hunting ground. A black market of scavengers operates from a hidden base in a partially intact warehouse, trading recovered artifacts to buyers from across the Northdark.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fallen Gate"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Duergar scavenger consortiums",
        "Ghosts of the fallen matron mothers",
        "Ettercap and spider colonies",
        "Black market artifact dealers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The duergar stonefire bombs were not the only cause of Ched Nasad's destruction. The calcified webs had been secretly weakened over decades by a traitor within House Melarn, who was working for an agent of the Jaezred Chaulssin — the hidden drow city of shadow dragons. The Jaezred Chaulssin sought to eliminate Ched Nasad as a rival and use its fall to destabilize Menzoberranzan, and the duergar were merely convenient pawns.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Neverlight Grove": {
      "name": "Neverlight Grove",
      "basicInfo": "A myconid colony in the eastern Northdark, Neverlight Grove is one of the quietest and most peaceful places in the Underdark — or it was, before the Demon Queen of Fungi arrived. The grove is a garden of luminous fungi, towering mushrooms, and gently phosphorescent mosses where myconids spend their lives in shared contemplation and fungal cultivation. When the demon lord Zuggtmoy was swept into the Underdark during the Rage of Demons, she was drawn to the grove's fungal riches and took up residence. Through telepathically shared dreams, she has corrupted the colony's elder sovereign Phylo and half the myconid population, who now embrace decay as a form of ecstatic worship. The younger sovereign Basidia leads the untainted myconids, struggling to maintain their peaceful way of life against the spreading corruption. The grove sits within a larger fungal forest called Yggmorgus, a garden of Zuggtmoy's design where mushrooms of titanic proportions sprout from rotting soil.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": -6,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Northdark",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Sporelight Entry": {
          "description": "The entrance to the grove, a natural tunnel whose walls are coated in phosphorescent moss that pulses with a gentle, hypnotic rhythm. The spores in the air induce calm and mild euphoria in humanoids — a natural defense mechanism of the myconids. Visitors are met by rapport spores, the telepathic communication of the myconid guards, who assess intentions with gentle but probing mental questions. The entry is peaceful, but those who harbor hostile intent find the spores turning noxious.",
          "paths": [
            "The Circle of Medicine",
            "Yggmorgus"
          ]
        },
        "The Circle of Medicine": {
          "description": "The heart of Basidia's untainted colony, a wide ring of cultivated mushrooms where myconids gather to share their experiences through rapport spores. The sovereign Basidia holds court here from a throne of woven mycelium, its mushroom-cap head crowned with a ring of bioluminescent nodules. The myconids tend fungal gardens that produce healing spores and medicinal mushrooms, and a gentle, communal consciousness pervades the circle. A melody of combined thought — part music, part memory — drifts through the spores.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sporelight Entry",
            "Fungal Gardens",
            "The Garden of Welcome"
          ]
        },
        "Fungal Gardens": {
          "description": "The agricultural heart of the grove, where myconids cultivate dozens of fungal species for food, medicine, and construction. Giant shelf mushrooms serve as bridges and platforms. Glowing puffballs provide lighting. The gardens are a symphony of earthy smells and soft, wet sounds as myconids tend their crops with gentle caresses. Some of the fungi are sentient, communicating with the myconids in slow, deliberate thought patterns.",
          "paths": [
            "The Circle of Medicine"
          ]
        },
        "The Garden of Welcome": {
          "description": "A deceptive paradise of flowers and fungi cultivated by Phylo's corrupted myconids, appearing as a beautiful refuge in the Underdark darkness. The fungal blooms release spores that induce rapturous visions, and visitors who stay too long find their thoughts turning to decay as something beautiful. Phylo's myconids welcome guests with wide, empty smiles, offering to 'share the Lady's gift.' The centerpiece is a massive corpse flower that reeks of rotting flesh — Zuggtmoy's personal offering to the grove.",
          "paths": [
            "The Circle of Medicine",
            "Yggmorgus",
            "Phylo's Circle"
          ]
        },
        "Phylo's Circle": {
          "description": "The seat of the corrupted elder sovereign, a ring of black, weeping mushrooms that drip viscous spores into a central pool. Phylo, its cap covered in pulsing nodules of sickly gray, communes with Zuggtmoy through the pool's depths. The myconids here are in various stages of corruption — some retain their original forms but speak of decay with religious fervor, while others have transformed into spore servants, their bodies covered in fruiting fungal bodies. The rapport spores here carry Zuggtmoy's telepathic voice, a seductive whisper promising eternal union in decay.",
          "paths": [
            "The Garden of Welcome"
          ]
        },
        "Yggmorgus": {
          "description": "The greater fungal forest surrounding Neverlight Grove, a garden of Zuggtmoy's design where mushrooms grow to titanic proportions — some stalks as thick as castle towers, their caps blotting out the cavern ceiling. The forest floor is a thick carpet of decomposing organic matter that releases sweet, heady odors. Brides of Zuggtmoy — humanoids transformed into fungal zombies — wander the forest, seeking to embrace travelers and draw them into the Demon Queen's fold. The forest is alive with Zuggtmoy's consciousness, and the terrain shifts to disorient intruders. At its heart grows Yggmorgus Prime, a mushroom of colossal proportions whose roots stretch for miles.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sporelight Entry",
            "The Garden of Welcome"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Sovereign Basidia (leader of untainted myconids)",
        "Sovereign Phylo (corrupted elder, Zuggtmoy's thrall)",
        "Zuggtmoy, Demon Queen of Fungi (hidden presence)",
        "Spore servants and bridesmaids (corrupted humanoids)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Zuggtmoy's true goal in Neverlight Grove is not merely to corrupt the myconids but to use them to access Araumycos, the Great Fungus — a single organism that fills caverns the size of the High Forest. By merging with Araumycos, Zuggtmoy would gain influence over a fungal network that spans the entire Northdark, allowing her to spread her corruption to every corner of the domain and challenge even the gods for control of decay itself.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Sloobludop": {
      "name": "Sloobludop",
      "basicInfo": "A kuo-toa village of approximately 500 souls on the eastern shore of the Darklake, Sloobludop is a place of mad faith and fish-eyed fanaticism. The village is built on a series of interconnected piers and platforms extending over the water, its structures of woven seaweed, driftwood, and salvaged ship timbers. The kuo-toa here worship Blibdoolpoolp, the Sea Mother, with a fervor that borders on collective psychosis. Their faith is so powerful that it can manifest minor miracles — and when their high priest declared that a god named Leemooggoogoon would rise from the Darklake to lead them to glory, the kuo-toa believed it with such conviction that something actually began stirring in the depths. The village is ruled by the archpriest Ploopploopeen, whose authority has recently been challenged by his own daughter, the fanatical Bloppblippodd, leading to a schism that threatens to tear the village apart. Sloobludop is a frequent stop for travelers crossing the Darklake, though the kuo-toa are unpredictable hosts — welcoming one moment and sacrificially murderous the next.",
      "x": 24,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Northdark",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Dockworks": {
          "description": "The main access point to Sloobludop, a series of swaying wooden piers extending over the Darklake's dark waters. Kuo-toa skiffs made of stretched hide bob at anchor, and the air smells of fish, brine, and the strange incense the kuo-toa burn to honor the Sea Mother. Visitors are met by whip-priests who demand declarations of faith to Blibdoolpoolp. Those who comply are welcomed; those who hesitate are watched with suspicious, unblinking fish eyes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Shrine",
            "The Sacrificial Pool"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Shrine": {
          "description": "The main temple to Blibdoolpoolp, a structure of fused shells and driftwood that rises from the center of the village on thick stilts. The shrine is dominated by a massive statue of the Sea Mother — a grotesque fusion of woman and lobster — carved from obsidian and encrusted with pearls. The walls are draped in nets filled with the bones of past sacrifices, and the floor is perpetually slick with sacrificial blood. Archpriest Ploopploopeen leads daily services here, his raspy voice reciting prophecies of the coming of Leemooggoogoon.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dockworks",
            "Ploopploopeen's Quarters"
          ]
        },
        "Ploopploopeen's Quarters": {
          "description": "The personal chambers of the archpriest, located behind the Grand Shrine's altar. The room is cluttered with clay tablets covered in mad prophecies, jars of preserved fish organs used for divination, and a collection of carved idols depicting Leemooggoogoon — a creature that resembles Demogorgon in vague, distorted form. Ploopploopeen's son Glooglugogg, a quiet kuo-toa who doubts his father's prophecies, maintains the shrine's cleanliness but secretly fears what his father's faith is summoning from the Darklake's depths.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Shrine"
          ]
        },
        "The Sacrificial Pool": {
          "description": "A netted enclosure at the village's edge where captured creatures — and sometimes captured humanoids — are held for sacrifice. The water is cold and murky, and the prisoners tread water constantly to avoid drowning. The kuo-toa believe that sacrifices fed to the Darklake appease Blibdoolpoolp and hasten the coming of Leemooggoogoon. The pool is guarded by a particularly large monitor, a kuo-toa warrior with a serrated coral spear.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dockworks",
            "Bloppblippodd's Shrine"
          ]
        },
        "Bloppblippodd's Shrine": {
          "description": "A smaller, cruder temple on the village's western edge, erected by Ploopploopeen's daughter after she split from her father's congregation. Bloppblippodd, a fanatical whip-priestess, claims to have received direct visions from the Sea Mother and believes her father's interpretation of Leemooggoogoon is heresy. Her shrine is built from the salvaged hull of a surface ship, and her followers mark themselves with ritual scars. The schism between father and daughter has divided families and threatens to erupt into open violence.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sacrificial Pool"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Archpriest Ploopploopeen (traditionalist leader)",
        "Whip-Priestess Bloppblippodd (schismatic daughter)",
        "Monitors (kuo-toa warrior guards)",
        "Glooglugogg (Ploopploopeen's doubting son)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "The entity the kuo-toa are summoning is not a god but Demogorgon, the Prince of Demons, who was cast into the Darklake during the Rage of Demons. The kuo-toa's collective faith is acting as a beacon, drawing the two-headed demon lord toward the village. When Demogorgon rises from the Darklake, Sloobludop will be the first to fall — and the resulting chaos will ripple across the entire Northdark.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Velkynvelve": {
      "name": "Velkynvelve",
      "basicInfo": "A drow outpost east of the Darklake, Velkynvelve — whose name means 'Unseen Knife' in the drow tongue — is a prison built high in a vast cavern, its structures suspended 100 feet above the rocky floor. The outpost consists of four hollowed-out stalactites connected by rope bridges, walkways, and precarious stairs, with smaller caves in the surrounding walls serving as storage and guard quarters. Velkynvelve is commanded by the cruel priestess Ilvara Mizzrym of House Mizzrym, who uses the outpost as a holding facility for surface-world captives destined for the slave markets of Menzoberranzan. The outpost is a harsh place of constant vigilance: quaggoth slave-soldiers patrol the bridges, giant spiders cling to the stalactites, and the priestess's personal guard of elite drow warriors ensures absolute obedience. A waterfall cascades from the cavern ceiling, feeding a pool on the floor far below and providing the outpost's water supply.",
      "x": 33,
      "y": 14,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Northdark",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Hanging Towers": {
          "description": "Four hollowed-out stalactites suspended from the cavern ceiling, connected by rope bridges and swaying walkways. The largest tower serves as Ilvara's personal quarters and command center, its interior decorated with spider-silk tapestries and the preserved heads of failed escapees. A second tower houses the barracks of the elite drow warriors, twelve soldiers armed with hand crossbows and poisoned short swords. The remaining towers contain storage and the quarters of the junior drow officers — Shoor Vandree, Ilvara's ambitious lieutenant, and Asha Vandree, the underpriestess. The towers sway slightly in the cavern's air currents, and the bridges creak ominously underfoot.",
          "paths": [
            "The Prison Cave",
            "The Guard Caves",
            "The Waterfall"
          ]
        },
        "The Prison Cave": {
          "description": "A natural cave in the cavern wall connected to the hanging towers by a single, heavily guarded rope bridge. The cave serves as the main cell block, its entrance sealed by an iron portcullis and a permanent web spell. Inside, prisoners are shackled to the walls with manacles of cold iron. The cave is perpetually dark, lit only by the faint glow of phosphorescent moss. The air is thick with the smell of unwashed bodies, despair, and the faint ozone tang of the anti-teleportation ward that prevents magical escape. During the Rage of Demons, notable prisoners included surface adventurers from across Faerûn, captured drow rivals, and a myconid sprout named Stool.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hanging Towers"
          ]
        },
        "The Guard Caves": {
          "description": "A cluster of smaller caves in the cavern walls that serve as quarters for the junior drow and the quaggoth slave-soldiers. The quaggoth den is a pit of matted fur, gnawed bones, and the rank smell of unwashed bear-men. The drow quarters are spartan but orderly, each containing a sleeping mat, a weapons rack, and a small shrine to Lolth. Jorlan Duskryn, Ilvara's horribly scarred former lover, maintains a separate chamber here — his face a ruin of scars from Ilvara's displeasure, his loyalty to the priestess now curdled into silent hatred.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hanging Towers",
            "The Spider Hatchery"
          ]
        },
        "The Spider Hatchery": {
          "description": "A warm, humid cave where giant spiders are bred and trained for use as guards, mounts, and messengers. The walls are covered in thick webs, and the floor is littered with the drained husks of creatures fed to the hatchlings. The spiders are controlled by an arachnomancer, a drow female whose lower body has been magically fused with that of a giant spider. She speaks in clicks and chitters, and the spiders obey her with absolute loyalty. The hatchery also houses a phase spider used for scouting missions.",
          "paths": [
            "The Guard Caves"
          ]
        },
        "The Waterfall": {
          "description": "A thin but constant waterfall that cascades from a crack in the cavern ceiling, plunging 100 feet to a rocky pool on the cavern floor. The outpost draws its water from this source via a series of enchanted buckets and pulleys. The pool below is home to a blind cave fish species farmed by the drow for food, and the area around it is patrolled by the quaggoth. The waterfall's mist creates a permanent thin veil across the lower portion of the cavern, reducing visibility and adding to the outpost's eerie atmosphere.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hanging Towers"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Ilvara Mizzrym (commander, priestess of Lolth)",
        "Shoor Vandree (ambitious lieutenant)",
        "Asha Vandree (underpriestess)",
        "Jorlan Duskryn (scarred former lover, potential ally)",
        "Quaggoth slave-soldiers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Jorlan Duskryn is not merely Ilvara's scarred former lover — he is actively plotting her downfall. The injuries she inflicted on him have festered into a cold, patient hatred, and he has been subtly sabotaging the outpost's security for weeks, leaving rope bridges frayed and 'forgetting' to reset magical wards. He will aid any escape attempt by the prisoners if it means Ilvara's humiliation or death, seeing it as his only path to revenge.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Shape of Water": {
      "name": "The Shape of Water",
      "basicInfo": "The largest known aboleth city in the Glimmersea — and indeed in all Toril — the Shape of Water is the seat of the Abolethic Sovereignty and the main residence of the race's leaders. It is a vast, alien metropolis of bizarre architecture located in the deepest trenches of the Glimmersea, its spires and domes constructed from psionically-shaped coral, organic stone, and materials not found on the Material Plane. The city is perpetually shrouded in an aura of psychic static that causes madness in non-aboleths who linger too long. Here, the eldest and most powerful aboleths hold council, plotting the eventual enslavement of all surface-dwelling races while sifting through the perfect racial memories inherited from ancestors who witnessed the creation of the world. The city is served by kuo-toa thralls, skum attendants, and enslaved sea creatures, and is considered the holiest site in aboleth culture.",
      "x": -25,
      "y": -28,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Glimmersea",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Psychic Perimeter": {
          "description": "The outer boundary of the Shape of Water, a region of cold, dark water saturated with the combined psychic presence of the city's aboleth population. Non-aboleths approaching this area experience crushing headaches, auditory hallucinations, and an overwhelming sense of dread that urges them to turn back. The water itself seems to thicken with a viscous, invisible resistance. Enslaved sea serpents and gargantuan anglerfish patrol the perimeter, their minds utterly dominated by the city's masters. The bones of countless ships and adventurers litter the seafloor.",
          "paths": [
            "The Coral Spires",
            "The Slave Pits"
          ]
        },
        "The Coral Spires": {
          "description": "The architectural heart of the city, a forest of twisting coral towers that rise from the trench floor to heights of hundreds of feet. Each spire is a living structure, shaped and maintained by aboleth psionics, and houses a single elder aboleth and its retinue. The towers are connected by organic bridges of sinew and cartilage that pulse with a faint, sickly bioluminescence. The water between the spires is filled with the constant, whispering telepathic chatter of the city's inhabitants — a sound that never ceases.",
          "paths": [
            "The Psychic Perimeter",
            "The Conclave Dome",
            "The Memory Vaults"
          ]
        },
        "The Conclave Dome": {
          "description": "The ruling center of the Abolethic Sovereignty, a massive dome of translucent, opalescent material that sits at the exact center of the city. Within, the Eldest — the most ancient aboleth on Toril, a creature of incalculable age and incomprehensible malevolence — floats in a pool of silvery, nutrient-rich fluid. The Conclave of Ancients, the ruling council of the most powerful aboleths, convenes here in a circle of mind-linked communion. The dome's interior is filled with holographic projections of the aboleths' collective memories, replaying scenes from the dawn of time. Non-aboleths are never permitted entry, and any who attempt are destroyed before they reach the threshold.",
          "paths": [
            "The Coral Spires",
            "The Eldest's Pool"
          ]
        },
        "The Eldest's Pool": {
          "description": "A shimmering pool of silver fluid within the Conclave Dome that serves as both the throne and the life-support system for the Eldest. The fluid is a concentrated form of the aboleth's own mucus, infused with psionic energy and the distilled memories of consumed victims. The Eldest rests here, its vast body coiled in the pool's depths, communicating telepathically with its council. The pool also functions as a scrying device, allowing the Eldest to view any location touched by water across the entire Glimmersea.",
          "paths": [
            "The Conclave Dome"
          ]
        },
        "The Memory Vaults": {
          "description": "A series of chambers carved into the bedrock beneath the Coral Spires, where the aboleths store their racial memories in crystalline matrices. Each crystal contains the perfect, unaltered experiences of an aboleth ancestor — including memories predating the arrival of the gods. The vaults are sacred ground, protected by psionic wards that trigger lethal seizures in any non-aboleth who touches the crystals. The knowledge stored here encompasses lost magic, forgotten histories, and secrets that could shatter the foundations of surface-world civilizations.",
          "paths": [
            "The Coral Spires",
            "The Breeding Pools"
          ]
        },
        "The Breeding Pools": {
          "description": "A network of shallow, warm pools where aboleth tadpoles are raised and where captured humanoids are subjected to the aboleth's transformative mucus. Victims submerged in these pools undergo a horrific metamorphosis: their skin sloughs away, replaced by a transparent, water-breathing membrane, and their minds are wiped clean, replaced by absolute loyalty to the aboleths. The resulting skum serve as laborers and soldiers. Failed transformations are fed to the tadpoles. The pools are tended by elder kuo-toa priests who have been psionically conditioned for centuries.",
          "paths": [
            "The Memory Vaults",
            "The Slave Pits"
          ]
        },
        "The Slave Pits": {
          "description": "A grim holding area where thousands of captured humanoids — kuo-toa, sahuagin, merfolk, sea elves, and surface-dwellers — are kept in psionically-maintained stasis. The slaves are stored in cocoons of solidified mucus, suspended in the water like grotesque ornaments, their life force slowly drained to sustain the aboleth masters. When needed for labor, they are awakened and compelled to work until they collapse, at which point they are returned to stasis or fed to the Breeding Pools. The pits are overseen by a particularly cruel aboleth known as the Taskmaster.",
          "paths": [
            "The Psychic Perimeter",
            "The Breeding Pools"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Eldest (supreme ruler of the Abolethic Sovereignty)",
        "Conclave of Ancients (ruling council of elder aboleths)",
        "Skum attendants",
        "Enslaved kuo-toa legions",
        "The Taskmaster (chief overseer)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Shape of Water is not merely a city but a living organism — the entire structure is slowly extending tendrils of psionically-active coral across the Glimmersea floor, creating a network that will eventually allow the Abolethic Sovereignty to project its mental influence across the entire Underdark. The Eldest has also been secretly communicating with the aboleth city of Xxiphu, the flying obelisk that arrived from Abeir during the Spellplague, negotiating a potential alliance that would unite all aboleths under a single will.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Chauslinn": {
      "name": "Chauslinn",
      "basicInfo": "An ancient drow city on the eastern shores of the Glimmersea, Chauslinn is one of the few settlements in the Lowerdark that maintains an active naval presence on the underground ocean. With over eighty thousand drow and three times that number in slaves, the city is a major power in the eastern Underdark. It is ruled by the Ulath-Taller, the High Priestess of Lolth, whose temple sits on a ridge near the northern edge of the Old City. Twelve noble houses compete for power under the Spider Queen's watchful eyes, their beautiful towers spiraling into the darkness lit by dim magical flames and glowing mosses in shades of blue, green, and purple. The city's navy consists of sleek, spider-silk-hulled vessels crewed by drow marines and slave oarsmen, patrolling the Glimmersea's dark waters for enemies — particularly aboleths and sahuagin. Chauslinn is largely unknown to the surface world, hidden in the lightless depths, and its isolation has allowed it to develop a distinct culture within drow society.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": -10,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Glimmersea",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Docks of Dark Water": {
          "description": "The naval harbor of Chauslinn, a series of stone piers and quays carved into the rocky shore of the Glimmersea. Sleek warships of spider-silk and shadow-wood bob at anchor, their hulls enchanted for silence and speed. Drow marines in dark leather armor drill on the docks, while slave oarsmen — goblins, svirfneblin, and captured sahuagin — are marched to their vessels in chains. The water here is cold and dark, occasionally illuminated by the phosphorescent glow of deep-sea creatures drawn up by the city's waste. The docks are overseen by the Master of Ships, a male drow naval commander who holds unusual influence in the matriarchal society due to the city's reliance on naval power.",
          "paths": [
            "The Old City",
            "The Temple of the Spider Queen",
            "The Naval Academy"
          ]
        },
        "The Old City": {
          "description": "The ancient heart of Chauslinn, a maze of narrow streets, noble compounds, and vertical architecture built into the rocky ridge overlooking the Glimmersea. The buildings are carved from dark stone and decorated with spider motifs, their windows glowing with faerie fire in shades of blue, green, and violet. The twelve noble houses maintain their estates here, each a self-contained fortress of towers and walls. The streets are crowded with drow commoners, merchants, and slaves, their movement controlled by priestesses and patrols of house soldiers. The air is thick with incense, intrigue, and the ever-present threat of assassination.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks of Dark Water",
            "The Temple of the Spider Queen",
            "The Council of Twelve",
            "The Bazaar of Dark Tides"
          ]
        },
        "The Temple of the Spider Queen": {
          "description": "The grand temple of Lolth, perched on a ridge near the northern edge of the Old City. The temple is a towering ziggurat of obsidian and spider silk, its summit crowned with a massive statue of the Spider Queen rendered in black adamantine. The High Priestess Ulath-Taller rules the city from this temple, her word absolute and her cruelty legendary. The temple's interior is a labyrinth of sacrificial chambers, arachnid breeding pits, and meditation halls where priestesses commune with Lolth through ritualized pain. The walls are covered in frescoes depicting drow history and the glory of the Spider Queen. A permanent cloud of incense and the sound of chanting priestesses saturates the air.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks of Dark Water",
            "The Old City",
            "The Spider Pits"
          ]
        },
        "The Council of Twelve": {
          "description": "The governing chamber of the noble houses, located in a domed structure of black marble at the center of the Old City. The Matron Mothers of the twelve noble houses convene here to debate policy, conduct trials, and plot against one another. The chamber is circular, with twelve thrones arranged around a central pit that opens directly into a spider-filled darkness — a reminder that displeasing Lolth has immediate consequences. Duels between houses are permitted under strict ritual conditions, and the floor is permanently stained with the blood of fallen champions.",
          "paths": [
            "The Old City"
          ]
        },
        "The Bazaar of Dark Tides": {
          "description": "A sprawling marketplace carved into a side cavern off the Old City, where drow merchants sell goods from across the Underdark and the Glimmersea. Stalls offer deep-sea pearls, aboleth ichor, sahuagin weapons, and exotic spell components harvested from the lightless depths. The bazaar is also a hub for information, with whisper-brokers selling secrets in sound-proofed alcoves. Non-drow traders are tolerated but watched closely, and surface-world goods command astronomical prices. The market is patrolled by temple guards who enforce the city's laws with casual brutality.",
          "paths": [
            "The Old City",
            "The Slave Pens"
          ]
        },
        "The Slave Pens": {
          "description": "A vast network of holding cells carved into the bedrock beneath the Old City, housing the city's enormous slave population. The pens are divided by species and purpose: goblins for manual labor, svirfneblin for mining, captured sahuagin for the galleys, and surface elves for sacrifice. Conditions are brutal, and escape attempts are punished by being fed to the Spider Pits. The pens are overseen by a caste of drow slavemasters who take grim pleasure in their work. A resistance network among the goblin slaves maintains a hidden shrine to Maglubiyet, praying daily for liberation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bazaar of Dark Tides",
            "The Spider Pits"
          ]
        },
        "The Spider Pits": {
          "description": "Deep breeding caverns beneath the Temple of the Spider Queen where monstrous spiders are raised for mounts, guardians, and sacrifices. The pits descend through multiple levels, each housing spiders of increasing size and lethality. The lowest pit contains the city's Broodmother — a spider of colossal proportions, fed on a steady diet of living slaves. The arachnomancers who tend the pits are a secretive order, their bodies scarred by ritual venom injections, their speech a clicking, chittering language they believe is the voice of Lolth herself.",
          "paths": [
            "The Temple of the Spider Queen",
            "The Slave Pens"
          ]
        },
        "The Naval Academy": {
          "description": "A walled compound near the Docks of Dark Water where the city's naval officers are trained. The academy is one of the few institutions in Chauslinn where males can achieve genuine authority, as the city's survival depends on its fleet. Young drow males learn navigation, naval tactics, deep-sea combat, and the handling of the various sea creatures used by the navy. The academy's commander, a veteran captain named Vorn Xyr'zath, is one of the most respected males in the city and has been known to successfully argue with priestesses on matters of naval strategy — a rare privilege in drow society.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks of Dark Water",
            "The Glimmersea Patrol Route"
          ]
        },
        "The Glimmersea Patrol Route": {
          "description": "A designated stretch of the Glimmersea's dark waters where Chauslinn's navy conducts regular patrols against aboleth incursions and sahuagin raiders. The route is marked by enchanted buoys that glow with dim light visible only to drow eyes. Sunken fortresses — hollowed-out stone towers resting on the seafloor — serve as way stations for patrol vessels, each garrisoned by a squad of marines and a cleric of Lolth. The waters are dangerous, prowled by the aboleth's servants and the occasional sea serpent, and patrol duty is considered both an honor and a likely death sentence.",
          "paths": [
            "The Naval Academy"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Ulath-Taller (High Priestess of Lolth)",
        "Council of Twelve Matron Mothers",
        "Naval Academy (male-dominated military)",
        "Goblin slave resistance",
        "Arachnomancers of the Spider Pits"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The High Priestess Ulath-Taller has received a troubling vision from Lolth: a massive aboleth army is massing in a trench not far from Chauslinn's patrol routes, preparing for an assault on the city. However, the vision also showed that the aboleths are being driven toward Chauslinn by a greater threat — a kraken that has recently claimed territory in the deepest reaches of the Glimmersea. The kraken is an agent of the Kraken Society, and its presence in the Glimmersea signals that the organization is extending its reach into the Underdark.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Holy Mother Cauldron": {
      "name": "Holy Mother Cauldron",
      "basicInfo": "A sacred shrine and monastery complex in the Lower Glimmersea, the Holy Mother Cauldron is the most important pilgrimage site for worshippers of Blibdoolpoolp in the entire Underdark. The Cauldron is located in a perilous section of the Glimmersea characterized by strong tides, fierce currents, and partially submerged caverns accessible only by underwater travel or secret dry routes descending from the Middledark. Each devout kuo-toa is obligated to visit this shrine at least once in their lifetime on a pilgrimage called 'going to meet the goddess' — a journey so dangerous that many of the elderly wait until they are near death to undertake it, viewing the trip as a final devotion from which they do not intend to return. The shrine shelters dozens of kuo-toa clerics, monks, and pilgrims at any given time. It is governed by an elderly but powerful cleric named Slowoopil and defended by Maumogga, a gargantuan kuo-toa leviathan of massive size who serves as the shrine's sacred champion.",
      "x": -13,
      "y": 22,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Glimmersea",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Tidal Approach": {
          "description": "The submerged entrance to the Holy Mother Cauldron, accessible only by swimming through a series of water-filled tunnels that pulse with strong, unpredictable currents. The walls are encrusted with phosphorescent algae that cast a faint, holy glow. Travelers must navigate by following the carved reliefs of Blibdoolpoolp — lobster-clawed female figures pointing the way. Those who attempt to use magical means of travel find themselves disoriented by ancient wards woven into the stone. The final approach is guarded by a pair of giant sea clams that snap shut on any who do not bear the Sea Mother's blessing.",
          "paths": [
            "The Monastery of the Sea Mother",
            "The Offering Grotto"
          ]
        },
        "The Monastery of the Sea Mother": {
          "description": "The main structure of the Holy Mother Cauldron, a temple and monastery built from fused coral, salvaged stone, and the shells of enormous sea creatures. The monastery's chambers are carved into a massive stalactite that descends into the Glimmersea's waters. The walls are draped in nets filled with the bones of pilgrims who completed their final journey here, and the air is thick with the smell of brine and incense. The monastery houses several dozen kuo-toa clerics and monks who chant hymns to Blibdoolpoolp in an endless, bubbling litany. Guest chambers for pilgrims are simple stone cells with pools of sacred seawater for meditation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tidal Approach",
            "The High Altar",
            "The Leviathan's Pool"
          ]
        },
        "The High Altar": {
          "description": "The central shrine of the monastery, located in a high-vaulted chamber where water from the Glimmersea cascades down the walls in a perpetual curtain. The altar is an enormous, perfectly preserved giant lobster claw, said to have been shed by Blibdoolpoolp herself in the dawn of time. The claw is encrusted with pearls and glows with a faint, divine radiance. Pilgrims approach the altar on their bellies, leaving offerings of fish, shells, and carved coral. Cleric Slowoopil conducts daily services here, his raspy voice recounting the legends of the Sea Mother's creation of the kuo-toa race.",
          "paths": [
            "The Monastery of the Sea Mother"
          ]
        },
        "The Leviathan's Pool": {
          "description": "A massive, deep pool at the base of the monastery's stalactite, where Maumogga, the gargantuan kuo-toa leviathan, resides. The creature is a sacred champion of Blibdoolpoolp, a unique kuo-toa of prodigious size and strength, its body covered in ritual scars and its eyes burning with fanatical devotion. Maumogga serves as both the shrine's primary defense and the enforcer of the high cleric's will. It speaks in a voice like grinding coral and consumes any who desecrate the shrine. Pilgrims may offer it food, and receiving its blessing — a gentle touch of its massive claw — is considered a sign of the Sea Mother's favor.",
          "paths": [
            "The Monastery of the Sea Mother"
          ]
        },
        "The Offering Grotto": {
          "description": "A small, flooded cave off the Tidal Approach where pilgrims leave offerings to Blibdoolpoolp before ascending to the monastery. The grotto is filled with centuries of accumulated tribute: coral statues, carved driftwood, pearl necklaces, and the bones of sacrificial victims. The water here is warmer than the surrounding Glimmersea, heated by geothermal vents, and is believed to have healing properties. The desperate and the ill sometimes linger here for days, praying for the Sea Mother's intervention. A school of bioluminescent fish circles the grotto in an endless, hypnotic dance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tidal Approach"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Slowoopil (elderly high cleric of Blibdoolpoolp)",
        "Maumogga (gargantuan kuo-toa leviathan)",
        "Monastic order of the Sea Mother",
        "Pilgrim devotees (various kuo-toa tribes)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The giant lobster claw on the High Altar is not merely a relic — it is a living fragment of Blibdoolpoolp's avatar, still connected to the goddess across the planes. Slowoopil has discovered that by bathing the claw in the blood of a surface-world priest of Umberlee, he can open a temporary gate to Blibdoolpoolp's divine realm. He has been secretly capturing Umberlee-worshipping sailors from vessels that cross the Sea of Fallen Stars above, draining their blood to empower this ritual. His ultimate goal is to summon Blibdoolpoolp permanently into the Glimmersea and establish the Sea Mother as the undisputed ruler of the underground ocean.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Blessed Seahaven": {
      "name": "Blessed Seahaven",
      "basicInfo": "A small kuo-toa thorp of approximately forty inhabitants located on the Glimmersea, beneath the coast of Sembia in the lower Deep Wastes. Blessed Seahaven is a quiet, insular settlement of stilted huts and driftwood docks built over the dark water. The thorp is centered around a modest temple to Blibdoolpoolp, attended by the settlement's ruler — a cleric named Pliil Seachild. A small militia of sixteen adult kuo-toa defends the settlement against sahuagin raiders, deep-water predators, and the occasional aboleth thrall. Despite its small size, Blessed Seahaven is a vital resupply point for the few brave sailors who traverse the Glimmersea, offering fresh water, dried fish, and a safe harbor from the dangers of the dark ocean.",
      "x": -26,
      "y": 1,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Glimmersea",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Dockworks": {
          "description": "A series of rickety wooden piers extending over the Glimmersea's dark waters, built from salvaged surface ship timbers and woven seaweed. Kuo-toa fishing skiffs made of stretched hide bob at anchor, and nets filled with bioluminescent fish are piled on the planks. The docks serve as the entrance to the thorp, and visitors are met by wary militia guards who demand proof of peaceful intent. A single stone column at the dock's end is carved with warnings in kuo-toan script, promising a watery grave to those who harm the settlement.",
          "paths": [
            "The Temple of the Sea Mother",
            "The Fisher's Huts"
          ]
        },
        "The Temple of the Sea Mother": {
          "description": "The heart of Blessed Seahaven, a small but lovingly maintained temple to Blibdoolpoolp. The temple is built from fused shells, driftwood, and the bones of a whale-like creature that beached itself on the thorp's shores centuries ago. Inside, a modest altar carved from obsidian holds a pearl the size of a fist — the settlement's most prized possession, believed to have been given by the Sea Mother herself to the thorp's founder. Cleric Pliil Seachild leads daily prayers here, her voice a melodic gurgle, and the temple's walls are decorated with murals depicting the history of Blessed Seahaven and the glory of Blibdoolpoolp.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dockworks",
            "The Pilgrim's Rest"
          ]
        },
        "The Pilgrim's Rest": {
          "description": "A small guesthouse adjoining the temple, where the few travelers who visit Blessed Seahaven are offered shelter. The rest is spartan but comfortable by kuo-toa standards — sleeping mats of woven seaweed, stone basins of fresh (if slightly fishy) water, and walls covered in carvings of the Sea Mother's blessings. Pliil Seachild greets each guest personally, asking for news of the wider Underdark and the surface world above. In exchange for hospitality, visitors are expected to offer a small tribute to the temple.",
          "paths": [
            "The Temple of the Sea Mother"
          ]
        },
        "The Fisher's Huts": {
          "description": "The residential quarter of Blessed Seahaven, a cluster of stilted huts built over the shallows. Each hut houses a kuo-toa family, and the structures are connected by narrow wooden walkways that creak and sway with the water's movement. The kuo-toa here are fisherfolk and seaweed farmers, their days spent tending nets and drying their catch on racks. The huts glow with a faint, warm light from phosphorescent moss cultivated in clay pots. The thorp's militia of sixteen fighters drills on a nearby sandbar when not occupied with their fishing duties.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dockworks"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Pliil Seachild (cleric and ruler)",
        "Kuo-toa militia (16 adult defenders)",
        "Fishing families of the thorp"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "The pearl in the temple's altar is not merely decorative — it is a scrying focus that allows the aboleths of the Shape of Water to observe Blessed Seahaven through Pliil Seachild's own eyes. The cleric is an unwitting thrall, her mind subtly conditioned during a pilgrimage to the Holy Mother Cauldron years ago. She believes she serves Blibdoolpoolp, but the visions she receives and the miracles she performs are actually granted by the aboleths, who use the thorp as an early warning outpost against incursions from the surface world.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Quartzel": {
      "name": "Quartzel",
      "basicInfo": "A spiraled city built into and around a colossal stalactite rising from the Glimmersea's surface, Quartzel is a rare neutral settlement where sahuagin, kuo-toa, and other aquatic Underdark races coexist in an uneasy truce. The city is a marvel of vertical architecture, with streets and buildings winding around the stalactite in a slow spiral from the waterline to the cavern ceiling, connected by bridges, ladders, and pulleys. Quartzel's most famous institution is the Library of the Hunt, a repository of knowledge dedicated to the study of monsters — their strengths, weaknesses, and methods of killing. The library is jointly operated by sahuagin and kuo-toa librarians who set aside their species' historic enmity in the name of knowledge. Quartzel is a destination for hunters, mercenaries, and adventurers seeking information on their quarry, though the information does not come cheaply.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 13,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Glimmersea",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Watergate": {
          "description": "The entrance to Quartzel at the waterline, a series of wide stone arches and docks built into the base of the massive stalactite. Boats of all kinds — sahuagin war-canoes, kuo-toa hide-skiffs, and the occasional drow vessel — tie up at the piers. The Watergate is perpetually busy with the comings and goings of hunters, traders, and information-seekers. Stone stairs carved into the stalactite spiral upward into the city. Guards of both sahuagin and kuo-toa monitor the gate, their mutual suspicion palpable but contained by the city's strict neutrality laws.",
          "paths": [
            "The Spiral Streets",
            "The Library of the Hunt"
          ]
        },
        "The Spiral Streets": {
          "description": "The main thoroughfare of Quartzel, a winding street that circles the stalactite from the Watergate to the upper reaches. The street is lined with shops, taverns, and residences carved into the living stone. Sahuagin weapon-smiths hawk serrated coral blades next to kuo-toa fishmongers and surface-world exiles selling their crafts. The street is narrow in places, forcing travelers to navigate cautiously, and the constant up-and-down traffic creates a chaotic but functional flow. The air is filled with the smell of brine, cooking fish, and the chittering arguments of sahuagin and kuo-toa haggling over prices.",
          "paths": [
            "The Watergate",
            "The Library of the Hunt",
            "The Hunter's Rest Tavern"
          ]
        },
        "The Library of the Hunt": {
          "description": "A wide, squat building of dark stone that clings to the side of the stalactite halfway up the spiral. The library is the greatest repository of monster-hunting knowledge in the Underdark, its shelves organized by species with a decided focus on skills, abilities, weaknesses, and methods of killing. There are treatises on mind flayer control and how to break it, fold-out diagrams of kindori anatomy with highlighted weak spots, and a whole wing dedicated to mortal sentient races — how to neutralize a dragonborn's breath weapon, how to slow a tabaxi, and guides to impersonating a human merchant. The head librarian is a kuo-toa named Glurpla, dismissive and rude to all who enter but genuinely helpful in guiding research. The library is dominated by a large stained-glass window looking out over the Glimmersea, depicting scenes of unimaginable carnage. Payment is accepted in coin, information, or favors — often hunts for new specimens.",
          "paths": [
            "The Watergate",
            "The Spiral Streets",
            "The Dissection Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Dissection Hall": {
          "description": "A grim chamber attached to the Library of the Hunt where fresh specimens are brought for study. Corpses of monsters — from owlbears to umber hulks to more exotic creatures — are dissected on stone slabs under the careful observation of librarian-scribes. The hall smells of preservative ichor and old blood, and the walls are lined with jars containing preserved organs and body parts. Hunters are encouraged to bring their kills here, and in exchange for allowing the librarians to document the dissection, they may receive free access to the library's resources.",
          "paths": [
            "The Library of the Hunt"
          ]
        },
        "The Hunter's Rest Tavern": {
          "description": "A rough establishment on the upper Spiral Streets, the Hunter's Rest is the primary watering hole for Quartzel's visiting hunters and mercenaries. The tavern serves a fermented drink made from Glimmersea algae and roasted deep-sea fish. The walls are covered in trophies — the heads, claws, and teeth of notable kills, each labeled with the hunter's name and the date of the hunt. Bounties are posted on a large board near the entrance, offering rewards for the slaying of specific monsters plaguing Underdark communities. Fights are common but rarely fatal, as the city's guards intervene quickly.",
          "paths": [
            "The Spiral Streets"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Glurpla (head librarian, kuo-toa)",
        "Sahuagin-Kuo-toa Librarian Council",
        "Hunters' Guild of Quartzel",
        "City neutrality enforcers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Glurpla the head librarian is not merely a kuo-toa — he is a polymorphed adult bronze dragon who assumed this form centuries ago to pursue his obsession with cataloguing the weaknesses of every living creature. His true nature is known only to the most senior librarians, and he uses his draconic powers to protect the library from threats that would destroy it. His ultimate goal is to compile a complete bestiary of Toril's monsters, a work he believes will take another thousand years to finish.",
      "known": true
    },
