    "Bronze Citadel": {
      "name": "Bronze Citadel",
      "basicInfo": "The mobile flying fortress of Archduchess Zariel, a brutal construct of black iron and infernal bronze that drifts across the Avernian skies. It serves as the primary command center for the Blood War legions, housing Zariel's throne room, war rooms, and the soul-forges where captured demons and failed devils are reforged into new soldiers. The Citadel is surrounded by a permanent cloud of choking smoke and ash, and its lower decks are patrolled by erinyes and horned devils. Mortals are rarely admitted, and those who enter must have a writ signed in Zariel's own blood.",
      "x": 9,
      "y": 28,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Avernus — West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Landing Platforms": {
          "description": "A series of wide, soot-stained iron platforms jutting from the Citadel's underbelly, where infernal war machines and flying devils land and depart. The air reeks of brimstone and hot metal, and the constant roar of engines and screams of the damned echo from below. Guards inspect all arrivals, demanding the Archduchess's seal. The platforms are perilous – one misstep sends a creature tumbling into the Blood War battlefields far below.",
          "paths": [
            "Throne of the Archduchess",
            "Soul-Forges"
          ]
        },
        "Throne of the Archduchess": {
          "description": "Zariel's seat of power, a vast chamber at the Citadel's heart. The throne itself is forged from the melted armor of defeated demon lords, and a permanent pillar of hellfire illuminates the room. Zariel, a fallen angel with skin of cracked bronze and burning eyes, sits here receiving reports from her generals. The walls are hung with battle standards captured in the Blood War, and a great scrying pool shows the current front lines. To approach the throne without permission is to be immolated on the spot.",
          "paths": [
            "Landing Platforms",
            "War Council Chamber",
            "Soul-Forges"
          ]
        },
        "War Council Chamber": {
          "description": "A circular room adjacent to the throne room, dominated by a massive illusory map of Avernus that updates in real-time. Zariel's generals – pit fiend commanders, erinyes captains, and the occasional allied daemon – plan offensives here. The table is scarred by countless heated arguments, and the walls are lined with the skulls of demons who dared to attack the Citadel. A subsidiary portal in this chamber can open directly to the River Styx for rapid troop deployment.",
          "paths": [
            "Throne of the Archduchess",
            "Styx Gate"
          ]
        },
        "Soul-Forges": {
          "description": "The industrial heart of the Citadel, where lemures and captured demons are melted down and reforged into higher-ranking devils. The air is thick with heat, the clang of hammers, and the shrieks of reforming souls. Overseen by a pit fiend forgemaster, the forges are a maze of molten metal channels and anvils. Mortal souls who prove exceptionally useful to Zariel are sometimes brought here to be transformed into infernal warriors, a process that destroys their mortal identity but elevates them to devil status.",
          "paths": [
            "Landing Platforms",
            "Throne of the Archduchess",
            "Prison Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Prison Pens": {
          "description": "A grim holding area where captured demons, traitorous devils, and unfortunate mortals await processing into the Soul-Forges. The pens are cages of barbed iron suspended over a lake of liquid fire. The heat is unbearable, and the constant screaming makes rest impossible. A few inmates have carved messages into the bars – pleas to gods who cannot hear them here.",
          "paths": [
            "Soul-Forges"
          ]
        },
        "Styx Gate": {
          "description": "A heavily guarded portal chamber that opens directly onto a dock on the River Styx. This allows the Citadel to rapidly move troops to any front in the Blood War. The gate is flanked by two immense statues of Zariel, their eyes burning with hellfire. Only Zariel or her direct adjutants may activate the portal, and the Styx-side dock is patrolled by merrenoloth ferrymen who have been bound to Zariel's service.",
          "paths": [
            "War Council Chamber"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Zariel (Archduchess of Avernus)",
        "Pit Fiend Commanders",
        "Erinyes Captains",
        "Soul-Forgemaster"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Zariel's former celestial sword, the Sword of Zariel, was recovered by adventurers from her fallen angelic form. She desperately seeks it, for it contains a fragment of her original grace that could be used to redeem or destroy her. Her spies have traced it to Elturel, and she has dispatched a squadron of black-barded nightmare riders to retrieve it from the city's Hellriders.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Elturel": {
      "name": "Elturel",
      "basicInfo": "The City of the Companion, dragged bodily into Avernus when its patron angel, Zariel, pulled it through the earth as a sacrifice to fuel her infernal war. The city now rests on the banks of the River Styx, its buildings shattered, streets split by chasms, and its populace enslaved by devils. The Companion – a blazing sphere that once served as a holy beacon – has been transformed into a soul-sucking anchor. Despite the devastation, a desperate resistance of surviving Hellriders and citizens holds out in the Grand Cemetery, while Zariel's forces use the city as a staging ground and a source of mortal souls.",
      "x": 2,
      "y": 5,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Avernus — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Crater's Edge": {
          "description": "The perimeter of the great crater that formed when Elturel crashed into Avernus. Chunks of the city's outer walls lie scattered in the desolate plains. The edge is patrolled by bearded devils and hell hounds who intercept any survivors trying to flee. A fallen siege tower has been repurposed as a watchpost, its ballista now firing infernal bolts. From here, the devastation of the city is fully visible – a smoking ruin under a blood-red sky.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Streets",
            "River Styx Docks"
          ]
        },
        "The Shattered Streets": {
          "description": "The main thoroughfares of Elturel, now a maze of collapsed buildings, open chasms, and scattered barricades. Civilians hide in cellars, while devil patrols march through the rubble looking for escaped souls. The streets are lit by the hellish glow of the Companion, and the air is thick with smoke, screaming, and the bleating of infernal war horns. A few intact taverns and shops have been taken over by devilish quartermasters or by bands of desperate survivors.",
          "paths": [
            "The Crater's Edge",
            "The Grand Cemetery",
            "The Companion"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Cemetery": {
          "description": "The last stronghold of the Elturgard resistance, a walled burial ground where the Hellriders and surviving citizens have fortified themselves against Zariel's forces. Crypts have been converted into barracks and supply stores, and the central mausoleum serves as a command center. The spirits of the honored dead linger here, offering cryptic advice and sometimes physical aid to the living. The cemetery is constantly under siege, but its hallowed ground repels weaker devils.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Streets",
            "Hellrider Headquarters"
          ]
        },
        "Hellrider Headquarters": {
          "description": "The main mausoleum of the cemetery, now serving as the headquarters of the Hellrider resistance. Maps of the city, marked with devil positions and survivor enclaves, cover the stone walls. The commander, a scarred paladin named Commander Ravengard, coordinates raids and rescue missions. A hidden basement contains the city's remaining holy relics, including a handful of angelic weapons that can permanently slay devils. The atmosphere is grim but determined.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Cemetery",
            "Relic Vault"
          ]
        },
        "Relic Vault": {
          "description": "A hidden chamber beneath the Hellrider headquarters, containing what remains of Elturel's sacred armory. Swords blessed by Torm, shields consecrated by Helm, and a single vial of water from the holy spring of the Companion (before its corruption) are stored here. The chamber is warded against devil entry, but the wards are weakening. The vault's most powerful artifact – a fragment of Zariel's celestial sword – hums softly, awaiting a wielder pure enough to claim it.",
          "paths": [
            "Hellrider Headquarters"
          ]
        },
        "The Companion": {
          "description": "The blazing sphere at the heart of Elturel's crater, once the symbol of the city's divine protection, now a soul-siphoning anchor that channels mortal essence directly to Zariel. The Companion floats above a chasm of liquid hellfire, accessible only by a shattered staircase of obsidian. The air here is blisteringly hot, and the light is so intense that unprotected eyes are seared blind. At the base of the sphere, an iron platform holds a control altar manned by a pit fiend named Gargauth, who manages the soul-harvesting process.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Streets",
            "Altar of Harvesting"
          ]
        },
        "Altar of Harvesting": {
          "description": "The iron platform directly beneath the Companion, where the pit fiend Gargauth oversees the extraction of mortal souls. Cages of captured citizens are levitated into the Companion's rays, their life force drained and channeled into infernal war machines. The altar is surrounded by a choir of chained erinyes whose song amplifies the Companion's draining power. Destroying the altar would cripple Zariel's soul supply, but Gargauth is one of the most powerful devils in Avernus, and he does not leave his post.",
          "paths": [
            "The Companion"
          ]
        },
        "River Styx Docks": {
          "description": "A new set of docks hastily constructed on the Styx after Elturel's arrival, where ferrymen transport souls, supplies, and troops to other parts of Avernus. The water is black and sluggish, and merely touching it can erase memory. A squadron of merrenoloths operates the ferry service under contract to Zariel, charging exorbitant prices in soul coins. The docks are a chaotic neutral ground where devils, scavengers, and desperate souls mingle.",
          "paths": [
            "The Crater's Edge"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Hellrider Resistance (Commander Ravengard)",
        "Zariel's Occupation Force",
        "Gargauth (Pit Fiend overseer)",
        "Merrenoloth Ferrymen"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Companion's energy is not merely siphoning souls – it is slowly tearing a permanent rift between Avernus and the Material Plane. If Zariel's plan succeeds, Elturel will become an infernal fortress that can project devil armies directly into Faerûn, bypassing the need for cultist summoning rituals. The Hellriders' relic vault holds the key to reversing this: the fragment of Zariel's sword, when reunited with the blade, can sever the connection.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Tiamat's Lair": {
      "name": "Tiamat's Lair",
      "basicInfo": "A colossal volcanic caldera on the edge of Avernus that houses the lair of Tiamat, the Chromatic Dragon Queen. The caldera is filled with five great chambers, each attuned to the element of one of Tiamat's heads. From here, Tiamat broods over her hoard of treasures from across the planes and oversees her draconic legions in the Blood War. The lair is guarded by abishai – draconic devils loyal to the Queen – and a legion of red, blue, green, black, and white dragons who serve as her elite enforcers. The entrance is a massive, tooth-lined cavern mouth that exhales sulfurous steam, and the lair itself is linked directly to Dis, the second layer of Hell.",
      "x": 16,
      "y": 23,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Avernus — Far East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Dragon's Maw": {
          "description": "The entrance to Tiamat's Lair, a cavern mouth shaped like a dragon's open maw, fifty feet high and lined with jagged obsidian teeth. The air is hot and humid, reeking of brimstone and dragon musk. Abishai guards (white, black, green, blue, and red) patrol the entrance, demanding tribute from all who wish to enter. The ground is littered with the bones of those who failed to satisfy the guardians. A permanent roar echoes from deep within the caldera.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fivefold Chamber",
            "The Hoard of the Dragon Queen"
          ]
        },
        "The Fivefold Chamber": {
          "description": "The central cavern of the lair, a vast space where five elemental zones converge: a frozen glacier, a acidic swamp, a electrified crystal forest, a volcanic lake of fire, and a poison-clouded abyss. Tiamat's five heads each rest within their respective zone, but her colossal body is capable of moving through all five simultaneously. The floor is a mosaic of battle trophies from conquered worlds, and the ceiling is lost in a swirling vortex of chromatic energies. The chamber is where Tiamat holds court, receiving emissaries from the other archdevils and dispatching her draconic legions.",
          "paths": [
            "Dragon's Maw",
            "The Hoard of the Dragon Queen",
            "Portal to Dis"
          ]
        },
        "The Hoard of the Dragon Queen": {
          "description": "A massive side cavern filled with Tiamat's legendary treasure – mountains of gold, gems the size of houses, magic items from across the multiverse, and the collected wealth of a hundred fallen civilizations. The hoard is patrolled by animated dragon skeletons and the ghosts of dead chromatic dragons. The air is thick with the psychic whispers of greedy spirits, urging any who enter to steal – a fatal temptation. At the center of the hoard rests the Dragon Queen's greatest treasure: the Orb of Dragonkind, its power capable of enslaving all dragonkind.",
          "paths": [
            "Dragon's Maw",
            "The Fivefold Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "Abishai Barracks": {
          "description": "A network of side tunnels where the abishai – draconic devils created from the souls of fallen chromatic dragons – dwell and train. The barracks are divided by color, each area reflecting the element of its inhabitants. The white abishai tunnel is frozen and silent, the green is humid and choked with spores, the black is flooded with acidic pools, the blue is crackling with electricity, and the red is a sweltering forge. A central training arena sees daily duels between the abishai, overseen by a pit fiend drillmaster.",
          "paths": [
            "Dragon's Maw"
          ]
        },
        "Portal to Dis": {
          "description": "A permanent, massive portal at the far end of the Fivefold Chamber that connects directly to the Iron City of Dis, the second layer of Hell. The portal is a ring of dragon bones and burning iron, flanked by two colossal statues of Tiamat. Only the Dragon Queen and her designated emissaries may pass through, and the portal is warded with a spell that incinerates any non-dragon who attempts transit. This gate is the primary route for Tiamat's influence into the deeper Hells.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fivefold Chamber"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Tiamat, the Chromatic Dragon Queen",
        "Abishai legions",
        "Chromatic dragon servitors",
        "Pit fiend drillmaster"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Tiamat's position in Avernus is not voluntary. Asmodeus bound her here as a warden and a deterrent against demon incursions. The Orb of Dragonkind in her hoard is actually a fragment of her divine essence, and if it were destroyed, her five heads would turn against one another, shattering her into five independent dragon gods. She guards it not only for its power, but out of fear for her own existence.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Wandering Emporium": {
      "name": "Wandering Emporium",
      "basicInfo": "A mobile infernal bazaar that roams the plains of Avernus on the back of a gargantuan enslaved demon named Cragg. The Emporium is a collection of tents, stalls, and wagons run by a coven of night hags and their infernal associates. Here, mercenaries, scavengers, and desperate souls can purchase weapons, armor, healing, and information. The Emporium also features a tavern called the Festering Hellfire, a fighting pit, and a bathhouse that offers rare respite from Avernus's grime. The hags, led by Mahadi, maintain strict neutrality – violence against customers is forbidden, and all debts are collected in soul coins.",
      "x": 4,
      "y": 9,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Avernus — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Cragg's Landing": {
          "description": "A rope ladder and winch system that allows visitors to climb up onto the back of Cragg, the enslaved demon. The demon's hide is scarred and steaming, and its occasional groans shake the entire structure. The entrance is guarded by two nycaloth bouncers who demand a soul coin toll. The view from the top shows the Emporium's sprawling collection of colorful tents, as well as the desolate Avernian horizon.",
          "paths": [
            "Mahadi's Pavilion",
            "The Festering Hellfire Tavern"
          ]
        },
        "Mahadi's Pavilion": {
          "description": "The headquarters of the Emporium's owner, a powerful rakshasa named Mahadi. The pavilion is a silk tent of opulent design, filled with comfortable cushions, hookah pipes, and the scent of exotic incense. Mahadi sits cross-legged on a divan, offering deals, information, and rare magical items in exchange for soul coins or favors. A permanent scrying pool allows him to watch events across Avernus. Behind the pavilion, a secret vault holds his most valuable wares – souls of particularly powerful mortals, captured in crystal vials.",
          "paths": [
            "Cragg's Landing",
            "Infernal Armory"
          ]
        },
        "The Festering Hellfire Tavern": {
          "description": "A smoky tavern tent where travelers can drink, gamble, and swap rumors. The ale is brewed from fermented devilweed, and the food – 'hellfire chili' – is famously spicy enough to knock out a dwarf. The tavern is run by a chain-smoking arcanaloth named V'juss, who trades stories for shots of rotgut. A fighting pit in the back allows patrons to wager on battles between captured demons, devils, and volunteer adventurers. The walls are covered in graffiti left by previous patrons.",
          "paths": [
            "Cragg's Landing",
            "Fighting Pit"
          ]
        },
        "Fighting Pit": {
          "description": "A circular pit behind the tavern, surrounded by betting counters and cheering patrons. Here, anything from lemures to chasme demons are pitted against each other – or against volunteer gladiators – for the entertainment of the crowd. The pit is overseen by a chain devil named Krikor, who ensures fairness (by infernal standards). Winners receive soul coins and a measure of infernal favor. The sand is permanently stained red.",
          "paths": [
            "The Festering Hellfire Tavern"
          ]
        },
        "Infernal Armory": {
          "description": "A stall run by a duergar ex-patriate and his merregon assistant, selling weapons and armor salvaged from Blood War battlefields. The armory offers everything from standard infernal iron longswords to demon-plate armor and siege engines. Prices are negotiable but always involve soul coins. The duergar, Brugg, also buys equipment from adventurers at a fraction of its value. A back room contains experimental war machines – half-demon constructs and captured demonic weapons that may be cursed.",
          "paths": [
            "Mahadi's Pavilion"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Mahadi (Rakshasa proprietor)",
        "Night hag coven (herbalists and soul-brokers)",
        "V'juss (Arcanaloth tavernkeeper)",
        "Krikor (Chain devil pit boss)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "Mahadi's true identity is not merely a rakshasa. He is an agent of Asmodeus, tasked with monitoring the Blood War and reporting directly to the Lord of the Nine. Every transaction in the Emporium feeds information to Nessus, and Mahadi's scrying pool can be activated by Asmodeus at will. The rakshasa's friendly demeanor masks a ruthless intelligence that has already identified and catalogued every potential threat to infernal power in Avernus.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Bel's Forge": {
      "name": "Bel's Forge",
      "basicInfo": "Hidden deep within a labyrinth of volcanic tunnels, Bel's Forge is the secret lair of the former Archduke of Avernus, the pit fiend Bel. After being deposed by Zariel, Bel retreated to this underground foundry, where he continues to forge weapons and war machines for the Blood War – including experimental super-weapons that Zariel officially does not sanction. The Forge is a sprawling complex of smithies, alchemical labs, and demonic prisons, all powered by an enslaved fire primordial. Bel is a master strategist and a bitter rival to Zariel, and he secretly aids anyone who might undermine her rule – for a price.",
      "x": 6,
      "y": 13,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Avernus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Magma Tunnels": {
          "description": "The approach to Bel's Forge, a winding network of lava tubes and volcanic fissures. The heat is intense, and the walls glow orange. Ember mephits and magma sprites flit through the tunnels, and the occasional lava fall blocks the path. Glyphs of warding are etched into the stone, triggered by any who approach without a passphrase. At the end lies a massive iron door guarded by two hellfire golems.",
          "paths": [
            "The Foundry Floor",
            "Bel's War Room"
          ]
        },
        "The Foundry Floor": {
          "description": "The heart of the Forge, a cavernous chamber where the enslaved fire primordial, a being of living magma, is chained in the center. Its heat powers a hundred smithing stations where duergar, azers, and bound devils hammer out infernal weapons. Conveyor belts of bone and sinew carry finished arms to loading platforms. The noise is deafening, and the air is thick with smoke and sulfur. Overseers crack whips of blue flame, and the clang of metal never ceases.",
          "paths": [
            "The Magma Tunnels",
            "Primordial Chamber",
            "Experimental Weapons Lab"
          ]
        },
        "Primordial Chamber": {
          "description": "A reinforced chamber at the center of the Foundry where the enslaved fire primordial is kept, its massive form bound by chains of cold iron and ice magic. The primordial's fury is palpable – it constantly strains against its bonds, causing earthquakes throughout the Forge. A control altar operated by Bel's chief torturer, a cornugon named Zal'gurath, adjusts the chains' tension to keep the creature on the edge of submission. If the primordial were ever freed, it would annihilate the Forge in a volcanic cataclysm.",
          "paths": [
            "The Foundry Floor"
          ]
        },
        "Experimental Weapons Lab": {
          "description": "A sealed wing of the Forge where Bel's most dangerous creations are developed: soul-powered bombs, demon-possessed siege engines, and a prototype infernal war machine that can level a city. The lab is filled with crackling arcane energy, bubbling vats of alchemical fluids, and the occasional explosion. Bel's chief arcanist, an amnizu named V'lek, oversees the research. The lab's greatest secret is a partially completed 'Soul Engine' capable of trapping the essence of an archdevil – something Bel may one day use against Zariel or even Asmodeus.",
          "paths": [
            "The Foundry Floor"
          ]
        },
        "Bel's War Room": {
          "description": "A strategic command center deep within the Forge, where Bel plots Blood War campaigns and, more importantly, his own return to power. The room contains a detailed map of Avernus with all devil and demon positions marked. Bel, a crimson pit fiend with scarred wings and a calculating gaze, meets visitors here. He speaks in measured tones and offers valuable intelligence, weapons, or troops to those willing to strike against Zariel. The walls are lined with trophies from the Reckoning – the hellfire-scorched armor of defeated rivals.",
          "paths": [
            "The Magma Tunnels"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Bel (former Archduke, master forger)",
        "Zal'gurath (Cornugon torturer)",
        "V'lek (Amnizu arcanist)",
        "Fire primordial (enslaved elemental)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Bel's Soul Engine is nearly complete, but it lacks a key component: a fragment of a god's essence. He has secretly learned of the Vestige of He Who Was deep in Nessus and is bargaining with agents of Mephistopheles to acquire a piece of that divine remnant. If he succeeds, his Soul Engine could theoretically imprison even Asmodeus, making Bel the new Lord of the Nine.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Styx (Avernian Mouth)": {
      "name": "The Styx (Avernian Mouth)",
      "basicInfo": "The Avernian stretch of the River Styx, the great black waterway that connects all layers of Hell. Here in Avernus, the river is particularly wide and treacherous, its waters teeming with memory-eating fish, merrenoloth ferrymen, and the wrecks of lost ships. The 'Mouth' is a great whirlpool on the western edge of the layer where the Styx plunges into a waterfall that descends directly into Dis. The banks are dotted with shanty towns of condemned souls, mercenary outposts, and the obsidian towers of the Styx Oarsmen – a guild of ferrymen who control transit on the river.",
      "x": 3,
      "y": -36,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Avernus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Whirlpool Gate": {
          "description": "The great maelstrom where the Styx descends in a massive waterfall into the iron city of Dis below. The whirlpool is surrounded by jagged rocks and the wrecks of ships that misjudged the approach. A permanent gateway above the falls allows flying devils to bypass the water entirely. The gate is guarded by a squadron of spined devils who demand transit permits from Zariel's Citadel. The roar of the falls is deafening, and the spray carries fragments of lost memories.",
          "paths": [
            "Oarsmen's Guild Tower",
            "Shanty Town of Forgotten Souls"
          ]
        },
        "Oarsmen's Guild Tower": {
          "description": "A tall obsidian tower on the riverbank that serves as the headquarters of the Styx Oarsmen – merrenoloth ferrymen who hold a monopoly on Styx transit. The tower is run by an ancient merrenoloth named Captain Ulthom, who negotiates passage fees in soul coins. Maps of the Styx's current condition (it shifts) are updated hourly by scrying imps. The lower levels house dockworkers and repair facilities for the soul-fueled ferries. Disputes are settled by the Captain's bone devil enforcer.",
          "paths": [
            "The Whirlpool Gate",
            "Ferry Docks"
          ]
        },
        "Ferry Docks": {
          "description": "The bustling docks where merrenoloth ferries load and unload passengers – souls, devils, and the occasional desperate mercenary. The docks are a chaotic mess of shouting, creaking wood, and splashing black water. Each berth is dedicated to a different destination: Dis, Stygia, Malbolge, and other layers. Prices are high, and the journey is perilous; many ferries are attacked by hydroloths or dragged under by the memories of the dead.",
          "paths": [
            "Oarsmen's Guild Tower",
            "Memory Market"
          ]
        },
        "Shanty Town of Forgotten Souls": {
          "description": "A sprawling slum on the banks of the Styx, inhabited by souls who have lost their memories to the river and now eke out a bleak existence. The shanty is built from driftwood, shipwrecks, and discarded infernal materials. Desperate souls sell the few fragments of identity they retain – a name, a face, a single memory – to night hags in exchange for protection. A mute, memory-wiped pit fiend named 'The Wordless' serves as an unofficial guardian, driving off devils who prey on the helpless.",
          "paths": [
            "The Whirlpool Gate",
            "Memory Market"
          ]
        },
        "Memory Market": {
          "description": "A black market stall run by a coven of night hags who trade in stolen memories extracted from the Styx. Customers can purchase 'memory vials' – captured recollections of mortal lives – for use in magic or as perverse entertainment. The market is hidden behind an illusory wall at the edge of the shanty town. The hags, led by a crone named Auntie Flem, also offer to 'cleanse' memories for those who wish to forget their mortal sins, for a steep price in soul coins.",
          "paths": [
            "Ferry Docks",
            "Shanty Town of Forgotten Souls"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Captain Ulthom (Merrenoloth guildmaster)",
        "Styx Oarsmen (ferrymen monopoly)",
        "Night hags of the Memory Market",
        "The Wordless (Pit fiend guardian)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Styx is sentient to a degree – it is a conduit for the collective memory of all damned souls. The Oarsmen's Guild has discovered a way to temporarily dam the river to access a submerged library of lost knowledge called the Well of Forgotten Names. Captain Ulthom is secretly using this knowledge to blackmail archdevils across all nine layers, a game that could get him destroyed if discovered.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Iron Tower": {
      "name": "The Iron Tower",
      "basicInfo": "The impregnable seat of Dispater, Archduke of Dis. This colossal fortress of black iron and adamantine rises from the city's heart like a clenched fist, its walls perfectly smooth and its windows mere slits. Dispater has ruled here for eons, never leaving the tower's innermost sanctum — a paranoia born of the Reckoning and the constant threat of assassination. The tower is a city in itself, housing his elite guard, the Iron Guard, and countless bureaucratic offices that manage Hell's trade. Mortals are almost never admitted, and those who are must navigate a series of deadly traps and magical wards that shift with Dispater's whims. At the tower's base sprawls the Iron City, a grid of streets radiating outward like spokes.",
      "x": 5,
      "y": -10,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Dis — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "Outer Ward": {
          "description": "The base of the Iron Tower, surrounded by a defensive ring of razor-sharp iron spikes and a moat of molten lead. The ward is patrolled by dozens of bearded devils and Iron Guard erinyes in polished black plate. Entry requires a writ of passage stamped with Dispater's own seal — a stylized iron tower surrounded by nine flames. Those without a writ are subject to immediate execution, their souls fed to the tower's forges.",
          "paths": [
            "Audience Chamber",
            "Iron Guard Barracks"
          ]
        },
        "Audience Chamber": {
          "description": "A vast, cylindrical room within the tower's lower levels where Dispater — cloaked in shadow and silence — receives petitions. The archduke never speaks directly; his will is conveyed through a chorus of erinyes who speak in perfect unison. The walls are lined with the petrified remains of those who displeased him, their terrified expressions frozen in iron. The floor is a map of the city, with real-time troop movements displayed by crawling lines of molten metal.",
          "paths": [
            "Outer Ward",
            "The Iron Vaults"
          ]
        },
        "Iron Guard Barracks": {
          "description": "The quarters and training grounds of Dispater's elite enforcers, the Iron Guard. These erinyes are fanatically loyal, their bodies augmented with iron grafts that make them living weapons. The barracks echo with the clash of practice swords and the screams of captured demons used for live-fire exercises. A war room displays intelligence reports from across the Nine Hells, with suspected traitors marked in red. The captain, an ancient erinyes named Furion, answers only to Dispater.",
          "paths": [
            "Outer Ward",
            "The Watchtower"
          ]
        },
        "The Iron Vaults": {
          "description": "Deep beneath the tower, these vaults hold Dispater's greatest treasures: mountains of soul coins, enchanted weapons, and the original copy of the Pact Primeval (a secret known only to Dispater and Asmodeus). The vaults are a labyrinth of trapped corridors, shifting walls, and anti-magic zones. The center of the labyrinth is a single chamber where the Pact rests on a black marble pedestal, guarded by two iron golems that were once the souls of archmages.",
          "paths": [
            "Audience Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The Watchtower": {
          "description": "The highest point of the Iron Tower, a spire that pierces the eternal smog of Dis. From here, Dispater's astral projection surveys his entire layer. The watchtower contains a scrying pool of liquid mercury that shows any location in Dis in real time. The pool is operated by a squad of obsidian dretches (lesser devils) whose eyes have been replaced with crystal lenses. A permanent portal to the city's defensive grid allows Dispater to reshape the streets at will.",
          "paths": [
            "Iron Guard Barracks"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Dispater (Archduke of Dis)",
        "Iron Guard (elite erinyes)",
        "Furion (Captain of the Guard)",
        "Pact Guardians (golems)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Pact Primeval in Dispater's vault is not the original — the true Pact is hidden in Nessus. This copy is a decoy, enchanted to drive any thief who touches it irrevocably insane. However, the decoy contains a subtle flaw: it holds Dispater's true name, encoded within the legalese. If someone were to extract that name, they could theoretically command the archduke.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Iron City": {
      "name": "The Iron City",
      "basicInfo": "The endless metropolis that is the second layer of Hell. A grid of black iron streets, soot-choked air, and towering tenements that stretch to the horizon. The city is a prison — Dispater can reshape its layout at will, trapping intruders in an infinite maze. It is the primary trading hub of the Nine Hells, where devils, soul merchants, and mercenary companies conduct business under the watchful eyes of the Iron Guard. The city is divided into districts, each serving a specific infernal function: forges, markets, slave pens, and the massive soul-processing facilities that give Dis its nickname, 'the City of the Damned.'",
      "x": 13,
      "y": 10,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Dis — West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Street of Iron": {
          "description": "The main thoroughfare, a wide avenue of black metal cobblestones that ring with every footstep. The street is lined with soul-forges and weapon smithies, their chimneys belching smoke that obscures the ceiling. Devils of every rank march alongside chained demon prisoners and soul merchants hawking their wares. The Iron Guard patrols constantly, and sudden street shifts can isolate any block. The air is filled with the clang of hammers and the cries of damned souls being processed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Soul Market",
            "The Forge District"
          ]
        },
        "The Soul Market": {
          "description": "A massive open-air bazaar where souls are traded like livestock. Lemures shuffle in pens, larval souls writhe in jars, and more valuable souls — wizards, warriors, saints — are auctioned from elevated platforms. Night hags and rakshasas haggle with devil buyers, while notaries of Dispater record every transaction. The smell of brimstone, ink, and despair saturates the air. A central auction house, the Pit, holds daily sales of captured celestial souls — the highest-priced commodity in Hell.",
          "paths": [
            "The Street of Iron",
            "The Pit Auction House"
          ]
        },
        "The Pit Auction House": {
          "description": "A sunken amphitheater at the Soul Market's heart where the rarest souls are auctioned. The bidders sit in iron galleries overlooking a central stage where the auctioneer, a bone devil named Carver, displays the merchandise. Celestials, ancient warriors, and once-pious clerics are sold here for tens of thousands of soul coins. The atmosphere is charged with greed and desperation. A vault behind the stage holds souls awaiting auction, their containers marked with their mortal sins.",
          "paths": [
            "The Soul Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Forge District": {
          "description": "A sprawling industrial sector where the weaponry of the Blood War is forged. Hundreds of anvils ring, powered by bound salamanders and fire elementals. Hellfire weaponry, infernal war machines, and demon-tainted explosives are crafted here by captured duergar, azers, and damned souls with artisan skills. The district is run by the Forgelord, a pit fiend named Vorgar, who answers directly to Dispater. The heat is punishing, and accidents — when a slave falls into a forge — are common.",
          "paths": [
            "The Street of Iron",
            "The Stygian Docks"
          ]
        },
        "The Stygian Docks": {
          "description": "The terminus where the River Styx arrives via the great waterfall from Avernus. Ferries unload soul shipments from the upper layer, and flat-bottomed barges carry forged weapons to the lower Hells. Merrenoloths pilot the vessels, their fares paid in soul coins. The docks are a chaotic zone of shouting, creaking wood, and splashing black water. A small market for Styx-water-derived memory potions operates here, sold by a coven of amnizu alchemists.",
          "paths": [
            "The Forge District"
          ]
        },
        "The Ghetto of Lost Souls": {
          "description": "A slum district where the most wretched souls dwell — those who have been stripped of all value and left to rot. Crumbling iron tenements house damned souls who exist in a state of permanent despair, their memories eroded by the Styx. Devils prey on them for sport, and the streets are strewn with broken soul coins and the husks of forgotten identities. A small, hidden chapel to Ilmater (the only one in Hell) is whispered to exist here, where a fallen angel named Seraphiel quietly offers comfort.",
          "paths": [
            "The Street of Iron"
          ]
        },
        "The Hidden Chapel": {
          "description": "A secret sanctuary in the Ghetto, disguised as a collapsed building. Inside, a single candle burns on an altar of salvaged wood. The fallen angel Seraphiel — wings torn, halo cracked — ministers to the most hopeless souls, granting them a measure of peace before they are inevitably discovered and dragged away. The chapel is warded against infernal scrying by a gift from a repentant devil, but the ward weakens each time a soul is saved.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ghetto of Lost Souls"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Dispater's Iron Guard",
        "Soul Merchants' Guild",
        "Forgelord Vorgar",
        "Seraphiel's hidden ministry"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Seraphiel is not merely hiding — he is a double agent. Dispater allows the chapel to exist as a honeypot, letting desperate souls flock to it so they can be rounded up periodically. The fallen angel's true allegiance is to Mephistopheles, who uses the chapel to identify potential recruits for his Cold Court in Cania. Seraphiel marks souls with an invisible sigil, and those marked are 'harvested' by agents of the eighth layer.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Stockade": {
      "name": "The Stockade",
      "basicInfo": "The great prison of Dis, where captured demons from the Blood War are held and interrogated. A grim fortress of iron bars, acid pools, and antimagic cells, the Stockade is run by a hierarchy of torturers — chain devils, bone devils, and the feared inquisitors of the Iron Court. Here, demons are broken for information, punishment, or simply for entertainment. The Stockade also houses high-value mortal prisoners destined for special torment, as well as political enemies of Dispater awaiting trial (a formality before execution). The prison is located on the edge of the Iron City, near the River Styx, to facilitate easy transport of prisoners to deeper layers.",
      "x": -7,
      "y": 16,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Dis — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Main Gate": {
          "description": "A massive portcullis of black iron etched with sigils of binding and pain. The gate is guarded by a pair of horned devils wielding chain weapons that writhe like living snakes. All prisoners arrive here, whether from the battlefield or other layers. The entrance hall is a single large room where prisoners are catalogued, their souls branded with a mark of ownership. The walls are covered in the scratched pleas of those who passed through.",
          "paths": [
            "Interrogation Wing",
            "Demon Pens",
            "High-Security Block"
          ]
        },
        "Interrogation Wing": {
          "description": "A labyrinth of sound-proofed cells where chain devils practice their craft. Each cell contains an array of instruments — branding irons, nerve-hooks, and soul-extractors — and the floor drains are permanently stained red. Screams are silenced by magical wards, but the psychic residue of agony is palpable. The head inquisitor, a chain devil named Malachar, is a master of extracting truth and falsehood alike, selling information to the highest bidder.",
          "paths": [
            "The Main Gate",
            "Demon Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Demon Pens": {
          "description": "The general holding area for captured demons: vrock, hezrou, glabrezu, and even the occasional marilith. The pens are cells of iron and binding magic, each keyed to a specific demon type's weaknesses. Demons are kept in constant torment — acid drips, holy water sprays, and sonic wails — to prevent them from marshaling their strength. A fighting pit at the center allows guards to pit demons against each other for sport and training.",
          "paths": [
            "The Main Gate",
            "Interrogation Wing",
            "Fighting Pit"
          ]
        },
        "Fighting Pit": {
          "description": "A gladiatorial arena within the Stockade, surrounded by galleries where off-duty devils watch captive demons tear each other apart. Bets are placed in soul coins, and the pit boss, a bearded devil named Scarr, ensures the fights are 'fair' — meaning the demons are evenly matched in their weakened state. Occasionally, mortal prisoners are thrown in as a special event, drawing large crowds.",
          "paths": [
            "Demon Pens"
          ]
        },
        "High-Security Block": {
          "description": "The deepest level, reserved for archdemons, traitorous devils, and mortal artifacts too dangerous to destroy. The cells here are antimagic spheres, each suspended in a void of permanent silence. Prisoners are fed through tubes and never see another living being. The block is commanded by an ancient pit fiend named Vorenthrax, who has been driven utterly mad by the isolation but remains Dispater's most loyal jailer. The block's most notorious inmate is the demon lord Shaktari, Queen of Mariliths, held in a cell warded by twenty layers of binding.",
          "paths": [
            "The Main Gate"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Malachar (Head Inquisitor, chain devil)",
        "Scarr (Fighting Pit boss)",
        "Vorenthrax (Jailer of the High-Security Block)",
        "Demon prisoners (Shaktari and others)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Shaktari's imprisonment is not merely punishment — she is a bargaining chip. Dispater keeps her alive to use as leverage against the Abyss. If a demonic incursion threatens Dis, Dispater will release Shaktari on the condition that she lead her marilith legions against the invaders. Her cell is rigged with a failsafe that will incinerate her if she refuses.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Iron Rose": {
      "name": "The Iron Rose",
      "basicInfo": "A perverse 'pleasure garden' carved into a side cavern of Dis, maintained by the succubus Lady Verin. A place of unsettling beauty, the Iron Rose features trees of black iron with leaves of razor-sharp silver, fountains of liquid lead, and flowers that weep acidic tears. Nobility from across the Nine Hells — dukes, counts, and barons of the infernal hierarchy — come here to relax, engage in intrigue, and indulge in sadistic pleasures. The garden is a neutral ground, protected by Dispater's decree, but treachery simmers beneath every silk-draped bower. The Iron Rose is lit by a permanent blood-red moon conjured by Verin's magic.",
      "x": 21,
      "y": -35,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Dis — Far East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Silver Gate": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Iron Rose, a filigree arch of silver and iron that grants access only to those bearing an invitation from Lady Verin or a devil of baron rank or higher. Two erinyes in elegant, blood-red gowns serve as greeters and bouncers, their true nature barely concealed. The gate is enchanted to strip all weapons from entrants, depositing them in a secure vault — a rule that no one, not even archdevils, may break.",
          "paths": [
            "The Garden of Thorns",
            "Verin's Pavilion"
          ]
        },
        "The Garden of Thorns": {
          "description": "The central courtyard of the Iron Rose, filled with black iron trees and razor-edged silver leaves that chime in a nonexistent wind. Stone benches are placed in secluded nooks, where infernal nobles whisper conspiracies and exchange gifts of souls. The centerpiece is a fountain of liquid lead that flows in hypnotic patterns. At night, the blood-moon casts eerie shadows, and the garden seems to rearrange itself to create private spaces for its guests.",
          "paths": [
            "The Silver Gate",
            "The Pavilion of Whispers",
            "The Orchid House"
          ]
        },
        "Verin's Pavilion": {
          "description": "The personal quarters of Lady Verin, a succubus of immense power and charm. The pavilion is a silk tent of deep crimson, filled with cushions, intoxicating incense, and a harp that plays itself. Verin sits on a divan of bone, receiving visitors and negotiating the endless intrigues of Dis. A mirror behind her shows not reflections but the true desires of those who gaze into it. Verin is a master of secrets, and nothing said in her pavilion ever leaves — unless she wills it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Silver Gate"
          ]
        },
        "The Pavilion of Whispers": {
          "description": "A secluded bower draped in black silk, where the most sensitive negotiations occur. The pavilion is magically soundproofed, and a permanent zone of truth (which high-ranking devils can resist) emanates from a crystal at its center. Assassinations within this pavilion are forbidden by Dispater's decree, but 'accidents' have been known to happen. The pavilion's most notable feature is a table carved with a map of Dis, its districts glowing with activity.",
          "paths": [
            "The Garden of Thorns"
          ]
        },
        "The Orchid House": {
          "description": "A greenhouse of black glass filled with infernal flora — flowers that scream when plucked, vines that entangle and slowly crush, and a single, perfect white orchid that is the source of Verin's power. The orchid, a gift from Dispater himself, blooms once a century and grants a wish to whoever plucks it — at a terrible cost. The Orchid House is guarded by animated topiaries of iron, their branches sharp as swords.",
          "paths": [
            "The Garden of Thorns"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Lady Verin (Succubus proprietor)",
        "Infernal nobility (guests)",
        "Erinyes greeters",
        "Garden's animated guardians"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The white orchid is a gift from Dispater, but it is also a test. Any devil who plucks it and makes a selfish wish is marked as ambitious and subsequently eliminated by the Iron Guard. Those who make a selfless wish (for the good of Hell, or for Dispater's benefit) are promoted. Verin records the choices and reports them directly to Dispater, making the garden a subtle tool for weeding out potential rivals.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Gate of the Second Layer": {
      "name": "The Gate of the Second Layer",
      "basicInfo": "The primary transit nexus between Avernus and Dis, located where the River Styx pours down from the great whirlpool above. A colossal portal of black iron and adamantine, it opens onto the Stygian Docks and is the primary entry point for souls, goods, and military forces moving between the first and second layers. The Gate is guarded by the Second Legion, an army of bearded devils, horned devils, and a pit fiend general named Morax. Beyond simple transit control, the Gate is also a choke point in Hell's defenses — if the demons of Avernus ever breached the Gate, they could flood into Dis. For that reason, it is one of the most heavily fortified positions in the entire Nine Hells.",
      "x": 2,
      "y": 15,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Dis",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Whirlpool Gate": {
          "description": "The actual portal through which the Styx plunges from the Avernian Mouth into Dis. A massive stone archway frames the waterfall, its edges carved with scenes of the Blood War's history. The roar is deafening, and the spray can erase memories. A reinforced iron platform extends over the whirlpool, where Morax's legion processes arrivals. The portal is always open, but a secondary iris of iron can be closed in emergencies, sealing the Styx temporarily.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks of Dis",
            "Legion Fortress"
          ]
        },
        "The Docks of Dis": {
          "description": "The arrival point for ferries and barges that survived the waterfall. The docks are a chaotic sprawl of piers, warehouses, and customs posts. Merrenoloths unload cargo while erinyes inspectors check manifests. A bustling black market for contraband — including mortal souls smuggled without proper paperwork — thrives in the shadows. The dockmaster, a bone devil named Tosk, takes bribes openly.",
          "paths": [
            "The Whirlpool Gate",
            "The Soul Processing Center"
          ]
        },
        "Legion Fortress": {
          "description": "A massive fortress of black iron built into the cliff beside the Whirlpool Gate, housing the Second Legion's garrison. Barracks, armories, and war rooms fill the structure. Morax, a pit fiend general with four arms wielding four hellfire axes, commands from a central throne room decorated with the banners of conquered demon hordes. The fortress's walls bristle with ballistas that fire soul bolts — weapons that can permanently destroy demons.",
          "paths": [
            "The Whirlpool Gate",
            "Morax's Throne Room"
          ]
        },
        "Morax's Throne Room": {
          "description": "The heart of the Legion Fortress, a grand hall of black marble and iron. Morax's throne is forged from the fused armor of demon lords he has personally slain. The walls display the severed heads of notable foes, magically preserved and still aware. A war map of Dis's defensive grid dominates one wall, with moving pieces representing troop deployments. Morax is a brutal but competent commander, utterly loyal to Dispater — or so it seems.",
          "paths": [
            "Legion Fortress"
          ]
        },
        "The Soul Processing Center": {
          "description": "A grim facility where newly arrived mortal souls are sorted, evaluated, and branded. Lines of terrified souls shuffle through processing chambers, where notary devils record their sins and assign them to their final destinations within Dis. The center is a masterpiece of infernal bureaucracy — efficient, cruel, and unforgiving. A soul who can prove their sins have been 'overpaid' (through mortal suffering) may be granted a lesser torment, but such cases are vanishingly rare.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks of Dis"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "General Morax (Pit fiend commander)",
        "Second Legion (defenders of the Gate)",
        "Merrenoloth ferrymen",
        "Soul notaries"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "General Morax is secretly a worshipper of Baalzebul, the Lord of the Seventh. He has been subtly weakening the Gate's defenses in preparation for a coup, hoping to allow a demonic incursion that would discredit Dispater and allow Baalzebul to install a new archduke. The severed heads on his wall are not all demons — one belongs to a high-ranking Iron Guard who discovered his treachery.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Sinking City of Minauros": {
      "name": "The Sinking City of Minauros",
      "basicInfo": "The capital of the third layer of Hell, a sprawling, crumbling metropolis built on endless wooden and iron pilings driven deep into the foul marsh. The city is a monument to Mammon's greed and neglect — palaces of marble sink slowly into the mire, their lower floors flooded with fetid water. Streets are canals navigated by pole-driven barges, and the air is thick with the stench of rot, sulfur, and incense burned to mask the decay. The city is the administrative heart of Mammon's financial empire, where infernal accountants, debt collectors, and soul-brokers conduct the business that funds the Blood War. The Archduke's palace, the Glitterhell, stands at the center, a garish tower of gold and jewels that grows taller each century as Mammon hoards more wealth. Yet beneath the opulence, the city is slowly dying — the pilings rot faster than they can be replaced, and Mammon cares only for his treasure.",
      "x": 20,
      "y": 14,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Minauros",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Canal Gates": {
          "description": "The main entrance to the city, a series of massive iron portcullises spanning the main canal. Barges loaded with goods, souls, and petitioners queue for inspection by erinyes customs agents. The water here is choked with the flotsam of Hell's commerce — broken soul coins, discarded contracts, and the occasional body. Tolls are paid in gold or soul coin, and those who cannot pay are conscripted into the piling-repair crews, a slow death sentence.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Canal",
            "The Piling Ghetto"
          ]
        },
        "The Glitterhell": {
          "description": "Mammon's palace, a grotesque tower of gold, silver, and precious gems that rises from the city's center. Every surface is encrusted with wealth — walls of gold coins, windows of diamond, and floors of melted-down tribute. The tower is a labyrinth of vaults, treasure halls, and the Archduke's private chambers. Mammon himself, a bloated pit fiend whose body is fused with his hoard, sits on a throne of solid platinum. The air is thick with the clink of coins and the whispered prayers of the greedy. The Glitterhell is defended by a legion of golden golems that animate when any treasure is touched without permission.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Canal",
            "The Golden Vaults",
            "The Counting House"
          ]
        },
        "The Golden Vaults": {
          "description": "The deepest levels of the Glitterhell, where Mammon's true wealth is stored. The vaults are a maze of trapped corridors, each lined with shelves of soul coins, enchanted artifacts, and the collateral from broken contracts. The central vault, sealed by a door of pure adamantine inlaid with a puzzle lock, contains the 'Soul of Greed' — the first coin Mammon ever earned, a relic of immense power. The vaults are patrolled by hoard golems and the ghosts of dead bankers who failed Mammon.",
          "paths": [
            "The Glitterhell"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Canal": {
          "description": "The main artery of the city, a wide, sluggish waterway that winds through the districts. Gondolas of black iron, poled by mute lemures, carry passengers. The banks are lined with counting houses, debtors' prisons, and the offices of soul-brokers. The water is opaque with filth, and strange shapes move beneath the surface. Bridges of marble, now cracked and slime-covered, arch overhead. The canal is the only reliable way to navigate the sinking city, and its twists are mapped by the Gondolier's Guild.",
          "paths": [
            "The Canal Gates",
            "The Glitterhell",
            "The Debtors' Prison",
            "The Piling Ghetto"
          ]
        },
        "The Counting House": {
          "description": "The infernal bank attached to the Glitterhell, where the financial records of Hell are maintained. A massive hall of ledger-stuffed shelves, staffed by bone devils with ink-stained claws and erinyes auditors. Every soul contract in the Nine Hells is recorded here, cross-indexed and filed. The head accountant, an ancient amnizu named Fiscus, knows the precise value of every soul in Hell. The Counting House is a place of absolute order — even Mammon respects its neutrality, as financial chaos would harm his own interests.",
          "paths": [
            "The Glitterhell"
          ]
        },
        "The Debtors' Prison": {
          "description": "A grim fortress on a small island in the Grand Canal where souls who have defaulted on infernal contracts are held. The prison is a vertical shaft of iron cages suspended over the mire; debtors are kept in cells according to the size of their debt. Interest accrues daily, meaning most will never be released. The warden, a chain devil named Creditor, offers 'work release' programs: souls can labor in the pilings or the forges to pay down their debt at a fraction of a soul coin per day. The prison's deepest cell holds the Arch-Liar, a mortal who once cheated Mammon himself, kept alive in perpetual torment.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Canal"
          ]
        },
        "The Piling Ghetto": {
          "description": "The slums of the Sinking City, where the poorest souls and the lowest devils dwell. Shacks of rotting wood cling to the pilings, connected by precarious walkways. The water here is shallow and stagnant, breeding clouds of infernal mosquitoes. A black market for stolen goods, illegal contracts, and forbidden magic thrives in the shadows. The ghetto is also home to a hidden cult of Levistus, the frozen prince of Stygia, who offer escape to those willing to swear fealty to the rival archduke.",
          "paths": [
            "The Canal Gates",
            "The Grand Canal"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Mammon (Archduke of Minauros)",
        "Fiscus (Amnizu head accountant)",
        "Creditor (Chain devil warden)",
        "Gondolier's Guild",
        "Cult of Levistus (hidden)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Mammon's palace is not merely a hoard — it is a prison of his own making. The Archduke is physically bound to his treasure; he cannot leave the Glitterhell. The more he hoards, the heavier the chain that binds him. The 'Soul of Greed' in the central vault is actually a fragment of Mammon's own divine spark, stolen from him by Asmodeus as punishment for the Reckoning. If it were returned, Mammon would regain his freedom — and his full power.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Endless Mire": {
      "name": "The Endless Mire",
      "basicInfo": "The vast, decaying swamp that covers most of Minauros, stretching from the city's outskirts to the layer's borders. The Mire is a fetid wasteland of black water, quicksand, and rotting vegetation. Eternal rain falls from the iron-gray sky, a downpour that never ceases and soaks everything in a cold, greasy damp. The Mire is home to hag covens, exiled devils, and the remnants of failed invasions swallowed by the swamp. Treasure hunters brave the Mire for lost hoards and the ruins of ancient fortresses, but most become lost in the trackless waste, their bodies sinking into the mud. The Mire is also the hunting ground of the Drowned Legion — undead soldiers from a failed celestial incursion, now cursed to wander eternally.",
      "x": 24,
      "y": -27,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Minauros — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Causeway of Lost Hope": {
          "description": "A crumbling stone road that extends into the Mire from the Sinking City, built by Mammon in an abandoned project to drain the swamp. The causeway is littered with the remains of work crews and the abandoned equipment of the draining operation. It leads to several important sites, but is constantly threatened by encroaching mud and attacks from swamp creatures. The road is patrolled by mercenary devils who demand tolls from travelers.",
          "paths": [
            "Hag's Hollow",
            "The Drowned Fortress"
          ]
        },
        "Hag's Hollow": {
          "description": "A grove of twisted, leafless trees rising from a small island of solid ground, home to a powerful coven of night hags. The hags — Auntie Gristle, Auntie Maggot, and Auntie Wretch — brew potions, trade in stolen memories, and broker deals between devils and mortals. The Hollow is a place of grudging neutrality; even Mammon's tax collectors avoid it. The hags are cruel but fair, and they offer safe passage through the Mire — for a price.",
          "paths": [
            "The Causeway of Lost Hope",
            "The Drowned Fortress"
          ]
        },
        "The Drowned Fortress": {
          "description": "The ruins of a celestial fortress that fell from the sky during an ancient incursion into Hell. The fortress is half-sunken in the mud, its white marble walls stained black by centuries of rot. Inside, the Drowned Legion — hundreds of celestial soldiers turned to undead by the Mire's corruption — still march in silent formation, attacking any living creature they encounter. The fortress's central chapel holds the shattered remains of a solar, its holy sword still clutched in its skeletal hand.",
          "paths": [
            "The Causeway of Lost Hope",
            "Hag's Hollow"
          ]
        },
        "The Solar's Chapel": {
          "description": "The heart of the Drowned Fortress, a once-beautiful chapel now filled with black water to the knees. The solar's skeleton kneels before a shattered altar, its wings reduced to tatters. The sword, 'Dawn's Last Light', still glows faintly with celestial fire — a weapon capable of permanently slaying archdevils. The Drowned Legion defends the chapel ferociously, and the solar's spirit lingers, offering cryptic guidance to those who would take up its blade.",
          "paths": [
            "The Drowned Fortress"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Night Hag Coven (Auntie Gristle, Maggot, Wretch)",
        "Drowned Legion (undead celestials)",
        "Treasure hunters and swamp scavengers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The solar's sword, Dawn's Last Light, is not merely a weapon — it contains the celestial's dying oath, a promise to return and cleanse Minauros. If a mortal of pure heart wields it and speaks the oath in the presence of Mammon, the archduke would be bound by celestial law to release all souls held in debt. The hags of Hag's Hollow know this and will trade the sword's location for a significant favor.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Quagmire of Despair": {
      "name": "The Quagmire of Despair",
      "basicInfo": "A vast area of the Endless Mire where the rain falls hardest and the mud is deep enough to swallow a giant. The Quagmire is a place of intense emotional weight — the psychic residue of millions of souls who have despaired and sunk into the mud here. Travelers through the Quagmire are afflicted by crushing apathy and must constantly fight the urge to simply stop, sink, and join the forgotten dead. The area is dotted with the protruding hands and faces of the drowned, their expressions frozen in eternal sorrow. The Quagmire is avoided by most devils, but it is said that at its center lies the 'Well of Forgotten Sorrows', a portal to the Shadowfell.",
      "x": 17,
      "y": -15,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Minauros — Far East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Sinking Fields": {
          "description": "A treeless expanse of black mud and steady rain. The ground here is soft and bubbling, with methane gas hissing from below. The faces of the despairing dead press against the surface, their mouths open in silent screams. The air is cold and damp, and a profound lethargy seeps into the bones of travelers. Those who stop moving begin to sink immediately, and the mud seems to actively pull them down.",
          "paths": [
            "The Well of Sorrows"
          ]
        },
        "The Well of Sorrows": {
          "description": "A deep, perfectly circular pit at the Quagmire's center, filled with black water that does not reflect the sky — only the viewer's deepest regrets. The Well is a natural portal to the Shadowfell, and the waters are infused with shadowstuff. Souls who leap into the Well are transported to the Plane of Shadow, where they become sorrowsworn. A cloister of sorrowsworn monks guards the Well, their bodies wreathed in shadow, their eyes weeping black tears. They speak in whispers and offer passage to the Shadowfell for those who share their grief.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sinking Fields"
          ]
        },
        "The Monks' Cloister": {
          "description": "A small stone monastery built beside the Well, its walls weeping black moisture. The sorrowsworn monks live here in silent contemplation, recording the grief of every soul that passes through the Quagmire. Their library is filled with books of lament — each page a life's story ending in despair. The head monk, a sorrowsworn named Sister Regret, can read a visitor's grief and offer either catharsis or eternal numbness. She knows many secrets of the dead buried in the Quagmire.",
          "paths": [
            "The Well of Sorrows"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Sorrowsworn monks (guardians of the Well)",
        "Sister Regret (head monk)",
        "The Drowned Dead (passive)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Sister Regret was once a cleric of Ilmater who came to Hell to rescue a loved one. She succeeded in finding her lover's soul but was captured by Mammon's forces. Rather than be broken, she voluntarily entered the Well of Sorrows and emerged as a sorrowsworn, using her new nature to guide other despairing souls. Her lover's soul coin is still held in the Glitterhell, and she trades secrets of Mammon's treasury to anyone who might retrieve it.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Goldmonger's Tomb": {
      "name": "The Goldmonger's Tomb",
      "basicInfo": "A forgotten vault on the outskirts of the Sinking City, the Goldmonger's Tomb is the final resting place of a mortal merchant who bargained his way into Hell and became so wealthy that even Mammon grew jealous. The merchant, a dwarf named Kharum Goldtongue, built this vault as his eternal treasury, filling it with wealth from across the planes. After his death (which Mammon arranged), the vault became his tomb, and his spirit still guards it. The Tomb is a popular target for treasure hunters, but its traps and its ghostly guardian have claimed every would-be thief. The vault is said to contain a single coin that can buy one soul's freedom from Hell.",
      "x": -2,
      "y": 9,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Minauros — West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gilded Entrance": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Tomb, a stone archway covered in gold leaf and carved with scenes of Kharum's life — his bargains, his rise to power, and his eventual betrayal by Mammon. The archway is guarded by two golden lions that animate when approached, breathing streams of molten gold. A riddle is carved above the door: 'I am more precious than gold, more fleeting than a breath. What am I?' The answer is 'Time', and speaking it deactivates the lions.",
          "paths": [
            "The Treasury of the Damned",
            "Kharum's Crypt"
          ]
        },
        "The Treasury of the Damned": {
          "description": "The main vault, a vast chamber piled high with gold coins, enchanted weapons, and artifacts from across the planes. The treasure is cursed — anyone who takes a coin without solving a second riddle is bound to the Tomb, their soul added to Kharum's ghostly retinue. The real wealth is hidden in secret compartments, each requiring a separate key. The room is lit by the glow of a thousand soul coins, their trapped essences providing a faint, mournful hum.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gilded Entrance",
            "Kharum's Crypt"
          ]
        },
        "Kharum's Crypt": {
          "description": "The inner sanctum of the Tomb, where Kharum's physical remains lie on a golden bier, his skeletal hand clutching a single, glowing coin — the 'Freedom Coin', said to be able to buy one soul's release from Hell. Kharum's ghost stands guard, a translucent figure of a dwarf in merchant's robes, his voice echoing with the clink of coins. He will negotiate with respectful visitors, offering the Freedom Coin in exchange for a story of true selflessness — a quality he never possessed in life and now eternally covets.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gilded Entrance",
            "The Treasury of the Damned"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Kharum Goldtongue (Ghost merchant)",
        "Treasure hunters",
        "The Ghostly Retinue (souls of past thieves)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Freedom Coin is genuine, but it has a catch: it can only be used to free a soul whose owner agrees to the trade. Since Mammon never releases souls willingly, the coin is effectively useless against him. Kharum keeps it as a final joke — a treasure that seems priceless but is worthless in Hell's economy. However, it can free a soul from any other devil, making it a powerful bargaining chip against Mammon's rivals.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Steaming Fen": {
      "name": "The Steaming Fen",
      "basicInfo": "A geothermal region of the Endless Mire where volcanic vents heat the swamp water to near-boiling, creating a perpetual field of geysers, hot springs, and scalding mud. The Fen is home to a unique ecology of fire-resistant swamp creatures and a tribe of exiled salamanders who were banished from the Elemental Plane of Fire. The salamanders, led by the Djinn-slayer Zorath, have built a settlement called Emberhollow around the largest vent. The Fen is also rumored to contain a sunken portal to the Plane of Fire, from which the salamanders originally emerged. Mammon tolerates their presence because they mine gold and gems from the deep strata, paying a steep tax in raw materials.",
      "x": -18,
      "y": 9,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Minauros — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Geyser Fields": {
          "description": "A treacherous expanse of boiling mud, steam vents, and unpredictable geysers. The ground is unstable, and a sudden burst of scalding water can strip flesh from bone. The air is thick with sulfur and steam, reducing visibility to near zero. Paths through the field shift as new geysers erupt and old ones die. Emberhollow salamanders navigate by instinct, but outsiders require a guide. The fields are dotted with the calcified remains of those who attempted to cross alone.",
          "paths": [
            "Emberhollow",
            "The Golden Geyser"
          ]
        },
        "Emberhollow": {
          "description": "The salamander settlement, a collection of buildings made from fused volcanic stone and scavenged infernal iron, clustered around a massive, stable geyser vent. The heat is intense, and the air ripples with distortion. The salamanders, led by the warlord Zorath, are a proud warrior culture exiled from their home plane. They mine the deep strata for gold and gems, trading with Minauros's merchants for weapons and supplies. The settlement's central forge produces the finest non-diabolic weapons in the layer, and Zorath's personal blade — 'Steamfang' — is said to be able to cut through any armor.",
          "paths": [
            "The Geyser Fields",
            "The Golden Geyser"
          ]
        },
        "The Golden Geyser": {
          "description": "A unique geyser that erupts not with water but with a spray of molten gold, sourced from a deep vein that Emberhollow's miners have never been able to trace to its origin. The geyser erupts once per hour, and the salamanders collect the solidified gold for tribute to Mammon. The source is believed to be a pocket of Mammon's own hoard, leaking through a planar rift. A small shrine to Mammon, built of collected gold bricks, sits beside the geyser — a grudging acknowledgment of the Archduke's claim.",
          "paths": [
            "The Geyser Fields",
            "Emberhollow"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Zorath (Salamander warlord)",
        "Emberhollow miners",
        "Mammon's tax collectors"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Zorath's exile from the Plane of Fire was not a punishment — he fled with a shard of the Elemental Prince of Fire's essence, which he uses to power Emberhollow's forges. The Prince's agents search Hell for Zorath, and if they find him, they will drag Emberhollow — and a large chunk of Minauros — back to the Plane of Fire as recompense. Mammon knows this but allows it, calculating that the destruction would allow him to rebuild and revalue the land for his own profit.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Abriymoch": {
      "name": "Abriymoch",
      "basicInfo": "The capital city of Phlegethos, built into the caldera of an immense volcano named Mount Flay. Abriymoch is a city of black glass towers, magma canals, and sprawling pleasure palaces. The city is openly ruled by Archduchess Fierna, a succubus-like devil of exquisite beauty and capricious cruelty, while her father Belial lurks in the shadows, the true master pulling her strings. The city is a hub of infernal entertainment: gladiatorial arenas, halls of sadistic art, and bathhouses fed by volcanic springs. The streets are paved with obsidian and lit by rivers of molten rock. Abriymoch is the only 'civilized' place in Phlegethos, where devils from across the Hells come to indulge, intrigue, and be seen. Fierna's palace, the Palace of Screaming Glass, dominates the caldera's rim, while Belial's hidden sanctum, the Obsidian Spire, is known only to a few.",
      "x": -23,
      "y": 23,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Phlegethos",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Caldera Gates": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Abriymoch, a massive archway of fused volcanic glass that spans the caldera's only break. The gates are guarded by fire giant sentinels and magma mephits. All visitors must submit to a 'burning test' — a brief walk across hot coals — to prove their infernal or mortal resilience. The air here is scorching, and the roar of distant lava falls is constant. From the gates, the entire city is visible: a bowl of black glass and orange fire.",
          "paths": [
            "The Palace of Screaming Glass",
            "The Crucible",
            "The Magma Canals"
          ]
        },
        "The Palace of Screaming Glass": {
          "description": "Fierna's official residence, a towering structure of semi-translucent obsidian that emits a constant, high-pitched wail as the wind passes through its tortured architecture. The palace is a monument to depravity: halls draped in the flayed skins of defeated rivals, chambers where the walls themselves weep scalding tears, and the Archduchess's throne room, where she reclines on cushions of still-smoldering soul-stuff. Fierna holds court here, surrounded by her coterie of erinyes handmaidens and charmed pit fiend suitors. The palace is the center of social life in Abriymoch, and an invitation to one of Fierna's 'fireside gatherings' is both an honor and a mortal danger.",
          "paths": [
            "The Caldera Gates",
            "The Garden of Burning Roses",
            "The Obsidian Spire"
          ]
        },
        "The Garden of Burning Roses": {
          "description": "A grotesque pleasure garden attached to the palace, filled with flora that blooms in flame. Roses of fire, trees with leaves of smoldering embers, and fountains of molten gold create a space of terrible beauty. The garden is patrolled by invisible stalkers who enforce Fierna's rules: no overt violence, but poison and whispered betrayal are encouraged. The garden's centerpiece is the 'Tree of a Thousand Sorrows', a black-barked tree whose branches are hung with the crystallized screams of Fierna's victims.",
          "paths": [
            "The Palace of Screaming Glass"
          ]
        },
        "The Crucible": {
          "description": "The gladiatorial arena of Abriymoch, a massive bowl of black stone where condemned souls, captured demons, and volunteer champions fight for the entertainment of the infernal nobility. The arena floor can be flooded with lava, filled with acid, or seeded with spike traps at the whims of the crowd. The grand stands are shaded by a permanent cloud of volcanic ash. The arena is run by a scarred pit fiend named Vulkar, who answers directly to Fierna. Champions who survive ten bouts are granted their freedom — a prize rarely achieved.",
          "paths": [
            "The Caldera Gates",
            "The Champion's Barracks"
          ]
        },
        "The Champion's Barracks": {
          "description": "Beneath the Crucible, a warren of cells and training rooms where the arena's gladiators are kept. Fighters are treated well by infernal standards — good food, medical care, and constant combat training — because a dead champion is a poor investment. The barracks house a motley assortment of mortal souls, lesser devils, and even a captured celestial (a deva bound in chains of cold iron). The gladiators form temporary alliances, but everyone knows the arena is a zero-sum game.",
          "paths": [
            "The Crucible"
          ]
        },
        "The Obsidian Spire": {
          "description": "Belial's secret sanctum, a slender tower of pure black obsidian hidden behind an illusory wall in the palace's lower levels. The Spire is warded against all scrying and teleportation. Inside, Belial — a pit fiend of ancient lineage, his horns carved with runes of power — conducts his true business: manipulating Fierna, corrupting the court, and forging infernal weapons in a private smithy. The walls are lined with the petrified bodies of those who discovered his secret. A scrying pool of magma shows him any location in Phlegethos.",
          "paths": [
            "The Palace of Screaming Glass"
          ]
        },
        "The Magma Canals": {
          "description": "The city's transport network, a series of wide channels cut through the obsidian streets, filled with glowing, slow-moving lava. Flat-bottomed barges of fire-resistant stone, poled by enslaved azers, ferry passengers and goods. The canals are the lifeblood of Abriymoch, and their junctions are bustling marketplaces. A permanent haze of heat shimmers above the lava, and the smell of sulfur is ever-present. The canals are also used for executions: the condemned are simply thrown in.",
          "paths": [
            "The Caldera Gates",
            "The Temple of Writhing Flame"
          ]
        },
        "The Temple of Writhing Flame": {
          "description": "A religious site dedicated to the worship of fire itself, in the form of Kossuth or a more primal infernal flame-deity. The temple is a ziggurat of black iron, its steps glowing red with internal heat. Priests in robes of asbestos lead chants to the eternal flame, and a massive fire elemental, bound for centuries, dances at the altar. The temple serves as neutral ground for negotiations between rival devil factions, as all respect the sanctity of the flame. The high priest, a fire giant named Ignius, is one of the few beings who speaks to Fierna as an equal.",
          "paths": [
            "The Magma Canals"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Fierna (Archduchess of Phlegethos)",
        "Belial (Shadow ruler, Fierna's father)",
        "Vulkar (Pit fiend arena master)",
        "The Writhing Flame priesthood"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Fierna is not merely Belial's puppet — she is fully aware of his manipulations and secretly resents him. She has been cultivating an alliance with Mephistopheles, offering Phlegethian fire magic in exchange for a weapon that could permanently bind Belial. The 'Tree of a Thousand Sorrows' in her garden is actually a prison for the soul of Belial's first consort, whose ghostly knowledge Fierna exploits.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The River of Fire": {
      "name": "The River of Fire",
      "basicInfo": "A great lava river that flows from the peak of Mount Flay down through Abriymoch and across the Phlegethian plains, eventually emptying into a sea of magma. The river is both a vital trade route and a place of punishment: damned souls are often sentenced to 'swim' in the lava, their bodies burning and reforming in endless agony. Barges of fireproof stone navigate the river, crewed by enslaved azers and magmins. The river's banks are dotted with way stations, mining camps, and the ruined fortresses of past archdukes. The river is also a spiritual boundary; Phlegethians believe that crossing the river cleanses infernal corruption, though in practice it merely burns away physical form.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -13,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Phlegethos — West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Sorrowful Bank": {
          "description": "A section of the river's edge where condemned souls are brought for punishment. A constant queue of chained lemures and mortal sinners waits under the lash of barbazu overseers. One by one, they are pushed into the lava, their screams joining the eternal chorus. The shore is studded with 'weepers' — statues of obsidian that shed tears of molten glass, said to be the collected grief of the punished. A small shrine to Levistus is hidden nearby, for those who wish to freeze rather than burn (a futile hope).",
          "paths": [
            "The Ashen Docks",
            "The Way Station of Cinders"
          ]
        },
        "The Ashen Docks": {
          "description": "A small port where lava barges load and unload cargo. The docks are built from fused volcanic rock, and the air shimmers with heat. A bustling black market operates here, dealing in obsidian weapons, infernal spices, and the crystallized tears of the punished (used as spell components). The dockmaster, a chain devil named Scald, ensures that all cargo pays the appropriate tax to Fierna's treasury.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sorrowful Bank",
            "The Way Station of Cinders"
          ]
        },
        "The Way Station of Cinders": {
          "description": "A fortified inn built on a rocky outcrop overlooking the river. The Way Station caters to travelers on the lava barges, offering rooms, food (infernal rations), and watered-down firewine. The innkeeper, a retired cornugon named Ember, maintains strict neutrality and keeps a 'do not burn' list of patrons who have paid for protection. The inn's common room is a popular meeting place for mercenaries and information brokers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sorrowful Bank",
            "The Ashen Docks"
          ]
        },
        "The Cascade of Ashes": {
          "description": "A massive lava fall where the river drops over a sheer cliff into a lower plain. The cascade is a mile wide and roars with a sound like constant thunder. A fine ash falls perpetually, coating the surroundings in gray. The cascade is a popular site for infernal duels: combatants fight on stone pillars jutting from the lava fall, and the loser is swept into the inferno below. A permanent gallery of obsidian benches allows spectators.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ashen Docks"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Scald (Chain devil dockmaster)",
        "Ember (Cornugon innkeeper)",
        "Barbazu punishment overseers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "Beneath the Cascade of Ashes, behind the curtain of falling lava, lies a hidden cave complex where Belial's secret forge is located. It is accessible only by diving through the lava fall (requiring fire immunity). The forge produces weapons of living magma, and its location is known only to Belial, his most trusted smiths, and now Fierna — who discovered it and has begun her own covert weapons program there.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Field of Broken Glass": {
      "name": "The Field of Broken Glass",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, flat plain of shattered obsidian that stretches for miles across Phlegethos, the result of an ancient battle between Belial and a rival pit fiend lord who was destroyed during the Reckoning. The plain is a deadly maze of razor-sharp shards, many still standing upright like frozen waves of black glass. It is considered cursed ground: the spirits of the fallen still howl in the wind that whips across the field. The Field is used as a punishment ground for disgraced devils, who are forced to wander the shards until they are shredded. It is also rumored to be the resting place of the Tear of the Sun, a legendary gem that fell from the heavens during the battle.",
      "x": 10,
      "y": -25,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Phlegethos — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Shattered Maw": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Field, where the landscape abruptly transitions from volcanic rock to a sea of broken glass. The shards here are the size of swords, and the ground crunches underfoot. A small outpost of bone devils monitors those sentenced to the Field, ensuring they do not attempt to flee. The outpost has a single, crude tavern called 'The Bleeding Foot', where guards and scavengers drink numbing firewine.",
          "paths": [
            "The Glass Canyons",
            "The Howling Spire"
          ]
        },
        "The Glass Canyons": {
          "description": "The central region of the Field, where the shards are as tall as buildings, forming canyon-like corridors that amplify the wind into ghostly howls. The spirits of the slain — pit fiends, cornugons, and mortal champions — manifest as fragmented images caught in the glass, reliving their final moments. The shards are sharp enough to cut through infernal armor, and one wrong step can sever a limb. Treasure hunters navigate these canyons, searching for the Tear of the Sun.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Maw",
            "The Pit of Whispers"
          ]
        },
        "The Howling Spire": {
          "description": "A natural obelisk of fused glass, the tallest point in the Field, that emits a constant, mournful wail as the wind passes through its fissures. The wail is the collective voice of the field's dead. Climbing the spire is a rite of passage for young devils seeking to prove their courage. At the top, a small platform offers a view of the entire field — and a single, frozen moment of the ancient battle plays out in a silent illusion: two titanic pit fiends locked in combat, their forms now part of the glass.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shattered Maw"
          ]
        },
        "The Pit of Whispers": {
          "description": "A deep, narrow sinkhole at the heart of the Glass Canyons, filled with shards so fine they are like powdered glass. The Pit is where the Tear of the Sun is rumored to have fallen. A permanent silence spell blankets the area, broken only by faint whispers that promise power to those who descend. At the bottom, a crystal pedestal holds the Tear — a fist-sized diamond that glows with a cold, white light. Reaching it requires navigating a maze of razor-walls that shift when unobserved.",
          "paths": [
            "The Glass Canyons"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Bone devil outpost guards",
        "Ghosts of the Reckoning",
        "Scavengers and treasure hunters"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Tear of the Sun is not a gem — it is the frozen tear of Lathander, shed when his celestial legion was slaughtered by Belial during the Reckoning. The tear contains a fragment of Lathander's divine radiance, and if returned to a temple of the Morninglord on the Material Plane, it could restore a dead sun or permanently blind the Archduke of Phlegethos. Belial has been searching for it for millennia, but its divine nature prevents any devil from touching it.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Fierna's Baths": {
      "name": "Fierna's Baths",
      "basicInfo": "A luxurious complex of geothermal pools, steam rooms, and massage chambers built on the outskirts of Abriymoch. The Baths are Fierna's private retreat, where she relaxes, entertains select guests, and conducts her most secret negotiations. The waters are naturally heated by the volcano and infused with rare minerals and infernal alchemy that promise rejuvenation — for a price. The Baths are staffed by veiled erinyes attendants and charmed mortal slaves. Entry is by invitation only, and the Baths are a den of intrigue, seduction, and quiet assassination. The complex is surrounded by a wall of black obsidian and guarded by invisible stalkers.",
      "x": -7,
      "y": 7,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Phlegethos — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Steam Curtain": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Baths, a corridor of perpetual, near-scalding steam that strips away illusions and disguises. All who enter are revealed as they truly are. The erinyes greeters assess guests and assign them to pools based on their social standing. The floor is heated mosaic tile depicting scenes of infernal indulgence. The steam is laced with a mild truth serum, encouraging honest conversation — and honest betrayal.",
          "paths": [
            "The Main Pool",
            "The Private Grottoes"
          ]
        },
        "The Main Pool": {
          "description": "A large, open-air (under the volcanic sky) pool of steaming, mineral-rich water. The pool is shaped like a five-pointed star, with each point occupied by a different faction of guests. Fierna's throne — a floating divan of obsidian — hovers over the center. Here, she holds 'salon sessions', where infernal nobles debate philosophy, art, and the latest Blood War gossip. The pool's waters have mild healing properties, but prolonged immersion causes obsession: guests never want to leave.",
          "paths": [
            "The Steam Curtain",
            "The Private Grottoes"
          ]
        },
        "The Private Grottoes": {
          "description": "A series of secluded, smaller pools and steam rooms branching from the Main Pool, each designed for intimate conversation or private assignations. The grottoes are soundproofed and warded against scrying, making them ideal for plotting. Many of Phlegethos's most infamous conspiracies were hatched in these pools. The walls are carved with scenes of pleasure and pain, and the air is thick with intoxicating incense.",
          "paths": [
            "The Steam Curtain",
            "The Main Pool"
          ]
        },
        "Fierna's Inner Sanctum": {
          "description": "A single, small pool hidden behind a secret door known only to Fierna and her most trusted servants. The pool is filled with a silvery liquid that is not water but concentrated soul-stuff. Bathing in it restores youth and vitality, but consumes the essence of a soul coin. Fierna uses this pool to maintain her ageless beauty. The walls are lined with shelves of soul coins, each representing a life consumed for vanity. A single, empty shelf is reserved for Belial's coin.",
          "paths": [
            "The Private Grottoes"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Fierna (host and Archduchess)",
        "Erinyes attendants",
        "Infernal nobles (guests)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Fierna's Inner Sanctum is not merely a beauty treatment — it is a soul-draining device keyed to the specific resonance of her father's essence. The empty shelf is not a threat; it is a countdown. Fierna has been secretly siphoning small amounts of Belial's power through the soul coins she sacrifices, and when she has drained enough, she will be powerful enough to challenge him openly. The ritual requires one final coin: a soul freely given by a mortal who knows exactly what they are sacrificing.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Pyre of the Damned": {
      "name": "The Pyre of the Damned",
      "basicInfo": "A massive, eternal bonfire on the outskirts of Abriymoch where the physical bodies of destroyed devils and the husks of demons slain in the Blood War are incinerated. The Pyre is a grim monument to Hell's endless cycle of destruction and rebirth. The flames are fed by a legion of lemures who endlessly haul corpses and fuel, overseen by a pit fiend named Cremator. The smoke from the Pyre rises in a black column visible across the entire layer, and the ashes are used to fertilize the strange crops that grow in Phlegethos's volcanic soil. The Pyre is also a place of execution for traitors, who are thrown into the central flame — a fire so hot it destroys even the soul, granting true oblivion.",
      "x": 2,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Phlegethos — Far East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Ash Heap": {
          "description": "The outer perimeter of the Pyre, a barren field covered in fine gray ash and charred bone fragments. The ground is warm underfoot, and the air is thick with smoke. Lemures work in silent, endless lines, hauling corpses from carts onto conveyor belts of bone that lead to the central flame. A small market for scavenged items — fragments of demon armor, broken weapons, and unburned soul coins — operates at the edge. The market is run by a one-armed nycaloth named Ash.",
          "paths": [
            "The Eternal Flame",
            "The Cremator's Tower"
          ]
        },
        "The Eternal Flame": {
          "description": "The central fire, a roaring column of white-hot flame that extends hundreds of feet into the air. The flame is contained within a pit of obsidian, and its roar is deafening. Any object or creature thrown in is annihilated utterly — body, soul, and memory. The flame is said to be a fragment of the Fire of Oblivion, a primordial force that predates the gods. Captured traitors are marched to the pit's edge and pushed in by Cremator himself, who watches with grim satisfaction.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ash Heap"
          ]
        },
        "The Cremator's Tower": {
          "description": "A squat, black tower on the edge of the Ash Heap that serves as Cremator's headquarters. The tower is filled with ledgers recording every corpse incinerated, every soul obliterated. Cremator, a pit fiend whose skin is perpetually smoldering, is a meticulous bureaucrat who views his work as a sacred duty. The tower's lower level contains a small prison for those awaiting execution. A single window looks out onto the Eternal Flame, and Cremator spends hours staring into it, contemplating oblivion.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ash Heap"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Cremator (Pit fiend master of the Pyre)",
        "Ash (Nycaloth scavenger merchant)",
        "Lemure laborers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "Cremator is not merely a functionary — he is a philosopher of annihilation. Over the millennia, he has come to believe that true peace can only be found in oblivion, and he has begun secretly obliterating souls that were not sentenced to true death, granting them a mercy that is against all infernal law. A ledger hidden in his tower records thousands of such 'unauthorized releases'. If discovered, he would be thrown into his own flame, but a part of him longs for that release.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Tartarus": {
      "name": "Tartarus",
      "basicInfo": "The prison of Archduke Levistus, a colossal iceberg of blue-black ice that drifts eternally through the frozen ocean of Stygia. Here, Levistus is entombed in a block of enchanted ice so cold that even the flames of Hell cannot melt it. His body is frozen solid, but his astral form can roam the Hells, scheming with those who dare to bargain. The iceberg is a monument to Asmodeus's wrath, carved with warning runes and guarded by the Chill Court — devils loyal to Levistus who hope for his eventual release. Tartarus is both a prison and a palace, with chambers hollowed out of the ice where Levistus's servants conduct his affairs. Mortals who seek the archduke's secret knowledge must brave the freezing winds and navigate the iceberg's treacherous interior.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 12,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Stygia — West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Frozen Approach": {
          "description": "A wide, creaking plain of sea ice that leads to the base of Tartarus. The ice is riddled with crevasses that plunge into the black, freezing water below. Sleet and hail lash continuously, and the wind carries faint whispers — fragments of Levistus's telepathic pleas for help. The approach is patrolled by ice mephits and cold-scaled barbazu, who demand a toll of a secret or a soul coin to allow passage. At the edge, a great staircase of ice leads up to the iceberg's main gate.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Frozen Tears",
            "The Astral Audience Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The Gate of Frozen Tears": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Tartarus, a massive archway formed by two frozen, screaming faces — said to be the last expressions of the pit fiends who dared to mock Levistus during the Reckoning. The gate opens into a long, sloping corridor of ice, lit by a cold blue glow that emanates from the walls. The temperature plummets as one enters, and metal armor begins to frost over. The gate is guarded by two gelugons (ice devils) whose crystalline spears can shatter steel.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Approach",
            "The Chamber of Frozen Echoes"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of Frozen Echoes": {
          "description": "A vast, domed chamber within the iceberg, where Levistus's voice — amplified by the ice — addresses his followers. The walls are covered in reliefs of his past victories, now distorted by frost. The floor is a mirror of black ice, reflecting the dark ceiling. When Levistus speaks, his words echo with the weight of millennia, and the very air vibrates. The chamber is used for audiences with the Chill Court and those who seek the archduke's counsel.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Frozen Tears",
            "The Astral Audience Chamber",
            "The Frozen Heart"
          ]
        },
        "The Astral Audience Chamber": {
          "description": "A smaller, more intimate room where Levistus's astral projection — a shimmering, ghostly figure of a beautiful humanoid encased in frost — meets with visitors. The chamber is furnished with chairs of ice and a table that seems to grow from the floor. Here, Levistus negotiates deals, offering secrets in exchange for favours that advance his long-term goal of freedom. A permanent scrying pool of liquid shadow allows him to view events across the Hells.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Approach",
            "The Chamber of Frozen Echoes",
            "The Vault of Forsaken Promises"
          ]
        },
        "The Frozen Heart": {
          "description": "The innermost chamber of Tartarus, where Levistus's physical body is encased in a block of indestructible ice, suspended from the ceiling by chains of black adamantine. The body is perfectly preserved, a pale, frozen king with a shattered crown. Around him, six ice pillars hold the souls of his most loyal servants, frozen in eternal vigil. Touching the ice block triggers a psychic assault — Levistus's fury and despair flooding the intruder's mind. The chamber is guarded by a lone, ancient gelugon named Kryost, who has not spoken in a thousand years.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chamber of Frozen Echoes",
            "The Vault of Forsaken Promises"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of Forsaken Promises": {
          "description": "A hidden side chamber accessible only by Levistus's command, containing the physical records of every oath broken to the Archduke. These broken promises manifest as shattered weapons, cracked soul coins, and frozen scrolls. The energy of betrayal is palpable, and the vault can be used to fuel a powerful ritual of vengeance against oathbreakers. The key to Levistus's prison — a single, perfect icicle that never melts — is hidden here, known only to the archduke himself.",
          "paths": [
            "The Astral Audience Chamber",
            "The Frozen Heart"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Levistus (imprisoned Archduke)",
        "The Chill Court (loyalist devils)",
        "Kryost (ancient gelugon guardian)",
        "Ice mephits and barbazu patrols"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The key to Levistus's prison is not a physical object but the 'Icicle of Forgiveness' — a single tear shed by a god for the fallen angel he once was. That tear exists, but it is held in the Lost Library of the Stygian Court, buried beneath the ice. Levistus seeks it desperately, but no devil can approach the Library without losing its memory to the Styx. He needs mortal agents to retrieve it.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Lost Library of the Stygian Court": {
      "name": "The Lost Library of the Stygian Court",
      "basicInfo": "Submerged beneath the frozen sea, the Lost Library is a repository of forbidden knowledge sealed by Asmodeus himself. Once the court library of the Stygian nobility, it was dragged beneath the ice after the Reckoning as punishment for its keepers' betrayal. The Library is a maze of crystallized bookcases, frozen in time, where every book contains a secret that could destabilize Hell. The waters around it are lethally cold, and the Library is guarded by the spirits of its former librarians — now banshees of ice. The Styx flows nearby, and its waters infuse the Library with forgetfulness: those who linger too long begin to lose their memories. Despite the dangers, the Library is sought by archdevils and mortal sages alike, for it contains the true names of all the Lords of the Nine, the location of the Rod of Seven Parts, and the secret of Levistus's imprisonment.",
      "x": 19,
      "y": -30,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Stygia — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Frozen Surface": {
          "description": "A barren stretch of ice marked only by a single, petrified tree — the Library's only surface marker. The ice here is thin, and beneath it, the Library's spires are visible as dark shapes. A permanent blizzard obscures vision. Ice mephits hover around, whispering warnings. A stone staircase, half-shattered, descends into a hole in the ice, leading to the submerged entrance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Frozen Tomes",
            "The Banshee's Atrium"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Frozen Tomes": {
          "description": "The main chamber of the Library, a long corridor lined with shelves of ice containing thousands of books. The books are frozen solid, and reading one requires carefully thawing it with a warmth spell — but the heat can damage the fragile pages. The books whisper when touched, offering snippets of forbidden lore. The hall is patrolled by the spectral librarians, whose icy touch drains memories. At the far end, a frozen archway leads deeper into the vaults.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Surface",
            "The Vault of True Names",
            "The Styx Seep"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of True Names": {
          "description": "A sealed chamber at the Library's heart, its door a puzzle of shifting ice runes. Inside, a single pedestal holds a crystalline book that contains the true names of the archdevils — including Asmodeus. The book is bound by a chain of cold iron to the pedestal, and any attempt to remove it triggers an avalanche of ice. The Librarian's banshee, a particularly powerful spirit named Chill Archivist, guards the vault and will negotiate with those who offer new knowledge for the Library.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Frozen Tomes"
          ]
        },
        "The Banshee's Atrium": {
          "description": "A circular room where the former librarians — now banshees — gather to sing a dirge of eternal sorrow. Their wails can shatter ice and sanity alike. In the center, a frozen fountain weeps not water but tears of memory. Drinking from the fountain restores a random memory lost elsewhere in the Library but inflicts a random memory loss in exchange. The banshees are not immediately hostile; they will answer one question truthfully for each memory offered to the fountain.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Surface",
            "The Hall of Frozen Tomes"
          ]
        },
        "The Styx Seep": {
          "description": "A crack in the Library's lowest floor where the River Styx seeps in, forming a black, sluggish pool. The pool's waters erase memory on contact, and several lost souls — devils who fell in and forgot everything — wander aimlessly nearby. A small dock of bone allows a merrenoloth ferry to occasionally dock, but no ferryman will stay long. The Seep is rumored to hold a passage to the deeper archive: the 'Well of Forgotten Names', where the true names of mortal souls are recorded.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Frozen Tomes"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Chill Archivist (head banshee librarian)",
        "Spectral librarians (banshees)",
        "Ice mephit guardians",
        "Lost souls of the Styx Seep"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Vault of True Names contains not only the archdevils' names but also the 'Oath of Broken Chains' — a legal document drafted by the original Stygian court that outlines the lawful method for deposing an Archduke. If this document were presented to the Infernal Court of Asmodeus, it could legally compel the release of Levistus. The Chill Archivist knows this and will trade the document for a complete set of memories from a truly happy mortal life — something no devil can provide.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Crystal Fortress": {
      "name": "The Crystal Fortress",
      "basicInfo": "The primary stronghold of the gelugons, the ice devils of Stygia. Carved from a single, colossal crystal of perma-ice, the Fortress rises from the frozen sea like a jagged tooth. It serves as the military command center for Stygia's defenses, where the legions of ice devils train and coordinate patrols of the frozen wastes. The Fortress is commanded by General Frostbite, a gelugon of immense size and cunning, who answers directly to Levistus (despite his imprisonment) and enforces the layer's brutal martial law. The interior is a maze of ice corridors, barracks, and strategic war rooms, kept at a temperature so low that even devils from warmer layers find it uncomfortable. The Fortress is also the primary breeding ground for ice mephits and the training academy for young gelugons.",
      "x": 19,
      "y": 8,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Stygia — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Ramparts of Frost": {
          "description": "The outer defenses, a wall of jagged crystal fifty feet high, lined with ballistas that fire bolts of solid cold. The ramparts are constantly manned by gelugon sentries who can see through blizzards. Entry is through a single gate of enchanted ice that opens only for those who speak the correct passphrase — a riddle that changes daily. The ground around the Fortress is littered with the frozen bodies of failed attackers, their forms preserved as statues.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Hall of War",
            "The Barracks of the Frozen Legion"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Hall of War": {
          "description": "The central chamber of the Fortress, a vast space with a ceiling of transparent ice that shows the dark sky above. A massive table of black ice in the center displays a three-dimensional map of Stygia, with troop positions marked by glowing blue runes. General Frostbite holds council here, surrounded by his subordinate officers. The walls are lined with frozen trophies from defeated demon lords. A permanent scrying portal in the ceiling allows the General to communicate with Tartarus.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ramparts of Frost",
            "General Frostbite's Sanctum",
            "The Hatchery"
          ]
        },
        "General Frostbite's Sanctum": {
          "description": "The private quarters of the gelugon commander, a chamber of perfect, smooth ice that reflects his cold, calculating nature. A throne of frozen bones sits at the center, and a weapon rack holds his personal glaive, 'Winter's Bite', a +3 icy burst weapon. Frostbite is a master strategist and a brutal enforcer; he keeps a collection of the broken insignia of officers he has executed for failure. A hidden safe contains his secret correspondence with Belial, for whom he spies on Levistus.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Hall of War"
          ]
        },
        "The Barracks of the Frozen Legion": {
          "description": "A sprawling network of ice tunnels and chambers where the rank-and-file gelugons live and train. The air rings with the clash of weapons and the crack of ice. A massive mess hall serves frozen rations, and a punishment block holds disobedient devils in ice cells. The barracks also house the 'Chillforge', where ice weapons are crafted from the heart-ice of ancient glaciers — a process that requires the sacrifice of a living creature's warmth.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ramparts of Frost",
            "The Chillforge"
          ]
        },
        "The Chillforge": {
          "description": "A forge of ice and cold energy where master smiths — captured azers adapted to cold — craft weapons of living ice. The forge is powered by a bound ice para-elemental, its heart pulsing with blue light. Weapons created here bypass fire resistance and deal cold damage. The forge's chief smith, an azer named Glacia (who was transformed by Stygian magic), is a bitter slave who will trade secrets for a promise of freedom. The weapons are distributed to the legion.",
          "paths": [
            "The Barracks of the Frozen Legion"
          ]
        },
        "The Hatchery": {
          "description": "A deep, warm cave (by Stygian standards) where ice mephits are bred from frozen eggs. The hatchery is tended by a group of night hags who have a contract with the Fortress. The eggs are harvested from the bodies of slain ice elementals, and the mephits are trained from birth to serve as messengers, scouts, and cannon fodder. A permanent cloud of icy vapor shrouds the area, and the chittering of young mephits is constant.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Hall of War"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "General Frostbite (Gelugon commander)",
        "The Frozen Legion (gelugon soldiers)",
        "Glacia (Azer smith)",
        "Night hag mephit breeders"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "General Frostbite's secret correspondence with Belial is not just opportunistic — he is Belial's illegitimate son, sired during the Reckoning. Belial has promised him the throne of Stygia if he can deliver Levistus's prison key. Frostbite searches for it obsessively, unaware that his own mother (an ice devil who died birthing him) was a keeper of the Lost Library, and her frozen spirit there knows the key's location.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Howling Abyss": {
      "name": "The Howling Abyss",
      "basicInfo": "A great chasm in the frozen ocean where the ice splits open, revealing the black, starless void below. The Abyss is a place of absolute darkness and freezing wind that screams through the crack with a sound like a chorus of the damned. It is believed to be a wound in Stygia's reality, a rift that leads not to another layer but to the space between planes. The Howling Abyss is avoided by all but the most desperate, for it is home to the 'Winds of Nothing' — invisible entities that feed on warmth and memory. Yet, at the very bottom of the chasm, a single structure clings to the wall: the 'Monastery of the Void', where a handful of exiled ice devils meditate on oblivion. Some say the Abyss is the source of Stygia's cold, the breath of a dead god.",
      "x": -25,
      "y": -34,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Stygia",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Edge of Warmth": {
          "description": "The lip of the chasm, where a line of warm(er) air meets the freezing wind, creating a constant fog of ice crystals. Devils who approach feel an unnatural urge to turn back, a whisper in their minds promising warmth and safety elsewhere. The ground is cracked and treacherous, and several broken bridges dangle into the darkness. A small shrine to Levistus, carved from a single block of black ice, stands at the edge — a place where the desperate leave offerings for the archduke's protection.",
          "paths": [
            "The Descent of Screams",
            "The Monastery of the Void"
          ]
        },
        "The Descent of Screams": {
          "description": "A treacherous path carved into the chasm wall, barely wide enough for one person. The screaming wind tears at climbers, and the cold intensifies with every step down. Strange, translucent shapes — the Winds of Nothing — flicker at the edge of vision, draining the warmth from those they touch. A series of ancient, iron platforms, half-rusted, provide precarious rest stops. The platforms are inscribed with prayers to dead gods, placed there by earlier explorers who never returned.",
          "paths": [
            "The Edge of Warmth",
            "The Monastery of the Void"
          ]
        },
        "The Monastery of the Void": {
          "description": "A cluster of ice buildings clinging to the chasm wall halfway down, built by a sect of gelugons who have rejected Hell's hierarchy and now seek enlightenment through absolute emptiness. The monks, led by an ancient gelugon named Zero, have removed their own emotions and desires through a ritual of exposure to the Winds. They speak in monotones and offer shelter to travelers — but only if they agree to share a memory, which the monks feed to the Winds. The monastery contains a library of silence, a meditation hall of pure ice, and a portal to the Plane of Vacuum (the Void).",
          "paths": [
            "The Edge of Warmth",
            "The Descent of Screams",
            "The Chamber of Zero"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of Zero": {
          "description": "The heart of the monastery, a perfectly spherical room of clear ice where absolute silence reigns. Gelugon monks sit in concentric circles around a central point — a single mote of absolute darkness, a fragment of the Void. Gazing into the mote erases one's memories gradually, starting with the most painful. The head monk Zero can be found here, his body almost transparent from centuries of meditation. He offers a simple bargain: one year of meditation in exchange for one answer about the nature of the multiverse.",
          "paths": [
            "The Monastery of the Void"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Zero (ancient gelugon abbot)",
        "Gelugon monks of the Void",
        "Winds of Nothing (hungry spirits)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The fragment of the Void in the Chamber of Zero is not merely a philosophical tool — it is a piece of the Shard of Ultimate Annihilation, a legendary artifact created by the baernoloths. The monks inadvertently protect it by containing its hunger. If removed from the monastery, the Shard would begin devouring the plane, starting with Stygia. Levistus knows this and has a plan to use it to threaten Asmodeus, but he needs the monks' cooperation — and they have no desires left to bargain with.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Shipbreaker's Reach": {
      "name": "The Shipbreaker's Reach",
      "basicInfo": "A stretch of the frozen Stygian coast where the currents of the Styx and the freezing sea clash, creating a graveyard of sunken ships. The Reach is littered with the wreckage of infernal barges, mortal vessels dragged into Hell, and even the frozen corpse of a kraken. It is the primary port for those traveling the Styx into Stygia, though 'port' is a generous term: a few rickety docks of black ice, a shantytown of salvagers, and the ever-present threat of ice pirates. The Reach is ruled by the Ice Pirate Queen, a marilith named Vrostha who was banished from the Abyss and has carved out a domain here, preying on ferries. She operates from the largest wreck, a galleon called the 'Bitter End', which is frozen upright in the ice.",
      "x": -47,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Stygia — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Docks of Black Ice": {
          "description": "A series of precarious piers built from frozen wreckage, where merrenoloth ferries grudgingly stop to offload passengers and cargo. The docks are a chaotic marketplace: a nycaloth arms dealer sells weapons, a night hag offers memory vials, and a duergar salvager trades in scrap metal. A permanent mist of freezing spray hangs over the area. The 'Harbormaster' is a chain devil named Rust, who collects docking fees for Vrostha.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bitter End",
            "The Shantytown of Frozen Hope",
            "The Kraken's Ribs"
          ]
        },
        "The Bitter End": {
          "description": "Vrostha's flagship, a galleon from a material plane world that was dragged into Stygia during a foolish attempt to sail the Styx. Now frozen upright in the ice, its hull has been converted into a palace of ice and bone. Vrostha holds court in the captain's cabin, surrounded by her ice pirate crew — a mix of exiled devils, captured sailors, and cold-resistant demons. She offers passage through the Reach, salvage rights, and plunder from her raids, for a share of the take. A hidden treasure hold beneath the ship contains her accumulated hoard.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks of Black Ice",
            "Vrostha's Treasure Hold"
          ]
        },
        "Vrostha's Treasure Hold": {
          "description": "A frozen vault beneath the Bitter End, accessible only by a secret door in the ship's bilge. Inside, Vrostha stores her loot: soul coins, weapons, and a map to the Lost Library (which she has never dared to pursue). The hold is guarded by a half-tamed remorhaz that sleeps coiled around the treasure. The map is Vrostha's most prized possession; she will trade it for a vial of demon ichor from the Abyss, as she wishes to return home.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bitter End"
          ]
        },
        "The Shantytown of Frozen Hope": {
          "description": "A collection of lean-tos and tents made from sailcloth and wreckage, clustered near the docks. Here, mortal souls who escaped slavery or shipwreck live in miserable poverty, fishing for the ghostly fish that swim in the Styx. A hidden chapel to Valkur (god of sailors) offers a sliver of hope. The shantytown is protected by a blind, elderly human wizard named Caldir, who was once an adventurer and now defends the helpless with cantrips and courage.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks of Black Ice"
          ]
        },
        "The Kraken's Ribs": {
          "description": "The frozen corpse of a massive kraken, its body half-submerged in the ice at the edge of the Reach. The ribs form a natural cathedral of bone, and ice crystals grow from its skin. The area is infested with ice mephits and the occasional frost worm. Inside the kraken's skull, a single, still-living eye remains frozen — it can be used as a scrying focus to view any location on the Styx. The eye is guarded by a ghostly crew of the kraken's last victims.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks of Black Ice"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Vrostha (Marilith Ice Pirate Queen)",
        "Ice pirate crew",
        "Caldir (Blind wizard protector)",
        "Rust (Chain devil harbormaster)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Vrostha's map to the Lost Library is not complete — it shows a safe route through the Howling Abyss, but the final segment is missing. That segment is held by the blind wizard Caldir, who found it in a wreck and keeps it as a bargaining chip. He will trade it for a promise to free the souls in the shantytown from Stygia, a seemingly impossible task that might be accomplished by the Freedom Coin from Minauros.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Sepulchre of the Damned": {
      "name": "The Sepulchre of the Damned",
      "basicInfo": "Glasya's seat of power, a colossal mausoleum of black bone, petrified sinew, and crystallised tears that rises from the fleshy plains of Malbolge like a festering tooth. The Sepulchre is simultaneously a palace, a prison, and a monument to the suffering that fuels Glasya's rule. Every surface is carved with scenes of betrayal, and the air is thick with the scent of embalming fluid and despair. Glasya holds court here on a throne of fused skulls, surrounded by her executioners, her erinyes tribunal, and the ever-watchful portraits of Asmodeus. The Sepulchre also houses the Hall of Final Judgment, where she dispenses infernal law with cruel precision, and the Silent Galleries, where the petrified bodies of failed assassins serve as a warning. The entire structure is a living entity, its walls pulsing with a slow heartbeat, and it is said that only those who have been betrayed or have betrayed another may find the entrance.",
      "x": -12,
      "y": -9,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Malbolge",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gate of Oaths": {
          "description": "The main entrance, a massive archway formed by two intertwined stone figures — a devil and a mortal, forever locked in a handshake that is slowly crushing their arms. To pass, one must speak a confession of a true betrayal they have committed. The gate judges honesty; liars are petrified and added to the Silent Galleries. Two erinyes in crimson half-plate, faces obscured by veils of chainmail, serve as silent guards. Beyond the gate, a long, carpeted corridor of flesh leads into the heart of the palace.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of Torment",
            "The Silent Galleries",
            "The Hall of Final Judgment"
          ]
        },
        "The Throne of Torment": {
          "description": "Glasya's audience chamber, a towering hall where the floor is made of the fused, still-conscious faces of traitors. She sits on a throne carved from the skulls of every devil who has tried to usurp her, their empty eye sockets weeping black ichor. Glasya herself appears as a devil of breathtaking beauty, her skin pale as alabaster, her horns wreathed in a crown of poisoned thorns. She listens to petitions with an expression of amused cruelty, occasionally gesturing for an executioner to remove a petitioner's tongue. The walls are draped with the contract of the Pact Primeval, inscribed in glowing infernal script.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Oaths",
            "The Garden of Writhing Delights"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Final Judgment": {
          "description": "A vast courtroom where Glasya presides over disputes between devils, mortals, and the damned. The room is a macabre parody of a mortal court: the benches are made of bound lemures, the witness stand is a rack, and the jury box contains twelve trapped souls who are forced to vote with Glasya or be tortured. A bone devil bailiff maintains order with a whip of frozen nerves. Proceedings are recorded in blood by a blind notary. The verdict is always the same — guilty, with a sentence of creative suffering. From here, the condemned are led to the fleshy tunnels that eventually connect to the Prison of the Apostate.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Oaths"
          ]
        },
        "The Silent Galleries": {
          "description": "A corridor of alcoves, each containing a petrified statue of a would-be assassin, traitor, or failed underling. The statues are arranged in chronological order, a history of Glasya's paranoia. Some are missing limbs, others are frozen mid-scream. A faint, collective whisper of regret fills the air. The gallery leads to the Garden of Writhing Delights and is patrolled by invisible stalkers who ensure no one lingers too long.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Oaths",
            "The Garden of Writhing Delights"
          ]
        },
        "The Garden of Writhing Delights": {
          "description": "A grotesque pleasure garden behind the Sepulchre, where the plants are made of writhing flesh, the flowers are blinking eyes, and the fountains spray blood. Glasya retreats here to relax, surrounded by her handmaidens — succubi and erinyes who gossip about infernal intrigue. The garden is a maze of hedges that scream when trimmed. At its centre is a single, perfect rose that blooms only when a mortal soul is willingly betrayed; Glasya uses it to test the loyalty of her courtiers. The garden is a favourite place for her to hatch plots, and the plants themselves act as listening devices for the archduchess.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of Torment",
            "The Silent Galleries"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Glasya (Archduchess of Malbolge)",
        "The Executioners (Glasya's personal guard, horned devils in crimson plate)",
        "The Erinyes Tribunal (prosecutors and judges)",
        "The Garden's whispering flora"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The single perfect rose in the Garden of Writhing Delights is not merely a test of loyalty — it is a phylactery that contains the soul of Glasya's first mortal lover, whom she betrayed to gain Asmodeus's favour. As long as the rose blooms, Glasya is protected from permanent death. If the rose is destroyed with the weapon that killed that lover (which is preserved in the Hall of Final Judgment as an exhibit), Glasya would become mortal for a single hour.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Flesh Mills": {
      "name": "The Flesh Mills",
      "basicInfo": "A sprawling industrial complex where the terrain of Malbolge is literally manufactured from the bodies of damned souls and captured demons. The Mills are a nightmare of grinding gears, vats of boiling protoplasm, and conveyor belts of sinew that stretch across the fleshy plains. Here, the raw materials of punishment are processed: souls are rendered into bricks, muscles are stretched into pavement, and eyes are canned for scrying devices. The Mills are operated by a caste of barbazu and chain devils under the direction of the pit fiend overseer Rendal. A permanent mist of atomised blood hangs over the area, and the noise — the screams of the processed, the grind of machinery — is deafening. The Mills are the source of Malbolge's ever-changing, living geography, and Glasya ensures that any terrain that displeases her is melted down and reshaped.",
      "x": 3,
      "y": 13,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Malbolge — West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Receiving Vats": {
          "description": "A series of enormous iron cauldrons where newly arrived souls and demon prisoners are unloaded from Styx barges and dumped into a bubbling, organic slurry. The vats are heated by hellfire and stirred by enslaved fire giants. The souls scream as they are dissolved, their identities stripped away. The air is thick with steam and the smell of boiling flesh. A gangway of bone allows overseers to inspect the process. The vats are the primary intake point for the Mills.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rendering Hall",
            "The Overseer's Platform"
          ]
        },
        "The Rendering Hall": {
          "description": "A vast hall where the dissolved soul-stuff is pumped through a network of veins into moulds and presses. Here, the pulp is shaped into bricks of flesh, sheets of skin, and coils of hair. The noise of the presses is rhythmic, almost hypnotic. Workers — lemures and lesser devils — operate the machinery under the whip. A side chapel dedicated to the 'Divinity of Production' (a mockery of mortal religion) offers sacrifices of broken machine parts. The hall is overseen by a chain devil named Pulveriser, who takes pride in his work's uniformity.",
          "paths": [
            "The Receiving Vats",
            "The Packaging Wing"
          ]
        },
        "The Packaging Wing": {
          "description": "The output end of the Mills, where finished products are sorted, labelled, and loaded onto wagons of bone for distribution across Malbolge. The Wing is a logistics hub, staffed by erinyes clerks who maintain meticulous records of every brick, every sheet. A black market operates in a corner, where workers smuggle out scraps of soul-flesh for use in illegal alchemy. The foreman, a bone devil named Ledger, is incorruptible — or so it seems.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rendering Hall",
            "The Overseer's Platform"
          ]
        },
        "The Overseer's Platform": {
          "description": "A suspended platform of iron and sinew high above the Mills, where Rendal the pit fiend monitors operations. The platform offers a panoramic view of the entire industrial complex. Rendal is a creature of efficiency and sadism: he rewards productivity with a longer work shift (as the work itself is torture) and punishes failure by feeding the offender into the vats. A secret panel in the platform's floor hides a trapdoor that leads to a private escape tunnel — Rendal is not without his own paranoia.",
          "paths": [
            "The Receiving Vats",
            "The Packaging Wing"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Rendal (Pit fiend overseer)",
        "Pulveriser (Chain devil foreman)",
        "Erinyes logistics clerks",
        "Smuggling ring"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Ledger the bone devil is corrupt. He has been secretly siphoning high-quality soul-flesh to a coven of night hags who use it to create doppelgangers. These doppelgangers replace influential mortals on the Material Plane, driving them to make Faustian bargains. Ledger reports directly to Glasya, who uses the scheme to expand her mortal influence. The smuggled flesh contains a tracking agent that allows Glasya to scry on anyone who consumes it.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Prison of the Apostate": {
      "name": "The Prison of the Apostate",
      "basicInfo": "A maximum-security prison for devils and souls who have broken infernal contracts. The Prison is a vertical shaft of iron cells suspended over a pit of acidic lymph, located on the outskirts of the fleshy plains. Each cell is a cage of cold iron that suppresses magic and telepathy. The prisoners are political enemies of Glasya, traitors to the Nine Hells, and souls who dared to escape their bargains. The Warden, a pit fiend named Jailer-Above-All, rules with absolute authority and is one of the few devils who can override Glasya's orders — he answers only to Asmodeus. Escape is unheard of, but the prison also holds a secret: the 'Cell of Echoes', where a former archduke is rumoured to be held in eternal silence.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -46,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Malbolge — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Entrance Foyer": {
          "description": "A sterile, white marble lobby that contrasts sharply with the rest of Malbolge. The foyer is staffed by erinyes receptionists who check credentials with cold efficiency. A massive iron door sealed with nine locks leads to the cell block. The walls bear a single inscription: 'All who enter here have already confessed.' The foyer is also where legal counsel (a chain devil advocate) meets clients before sentencing. The floor is polished so bright that it reflects the darkness above.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cell Block",
            "The Advocate's Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The Advocate's Chamber": {
          "description": "A small, soundproofed room adjacent to the Entrance Foyer. Here, a chain devil advocate — known simply as 'Counsel' — meets with prisoners prior to their hearings. The walls are lined with law books bound in the skin of perjurers. Counsel is a master of infernal law who can twist the wording of any contract, but his services are available only to those who can pay in secrets. A single desk, two chairs, and a ever-burning candle (to mark billable hours) furnish the room. The advocate's loyalty is to the letter of the law, not to his clients.",
          "paths": [
            "The Entrance Foyer"
          ]
        },
        "The Cell Block": {
          "description": "A towering shaft of cells, each a cage of iron bars and silence. Prisoners are held in solitary confinement, fed through tubes, and never see another living being. The cells are numbered by the severity of their crime, with the lowest numbers (those who betrayed Asmodeus) held at the bottom, nearest the acid pit. The block is patrolled by invisible jailers who can phase through walls. A constant, low hum of suffering permeates the air. Occasionally, a prisoner is extracted for 'rehabilitation' — a trip to the Pit of Reckoning.",
          "paths": [
            "The Entrance Foyer",
            "The Pit of Reckoning",
            "The Cell of Echoes"
          ]
        },
        "The Pit of Reckoning": {
          "description": "A deep, conical pit at the base of the cell block, filled with boiling acid that smells of burnt soul. Prisoners who have been condemned to final punishment are hurled in from a platform above. They dissolve slowly, their existence erased from infernal records. The pit is overseen by a single, silent bone devil who records each execution with a quill made from a vrock's feather. The walls are scarred with the claw marks of the last moments of thousands.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cell Block"
          ]
        },
        "The Cell of Echoes": {
          "description": "A single, maximum-security cell at the very bottom of the block, accessible only by a warded elevator. Inside, a figure sits cross-legged on the floor, its body wrapped in chains of silence and its face obscured by a hood of lead. This is (rumour says) the imprisoned Hag Countess, the previous ruler of Malbolge, who was not destroyed but bound by Glasya as a trophy and a source of wisdom. The cell is completely silent, save for the slow, rhythmic breathing of the prisoner. The walls are etched with prophecies of the Day of Unbinding, when all contracts will be void.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cell Block"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Jailer-Above-All (Pit fiend warden)",
        "Erinyes legal advocates",
        "Invisible jailers",
        "The Prisoner of Echoes (the Hag Countess)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Hag Countess is not truly imprisoned — she is a willing oracle, trading her knowledge of Malbolge's secrets for the promise that Glasya will one day restore her to power over a different layer. The prophecies on the walls are her way of manipulating future events. She has foreseen a mortal agent who will retrieve the 'Key of Seven Agonies' (which opens all cells) and will guide that agent to the Prison's heart in exchange for a vial of Glasya's blood.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Viper's Nest": {
      "name": "The Viper's Nest",
      "basicInfo": "The covert training academy for Glasya's assassins, hidden within a warren of tunnels beneath the fleshy plains. Here, the most promising erinyes and mortal soul-slaves are trained in the arts of infiltration, poison, and silent murder. The Nest is a labyrinth of trapped corridors, illusion chambers, and dissection theatres. The instructors are a cadre of night hags and veteran assassins who have never failed a contract — because failure means being fed to the Pit. The Nest is also where Glasya's signature poison, 'Glasya's Kiss', is brewed from the tears of betrayed lovers. Graduates are sent to the Material Plane to eliminate enemies of Hell, collect souls, and sow discord. The entrance is a seemingly innocuous, weeping sore on the landscape.",
      "x": 2,
      "y": -38,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Malbolge — Far East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Sore Entrance": {
          "description": "A pulsating, organic opening in the ground, resembling an infected wound. To enter, one must press a hand into the sore and speak a vow of loyalty to Glasya. The entrance closes behind, sealing the initiate in darkness. A steep, fleshy staircase descends, the walls wet with a caustic mucus. The air smells of copper and sweat. At the bottom, a training ground of bone and rubber-like flesh awaits.",
          "paths": [
            "The Poison Kitchen",
            "The Gauntlet of Shadows",
            "The Dissection Theatre"
          ]
        },
        "The Poison Kitchen": {
          "description": "A laboratory where the night hag coven 'The Sorrowful Sisters' brews toxins. Shelves are lined with jars of preserved emotions, vials of ichor, and the crystallised tears used for Glasya's Kiss. A central cauldron simmers with a broth of nightmare fish. The head hag, Auntie Blight, is a master alchemist who can concoct a poison to kill a pit fiend — or a mortal without leaving a trace. She will trade formulas for rare ingredients, such as a solar's feather or the laughter of a happy child.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sore Entrance",
            "The Gauntlet of Shadows"
          ]
        },
        "The Gauntlet of Shadows": {
          "description": "A training course that mimics mortal cities, filled with magical darkness, illusionary walls, and animated target dummies that scream when struck. Trainee assassins must navigate the course while avoiding detection and eliminating 'marks'. Instructors — invisible stalkers and erinyes — grade each run. Failure results in a dose of agony poison. A leaderboard carved into a bone slab lists the top assassins, and the current champion is a soul named Silhouette, a former mortal shadow dancer.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sore Entrance",
            "The Poison Kitchen",
            "The Dissection Theatre"
          ]
        },
        "The Dissection Theatre": {
          "description": "A grim amphitheatre where captured demons, devils, and mortals are dissected alive to teach anatomy and interrogation techniques. The theatre is run by a chain devil surgeon named Carver, who lectures while he cuts. The audience is filled with trainees taking notes. A 'library' of preserved organs, each labelled with the victim's name and the method of extraction, lines the walls. The theatre is also used for 'graduation ceremonies', where trainees must perform a flawless dissection on a still-living victim.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sore Entrance",
            "The Gauntlet of Shadows"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Silhouette (champion assassin)",
        "Auntie Blight (night hag alchemist)",
        "Carver (chain devil surgeon)",
        "Erinyes instructors"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Silhouette is not merely the top graduate — she is a double agent for the Harpers, who was captured and turned by Glasya but secretly retains her mortal soul and memory through a magical amulet hidden in her cell. She plans to assassinate Glasya using a poison she has been developing in secret — a variant of Glasya's Kiss that is harmless to devils but deadly to the archduchess herself. She needs one final ingredient: the kiss of a true lover, which she cannot obtain in Hell.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Field of Regret": {
      "name": "The Field of Regret",
      "basicInfo": "A desolate expanse of Malbolge's living terrain where the ground is composed of the twisted bodies of those who died with profound regret. Here, the damned are bound face-up, their expressions frozen in eternal sorrow. The Field serves as a monument to missed opportunities, and a place where Glasya punishes souls who broke infernal contracts with good intentions — 'for their own good' betrayals. Those who walk the Field must tread upon the faces of the regretful, feeling a ghost of their sorrow with each step. It is said that anyone who lies down in the Field will be absorbed, their body added to the terrain, but also that a single flower of hope blooms here once every thousand years — a blossom that can absolve one regret of the living.",
      "x": -49,
      "y": 23,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Malbolge — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Edge of Sorrow": {
          "description": "The border of the Field, marked by a line of stone pillars shaped like weeping women. A faint psychic weight presses on the mind, urging visitors to turn back. A single, ancient tree of black bone stands here, its branches hung with the rotting remains of those who attempted to cross the Field without purpose. A guide — a mournful erinyes named Elegy — offers to accompany travellers for a story of true regret. The ground is soft, like walking on flesh.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weeping Plains",
            "The Shrine of the Lost"
          ]
        },
        "The Weeping Plains": {
          "description": "The main body of the Field, an endless-seeming stretch of upturned, sorrowful faces. The ground beneath is lumpy and warm, and the air is filled with a collective, silent sigh. Souls here are not in eternal torture; they are in eternal contemplation of their mistakes. Visitors experience flashes of the regrets of those they step on. A path of smooth obsidian, the 'Path of Reluctance', winds through, sparing the walker from direct contact, but the path is said to lengthen if the walker's purpose is impure.",
          "paths": [
            "The Edge of Sorrow",
            "The Shrine of the Lost"
          ]
        },
        "The Shrine of the Lost": {
          "description": "A small, domed structure of woven sinew at the Field's centre. Inside, an altar holds a single, perpetually dripping candle — the 'Light of What-If'. Pilgrims (rare devils or mortals seeking absolution) can whisper a regret to the candle, which will burn brighter or dimmer depending on the sincerity of the confession. If the regret is truly selfless, the candle may shed a tear: a single drop that is the 'Flower of Hope' — a crystallised tear that can be used to undo one small, regretted action in the mortal world. The shrine is tended by a silent, hooded figure that never moves but is sometimes missing.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weeping Plains"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Elegy (Erinyes guide)",
        "The Silent Tender (entity, possibly a shard of Glasya)",
        "The Regretful Damned (passive)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Flower of Hope is real, but its power has a catch: the regret it undoes must be replaced by another, equally profound regret from the one who uses it. The Silent Tender is a manifestation of Glasya's own buried remorse — her regret for betraying her first lover. If someone could convince Glasya to confess to the candle, the entire Field of Regret would be undone, and the souls within would be freed. The candle's tear that falls for Glasya would be a weapon capable of killing an archdevil, forged from her own remorse.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Malagard": {
      "name": "Malagard",
      "basicInfo": "The crumbling capital of Maladomini, a sprawling, decaying metropolis of shattered spires, broken bridges, and half-finished palaces. Every building in Malagard is a monument to failure: the Archduke Baalzebul, cursed by Asmodeus, can never complete his grand designs. The city is a maze of abandoned construction, where scaffolds rot and marble cracks before it can be set. At the city's center lies the Shrine of the Broken Throne, where Baalzebul holds court in a perpetual state of wounded pride. The streets are flooded with black sludge, the air buzzes with clouds of fat flies, and the wails of frustrated architects echo through the ruins. Despite the decay, Malagard is the bureaucratic heart of the seventh layer, where infernal nobles jockey for Baalzebul's favour and the souls of the damned are processed into a workforce that never finishes anything.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -8,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Maladomini",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gate of Unfinished Works": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Malagard, a colossal archway of half-carved marble flanked by two statues of Baalzebul — one with a missing arm, the other with a face only partially chiselled. The gates themselves are massive iron doors that hang crookedly, as their hinges were never properly forged. To enter, one must present a 'permit of passage', which the gate guards (barbazu with bureaucratic sashes) will inspect with ponderous slowness. The gates open into the Processional of Broken Dreams, a wide boulevard paved with cracked cobblestones.",
          "paths": [
            "The Processional of Broken Dreams",
            "The Shrine of the Broken Throne"
          ]
        },
        "The Processional of Broken Dreams": {
          "description": "The main thoroughfare of Malagard, a wide avenue lined with half-finished statues, empty plinths, and buildings whose upper floors are mere skeletons of iron and wood. The air is thick with the stench of decay and the buzzing of flies. Souls of the damned trudge along, hauling carts of rotten stone, endlessly working on projects that will never be completed. The street is patrolled by 'Overseers', cornugons with whips that crack like thunder, ensuring the work never stops. Along the way, small plazas feature defaced fountains that sputter black sludge instead of water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Unfinished Works",
            "The Shrine of the Broken Throne",
            "The Sludge Docks"
          ]
        },
        "The Shrine of the Broken Throne": {
          "description": "Baalzebul's palace-temple, a grotesque fusion of cathedral and fortress. Its spires are of varying heights, some snapped off halfway, others leaning at impossible angles. The entrance is a giant maw of jagged stone, its doors forever jammed open. Inside, the Archduke sits on a throne of shattered mirrors, his once-angelic form now a bloated, slug-like mass of filth and grandeur. The floor is littered with the discarded petitions of supplicants, slowly dissolving in the slime. Baalzebul communicates through a chorus of buzzing flies that form words in the air. The Shrine is the center of Maladomini's bureaucracy; side chambers house the 'Ministers of Ruin', pit fiend nobles who manage the layer's affairs.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Unfinished Works",
            "The Processional of Broken Dreams",
            "The Hall of Echoing Ambition"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Echoing Ambition": {
          "description": "A vast, roofless hall adjacent to the Shrine, where the souls of those who made Faustian bargains are forced to plan their own monuments. They scribble on rotting parchment, draw on crumbling walls, and shout orders to phantom workers. Every design is flawed, every dream a mockery, and the hall echoes with the sound of creative despair. A bone devil librarian, the 'Curator of Failures', catalogs the most spectacular disappointments. The hall is a warning to all who would seek power through infernal means.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shrine of the Broken Throne"
          ]
        },
        "The Sludge Docks": {
          "description": "Malagard's port, located on a tributary of the River Styx known as the 'Black Effluent'. Here, barges of garbage, raw soul-stuff, and construction materials arrive, only to be left to rot on the quays. The docks are a chaotic mess of sinking piers and overturned cargo. A merrenoloth ferryman, Charon's-Nephew, offers passage to other layers but demands payment in 'completed contracts', which are extremely rare in Maladomini. A shantytown of lost souls clings to the dock pilings.",
          "paths": [
            "The Processional of Broken Dreams"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Baalzebul (Archduke of Maladomini)",
        "Ministers of Ruin (pit fiend nobles)",
        "Overseers (cornugon enforcers)",
        "Charon's-Nephew (merrenoloth ferryman)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The shattered mirrors of Baalzebul's throne are not mere decor — they are fragments of his original angelic form's halo, which Asmodeus shattered when he cursed him. If the shards are reassembled, the curse could be broken, and Baalzebul would complete his greatest work: the 'Perfect City', which would be an infernal paradise capable of rivaling the Seven Heavens. The Curator of Failures secretly guards a piece of the halo and will trade it for a vision of a truly selfless act.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Scab": {
      "name": "The Scab",
      "basicInfo": "A mobile fortress that lurches across the sludge plains of Maladomini on a thousand rotting legs, resembling a colossal, festering louse. The Scab is the primary military garrison of the layer, housing the 'Legion of the Fly' — soldiers who have been mutated by Baalzebul's curse into humanoid insects. The fortress is a maze of chitin corridors, cocoon barracks, and the stench of formic acid. The general, a pit fiend named Verminus, answers only to Baalzebul. The Scab's movements are unpredictable, and its path is marked by a trail of sloughed-off carapace. It is used to enforce Baalzebul's will across the layer, crushing rebellions before they can be completed. The fortress also contains a breeding ground for the 'Hellwasps', infernal insects used as messengers and weapons.",
      "x": 12,
      "y": 29,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Maladomini — Far East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Underbelly": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Scab, located beneath the massive creature, accessible by climbing a ladder of fused bone that hangs from a pulsing orifice. The ground around the Scab is perpetually shadowed, and the stench of ammonia is overwhelming. Guards — insectoid barbazu with compound eyes — skitter along the legs, demanding the 'mandate of passage' (a written order from a Minister of Ruin). The orifice opens into a vertical shaft lined with pulsating tissue.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chitin Corridors",
            "The Hatchery"
          ]
        },
        "The Chitin Corridors": {
          "description": "The main transit network of the Scab, a labyrinth of tunnels with walls of slick, brown chitin. The floor trembles with the beast's movements, and the air is thick with the drone of wings. Signs written in infernal ichor point to various barracks and the command center. The tunnels periodically contract, and slow-moving visitors risk being crushed. Patrols of 'Vespidians' (insectoid devils with wasp-like features) enforce discipline with venomous stings.",
          "paths": [
            "The Underbelly",
            "Verminus's Command Chamber",
            "The Hellwasp Aviary"
          ]
        },
        "Verminus's Command Chamber": {
          "description": "A spherical room at the heart of the Scab, its walls made of translucent insect wings that show a distorted view of the outside. General Verminus, a pit fiend whose flesh has hardened into a beetle-like carapace, stands over a living map — a fleshy table that displays the Scab's current path and troop positions. He communicates through clicks and hisses, translated by a chain devil adjutant. The chamber also holds a 'hive-mind' telepathic node, allowing Verminus to command his troops directly.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chitin Corridors",
            "The Hatchery"
          ]
        },
        "The Hellwasp Aviary": {
          "description": "A vast, echoing chamber filled with hexagonal cells, each containing a giant hellwasp pupa. The aviary is tended by 'Larval Keepers', erinyes who have been stripped of their beauty and now have insectoid mandibles. Fully grown hellwasps — the size of horses, with stingers dripping infernal venom — patrol the upper reaches. The aviary's ceiling is a retractable membrane, allowing the wasps to swarm out when the Scab is attacked. The air is thick with a droning lullaby.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chitin Corridors"
          ]
        },
        "The Hatchery": {
          "description": "A warm, moist chamber deep in the Scab's bowels where new soldiers are bred. Giant cocoons hang from the ceiling, each containing a transforming soul or captured demon. The process is overseen by a night hag named Auntie Larva, who injects the cocoons with the 'Gift of the Fly Lord'. The hatchery is also where the Scab's own larvae are nurtured — a sight that can drive mortals to madness. Auntie Larva will trade knowledge of infernal mutations for 'fresh genetic material' from a mortal of notable lineage.",
          "paths": [
            "The Underbelly",
            "Verminus's Command Chamber"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "General Verminus (Pit fiend commander)",
        "Legion of the Fly (insectoid devils)",
        "Auntie Larva (Night hag geneticist)",
        "Hellwasp swarm"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Scab is not merely a fortress — it is a living weapon. If Baalzebul ever completes a grand work, the Scab will metamorphose into a giant fly, the 'Lord of the Flies', capable of carrying the Archduke's completed city into the Material Plane. The metamorphosis requires the sacrifice of a completed soul (one that has achieved its purpose), an impossibility in Maladomini. The key is a soul that has been redeemed — something Auntie Larva secretly hunts for.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Black Effluent": {
      "name": "The Black Effluent",
      "basicInfo": "The primary river of Maladomini, a foul tributary of the Styx that winds through the layer like an open wound. Its waters are a thick, black sludge that moves with painful slowness, carrying the detritus of abandoned construction, the corpses of failed projects, and the collective despair of the layer. The Effluent is navigable only by flat-bottomed boats poled by mute lemures, as the sludge is too dense for oars. The river's banks are dotted with 'Silt Palaces' — crumbling mansions of infernal nobles who have fallen from favour. The Effluent is also the layer's main conduit for the memory-eroding waters of the Styx, and a 'Fog of Oblivion' rises from its surface, erasing the memories of those who breathe it too long. At the river's widest point lies the 'Tower of the Drowned Lament', where the souls of architects who displeased Baalzebul are forever immersed.",
      "x": -49,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Maladomini",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Stygian Inlet": {
          "description": "The point where the true Styx meets the Black Effluent, a swirling maelstrom of black water and sludge. The inlet is guarded by a squadron of merrenoloths who demand a toll of 'one memory' to cross. A small dock of bone allows boats to transfer from the Styx to the Effluent. The air is thick with the Fog of Oblivion, and those who linger too long risk losing their identities. A faded sign reads: 'Abandon all progress, ye who enter here.'",
          "paths": [
            "The Silt Palaces",
            "The Tower of the Drowned Lament"
          ]
        },
        "The Silt Palaces": {
          "description": "A row of once-grand mansions that slump into the river, their façades cracked, their gardens drowned in sludge. These palaces belong to the 'Disgraced Dukes', pit fiends who failed Baalzebul and are now condemned to watch their homes slowly sink. The dukes host bitter salons, plotting impossible coups. A black market for stolen blueprints operates here, run by a disguised arcanaloth who calls herself 'The Draftswoman'. She sells plans for structures that could theoretically be completed, and she knows the secret of the Perfect City.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stygian Inlet",
            "The Tower of the Drowned Lament"
          ]
        },
        "The Tower of the Drowned Lament": {
          "description": "A twisted tower of black iron that rises from the center of the Effluent, accessible only by boat. Inside, the tower is a vertical prison. The souls of architects who designed the failed structures of Maladomini are chained to the walls, their heads held just above the rising sludge. They wail in chorus, a song of creative despair that can be heard for miles. The warden, a chain devil named 'The Critic', grades their suffering. At the top of the tower, a single, dry cell holds the 'Architect of the First Failure', a mortal who once drafted a plan that almost redeemed Baalzebul.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stygian Inlet",
            "The Silt Palaces"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Draftswoman (Arcanaloth information broker)",
        "Disgraced Dukes (fallen pit fiends)",
        "The Critic (chain devil warden)",
        "Merrenoloth ferrymen"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Architect of the First Failure holds the original draft of Baalzebul's Perfect City, which was not a plan for a physical place but a metaphysical concept: a 'City of Shared Purpose'. If that draft were read aloud in the Shrine of the Broken Throne, it would break the curse by completing the conceptual work. The Draftswoman knows this and wants to trade the draft for a copy of the Pact Primeval from Dis, but the Architect will only give it to someone who sings a song of true hope (anathema in Maladomini).",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Gluttony Pits": {
      "name": "The Gluttony Pits",
      "basicInfo": "A series of massive, bile-filled craters in the sludge plains where the souls of gluttons are punished. The Pits are a mockery of feasting: souls are force-fed a never-ending stream of rotting offal, only to regurgitate it and be fed again. The air is thick with the stench of vomit and decay, and the ground squelches with a mixture of bile and mud. The Pits are overseen by a massive, bloated pit fiend named Gormandizer, who sits on a throne of fused bones and demands tributes of 'exotic' foods (like the still-beating hearts of celestial beasts) from the lesser devils who serve him. Despite the horror, the Pits are also a source of rare alchemical ingredients: the 'Tears of Satiety' (crystallized vomit) and 'Bile of Regret' are valued by night hags across the Hells.",
      "x": 3,
      "y": 6,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Maladomini — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Feeding Trenches": {
          "description": "Long, sloping trenches filled with a knee-deep mixture of vomit and sludge. Souls of gluttons are chained in rows, their mouths held open by iron hooks as they are force-fed by mechanical, spider-like constructs that pump slurry directly into their throats. The noise is a cacophony of gagging, sobbing, and the squelch of machinery. The trenches are patrolled by 'Butcher Devils' (obese barbazu with cleavers) who keep order. At the end of each trench, a 'Draining Station' collects the regurgitated material for re-feeding.",
          "paths": [
            "Gormandizer's Throne",
            "The Alchemical Vats"
          ]
        },
        "Gormandizer's Throne": {
          "description": "A raised platform of fused bones overlooking the Feeding Trenches, where the bloated pit fiend Gormandizer sits on a throne carved from the skull of a celestial mammoth. He wears a bib made from the flayed skin of a failed chef, and his voice rumbles like an upset stomach. Gormandizer demands tributes of 'delicacies' — the rarer and more sentient, the better — and rewards his favourites with scraps of power. The throne is surrounded by a 'kitchen' where lemures prepare the 'meals' under the lash of a chain devil chef named Saucier.",
          "paths": [
            "The Feeding Trenches",
            "The Alchemical Vats"
          ]
        },
        "The Alchemical Vats": {
          "description": "A side cavern where night hags and duergar alchemists process the byproducts of the Pits. Great copper vats simmer with 'Tears of Satiety', 'Bile of Regret', and the more potent 'Essence of Gluttony', which can be used to brew potions of irresistible craving. The head alchemist, a night hag named Auntie Acidbelly, tests her concoctions on captive demons. She will buy any rare digestive byproduct (such as the stomach acid of a tarrasque) and sell her wares for soul coins or memories of exquisite meals.",
          "paths": [
            "The Feeding Trenches",
            "Gormandizer's Throne"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Gormandizer (Bloated pit fiend overseer)",
        "Saucier (Chain devil chef)",
        "Auntie Acidbelly (Night hag alchemist)",
        "Butcher Devils (barbazu enforcers)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "Gormandizer is not merely a glutton — he is a collector of culinary secrets. He believes that the 'Primordial Recipe' for the universe is hidden in the stomach of a dead god at the bottom of the Astral Plane. He has been secretly funding expeditions to find it, using the Tears of Satiety as currency. If he learns the Recipe, he could cook a dish that would make the consumer an archduke. His chef, Saucier, is actually an agent of Mephistopheles, sent to steal Gormandizer's research.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Slime Temple": {
      "name": "The Slime Temple",
      "basicInfo": "A hidden temple dedicated to Ghaunadaur, the Elder Eye, the god of oozes and abominations. Located in a deep, fetid swamp at the edge of Maladomini, the temple is a place of worship for those devils and souls who have abandoned the hierarchy of Hell in favour of primordial chaos. The temple is built from petrified ooze, its walls slick with a constantly regenerating layer of slime. It is ruled by a self-appointed high priest, a gibbering mouther named 'The Chanting Chaos', that is said to have consumed the original high priest and now speaks with his voice. The temple's central altar is a pool of 'Protean Slime', capable of transforming any creature into a chaos spawn. The temple is considered a blight even by the standards of Maladomini, and Baalzebul's forces are constantly hunting for it, but its location shifts as the swamp itself moves.",
      "x": 33,
      "y": -11,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Maladomini — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Slime Marsh": {
          "description": "The approach to the temple, a bog of thick, green-grey ooze and twisted, leafless trees. The marsh is filled with the croaking of unseen things, and the air is heavy with a sweet, rotting smell. The ground is treacherous, and many paths lead to stagnant pools where the unwary are absorbed. A single, winding path of solid, petrified slime leads to the temple entrance, guarded by two 'Slime Giants' — massive, humanoid mounds of protoplasm that form and dissolve.",
          "paths": [
            "The Slime Nave",
            "The Protean Pool"
          ]
        },
        "The Slime Nave": {
          "description": "The main worship hall, a cavernous space with walls of translucent, writhing slime. Rows of petrified ooze pews hold a congregation of gibbering worshipers: some are devils whose forms have melted, others are lost souls, all chanting in unison. The 'Chanting Chaos' — a massive gibbering mouther composed of dozens of faces and limbs — floats above the altar, its incoherent babble somehow forming words of prayer. The nave is lit by bioluminescent fungi that pulse in time with the chanting. The air is thick with spores that cause hallucinations of oneness with chaos.",
          "paths": [
            "The Slime Marsh",
            "The Protean Pool"
          ]
        },
        "The Protean Pool": {
          "description": "The altar of the temple, a pool of iridescent, shifting slime in a circular chamber. Any creature that immerses itself in the pool is subject to a 'Chaotic Transformation' — a random and usually horrific mutation. The Chanting Chaos uses it to 'bless' its followers, turning them into slime-spawned warriors. The pool is also a gateway to the 'Slime Pits of Ghaunadaur' in the Abyss. A vial of the pool's water is a powerful reagent for potions of polymorph, and alchemists will pay dearly for it, but removing it from the temple is nearly impossible as it tries to return.",
          "paths": [
            "The Slime Marsh",
            "The Slime Nave"
          ]
        },
        "The Chanting Chaos's Alcove": {
          "description": "A side chamber where the high priest retreats to commune with Ghaunadaur. The walls are covered in shifting symbols that form prophecies and then dissolve. Here, the Chanting Chaos — or rather, the mind of the consumed high priest, a former pit fiend named Murmur — will speak coherently to visitors. It offers the 'Blessing of the Eye' (an ability to see through lies) in exchange for a memory of order, such as a law or a contract. The alcove also holds a secret: the original high priest's soul is still alive inside the mouther, and can be freed with a dispel chaos spell.",
          "paths": [
            "The Slime Nave"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Chanting Chaos (Gibbering mouther high priest)",
        "Slime Giants and ooze-spawned defenders",
        "Congregation of the Lost (worshippers)",
        "Auntie Acidbelly (occasional customer for slime samples)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Chanting Chaos is not just a priest — it is a piece of Ghaunadaur himself, a fragment of the god that was severed during a battle with Asmodeus. If the pit fiend Murmur's soul is freed, the fragment will lose its anchor and dissolve, but Murmur will gift the liberator with a 'Tear of Ghaunadaur', a crystal of pure chaos that can dissolve any infernal contract without penalty. The tear is what Baalzebul's agents seek, as it could be used to undo the Archduke's curse.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Mephistar": {
      "name": "Mephistar",
      "basicInfo": "The palace of Mephistopheles, a towering citadel of blue-black ice and frozen hellfire built atop a volcanic vent on the Nargus, Cania's frozen sea. The palace is a marvel of infernal architecture, its spires shaped by mathematical incantations rather than physical construction. It is the warmest place in Cania, heated by the vent, and thus home to Mephistopheles's court of exiled nobility, arcanists, and petitioners. The Archduke himself dwells in the central spire, 'The Glacier of Dreams', where he conducts arcane research that rivals the gods. Mephistar is defended by frost giant guards, ice golems, and the 'Sons of Mephistopheles' — erinyes who have pledged their souls to his cold fire. The palace also houses the Grand Library of Cania, a repository of forbidden knowledge that Mephistopheles has collected over millennia, and the Hellfire Crucible, where he forges artifacts of immense power. Entry is forbidden without an invitation, and those who trespass are frozen into statues that decorate the approach.",
      "x": -13,
      "y": 9,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Cania",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Avenue of Frozen Supplicants": {
          "description": "The approach to Mephistar across the frozen Nargus sea. The wind screams across the ice, and the temperature is lethal to unprotected mortals. Lining the path are hundreds of ice statues — trespassers, failed petitioners, and rivals frozen in agonized poses, still alive and aware. A pair of massive frost giant sentries, clad in plate armor of enchanted permafrost, stand at the gate, demanding the 'Word of Entry', a passphrase that changes with the phases of Cania's non-existent moon (a riddle given to invited guests). The gate itself is an archway of pure, unmelting hellfire frozen into solid form.",
          "paths": [
            "The Crimson Spire",
            "The Grand Library",
            "The Hellfire Crucible"
          ]
        },
        "The Crimson Spire": {
          "description": "The throne room of Mephistopheles, a vast circular chamber of red ice where the Archduke holds court. He sits on a throne of frozen hellfire, his form a towering pit fiend with elegant, academic features and fingers like calligrapher's quills. The walls are covered in moving, living equations — Mephistopheles's ongoing research on the nature of divinity. To speak with him is to feel one's thoughts becoming sharper, then overwhelmed, then frozen solid. He communicates in precise, logical sentences and is prone to sudden, cold rages. A pool of 'Chilling Insight' at the room's center can reveal any mortal's true name.",
          "paths": [
            "The Avenue of Frozen Supplicants",
            "The Glacier of Dreams",
            "The Grand Library"
          ]
        },
        "The Glacier of Dreams": {
          "description": "Mephistopheles's private sanctum, a spire within a spire that is accessible only by a spiral staircase of compressed blizzard. Inside, the Archduke conducts his most dangerous experiments: the creation of the 'Eighth Word', a spell that would unravel the distinction between fire and ice, and the 'God Engine', a machine powered by the souls of dead gods that could theoretically kill an archdevil permanently. The chamber is a chaotic mess of alchemical apparatuses, floating books, and frozen lightning. The walls are transparent, showing the black void of Cania's sky. A single, star-shaped scar on the floor marks where Mephistopheles once attempted to summon a primordial and failed. The room is guarded by two 'Crystalline Sentinels', golems of black ice that reflect no light.",
          "paths": [
            "The Crimson Spire"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Library": {
          "description": "A library carved from a single iceberg, its shelves filled with books bound in the skin of frost giants, scrolls of ice that never melt, and crystalline memory-orbs. The Library's catalog includes the true names of countless devils, the secret histories of the Blood War, and a complete copy of the Pact Primeval (which Mephistopheles has annotated in contemptuous marginalia). The Head Librarian, an arcanaloth named Quillion the Cold, is a pedantic but oddly helpful scholar who will trade information for new knowledge. The library's forbidden section, the 'Vault of Frozen Blasphemies', holds texts so dangerous that reading them can freeze the soul.",
          "paths": [
            "The Avenue of Frozen Supplicants",
            "The Crimson Spire",
            "The Vault of Frozen Blasphemies"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of Frozen Blasphemies": {
          "description": "A sub-chamber of the Grand Library, sealed behind a door of solid anti-magic ice. Within are books that describe the death of gods, the formulas for unmaking planes, and the 'Enuma Elish' — an account of the creation of the multiverse that contradicts all known theology. The vault is protected by a curse that causes any unauthorized reader's blood to crystallize. Only Mephistopheles and Quillion know the command word to disable it. A single pedestal holds the 'Book of Eternal Winter', a tome that, if read aloud, would begin a new Ice Age across the Material Plane.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Library"
          ]
        },
        "The Hellfire Crucible": {
          "description": "Mephistopheles's forge, located at the base of the volcanic vent that heats the palace. Here, the Archduke and his bound fire giant smiths craft artifacts from the 'Cold Flame' — a paradoxical substance that burns at absolute zero. The forge is a massive basin of blue-and-white fire, its heat paradoxically draining warmth from the room. The chief smith, a fire giant named Surtr Coldheart (who was captured and magically adapted to cold), hammers out weapons of living frost. The Crucible is where Mephistopheles created his own 'Sword of Mephistopheles', a blade capable of sundering divine wards.",
          "paths": [
            "The Avenue of Frozen Supplicants"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Mephistopheles (Archduke of Cania)",
        "Quillion the Cold (Arcanaloth librarian)",
        "Surtr Coldheart (Fire giant smith)",
        "Sons of Mephistopheles (erinyes guard)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Mephistopheles's God Engine is almost complete. It requires one final component: the heart of a still-living god, which he plans to harvest from the Vestige of He Who Was in Nessus. He has a secret pact with Bel to acquire it, promising Bel a copy of the Eighth Word in exchange. If he succeeds, he will have the power to challenge Asmodeus directly, but the God Engine has a flaw: it can only be activated by a mortal soul that has willingly chosen to serve Mephistopheles, a near-impossible requirement in Cania.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cold Court": {
      "name": "The Cold Court",
      "basicInfo": "The social center of Cania, a sprawling complex of ice palaces and frozen gardens surrounding Mephistar, where the infernal nobility exiled from warmer layers gather. Here, pit fiends, erinyes, and outcast archdevils plot in whispered conversations, their words crystallizing in the air. The Court is ruled by the Countess of Cania, a powerful erinyes named 'Lady Glacia', who serves as Mephistopheles's seneschal and social enforcer. The Court's politics are brutally cold — both in temperature and in sentiment — and a single wrong word can lead to 'expulsion', which means being stripped of infernal protections and left to freeze on the Nargus. The centerpiece of the Court is the 'Sculpture Garden of Lost Ambitions', where failed schemers have been transformed into living ice sculptures.",
      "x": -13,
      "y": 15,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Cania — West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Mirrored Ballroom": {
          "description": "A great hall of ice mirrors where the nobility dances on a floor of frozen lightning, their steps accompanied by the wailing of captured frost spirits (a mockery of music). The mirrors show not reflections but the darkest ambitions of those who gaze into them. It is considered a compliment to see another's ambition in a mirror, and the highest social currency is the 'shared gaze' — a mutual viewing of each other's secret desires. Lady Glacia holds court from a throne of frozen blood, adjudicating disputes with a smile that never reaches her eyes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sculpture Garden of Lost Ambitions",
            "The Whispering Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Sculpture Garden of Lost Ambitions": {
          "description": "A courtyard of pure, still air, filled with dozens of ice statues. Each is a former courtier who failed in their schemes against Mephistopheles or Lady Glacia. They are frozen in mid-sentence, their expressions a mix of shock and horror. The statues are arranged in a maze, and courtiers walk among them to remind themselves of the price of failure. A gardener — a gelugon named 'The Sculptor' — maintains the garden, occasionally adding a new piece with a chisel of spite.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mirrored Ballroom",
            "Lady Glacia's Salon"
          ]
        },
        "Lady Glacia's Salon": {
          "description": "A private chamber adjacent to the Ballroom, decorated with the preserved wings of fallen celestial commanders. Here, Lady Glacia receives her most trusted (and useful) courtiers, exchanging secrets and favors over cups of warm, spiced blood. She is a master of intrigue, and her salon is the true seat of social power in the Cold Court. A permanent scrying mirror on one wall allows her to observe any part of the court. She is also the informal spymaster for Mephistopheles, reporting on disloyalty.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sculpture Garden of Lost Ambitions",
            "The Whispering Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Whispering Gallery": {
          "description": "A long corridor of ice where the voices of anyone speaking elsewhere in the court are magically carried. By walking the corridor and listening carefully, one can piece together conspiracies. The Gallery is a double-edged sword: its existence is known, so the clever speak in code, and the paranoid spread false rumors. A blind, ancient erinyes named 'Listener' sits at the Gallery's end, memorizing every whisper and reporting directly to Mephistopheles.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mirrored Ballroom",
            "Lady Glacia's Salon"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Lady Glacia (Erinyes Countess of Cania)",
        "The Sculptor (Gelugon gardener)",
        "Listener (Blind erinyes spy)",
        "Infernal nobles and exiles"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Lady Glacia is not merely a seneschal; she is Mephistopheles's daughter, sired during a brief, cold passion with a mortal sorceress who died during childbirth. Her mortal half-soul makes her capable of true emotion, which she has suppressed for millennia. If that half-soul were reawakened (perhaps by a powerful love), she would betray Mephistopheles and could be convinced to shut down the Cold Court's defenses, leaving the Archduke vulnerable. Her mortal mother's frozen locket is hidden in the Sculpture Garden, inside the statue of a particularly ambitious duke.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Nargus": {
      "name": "The Nargus",
      "basicInfo": "The frozen sea that covers most of Cania, a vast, white expanse of ice miles thick, broken only by the volcanic vent of Mephistar and the occasional ice citadel. The Nargus is not dead: beneath the ice, massive creatures swim in the liquid methane, and strange, frozen lights dance in the sky. It is a place of testing for those who seek Mephistopheles's favor, as the ice is riddled with natural traps and 'Glacial Rifts' that plunge into the dark. The Nargus is also home to the 'Hermits of the Ice', devils and souls who have rejected the court and now live in isolation, contemplating the cold. The frozen sea is a vital trade route, as sleds pulled by remorhaz traverse it, carrying goods between Mephistar and the layer's outposts.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -2,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Cania — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Remorhaz Docks": {
          "description": "A settlement carved into the edge of a massive glacial rift, where the giant, heat-producing remorhaz are corralled and harnessed to sleds. The docks are a rough-and-tumble place, staffed by frost barbarians (descendants of mortal worshippers who were brought to Cania millennia ago) and a few exiled duergar. They will hire out sleds and guides to traverse the Nargus, for a price in soul coins or warm furs. The air is filled with the chittering of the remorhaz and the smell of burning blubber.",
          "paths": [
            "The Glacial Rift",
            "The Hermit's Peak"
          ]
        },
        "The Glacial Rift": {
          "description": "A massive crevasse that splits the Nargus for miles, its walls shimmering with veins of 'Ice Diamonds' — crystallized souls who fell into the rift eons ago. The rift is traversed by bridges of bone and sinew, but the ice is unstable, and sudden shifts can crush travelers. Strange lights dance in the depths, and the echoes of a mournful song can be heard. A small shrine to Levistus, carved into the ice wall, marks where a group of his followers died trying to cross. The rift is a shortcut to the far side of the Nargus, but many have died attempting it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Remorhaz Docks",
            "The Hermit's Peak"
          ]
        },
        "The Hermit's Peak": {
          "description": "An isolated, jagged mountain of ice on the edge of the Nargus, where the 'Hermits of the Ice' dwell. These are outcast devils and lost souls who have chosen to live in absolute silence and cold, meditating on the nature of emptiness. Their leader, a pit fiend named 'Zero' (not to be confused with the gelugon of the same name in Stygia, this one is a former general), has achieved a state of perfect cold logic. He will answer one question truthfully for any visitor, but the question must be asked without speaking — in writing, and the answer will be frozen in ice, only readable if the asker's intent is pure. The Peak is a place of peace, but also a place of testing: many who come here freeze to death, unable to leave the cold comfort.",
          "paths": [
            "The Remorhaz Docks",
            "The Glacial Rift"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Frost barbarian sled-drivers",
        "Zero (Hermit pit fiend)",
        "Remorhaz wranglers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Ice Diamonds in the Glacial Rift are not mere crystals; they contain the memories of the souls trapped within. If a skilled necromancer were to extract them, they could reconstruct the entire history of the Blood War from the perspective of the dead. Zero knows this and protects the rift from desecration. He will trade a map of the safest path through the rift in exchange for a promise to destroy any Ice Diamonds already taken from the Nargus, which would reveal a hidden infernal archive beneath the Hermit's Peak.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Crystalline Labyrinth": {
      "name": "The Crystalline Labyrinth",
      "basicInfo": "A maze of towering, transparent ice crystals on the far side of Cania, where Mephistopheles tests the intellect of potential servants. The labyrinth is a living puzzle, its walls shifting to form new paths, dead ends, and logic traps that require arcane or philosophical insight to bypass. Those who reach the center are granted an audience with the Archduke and a boon; those who fail are absorbed into the crystals, their minds becoming part of the labyrinth's intelligence. The labyrinth is also a repository of frozen thought, containing the mental blueprints of every experiment Mephistopheles has ever conceived. The entrance is marked by a single, black obelisk that asks a riddle in the language of the visitor's heart.",
      "x": -7,
      "y": 17,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Cania — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Obelisk of Questions": {
          "description": "A ten-foot-tall obelisk of pure black ice, standing alone on the ice plain. As visitors approach, the obelisk speaks a riddle in their native tongue, a question that has no logical answer — only an emotional or philosophical one. Answering it correctly (by satisfying the obelisk's inscrutable standards) opens a path into the labyrinth. Those who answer incorrectly are teleported to a random location in Cania, often miles from shelter. The obelisk is sentient to a degree, remembering all who have attempted the riddle and chuckling softly when they fail.",
          "paths": [
            "The Path of Shifting Walls",
            "The Chamber of Frozen Thought"
          ]
        },
        "The Path of Shifting Walls": {
          "description": "The main body of the labyrinth, a maze of corridors whose walls shift silently and constantly. The walls reflect the inner thoughts of the wanderer, showing possible futures, past failures, and arcane equations. To navigate, one must solve a series of logic puzzles, each failure leading to a dead end or a trap (a sudden cold snap that can freeze a limb). The puzzles are drawn from Mephistopheles's own research; some are mathematical, others are moral paradoxes. A small, persistent snow devil named 'Quirk' follows travelers, offering unhelpful commentary and occasional cryptic hints.",
          "paths": [
            "The Obelisk of Questions",
            "The Chamber of Frozen Thought",
            "The Labyrinth's Heart"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of Frozen Thought": {
          "description": "A side chamber accessible only through a particularly difficult puzzle (solving the 'Paradox of the Cold Flame'). Inside, the walls are covered in frozen, translucent sculptures of Mephistopheles's greatest discoveries: the formula for the Eighth Word, the schematics for the God Engine, and the true shape of the Weave. The knowledge is complete, but the chamber is so cold that understanding it requires a Constitution saving throw; failure means the knowledge literally freezes the brain, leaving the victim a genius vegetable. A single, perfect ice rose blooms in the center, representing the beauty of absolute knowledge.",
          "paths": [
            "The Obelisk of Questions",
            "The Path of Shifting Walls"
          ]
        },
        "The Labyrinth's Heart": {
          "description": "The center of the maze, a small, perfectly round chamber of clear ice where the air is still and warm. Here, a projection of Mephistopheles awaits, offering the successful supplicant a single boon: a piece of knowledge, a minor artifact, or a pact that grants power in exchange for service. The boon is always fair, but always serves Mephistopheles's long-term plans. The floor is inscribed with the names of all who have ever reached the center — a short list that includes some of the greatest minds in the multiverse. The chamber also contains a portal back to the Obelisk.",
          "paths": [
            "The Path of Shifting Walls"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Mephistopheles's projection (the Labyrinth's master)",
        "Quirk (Snow devil guide/trickster)",
        "The Labyrinth's intelligence (absorbed minds)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Eighth Word, when finally completed, will be a single syllable that can reshape reality on a fundamental level. Its formula is hidden in the Chamber of Frozen Thought, but the final missing component is a 'word of pure selflessness', a concept that no devil can conceive. A mortal who reaches the chamber and contributes a truly selfless act (such as giving a boon to another without expectation of reward) would accidentally complete the formula, and Mephistopheles would grant them anything — including the power to challenge him. The labyrinth's puzzles are secretly designed to test for selflessness, a quality the Archduke despises but needs.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Frozen Pit of Baator": {
      "name": "The Frozen Pit of Baator",
      "basicInfo": "A deep, vertical shaft of unknown depth in the southern ice plains, believed to be a direct conduit to the heart of Nessus, the ninth layer. The Pit is so cold that even the ice devils of Cania avoid it, and the air around it is filled with the whispers of the damned echoing up from below. It is a place of execution for those who have committed the ultimate infernal treason: defiance of Asmodeus. The condemned are thrown in, and their fall is eternal — they never hit bottom, instead frozen in time and ice. The Pit is surrounded by the 'Ring of Penitence', a circle of kneeling statues — devils who were forced to watch the execution and then froze in place. At the Pit's edge, a single, ancient sign reads: 'All debts are settled here.'",
      "x": 7,
      "y": -18,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Cania — Far East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Ring of Penitence": {
          "description": "A circle of ice statues, each a kneeling devil with its head bowed toward the Pit. They were forced to witness the execution of a Lord of the Nine (a fate that has never actually occurred, but the threat remains). The statues are arranged in concentric rings, with the innermost ring containing the most powerful devils — pit fiends and dukes. The statues are sentient, able to speak in frozen whispers, offering warnings or (for a price) ancient secrets. The ring is patrolled by a single, silent gelugon executioner known as 'The Final Witness', who carries an axe of pure void.",
          "paths": [
            "The Pit's Edge",
            "The Gallery of Frozen Traitors"
          ]
        },
        "The Pit's Edge": {
          "description": "A narrow lip of obsidian at the very edge of the shaft, worn smooth by the last steps of the condemned. Looking down, one sees only a blue-white vortex of endless frost, and the faint, distant screams of those who fell. Standing too close for too long can pull the warmth from one's soul, causing a slow, permanent loss of empathy. A small, iron winch is mounted on the edge, but its chain is broken, and no one knows where it ends. Tossing a soul coin into the Pit is said to reduce one's own infernal debt by a fraction, a superstition that many still practice.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ring of Penitence",
            "The Gallery of Frozen Traitors"
          ]
        },
        "The Gallery of Frozen Traitors": {
          "description": "A series of ice caverns carved into the Pit's wall, accessible by a treacherous stairway of frozen blood. The caverns house the frozen bodies of devils who were found guilty of treason but not executed, instead preserved as warnings. Each traitor is displayed in a crystalline cell, their crimes inscribed on a plaque of bone. The gallery is maintained by a blind, ancient barbazu named 'The Chronicler', who recites their crimes in a monotone. The most recent addition is a former Minister of Ruin from Maladomini, who attempted to sell Mephistopheles's secrets to Dispater and is now frozen mid-scream.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ring of Penitence",
            "The Pit's Edge"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Final Witness (Gelugon executioner)",
        "The Chronicler (Barbazu historian)",
        "The Penitent Statues (sentient ice)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Pit is not bottomless; it ends in Nessus, in a chamber directly beneath Malsheem, where Asmodeus sits. The eternal fall is an illusion — the condemned actually arrive at the bottom after nine days, where they are broken down into soul-stuff and fed to the Lord of the Nine. The winch is a secret escape route built by Mephistopheles, who plans to use it to descend into Nessus and confront Asmodeus with his God Engine. The chain is not broken; it is coiled at the bottom, waiting for Mephistopheles's signal, which will be the completion of the Eighth Word.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Malsheem": {
      "name": "Malsheem",
      "basicInfo": "The fortress-temple of Asmodeus, Lord of the Nine, located at the heart of Nessus. It is a structure of impossible scale, its spires piercing the lightning-wracked sky and its foundations burrowing into the bedrock of Hell itself. Malsheem is both a palace and a cathedral, where Asmodeus holds court over the archdevils and orchestrates the grand strategy of the Blood War. The architecture is ancient and cyclopean, carved from black stone that drinks light, and the walls are inscribed with the original text of the Pact Primeval. The air hums with the power of the Lord of the Nine, and his presence can be felt as a weight on the soul. Entry is forbidden to all but the most powerful archdevils, and even they must be summoned. Malsheem is guarded by the Serpent's Coil, Asmodeus's elite force of pit fiends and ancient erinyes, and the entire fortress is a living extension of his will.",
      "x": 19,
      "y": -2,
      "radius": 8,
      "region": "Nessus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gate of the Damned": {
          "description": "The only entrance to Malsheem, a towering archway of black adamantine set into a wall of eternal lightning. The gate is sealed by nine seals, each requiring a key held by one of the archdukes. Only when all nine are present (or Asmodeus wills it) does the gate open. Before it lies a bridge of bone spanning a chasm of screaming souls. The gate is guarded by two of the Serpent's Coil — pit fiends whose eyes have been replaced with rubies that allow Asmodeus to see through them. To approach without permission is to be annihilated by a word of unmaking.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of the Nine Hells",
            "The Chamber of the Pact Primeval",
            "The Serpent's Coil Barracks"
          ]
        },
        "The Throne of the Nine Hells": {
          "description": "Asmodeus's throne room, a vast, cathedral-like hall of black marble and ruby light. At its center, on a dais of fused soul-stuff, sits the Lord of the Nine. His form is cloaked in shadow and majesty; those who see him perceive a being of immense power and ancient sorrow, his eyes burning with the light of a dying star. The walls are draped with the banners of the archdukes, and nine pits in the floor channel the screams of the damned directly from the Pit of Flame. Here, Asmodeus hears petitions from the archdevils, dispenses justice, and plots the fate of the multiverse. A permanent scrying pool of liquid silver shows any location in the Nine Hells.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of the Damned",
            "The Chamber of the Pact Primeval",
            "The Serpent's Coil Barracks"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of the Pact Primeval": {
          "description": "A sanctum adjacent to the throne room, where the original Pact Primeval is displayed under a dome of unbreakable crystal. The Pact is a scroll of divine parchment, signed in the blood of Asmodeus and the gods, granting him dominion over Hell and the punishment of sinners. The room is warded by a spell of absolute truth; any lie spoken here causes the liar to burst into hellfire. A single pedestal holds a copy of the contract, while the original is hidden within Asmodeus's own vault. The chamber is used for the signing of new infernal contracts of great importance, witnessed by the Lord of the Nine himself.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of the Nine Hells"
          ]
        },
        "The Serpent's Coil Barracks": {
          "description": "The quarters and training grounds of the Serpent's Coil, Asmodeus's personal guard. The barracks are a fortress within a fortress, filled with the most powerful pit fiends and erinyes in Hell. The air is thick with the smell of ozone and discipline. A war room maps the entire Blood War front, and a trophy hall displays the skulls of demon lords and rebellious archdevils. The commander, a pit fiend of immense age named Serpentine, answers only to Asmodeus. The Coil's loyalty is absolute, enforced by a magical oath that binds their souls to the Lord of the Nine.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of the Damned",
            "The Throne of the Nine Hells"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Asmodeus (Lord of the Nine)",
        "Serpent's Coil (elite guard)",
        "Serpentine (Pit fiend commander)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The true Pact Primeval is not in the Chamber — it is hidden in a pocket dimension accessible only by Asmodeus's own will. The displayed copy is a decoy that would self-destruct if tampered with. The real Pact contains a clause unknown to the gods: a provision that allows Asmodeus to reclaim the divine spark from any god who breaks their word. He has never invoked it, but it is his ultimate weapon against the Upper Planes.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Pit of Flame": {
      "name": "The Pit of Flame",
      "basicInfo": "The original wound in reality from which Asmodeus first emerged, located deep beneath Malsheem. The Pit is a yawning abyss of pure, crimson hellfire, the source of all infernal power. It is here that the souls of the damned are ultimately consumed, their energy fueling the Nine Hells. The Pit is also the prison of He Who Was, a dying god whose divine essence Asmodeus drained to fuel his own ascension. The area around the Pit is a blasted wasteland of glass and ash, lit by the eternal glow of the flames. Only Asmodeus and his most trusted servants may approach the Pit, and its power is so great that even archdevils are burned by its proximity. At the edge stands the 'Altar of Unmaking', where Asmodeus can unmake any creation, reducing it to its fundamental components.",
      "x": 5,
      "y": -36,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Nessus — Far East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Ashen Approach": {
          "description": "A desolate plain of grey ash and jagged obsidian that surrounds the Pit. The air is blisteringly hot, and the ground rumbles constantly. Massive chains of black iron stretch from the edge of the plain into the Pit, anchoring the Lord of the Nine's power. The ashen soil is littered with the crystallized remains of souls that have been completely consumed. A single, winding path of fused bone leads to the Pit's edge. The approach is patrolled by 'Ashen Wraiths' — the spirits of those who tried to reach the Pit and were burned to nothing but a last, eternal scream.",
          "paths": [
            "The Altar of Unmaking",
            "The Prison of He Who Was"
          ]
        },
        "The Altar of Unmaking": {
          "description": "A massive, flat stone altar of obsidian perched at the very edge of the Pit. The altar is inscribed with runes of negation, and any object or creature placed upon it can be unmade — reduced to raw soul-stuff and fed to the flames. Asmodeus uses it to destroy artifacts that threaten Hell, and occasionally to execute archdevils who have failed him utterly. The altar is overseen by a single, silent pit fiend known as 'The Unmaker', who has served Asmodeus since the dawn of Hell. Its eyes are pits of pure void.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ashen Approach",
            "The Pit's Edge"
          ]
        },
        "The Pit's Edge": {
          "description": "The rim of the Pit itself, where the heat is so intense that reality warps. Looking down, one sees an infinite vortex of crimson fire, with the faint, twisted faces of consumed souls swirling within. The edge is made of a strange, cold metal that does not heat, a gift from the Plane of Law. A single, thin bridge of this metal extends a few feet into the Pit, from which the Lord of the Nine delivers his most terrible judgments. Standing here for too long causes the viewer to begin losing their own identity, drawn into the flames.",
          "paths": [
            "The Altar of Unmaking",
            "The Prison of He Who Was"
          ]
        },
        "The Prison of He Who Was": {
          "description": "A side chasm branching from the main Pit, where the dying god He Who Was is imprisoned. The god appears as a colossal, emaciated figure of fading light, bound by chains of darkness and silence. Its eyes are closed, but its breath still stirs the flames. A constant, mournful hymn emanates from the prison, the last echoes of a dying divinity. The chains are inscribed with the Pact Primeval, symbolizing Asmodeus's legal right to his power. A single tear from the god's eye falls once every thousand years, crystallizing into a 'Tear of the Fallen', a component for artifacts of immense holy power. The prison is guarded by a pair of ancient pit fiends who have been driven mad by the god's sorrow.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ashen Approach",
            "The Pit's Edge"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Unmaker (Pit fiend guardian)",
        "Ashen Wraiths (spirit guards)",
        "He Who Was (imprisoned dying god)",
        "Mad pit fiend wardens"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "He Who Was is not entirely dead; his consciousness still lingers in the hymn. If the chains were broken (by speaking the Pact's hidden clause aloud, for example), the god would briefly awaken and could grant a single, reality-altering boon before passing into true death. Asmodeus knows this and has placed a contingency: any who free the god will be instantly dragged into the Pit of Flame by the Lord of the Nine himself, unless they bear the 'Ruby Rod', which is currently in Asmodeus's hand.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Iron City of Nessus": {
      "name": "The Iron City of Nessus",
      "basicInfo": "The outermost ring of Nessus, a colossal, fortified metropolis of black iron that surrounds the chasms leading inward to Malsheem. The city is a grim, martial sprawl of barracks, forges, and armories, where the legions of the Nine Hells muster for the Blood War. It is the most heavily defended location in the multiverse, with walls miles high and siege engines capable of leveling demonic hordes. The city is governed by the Dark Eight, the pit fiend generals who command Hell's armies, and serves as the primary staging ground for infernal offensives. The Iron City is also the final destination for souls consigned to Nessus, who are processed in the 'Soul-Forge of the Damned' and either forged into new devils or consigned to the Pit of Flame. The constant clang of forges and the march of drilling troops fills the air, and the sky is lit by the glow of hellfire and the sparks of war machines.",
      "x": 8,
      "y": 9,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Nessus — Far West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Outer Ramparts": {
          "description": "The outermost walls, a sheer cliff of black iron that stretches for miles in either direction. The walls are manned by legions of barbazu and erinyes, and ballistas firing soul-bolts line every parapet. The main gate is a massive portcullis of enchanted adamantine, flanked by two statues of Asmodeus that animate if the gate is breached. A constant traffic of supply wagons, marching troops, and condemned souls passes through. The gate commander, a pit fiend named General Ironheart, is a brutal but fair taskmaster who demands absolute discipline.",
          "paths": [
            "The Soul-Forge of the Damned",
            "The Dark Eight War Council",
            "The Mustering Fields"
          ]
        },
        "The Soul-Forge of the Damned": {
          "description": "A massive industrial complex where the souls of the damned are processed. Here, souls are sorted by their sins, their memories extracted and stored, and their essences reforged into lemures — the lowest form of devil. The forges are powered by the heat of the Pit of Flame, channeled through a network of hellfire pipes. The chief forgemaster, a pit fiend named Hephastion, is a genius of infernal engineering. He will trade technical knowledge for rare materials. The air is filled with the screams of the re-forged and the clang of soul-hammers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outer Ramparts",
            "The Dark Eight War Council"
          ]
        },
        "The Dark Eight War Council": {
          "description": "The strategic command center of the Iron City, a massive hall lined with maps, scrying pools, and communication crystals. Here, the eight pit fiend generals of the Dark Eight plan the Blood War. They sit on thrones of iron, each representing a different aspect of warfare (strategy, logistics, intelligence, etc.). The council chamber is a place of intense, quiet power; even the archdukes hesitate to interfere with the Dark Eight's domain. The leader, General Baalberith, is a pit fiend of immense tactical genius. A huge scrying map in the center of the room shows the entire Blood War front in real time.",
          "paths": [
            "The Soul-Forge of the Damned",
            "The Mustering Fields"
          ]
        },
        "The Mustering Fields": {
          "description": "A vast, open plain of packed ash and iron where the legions of Hell assemble for deployment. The fields are covered in troop formations, war machines, and the banners of the archdukes. The air is filled with the roar of drills, the clash of practice weapons, and the bellowing of pit fiend commanders. A massive portal gate at the far end connects directly to Avernus, allowing rapid deployment to the front lines. The fields are also used for the 'Reaping', a periodic culling of underperforming devils to be reforged.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outer Ramparts",
            "The Dark Eight War Council"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Dark Eight (pit fiend generals)",
        "General Ironheart (gate commander)",
        "Hephastion (Pit fiend forgemaster)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Dark Eight are not merely generals — they are a check on the archdukes' power. Asmodeus created them to ensure that no single archduke could control the legions of Hell. One of the Eight, General Malphas, is secretly an agent of Mephistopheles, feeding him troop movements in exchange for the formula of the Eighth Word. The other seven suspect but cannot prove his treachery, and they would reward anyone who exposes him with a powerful infernal boon.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Chasm of Ultimate Woe": {
      "name": "The Chasm of Ultimate Woe",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, deep rift in the surface of Nessus, one of the many chasms that scar the layer. This particular chasm is the site of the 'Well of Tears', a shaft of perpetual sorrow where the soul-shards of those who died of grief are collected. The chasm is also home to the 'Weeping Sisters', a coven of night hags who distill the tears into powerful emotional toxins. The walls of the chasm are carved with the laments of the dead, and the air is filled with a constant, mournful keening. At the bottom lies the 'Lake of Forgotten Sorrows', a pool of black water that reflects not images but the deepest regrets of the viewer. The chasm is a place of pilgrimage for those seeking to forget their sorrows, as a drink from the lake's water can erase a painful memory — at the cost of a cherished one. The Weeping Sisters guard the lake and trade their toxins to the Lords of the Nine for use in political assassination and psychological warfare.",
      "x": 12,
      "y": -10,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Nessus — West",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Lamentation Path": {
          "description": "A narrow, winding path that descends into the chasm, its walls covered in carved, weeping faces. The path is treacherous, and the keening wind threatens to push climbers off. Small niches along the path hold offerings to the dead: broken dolls, withered flowers, and letters to lost loved ones. The Weeping Sisters leave these as bait; touching an offering can trigger a memory of the associated loss. A single, ancient tree of black crystal grows at the halfway point, its branches hung with the crystallized tears of past mourners.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weeping Sisters' Cauldron",
            "The Lake of Forgotten Sorrows"
          ]
        },
        "The Weeping Sisters' Cauldron": {
          "description": "A cave opening onto the chasm wall, where the three Weeping Sisters — a coven of ancient night hags named Misery, Lament, and Despair — brew their concoctions. Their cauldron is a massive, black iron pot filled with the tears they have collected, simmering over a hellfire. They are terrifyingly empathetic, able to sense the sorrow of any who enter and use it to manipulate them. They trade toxins that can induce specific emotions (grief, rage, suicidal despair) in exchange for the happy memories of their customers. The walls are lined with jars containing captured laughter, which the sisters consume as a delicacy.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lamentation Path",
            "The Lake of Forgotten Sorrows"
          ]
        },
        "The Lake of Forgotten Sorrows": {
          "description": "A still, black pool at the bottom of the chasm, its surface perfectly reflective. Gazing into the lake shows the viewer their deepest regret, replayed endlessly. Kneeling to drink from the lake erases that regret from the drinker's memory, but also removes a random, cherished memory of equal emotional weight. The lake is guarded by the 'Drowned Ones', the soulless husks of those who drank too deeply and lost all memory of themselves. The Weeping Sisters use the lake as their primary source of tears, harvesting them from the surface each 'morning' (as defined by the tolling of a distant, unseen bell).",
          "paths": [
            "The Lamentation Path",
            "The Weeping Sisters' Cauldron"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Weeping Sisters (Misery, Lament, Despair)",
        "Drowned Ones (guardians of the lake)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Lake of Forgotten Sorrows is not a natural formation; it is a tear of Selûne, the moon goddess, shed when she witnessed the first mortal death. It fell here, and its divine origin gives it the power to manipulate memory. The Weeping Sisters do not know this, but if a worshipper of Selûne were to pray at the lake's edge during a full moon (the phases of which can somehow be felt even in Hell), the lake would reveal its true nature and could be used to restore the memories of the Drowned Ones. This would break the sisters' power and free the trapped souls.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Ruby Palace of Asmodeus": {
      "name": "The Ruby Palace of Asmodeus",
      "basicInfo": "A secondary residence of Asmodeus, located in a pocket dimension attached to Nessus, accessible only through a hidden portal in Malsheem. The Palace is a place of opulent, terrifying beauty, where the Lord of the Nine retreats for private contemplation and the entertainment of his most trusted allies. Its walls are made of solid ruby, and its gardens are filled with plants made of crystal and fire. Here, Asmodeus displays his most prized possessions: the original Pact Primeval (the true original, hidden from even his archdukes), the Ruby Rod of Asmodeus (a supreme artifact of rulership), and the 'Mirror of All Desire', which shows the viewer the path to their deepest ambition. The Palace is also where Asmodeus keeps his personal library, the 'Archives of the First Sin', containing knowledge that predates the multiverse. Only the most favored servants are ever invited, and the Palace's location shifts constantly to prevent any attempt at invasion.",
      "x": 12,
      "y": 17,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Nessus — East",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Portal of the Ruby Rod": {
          "description": "A shimmering, ruby-colored portal in a nondescript corridor of Malsheem, which activates only when touched by the Ruby Rod or when Asmodeus himself wills it. Stepping through leads to the Palace's entrance hall. The portal is guarded by two of the Serpent's Coil, who stand motionless for centuries at a time. The entrance hall itself is a vast, circular room of ruby and gold, with a ceiling painted with the stars of the Material Plane as they were on the day of creation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of Contemplation",
            "The Garden of Crystal Delights",
            "The Archives of the First Sin"
          ]
        },
        "The Throne of Contemplation": {
          "description": "A secondary throne room, smaller and more intimate than the one in Malsheem. Here, Asmodeus meets with his archdukes in a less formal setting, often while sipping a glass of distilled starlight. The throne is a single, massive ruby that glows with an inner light. The walls are covered in shelves holding the 'Orbs of Soul-Sight', which allow the viewer to experience the entire life of a mortal soul in an instant. Asmodeus uses these to pass judgment on particularly complex cases, immersing himself in the totality of a mortal's choices.",
          "paths": [
            "The Portal of the Ruby Rod",
            "The Mirror of All Desire"
          ]
        },
        "The Mirror of All Desire": {
          "description": "A full-length mirror of silvered glass, framed in the bones of a forgotten god. Gazing into the mirror shows the viewer not their reflection, but the path to their deepest, most secret ambition — step by step. The mirror does not lie, but the path shown often involves terrible costs. Asmodeus uses it to tempt mortals into bargains, and to test the loyalty of his servants. Anyone who looks into the mirror must make a Wisdom save or become obsessed with pursuing the path shown, regardless of the cost.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of Contemplation",
            "The Archives of the First Sin"
          ]
        },
        "The Archives of the First Sin": {
          "description": "Asmodeus's personal library, a circular room filled with scrolls, books, and memory crystals. Here is stored the original Pact Primeval, written in the blood of the gods and Asmodeus. Also present is the 'Book of the Damned', a tome that records the name of every soul ever consigned to Hell, and the 'Codex of Infinite Planes', a book that maps the multiverse in such detail that reading it can drive a mortal insane. The Archivist, an ancient arcanaloth named Memnon, is the only creature other than Asmodeus permitted to touch the books. He will trade copies of non-unique texts for rare knowledge, but his loyalty is absolute.",
          "paths": [
            "The Portal of the Ruby Rod",
            "The Mirror of All Desire"
          ]
        },
        "The Garden of Crystal Delights": {
          "description": "A conservatory of crystal plants and fire-flowers, maintained by silent, invisible gardeners. The garden is a place of strange, alien beauty, and the air is filled with the scent of ozone and burning sugar. A fountain of liquid ruby at the center grants temporary invulnerability to anyone who drinks from it, but the effect lasts only one hour per sip, and the next sip is twice as addictive. The garden is also home to the 'Phoenix of the First Flame', a primordial firebird that is the ancestor of all phoenixes, and which serves as Asmodeus's pet and messenger.",
          "paths": [
            "The Portal of the Ruby Rod"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Asmodeus (Lord of the Nine)",
        "Memnon (Arcanaloth archivist)",
        "Phoenix of the First Flame (messenger)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The true Pact Primeval is a contract not merely between Asmodeus and the gods, but a living, sentient document that holds Asmodeus to his own word. If he ever broke a clause, the Pact would dissolve, and his power would vanish. This is his deepest secret: he is not absolute; he is bound by law, and a clever mortal who found a loophole could theoretically challenge him. The Mirror of All Desire shows the path to such a loophole, but only to someone whose deepest ambition is to save others from damnation — a quality almost nonexistent in Hell.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Protector's Enclave": {
      "name": "Protector's Enclave",
      "basicInfo": "The administrative heart of Neverwinter, a walled district that survived the cataclysm relatively intact. Here, Lord Protector Dagult Neverember rules from the Hall of Justice, surrounded by his mercenary guard, the Mintarn soldiers who enforce his will. The streets are clean and well-patrolled, lined with shops, guildhalls, and the offices of the Many-Starred Cloaks. The towering Moonstone Mask, an inn and festhall carved from a single great rock, dominates the skyline. The Enclave also houses the Shard of Night, a spire where the city's wizards study the fractured Weave. Tension simmers between Neverember's imported Mintarn enforcers and the native Neverwintans, who resent the foreign presence.",
      "x": 40,
      "y": -6,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "A1 — Northern Trackless Sea & Sword Coast",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Hall of Justice": {
          "description": "A newly-built fortress of grey granite, serving as Lord Neverember's palace and the headquarters of his government. The hall is defended by a contingent of Mintarn mercenaries in gleaming plate. Inside, Neverember holds open court, listening to petitions and dispensing swift justice. The walls are decorated with maps of the city's reconstruction efforts, and a grand staircase leads to his private quarters, which are rumoured to contain a hidden exit to the sewers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Moonstone Mask",
            "The Shard of Night",
            "The Wall Ward"
          ]
        },
        "The Moonstone Mask": {
          "description": "A legendary tavern and inn, built into a massive stone outcropping that glows faintly under moonlight. Run by the enigmatic Ophala Cheldar, it caters to adventurers, nobles, and spies alike. The common room is a cacophony of music, brawling, and whispered deals. A back room, the 'Silver Veil', hosts secret meetings under a permanent silence spell. The upper floors offer luxurious suites, some with views of the Chasm.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Justice",
            "The Shard of Night",
            "The Market Square"
          ]
        },
        "The Shard of Night": {
          "description": "A slender tower of black stone, remnant of a pre-cataclysm mage academy. Now it houses the Many-Starred Cloaks, the city's official wizards. Archmage Vaelan Sunspear leads a small cadre of arcanists who monitor magical threats and maintain the wards around the Enclave. The tower's interior is a dizzying labyrinth of spiralling staircases and extradimensional rooms. A scrying pool in the basement shows a live feed of the Chasm's activity.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Justice",
            "The Moonstone Mask",
            "The Wall Ward"
          ]
        },
        "The Market Square": {
          "description": "A bustling plaza at the centre of the Enclave, where merchants hawk goods from across the Sword Coast. Stalls sell fresh produce, Dwarven steel, enchanted trinkets, and the famous Neverwinter glass. The square is overlooked by the House of Knowledge, a temple to Oghma, whose monks serve as legal scribes. Pickpockets from the River District's underworld often work the crowd, and the city guard patrols heavily.",
          "paths": [
            "The Moonstone Mask",
            "The House of Knowledge"
          ]
        },
        "The House of Knowledge": {
          "description": "A temple to Oghma, god of knowledge and invention. Its marble halls are filled with scribes, scholars, and a vast library of public records. High Priest Lissan Alamander oversees the temple's mission to restore Neverwinter's lost archives. The basement contains the 'Vault of Forbidden Tomes', a collection of dangerous texts salvaged from the ruins of the Academy, accessible only to those with a writ from Neverember himself.",
          "paths": [
            "The Market Square"
          ]
        },
        "The Wall Ward": {
          "description": "The fortified perimeter of the Enclave, where the Wall divides the safe districts from the Chasm's edge. The ward is patrolled by the Wall Guard, a special unit of Mintarn soldiers who wield flame-enchanted weapons to turn back the undead. A gate here leads to the Blacklake District, but it is barred after sunset. The guard barracks houses a small shrine to Torm, where soldiers pray for protection before patrols.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Justice",
            "The Shard of Night"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Lord Dagult Neverember",
        "Mintarn Mercenaries",
        "Many-Starred Cloaks",
        "Church of Oghma"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Moonstone Mask's owner, Ophala Cheldar, is secretly a member of the Harpers. She uses the inn as a safe house for agents and a listening post for information from across the North. The Silver Veil room is warded against all forms of scrying except one: a hidden crystal in the ceiling that allows Lord Neverember to spy on the meetings that take place there. The two of them are locked in a silent battle of wits.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Blacklake District": {
      "name": "Blacklake District",
      "basicInfo": "Once the richest neighbourhood in Neverwinter, Blacklake District still bears the scars of the cataclysm. The district is built around Blacklake itself, a small, stagnant lake that survived the eruption but became tainted by the Chasm's corruption. Elegant manors, now half-crumbled, line the lake's shores, occupied by noble families who refuse to abandon their ancestral homes. The Chasm cuts a jagged scar through the district, and the air is often thick with the stench of sulphur and decay. The Wall separates Blacklake from the Protector's Enclave, but the district is a buffer zone between civilization and the horrors that crawl from the Chasm. The House of a Thousand Faces, once a temple to a dead god, now serves as a front for the Ashmadai cult. At night, the district is patrolled by the 'Chasm Wardens', a grim group of volunteers who hunt the undead.",
      "x": 39,
      "y": -5,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "A1 — Northern Trackless Sea & Sword Coast",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Lakefront": {
          "description": "A promenade of cracked flagstones along Blacklake's shore, lined with dead trees and the crumbling mansions of the nobility. The lake's water is dark and murky, and sometimes bubbles with foul gases. A few fishing boats still operate, but the catch is often mutated and inedible. The mansion of House Boromar, the most stubborn of the noble families, stands here, its windows boarded but its lights still burning within. A hidden dock leads to a smuggler's tunnel into the River District.",
          "paths": [
            "The House of a Thousand Faces",
            "The Chasm Scar",
            "The Wall Gate"
          ]
        },
        "The House of a Thousand Faces": {
          "description": "A crumbling temple, built to honour a deity whose name is now forgotten. Its façade is carved with hundreds of faces, some weeping, some screaming, some serene. Now, it serves as the headquarters of the Ashmadai, a cult of devil-worshippers who have infiltrated the city. Beneath the temple, in the catacombs, they conduct dark rituals, branding new members with the mark of Asmodeus and summoning lesser devils. The cult's leader, a tiefling sorcerer named Mordai Vell, poses as a merchant of fine antiquities. The temple is guarded by hell hounds and dominated cultists.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lakefront",
            "The Chasm Scar"
          ]
        },
        "The Chasm Scar": {
          "description": "The edge of the great rift that splits the district. The ground here is perpetually warm, and a foul mist rises from the depths. The buildings along the edge are tottering ruins, abandoned to the creatures that climb from the Chasm: plaguechanged ghouls, shadow mastiffs, and occasional wraiths. A rickety bridge of rope and planks, maintained by the Chasm Wardens, spans the narrowest point, but crossing is perilous. The Wardens' outpost, a fortified stone watchtower, stands on the safe side, manned by grim-faced veterans who keep careful count of the undead they've slain.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lakefront",
            "The House of a Thousand Faces",
            "The Chasm Wardens' Post"
          ]
        },
        "The Chasm Wardens' Post": {
          "description": "A squat, reinforced watchtower of grey stone, festooned with holy symbols and the trophies of past hunts: the skulls of plaguechanged monsters. Inside, a handful of Wardens maintain a vigil, repairing their gear and planning patrols. Their commander, a grizzled human paladin of Torm named Sir Valas, greets visitors with weary suspicion. The post has a small armory and a shrine where the Wardens pray for protection. A logbook records every incursion from the Chasm, a grim chronicle of endless battle.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chasm Scar",
            "The Wall Gate"
          ]
        },
        "The Wall Gate": {
          "description": "The heavily fortified gate that separates Blacklake District from the safety of the Protector's Enclave. The Wall is tall and thick, built of fused stone and iron. The gate is guarded by a squad of Mintarn soldiers, who check the papers of all who pass. At sunset, the gate is locked and barred, and anyone left in Blacklake must fend for themselves until dawn. The gate's inner archway is carved with the names of those who died defending the Wall during the first years after the cataclysm.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lakefront",
            "The Chasm Wardens' Post"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Nobility of Blacklake (House Boromar etc.)",
        "Ashmadai Cult (Mordai Vell)",
        "Chasm Wardens (Sir Valas)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Ashmadai are not merely a local cult; they are an extension of Glasya's infernal network, seeking to open a permanent gate to Malbolge. The catacombs beneath the House of a Thousand Faces contain a partially completed portal. Mordai Vell believes that the blood of a noble who has willingly signed a Faustian pact will complete the ritual. He has been secretly courting Lord Boromar's daughter, hoping to secure her soul and her blood to finish his work.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Chasm": {
      "name": "The Chasm",
      "basicInfo": "A massive, jagged rift that cuts through the centre of Neverwinter, a festering wound in the earth. It was born when the fire primordial Maegera's eruption shattered the city, tearing open a passage to the Shadowfell. Now, the Chasm is a nightmare landscape of crumbling architecture, steam vents, and perpetual twilight. It is filled with plaguechanged undead, shadow beasts, and pockets of the Spellplague's lingering blue fire. The air is toxic, causing illness in those who breathe it too long, and the ground trembles with aftershocks. Despite the danger, treasure hunters brave the depths, seeking artefacts from the city's past, and the desperate or outcast have built shantytowns in the less active crevices. At the very bottom lies the 'Screaming Depths', a sinkhole that plunges directly into the Shadowfell.",
      "x": 38,
      "y": -7,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "A1 — Northern Trackless Sea & Sword Coast",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Chasm Rim": {
          "description": "The jagged, unstable edge of the rift, littered with the remains of buildings that collapsed during the eruption. A permanent haze of dust and foul mist hangs in the air. Several rope-and-plank descents have been established by scavengers, but the rim is guarded by undead that crawl up from below. The city guard maintains a few watchtowers, but they are perpetually undermanned. A market of sorts, the 'Fringe Bazaar', operates on the rim, where scavengers sell their finds and desperate souls buy passage into the depths.",
          "paths": [
            "The Plaguegarden",
            "The Sunken Streets",
            "The Screaming Depths"
          ]
        },
        "The Plaguegarden": {
          "description": "A shantytown built into a relatively stable shelf of the Chasm wall, inhabited by the 'Plaguechanged' – those who survived the Spellplague's blue fire and were transformed, gaining strange mutations but also physical and mental scars. The Plaguechanged eke out a grim existence here, shunned by the city above. Their leader, a mutated elf known as 'Mother Gargoyle', rules through a combination of fear and a strange, gentle wisdom. The Plaguegarden is a maze of scrap metal huts and rope ladders, and its inhabitants offer a grudging welcome to outsiders who bring medicine or food.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chasm Rim",
            "The Blue Fire Scar"
          ]
        },
        "The Sunken Streets": {
          "description": "The crumbling remains of ancient Neverwinter, streets and buildings that fell into the Chasm and now lie at the bottom, shrouded in shadow. The architecture is a mix of pre-cataclysm stone and more recent, jerry-built structures. This area is crawling with undead: plaguechanged ghouls, skeletal warriors, and the occasional wraith. A ruined temple to Tyr, its statue of the blind god cracked and toppled, serves as a lair for a pack of shadow mastiffs. Hidden vaults in the old merchant halls still hold treasures from before the disaster.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chasm Rim",
            "The Screaming Depths",
            "The Vault of the Nine"
          ]
        },
        "The Blue Fire Scar": {
          "description": "A section of the Chasm still tainted by the raw blue fire of the Spellplague. The area glows with an eerie azure light, and the rocks are fused into strange, organic shapes. Mutated creatures – fire bats, chaos beasts, and worse – make their lairs here. The fire itself is unpredictable, sometimes flaring and granting temporary magical power to those it touches, more often consuming them. A renegade Thayan wizard, Orin, has set up a camp here, studying the blue fire's properties in hopes of harnessing it for his own ends.",
          "paths": [
            "The Plaguegarden",
            "The Sunken Streets"
          ]
        },
        "The Screaming Depths": {
          "description": "A bottomless sinkhole at the very heart of the Chasm, from which a constant, chilling wind howls. The wind sounds like the screams of the damned, and those who listen too long begin to hear their own name in the wailing. This is the direct portal to the Shadowfell, a rift between worlds. Occasionally, creatures of pure shadow emerge: nightwalkers, shadow dragons, or worse. At the very edge, a shattered bridge of elven make, the 'Bridge of Souls', once connected to the now-ruined Sharandar, now hangs precariously over the void.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chasm Rim",
            "The Sunken Streets"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of the Nine": {
          "description": "A hidden chamber, buried deep beneath the Sunken Streets, accessible only by a secret passage hidden behind the collapsed altar of Tyr's temple. The Vault was built by a cabal of nine powerful wizards before the Spellplague, intended to store dangerous artifacts. It is warded by ancient magic that still holds. Inside, shelves hold crumbling tomes, enchanted weapons, and, at the centre, the 'Orb of Planar Binding', a fist-sized sphere of crystallised magic capable of sealing the Chasm. However, the Vault is guarded by Olothontor, an ancient blue dragon who was trapped here during the eruption and has slumbered ever since, curled around the pedestal. Disturbing the Orb will surely wake him.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sunken Streets"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Plaguechanged community (Mother Gargoyle)",
        "Thayan researcher Orin",
        "Chasm Wardens (reluctant patrols)",
        "Olothontor (slumbering blue dragon)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Orb of Planar Binding is not a Netherese artifact; it is a piece of the Sharn Wall, the ancient barrier that once imprisoned the Phaerimm. A fragment broke off during the Spellplague and was retrieved by the Nine. If used to seal the Chasm, it would also send a pulse of magical energy across the Weave, revealing the location of all active planar portals in Faerûn to the user. This is the true reason Neverember seeks it – not just to save his city, but to gain a strategic advantage over all his rivals.",
      "known": true
    },
    "River District": {
      "name": "River District",
      "basicInfo": "The mercantile heart of Neverwinter, located on the south bank of the Neverwinter River. A bustling, chaotic quarter of warehouses, shipwrights, markets, and taverns, it is the city's link to the wider world through its docks. The architecture is a hodgepodge of old stone and new timber, crowded with sailors, traders, and thieves. The district never sleeps; even at night, the docks glow with lantern light and the sound of drunken shanties. The Zhentarim have a strong presence here, operating out of a waterfront warehouse. The Driftwood Tavern, a popular watering hole, serves as a neutral ground for all factions. The River District is also home to the Shipwright's Guild, whose master, a dwarf named Gundren Rockseeker (a cousin of the Phandelver heroes), builds the famed Neverwinter longships.",
      "x": 39,
      "y": -8,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "A1 — Northern Trackless Sea & Sword Coast",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Docks": {
          "description": "A long stretch of wooden piers and stone wharves, bustling with ships from Waterdeep, Luskan, and even distant Maztica. The air smells of salt, fish, and tar. Stevedores haul crates of goods, and customs officials inspect cargo with practiced suspicion. A lighthouse, the 'Lantern of Lathander', stands at the harbour entrance, its magical beam sweeping the sea. The docks are controlled by the Dockmaster's Guild, which answers to Lord Neverember, but the Zhentarim have infiltrated the guild and use it to smuggle contraband.",
          "paths": [
            "The Driftwood Tavern",
            "The Shipwright's Guild",
            "The Fishmarket"
          ]
        },
        "The Driftwood Tavern": {
          "description": "A ramshackle tavern built from the salvaged timbers of wrecked ships, the Driftwood is the social hub of the River District. Its proprietor, a retired half-orc adventurer named Borga, serves strong ale and stronger gossip. A central hearth fire is always lit, and the walls are covered with nautical curiosities. The tavern is neutral ground; the Zhentarim, the Harpers, and the city guard all drink here, their weapons checked at the door. A back room, the 'Skipper's Snug', can be rented for private meetings.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks",
            "The Shipwright's Guild",
            "The Warehouse of the Black Network"
          ]
        },
        "The Shipwright's Guild": {
          "description": "A massive, open-fronted building on the water's edge, where the dwarf master shipwright Gundren Rockseeker oversees the construction of Neverwinter's legendary longships. The air rings with the sound of hammers and the smell of fresh-sawn timber. Gundren is a gruff but honest craftsman, and he can provide passage on a new vessel for a price. The guild also repairs damaged ships and sells maritime equipment. A dry dock in the back houses a half-built galleon, rumoured to be Neverember's personal flagship.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks",
            "The Driftwood Tavern"
          ]
        },
        "The Fishmarket": {
          "description": "An open-air market next to the docks, where the day's catch is sold fresh. Stalls offer salmon, trout, and the famous Neverwinter silver eel. The market is also a front for the city's thieving guild, the 'Silent Sails', who run pickpocketing operations and fencing of stolen goods from a hidden basement beneath a fishmonger's stall. The guild's leader, a halfling named Fin, is a cheerful rogue who will trade information for coin.",
          "paths": [
            "The Docks",
            "The Warehouse of the Black Network"
          ]
        },
        "The Warehouse of the Black Network": {
          "description": "A nondescript stone warehouse near the docks, its windows sealed and its doors guarded by hard-eyed men. This is the Zhentarim headquarters in Neverwinter. Inside, the warehouses are filled with legitimate cargo: grain, iron, and timber. But the back rooms hold smuggled contraband: poisons, illicit magic items, and slaves. The local Zhentarim chief, a smooth-talking human named Marek, runs the operation with ruthless efficiency. He is always looking for agents to carry out tasks, and he pays well.",
          "paths": [
            "The Driftwood Tavern",
            "The Fishmarket"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Dockmaster's Guild",
        "Zhentarim (Marek)",
        "Silent Sails (Fin)",
        "Shipwright's Guild (Gundren Rockseeker)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "Marek is not merely a Zhentarim; he is a spy for the Arcane Brotherhood of Luskan. He feeds information about Neverwinter's defenses and trade routes to his true masters, preparing the way for a potential Luskanite invasion. He has a magical tattoo on his chest that allows him to communicate with the Hosttower. If discovered, he will use a contingency spell to teleport away, leaving behind a fireball trap to cover his escape.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Helm's Hold": {
      "name": "Helm's Hold",
      "basicInfo": "A fortified abbey located north of Neverwinter, built to honour the dead god Helm. After the cataclysm, the Hold was converted into an asylum for the spellscarred, those poor souls twisted by the Spellplague. Now, it is a grim, isolated place, its walls patrolled by the Order of Blue Fire, a cult that worships the Spellplague as a divine blessing. The leader, a charismatic tiefling named Brother Vartan, preaches that the Spellplague was a gift from a new god, the 'Azure Flame', and that those afflicted are its chosen. The asylum houses hundreds of spellscarred, ranging from the merely mutated to the hopelessly insane. The original catacombs beneath the Hold, once the crypt of Helm's faithful, now serve as the cult's inner sanctum, where they perform rituals to the Azure Flame. Despite its dark purpose, the Hold also offers a tenuous sanctuary; the city guard does not venture here, and the spellscarred are treated with a twisted form of compassion.",
      "x": 43,
      "y": -9,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "A1 — Northern Trackless Sea & Sword Coast",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Outer Bailey": {
          "description": "The fortified entrance to the Hold, a large walled courtyard with a heavy portcullis. Guards in blue tabards, their faces scarred by blue fire, challenge all who approach. The courtyard is filled with makeshift tents where the less severely afflicted spellscarred live, waiting for admission to the inner sanctuary. A bell tower rings the hours, a melancholy sound. The atmosphere is tense but not immediately hostile; the cultists try to convert visitors with passionate sermons.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hospice of the Azure Flame",
            "The Catacombs of Helm",
            "The Tower of the Mind"
          ]
        },
        "The Hospice of the Azure Flame": {
          "description": "The main building of the Hold, a converted abbey that now serves as a hospital and asylum. The hall is lined with cots where the spellscarred lie, some wailing, some catatonic. Cultist healers in blue robes tend to them, using a combination of herbal medicine and channelling the raw blue fire. A central altar holds a 'Flame of Azure', a perpetually burning blue candle that the cult worships. Visitors can speak to patients, but the atmosphere is one of profound sadness mixed with fervent hope.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outer Bailey",
            "Brother Vartan's Chapel"
          ]
        },
        "Brother Vartan's Chapel": {
          "description": "A private chapel off the hospice, where Brother Vartan, the tiefling leader of the Order, receives visitors. The room is filled with incense and the sound of a single, keening flute. Vartan is a charismatic figure with blue fire flickering in his eyes. He speaks eloquently about the 'Great Blessing' and seeks to convert those who doubt. He also controls the cult's finances and has a hidden safe containing a sizeable donation from a mysterious benefactor (who is actually a Thayan agent seeking to study the spellscarred).",
          "paths": [
            "The Hospice of the Azure Flame",
            "The Catacombs of Helm"
          ]
        },
        "The Catacombs of Helm": {
          "description": "The ancient burial chambers beneath the abbey, now used by the Order for secret rituals. The walls are lined with the sarcophagi of long-dead paladins of Helm, their effigies now defaced with blue fire symbols. A central chamber has been converted into a ritual circle, where cultists summon and bind blue fire elementals. The deeper crypts lead to a natural cave where the 'Voice of the Azure Flame' can be heard – a psychic resonance of the Spellplague itself, offering maddening prophecies.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outer Bailey",
            "Brother Vartan's Chapel",
            "The Voice of the Azure Flame"
          ]
        },
        "The Voice of the Azure Flame": {
          "description": "A subterranean cavern lit by a pool of undulating blue fire, the source of the cult's power. The flames whisper in a hundred voices, promising power, revealing secrets, and demanding sacrifices. Any spellscarred creature that bathes in the pool finds their mutations enhanced but their mind further shattered. Brother Vartan consults the Voice for guidance, and sometimes the Voice answers with a name – a target for the cult's assassins, the 'Sapphire Hands'.",
          "paths": [
            "The Catacombs of Helm"
          ]
        },
        "The Tower of the Mind": {
          "description": "A separate tower attached to the outer bailey, used as a prison for the most dangerously insane spellscarred. The cells are padded with sound-dampening moss, and the guards wear heavy helmets to block out the constant screaming. The tower is overseen by a stern, grim woman named Warden Mara, who has no patience for the cult's theology but keeps the dangerous inmates contained. Some of the inmates are powerful mages or warriors, driven mad by the plague, and they might be lucid enough to impart knowledge if calmed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Outer Bailey"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Order of Blue Fire (Brother Vartan)",
        "Spellscarred patients",
        "Warden Mara (prison keeper)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Voice of the Azure Flame is not a natural phenomenon; it is the fractured consciousness of a dead primordial, the entity known as the 'Azure Flame', that was destroyed during the Dawn War and its remnants scattered into the Spellplague. Brother Vartan is its puppet, and the cult's rituals are slowly feeding it enough power to manifest physically. If it succeeds, it will rise from the catacombs as a new, insane god, consuming Helm's Hold and then spreading its mad faith across Faerûn.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Neverwinter Wood": {
      "name": "Neverwinter Wood",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, ancient forest east of Neverwinter, a place of deep shadows, fey magic, and old ruins. The Wood has always been wild and dangerous, but since the Spellplague, it has become even more so, with pockets of fey crossings, portals to the Feywild, and the ever-present threat of the Sharandar ruins. The forest is home to the Uthgardt Gray Wolf tribe, who roam its northern reaches, and to the aggressive Thornbacks, goblinoids who serve darker masters. At its heart lies the Dire Wood, a place so corrupted by shadow that even the fey avoid it. The Wood also contains the ruined Academy of Neverwinter, a once-great school of magic that was destroyed during the eruption and is now overgrown and haunted. Adventurers are drawn to the Wood by rumours of lost treasures, fey bargains, and the secrets of the ancient elven kingdom of Iliyanbruen, whose ruins lie beneath the canopy.",
      "x": -48,
      "y": -3,
      "radius": 8,
      "region": "B1 — Frozenfar & Savage North",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Forest's Edge": {
          "description": "The transition from farmland to wilderness, a border of thinning trees and old logging camps. The atmosphere grows heavier as one enters the Wood. A trading post, the 'Stag's Rest', stands at the edge, run by a ranger of the Emerald Enclave named Lyra Silvanus, who sells supplies and information. She can warn travelers of the latest dangers and give directions to the major landmarks. A faint, silvery mist often drifts from the trees, carrying the scent of wildflowers and rot.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ruined Academy",
            "The Dire Wood",
            "The Fey Crossing"
          ]
        },
        "The Ruined Academy": {
          "description": "The skeletal remains of the Neverwinter Academy of Magic, a once-proud tower now shattered and overgrown. The upper floors have collapsed, but the lower halls and basement vaults remain, filled with old spellbooks, magical residue, and the occasional unstable golem. The ghost of the last headmaster, Archmage Eldrith, wanders the ruins, mourning the students she could not save. She will attack anyone who she perceives as a looter, but a kind word or an offer to retrieve a lost memento (her spellbook, still in her office) can calm her spirit and earn her gratitude.",
          "paths": [
            "The Forest's Edge",
            "The Dire Wood"
          ]
        },
        "The Dire Wood": {
          "description": "A region of perpetual twilight, where the trees are gnarled and black, and the ground is carpeted with silvery moss that seems to drink light. The Dire Wood is a portal to the Shadowfell, a place where the veil between worlds is thin. Shadow creatures roam freely, and the air whispers with dark promises. At its centre stands a massive, petrified tree, the 'Heart of Midnight', from which a permanent shadow gate pulses. The Uthgardt Gray Wolves call this place cursed and will not enter. The Thornback goblins use it as a base, their camp built around the Heart, where they worship a shadowy entity they call 'Grandfather Dusk'.",
          "paths": [
            "The Forest's Edge",
            "The Ruined Academy",
            "The Heart of Midnight"
          ]
        },
        "The Heart of Midnight": {
          "description": "A titanic, dead tree in the centre of the Dire Wood, its bark black as coal and its branches forming a skeletal canopy. A permanent shadow portal flickers at its base, shaped like a gaping maw. Goblins of the Thornback tribe sacrifice captured animals and travelers to the portal, and their shaman, a twisted hobgoblin named Duskfang, draws power from the shadow. Inside the tree, a hollow chamber houses the 'Shadow Obelisk', a monument of the ancient Shadovar, which anchors the portal. Destroying the obelisk would close the gate.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dire Wood",
            "The Fey Crossing"
          ]
        },
        "The Fey Crossing": {
          "description": "A beautiful, glimmering pool in a sun-dappled glade, where the boundary to the Feywild is thin. The water reflects the sky in colours too vivid for the mortal world. A small, magical bridge of living wood arches over the pool, leading to an archway of woven vines that opens into the Feywild. The arch is guarded by an eladrin knight, Sir Lathanderis, who tests all who wish to cross with a riddle. The glade is a neutral ground, and fey creatures often gather here: pixies, satyrs, and the occasional dryad. The crossing leads to the Summer Court's domain, but venturing deeper without guidance is dangerous.",
          "paths": [
            "The Forest's Edge",
            "The Heart of Midnight",
            "The Ruins of Iliyanbruen"
          ]
        },
        "The Ruins of Iliyanbruen": {
          "description": "Deep in the Wood, the crumbling towers and arches of the ancient elven kingdom of Iliyanbruen still stand, overgrown but beautiful. This is the heart of the fey presence in the Wood. The ruins are home to a community of moon elves who have recently returned from Evermeet, attempting to reclaim their ancestral home. They are led by Princess Ellarian, a young but fierce warrior. However, the ruins also harbour darker fey: a rival court of unseelie fey, led by a sinister creature known as the 'Prince of Frost', who claims the ruins as his own and challenges the elves for dominance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Fey Crossing"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Emerald Enclave (Lyra Silvanus)",
        "Thornback Goblins (Duskfang)",
        "Moon Elves of Iliyanbruen (Princess Ellarian)",
        "Unseelie Fey (Prince of Frost)",
        "Gray Wolf Uthgardt tribe"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Prince of Frost is not merely an unseelie fey; he is a fallen eladrin who was corrupted by the Shadowfell and seeks to drag all of Neverwinter Wood into the Plane of Shadow. The Heart of Midnight is his creation, a waypoint for his planned invasion. The Shadow Obelisk is also a key component of the Shadovar's network, linking the Wood to the fallen city of Thultanthar. If the obelisk is activated, it could summon a force of Shadovar shades to overwhelm the city.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Unfinished Works": {
      "name": "Unfinished Works",
      "basicInfo": "A vast construction belt west of Malagard where Baalzebul’s endless attempts at architectural perfection are built, abandoned, demolished, and begun again. Half-finished aqueducts cross collapsed forums, marble palaces stand behind forests of rotting scaffolds, and monumental statues remain trapped between rough stone and finished form. Possessed labor crews work without rest under infernal overseers, rebuilding structures that inevitably crack, sink into sludge, or collapse before completion. The district gradually thickens toward Malagard, making it one of the clearest manifestations of Baalzebul’s curse: magnificent ambition condemned to permanent incompletion.",
      "x": -2,
      "y": 7,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Maladomini — West",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "Several restricted construction sites within the Unfinished Works are secretly testing Baalzebul’s discovery that materials stolen directly from the Material Plane resist his curse longer than native Maladomini stone. The Ministers of Ruin disguise these imports as ordinary salvage while selected architects attempt to build components of Baalzebul’s imagined “Perfect City.” One sealed foundation has remained structurally flawless far longer than any infernal construction should, making it both Baalzebul’s greatest hope and evidence that his curse may actually be circumvented."
    },
    "The Queen's Palace": {
      "name": "The Queen's Palace",
      "basicInfo": "The obsidian seat of Vlaakith CLVII, rising from the god-corpse's sternum like a blade. The palace is a fortress of dark glass and petrified bone, its spires reaching into the astral sky. It is here that the Lich-Queen holds court, dispenses judgment, and consumes the souls of her most powerful followers. The palace is guarded by elite 'Vlaakith's Swords', githyanki knights who have sworn eternal loyalty in exchange for a sliver of her power. The halls are filled with the trophies of conquered worlds and the skulls of illithids. Time flows erratically within, and illusions of Vlaakith's past victories play along the corridors. Only the most trusted may enter, and even they must be prepared to offer their souls should the Queen demand it.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 8,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Tu'narath",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Throne of Bones": {
          "description": "The central audience chamber, a vast hall of polished obsidian. Vlaakith sits on a throne carved from the fused vertebrae of the dead god, a crown of ever-burning astral silver upon her head. She appears as a gaunt, regal githyanki, her eyes pits of black fire. Around her, the walls are lined with the mummified remains of past queens, each seated on a smaller throne. To approach is to feel the weight of thousands of years of tyranny. A permanent silence spell fills the room, broken only by the Queen's whispers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Soul Crucible",
            "The Gallery of Conquests"
          ]
        },
        "The Soul Crucible": {
          "description": "A hidden chamber beneath the throne, where Vlaakith performs the ritual that consumes the souls of her most powerful subjects. The room is a circle of black pillars, each carved with a githyanki rune of binding. At the center, a pool of liquid silver boils, fed by the souls of the consumed. A single sip from the pool can restore a lich's power, but a living creature would be annihilated. The chamber is guarded by two 'Soul Reavers', githyanki wraiths bound to the Queen's will.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of Bones"
          ]
        },
        "The Gallery of Conquests": {
          "description": "A long corridor lined with enchanted paintings that depict the githyanki's greatest victories: the rebellion against the illithids, the sacking of Menzoberranzan, and the subjugation of countless worlds. The paintings move, replaying the battles in silent glory. At the end of the gallery stands a statue of Gith herself, a colossal warrior woman brandishing a silver sword. Hidden niches contain the actual weapons used in these battles, each a powerful artifact. The gallery is often used for political indoctrination of young githyanki.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of Bones",
            "The Queen's Vaults"
          ]
        },
        "The Queen's Vaults": {
          "description": "A series of extradimensional spaces connected to the palace, where Vlaakith stores her greatest treasures: the original Pact of the Gith (the agreement between Gith and Tiamat), the sword of a fallen solar, and the phylactery of a rival lich she destroyed. Each vault is warded with unique traps and guarded by a bound elemental or golem. The Vault of the Pact is sealed with a riddle only a true githyanki can answer. The vaults also contain the 'Silver Flame of Gith', a relic that could theoretically restore the gith people to their original, mortal form.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gallery of Conquests"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Vlaakith CLVII (Lich-Queen)",
        "Vlaakith's Swords (Elite knights)",
        "Soul Reavers (Wraith guardians)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Soul Crucible is not merely a feeding mechanism; it is a phylactery for Vlaakith's own soul. As long as it is fed, she cannot be permanently destroyed. A daring rebel could sabotage the Crucible by introducing a single, pure soul – a sacrifice of true love – which would poison the pool and render Vlaakith mortal for a single day."
    },
    "The Creche of the Red Dragon": {
      "name": "The Creche of the Red Dragon",
      "basicInfo": "A massive hatchery and training ground located within the god-corpse's left lung, where young githyanki are raised, trained, and indoctrinated. The Creche is a brutal academy, with a strict curriculum of combat, psionics, and draconic lore. The corridors echo with the sounds of sparring, the crackle of psionic energy, and the roar of adolescent red dragons. The adult dragons, bound by the ancient pact with Tiamat, serve as both mentors and enforcers. The Creche is overseen by the 'Kith'rak Voss', a legendary githyanki knight who is both a stern teacher and a hidden dissident. Here, the strongest are forged into the warriors and dragon riders who will serve the githyanki empire.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": -5,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Tu'narath",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Hatchery": {
          "description": "A warm, humid chamber filled with massive, leathery eggs cradled in iron nests. Red dragon wyrmlings and githyanki hatchlings are raised side-by-side, forming a lifelong bond. The walls are decorated with murals of dragons in battle. The hatchery is overseen by ancient female githyanki, the 'Brood Mothers', who sing lullabies that weave loyalty to Vlaakith into the young minds.",
          "paths": [
            "The Training Grounds",
            "The Dragon's Roost"
          ]
        },
        "The Training Grounds": {
          "description": "A series of interconnected arenas where young githyanki are drilled in martial combat and psionics. Platforms of bone rise from a bottomless pit, and the rules are simple: fall, and you die. Instructors, scarred veterans with silver swords, bark orders. A central 'Duelling Grounds' hosts formal challenges, often to the death. The ground is stained with generations of blood. The walls are lined with the broken blades of the defeated.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hatchery",
            "Kith'rak Voss's Office"
          ]
        },
        "Kith'rak Voss's Office": {
          "description": "A spartan chamber overlooking the training grounds, filled with maps of illithid strongholds and tactical diagrams. Kith'rak Voss is a formidable warrior, his face a web of scars, his eyes burning with a cold intelligence. He is a harsh teacher, but he secretly believes Vlaakith's rule is a corruption of Gith's vision. His desk holds a hidden drawer containing a silver shard – a piece of a silver sword – that can be used to unlock the Vault of the Pact. He will test any who seem promising with cryptic questions, seeking allies for a silent rebellion.",
          "paths": [
            "The Training Grounds",
            "The Dragon's Roost"
          ]
        },
        "The Dragon's Roost": {
          "description": "A vast, open chamber at the top of the Creche, where the adult red dragons of the Tu'narath garrison rest and socialize. The roost is filled with the smell of smoke and brimstone, and the floor is scattered with the remains of large meals (often failed students). The dragons, while bound to the githyanki, are proud and fierce. Their leader, an ancient wyrm named Incendiax, lounges on a pile of treasure and deigns to speak to those who bring worthy gifts. He knows much about the Astral Plane's secrets and can be bargained with, but his loyalty is to the red dragon flight, not to any githyanki faction.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hatchery",
            "Kith'rak Voss's Office"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Kith'rak Voss (Headmaster)",
        "Brood Mothers",
        "Red Dragon Consort (Incendiax)",
        "Student cadres"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Kith'rak Voss is the leader of the Sha'sal Khou cell within Tu'narath. His hidden drawer contains not only the silver shard, but also a list of other githyanki who are disillusioned with the Lich-Queen. He is waiting for a sign – the emergence of a true heir to Gith – to start an open rebellion. He believes a githyanki who has escaped the soul consumption ritual and possesses a silver sword could unite the people."
    },
    "The Market of Lost Souls": {
      "name": "The Market of Lost Souls",
      "basicInfo": "A sprawling bazaar carved into the god-corpse's ribcage, where the githyanki trade in slaves, artifacts, and psionic oddities. The market is a chaotic maze of stalls made from salvaged nautiloid hulls, astral canvas, and petrified flesh. Here, one can buy anything – a mind flayer tadpole preserved in amber, a memory crystal containing a dead lover's last kiss, or a living spell bound in a cage of silence. The air is thick with the murmur of a hundred languages and the clink of astral diamonds. The market is ruled by the 'Shath'mag', the slave master guild, who ensure that all transactions are lawful (by githyanki standards). The market is also a hub for spies, mercenaries, and those seeking passage off Tu'narath.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -17,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Tu'narath",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Slave Pens": {
          "description": "A grim quarter at the market's edge, where sentient beings from across the multiverse are held in cages of psionically-shielded bone. Humans, elves, dwarves, and even captured mind flayers are sold here. The slave masters are brutal, their whips imbued with psychic pain. A small resistance, the 'Freed Ones', operates from a hidden alcove, helping escapees reach the Astral Wilderness. The leader, a former elven wizard named Illyria, uses illusion magic to smuggle slaves out disguised as cargo.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bazaar of Curiosities",
            "The Shath'mag Guildhall"
          ]
        },
        "The Bazaar of Curiosities": {
          "description": "The central market, a kaleidoscope of stalls. A tiefling sells bottled dreams; a githyanki crone peddles prophecies; a modron (unheard of in the Astral) trades in pure mathematical concepts. A permanent auction stage hosts the sale of rare artifacts, like a fragment of a Netherese mythallar or a sword that can cut time. The air is thick with the noise of haggling. Pickpockets (often trained psionic children) weave through the crowd. The market is neutral ground by decree of the Shath'mag, but a knife in the back is still a sale.",
          "paths": [
            "The Slave Pens",
            "The Gateway of Exiles"
          ]
        },
        "The Shath'mag Guildhall": {
          "description": "A fortified building of black bone, where the slave master guild holds its meetings. The hall is decorated with the branded flesh of failed escapees. The current guildmaster, a corpulent githyanki named Master Xyphos, sits on a throne of chained minds, his psionic powers so strong that he can dominate a room with a thought. He controls the flow of slaves in the Astral, and he charges a tax in 'souls' – a percentage of the slaves' life force. He is always looking for new sources of sentient cargo.",
          "paths": [
            "The Slave Pens",
            "The Gateway of Exiles"
          ]
        },
        "The Gateway of Exiles": {
          "description": "A massive archway at the market's far end, formed from the fused ribs of the dead god. It leads to the Astral Docks, where spelljamming ships and astral skiffs can be chartered. A permanent colour pool to the Outlands flickers within the arch, guarded by a squad of githyanki marines. Here, those who have been banished from Tu'narath or seek to leave can find passage – for a price. A small shrine to the Traveler (a god of chaos) is tucked into an alcove, where offerings are left for safe journeys.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bazaar of Curiosities",
            "The Shath'mag Guildhall"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Shath'mag Guild (Master Xyphos)",
        "The Freed Ones (Illyria)",
        "Various merchants"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Master Xyphos is not merely a slaver; he is a pawn of the mind flayers. An illithid tadpole, dormant and undetectable, lies in his brainstem, planted decades ago. At a signal from an elder brain, he will seize control of the market and open the Gateway of Exiles to an illithid invasion fleet. The Freed Ones have recently discovered this and are desperately seeking a way to remove the tadpole without killing Xyphos, which would trigger the invasion immediately."
    },
    "The Citadel of Gith": {
      "name": "The Citadel of Gith",
      "basicInfo": "A fortress-library buried in the god-corpse's heart, the holiest site in githyanki culture. It was here that Gith herself forged the original pact with Tiamat and penned the teachings that would become the foundation of their society. The Citadel is a place of pilgrimage and study, filled with the original manuscripts of the Githyanki code, the histories of the rebellion, and the reliquaries of saints. It is guarded by the 'Order of the Silver Sword', paladins who have sworn oaths directly to the memory of Gith, not to the Lich-Queen. The atmosphere is one of solemn reverence, and even Vlaakith treads carefully here. The inner sanctum contains the 'Mind-Wall of Gith', a psionic construct that repels illithids and holds the accumulated knowledge of the githyanki race. Access to the inner chambers requires proving one's lineage to Gith or passing a series of psionic trials.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Tu'narath",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Courtyard of the Pact": {
          "description": "An open courtyard at the Citadel's entrance, dominated by a colossal statue of Gith, her hand extended in oath. The walls are carved with the scenes of the Pact: Tiamat descending in a storm of chromatic fire, bestowing the dragons upon the githyanki. Pilgrims kneel here in meditation. A single, ancient red dragon, the 'Guardian of the Oath', coils around the statue, its scales shimmering with the Pact's magic. It will only allow those who speak the 'Words of Gith' to pass deeper.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Histories",
            "The Mind-Wall of Gith"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Histories": {
          "description": "A long, hallowed corridor lined with illuminated manuscripts and crystalline memory orbs. Here, the story of the githyanki is preserved: the enslavement by the illithids, the rebellion led by Gith and Zerthimon, the schism that created the githzerai, and the forging of the Pact. Githyanki scholars (many of them ancient, near-lich state) study here, adding new chapters to the ever-growing record. A hidden wing, accessible only to high-ranking members of the Order, contains the 'Apocrypha of Zerthimon', a version of history that depicts Zerthimon not as a traitor but as a necessary balance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Courtyard of the Pact",
            "The Reliquary of the First Knight"
          ]
        },
        "The Reliquary of the First Knight": {
          "description": "A chamber off the Hall of Histories, holding the relics of the greatest githyanki heroes. The silver sword of Gith's first general, Kith'rak Al'zahr, hangs on the wall, still glowing with the light of its first kill. A vial of Gith's blood, kept liquid by perpetual psionic vibration, is displayed in a crystal case. The relics are not merely artifacts; they are conduits to the spirits of the heroes, who can be summoned for advice by those who prove worthy. The guardian of the reliquary, a blind psionic knight named Yrth, can test a visitor's spirit by entering their mind.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Histories",
            "The Mind-Wall of Gith"
          ]
        },
        "The Mind-Wall of Gith": {
          "description": "The inner sanctum of the Citadel, a vast, domed chamber that houses a living psionic construct – a wall of shimmering silver energy. The wall is the accumulated knowledge, memories, and will of all githyanki who have ever lived. To touch the wall is to experience the collective consciousness of the race. It is also a powerful defensive weapon against illithids. Only those who have been purified by the trials may approach. The wall can answer any question about githyanki history or psionics, but it speaks in a torrent of overlapping voices. The Order's leader, the 'Voice of Gith', communes with the wall daily, interpreting its will.",
          "paths": [
            "The Courtyard of the Pact",
            "The Reliquary of the First Knight"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Order of the Silver Sword (paladins of Gith)",
        "Githyanki Historians",
        "The Voice of Gith (leader)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Apocrypha of Zerthimon contains a prophecy: that a child of both githyanki and githzerai will one day mend the schism and lead the united gith people to their true destiny beyond the Astral. This prophecy is the reason Vlaakith so ruthlessly suppresses the Sha'sal Khou; the Lich-Queen knows that the union of the two branches would produce an heir capable of challenging her. The Voice of Gith secretly protects the Apocrypha and waits for the prophesied child."
    },
    "The Astral Docks of Tu'narath": {
      "name": "The Astral Docks of Tu'narath",
      "basicInfo": "A sprawling anchorage built on the outermost ribs of the dead god, where astral vessels from across the planes dock. The Docks are a chaotic forest of mooring lines, cargo cranes, and gangplanks, with ships ranging from sleek githyanki astral skiffs to spelljammers of the gnomish and elven fleets. The air is filled with the shouts of dockworkers, the hum of psionic engines, and the occasional roar of a dragon taking flight. The Docks are governed by the 'Harbormaster', a githyanki bureaucrat named Thraxis, who enforces the docking fees and maintains the official trade logs – but a brisk black market thrives in the shadows of the larger vessels. Here, one can hire a ship, sell exotic goods, or simply stow away on a vessel heading to distant worlds.",
      "x": -31,
      "y": -22,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Tu'narath",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Mooring Fields": {
          "description": "A vast, open 'sky' where ships are tethered to the ribs by ropes of enchanted sinew. Floating platforms of bone serve as gangways and loading zones. Dozens of ships bob gently in the astral currents, their hulls illuminated by the glow of astral beacons. A constant flow of cargo – crates of astral crystals, caged beasts, and barrels of wine (a luxury in the Astral) – is carried by grunting ogre stevedores and bound elementals. The field is overseen by Harbormaster Thraxis's tower, a spire of fossilized coral.",
          "paths": [
            "Harbormaster's Tower",
            "The Free Trader's Enclave",
            "The Shadow Wharf"
          ]
        },
        "Harbormaster's Tower": {
          "description": "A tall, narrow spire of fossilized coral that rises from the largest docking platform. Here, Thraxis, a weary-looking githyanki with a perpetual ledger and a quill made from a roc's feather, records every ship's arrival and departure. He demands a docking fee of astral diamonds or 'equivalent value' (which he defines arbitrarily). He can be bribed with rare lore, which he collects. The tower has a scrying pool that monitors the entire Docks. A hidden compartment in his desk contains a secret treaty between the githyanki and a faction of the elven imperial navy.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mooring Fields"
          ]
        },
        "The Free Trader's Enclave": {
          "description": "A neutral zone built into a hollowed-out rib, where independent merchants (non-githyanki) are allowed to set up shop. The Enclave is a miniature Bazaar of Lost Souls, with stalls selling everything from surface-world spices to magical weapons from Krynn. The atmosphere is tense, as the githyanki guards watch for any sign of trouble. A small tavern, 'The Bottomless Mug', serves drinks that float in zero gravity. The barkeep, a jovial halfling named Rosie, has somehow thrived here for years and knows every rumour in the Astral.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mooring Fields",
            "The Shadow Wharf"
          ]
        },
        "The Shadow Wharf": {
          "description": "The hidden, illegal docking area, concealed behind an illusionary wall of star-stuff. Here, pirates, smugglers, and slavers conduct their business away from the Harbormaster's gaze. The Wharf is a rough place, lit by flickering, sickly flames and guarded by hired mercenaries. It is run by a scarred githyanki pirate captain, Kala, who offers no questions asked. She can arrange passage to any plane for a price, and she knows the secret routes that avoid the illithid patrols. The Wharf also houses a hidden entrance to the Tu'narath catacombs, for those who need to enter the city unseen.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mooring Fields",
            "The Free Trader's Enclave"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Harbormaster Thraxis",
        "Free traders",
        "Githyanki pirates (Captain Kala)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "The secret treaty in Thraxis's desk is a pact between Vlaakith and an elven admiral from Evermeet to allow githyanki raiding parties safe passage in exchange for intelligence on drow movements. This is a betrayal of the ancient elven-githyanki enmity, and if revealed, it could shatter the fragile trust between the githyanki and the surface elves. Thraxis is not merely a bureaucrat; he is Vlaakith's personal spy, and his true loyalty is to the Lich-Queen alone."
    },
    "The Catacombs of the Unworthy": {
      "name": "The Catacombs of the Unworthy",
      "basicInfo": "A sprawling necropolis carved into the dead god's lower limbs, where the githyanki bury their failed and disgraced. The Catacombs are a labyrinth of bone-lined tunnels and silent mausoleums, where the spirits of the unworthy linger in eternal shame. It is a place of sorrow and danger, for the githyanki's psionic nature persists after death, and the catacombs are filled with the psychic echoes of rage, regret, and despair. The Order of the Silver Sword sends penitent knights here to meditate on failure. Wild psionic phenomena, such as 'crying walls' (surfaces that emit mournful wails) and 'memory pools' (liquid that replays the death of a githyanki), are common. Deeper still, in the 'Crypt of the Nameless', the most despised individuals are walled up alive, their identities erased from history. The catacombs are also rumoured to be the hiding place of the 'Sha'sal Khou's leader, The Liberator, who uses the psychic noise to mask their presence.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": -44,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Tu'narath",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Weeping Path": {
          "description": "The entrance to the catacombs, a long, sloping tunnel where the walls are covered in a damp, salty resin that smells of tears. The psychic anguish is palpable, and the tunnel is known to cause sudden, uncontrollable weeping. A single, silent githyanki guardian, the 'Watcher of Sorrows', sits at the entrance, a living statue who has not moved in centuries. He will only allow entry to those who speak a name of the dead they seek. The path is lined with small niches containing ossuaries of lesser families.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of the Fallen",
            "The Crypt of the Nameless"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of the Fallen": {
          "description": "A massive, vaulted chamber filled with stone sarcophagi, each carved with the likeness of a fallen githyanki knight who died in dishonor. The spirits of these knights manifest as ghostly guardians, challenging intruders to ritual combat. Defeating them earns their respect and safe passage. A central altar holds the 'Sword of Broken Oaths', a cursed silver blade that causes its wielder to relive their greatest failure. The hall is also a place of trial for the Order of the Silver Sword; a knight who can wield the Sword without succumbing is deemed worthy of redemption.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weeping Path",
            "The Pool of Echoes"
          ]
        },
        "The Pool of Echoes": {
          "description": "A still, black pool in a side cavern, its surface perfectly reflective. Gazing into it replays the final moments of any dead githyanki whose name is spoken. The pool is used by the Order to teach lessons about the cost of failure. However, it can also show the deaths of those still alive, a dark omen. The ghost of a former Voice of Gith, drowned in the pool centuries ago, sometimes rises to speak riddles about the future.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of the Fallen",
            "The Crypt of the Nameless"
          ]
        },
        "The Crypt of the Nameless": {
          "description": "The deepest, most dreaded part of the catacombs. A series of sealed alcoves, each containing a living githyanki who has been entombed forever as punishment for ultimate betrayal. They are kept alive by psionic energy, unable to die, their names struck from all records. One alcove is empty – reserved for the greatest traitor of all, which some whisper is meant for the Lich-Queen herself. Here, in the oppressive silence, the Sha'sal Khou have hidden their leader, The Liberator, a githzerai monk who meditates in the void, shielded from psionic detection by the overwhelming sorrow.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weeping Path",
            "The Pool of Echoes"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Watcher of Sorrows (guardian)",
        "Spirits of the dishonoured",
        "The Sha'sal Khou (hidden)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Liberator is not merely a githzerai; they are the direct descendant of Zerthimon, possessing his original silver sword, which can cut through the mind of any githyanki and reveal the truth of their soul. They are waiting for the prophesied child of both races to appear, and their presence in the catacombs is a beacon of hope for the Sha'sal Khou. The empty alcove in the Crypt of the Nameless is a promise: one day, Vlaakith will be entombed there, and the githyanki will be free."
    },
    "The Crystal Spire": {
      "name": "The Crystal Spire",
      "basicInfo": "The seat of Kelemvor's power, a towering needle of crystalline stone rising from the heart of the City of Judgment. It is here that the Lord of the Dead sits upon his throne and pronounces the final judgment on every soul that comes before him. The Spire is a place of absolute neutrality and law; no emotion stirs within its walls, and even the most powerful deities must wait their turn to plead for a soul. The inner chambers are filled with the Books of the Dead, which record the deeds of every mortal that ever lived. The Spire is guarded by the 'Inevitable of Death', a powerful construct that ensures no soul escapes its judgment. Access is strictly limited, and only the most senior Doomguides and the psychopomps may enter.",
      "x": -8,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "The Fugue Plane",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Hall of Judgment": {
          "description": "A vast, circular chamber of polished obsidian at the base of the Spire. Kelemvor sits here, a towering, cloaked figure of bone and shadow, his eyes glowing with a cold, grey light. Before him extends a single, endless queue of waiting souls, each one approaching the throne when their name is called from the Book of the Dead. The Hall is silent except for the Lord's pronouncements. He speaks each soul's final destination with absolute certainty. The walls are carved with the 103 tenets of the Lord's Creed. A raised gallery allows the gods or their emissaries to observe, though Kelemvor brooks no interference.",
          "paths": [
            "The Library of Souls",
            "The Chamber of the Inevitable"
          ]
        },
        "The Library of Souls": {
          "description": "A subsidiary chamber where the Books of the Dead are stored. Each book is a massive, iron-bound tome that writes itself, recording every life from birth to death. Dozens of scribes – reanimated scholars – float among the shelves, updating the records. A single reading table holds a 'Book of the Condemned', which lists those whose judgment is temporarily suspended. A secret, hidden annex contains the 'Book of the False', detailing the contracts signed by souls who made Faustian bargains. Only Kelemvor and his most trusted Doomguide, the 'Master Librarian', can access it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Judgment",
            "The Chamber of the Inevitable"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of the Inevitable": {
          "description": "A secure vault beneath the Spire, where the Inevitable of Death resides. It is a towering, clockwork construct of adamantine and soul-stuff, its single eye a burning sapphire. It ensures that every soul that enters the Spire departs for its assigned plane, and none escape. The Inevitable is silent and unyielding, its only purpose to enforce Kelemvor's judgments. The walls are lined with the 'Seals of Binding', which imprison the souls of those who tried to flee, held in stasis forever. A control panel allows the Master Librarian to summon the Inevitable in emergencies.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Judgment",
            "The Library of Souls"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Kelemvor (Lord of the Dead)",
        "The Master Librarian (Doomguide)",
        "The Inevitable of Death (construct)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Book of the False contains a single blank page, waiting for a name. According to an ancient prophecy, this page is reserved for a soul that will come to Kelemvor having never been recorded – a being born outside of fate. This soul, it is said, will have the power to overturn the Pact of the Gods and rewrite the laws of death itself. The Master Librarian watches for this with a mixture of fear and hope."
    },
    "The City of Judgment": {
      "name": "The City of Judgment",
      "basicInfo": "A sprawling necropolis that surrounds the Crystal Spire, the City of Judgment is the primary processing center for all incoming souls. Its streets are a labyrinth of grey marble and obsidian, filled with countless administrative buildings, where the bureaucracy of death is conducted. Souls are sorted, their mortal records verified, and their passage to the appropriate outer plane arranged. The city is populated by the Skeletal Guard, the Doomguides, and legions of psychopomps. It is a place of quiet, tireless work, where the only sounds are the shuffling of the dead, the scratch of quills, and the distant tolling of the Bell of Passing. The city is divided into several districts, each handling a different aspect of death's administration. At its edge lies the Gate of the Faithless, a forbidding archway that leads to the Wall.",
      "x": -1,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "The Fugue Plane",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Receiving Halls": {
          "description": "A series of massive, interconnected halls where souls first arrive from the Material Plane. The air is cold and still, and the souls, disoriented, are gathered by psychopomps in grey robes. They are given a number and placed in a queue for initial processing. The walls are lined with mirrors that reflect the soul's last mortal moments. A small shrine to Jergal, Kelemvor's predecessor, offers a place for souls to make final prayers before judgment.",
          "paths": [
            "The Administrative District",
            "The Gate of the Faithless"
          ]
        },
        "The Administrative District": {
          "description": "The bureaucratic heart of the City, a grid of identical, grey stone buildings. Each building houses a department: Record Verification, Divine Claim Disputes, Faustian Contract Review, etc. Here, Doomguide clerks and psychopomps process the endless paperwork. The air is filled with the rustle of parchment and the quiet, patient hum of a thousand queues. A central archive, the 'Hall of Final Records', stores copies of every soul's file in extradimensional vaults.",
          "paths": [
            "The Receiving Halls",
            "The Embassy Quarter"
          ]
        },
        "The Embassy Quarter": {
          "description": "A district where the various gods and planar powers maintain embassies to contest or claim souls. Each embassy is a small, ornate building reflecting its deity's aesthetic: a mossy grove for Silvanus, a tiny iron fortress for Bane, a silken tent for Sune. Emissaries from these powers negotiate with Doomguide arbitrators. The atmosphere is tense, and assassinations are not unknown, though Kelemvor's law forbids violence. The Quarter also houses a small, neutral tavern, 'The Last Rest', where emissaries and psychopomps can meet in private.",
          "paths": [
            "The Administrative District",
            "The Gate of the Faithless"
          ]
        },
        "The Gate of the Faithless": {
          "description": "A massive, black iron archway at the city's edge, flanked by two colossal statues of weeping angels. It leads directly to the Wall of the Faithless. A constant, mournful wind blows through the gate, carrying the faint cries of those entombed. A squad of Skeletal Guard stands vigil, and no soul passes through willingly. Occasionally, a Doomguide leads a procession here to seal a new soul into the Wall. The arch is covered in the scratched, fading names of those who were once people.",
          "paths": [
            "The Receiving Halls",
            "The Embassy Quarter"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Doomguides (clerks and judges)",
        "Psychopomps (soul guides)",
        "Skeletal Guard",
        "Planar emissaries"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "A hidden room in the Administrative District, known only to the highest-ranking Doomguides, contains the 'Scale of Jergal'. This ancient artifact can weigh a soul against the sum total of its deeds, revealing any hidden acts of kindness or malice that even the Books of the Dead might have missed. It is used only in the most disputed cases, and its verdict is absolute. However, it has been tampered with once before; a devilish agent, disguised as a Doomguide, shifted the scale to condemn a soul to the Hells. Kelemvor still searches for the agent."
    },
    "The Wall of the Faithless": {
      "name": "The Wall of the Faithless",
      "basicInfo": "A terrible, endless wall of greenish stone, stretching across the grey plains of the Fugue Plane. Its surface is a mass of fused, semi-conscious souls, their faces contorted in eternal despair. These are the Faithless, those who lived without worshipping any god. Having no deity to claim them, they are bound into the Wall by Kelemvor, where they slowly dissolve into the stone, their identities and essence fading over centuries into oblivion. The Wall is a monument to the necessity of faith in the Realms. It is guarded by the Skeletal Guard, who prevent the souls from being stolen by demons or devils. The area around the Wall is perpetually cold and silent, and the cries of the Faithless are a constant, low murmur. A single, hidden path, the 'Path of the Unbeliever', leads along the Wall's base, and it is said that one can still speak to the fading consciousnesses within, though they can only whisper fragments of their past lives.",
      "x": -2,
      "y": 38,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "The Fugue Plane",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Base of the Wall": {
          "description": "A wide, empty field of grey dust at the foot of the Wall. A permanent fog hangs low, and the air is frigid. A few benches of petrified wood are scattered about for the rare visitors who come to mourn. The Skeletal Guard patrol silently, their empty eye sockets fixed on the souls above. A small, cracked altar stands here, where the occasional priest of Kelemvor performs rites of release for those whose sentence is almost complete. It is a place of profound emptiness and sorrow.",
          "paths": [
            "The Path of the Unbeliever",
            "The Gate of the Faithless"
          ]
        },
        "The Path of the Unbeliever": {
          "description": "A narrow, winding trail that runs along the foot of the Wall, accessible only through a secret door known to Kelemvor's most trusted servants. As one walks, the whispers of the Faithless grow clearer. They speak of their mortal lives, their loves, their regrets, and their final, desperate moments. Some offer fragments of lost knowledge in exchange for a moment of attention. A hidden alcove, the 'Niche of the Last Hope', contains a single, perpetually fresh flower, placed there by an unknown hand. It is the only living thing near the Wall.",
          "paths": [
            "The Base of the Wall"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of the Dissolved": {
          "description": "A subterranean chamber beneath the Wall, where the fully dissolved souls of the Faithless have pooled into a grey, psychic ooze. The ooze is a repository of forgotten memories, a collective unconscious of the faithless. Those who touch it risk losing their own identity, but can also experience vivid, fragmented memories of thousands of lives. A psychopomp scholar, an ancient, hooded figure, studies the ooze, trying to understand if anything of the Faithless truly remains. The Vault is heavily warded against intrusion.",
          "paths": [
            "The Path of the Unbeliever"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Skeletal Guard (patrols)",
        "The Faithless (trapped souls)",
        "The Psychopomp Scholar"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The single flower in the Niche of the Last Hope is a gift from Kelemvor himself. It is a soul that was once Faithless but performed a single, selfless act of such purity that even the Lord of the Dead could not condemn it. It waits, still conscious, for a mortal to offer it a true prayer. If that happens, it will bloom fully and be reborn as a celestial being of incredible power. The promise of this keeps Kelemvor hopeful, despite his grim duty."
    },
    "The Ashen Fields": {
      "name": "The Ashen Fields",
      "basicInfo": "The endless grey plains that make up the rest of the Fugue Plane, a desolate expanse of fine, grey dust and cold, still air. The Fields are mostly empty, but they serve as a buffer zone between the City of Judgment and the wild planes beyond, as well as a place where souls who have been judged but are awaiting transport linger. Occasionally, the Fields are disturbed by planar storms or the incursions of soul-thieves. A few notable landmarks dot the waste: the 'Pillar of Skulls', a remnant of a forgotten god of death who opposed Kelemvor, and the 'Lake of the Forgiven', a pool of silver water where souls whose sins were truly atoned are cleansed before entering their afterlife. Psychopomps travel the Fields in small caravans, protecting their charges.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -25,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "The Fugue Plane",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Pillar of Skulls": {
          "description": "A twisted spire of fused, blackened bone, hundreds of feet high, all that remains of the god Myrkul's original temple when he ruled the dead. The Pillar is a place of dark energy, avoided by most. The skulls still whisper dark secrets and utter curses. A cult of necromancers, the 'Ashen Circle', secretly gathers here to try and siphon the lingering divine power. They will attempt to capture any powerful soul that passes by. The ground around the pillar is eternally cold and absorbs light.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lake of the Forgiven",
            "The Caravan Way"
          ]
        },
        "The Lake of the Forgiven": {
          "description": "A still, perfectly circular lake of liquid silver, hidden in a shallow valley. Its waters are warm and soothing. Souls who have been absolved of their sins are bathed here by psychopomps, and they emerge with their ethereal forms restored to a pristine state before passing to their deity's realm. The lake is also a source of potent holy water. A solitary, ancient Doomguide, Brother Absolution, lives in a small hut on the shore, offering counsel to those who seek it. He possesses the ability to see the true weight of a soul's guilt.",
          "paths": [
            "The Pillar of Skulls",
            "The Caravan Way"
          ]
        },
        "The Caravan Way": {
          "description": "A faint, well-trodden path through the ash, marked by small, glowing runestones. It is used by psychopomp caravans: groups of cloaked guides leading lines of silent souls towards their designated colour pools. The caravans are protected by summoned spectral guardians. A small, fortified waystation, the 'Hospice of the Passing', offers shelter for psychopomps during planar storms and provides a place for souls to rest before their final journey. The waystation is run by a stern but compassionate erinyes named Penitence, who has foresworn her demonic nature to serve Kelemvor.",
          "paths": [
            "The Pillar of Skulls",
            "The Lake of the Forgiven"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Psychopomps (caravan guides)",
        "Ashen Circle (necromancer cult)",
        "Brother Absolution (hermit)",
        "Penitence (erinyes waystation keeper)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Pillar of Skulls is not merely a remnant; it is a phylactery for the last remaining vestige of Myrkul's consciousness. The Ashen Circle's rituals are unknowingly feeding this vestige, and if they succeed in their ultimate ritual, Myrkul could be reborn as a demilich on the Fugue Plane, challenging Kelemvor for control. Brother Absolution knows this and tries to deter visitors from the Pillar, but he cannot reveal the truth without risking a panic."
    },
    "The Silver Strand": {
      "name": "The Silver Strand",
      "basicInfo": "The pearl-grey beach where the silver sea of Lunia meets the base of the sacred mountain, and the first place every soul awakens after being called from the Fugue Plane to Celestia. The Strand stretches for what seems like infinite miles in both directions, its sand made of crushed pearl and moonstone that glows softly underfoot. Here the newly arrived petitioners gather in their thousands, blinking away the last of mortal death, and here the lantern archons drift down from the star-strewn sky to welcome them, each a mote of gentle silver fire. The sea itself is warm and calm, and its waves whisper reassurance. The Strand is the true threshold of the Seven Heavens: beyond it rises the mountain, its switchback roads climbing into golden Mercuria far above, and behind it lies only the endless holy water from which no soul wishes to turn back. It is a place of arrival, orientation, and first hope, watched over by the archon host who ensure that no fiend and no despair follows the souls up from death.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 5,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Lunia",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Waking Sands": {
          "description": "The stretch of glowing pearl-sand where souls first open their eyes in Celestia. Newly arrived petitioners lie scattered across the beach like sleepers waking from a long night, rising one by one in confusion and wonder. Lantern archons drift among them, their soft silver light easing the disorientation of death. The sand is warm and yielding, and the sound of the gentle sea calms even the most frightened soul. Doomguides of Kelemvor sometimes accompany a soul this far before returning to the Fugue Plane, handing their charge into the archons' care at the water's edge.",
          "paths": [
            "The Welcoming Circle",
            "The Tide Line of Sorrows"
          ]
        },
        "The Welcoming Circle": {
          "description": "A wide ring of standing moonstone pillars where petitioners gather after waking to be greeted and oriented by the archon host. A hound archon herald addresses the newcomers in a voice like distant bells, explaining the nature of the mountain and the long, hopeful climb ahead. Here souls are gently told which of their mortal burdens they may set down and which lessons they still carry. The circle glows brighter when a soul finally understands and accepts its own death, and a soft chime rings out across the beach.",
          "paths": [
            "The Waking Sands",
            "The Pilgrim's Road Head",
            "The Hall of First Questions"
          ]
        },
        "The Tide Line of Sorrows": {
          "description": "The wet margin where the silver waves lap the sand, and where petitioners come to wash away the last griefs of their mortal lives. Kneeling in the shallows, a soul may weep out its final regrets, and the water carries them away into the glowing depths. The sand here is strewn with small silver shells, each said to hold a single released sorrow. Lantern archons hover close, ready to comfort any soul whose grief runs too deep to release alone. It is the most emotional place on the Strand, and the most healing.",
          "paths": [
            "The Waking Sands",
            "The Cistern Stair"
          ]
        },
        "The Pilgrim's Road Head": {
          "description": "The place where the beach ends and the first switchback of the sacred road begins its climb up the mountain. A great archway of white stone, carved with the symbols of Tyr, Torm, Ilmater, and Bahamut, marks the threshold. Petitioners who have accepted their deaths and released their sorrows gather here to begin the ascent, some eager, some fearful. Warden archons check that no soul begins the climb still clutching a burden too heavy to bear, and gently turn back those who are not yet ready.",
          "paths": [
            "The Welcoming Circle",
            "The First Switchback"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of First Questions": {
          "description": "An open pavilion of silver columns just off the Welcoming Circle, where petitioners with lingering doubts may speak with deva counselors before beginning their climb. Here a soul may ask why it was judged worthy, what became of those it loved, or whether its sins are truly forgiven. The devas answer with patience and truth, for no lie can comfort a soul on the road to good. Many petitioners spend what feels like years in this hall, wrestling with their pasts, before they are ready to move on.",
          "paths": [
            "The Welcoming Circle"
          ]
        },
        "The Cistern Stair": {
          "description": "A spiral stair of luminous stone descending from the Tide Line down beneath the silver sea toward the drowned Cistern of First Tears. The stair is guarded at its head by a sword archon of unusual gravity, who permits only celestial healers and those on sanctioned errands to descend. The water does not flood the stair, held back by ancient blessing, but grows colder and heavier with each step down, and the silver light dims to a deep, sorrowful blue.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tide Line of Sorrows",
            "The Cistern of First Tears"
          ]
        },
        "The Cistern of First Tears": {
          "description": "A drowned cavern at the base of the Cistern Stair, its still black water distilled from the grief of every soul that ever wept in a righteous cause. A single drop can heal any wound or restore a shattered mind. The cavern is silent but for the drip of new tears seeping through the stone from the Tide Line above. Celestial healers come here to fill crystal vials under strict watch, for the water is as coveted by night hags and fiendish agents as it is precious to the good. An ancient pact-stone stands at the cavern's heart.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cistern Stair",
            "The Pact-Stone Vault"
          ]
        },
        "The Pact-Stone Vault": {
          "description": "A sealed chamber beyond the Cistern where the pact-stone marking the fiends' sworn boundary is kept and watched. The stone is carved with the oath the Lower Planes swore never to cross Celestia's shores, but its surface is slowly, imperceptibly eroding — and with it, the boundary weakens. Trumpet archons descend from the higher layers to inspect the erosion and argue in hushed, worried tones about its cause. The vault is the most secret place on the Strand, and its slow decay is the true reason the Blood War laps ever closer.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cistern of First Tears"
          ]
        },
        "The Lantern Roost": {
          "description": "A high shelf of glowing rock above the beach where the lantern archons gather between their duties, drifting together in a great luminous cloud like a galaxy come to rest. From the roost the archons watch over all the Strand, ready to descend at a moment's need. Petitioners sometimes climb the short path here to sit among the resting lanterns and feel their gentle warmth, a comfort that eases even the deepest fear of the long climb ahead.",
          "paths": [
            "The Welcoming Circle"
          ]
        },
        "The First Switchback": {
          "description": "The lowest bend of the sacred road, where the climb up Mount Celestia truly begins. The path rises gently at first, paved in white stone worn smooth by countless ascending souls, and offers the first backward view of the whole silver sea glittering under the eternal stars. Warden archons patrol this stretch, encouraging the weary upward and turning back any fiend or false pilgrim who slipped past the beach. From here the road winds up and out of sight toward the fortress of Nemorga's Watch.",
          "paths": [
            "The Pilgrim's Road Head"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Lantern Archon host",
        "Deva counselors",
        "Warden Archon patrols",
        "Petitioner throngs",
        "Doomguides of Kelemvor (visiting)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "1",
      "hiddenInfo": "The erosion of the Pact-Stone Vault is not natural. A night hag named Sorrowdrink has been ferrying stolen drops of the Cistern's tear-water to the pact-stone and using their concentrated grief to weaken the oath-magic that binds the fiends' boundary. Each drop dissolves a hair's breadth of the sworn line. If the archons do not discover her scheme, the fiends of the Blood War will one day be able to land upon the Silver Strand itself and slaughter the newly arrived souls before they ever begin the climb.",
      "known": true
    },
    "Nemorga's Watch": {
      "name": "Nemorga's Watch",
      "basicInfo": "The great fortress-city that stands upon the shores of Lunia and forms the first and mightiest line of Celestia's defense against the Lower Planes. Named for an ancient archon-lord who first mustered the mountain's guard, Nemorga's Watch is a citadel of white and silver stone, its walls rising sheer from the beach and its towers crowned with the eternal light of banked archon fire. From here the legions of the Silver Sea sally forth to meet any fiendish incursion that dares approach the sacred mountain, and here the wounded of the celestial host are borne to heal in the fortress infirmaries. The Watch is a city as much as a fortress: within its walls dwell the archon soldiery, the smiths who forge their arms, the healers who mend them, and the strategists who coordinate the endless vigil. It is a place of discipline and purpose, of hymns sung at the changing of the guard and blades kept ever ready. Though goodness rules here, the Watch never sleeps, for the fiends never rest.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -21,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Lunia",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Silver Gate": {
          "description": "The great outer gate of the fortress, a pair of towering doors of white metal chased with silver, standing open to welcome petitioners and allies but able to seal in an instant against any threat. Sword archons stand guard in gleaming array, and the gate is warded so that no creature of evil intent can pass its threshold without its true nature being revealed in a blaze of silver light. The road from the Silver Strand ends here, and pilgrims pass beneath the gate's blessing before beginning their climb.",
          "paths": [
            "The Muster Yard",
            "The Wall of Vigil"
          ]
        },
        "The Muster Yard": {
          "description": "A vast parade ground within the walls where the legions of the Silver Sea assemble before battle and drill in the intervals between. The yard rings with the ordered tramp of archon feet and the calls of the trumpet archons who command the host. Banners of the celestial gods hang from the surrounding walls, and a great silver bell tolls to summon the legions to muster. Petitioners who wish to join the eternal defense of the mountain are trained here, learning that even in paradise, goodness must be defended.",
          "paths": [
            "The Silver Gate",
            "The Armory of Dawn",
            "The Legion Barracks"
          ]
        },
        "The Wall of Vigil": {
          "description": "The seaward battlement of the fortress, from which archon sentinels watch the silver horizon for the first sign of fiendish sails or the churning of the water that heralds an incursion from below. The wall is lined with weapons of holy light and manned at every hour. From here, on a clear eternal night, one can see all the way to the pact-stone boundary far out in the sea, and the sentinels watch it with growing unease as it dims.",
          "paths": [
            "The Silver Gate",
            "The Beacon Tower"
          ]
        },
        "The Armory of Dawn": {
          "description": "The forge-halls and weapon-vaults of the Watch, where celestial smiths hammer out blades of blessed metal and armor that turns aside the weapons of Hell. The forges burn with a clean white fire drawn from the mountain's own holiness, and every weapon made here is quenched in Cistern-water so that it heals as readily as it harms. The master smith, an ancient hound archon named Brannoch, oversees the making of every blade destined for the front lines.",
          "paths": [
            "The Muster Yard",
            "The Reliquary of Broken Blades"
          ]
        },
        "The Legion Barracks": {
          "description": "The living quarters of the archon soldiery, austere but warm, where the defenders of Celestia rest between watches. Unlike the barracks of the Lower Planes, there is no fear or cruelty here; the archons care for one another as kin, and the halls ring with song and shared meals as often as with the sharpening of blades. Wounded archons recover here under the tending of their comrades before returning to the vigil.",
          "paths": [
            "The Muster Yard",
            "The Infirmary of Mercy"
          ]
        },
        "The Infirmary of Mercy": {
          "description": "The great healing-hall of the fortress, where archons wounded in the Blood War are borne to mend. Priests of Ilmater and celestial healers work here without rest, their hands glowing with restorative light, and the air is sweet with the scent of Cistern-water. Even fiends captured in battle are treated here, for the archons will not deny mercy even to their enemies — a policy that has occasionally been exploited by cunning devils feigning defeat.",
          "paths": [
            "The Legion Barracks",
            "The Chapel of the Even Hand"
          ]
        },
        "The Beacon Tower": {
          "description": "The tallest spire of Nemorga's Watch, atop which burns a great beacon of archon fire visible across all of Lunia's sea. The beacon serves both to guide lost petitioners toward the mountain and to signal the muster of the legions when danger threatens. The tower's keeper, a trumpet archon named Vaellum, has stood watch here since the fortress was raised, and knows the shape of every threat that has ever crossed the silver water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wall of Vigil",
            "The War Council Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The War Council Chamber": {
          "description": "A round hall near the summit of the Beacon Tower where the archon commanders and the emissaries of the celestial gods plan the defense of the mountain. A great map of the silver sea and the Lower Planes' approaches covers the floor, marked with the positions of every legion and every known fiendish force. Here the hard truths of the eternal war are spoken plainly, for no deceit can survive in Celestia, and here the growing threat of the eroding pact-stone is debated with mounting alarm.",
          "paths": [
            "The Beacon Tower"
          ]
        },
        "The Chapel of the Even Hand": {
          "description": "The great temple at the heart of Nemorga's Watch, dedicated jointly to Tyr, Torm, and Ilmater, where the defenders of Celestia pray before battle and give thanks after. The chapel is a place of profound peace amid the fortress's readiness, its altar a single block of white stone lit from within. Paladins of the living world who die in righteous cause sometimes wake here rather than on the beach, called directly to the defense of the mountain they served in life.",
          "paths": [
            "The Infirmary of Mercy",
            "The Hall of the Fallen Faithful"
          ]
        },
        "The Reliquary of Broken Blades": {
          "description": "A solemn vault beneath the Armory where the shattered weapons of archons slain in the Blood War are laid to rest with honor. Each broken blade is inscribed with the name of its bearer and the cause for which it fell. The reliquary is a quiet, sacred place, and the smiths sometimes reforge a fragment of an honored blade into a new weapon, so that the courage of the fallen passes on to the next defender.",
          "paths": [
            "The Armory of Dawn"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of the Fallen Faithful": {
          "description": "A memorial gallery attached to the Chapel, honoring the mortal paladins, clerics, and champions of the living world who died defending the causes of Celestia's gods. Their names are carved into pillars of white stone, and their most famous deeds are depicted in luminous mosaics. Petitioners who were such champions in life often come here and are startled to find themselves remembered.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chapel of the Even Hand"
          ]
        },
        "The Gaol of Repentance": {
          "description": "A rare and unusual place: a prison within paradise, where captured fiends who have shown a genuine flicker of the desire to change are held and slowly, patiently offered the chance at redemption. Most never take it, and the archons keep them here rather than destroy them, for even a fiend's free will is sacred. The gaol is watched with extreme care, for a fiend feigning repentance is among the most dangerous things in the multiverse.",
          "paths": [
            "The Infirmary of Mercy"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Legions of the Silver Sea",
        "Sword Archon garrison",
        "Trumpet Archon command",
        "Celestial Smiths of the Armory",
        "Church of the Triad (Tyr, Torm, Ilmater)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "One of the 'repentant' fiends held in the Gaol of Repentance is a pit fiend named Vharzal who has feigned the desire to change for over a century, patiently earning the trust of his gentle keepers. He is in truth an agent of the Blood War, mapping the fortress's defenses and the muster patterns of the legions, and communicating them to Hell through a sliver of soul-stone hidden within his own body. When the pact-stone finally fails, his intelligence will let the fiends strike Nemorga's Watch at its weakest hour.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Court of Stars-Below": {
      "name": "The Court of Stars-Below",
      "basicInfo": "A shining diplomatic city built on a great terrace of white stone above the Silver Strand, where the good powers of the multiverse send their emissaries to treat with one another and with the archon host. Where Nemorga's Watch is Celestia's fist, the Court of Stars-Below is its open hand: a place of embassy, negotiation, and alliance among the Upper Planes. The Court takes its name from the silver constellations of Lunia's sky, which are mirrored in the luminous mosaic that paves its central plaza, so that ambassadors walk upon a reflection of the heavens. Here the guardinals of Elysium, the eladrin of Arborea, the aasimon of the higher spheres, and the celestial servants of every good god gather to coordinate the endless struggle against evil, to settle disputes among the forces of good, and to welcome mortal heroes summoned to the planes. The Court is a place of beauty, courtesy, and quiet intrigue, for even among the good, not every interest aligns, and the shape of goodness itself is sometimes fiercely debated.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 18,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Lunia",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Mirror Plaza": {
          "description": "The great central square of the Court, paved with a luminous mosaic that perfectly reflects the silver constellations of Lunia's sky, so that those who cross it seem to walk among the stars. Emissaries of the good powers gather here to meet informally, and grand ceremonies of alliance are held beneath the reflected heavens. The plaza is ringed with the embassy-houses of the Upper Planes, each in the style of its home, and the air hums with the courteous murmur of a hundred good tongues.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Concord",
            "The Embassy Row",
            "The Petitioner's Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Concord": {
          "description": "The great domed assembly-hall where the formal councils of the good powers are held. A ring of seats, each carved for a different celestial race and each of exactly equal height, surrounds a central speaking-floor, for in the Court of Stars no power holds precedence over another. Here the strategies of the Upper Planes against Hell and the Abyss are debated, and here the occasional bitter dispute between good powers is aired and, ideally, resolved. The hall is warded so that no word spoken within it can carry to hostile ears.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mirror Plaza",
            "The Chamber of Quiet Words"
          ]
        },
        "The Embassy Row": {
          "description": "A grand avenue lined with the embassy-houses of the good planes: the sunlit garden-embassy of Elysium's guardinals, the wild singing-tower of Arborea's eladrin, the crystalline hall of the aasimon, and more. Each embassy is a small piece of its home plane, and to walk the Row is to sample the whole spectrum of goodness. Ambassadors and their retinues come and go, and the Row is the best place in all of Lunia to hear news from across the Upper Planes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mirror Plaza",
            "The Guardinal Garden",
            "The Eladrin Spire"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of Quiet Words": {
          "description": "A small, private council-room adjoining the Hall of Concord, where the most sensitive negotiations among the good powers are held away from the wider assembly. Its walls are hung with tapestries that absorb sound, and its single table is carved from a wood that is said to compel gentle honesty. Here disputes too delicate for the open hall are quietly settled, and here the darkest news of the war is shared among those who most need to know it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Concord"
          ]
        },
        "The Petitioner's Gallery": {
          "description": "A wing of the Court set aside for mortal heroes summoned to the Upper Planes and for exceptional petitioners called to serve the good powers directly. Here champions of the living world are received, briefed, equipped, and sent forth on errands too important to entrust to celestials alone. The gallery is a place of excitement and gravity, where mortals stand awed among the servants of the gods and receive quests that may shape the fate of worlds.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mirror Plaza",
            "The Armory of Champions"
          ]
        },
        "The Guardinal Garden": {
          "description": "The embassy of Elysium, a walled garden of impossible fertility where the guardinal emissaries hold their audiences among flowering trees and gentle beasts. The garden is a fragment of Elysium's own peace, and simply sitting within it soothes the most troubled heart. The guardinal ambassador, a noble avoral named Skyreach, is among the wisest and most beloved figures in the Court.",
          "paths": [
            "The Embassy Row"
          ]
        },
        "The Eladrin Spire": {
          "description": "The embassy of Arborea, a soaring tower of living crystal that sings in the wind and shifts its colors with the moods of those within. The eladrin ambassadors are passionate and mercurial, and their embassy is the liveliest on the Row, given to feasts, music, and impassioned debate. Their fierce, chaotic goodness sometimes puts them at odds with Celestia's lawful host, and the friction between them is a constant, if usually friendly, feature of the Court's politics.",
          "paths": [
            "The Embassy Row"
          ]
        },
        "The Armory of Champions": {
          "description": "A vault beneath the Petitioner's Gallery where the celestial powers keep arms and relics to equip the mortal heroes they summon. Blessed blades, wings of flight, cloaks of protection, and stranger gifts hang ready for those deemed worthy. The armory's keeper, a hound archon named Wist, assesses each champion with a shrewd eye and grants them only what their quest and their character warrant — no more, lest the gift become a temptation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Petitioner's Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Records-Kept": {
          "description": "The great archive of the Court, where the treaties, alliances, and shared histories of the good powers are preserved. Deva archivists tend scrolls and crystals that record every accord ever struck among the Upper Planes, and every betrayal — rare as those are — is likewise remembered, that its lesson not be lost. The hall is a place of study and reflection, and its keepers hold the long memory of the alliance of good.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Concord"
          ]
        },
        "The Beacon of Summoning": {
          "description": "A high tower topped with a great lens of blessed crystal, through which the celestial powers reach across the planes to call worthy mortals to their service. When a champion is summoned, the beacon flares with light, and the mortal awakens in the Petitioner's Gallery below. The beacon's keepers, a circle of devas, choose whom to call with enormous care, for to summon a mortal to the planes is to change the course of two worlds at once.",
          "paths": [
            "The Petitioner's Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Garden of Reconciliation": {
          "description": "A quiet retreat at the edge of the Court where good powers who have fallen into dispute may walk together in peace until their differences soften. The garden is tended by a solitary, ancient deva who speaks little but whose mere presence eases anger and pride. Many a rift among the forces of good has been healed on these still paths, and the archons regard the garden's keeper as one of the most important figures in the whole struggle against evil.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mirror Plaza"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Archon diplomatic corps",
        "Guardinal emissaries of Elysium",
        "Eladrin emissaries of Arborea",
        "Aasimon envoys",
        "Summoned mortal champions"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "1",
      "hiddenInfo": "One of the long-serving ambassadors in the Court of Stars-Below is not what it appears. An arcanaloth of the yugoloths, master shapeshifter and mercenary of the Blood War, has for decades impersonated a minor celestial envoy, feeding the deliberations of the good powers to the highest bidder among the fiends. It has never acted against the Court directly — only listened, and sold what it heard — which is precisely why it has never been caught. The Garden of Reconciliation's ancient keeper has begun to sense a subtle wrongness in one recurring visitor, but has not yet named it.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Ascending Road": {
      "name": "The Ascending Road",
      "basicInfo": "The sacred switchback road that climbs the flank of Mount Celestia from the beaches of Lunia up toward golden Mercuria, and the pilgrim-communities that have grown along its lower length. Every soul that ascends the mountain walks this road, and over the ages the way has become dotted with rest-houses, shrines, and small settlements of petitioners who paused in their climb to serve those who come after. The lower Ascending Road is the busiest place in Lunia after the Strand itself, a ribbon of white stone winding ever upward, crowded with pilgrims of every mortal race and faith united in their journey toward the good. Warden archons patrol its length, guiding the weary and turning back the unworthy, and the road grows brighter and steeper as it climbs. To travel the Ascending Road is to watch the silver night of Lunia slowly warm toward the golden dawn of the layer above — a passage that can take a soul what feels like a mortal lifetime, or the blink of an eye, depending on the burdens it still carries.",
      "x": 1,
      "y": 41,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Lunia",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Lower Switchbacks": {
          "description": "The first long climb of the road above the beach, a series of gentle bends paved in white stone and lined with silver lamps. Here the newly departed petitioners begin their ascent in great numbers, and the way is crowded with pilgrims helping one another upward. Warden archons walk among them, offering encouragement and easing the burdens of the weary. The view back down to the Silver Strand grows more breathtaking with every bend.",
          "paths": [
            "The First Rest-House",
            "The Shrine of the Turning Point"
          ]
        },
        "The First Rest-House": {
          "description": "The lowest of the pilgrim rest-houses, a large open hall of white stone where climbing souls may pause, share food and stories, and gather their strength. It is run by petitioners who chose to remain and serve rather than continue their own ascent, and its hearth-fire never dies. The rest-house is a warm and joyful place, full of the songs and tales of a thousand mortal worlds, and many a discouraged pilgrim has found the heart to continue here.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lower Switchbacks",
            "The Almsgiver's Hollow"
          ]
        },
        "The Shrine of the Turning Point": {
          "description": "A small roadside shrine at a sharp bend in the road, dedicated to the moment of moral choice — the instant when a soul first turned away from evil and toward the good. Pilgrims stop here to remember their own turning points, and the shrine's altar glows more brightly for each soul that recalls the choice that set it on the path to the mountain. A gentle deva tends the shrine and listens to the stories pilgrims wish to share.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lower Switchbacks",
            "The Middle Switchbacks"
          ]
        },
        "The Almsgiver's Hollow": {
          "description": "A sheltered dell just off the road where a community of Ilmatari petitioners has built a haven for the weakest and most burdened pilgrims — those souls so weighed down by mortal suffering that they cannot climb alone. Here the almsgivers take on a portion of each struggling soul's burden, carrying it upward on their behalf, so that none is left behind on the road to good. It is one of the most quietly heroic places in all of Celestia.",
          "paths": [
            "The First Rest-House"
          ]
        },
        "The Middle Switchbacks": {
          "description": "The steepening middle stretch of the road, where the silver light of Lunia begins to warm toward gold and the air grows lighter and sweeter. The climb is harder here, and only those pilgrims who have truly released their mortal burdens can manage it with ease; the rest must labor, and some turn back for a time to rest below. The archon patrols grow fewer, for the souls who reach this height need less guarding.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shrine of the Turning Point",
            "The Watchtower of the Way",
            "The Pilgrim's Overlook"
          ]
        },
        "The Watchtower of the Way": {
          "description": "A slender tower of white stone standing where the road makes its highest turn in Lunia, from which warden archons keep watch over the whole ascending length below. The tower's keeper, a warden archon named Halloran, has guided souls up this road since before memory, and knows the face of every kind of pilgrim who has ever climbed it — and the subtle signs of the rare fiend or false soul who tries to slip upward in disguise.",
          "paths": [
            "The Middle Switchbacks",
            "The Border of Dawn"
          ]
        },
        "The Pilgrim's Overlook": {
          "description": "A wide ledge jutting from the mountainside where climbing souls pause to look back over the whole of Lunia — the silver sea, the distant fortress of Nemorga's Watch, the shining Court of Stars, and the glittering Strand where they first awoke. Many pilgrims weep here, not from sorrow but from the enormity of how far they have come. The overlook is a place of gratitude and reflection, and a favorite resting spot on the long climb.",
          "paths": [
            "The Middle Switchbacks"
          ]
        },
        "The Border of Dawn": {
          "description": "The high threshold where Lunia's silver night finally gives way to Mercuria's golden dawn, and the road passes from the first layer to the second. The light here is a breathtaking blend of silver and gold, and pilgrims who cross it feel a surge of renewed hope. A great arch of pale stone marks the boundary, and warden archons of both layers keep a joint watch here, for this passage is the natural point at which to guard the ascent.",
          "paths": [
            "The Watchtower of the Way"
          ]
        },
        "The Hermit's Path": {
          "description": "A narrow side-trail branching from the middle switchbacks, leading to the cells of solitary petitioners who have chosen to pause their ascent and contemplate the good in silence and isolation. These hermits are sought out by pilgrims wrestling with hard questions, for their long meditation has given them unusual insight. The path is quiet and little-traveled, and those who walk it do so in respectful silence.",
          "paths": [
            "The Middle Switchbacks"
          ]
        },
        "The Fallen Pilgrim's Rest": {
          "description": "A somber waystation for the rare soul who, partway up the road, discovers a corruption or unresolved sin it cannot yet overcome, and must pause its ascent — or even descend to try again. Rather than shame such souls, the archons have built this gentle place for them, where they may rest, reflect, and prepare to resume the climb when they are ready. No soul is ever truly turned away from the mountain; it may only be asked to wait.",
          "paths": [
            "The First Rest-House"
          ]
        },
        "The Singing Falls": {
          "description": "A cascade of silver Cistern-water that pours down the mountainside beside a stretch of the road, its waters singing with a sound like distant celestial choirs. Pilgrims drink from the falls to renew their strength, and the water's song is said to carry the prayers of those who listen upward toward the summit. The falls are one of the most beautiful sights on the Ascending Road, and their music can be heard for a great distance in either direction.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lower Switchbacks"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Warden Archon patrols",
        "The Almsgivers of Ilmater",
        "Rest-house petitioner communities",
        "Solitary hermits of the Way",
        "Pilgrim throngs"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "1",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Fallen Pilgrim's Rest hides a painful secret known only to its archon keepers: a small number of the souls who pause there are not merely delayed but are being subtly drawn back downward by a corruption they carry without knowing it — a taint planted in life by a devil's bargain their descendants forgot. These souls cannot ascend until the ancient bargain is broken in the living world, far away and long after their deaths. The archons keep records of each such soul, hoping that someday a mortal hero will trace and shatter the old contracts and free the pilgrims to finish their climb.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Palace of Platinum Scales": {
      "name": "The Palace of Platinum Scales",
      "basicInfo": "The great celestial court of Bahamut the Platinum Dragon, god of good dragons, justice, and protection, rising from the golden hills of Mercuria as a fortress of gleaming platinum and white marble. The palace is built to a dragon's scale, its halls vast enough for the greatest wyrms to walk with folded wings, its gates high enough for silver dragons to soar through in formation. From here Bahamut watches over metallic dragonkind across the multiverse, receives the souls of good dragons who have died in righteous cause, and dispatches his seven great gold wyrm consorts on errands of mercy and war. The palace is a place of noble splendor without vanity, its treasures held not for greed but as symbols of the honor its master embodies. Petitioner dragons, dragonborn souls, and the rare mortal draconic champion are welcomed here, and the courts ring with the deep, resonant voices of dragons in council. Though Bahamut himself is often absent, wandering the planes disguised as an old hermit accompanied by seven canaries, his presence suffuses the palace, and none who enter it can speak falsehood or harbor cruelty without the platinum walls ringing in silent rebuke.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Mercuria",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Platinum Gate": {
          "description": "The towering entrance to Bahamut's palace, a pair of doors of solid platinum each carved with the likeness of a metallic dragon in flight, standing high and wide enough for the greatest wyrm to pass. The gate is guarded by seven gold dragons, Bahamut's consorts when they are present, and by an honor guard of dragonborn petitioner-knights. The doors open only to those of honest heart, and to lie in their presence is to be gently but firmly barred from entry.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Wings",
            "The Ward of the Seven Canaries"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Wings": {
          "description": "The vast central hall of the palace, its ceiling lost in golden light far overhead, its floor polished to a mirror that reflects the wings of the dragons who fill it. Here good dragons of every metallic kind gather in council, their voices rolling like distant thunder as they debate the defense of dragonkind and the wider struggle against Tiamat's chromatic brood. The hall can hold a hundred wyrms with room to spare, and its acoustics carry every word with perfect clarity.",
          "paths": [
            "The Platinum Gate",
            "The Throne of the Platinum Father",
            "The Hoard of Honor"
          ]
        },
        "The Throne of the Platinum Father": {
          "description": "The dais at the heart of the Hall of Wings, where Bahamut sits in his true form when he holds court — a dragon of scales like living platinum, his eyes the deep blue of a twilight sky. More often the throne stands empty, for the Platinum Father wanders the mortal worlds in the guise of an old beggar. When it is empty, his eldest gold consort presides in his stead, and the throne glows softly to show that his attention, at least, is present even when his body is not.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Wings",
            "The Chamber of Just Judgment"
          ]
        },
        "The Hoard of Honor": {
          "description": "The treasure-vault of the palace, but unlike the greedy hoards of evil dragons, this is a gallery of honor: every item within was won or given in a deed of great virtue, and each is kept as a monument to the courage of its former bearer. Blessed blades, dragon-scale shields, and relics of legendary heroes hang in ordered array, tended by petitioner curators. Nothing here is taken for wealth; it is preserved as memory, and the greatest treasures are simply the stories attached to each piece.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Wings",
            "The Vault of Fallen Wyrms"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of Just Judgment": {
          "description": "A council-room where disputes among good dragons, and grievances between dragons and the other good powers, are heard and settled according to Bahamut's principles of honor and mercy. A gold dragon justiciar presides, and the chamber is warded so that only truth may be spoken within. Even the proud and ancient wyrms who come here submit to its judgments, for to defy Bahamut's justice is to forsake the very honor that makes them good.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of the Platinum Father",
            "The Gallery of Oaths"
          ]
        },
        "The Ward of the Seven Canaries": {
          "description": "A curious garden-ward near the gate, filled with singing birds, where Bahamut's seven gold dragon consorts take the form of canaries to accompany him on his wanderings among mortals. When the consorts are present in dragon form, the garden is empty of birds; when they are away with their master, seven particularly bright canaries sing here, a sign to all that the Platinum Father walks the mortal world in disguise. Petitioners find great comfort in this humble garden.",
          "paths": [
            "The Platinum Gate"
          ]
        },
        "The Aerie of the Metallic Host": {
          "description": "The high roosts and landing-terraces where the palace's dragon defenders rest between their patrols of Mercuria's golden skies. Silver, gold, bronze, brass, and copper dragons share the aerie in noble comity, and young petitioner-wyrms are trained here in the arts of aerial defense. The aerie offers a breathtaking view over the whole of Mercuria, and the constant coming and going of dragons fills the golden air with the beat of mighty wings.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Wings"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of Fallen Wyrms": {
          "description": "A solemn crypt beneath the palace where the souls of good dragons who have finally chosen to end their long celestial existence are honored, their essences released gently into the light of the higher layers. The vault is quiet and reverent, lined with platinum plaques bearing the names and deeds of dragons who spanned mortal ages. Petitioner dragons come here to remember their fallen kin, and the archons hold this place in special respect.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hoard of Honor"
          ]
        },
        "The Gallery of Oaths": {
          "description": "A long hall hung with the crystallized vows of paladins, dragonborn knights, and dragon-riders who swore their lives to Bahamut's service. Each oath glows with a soft light, brightest for those still faithfully kept in the living world and dimming should the swearer stray. The gallery's keeper, an ancient gold wyrm named Aurendil, watches the fading oaths with concern, for a paladin's fall is felt here as a small extinguishing.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chamber of Just Judgment"
          ]
        },
        "The Roost of the Old Hermit": {
          "description": "A small, humble chamber, almost a hovel, kept within the grand palace at Bahamut's own insistence — a plain room with a straw pallet and a beggar's staff, mirroring the guise he wears when he walks among mortals. It reminds all who serve him that true nobility needs no gold, and that the greatest god of dragons chooses to move through the world as its lowliest wanderer. Petitioners are moved to humility simply by standing in its doorway.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of the Platinum Father"
          ]
        },
        "The Hatchery of Second Lives": {
          "description": "A warm, luminous chamber where the souls of good dragons slain too young in the mortal world are cradled and healed before being offered the choice to ascend the mountain or be reborn into the living world to try again. Petitioner nurses of every metallic kind tend the wounded dragon-souls with infinite patience. It is one of the most hopeful places in the palace, for here a life cut short is given the chance to become something greater.",
          "paths": [
            "The Aerie of the Metallic Host"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Bahamut's dragon court",
        "The Seven Gold Consorts",
        "Dragonborn petitioner-knights",
        "The Metallic Host",
        "Church of Bahamut (celestial seat)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Gallery of Oaths holds a terrible secret in one dimmed and cracked crystal: the vow of a paladin who did not merely fall but was deliberately corrupted, his broken oath twisted into a weapon by the same forces that seek the Sepulcher of the Unbroken Oath elsewhere in Mercuria. As long as this cracked oath remains in the gallery, it slowly leaks a subtle wrongness into the crystallized vows around it, and Aurendil the keeper has begun to fear that the entire gallery — and with it the faith of countless living paladins — could be poisoned from within if the corrupted oath is not found and destroyed.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Processional of the Martyred Dead": {
      "name": "The Processional of the Martyred Dead",
      "basicInfo": "The great memorial highway of Mercuria, a broad golden road lined for uncounted miles with the tombs, monuments, and cenotaphs of those who died in righteous cause across every mortal world. The Processional is the busiest place in the second layer, for every soul ascending from Lunia passes along it, and every archon returning from the Blood War bears its fallen here to be honored. The road is not a place of grief but of remembrance turned to resolve: the golden light transmutes mourning into purpose, and pilgrims who walk it emerge with renewed determination to honor the sacrifices of those who came before. Along its length stand memorials of every kind — humble roadside cairns for forgotten heroes, soaring mausoleums for legendary champions, and vast joint tombs for the archon legions who fell together in single terrible battles. To walk the Processional is to receive the whole history of good's long war against evil, written in stone and gold, and to understand that one is only the latest link in an unbroken chain of the courageous dead.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 42,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Mercuria",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gate of Golden Dawn": {
          "description": "The lower entrance to the Processional, where the road from Lunia's Border of Dawn passes into Mercuria proper beneath a great arch of amber stone. Souls arriving from the layer below pause here to adjust to the warm golden light, and warden archons of both layers welcome them onward. The gate is inscribed with the names of the first martyrs ever honored on the road, and its light seems to embrace each pilgrim who passes beneath it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Avenue of Cairns",
            "The Legion Cenotaph"
          ]
        },
        "The Avenue of Cairns": {
          "description": "The lower stretch of the Processional, lined with the humble cairns of countless ordinary souls who died in small acts of great courage — the farmer who sheltered refugees, the child who warned a village, the soldier who held a bridge alone. These are the forgotten heroes of the mortal worlds, remembered here where they were nowhere else. Pilgrims often stop to read the simple inscriptions and are moved to tears by the quiet enormity of so much unsung sacrifice.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Golden Dawn",
            "The Hall of Named Heroes"
          ]
        },
        "The Legion Cenotaph": {
          "description": "A vast circular memorial to the archon legions who have fallen defending Celestia, its walls carved with the sigils of every unit lost in the endless war. Returning archons bear their fallen comrades here, and the cenotaph's eternal flame burns in their honor. Trumpet archons hold solemn ceremonies of remembrance here after every major battle of the Blood War, and the memorial grows ever larger as the war grinds on.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Golden Dawn",
            "The Hall of Named Heroes"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Named Heroes": {
          "description": "The grand central mausoleum of the Processional, where the greatest champions of the mortal worlds are entombed with full honors — the legendary paladins, the saint-kings, the martyr-prophets whose deeds shaped history. Their effigies are rendered in gold and platinum, and their most famous deeds are depicted in luminous mosaics along the walls. Petitioners who were such heroes in life are sometimes startled to find their own tombs already prepared and waiting.",
          "paths": [
            "The Avenue of Cairns",
            "The Legion Cenotaph",
            "The Vault of the Unremembered"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of the Unremembered": {
          "description": "A solemn chamber dedicated to the martyrs whose names were lost to history but whose sacrifices still shaped the world. A single eternal flame burns here for all of them, and a deva keeper tends the vault, whispering the deeds of the nameless dead so that they are never truly forgotten. It is a place of profound humility, reminding all who enter that goodness is upheld far more often by the unremembered than by the celebrated.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Named Heroes"
          ]
        },
        "The Sculptor's Terrace": {
          "description": "A workshop-terrace beside the road where celestial sculptors and petitioner artisans carve the tombs and monuments of the Processional. The terrace rings with the gentle sound of chisels, and its masters work not for pay but out of devotion, capturing the likeness and the story of each honored soul in golden stone. New arrivals of great deed are sometimes brought here to sit for their own memorials, an experience both humbling and strange.",
          "paths": [
            "The Avenue of Cairns"
          ]
        },
        "The Reflecting Road": {
          "description": "A stretch of the Processional paved with polished amber that reflects not the walker's face but the faces of those they have loved and lost, walking beside them. Grieving pilgrims come here to feel the presence of the dead they mourn, and many find comfort in the golden reflections. The archons keep gentle watch, for some souls linger too long, reluctant to leave the company of remembered loved ones and continue their climb.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Named Heroes"
          ]
        },
        "The Chapel of Torm": {
          "description": "A great chapel to Torm the True, god of duty and loyalty, standing at the midpoint of the Processional where the road turns toward the higher layers. Torm's petitioner-paladins keep the chapel and honor the loyal dead, and the god's presence lends the whole memorial road its steadfast, dutiful character. Champions of loyalty who died keeping faith with their lords and companions are honored here above all.",
          "paths": [
            "The Reflecting Road",
            "The Loyal Guard's Rest"
          ]
        },
        "The Loyal Guard's Rest": {
          "description": "A quiet garden behind Torm's chapel where the souls of bodyguards, shield-bearers, and loyal retainers who died protecting others find their peace. These are the faithful who gave their lives not for glory but for duty, and they are honored here with special tenderness. The garden's centerpiece is a statue of an unknown guard falling to shield an unseen charge, a monument to selfless loyalty.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chapel of Torm"
          ]
        },
        "The Upper Processional": {
          "description": "The steepening upper reach of the memorial road, where the tombs grow grander and the golden light brighter as the way climbs toward Venya. Here rest the mightiest of the martyred dead, and the road narrows so that only pilgrims who have truly honored the sacrifices below may pass with ease. The view back down the whole length of the Processional, glittering with a million points of memorial light, is one of the great sights of Mercuria.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chapel of Torm",
            "The Pearly Threshold"
          ]
        },
        "The Pearly Threshold": {
          "description": "The high boundary where Mercuria's golden light softens toward the pearly glow of Venya above, and the Processional passes from the second layer to the third. A pale arch marks the crossing, and warden archons of both layers keep a joint vigil. Pilgrims who cross it have absorbed the full weight of the martyred dead and carry that resolve upward into the layer of mercy and sacrifice beyond.",
          "paths": [
            "The Upper Processional"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Trumpet Archon rememberers",
        "Church of Torm (Processional chapter)",
        "Celestial sculptors' guild",
        "Deva keepers of the dead",
        "Pilgrim processions"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "1",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Vault of the Unremembered contains, among its nameless dead, one tomb that is deliberately kept nameless not because the hero's name was lost but because it must never be spoken: it holds a martyr whose sacrifice sealed a terrible entity away, and whose name is the key that would unseal it. The deva keeper who whispers the deeds of the nameless dead has been forbidden to ever speak this one, and guards the silence as fiercely as any archon guards a gate — for a fiend that learned the name could undo a victory bought at the highest price.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The House of the Broken God": {
      "name": "The House of the Broken God",
      "basicInfo": "The primary celestial realm of Ilmater, the Crying God of endurance, mercy, and the relief of suffering, standing amid the pearly terraces of Venya as a great hospice-temple of white and grey stone. It is called the House of the Broken God because Ilmater takes into himself the pain of all who suffer, and his form is forever marked by the torments he has borne on behalf of others. The House is the beating heart of mercy in the multiverse: a place where suffering is transformed into compassion, where the martyrs and healers of Ilmater's faith gather to take up the burdens of the wounded, and from which flows the endless charity that feeds and heals the pilgrims of the lower layers. Unlike the grand palaces of the warrior gods, the House of the Broken God is humble, its beauty found in the tenderness of its purpose rather than in gold or platinum. Here the willingly suffering learn that strength lies not in power but in the capacity to bear another's pain, and here the deepest wounds of the soul are gently, patiently healed. Ilmater himself walks these halls when he is present, and his mere passing eases the anguish of all he moves among.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 21,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Venya",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Weeping Gate": {
          "description": "The humble entrance to the House, a simple archway of grey stone from which a gentle mist of warm tears perpetually falls — the shared weeping of the Crying God for all who suffer. To pass beneath the gate is to feel one's own pain acknowledged and shared, and many souls weep here for the first time since their deaths, releasing griefs they had held tight for ages. The gate is unguarded, for Ilmater's House turns no one away, not even the cruel who come seeking to mock, whom it instead moves quietly toward mercy.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Shared Burdens",
            "The Court of the Suffering"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Shared Burdens": {
          "description": "The great central hall of the House, where the martyrs of Ilmater take up the pains of the wounded souls brought here, bearing a portion of each burden so that no one suffers alone. The hall is quiet and reverent, filled with the gentle murmur of comfort given and the soft glow of shared strength. Here a soul crushed by the weight of its mortal suffering may finally set that weight down, knowing that willing hands will help to carry it up the mountain.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weeping Gate",
            "The Altar of the Crying God",
            "The Infirmary of Souls"
          ]
        },
        "The Court of the Suffering": {
          "description": "An open courtyard where souls still bound by the traumas of their mortal lives — the tortured, the abused, the broken — are received with infinite gentleness and given the time and care they need to heal. The court is tended by the Sisters of the Pearly Terrace, healers of surpassing patience, and no soul is ever rushed here. Some remain for what feels like ages, slowly unknotting the pain of lifetimes, until at last they are whole enough to continue their climb.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weeping Gate",
            "The Garden of Slow Healing"
          ]
        },
        "The Altar of the Crying God": {
          "description": "The sacred heart of the House, a simple altar of grey stone worn smooth by the tears of the god himself, upon which Ilmater's presence rests most strongly. Pilgrims who kneel here feel the Crying God take a portion of their deepest anguish into himself, and rise lighter than they have felt since death. When Ilmater is present in the House, it is here that he most often stands, his broken form radiating a mercy so profound that even fiends have been known to weep before it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Shared Burdens",
            "The Martyr's Chapel"
          ]
        },
        "The Infirmary of Souls": {
          "description": "The great healing-ward of the House, where the deepest wounds of the spirit are tended by celestial healers and Ilmatari martyrs. Unlike the infirmary at Nemorga's Watch, which heals wounds of the body, this ward heals wounds of the soul — despair, self-hatred, guilt, and the scars of cruelty endured. The healing here is slow and gentle, for the wounds are deep, but it is sure, and no soul that enters the infirmary is ever beyond mending.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Shared Burdens",
            "The Ward of the Self-Condemned"
          ]
        },
        "The Martyr's Chapel": {
          "description": "A chapel honoring those who died taking on the suffering of others — the healers who caught the plague they cured, the protectors who bore the blow meant for another, the martyrs who endured torture to spare the innocent. Their sacrifice is held as the highest expression of Ilmater's creed, and their spirits are honored here with special reverence. Many of them chose to remain in the House rather than ascend, taking up eternal service in the Hall of Shared Burdens.",
          "paths": [
            "The Altar of the Crying God"
          ]
        },
        "The Ward of the Self-Condemned": {
          "description": "A tender, difficult ward for souls who cannot forgive themselves — those whose guilt over their mortal failings is so great that they refuse the healing offered them, believing they deserve to suffer. The healers here work with infinite patience to teach these souls that mercy is for them too, and that a good afterlife is not earned by endless self-punishment. It is among the hardest healing in the House, for the soul's own will resists it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Infirmary of Souls"
          ]
        },
        "The Garden of Slow Healing": {
          "description": "A terraced garden of pale flowers and warm, gentle snow where recovering souls walk in quiet contemplation as their wounds slowly close. The garden's snow never chills, falling soft and warm as blessing, and its flowers bloom in shades of pearl and silver. Souls tend the garden as part of their own healing, for the Sisters have long known that caring for growing things helps to mend a wounded spirit. It is a place of profound, unhurried peace.",
          "paths": [
            "The Court of the Suffering",
            "The Terrace of Returning Strength"
          ]
        },
        "The Terrace of Returning Strength": {
          "description": "The uppermost terrace of the House, where souls who have been healed of their suffering gather their renewed strength before returning to the ascent. Here they learn to carry their hard-won compassion forward, transformed by what they endured into souls capable of bearing others' burdens in turn. It is a hopeful place, full of souls on the verge of continuing their climb, and the Sisters send each one onward with a blessing and a portion of the House's endless mercy.",
          "paths": [
            "The Garden of Slow Healing"
          ]
        },
        "The Kitchens of Charity": {
          "description": "The vast kitchens from which the House feeds not only its own residents but the pilgrims of the layers below, preparing the harvest of Venya's terraces into food carried down to Mercuria and Lunia. The kitchens are a place of cheerful, tireless labor, staffed by petitioners who find joy in the humble work of feeding others. The bread baked here is said to nourish the spirit as well as the body, and no pilgrim on the whole mountain ever goes hungry because of the work done in these halls.",
          "paths": [
            "The Court of the Suffering"
          ]
        },
        "The Cloister of Willing Hands": {
          "description": "The dwelling of Ilmater's most devoted martyrs, who have chosen eternal service in the House over their own ascent, taking up the endless work of bearing others' burdens. Their cells are austere and their labor unending, yet they are among the most joyful souls in Celestia, for they have found the deepest meaning in service. The cloister is a place of quiet dedication, and to be admitted to it is considered one of the highest callings on the mountain.",
          "paths": [
            "The Martyr's Chapel"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
