      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "The great prism has begun, subtly, to refract the summit's light imperfectly — a new, dim band has appeared in its spectrum, a grey light that corresponds to no known virtue. The deva keepers are alarmed, for the prism has been flawless since the plane's creation. The grey band is in truth the shadow of the modron ideology of compelled order, which is beginning to distort the very light of the good as it spreads through Solania: a 'virtue' of pure lawful inevitability that has no place in a goodness founded on free choice. If the keepers cannot understand and cleanse this false light, it will spread through the prism and, from there, taint the scholars' entire understanding of what goodness is.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Court of the Unblinking Scale": {
      "name": "The Court of the Unblinking Scale",
      "basicInfo": "The supreme celestial court of Tyr the Even-Handed, god of justice, standing at the heart of Mertion as a vast tribunal of blinding platinum light where the moral balance of the entire multiverse is weighed. This is the highest court of justice in all the good planes, and at its center stands the Unblinking Scale — a genuine fragment of the primordial balance upon which all cosmic justice rests, so sacred and so perilous that Tyr himself sacrificed his hand and his eye to keep it true. Here the gravest questions of justice are adjudicated, here the deeds of gods and mortals alike are weighed against the eternal law, and here the trumpet archons hold their solemn court beneath a sky of hard, brilliant white. The light of the Court is so intense that no lie can survive within it; falsehoods turn to ash in the speaker's mouth, and every heart is laid bare before the Scale. It is a place of absolute justice, rigorous and unflinching, yet Tyr's justice is always tempered by the mercy he learned through his own suffering, and the Court is thus not merely a place of judgment but of justice made whole by compassion. To stand before the Unblinking Scale is to be judged truly, without deceit or evasion, by the highest standard of righteousness in the multiverse.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Mertion",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gate of Truth": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Court, a great arch of platinum through which all who would come before the Scale must pass. The gate's light burns away all deceit, so that none may enter the Court bearing a lie, and those who attempt to pass with falsehood in their hearts find their deceptions laid bare in a blaze of platinum radiance. Trumpet archon wardens greet each petitioner and confirm the nature of the justice they seek, for the Court hears only genuine questions of righteousness.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tribunal Floor",
            "The Hall of the Accused"
          ]
        },
        "The Tribunal Floor": {
          "description": "The vast central floor of the Court, where cases are heard and judgments rendered beneath the hard white sky. The floor is a marvel of platinum and white marble, and at its center stands the Unblinking Scale itself. The trumpet archon justices preside from a raised dais, and the accused, the accusers, and the witnesses take their places upon the floor to have their truths weighed. The whole space thrums with the weight of justice being done, and no falsehood can survive here.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Truth",
            "The Dais of the Unblinking Scale",
            "The Witness Stand of Bared Hearts"
          ]
        },
        "The Dais of the Unblinking Scale": {
          "description": "The sacred central dais bearing the Unblinking Scale, the fragment of primordial balance upon which all cosmic justice rests. The Scale is a thing of terrible purity, its two platinum pans held in perfect equilibrium, and it responds to the moral weight of every deed brought before it, tipping toward justice or injustice with absolute accuracy. Tyr himself tends the Scale when he presides, and it cannot be moved without unbalancing justice across every plane. It is among the most sacred and most guarded objects in the whole multiverse.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tribunal Floor",
            "The Sanctum of the Even Hand"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of the Accused": {
          "description": "A hall where those who are to be judged by the Court await their hearings, tended by advocate-archons who ensure that even the accused receive a fair defense. Tyr's justice demands that no one be condemned unheard, and so the accused are treated with dignity and given every opportunity to present their case. The hall is solemn but not cruel, for the Court seeks truth rather than vengeance, and even the guilty are judged with the mercy Tyr learned through his own suffering.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Truth",
            "The Advocates' Preparation"
          ]
        },
        "The Witness Stand of Bared Hearts": {
          "description": "The place where witnesses give their testimony before the Scale, their hearts laid bare by the Court's truth-light so that no false witness can deceive the tribunal. To stand here is to have one's true knowledge and true intentions revealed, and thus the testimony given is always true, whether the witness wills it or not. It is a place of profound exposure, and even the honest tremble to stand where every secret of the heart is revealed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tribunal Floor"
          ]
        },
        "The Sanctum of the Even Hand": {
          "description": "The private sanctum of Tyr the Even-Handed, adjoining the dais of the Scale, where the god of justice withdraws to contemplate the hardest cases and to tend the Scale that his sacrifice keeps true. The sanctum bears the marks of Tyr's suffering — his sacrificed hand and eye are commemorated here — and it radiates the hard-won wisdom of a god who learned mercy through his own pain. Only Tyr and his highest servants may enter, and the sanctum is the true heart of justice in the multiverse.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dais of the Unblinking Scale"
          ]
        },
        "The Advocates' Preparation": {
          "description": "A hall where the advocate-archons prepare the defenses of the accused, ensuring that every case brought before the Scale is argued as well as it can be. Tyr's justice requires that truth emerge through fair contest, and so the advocates labor to present every side of every case with full vigor. The hall rings with the careful construction of arguments, and its work ensures that no judgment is rendered without the fullest possible deliberation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of the Accused"
          ]
        },
        "The Gallery of Rendered Judgments": {
          "description": "An archive where the judgments of the Court are recorded and preserved, building the great body of celestial jurisprudence that guides justice throughout the good planes. Deva clerks maintain the records with exacting care, for the precedents set by the Court shape the administration of justice across the multiverse. The gallery is a monument to the consistency of true justice, that like cases are judged alike and that the wisdom of past judgments informs the present.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tribunal Floor"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of Tempered Verdicts": {
          "description": "A chamber where the Court deliberates on how justice may be tempered with mercy — where the letter of the law is weighed against the compassion that Tyr learned through his suffering. It is here that the Court's hardest work is done, for perfect justice and perfect mercy do not always agree, and the reconciliation of the two is the deepest challenge of Tyr's faith. The deliberations here embody the god's central teaching: that justice without mercy is cruelty, and mercy without justice is weakness.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sanctum of the Even Hand"
          ]
        },
        "The Vigil of the Scale-Wardens": {
          "description": "The watch-post of the sword archon wardens who guard the Unblinking Scale against any who would move, corrupt, or unbalance it. Their vigil is the most solemn duty in Mertion, for the Scale's perfect balance sustains justice across every plane, and its corruption would be a catastrophe beyond measure. The wardens watch the Scale unceasingly, and they have grown troubled of late, for they have sensed a subtle wrongness in the Court's own tribunal — a doubt spreading among the justices themselves.",
          "paths": [
            "The Dais of the Unblinking Scale"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of the Turned-Back": {
          "description": "A compassionate hall where souls judged not yet worthy to ascend further are gently informed and prepared to return down the mountain to grow and try again. Tyr's justice includes this mercy: that no soul is ever condemned forever, only asked to become more than it is. The advocates of the turned-back comfort these souls and set them on the path of return with encouragement rather than shame, for the Court's judgment is always aimed at the soul's ultimate perfection.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of the Accused"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Church of Tyr (the High Court of Justice)",
        "Trumpet Archon Tribunal",
        "Sword Archon Scale-Wardens",
        "Advocate-archons",
        "The Advocates of the Turned-Back"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "A faction of trumpet archon justices, wrestling with the hardest cases the Court has ever faced, has begun to doubt whether perfect justice can ever truly be reconciled with mercy — and their quiet schism is beginning to crack the Unblinking Scale from within, for the Scale reflects the moral certainty of those who tend it. Each doubt introduces a hairline flaw in the primordial balance. The fiends of Hell have learned of this schism and are subtly feeding it, arranging for impossible moral dilemmas to be brought before the Court, cases with no just and merciful answer, designed to deepen the justices' doubt until the Scale shatters and justice itself is unbalanced across every plane.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Proving Grounds of the Host": {
      "name": "The Proving Grounds of the Host",
      "basicInfo": "The great testing-grounds of Mertion, where every archon of the celestial host must prove the purity of its devotion before it may ascend to the higher layers of the mountain. The Proving Grounds are the crucible of the archon host, a vast complex of trials, tests, and challenges arranged across the platinum terraces, where the servants of the good are examined in every virtue they claim to embody. Here an archon's courage is tested against fear, its mercy against cruelty, its justice against temptation, and its devotion against despair, and only those who pass every trial with a pure heart are permitted to rise toward the glory of the higher layers. The trials are rigorous beyond mortal comprehension, for the standard of the good grows ever higher as one ascends the mountain, and an archon that would have been counted a paragon on a lower layer may find itself found wanting here. Yet the Proving Grounds are not cruel; their purpose is not to fail the unworthy but to perfect the worthy, to reveal and strengthen the virtues of those who serve the good. To pass the Proving Grounds is to be counted among the truly perfected servants of righteousness, and the archons who emerge from them carry themselves with the quiet certainty of those who have been tested to their very core and found true.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 39,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Mertion",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gate of Trials": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Proving Grounds, where archons and worthy souls present themselves to be tested and are assessed to determine which trials they must face. The gate is stern but fair, and the trumpet archon proctors who preside explain to each aspirant the nature of the proving that awaits. None may enter who are not genuinely ready to be tested, for the trials are too demanding to be undertaken lightly, and the proctors turn back gently those who are not yet prepared.",
          "paths": [
            "The Trial of Courage",
            "The Trial of Mercy",
            "The Aspirant's Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Trial of Courage": {
          "description": "A testing-ground where the courage of aspirants is proven against manifestations of their deepest fears, conjured by the platinum light to test whether their bravery is true. The trial is harrowing, for it confronts each aspirant with exactly what they most dread, and only those whose courage is genuine can face it unflinching. Those who pass emerge with their bravery tempered and confirmed; those who fail are shown mercy and given the chance to prepare and try again.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Trials",
            "The Trial of Justice"
          ]
        },
        "The Trial of Mercy": {
          "description": "A testing-ground where the mercy of aspirants is proven against the temptation to cruelty or vengeance, presenting them with those who have wronged them and testing whether they can choose compassion over retribution. The trial is subtle and difficult, for the desire for justice can easily curdle into the desire for revenge, and only those whose mercy is true can pass. Those who succeed have proven that their compassion extends even to those who least deserve it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Trials",
            "The Trial of Justice"
          ]
        },
        "The Trial of Justice": {
          "description": "A testing-ground where the justice of aspirants is proven against the temptation to partiality, presenting them with cases where the just verdict conflicts with their own interests or affections. The trial tests whether an aspirant can render true justice even when it costs them, even against those they love. Only those whose commitment to justice is absolute can pass, and those who do have proven themselves worthy to serve in the courts of Tyr himself.",
          "paths": [
            "The Trial of Courage",
            "The Trial of Mercy",
            "The Trial of Devotion"
          ]
        },
        "The Trial of Devotion": {
          "description": "The final and hardest trial, where the aspirant's devotion to the good is proven against despair, isolation, and the temptation to abandon the endless struggle. The trial confronts each aspirant with the apparent futility of the eternal war, the seeming endlessness of evil, and the cost of their service, and tests whether their devotion to the good can endure even without hope of final victory. Those who pass have proven that they serve the good not for reward but because it is good, and they are counted among the most perfected servants of righteousness.",
          "paths": [
            "The Trial of Justice",
            "The Hall of the Proven"
          ]
        },
        "The Aspirant's Hall": {
          "description": "A hall where archons and souls preparing for the trials gather to ready themselves, to study the virtues they will be tested in, and to support one another through the demanding process of proving. The hall rings with the earnest preparation of those about to face the crucible, and experienced archons who have passed the trials often return here to counsel and encourage the aspirants. It is a place of solidarity in the face of a demanding ordeal.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Trials"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of the Proven": {
          "description": "The hall where archons who have passed all the trials are honored and prepared to ascend toward the higher layers of the mountain. To reach this hall is to have been tested to one's very core and found true, and the archons who gather here carry themselves with the quiet certainty of the truly perfected. From here they ascend toward the glory of Jovar and the trials that await even beyond, ever climbing toward the ultimate good.",
          "paths": [
            "The Trial of Devotion",
            "The Ascent of the Perfected"
          ]
        },
        "The Proctor's Sanctum": {
          "description": "The dwelling of the trumpet archon proctors who design and administer the trials, ensuring that each is rigorous yet fair, demanding yet merciful. The proctors are masters of the virtues they test, and they take great care that the trials perfect rather than merely challenge the aspirants. Their sanctum is a place of deep wisdom about the nature of virtue and the process of spiritual perfection, and their judgments about who has truly passed are trusted absolutely.",
          "paths": [
            "The Aspirant's Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Grove of Tested Souls": {
          "description": "A garden where aspirants rest and reflect between their trials, tending luminous plants as a meditation on the virtues they are proving. The grove offers respite from the demanding trials, a place to gather one's strength and contemplate the lessons of each ordeal. Aspirants often form deep bonds here, for the shared experience of the trials creates a fellowship among those who face the crucible together.",
          "paths": [
            "The Aspirant's Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Honorable Failure": {
          "description": "A compassionate hall for those who have failed a trial, where they are shown that failure is not disgrace but merely a sign that they are not yet ready, and are encouraged to prepare and try again. Tyr's justice includes this mercy: that no aspirant is condemned for falling short, only asked to grow. The counselors here help each failed aspirant understand what they lacked and how they may perfect it, so that their next attempt may succeed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Aspirant's Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Ascent of the Perfected": {
          "description": "The path by which archons who have passed all the trials leave the Proving Grounds to ascend toward the higher layers, their devotion proven and their virtues confirmed. The ascent is lined with platinum banners honoring the proven, and each archon who walks it does so with the quiet pride of having been tested to the utmost and found true. The path leads upward toward Jovar, the glorious sixth layer, and the even greater perfection that awaits beyond.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of the Proven"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Trumpet Archon proctors",
        "The Aspirant archons",
        "The Proven Host",
        "Church of Tyr (the trials)",
        "Counselors of Honorable Failure"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Trial of Devotion has grown subtly harder in recent ages, and more aspirants fail it than ever before — not because the archons are less devoted, but because the trial draws on the moral certainty of the Court of the Unblinking Scale, and the schism among the justices there has infected the trial with doubt. Aspirants facing the Trial of Devotion now confront not only the ordinary temptation to despair but the justices' own creeping question of whether the good can truly be reconciled with mercy, a doubt no aspirant should have to bear. The proctors have noticed the rising failure rate but have not traced its source to the cracking Scale.",
      "known": true
    },
    "The Terrace of Weighed Souls": {
      "name": "The Terrace of Weighed Souls",
      "basicInfo": "The great terrace of Mertion where the souls ascending the mountain are weighed against the standard of justice before they may continue toward the higher layers, and where the moral progress of each pilgrim is measured against the ever-rising standard of the good. The Terrace of Weighed Souls is where the ascent grows truly demanding, for here each soul must prove that it has grown enough in righteousness to bear the harder, purer light of the layers above. The weighing is not a punishment but a measurement, an honest assessment of how far each soul has come and how far it has yet to go, conducted with rigorous fairness and genuine compassion. Souls found ready ascend with joy toward Jovar and the glory beyond; souls found wanting are gently turned back to grow further, without shame or condemnation. The Terrace is a place of profound moral seriousness, where the pretenses and self-deceptions that a soul may have carried up the mountain are finally stripped away, and each soul sees itself weighed truly for the first time. It is a hard mercy, but a necessary one, for the higher layers demand a purity that can only be attained through honest self-knowledge and continued growth.",
      "x": -5,
      "y": -43,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Mertion",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Approach of Self-Knowledge": {
          "description": "The path leading up to the Terrace, along which ascending souls are gently prepared for the weighing that awaits by being encouraged to examine themselves honestly. Deva counselors walk with each soul, helping them face the truth of their own moral state before they are weighed, so that the weighing brings no unfair surprise. The approach is a place of honest self-examination, and many souls find that the hardest part of the weighing is the self-knowledge it demands even before they reach the Scale.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weighing Terrace",
            "The Hall of Honest Reflection"
          ]
        },
        "The Weighing Terrace": {
          "description": "The great open terrace where souls are weighed against the standard of justice, their moral progress measured by lesser scales attuned to the Unblinking Scale itself. Each soul stands upon the weighing-place and feels its own righteousness measured against the ever-rising standard of the good, an experience of profound honesty. The trumpet archon weighers preside with rigorous fairness and genuine compassion, and the terrace is heavy with the moral seriousness of the judgment enacted here.",
          "paths": [
            "The Approach of Self-Knowledge",
            "The Ascent of the Worthy",
            "The Path of Return"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Honest Reflection": {
          "description": "A hall where souls preparing to be weighed contemplate their own moral state in mirrors of platinum that reflect not their faces but their true righteousness. The mirrors reveal each soul's virtues and flaws with perfect honesty, and the reflection can be humbling, for few souls are as good as they believe themselves to be. The hall's purpose is to prepare souls for the weighing through honest self-knowledge, and the counselors here help each soul face what the mirrors reveal.",
          "paths": [
            "The Approach of Self-Knowledge"
          ]
        },
        "The Ascent of the Worthy": {
          "description": "The path by which souls found ready in the weighing ascend toward Jovar and the higher layers, their righteousness proven sufficient to bear the purer light above. The ascent is a joyful path, lined with platinum banners honoring those who have grown enough to continue, and each soul who walks it does so with the deep satisfaction of genuine moral progress. The path leads upward toward the glory of the sixth layer and the ultimate good beyond.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weighing Terrace"
          ]
        },
        "The Path of Return": {
          "description": "The compassionate path by which souls found not yet ready are gently guided back down the mountain to grow further before attempting the weighing again. The path is not a road of shame but of encouragement, and the counselors who walk it with the turned-back souls help them understand what they lack and how they may attain it. Tyr's justice includes this mercy: that no soul is ever condemned for falling short, only asked to become more than it is.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weighing Terrace",
            "The Garden of Patient Growth"
          ]
        },
        "The Weigher's Sanctum": {
          "description": "The dwelling of the trumpet archon weighers who conduct the measurement of souls, masters of the fair and compassionate assessment of moral progress. The weighers take their solemn duty with great seriousness, for the weighing shapes each soul's journey up the mountain, and they strive always to measure truly, neither too harshly nor too leniently. Their sanctum is a place of deep wisdom about the nature of moral growth and the honest assessment of the soul.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weighing Terrace"
          ]
        },
        "The Garden of Patient Growth": {
          "description": "A garden where souls turned back from the weighing may rest and reflect before beginning their descent to grow further, tending plants that grow slowly toward the light as a meditation on their own gradual moral progress. The garden teaches patience and hope, reminding the turned-back souls that growth takes time and that their eventual ascent is assured if they persevere. It is a place of gentle encouragement, softening the disappointment of a weighing not yet passed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Path of Return"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Recorded Progress": {
          "description": "An archive where the moral progress of each ascending soul is recorded across its many weighings, so that its growth over the ages may be tracked and honored. The records show the long, patient journey of each soul toward righteousness, and they are a source of encouragement, for they reveal how far even the most struggling soul has come. Deva clerks maintain the records with care, celebrating each soul's progress as it is measured anew.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weigher's Sanctum"
          ]
        },
        "The Chamber of Impossible Weighings": {
          "description": "A chamber set aside for the hardest cases — souls whose moral state is so complex or so ambiguous that ordinary weighing cannot measure them, and who require the deepest deliberation of the weighers. These are souls torn between great virtues and great flaws, or shaped by dilemmas with no clear right answer, and their weighing demands the finest moral judgment. The chamber has grown busier of late, for the same doubt that cracks the Unblinking Scale makes even ordinary weighings harder to resolve.",
          "paths": [
            "The Weigher's Sanctum"
          ]
        },
        "The Overlook of the Higher Layers": {
          "description": "A high vantage at the edge of the Terrace from which souls may glimpse the glory of Jovar and the higher layers they aspire to reach, a sight that inspires and encourages them in their striving toward the good. The overlook offers a breathtaking view of the platinum light giving way to the ruby radiance of Jovar above, a glimpse of the reward that awaits those who grow enough to ascend. Souls come here to renew their resolve and remember what they climb toward.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ascent of the Worthy"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
