    "The Spawning Stone": {
      "name": "The Spawning Stone",
      "basicInfo": "The pulsating heart of Limbo, a massive, irregular obelisk of shifting, iridescent matter that drifts through the chaotic currents. It is from this Stone that all slaadi are born, spawning fully formed from its surface in a shower of chaotic energy. The Stone is not fixed; it moves erratically, but its location can be sensed by the slaadi and by those attuned to chaos. Surrounding it is a region of intense elemental instability, where fire and water, earth and air collide and transform. The Stone is a place of profound danger and raw creative power, and it is the focus of numerous slaadi rituals and anarchic cults. The ground around it shimmers and shifts, forming temporary landscapes that last only minutes. Approaching it requires constant mental focus to maintain one's own form, and even then, exposure to its energies can mutate the unprepared.",
      "x": -10,
      "y": 6,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Limbo",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Stone's Heart": {
          "description": "The central chamber within the Spawning Stone, accessible only through a cleft that opens and closes with chaotic rhythm. Inside, the walls are a swirling vortex of pure, undiluted chaos, and the air vibrates with a maddening hum. A pool of shimmering protomatter bubbles at the center, from which slaadi emerge in a violent, messy birth. The pool is the true source of the slaad race, and any creature that falls into it is transformed into a slaad of random color within seconds. The chamber is guarded by a permanent chaos elemental that attacks any non-slaad intruder.",
          "paths": [
            "The Whispering Perimeter",
            "The Slaad Birthing Pools"
          ]
        },
        "The Slaad Birthing Pools": {
          "description": "A series of small, shallow pools of liquid chaos that ring the Stone's base, where newly spawned slaadi gather to absorb chaotic energy and mature. The pools are constantly bubbling, and the surface shows fleeting images of other planes. Slaadi of all colors can be found here, from blue strategists to red berserkers, engaging in brutal social interactions. A particularly ancient death slaad, named Xor'zyth, serves as an informal guardian, demanding tribute from any who approach the Stone.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stone's Heart",
            "The Whispering Perimeter"
          ]
        },
        "The Whispering Perimeter": {
          "description": "A zone of shimmering, semi-solid ground surrounding the Spawning Stone, where the air is filled with whispers of impossible truths and the ground occasionally forms into faces that scream or laugh. It is here that the Anarch cultists often gather, performing rituals that draw power from the Stone's energy. The perimeter is unstable; one moment it is solid rock, the next a sea of acid. Navigation requires constant attunement to the shifting patterns. A hidden path, the 'Path of the Unmade', leads to the Stone's Heart and is known only to the Unmakers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Stone's Heart",
            "The Slaad Birthing Pools"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Slaadi Broods (all colors)",
        "The Unmakers (chaos cult)",
        "Xor'zyth (Death slaad guardian)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Spawning Stone is not merely a source of slaadi; it is a prison for the 'First Slaad', an entity of pure chaos that would consume all order if released. The First Slaad is trapped within the protomatter pool, and the slaadi are its fragmented offspring. The Unmakers mistakenly believe destroying the Stone will free true chaos, but it will actually release the First Slaad, which would unmake even them."
    },
    "Shra'kt'lor": {
      "name": "Shra'kt'lor",
      "basicInfo": "The greatest of the githzerai monasteries in Limbo, a fortress of pure mental discipline built upon a floating island of solidified chaos. Shra'kt'lor is a place of austere beauty: its buildings are formed from psionically shaped stone and crystal, their edges sharp and their lines precise. The monastery is a sanctuary of order within the storm, maintained by the collective will of its inhabitants. Here, githzerai monks train in martial arts, psionics, and the philosophy of Zerthimon. The monastery is ruled by the Great Githzerai Council, a council of elders who guide the community. Visitors are rare and must prove their mental stability to enter. The air is calm, and the constant roar of Limbo's chaos is muted to a distant hum. The monastery also serves as a prison for captured slaadi, held in cells that nullify their chaotic powers.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 11,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Limbo",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gate of Ordered Mind": {
          "description": "The single entrance to Shra'kt'lor, a tall archway of white crystal that glows faintly. To pass, one must clear their mind of all extraneous thought and maintain perfect focus while stepping through; any lapse results in being shunted back into the chaos outside. The gate is guarded by two githzerai monks in simple grey robes, their faces serene but their eyes alert. Beyond lies the main courtyard, a stark plaza of polished stone where students practice kata in silence.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Swords",
            "The Meditation Gardens",
            "The Anarch Holding Cells"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Swords": {
          "description": "A training hall where githzerai warriors practice the art of the silver sword, a weapon that draws on the wielder's mental discipline. The hall is lined with weapons racks holding practice blades and a few true silver swords used by masters. The floor is marked with concentric circles for dueling. The current swordmaster, a scarred but calm githzerai named Rhek, oversees the training and will challenge any visitor who claims martial skill. Defeating him earns respect and access to the monastery's deeper secrets.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Ordered Mind",
            "The Meditation Gardens"
          ]
        },
        "The Meditation Gardens": {
          "description": "A tranquil courtyard at the center of the monastery, filled with carefully raked sand, bonsai-like crystals, and small pools of still water. Here, monks meditate for hours, using their psionic powers to subtly shape the garden's patterns. A single, ancient tree grows at the center, its branches swaying without wind. The tree is said to be a gift from the githzerai hero Dak'kon, and meditating beneath it grants a vision of Zerthimon's teachings. The air is filled with a profound, peaceful silence.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Ordered Mind",
            "The Hall of Swords",
            "The Great Council Chamber"
          ]
        },
        "The Great Council Chamber": {
          "description": "The seat of the Great Githzerai Council, a circular room of white stone at the highest point of the monastery. The council consists of seven elders, each a master of one of the Seven Disciplines. They debate matters of policy and philosophy, and their decisions are final. A large, ever-changing map of Limbo floats in the center, showing the current positions of major chaos storms and slaadi broods. Visitors are rarely admitted, and only after proving their worth to the entire council.",
          "paths": [
            "The Meditation Gardens"
          ]
        },
        "The Anarch Holding Cells": {
          "description": "A block of featureless, white cells below the main monastery, where captured slaadi and other chaotic threats are held. The cells are reinforced with psionic nullifiers that suppress chaotic mutation and teleportation. The prisoners are not tortured; they are studied and, in some cases, offered the chance to renounce chaos and join the monastery as converts (a rare occurrence). The chief jailer, a stony-faced githzerai named Vharr, maintains absolute control over the block.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Ordered Mind"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Great Githzerai Council",
        "Rhek (Swordmaster)",
        "Vharr (Jailer)",
        "Githzerai monks and students"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "One of the prisoners in the Anarch Holding Cells is not a slaad but a renegade githyanki who has information about Vlaakith's soul consumption rituals. He was captured while fleeing Tu'narath and seeks asylum in Shra'kt'lor. The council is divided on whether to trust him, and his presence threatens to draw the githyanki's wrath down on the monastery. A faction of elders, led by the Master of the Third Discipline, believes his knowledge is vital to uniting the gith people."
    },
    "Zerth'Ad'Lun": {
      "name": "Zerth'Ad'Lun",
      "basicInfo": "A second, smaller githzerai monastery floating in Limbo, often called the 'Monastery of the Riddle'. Unlike Shra'kt'lor, which is a fortress of martial discipline, Zerth'Ad'Lun is a center of psionic philosophy, prophecy, and arcane study. Its buildings are made of translucent crystal that refracts the chaotic light of Limbo into calming, geometric patterns. The monastery is less accessible, as its location shifts according to an esoteric pattern known only to its inhabitants. It is ruled by a single master, the 'Keeper of the Riddle', a githzerai who has achieved perfect mental clarity and can see possible futures. Here, students learn to shape chaos not just into order, but into new forms of possibility. The monastery also houses a library of chaos lore and a sanctuary for those seeking to transcend the cycle of incarnation.",
      "x": -16,
      "y": -8,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Limbo",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Floating Causeway": {
          "description": "A bridge of solidified thought that extends from the chaos to the monastery's entrance. The causeway is not physical; it exists only as long as a creature maintains the mental image of its form. Those who doubt its reality will fall through into the chaos below. It is guarded by a silent githzerai sentinel who tests the mental stability of all who approach. The bridge offers a breathtaking view of Limbo's ever-shifting storm.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tower of the Riddle",
            "The Library of Chaos"
          ]
        },
        "The Tower of the Riddle": {
          "description": "The highest spire of the monastery, where the Keeper of the Riddle resides. The tower's walls are covered in shifting, glowing runes that pose philosophical questions to all who read them. The Keeper, an ancient githzerai with eyes like still pools, speaks only in riddles and parables. He can answer questions about the future, but each answer comes with a condition: the asker must solve a riddle of their own soul. A permanent scrying pool shows the probability currents of Limbo.",
          "paths": [
            "The Floating Causeway",
            "The Sanctuary of the One"
          ]
        },
        "The Library of Chaos": {
          "description": "A repository of knowledge about the nature of chaos, the history of the githzerai, and the prophecies of Zerthimon. The books are not paper but thought-forms; a reader must project their mind into a crystal to absorb the information. The library is tended by a group of blind monks who can navigate the crystal shelves by touch. A hidden vault contains the 'Unwritten Prophecy', a prophecy that has not yet been fulfilled and is therefore dangerous to know.",
          "paths": [
            "The Floating Causeway",
            "The Sanctuary of the One"
          ]
        },
        "The Sanctuary of the One": {
          "description": "A small, circular chamber at the heart of the monastery, where a single, perfectly still point of white light hovers. This is the 'One', a manifestation of the ideal of unity between law and chaos. Meditating before the One allows a creature to resolve inner conflicts and find peace. The chamber is open to all, but prolonged exposure can dissolve a person's sense of self, merging them with the One. A single githzerai monk, the 'Guardian of Silence', ensures that no one stays too long.",
          "paths": [
            "The Tower of the Riddle",
            "The Library of Chaos"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Keeper of the Riddle",
        "Blind monks of the Library",
        "Guardian of Silence"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Unwritten Prophecy states that a being born of both githyanki and githzerai will unite the gith people and free them from both Vlaakith and the chaos of Limbo. The Keeper of the Riddle knows this and has foreseen that such a child has already been born, hidden in the Material Plane. He is actively searching for the child, but must do so secretly, as both Vlaakith's spies and the Slaadi Lords would seek to destroy it."
    },
    "The Chaos Wastes": {
      "name": "The Chaos Wastes",
      "basicInfo": "The vast, ungoverned expanse of Limbo, a region of pure, unstructured creation where elemental matter, psychic static, and raw possibility collide. The Wastes have no permanent landmarks; landscapes form and dissolve in moments, often reflecting the subconscious fears or desires of those who traverse them. Slaadi hunt here in packs, elementals rage in storm-like swarms, and the insane or desperate find a twisted freedom. Navigation is possible only through constant mental focus, and even that can be undone by a sudden surge of chaos. The Wastes are also the breeding ground for new forms of life, some beautiful, many monstrous. It is a place of ultimate potential, where a single strong thought can create a temporary paradise or a permanent prison.",
      "x": -6,
      "y": -6,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Limbo",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Screaming Vortex": {
          "description": "A massive, swirling storm of elemental energy and psychic noise, where the winds howl with the screams of a million lost thoughts. The vortex is a natural (if such a word applies) phenomenon, and it wanders the Wastes, consuming any structure or creature too weak to resist. Within the vortex, time and space are twisted, and travelers may find themselves caught in loops or thrown into random parts of the plane. A few hidden caves on the vortex's edge offer temporary shelter, carved by desperate githzerai or mad wizards.",
          "paths": [
            "The Elemental Confluence",
            "The Mirage Fields"
          ]
        },
        "The Elemental Confluence": {
          "description": "A region where pockets of pure elemental matter collide and merge, creating hybrid landscapes: seas of fire that turn to ice, mountains of crystal that melt into rivers of lightning. The confluence is a rich source of elemental artifacts, but harvesting them is risky, as the terrain can shift instantly. Elemental creatures of mixed ancestry (fire-water, earth-air) are born here and often attack intruders. A hidden, stable platform of translucent stone, the 'Isle of Four Tongues', allows communication with the elemental lords, who trade in raw elemental power for stories of the ordered planes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Screaming Vortex",
            "The Mirage Fields",
            "The Slaadi Hunting Grounds"
          ]
        },
        "The Mirage Fields": {
          "description": "A deceptive area where the chaos manifests as vivid, almost real illusions drawn from the traveler's memories. Entire villages, loved ones, or past battles appear, only to dissolve when touched. The fields are a trap for the unwary, as many become lost in their own reveries and are consumed by the chaos. A small group of 'Dreamweavers', chaotic neutral psionicists, have learned to navigate the fields and will guide travelers for a price: a single, precious memory to add to their collection.",
          "paths": [
            "The Screaming Vortex",
            "The Elemental Confluence"
          ]
        },
        "The Slaadi Hunting Grounds": {
          "description": "A section of the Wastes where slaadi broods gather to hunt. The terrain here is constantly in flux, but the slaadi thrive, their natural chaos attunement allowing them to navigate instinctively. Packs of red and blue slaadi coordinate ambushes, while green slaadi use illusion to lure prey. A large, white slaad named Ygorl's Chosen acts as a sort of chieftain, ruling through raw power. The ground is littered with the petrified remains of failed hunters, a warning to the arrogant.",
          "paths": [
            "The Elemental Confluence"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Slaadi Broods",
        "Elemental Lords",
        "Dreamweavers",
        "Ygorl's Chosen (white slaad)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Isle of Four Tongues is not merely a trading post; it is a convergence point where the four elemental lords can be contacted simultaneously. If one could gain the four lords' cooperation, they could theoretically impose a temporary, localized order on a region of Limbo, creating a safe haven or a fortress of pure elemental power. However, the lords are notoriously fickle and demand equally chaotic prices."
    },
    "The Anarchic Gate": {
      "name": "The Anarchic Gate",
      "basicInfo": "A semi-stable region of Limbo that serves as a crossroads for travel to other chaotic planes and the Material Plane. The Gate is not a constructed portal but a natural confluence of chaotic energy that occasionally coalesces into temporary rifts. These rifts open to random locations in the planes of chaos, including the Abyss, Ysgard, Pandemonium, and sometimes the Material Plane. The area is a gathering place for planar travelers, chaotic cultists, and opportunistic merchants. The ground here is slightly more stable than the rest of Limbo, as the constant focus of travelers has imposed a rough semblance of order. A few permanent structures exist, formed by the will of their inhabitants, including a tavern run by a retired githzerai. The Gate is also the primary hunting ground for the 'Unmakers', who seek to shatter the Spawning Stone and release true chaos.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": -20,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Limbo",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Portal Nexus": {
          "description": "A large, open area where chaotic rifts flicker in and out of existence, each showing a different destination. The rifts are unstable, and attempting to pass through one without focusing on a destination can result in being scattered across the planes. A small guild of 'Rift Guides' sells their services, using psionic navigation to help travelers reach their intended planes. The nexus is patrolled by a chaotic neutral genie named Zephyros, who maintains a rough peace and collects a toll in stories or magic.",
          "paths": [
            "The Warped Shrine",
            "The Gatekeeper's Isle"
          ]
        },
        "The Warped Shrine": {
          "description": "A twisted, half-collapsed temple built by an unknown cult, dedicated to the concept of pure chaos. The walls are carved with shifting, non-Euclidean symbols that cause headaches in lawful creatures. A permanent altar of swirling protomatter allows a worshipper to receive a vision of the Far Realm, but at the cost of a piece of their sanity. The shrine is a favorite meeting place for the Unmakers, who use it to perform rituals that weaken the boundaries of reality. A hidden trapdoor leads to a cavern where a imprisoned marilith, bound by chaos energy, offers dark pacts.",
          "paths": [
            "The Portal Nexus",
            "The Echoing Corridor"
          ]
        },
        "The Gatekeeper's Isle": {
          "description": "A small, floating island of solidified chaos at the edge of the Gate, where the genie Zephyros resides in a palace of solidified cloud. The isle is a haven for planar travelers, offering food, drink, and a place to rest. Zephyros is a genie of neutral disposition, fascinated by the stories of other planes. He can provide maps of the current rift patterns and will trade passage to a specific plane for a unique magical item. His palace also contains a scrying pool that shows random locations in Limbo.",
          "paths": [
            "The Portal Nexus"
          ]
        },
        "The Echoing Corridor": {
          "description": "A long, narrow tunnel of solidified sound, where every word spoken echoes and re-echoes, sometimes with the voice of the original speaker, sometimes with a distorted version. The corridor connects the Portal Nexus to the Warped Shrine, but it is also a place of testing: those who can walk its length without speaking a falsehood emerge with a temporary blessing of truth-telling. The walls are covered in the etched words of past travelers, some prophetic, some mad. A ghostly choir of lost voices sings intermittently.",
          "paths": [
            "The Warped Shrine",
            "The Portal Nexus"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Rift Guides (psionic navigators)",
        "Zephyros (Genie gatekeeper)",
        "The Unmakers (chaos cult)",
        "Marilith prisoner"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Zephyros is not merely a neutral gatekeeper; he is a spy for the Wind Dukes of Aaqa, the ancient enemies of chaos. He observes the Unmakers and reports their activities, hoping to prevent the release of the First Slaad. His palace contains a hidden archive of the Unmakers' plans. He will only reveal this to a traveler who has proven their dedication to order and good, and who can help him thwart the cult's next move."
    },
    "The Howling Halls": {
      "name": "The Howling Halls",
      "basicInfo": "A series of vast, interconnected caverns in Pandesmos where the wind is so strong that it creates a permanent, deafening roar. The Halls are a natural amphitheater of madness, where the insane gather to scream their anguish into the gale. The walls are covered in wind-carved spirals and the scratchings of the deranged. At the center, a massive, vertical shaft, the 'Throat of the World', channels the wind upward, creating a vortex that can lift a creature and dash it against the ceiling. The Halls are also home to a tribe of howlers led by an ancient, intelligent alpha named Shriek. They hunt in coordinated packs, using the wind to mask their approach. Those who survive the Halls emerge with their hearing permanently damaged and their minds frayed.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Pandesmos",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Wind-Carved Gallery": {
          "description": "A long, twisting corridor where the walls are polished to a mirror finish by the wind. The surface reflects distorted, screaming faces—images of past victims imprinted by the constant erosion. The wind here is not as strong, but the noise is still overwhelming. Small alcoves offer brief respite. A single, ancient statue of a forgotten god of wind stands at the entrance, its features worn smooth.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throat of the World",
            "The Howler Den"
          ]
        },
        "The Throat of the World": {
          "description": "A massive, circular shaft that rises hundreds of feet, its walls lined with ledges and tunnels. The wind howls upward with hurricane force, and loose stones are carried aloft. At the bottom, a pit of swirling debris and bones forms a grisly whirlpool. The Throat is a place of pilgrimage for the mad, who climb to its ledges to scream their confessions into the gale. A hidden tunnel at the base leads to the lower layers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wind-Carved Gallery",
            "The Howler Den"
          ]
        },
        "The Howler Den": {
          "description": "A side cavern where the howler pack makes its lair. The floor is covered in the remains of their prey, and the walls are marked with territorial scratchings. The alpha, Shriek, is a massive howler with three heads, each capable of a different sonic attack. The pack is loyal but vicious, and they will negotiate only with those who speak their language—a series of ululating howls. A small, wind-sheltered alcove contains the pack's treasure: the gear of past victims.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wind-Carved Gallery",
            "The Throat of the World"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Howler pack (Shriek, the three-headed alpha)",
        "Mad pilgrims"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Throat of the World is not merely a wind shaft; it is a resonance chamber for the primordial wind entity's voice. If someone were to sing the exact inverse frequency of the howl, the wind would momentarily reverse, exposing a hidden entrance to the entity's prison. Shriek knows this and guards the secret, as the entity's release would destroy the howlers' hunting grounds."
    },
    "The Madhouse": {
      "name": "The Madhouse",
      "basicInfo": "The fortress of Diinkarazan, the derro god of madness, located in a remote corner of Pandesmos. The Madhouse is a rambling, nonsensical structure of black stone and twisted iron, its corridors designed to confuse and disorient. The walls are covered in gibbering faces that whisper secrets and lies. Within its halls, the derro conduct experiments on captured creatures, seeking to understand the nature of insanity and to spread it across the planes. The fortress is ruled by the High Priest of Diinkarazan, a bloated derro named Vulgath, who communicates only in shrieks and laughter. The Madhouse is also a prison for those who have angered the derro, kept in cells that amplify their fears. It is a place of pure, unrelenting madness.",
      "x": 11,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Pandesmos",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gibbering Gate": {
          "description": "The only entrance to the Madhouse, a massive iron gate shaped like a screaming mouth. To open, one must answer a riddle posed by the gate's guardian, a derro whose face has been stitched into a permanent grin. The riddle is impossible to answer correctly, as the 'correct' answer changes each time. Those who fail are subjected to a blast of psychic noise that leaves them catatonic. The gate opens onto a corridor that slopes downward into the fortress.",
          "paths": [
            "The Experiment Halls",
            "The Screaming Cells"
          ]
        },
        "The Experiment Halls": {
          "description": "A series of large, circular rooms where the derro conduct their experiments. The air is thick with the smell of chemicals and fear. Captured creatures—humans, elves, even lesser devils—are strapped to tables and subjected to devices that amplify their worst fears. The results are often fatal, but some subjects are transformed into 'Screaming Ones', deranged servants who obey the derro. The head experimenter, a derro savant named Blix, cackles as he works.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gibbering Gate",
            "The High Priest's Sanctum",
            "The Screaming Cells"
          ]
        },
        "The High Priest's Sanctum": {
          "description": "The private chambers of Vulgath, located at the deepest point of the Madhouse. The room is a chaotic jumble of broken mirrors, scattered bones, and piles of torn tapestries. Vulgath sits on a throne of fused skulls, his body twitching with involuntary spasms. He speaks in a stream of gibberish that nonetheless conveys meaning to those attuned to madness. A hidden door behind the throne leads to a secret escape tunnel.",
          "paths": [
            "The Experiment Halls"
          ]
        },
        "The Screaming Cells": {
          "description": "A block of cells where the derro keep their captives. Each cell is soundproofed from the outside, but inside, the walls emit a constant, low hum that induces paranoia and despair. Prisoners are left alone for days, then dragged to the Experiment Halls. A single cell, the 'Quiet Cell', has been enchanted to produce absolute silence, and its occupant, a githzerai monk, has survived by meditating for decades.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gibbering Gate",
            "The Experiment Halls"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Vulgath (High Priest of Diinkarazan)",
        "Derro experimenters",
        "Screaming Ones (transformed servants)",
        "Githzerai prisoner"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The githzerai monk in the Quiet Cell is not merely surviving; he has achieved a state of perfect mental stillness that allows him to project his consciousness beyond the Madhouse. He is secretly a spy for the githzerai of Shra'kt'lor, gathering intelligence on the derro's experiments. He will offer his knowledge in exchange for rescue, but his rescue would alert the derro to the githzerai's interest in Pandemonium."
    },
    "The Gate of Winds": {
      "name": "The Gate of Winds",
      "basicInfo": "A natural nexus in Pandesmos where the wind originates, a cavern of swirling air currents and blinding dust. The Gate is not a constructed portal but a permanent, roaring vortex that draws air from across the plane and expels it with hurricane force. It is here that the wind is born, or so the mad believe. The cavern floor is worn into deep channels by the constant blast, and the walls are lined with the petrified remains of those who were caught in the vortex. A small, unstable platform of rock at the edge, the 'Ledge of the Windborn', offers a place where one can feel the full force of the gale. The Gate is a site of pilgrimage for the Wind Dukes of Aaqa, who leave offerings to the imprisoned entity. It is also a place of execution for the worst criminals, who are thrown into the vortex and torn apart.",
      "x": -11,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Pandesmos",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Ledge of the Windborn": {
          "description": "A narrow shelf of rock that juts into the vortex, where the wind is strongest. Standing here, one can feel the raw power of the plane. The Wind Dukes leave small, polished stones on the ledge as offerings. A single, ancient flagstone is inscribed with a warning in Auran: 'All air returns to the First Breath.' The ledge is often used by mad cultists to achieve a state of 'wind-born ecstasy' by exposing themselves to the gale.",
          "paths": [
            "The Vortex Core",
            "The Offering Cairn"
          ]
        },
        "The Vortex Core": {
          "description": "The very heart of the Gate, a spinning column of air and debris that rises into the darkness. The wind here is so strong that it can strip the flesh from bone in seconds. At the core's center, a single point of absolute calm exists—the 'Eye of the Storm'. Reaching it requires perfect timing and a strong will. Within the Eye, a creature can hear the thoughts of the imprisoned wind entity, a maddening babble of ancient secrets.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ledge of the Windborn"
          ]
        },
        "The Offering Cairn": {
          "description": "A pile of smooth stones and broken weapons at the base of the Ledge, where the Wind Dukes and others leave tributes to the wind. The cairn is periodically swept away by the gale, and the offerings are carried across the plane. A hidden compartment within the cairn contains a small, wind-etched tablet that records the true name of the primordial entity—a secret that could be used to command or free it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ledge of the Windborn"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Wind Dukes of Aaqa (offerers)",
        "Mad wind cultists",
        "The imprisoned wind entity"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The tablet in the Offering Cairn is not the true name, but a fragment. The full name is divided into four parts, one hidden on each layer of Pandemonium. The Wind Dukes know this and seek the fragments to prevent anyone from freeing the entity. They have placed the tablet here as a test: only those who can survive the wind's full force and show no fear may find it."
    },
    "The River of Lamentation": {
      "name": "The River of Lamentation",
      "basicInfo": "A sluggish, black river that winds through Cocytus, its waters never freezing despite the bitter cold. The river is said to be composed of the tears of all who have ever mourned, and its touch brings a profound sense of loss. The banks are lined with the frozen forms of those who drowned their sorrows in the river, their bodies preserved in ice. A single, ancient ferryman, a hooded figure made of ice and shadow, offers passage across the river in exchange for a cherished memory. The river is also the source of 'frost-shards', crystals that form where the tears freeze. These shards are potent components for ice magic and are highly sought after. The water is dangerous to drink, as it erases the drinker's ability to feel joy, but a single drop placed on the tongue of a dying creature can ease their passing.",
      "x": -10,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Cocytus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Frozen Bank": {
          "description": "A wide, icy shore where the river slows and thickens. The ground is covered in frost-shards that glitter like tears. Derro miners work here, chipping away at the ice under the watchful eye of their guildmaster, a sneering derro named Glacia. The shards are stored in enchanted containers to prevent them from melting. A small shrine to Auril, the Frostmaiden, stands on the bank, where her exiled cultists pray. The air is filled with the sound of cracking ice and soft sobbing.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ferryman's Crossing",
            "The Spring of First Tears"
          ]
        },
        "The Ferryman's Crossing": {
          "description": "A narrow point in the river where the current is calm. A single, ancient dock of black ice extends into the water. The ferryman, a silent figure in a hooded cloak of frost, waits here with his boat—a hollowed-out block of ice. To cross, one must hand over a memory of a loved one, which the ferryman places in a lantern that glows with the warmth of the memory. The crossing is brief but the cold is intense. The ferryman never speaks; his price is always the same.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Bank",
            "The Spring of First Tears"
          ]
        },
        "The Spring of First Tears": {
          "description": "A hidden source at the river's origin, a small pool of liquid sorrow that bubbles up from a crack in the bedrock. The water here is pure and silver, not black. A night hag, Mother Sorrow, guards the spring, trading its water for rare ingredients and secrets. The water can restore a lost memory, but only if the drinker is willing to feel the full weight of the associated grief. The pool is warded against scrying, and the hag's lair is nearby, filled with jars of preserved memories.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Bank",
            "The Ferryman's Crossing"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Derro mining guild (Glacia)",
        "The Ferryman",
        "Mother Sorrow (night hag)",
        "Cult of Auril"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Mother Sorrow is not merely a trader; she is a collector of first tears—tears shed at the moment of a great loss. These tears are used to brew a potion that can force an enemy to relive their worst grief. She is secretly working with the Queen of Air and Darkness to harvest enough tears to create a 'Potion of Eternal Winter' that would plunge all of Pandemonium into a permanent ice age."
    },
    "The Screaming Cliffs": {
      "name": "The Screaming Cliffs",
      "basicInfo": "A vast chasm in Cocytus where the wind is funneled and amplified into a physical force. The cliffs are sheer walls of black ice, and the wind howls through the gap with a sound like a thousand banshees. The edges are lined with the frozen remains of those who were caught in the blast. The cliffs are a natural barrier, and crossing them requires either a bridge of solid ice (which the wind constantly erodes) or a path through the tunnels that run beneath. The area is home to the Frozen Choir, a group of undead banshees who were once singers and now wail their eternal lament. They attack any who approach, but their song can be appeased with an offering of a new melody. At the bottom of the chasm, a river of liquid air flows, cold enough to shatter stone.",
      "x": 14,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Cocytus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Wind-Eroded Bridge": {
          "description": "A narrow bridge of translucent ice that spans the chasm, constantly being worn away by the wind. Crossing requires careful footing and a strong nerve. The bridge is maintained by a small guild of ice mages who charge a toll in frost-shards. At the center, the wind is strongest, and a single misstep means death. The bridge offers a breathtaking, terrifying view of the chasm's depths.",
          "paths": [
            "The Banshee's Perch",
            "The Under-Tunnels"
          ]
        },
        "The Banshee's Perch": {
          "description": "A ledge on the chasm wall where the Frozen Choir gathers. The banshees hover in a circle, their mouths open in an eternal scream. Their leader, a banshee named Elegy, was once a famous elven singer who was betrayed and died of grief. She can be reasoned with if presented with a song of true beauty; she will then allow passage and may even teach a fragment of her death-song, a spell that can drive enemies to despair. The perch is littered with the frozen tears of the choir.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wind-Eroded Bridge",
            "The Under-Tunnels"
          ]
        },
        "The Under-Tunnels": {
          "description": "A network of narrow, twisting passages that run beneath the chasm, providing an alternative route that avoids the wind. The tunnels are dark and cramped, and they are patrolled by ice mephits and the occasional frost salamander. They emerge on the far side of the cliffs, near a small encampment of derro miners. The air is still but freezing, and the walls are coated in a thin layer of frost.",
          "paths": [
            "The Wind-Eroded Bridge",
            "The Banshee's Perch"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Frozen Choir (banshees, led by Elegy)",
        "Ice mage guild",
        "Ice mephits"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Elegy's death-song is more than a spell; it is a key. If the full melody is sung at the exact center of the chasm during a rare lull in the wind, it will open a hidden passage to the layer of Agathion. The banshees guard this secret jealously, as the passage leads to the domain of the Queen of Air and Darkness, whom they fear above all else."
    },
    "The Firefalls": {
      "name": "The Firefalls",
      "basicInfo": "A spectacular cascade of liquid fire that pours from a fissure in Phlegethon's ceiling, falling hundreds of feet into a lake of magma. The heat is so intense that the air itself shimmers and burns. The Firefalls are a source of power for the fire-based denizens of the layer, and many forge their weapons in the falling flames. The area is contested by fire giants, salamanders, and fire howlers, each seeking to control the falls. A single, narrow path of cooled lava, the 'Causeway of Cinders', leads to the base, but it is constantly shifting and cracking. At the very bottom, where the fire meets the magma lake, a legendary forge, the 'Anvil of the Burning Wind', is said to exist, capable of crafting weapons of pure elemental fire.",
      "x": -10,
      "y": -4,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Phlegethon",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Causeway of Cinders": {
          "description": "A narrow, winding path of cooled lava that descends from the upper tunnels to the magma lake. The path is unstable, and chunks break off underfoot, falling into the fire below. The heat is intense, and unprotected travelers quickly succumb. Small ledges offer brief respites, where one can observe the Firefalls. A group of fire giant guards patrols the upper section, demanding tribute from all who pass.",
          "paths": [
            "The Anvil of the Burning Wind",
            "The Magma Lake Shore"
          ]
        },
        "The Anvil of the Burning Wind": {
          "description": "A legendary forge located at the base of the Firefalls, where the falling flames strike a massive, black anvil of obsidian. The anvil is said to have been placed here by a primordial smith, and it can shape metal with a single strike. The forge is guarded by a fire giant smith named Surtr, who has mastered the art of forging with liquid fire. He will craft a weapon of elemental fire for anyone who brings him a piece of a fire elemental's core. The heat around the anvil is such that even fire-immune creatures feel discomfort.",
          "paths": [
            "The Causeway of Cinders",
            "The Magma Lake Shore"
          ]
        },
        "The Magma Lake Shore": {
          "description": "A black sand beach at the edge of the magma lake, where the Firefalls' flow meets the molten rock. The shore is dotted with the nests of fire howlers, who use the heat to incubate their young. A small encampment of salamanders, exiled from the City of Brass, has built crude shelters from cooled lava. Their chieftain, a salamander named Ignis, can provide information about the layer in exchange for weapons or food. The air is thick with the smell of sulfur and the roar of the falls.",
          "paths": [
            "The Causeway of Cinders",
            "The Anvil of the Burning Wind"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Fire giant guards",
        "Surtr (fire giant smith)",
        "Salamander exiles (Ignis)",
        "Fire howlers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Surtr is not a mere smith; he is a fallen azer who was transformed by the primordial smith into a fire giant as a reward for his craftsmanship. He secretly seeks to reforge the 'Heart of the Inferno' shard beneath the Firefalls into a weapon that could challenge the gods. He will trade his knowledge for a single tear from the River of Lamentation, which he needs to quench the blade's edge."
    },
    "The Ash Warrens": {
      "name": "The Ash Warrens",
      "basicInfo": "A network of tunnels filled with choking, grey ash that lies beneath Phlegethon's fiery surface. The ash is warm and soft, muffling all sound, and it fills the air with a fine, grey dust that clogs the lungs. The Warrens are a place of hiding for those who wish to avoid the open flames, but they are not safe: ash mephits, fire bats, and the occasional young red dragon lair here. The tunnels twist and fork endlessly, and without a guide, one can easily become lost in the grey silence. A few hidden chambers, carved by long-dead exiles, contain caches of weapons and supplies, as well as the remnants of failed escapes. At the center lies the 'Cavern of Unending Pyre', a vast chamber where a single, eternal flame burns, feeding on the ash itself.",
      "x": 8,
      "y": 3,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Phlegethon",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Grey Maze": {
          "description": "The outer tunnels of the Warrens, a confusing labyrinth of identical, ash-filled passages. Visibility is near zero, and the ash deadens sound, making it difficult to detect threats. Scattered throughout are the petrified remains of those who suffocated, their forms preserved in ash. A small, hidden camp of escaped slaves, led by a fire genasi named Cinder, offers shelter and guidance to those who can find it. They know several safe routes through the maze.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cavern of Unending Pyre",
            "The Ash Mephit Nest"
          ]
        },
        "The Cavern of Unending Pyre": {
          "description": "A huge, circular chamber at the Warrens' center, where a single, white-hot flame burns continuously, consuming the ash that falls into it. The flame provides the only light, casting long shadows. The heat is intense, but the chamber's edges are cooler. A ring of blackened stone pillars surrounds the flame, each carved with warnings in Infernal. A group of fire cultists, the 'Keepers of the Pyre', tends the flame, believing it to be a fragment of the primordial fire. They are fanatical and will attack intruders.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grey Maze",
            "The Ash Mephit Nest"
          ]
        },
        "The Ash Mephit Nest": {
          "description": "A series of small, warm cavities in the tunnel walls where ash mephits dwell. The mephits are mischievous and will trade information for shiny objects or for the chance to play tricks on travelers. Their leader, a larger mephit named Puff, knows the locations of several hidden treasure caches in the Warrens. The nest is filled with the glittering detritus of their collections.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grey Maze",
            "The Cavern of Unending Pyre"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Cinder (escaped slave leader)",
        "Keepers of the Pyre (fire cult)",
        "Ash mephits (Puff)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Cavern of Unending Pyre is actually a dying fire primordial, the last ember of a being that once rivaled the gods. The Keepers are not tending it; they are slowly draining its power to feed their own ambitions. Cinder knows this and seeks to free the primordial, which would cause a volcanic eruption that could collapse the Warrens—but also destroy the Keepers."
    },
    "The Bottomless Pit": {
      "name": "The Bottomless Pit",
      "basicInfo": "The deepest known point in Agathion, a vertical shaft of absolute darkness that plunges into oblivion. The walls are smooth and featureless, and there is no sound, no wind, no light. The Pit is the final destination for the most irredeemable souls and the most dangerous exiles. Those thrown in do not die; they fall forever, their minds slowly unraveling in the silent dark. At the very edge, a single, broken platform of black stone offers a place to stand, but lingering too long invites the attention of the 'Pitwardens', silent guardians of the void who drag the unwary over the edge. The Pit is also a source of 'void-shards', crystals of pure darkness that can be used to craft weapons of shadow. These shards are collected by derro miners who risk the edge, roped together and praying to the silence.",
      "x": -1,
      "y": 6,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Agathion",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Broken Platform": {
          "description": "A jagged slab of black stone that juts over the Pit. It is cracked and unstable, and pieces occasionally break off, falling silently into the void. Here, the Pitwardens perform their duties: binding prisoners, reciting final judgments, and then casting them into the abyss. A single, ancient chair sits at the platform's edge, where the last Chief Pitwarden once sat. The chair is empty now, but its shadow moves.",
          "paths": [
            "The Void-Shard Quarry",
            "The Pitwardens' Barracks"
          ]
        },
        "The Void-Shard Quarry": {
          "description": "A side cavern where derro miners chip away at the black crystal formations that grow near the Pit. The shards are cold to the touch and seem to absorb light. The miners work in silence, their picks wrapped in cloth to muffle the sound. A single foreman, a blind derro named Nul, coordinates the operation using a series of taps on the stone. He can appraise a void-shard's quality and will buy them from travelers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Broken Platform",
            "The Pitwardens' Barracks"
          ]
        },
        "The Pitwardens' Barracks": {
          "description": "A low, featureless building near the quarry where the Pitwardens (silent, hooded figures) rest between duties. The interior is spartan: rows of stone beds, a small shrine to an unknown god of endings, and a rack of dark, hooked spears used to pull prisoners to the edge. The Pitwardens do not speak, but they will communicate through written notes. Their leader, a figure known only as 'The Final Word', is said to be able to see the future of any soul cast into the Pit.",
          "paths": [
            "The Broken Platform",
            "The Void-Shard Quarry"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Pitwardens",
        "Nul (blind derro foreman)",
        "Derro miners"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Bottomless Pit is not truly bottomless; it ends at the 'Maw of Oblivion', a gate to the Negative Energy Plane. The Pitwardens are not merely jailers; they are the last line of defense against a horde of nightshades and other negative-energy creatures that would pour through if the gate were ever opened. The 'Final Word' is a powerful undead who can seal the gate, but only by sacrificing a soul of pure darkness—a rarity in Agathion."
    },
    "The Silent Dark": {
      "name": "The Silent Dark",
      "basicInfo": "A region of Agathion where all sound is magically suppressed, creating an oppressive, suffocating silence. The tunnels are narrow and winding, and the darkness is absolute, so thick that darkvision fails. Only creatures with blindsight or other non-visual senses can navigate. The Silent Dark is home to the Queen of Air and Darkness, whose invisible court holds sway in a palace of frozen shadows. It is also a hunting ground for 'quiet ones', creatures that move without sound and strike without warning. Travelers who enter must rely on touch and instinct, and many lose their way, wandering until they collapse from exhaustion or are taken by the Queen's servants. The only landmark is a single, faintly glowing crystal, the 'Lamp of Lost Souls', which appears to those who are about to die, offering a final, false hope.",
      "x": -6,
      "y": -6,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Agathion",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Absence": {
          "description": "A long, featureless corridor where sound does not exist. The silence is so complete that one can hear their own heartbeat, and then even that fades. The walls are cold and smooth, and there are no landmarks. A few scattered bones lie on the floor, but no sign of how their owners died. The Absence is a test of mental fortitude; those who cannot endure the silence begin to hallucinate sounds that are not there, eventually driven mad.",
          "paths": [
            "The Queen's Palace",
            "The Lamp of Lost Souls"
          ]
        },
        "The Queen's Palace": {
          "description": "A castle of black ice and frozen shadow, hidden within the Silent Dark. It is invisible to the naked eye, and only those with true sight or the Queen's invitation can find it. Inside, the halls are lit by a cold, grey light that seems to come from nowhere. The Queen of Air and Darkness sits on a throne of crystallized winter, her form shifting between a beautiful elven woman and a nightmare of frost and shadow. She speaks in a voice that is felt rather than heard, and her words are always laced with hidden meaning. Her court consists of frozen fey, undead bards, and the spirits of those who died of despair. She can grant boons of winter magic, but her price is always a piece of the supplicant's warmth.",
          "paths": [
            "The Absence",
            "The Lamp of Lost Souls"
          ]
        },
        "The Lamp of Lost Souls": {
          "description": "A single, dimly glowing crystal that floats in the darkness, appearing only to those who are near death or who have lost all hope. The light is cold and grey, and it shows the viewer a vision of their final moments. Some follow the lamp, hoping for rescue, but it leads only deeper into the Silent Dark. A small, twisted figure—a spectral gnome—guards the lamp, cackling silently at the despair of those who approach. It will trade a single ray of true hope for a memory of warmth.",
          "paths": [
            "The Absence",
            "The Queen's Palace"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Queen of Air and Darkness",
        "Frozen fey courtiers",
        "The spectral gnome (Lamp guardian)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Queen of Air and Darkness is not the true ruler of the Silent Dark; she is a prisoner, bound by a pact with the gods to remain here. The silence is her cage. If someone were to speak a single, true word of love within the Silent Dark, the pact would weaken, and the Queen could escape—an event that would plunge the upper layers of Pandemonium into eternal winter. The spectral gnome was once a bard who spoke that word and was punished by being bound to the lamp, forever showing false hope."
    },
    "The Sighing Vaults": {
      "name": "The Sighing Vaults",
      "basicInfo": "A series of ancient, vaulted chambers in Agathion, built by a long-dead civilization that sought to create a place of absolute peace. The vaults are carved from smooth, grey stone, and the air is still and cold. The only sound is a faint, rhythmic sighing—the echo of the builders' last breaths, preserved by the magic of the place. The vaults are a sanctuary for those who seek to escape the noise of the other layers, but they are also a tomb: those who stay too long become part of the sighing, their souls absorbed into the stone. A few intact artifacts remain, including the 'Sighing Bell', a large bell of grey metal that, when struck, emits a tone of perfect calm that can quell even the most violent rage. The bell is guarded by the last descendant of the builders, an ancient, ghostly dwarf named Stone-Whisper, who seeks to restore the vaults to their original purpose.",
      "x": 9,
      "y": -5,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Agathion",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Hall of Echoes": {
          "description": "A long hall where the sighing is most prominent. The walls are covered in faint, carved faces, their mouths open as if sighing. The sound resonates, and those who listen can almost hear words—whispers of forgotten grief and long-lost peace. A few benches of stone offer places to rest, but sitting for too long causes one's own breathing to slow, and eventually stop. The hall leads to the Bell Chamber.",
          "paths": [
            "The Bell Chamber",
            "The Tombs of the Builders"
          ]
        },
        "The Bell Chamber": {
          "description": "A circular room at the vaults' center, dominated by the Sighing Bell. The bell is large enough to require two people to ring it. Its surface is unadorned, but it hums faintly even at rest. Stone-Whisper, the ghostly dwarf, kneels before it, whispering prayers. He will allow the bell to be rung only if the ringer swears to use its sound for peace, never for war. The sound, when rung, fills the entire vaults with a wave of calm that can stop a battle. The chamber's acoustics are perfect, and the bell's tone lingers for minutes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Echoes",
            "The Tombs of the Builders"
          ]
        },
        "The Tombs of the Builders": {
          "description": "Rows of stone sarcophagi where the original builders were laid to rest. Their bodies have long since crumbled to dust, but their sighing spirits remain, drifting through the vaults. A few tombs contain intact grave goods: rings of grey metal, scrolls of forgotten philosophy, and a single, silver key that opens a hidden door to a chamber of absolute stillness. The key is held by the spirit of the first builder, a woman named Alira, who appears only to those who can answer her riddle about the nature of peace.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Echoes",
            "The Bell Chamber"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Stone-Whisper (ghostly dwarf guardian)",
        "The Sighing Spirits (builders)",
        "Alira (spirit of the first builder)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Sighing Bell's true power is not just to calm; it can also release the trapped souls of the vaults, allowing them to pass on to their final rest. Stone-Whisper does not know this; he believes ringing the bell only maintains the peace. If the bell were rung in a specific sequence, the sighing would cease, the vaults would fall silent, and the spirits would ascend—but the sanctuary would lose its protective aura, leaving it vulnerable to the horrors of Agathion."
    },
    "The Demonweb Pits": {
      "name": "The Demonweb Pits",
      "basicInfo": "The 66th layer of the Abyss, the domain of Lolth, the Spider Queen. Here, the entire layer is a vast, tangled web of impossibly strong silk, stretching across an infinite chasm. Great iron citadels and black temples hang from the strands, and the only light comes from the faint, sickly glow of the web itself. Souls of the drow and their enemies are brought here to be tormented in Lolth's name. The Demonweb Pits are also home to the yochlol, the handmaidens of Lolth, and to countless spiders of monstrous size. Travelers can navigate the web by following the main strands, but a single misstep leads to a fall into the endless dark below. At the center lies the Iron City of Lolth, a fortress of black metal where the Spider Queen sits on her throne, watching all that crawls.",
      "x": -18,
      "y": 13,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "The Infinite Layers",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Webwalk": {
          "description": "The main pathway across the Demonweb Pits, a wide bridge of woven silk that spans the chasm. The web is sticky in places, and smaller strands form a labyrinth of side passages. Drow souls, transformed into driders, patrol the web as guardians. A constant, faint vibration runs through the silk, as if the whole layer is alive. Along the edges, cocoons of silk contain the still-living bodies of Lolth's sacrifices, their eyes moving in eternal terror.",
          "paths": [
            "The Iron City",
            "The Chasm of Spiders"
          ]
        },
        "The Iron City": {
          "description": "The seat of Lolth's power, a fortress of black iron built at the web's center. The city is a maze of narrow streets, shadowed towers, and sacrificial altars. Here, Lolth sits on a throne of fused drider skulls, her form shifting between a beautiful drow woman and a monstrous spider. The walls are hung with the banners of her most loyal houses, and the streets are filled with her servants: drow priestesses, yochlol, and enslaved demons. The air is thick with the scent of blood and incense. Visitors are rarely admitted, and those who are must crawl on their bellies before the Spider Queen.",
          "paths": [
            "The Webwalk",
            "The Chasm of Spiders",
            "The Temple of the Spider Queen"
          ]
        },
        "The Chasm of Spiders": {
          "description": "A deep, vertical shaft that descends into the lower layers of the Abyss, but is also home to millions of spiders of all sizes. The walls are covered in webs, and the floor is hidden in darkness. Giant spiders, phase spiders, and worse crawl in the depths. A narrow stairway of silk and bone leads downward, but few dare to use it. At the very bottom, a portal to the 67th layer is guarded by a colossal, ancient spider that has never been defeated.",
          "paths": [
            "The Webwalk",
            "The Iron City"
          ]
        },
        "The Temple of the Spider Queen": {
          "description": "A grand temple within the Iron City, dedicated to Lolth. The temple is shaped like a gigantic spider, its legs forming the outer pillars. Inside, the walls are covered in carvings of drow history and the torments of Lolth's enemies. The central altar is a living spider that consumes sacrifices. Priestesses perform rituals that grant them power, and the air is filled with chittering and the screams of victims. A hidden chamber beneath the altar contains Lolth's phylactery, a black pearl the size of a fist, guarded by the yochlol.",
          "paths": [
            "The Iron City"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Lolth (Spider Queen)",
        "Yochlol handmaidens",
        "Drow priestesses",
        "Drider guardians"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Lolth's phylactery is not a typical lich's phylactery; it is a vessel for her divine essence, created as a backup in case her worshipers ever turn against her. If it were destroyed, Lolth would be greatly weakened, and her control over the Demonweb Pits would falter. The yochlol know this and will sacrifice themselves to protect it. A rival demon lord, Graz'zt, has been seeking the phylactery for centuries, hoping to use it as leverage against Lolth."
    },
    "Thanatos": {
      "name": "Thanatos",
      "basicInfo": "The 113th layer of the Abyss, the domain of Orcus, the Demon Prince of Undeath. Thanatos is a frozen, desolate wasteland of black ice, grey ash, and the ruins of ancient cities. The sky is a perpetual, leaden grey, and the air is cold and still. The land is dotted with tombs, mausoleums, and the bones of colossal creatures. Here, the dead do not rest; they rise as undead servants of Orcus. The River Styx flows along the layer's edge, carrying souls to Orcus's court. At the center lies the fortress of Naratyr, a city of bone and shadow, where Orcus sits on his throne, commanding legions of zombies, wights, and liches. Thanatos is a place of absolute despair, where the only warmth is the cold flame of undeath.",
      "x": 4,
      "y": -29,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "The Infinite Layers",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Ashen Plain": {
          "description": "A vast, flat expanse of grey ash and black ice that stretches for miles. The ground is frozen solid, and the air is deathly cold. Scattered across the plain are the petrified corpses of ancient demons and the ruins of fortresses destroyed in past wars. Wandering undead, including packs of ghouls and the occasional zombie giant, roam the plain, attacking any living creature. A few crumbling watchtowers offer temporary shelter, but they are often occupied by wights. The plain is a killing ground, and only the strongest can cross it.",
          "paths": [
            "Naratyr",
            "The Bone Fields"
          ]
        },
        "Naratyr": {
          "description": "The city of the dead, the capital of Thanatos and the seat of Orcus's power. The city is a sprawling necropolis of bone, stone, and shadow, built around a central citadel. The streets are paved with gravestones, and the buildings are mausoleums and temples to undeath. The city is filled with undead of all types, from simple zombies to powerful liches. At the center, the citadel of Orcus rises, a tower of fused skulls and black iron. Inside, Orcus, a bloated demon of immense power, sits on his throne, his Wand of Orcus at his side. The citadel also houses the 'Well of Souls', a pit where souls are transformed into undead. The air is thick with the smell of decay and the moans of the damned.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ashen Plain",
            "The Bone Fields",
            "The Well of Souls"
          ]
        },
        "The Bone Fields": {
          "description": "A region where the bones of a thousand ancient battles lie scattered, forming dunes and ridges of skeletal remains. The field is home to bone devils, skeletal champions, and other undead who feed on the marrow of the fallen. Hidden among the bones are caches of weapons and armor left by fallen warriors, some of which are still enchanted. A small, fortified camp of the 'Order of the Final Word', a group of necromancers who study undeath, is located at the edge, but they are hostile to outsiders.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ashen Plain",
            "Naratyr"
          ]
        },
        "The Well of Souls": {
          "description": "A massive pit in the center of Naratyr's citadel, where souls are dropped and transformed into undead. The pit glows with a sickly green light, and the walls are covered in the faces of the condemned. The process is agonizing, and the souls' screams can be heard across the city. A group of night hags, the 'Soul Weavers', oversees the transformation, extracting the most powerful souls for Orcus's personal guard. The pit is guarded by a pair of nightwalkers, giant undead of immense power.",
          "paths": [
            "Naratyr"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Orcus (Demon Prince of Undeath)",
        "Night hags (Soul Weavers)",
        "Undead legions",
        "Order of the Final Word (necromancers)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Orcus's Wand is not the true source of his power; it is a focus. His true power lies in his ability to command the dead, which is tied to his soul, hidden in a phylactery shaped like a small, black pearl. That phylactery is not in Thanatos but in the Astral Plane, hidden among the god-corpses. Orcus has been searching for it for centuries, unaware that it is guarded by the githyanki of Tu'narath, who do not know its true nature."
    },
    "Azzagrat": {
      "name": "Azzagrat",
      "basicInfo": "The realm of Graz'zt, the Dark Prince, encompassing the 45th, 46th, and 47th layers of the Abyss. Azzagrat is a place of dark beauty and perverse luxury, where pleasure and pain are intertwined. The landscape is a mixture of elegant cities, lush gardens, and shadowed valleys, all tainted by the demon prince's influence. The three layers are connected by a vast silver causeway, and each has its own character: the 45th is a city of decadent palaces, the 46th is a realm of illusions and mirrors, and the 47th is a shadowy forest of twisted trees. Graz'zt rules from the Argent Palace, a vast structure of white marble and black silk, where he indulges in endless hedonism and plots against his rivals. Souls who worshipped beauty or desire are drawn here, becoming his playthings or his soldiers.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "The Infinite Layers",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Argent Palace": {
          "description": "The seat of Graz'zt, a sprawling palace of white marble, mirrored halls, and silk-draped chambers. The palace is a labyrinth of pleasure and danger, where the Dark Prince entertains guests with feasts of forbidden delights and hunts of terrified victims. The walls are covered in shifting tapestries that depict scenes of both ecstasy and agony. The throne room is a vast, circular hall of black glass, where Graz'zt sits on a throne of fused silver and bone. He is a towering, handsome demon with ebony skin and six fingers on each hand, his eyes burning with a violet fire. The palace also houses his harem of succubi and incubi, his libraries of forbidden lore, and his treasure vaults.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gardens of Flesh",
            "The Mirror Maze",
            "The Sable Forest"
          ]
        },
        "The Gardens of Flesh": {
          "description": "A grotesque garden of plants made from living flesh, bone, and sinew, located behind the Argent Palace. The flowers weep blood, the trees are twisted into screaming shapes, and the paths are paved with teeth. The garden is a place of dark meditation for Graz'zt, and its caretakers are a group of enslaved druids who have been corrupted by his will. At its center stands a fountain of perfume and blood, where Graz'zt's concubines bathe. The garden is also a source of rare alchemical ingredients, like the 'Tears of Desire' and the 'Petals of Regret'.",
          "paths": [
            "The Argent Palace",
            "The Mirror Maze"
          ]
        },
        "The Mirror Maze": {
          "description": "A section of the palace complex that is an endless labyrinth of mirrored walls, designed to trap and confuse. The mirrors show not reflections but the deepest desires and fears of the viewer, leading many to become lost in their own minds. The maze is guarded by creatures of illusion, and at its heart lies a single, clear pool where Graz'zt can view any location in his realm. A hidden door in the maze leads to the 46th layer's silver causeway.",
          "paths": [
            "The Argent Palace",
            "The Gardens of Flesh"
          ]
        },
        "The Sable Forest": {
          "description": "A vast, shadowy forest on the 47th layer, where the trees are black and leafless, and the ground is covered in a fine, grey mist. The forest is home to the 'Sable Riders', Graz'zt's elite cavalry, who patrol on shadowy steeds. The trees themselves are sentient and will whisper secrets to those who listen, but their words are always twisted. A hidden grove at the forest's center holds the 'Heart of Azzagrat', a black crystal that beats like a heart and is the source of the realm's dark beauty. Destroying it would cause the three layers to collapse into chaos.",
          "paths": [
            "The Argent Palace"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Graz'zt (Dark Prince)",
        "Succubi and incubi court",
        "Sable Riders",
        "Enslaved druids"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Graz'zt's true name is known only to himself and to his mother, the witch-queen Iggwilv. If his true name were spoken in his presence, he would be bound to the speaker's will for a single command. The name is hidden in the Mirror Maze, inscribed on the back of the one mirror that shows a true reflection. Graz'zt's agents have been searching for it for centuries, but the maze keeps it hidden from even him."
    },
    "The Gaping Maw": {
      "name": "The Gaping Maw",
      "basicInfo": "The 88th layer of the Abyss, the domain of Demogorgon, the Prince of Demons. The Gaping Maw is a realm of primordial chaos, where the land itself is alive with savage fury. The layer is dominated by two great features: the Maelstrom, a swirling vortex of water and wind that forms a permanent storm at the center, and the twin volcanoes known as the 'Eyes of Demogorgon', which belch fire and ash. The terrain is a hellish mix of jagged rocks, boiling seas, and jungles of venomous plants. Demogorgon's palace, Abysm, is a fortress of black coral and bone built on the edge of the Maelstrom. Here, the two-headed demon lord plots the destruction of all other demon lords, his two heads constantly arguing and scheming. The layer is home to a horde of savage demons, including the hissing, snake-like abyssal creatures and the amphibious skum.",
      "x": -18,
      "y": -17,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "The Infinite Layers",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Maelstrom": {
          "description": "A vast, permanent storm of water and wind at the layer's center, where the seas of the Abyss churn endlessly. The Maelstrom is a natural barrier, and its currents are strong enough to shred even a demon lord. Flying above it is impossible; ships are torn apart. At its heart, a single eye of calm exists, but only Demogorgon can navigate the treacherous currents to reach it. The Maelstrom is also a source of power, and many water demons worship it as a god.",
          "paths": [
            "Abysm",
            "The Serpentine Jungles"
          ]
        },
        "Abysm": {
          "description": "Demogorgon's fortress, a sprawling palace of black coral, bone, and petrified wood built on the edge of the Maelstrom. The palace is a maze of twisting corridors, each designed to disorient and terrify. The two heads of Demogorgon, Aameul and Hethradiah, share a single body but have separate consciousnesses, and their bickering echoes through the halls. The throne room is a vast, open chamber where Demogorgon receives envoys, his heads hissing and roaring. A great pit in the floor, the 'Mouth of the Abyss', leads to the lower layers, but only Demogorgon dares to descend. The palace also houses a menagerie of mutated creatures and a library of forbidden tomes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Maelstrom",
            "The Serpentine Jungles",
            "The Twin Volcanoes"
          ]
        },
        "The Serpentine Jungles": {
          "description": "A dense jungle of twisted, venomous plants and reptilian horrors, located on the layer's edge. The trees are serpentine, their branches reaching like grasping hands. The ground is soft with decay, and the air is thick with spores that cause hallucinations. The jungle is home to the 'Yuan-ti of the Abyss', corrupted serpentfolk who worship Demogorgon as a god. Their temples, built from bone and stone, are scattered among the trees. The jungle is also a hunting ground for Demogorgon's sahuagin and skum servants. Hidden in the depths is a pool of black water that grants visions of the deep sea, but its touch is fatal to surface dwellers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Maelstrom",
            "Abysm"
          ]
        },
        "The Twin Volcanoes": {
          "description": "Two massive volcanoes that rise from the layer's surface, their craters constantly erupting with fire and ash. The volcanoes are the 'Eyes of Demogorgon', and he uses them as both a source of power and a warning system. The slopes are covered in fire-resistant demons, and the craters are guarded by fire giants who have pledged loyalty to the Prince of Demons. At the base of the volcanoes, a war camp of tanar'ri prepares for the next offensive in the Blood War. A hidden forge within the volcanoes creates weapons of obsidian and hellfire.",
          "paths": [
            "Abysm"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Demogorgon (Prince of Demons)",
        "Yuan-ti of the Abyss",
        "Fire giant guards",
        "Sahuagin and skum"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Demogorgon's two heads are actually two separate demon lords who were fused together by a curse from an ancient god. They hate each other and constantly seek to separate, but their bond is unbreakable. A single artifact, the 'Crown of the Two', could split them into two distinct beings, but each head would rather destroy the other than share power. The crown is hidden in the Serpentine Jungles, in a temple dedicated to a forgotten serpent god."
    },
    "The Death Dells": {
      "name": "The Death Dells",
      "basicInfo": "The 422nd layer of the Abyss, the domain of Yeenoghu, the Demon Prince of Gnolls. The Death Dells is a bleak, dry landscape of cracked earth, thorny scrub, and endless hills under a blood-red sky. The land is silent except for the howls of gnoll packs and the cries of their victims. It is a place of hunger and savagery, where the only law is the hunt. Yeenoghu rules from a massive throne of bones, surrounded by his personal pack of half-fiend gnolls. The layer is also home to the 'Butcher's Pit', a deep chasm where sacrifices are flung, and the 'Feast Halls', where gnolls devour their prey in gluttonous rituals. The Death Dells are a reminder of the Abyss's capacity for pure, mindless destruction.",
      "x": 19,
      "y": -37,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "The Infinite Layers",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Throne of Bones": {
          "description": "Yeenoghu's seat of power, a massive, crude throne built from the bones of countless victims. The Demon Prince of Gnolls sits here, a towering, hyena-headed demon with glowing red eyes and a bloody flail. Around him, his pack of half-fiend gnolls and other demonic hyenas lounge, gnawing on bones. The throne is located on a raised hill, giving Yeenoghu a view of his entire layer. To approach, one must pass through a gauntlet of gnoll guards who test strength and savagery. The ground around the throne is littered with offerings—skulls, weapons, and the occasional living captive.",
          "paths": [
            "The Butcher's Pit",
            "The Feast Halls"
          ]
        },
        "The Butcher's Pit": {
          "description": "A deep, narrow chasm where sacrifices to Yeenoghu are hurled. The pit is lined with jagged rocks and the bones of the fallen, and the bottom is lost in darkness. The walls are covered in dried blood and gnawed flesh. A pack of giant hyenas lives at the bottom, waiting for the next meal. Some say the pit leads to the 423rd layer, but no one has ever returned to confirm it. The area around the pit is a gathering place for gnoll tribes preparing for war.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of Bones",
            "The Feast Halls"
          ]
        },
        "The Feast Halls": {
          "description": "A series of crude halls and caves where gnolls hold their feasts after a successful hunt. The feasts are orgies of gluttony and violence, where the pack devours both prey and weak members. The air is thick with the smell of roasting meat and blood. The walls are covered in carvings depicting Yeenoghu's hunts. The largest hall, the 'Maw of Hunger', is where the Demon Prince himself sometimes feasts, his laughter echoing as he tears flesh from bone. A hidden storehouse contains the tribe's treasure: stolen weapons, armor, and the occasional magical item from fallen adventurers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of Bones",
            "The Butcher's Pit"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Yeenoghu (Demon Prince of Gnolls)",
        "Half-fiend gnoll pack",
        "Giant hyenas"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Yeenoghu's flail is not just a weapon; it contains the soul of the first gnoll he ever created, which gives him control over all gnolls. If the flail were destroyed, the gnoll race would be freed from his influence, but they would also lose their demonic power. The flail is kept in Yeenoghu's possession at all times, and he will fight to the death to protect it. A prophecy states that a gnoll born with white fur will one day shatter the flail and lead the gnolls to freedom, but Yeenoghu has killed every white-furred gnoll he has ever seen."
    },
    "The Stygian Marsh": {
      "name": "The Stygian Marsh",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, fetid swamp in Othrys where the River Styx spreads into a maze of black channels and stagnant pools. The air is thick with mosquitoes and the croaking of unseen things. The marsh is the primary arrival point for souls condemned to Carceri, brought here by merrenoloth ferrymen. A small, ramshackle dock, the 'Pale Quay', serves as the entrance. The swamp is patrolled by demodands and giant crocodiles. A few hovels of exiled mortals cluster on the higher ground, offering meager shelter for a price. The Styx's waters are especially dangerous here, as they erode memory and identity with every touch.",
      "x": -11,
      "y": 1,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Othrys",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Pale Quay": {
          "description": "A rickety wooden dock extending into the Styx, where the merrenoloths disembark their passengers. A single, skeletal figure in a grey cloak, the 'Harbinger', checks the manifests of the newly damned. The quay is lit by a few guttering torches that never go out. The air smells of rot and old blood. A toll is exacted in memories; those who cannot pay are thrown directly into the marsh.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shantytown",
            "The Demodand Watchpost"
          ]
        },
        "The Shantytown": {
          "description": "A cluster of wooden shacks and tents built on stilts above the swamp, home to the few exiles who have survived long enough to build something. The inhabitants are a mix of escaped souls, failed planar travelers, and minor fiends fallen from favor. They trade in stolen goods, information, and swamp remedies. The leader, a one-eyed tiefling named Rask, keeps order with a mix of fear and pragmatism.",
          "paths": [
            "The Pale Quay",
            "The Demodand Watchpost"
          ]
        },
        "The Demodand Watchpost": {
          "description": "A fortified tower of black stone on a small island, where a squad of demodands monitors the marsh. The tower is filled with chains, cages, and instruments of interrogation. The captain, a farastu demodand named Gristle, demands a tithe from all who pass. A hidden cell beneath the tower holds prisoners awaiting transport to deeper layers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Pale Quay",
            "The Shantytown"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Harbinger (merrenoloth clerk)",
        "Rask (shantytown leader)",
        "Gristle (demodand captain)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Harbinger is not a merrenoloth; he is a fallen celestial who was bound to the Styx as punishment for guiding souls away from their judgment. He secretly helps those who show genuine remorse, marking them with a hidden sigil that grants safe passage through the swamp. The demodands are unaware of this treason."
    },
    "The Titan's Cage": {
      "name": "The Titan's Cage",
      "basicInfo": "A crumbling temple-fortress deep in the Othrys mountains, where several exiled Titans are imprisoned. The fortress is built from cyclopean stones, its columns carved with scenes of the ancient war against the gods. The Titans are chained to the walls with bonds of adamantine and divine magic, their bodies gaunt but their eyes burning with undying hatred. The air is heavy with their psychic rage, and weaker minds are overwhelmed by visions of cosmic war. The demodands guard the perimeter, but they fear the Titans and rarely enter the inner sanctum. A few mortal cultists, worshipping the Titans as gods, have established a hidden shrine in the lower chambers, offering sacrifices in exchange for fragments of power.",
      "x": 10,
      "y": 1,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Othrys",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Cyclopean Gate": {
          "description": "The entrance to the fortress, a massive archway of grey stone, carved with images of Titans battling gods. The gate is sealed by a riddle spoken by a stone face; answering incorrectly triggers a magical trap that turns the speaker to stone. The demodands have learned the answer and use it to enter, but they are watched by the Titans' servants.",
          "paths": [
            "The Chained Hall",
            "The Cultist Shrine"
          ]
        },
        "The Chained Hall": {
          "description": "The main chamber, a vast, echoing space where the Titans hang from chains suspended from the ceiling. Their forms are colossal, their skin pale and cracked, but their presence is overwhelming. They whisper to visitors, offering power in exchange for freedom. The floor is littered with the shattered remains of those who failed to resist. A single, golden chain, the 'Chain of the First Titan', is the master link; breaking it would free them all.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cyclopean Gate",
            "The Cultist Shrine"
          ]
        },
        "The Cultist Shrine": {
          "description": "A hidden chamber below the fortress, where the mortal cultists gather. The walls are painted with crude images of the Titans' victory over the gods. An altar of black stone holds the still-beating heart of a sacrificed celestial, which the cultists use to commune with their imprisoned masters. The leader, a mad human named Themis, believes the Titans will remake the world in their image.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cyclopean Gate",
            "The Chained Hall"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Exiled Titans",
        "Titan cultists (Themis)",
        "Demodand guards"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Titans are not merely prisoners; they are a prison for something worse. Bound within the heart of the eldest Titan is a fragment of the primordial chaos that once threatened the gods. If the Titans are freed, that fragment will be released, consuming the multiverse. The cultists are unknowingly working toward their own destruction."
    },
    "The Crimson Citadel": {
      "name": "The Crimson Citadel",
      "basicInfo": "A fortress of red stone in Cathrys, built by a powerful demodand lord named Vharag. The Citadel rises from the acid jungle like a wound, its towers dripping with caustic rain. It is the administrative center for the layer's demodand patrols and a staging ground for raids on other layers. The walls are covered in the flayed skins of escaped prisoners, and the air is thick with the smell of blood and vinegar. Inside, the corridors are a maze of barracks, torture chambers, and throne rooms. Vharag rules with a cruel intelligence, and he has begun to question his own imprisonment, seeking a way to escape Carceri entirely.",
      "x": -1,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Cathrys",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Acid Gate": {
          "description": "The main entrance, a portcullis of rusted iron that drips with acid. To pass, one must present a writ from a demodand overseer or pay a toll in souls. The gate is guarded by a pair of kelubar demodands who wield acid-coated whips. Beyond lies the outer courtyard, where prisoners are processed and assigned to work details in the jungle.",
          "paths": [
            "Vharag's Throne Room",
            "The Prison Pens"
          ]
        },
        "Vharag's Throne Room": {
          "description": "A grand hall of red marble and black iron, where Lord Vharag sits on a throne of fused prisoner bones. He is a towering farastu demodand, his skin covered in ritual scars and his eyes burning with cold fire. He receives reports from his subordinates and dispenses justice with a single, dismissive gesture. The walls are lined with the skulls of those who displeased him. A hidden door behind the throne leads to his private chambers.",
          "paths": [
            "The Acid Gate",
            "The Prison Pens"
          ]
        },
        "The Prison Pens": {
          "description": "A block of cramped cells where captured exiles await processing or torture. The cells are flooded with ankle-deep acid, and the prisoners are shackled to the walls. A group of escaped prisoners has formed a resistance, the 'Free Flame', and they use a network of secret tunnels to smuggle supplies. Their leader, a fire genasi named Ember, hides in a false cell.",
          "paths": [
            "The Acid Gate",
            "Vharag's Throne Room"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Lord Vharag (demodand ruler)",
        "Free Flame resistance",
        "Demodand guards"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "Vharag has discovered a hidden portal in the Citadel's depths that leads to the Plane of Fire. He plans to use it to escape Carceri and establish a new domain, but the portal is guarded by a fire primordial who demands a sacrifice of a true traitor. Vharag is currently seeking a suitable victim, and the Free Flame resistance is his primary target."
    },
    "The Spire of the Oinoloth": {
      "name": "The Spire of the Oinoloth",
      "basicInfo": "A massive tower of black iron in the Minethys desert, home to the Oinoloth, a powerful yugoloth lord who rules the layer's trade and information networks. The Spire is visible for miles, a needle of darkness against the red sky. Its interior is a labyrinth of markets, archives, and summoning chambers, where fiends, mortals, and undead come to trade. The Oinoloth, named Mydianchlarus, is a creature of immense greed and cunning, and he deals in secrets, souls, and the location of the Vault of Forgotten Suns. The Spire is protected by a legion of yugoloth mercenaries and a permanent anti-scrying ward.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Minethys",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Market of Dust": {
          "description": "The ground floor, a bustling bazaar of stalls and tents where anything can be bought or sold. Dust mephits flit through the crowd, and the air is filled with the clink of coins and the murmur of a dozen languages. Stalls sell weapons, potions, slaves, and information. A central auction block handles the sale of particularly valuable souls. The market is neutral ground, and violence is met with instant death by the Oinoloth's guards.",
          "paths": [
            "Mydianchlarus's Throne",
            "The Archive of Secrets"
          ]
        },
        "Mydianchlarus's Throne": {
          "description": "A private audience chamber at the Spire's midpoint, where the Oinoloth holds court. He is a gaunt figure in a grey robe, his face hidden behind a mask of polished bone. He speaks in a soft, measured voice, and his words carry the weight of eons. He trades in secrets, and his price is always a secret of equal value. The walls are lined with jars containing the trapped voices of those who broke their bargains.",
          "paths": [
            "The Market of Dust",
            "The Archive of Secrets"
          ]
        },
        "The Archive of Secrets": {
          "description": "A vast library of scrolls, crystals, and bound spirits, containing the accumulated knowledge of the yugoloths. The archive is patrolled by ethereal guardians, and only those with a token from Mydianchlarus may enter. A single, sealed section holds the location of the Vault of Forgotten Suns, but opening it requires a key forged from a dead star's core. The archivist, a blind arcanaloth named Quill, will trade information for rare tomes.",
          "paths": [
            "The Market of Dust",
            "Mydianchlarus's Throne"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Mydianchlarus (Oinoloth)",
        "Yugoloth mercenaries",
        "Quill (arcanaloth archivist)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Oinoloth is not the true ruler of the Spire; he is a puppet of the General of Gehenna, a yugoloth lord who pulls strings from the shadows. Mydianchlarus seeks to free himself by finding the Vault of Forgotten Suns, but he fears the General's wrath. A hidden faction of yugoloth dissidents, led by a nycaloth named Sorrow, seeks to overthrow the General and would ally with any who can provide the key."
    },
    "The Chained Peaks": {
      "name": "The Chained Peaks",
      "basicInfo": "The jagged mountain range of Colothys, where the most dangerous prisoners of Carceri are chained to the rocks. The peaks are so sharp they tear the clouds, and the wind howls through the ravines with a sound like screams. The prisoners, many of them former demons, devils, or traitorous celestials, are bound by chains of black iron that drain their strength. The demodand wardens patrol the paths, but they fear the prisoners' rage. At the highest peak, the 'Summit of Woe', the primordial Father of Mountains lies sleeping, his spine forming the range. A single, hidden path leads to the summit, but it is guarded by the 'Wind Howlers', creatures made of living wind and hatred.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Colothys",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Prisoner's Path": {
          "description": "A narrow, winding trail that ascends through the peaks, passing dozens of chained prisoners. Some hang limp, their spirits broken; others struggle against their bonds, their curses audible over the wind. The path is patrolled by kelubar demodands, but they keep a wary distance from the most dangerous prisoners. A small shrine to the Father of Mountains, built by the demodands, offers a place to rest—but the shrine is also a trap.",
          "paths": [
            "The Summit of Woe",
            "The Ravine of Screams"
          ]
        },
        "The Summit of Woe": {
          "description": "The highest peak, where the Father of Mountains' head would be. The summit is a flat platform of dark stone, covered in frost and ancient runes. A single, massive chain, the 'Chain of the World', rises from the center, disappearing into the sky. The runes are a binding spell that keeps the primordial asleep. Touching the chain triggers a vision of the primordial's dream—a world of endless mountains and grinding stone. The demodands have sealed the summit with a ward that incinerates any who approach without a specific talisman.",
          "paths": [
            "The Prisoner's Path"
          ]
        },
        "The Ravine of Screams": {
          "description": "A deep chasm that splits the mountain range, its walls lined with the bodies of those who tried to escape. The wind funnels through the ravine, amplifying the screams of the dying into a deafening roar. At the bottom, a river of black blood flows, carrying the remains of the fallen. A hidden ledge halfway down holds a cache of weapons and supplies left by a previous escape attempt. The demodands have set traps in the ravine, including falling boulders and razor wire.",
          "paths": [
            "The Prisoner's Path"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Demodand wardens",
        "Chained prisoners",
        "Wind Howlers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Chain of the World is not merely a binding; it is a tether that connects the Father of Mountains to the material plane. If it were severed, the primordial would rise, causing earthquakes that would shatter Colothys and release all prisoners. A single key, the 'Key of the Deep Earth', is hidden in the Ravine of Screams, but the demodands have never found it."
    },
    "The Prison of Black Ice": {
      "name": "The Prison of Black Ice",
      "basicInfo": "A fortress of black ice in Porphatys, where the most traitorous souls are kept frozen and aware. The fortress is carved from the ice itself, its walls translucent and blue-black. Inside, the corridors are silent and cold, and the prisoners are displayed in alcoves, their bodies encased in ice, their eyes moving in eternal torment. The fortress is ruled by the Night Hag Mother, a powerful night hag named Chill, who trades in frozen souls and ice magic. The only warmth in the fortress is the 'Heart of the Sea', a chamber of liquid warmth deep below, where Chill brews her potions. The demodand wardens are present but defer to Chill's authority.",
      "x": 1,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Porphatys",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Frozen Halls": {
          "description": "A long corridor lined with niches, each containing a frozen prisoner. The ice is clear, and the prisoners' faces are visible, their expressions frozen in various states of fear, rage, and sorrow. A faint, psychic whisper fills the air—the collective thoughts of the imprisoned. Some prisoners are still lucid and can communicate, offering information in exchange for a moment of warmth. The hall is patrolled by ice mephits and the occasional white dragon.",
          "paths": [
            "Chill's Throne Room",
            "The Heart of the Sea"
          ]
        },
        "Chill's Throne Room": {
          "description": "A large chamber at the fortress's center, where the Night Hag Mother Chill sits on a throne of frozen souls. She is a tall, gaunt night hag, her skin pale as frost and her eyes white. She trades in frozen souls, ice magic, and secrets, and her prices are always cold. A brazier of blue fire provides the only light, and the air is filled with the sound of her attendants—ice mephits and frost salamanders. A hidden trapdoor leads to her private vault.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Halls",
            "The Heart of the Sea"
          ]
        },
        "The Heart of the Sea": {
          "description": "A deep chamber where a pool of liquid warmth remains, the last remnant of Porphatys's original ocean. The water is steaming, and the walls are covered in exotic frost-flowers. Chill uses this chamber to brew potions of cold resistance and other elixirs. A small, hidden shrine to the forgotten sea goddess stands at the pool's edge, where a single candle burns. The warmth is addictive, and those who linger too long find themselves unable to leave.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Halls",
            "Chill's Throne Room"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Chill (Night Hag Mother)",
        "Ice mephits",
        "Frost salamanders",
        "Frozen prisoners"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Heart of the Sea is not merely a warm pool; it is a portal to the Elemental Plane of Water, sealed by the ice. If it were opened, the ocean would flood back into Porphatys, melting the layer and releasing all prisoners. Chill knows this and guards the portal with her life. She has a key, the 'Tear of the Sea', hidden in her vault, which can unseal the portal."
    },
    "The Demodand Court": {
      "name": "The Demodand Court",
      "basicInfo": "The ruling council of the demodand race, located in the absolute darkness of Agathys. The Court is not a building but a gathering of the oldest and most powerful demodands, who have transcended their physical forms and exist as frozen consciousnesses. They meet in a circular chamber of black ice, their thoughts communicating in a language older than the multiverse. Here, they decide the fate of all demodands and coordinate their efforts to maintain Carceri's prisons. The chamber is sealed by a barrier of pure cold that only the demodand lords can pass. The Court is also the guardian of the 'Gate of Absolute Nothing', the only theoretical exit from Agathys. The environment is so hostile that even the demodand lords suffer, their movements slow and their thoughts sluggish.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Agathys",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Frozen Council": {
          "description": "A circle of ten frozen thrones, each occupied by a demodand lord of immense age. Their bodies are translucent ice, and their eyes are pits of absolute cold. They do not speak aloud; their thoughts are projected directly into the minds of visitors. The Council is a place of judgment and planning, and any non-demodand who enters is judged. The floor is a mirror of black ice, reflecting not images but the innermost fears of the observer.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Absolute Nothing",
            "The Silent Corridor"
          ]
        },
        "The Gate of Absolute Nothing": {
          "description": "A shimmering, colorless void at the far end of the chamber, guarded by the eldest demodand, known as 'The Still One'. The Gate is not a physical door but a point where the cold and darkness become absolute. Passing through requires achieving perfect emptiness—no thought, no emotion, no soul. The demodands have not succeeded. A single, frozen figure, a monk who came closest, stands before the Gate, his face serene.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Council"
          ]
        },
        "The Silent Corridor": {
          "description": "A long, featureless passage of black ice that connects the Court to the rest of Agathys. The silence is absolute, and the cold is a physical weight. A single, faint light glows at the far end—the only source of illumination in the entire layer. The corridor is lined with the frozen forms of those who attempted to leave, their bodies preserved as warnings.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frozen Council"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Demodand Court",
        "The Still One (eldest demodand)",
        "The frozen monk (potential key)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The frozen monk before the Gate has not died; he is in a state of perfect suspended animation, having achieved a moment of pure emptiness but unable to hold it. If he were awakened and given a single reason to live, he might complete the transcendence and open the Gate. The demodands do not know this, and they view him as a failure. A hidden faction of demodands, who wish to escape Carceri, seek to awaken him but lack the warmth needed."
    },
    "The Dismal Marsh": {
      "name": "The Dismal Marsh",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, gray swamp in Oinos, where the Styx spreads into a tangle of black channels and oozing mud. The air is thick with the croaking of unseen things and the stench of rot. This is the primary arrival point for souls entering Hades, ferried here by merrenoloths along the Styx. The marsh is a place of transition and despair, where newly arrived souls are sorted, sold, or simply left to sink. A ramshackle dock of black wood, the 'Quay of the Damned', marks the main landing. The marsh is patrolled by yugoloth press gangs, night hags, and opportunistic scavengers.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Oinos",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Quay of the Damned": {
          "description": "A rickety pier of black timbers extending into the Styx. Merrenoloth ferrymen offload their passengers here, collecting payment in soul shells or memories. A skeletal clerk in a gray robe records the names of the newly arrived, then directs them to the sorting grounds. The wood groans underfoot, and the water laps with a sound like weeping.",
          "paths": [
            "The Sorting Grounds",
            "The Hag's Hut"
          ]
        },
        "The Sorting Grounds": {
          "description": "A flat expanse of packed mud where souls are gathered and assessed by a council of yugoloth overseers. Souls are weighed, judged, and assigned to various fates: some are sold to night hags, some are conscripted into the Blood War, and some are simply released into the marsh to fade. The air is filled with the low murmur of despair and the occasional scream of a soul being dragged away. A single, twisted gallows stands at the center, used for public examples.",
          "paths": [
            "The Quay of the Damned",
            "The Hag's Hut"
          ]
        },
        "The Hag's Hut": {
          "description": "A small cottage on stilts at the edge of the marsh, home to Auntie Bog, a night hag of considerable power. She trades in misery, buying the memories of the newly arrived and selling potions of forgetfulness. Her hut is filled with jars of preserved laughter, tears, and last words. She will offer guidance through the marsh for a price—usually a happy memory, which she considers a delicacy.",
          "paths": [
            "The Quay of the Damned",
            "The Sorting Grounds"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Yugoloth overseers",
        "Merrenoloth ferrymen",
        "Auntie Bog (night hag)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Auntie Bog is not merely a trader; she is a spy for the Keepers of the Balance. She records the names of all who pass through the Dismal Marsh and reports any soul that shows unusual resilience or power. Such souls are either recruited into the yugoloth ranks or eliminated before they can disrupt the Balance."
    },
    "Khin-Oin, the Wasting Tower": {
      "name": "Khin-Oin, the Wasting Tower",
      "basicInfo": "The fortress of the yugoloths, a massive tower of rusted iron and blackened bone rising from the gray fields of Oinos. Khin-Oin is the seat of the General of Gehenna, the commander of the yugoloth race, and the meeting place of the ultroloth council. The tower is a place of pure evil, where the yugoloths forge weapons, train their armies, and plot the endless continuation of the Blood War. The interior is a labyrinth of war rooms, slave pens, and torture chambers. The air is thick with the smell of oil, blood, and old paper. Mortals are admitted only as mercenaries or slaves, and even then, their lives are measured in hours. The tower is also the location of the 'Muster', where yugoloth legions are deployed to both sides of the conflict.",
      "x": 1,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Oinos",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Iron Gate": {
          "description": "The only entrance to Khin-Oin, a massive gate of rusted iron etched with runes of binding and torment. The gate is guarded by a pair of yugoloth behemoths and a chorus of howling mezzoloths. To pass, one must present a token of service or a writ of passage from a high-ranking yugoloth. The gate opens onto a long, sloping ramp that descends into the tower's depths.",
          "paths": [
            "The War Council Chamber",
            "The Slave Pens"
          ]
        },
        "The War Council Chamber": {
          "description": "A vast, circular room at the tower's heart, where the ultroloth council meets. The walls are covered in maps of the Blood War, annotated in a script that shifts and changes. At the center, a huge scrying pool shows the current state of the battlefields. The council, composed of nine ultroloths, sits on thrones of fused souls, their faces hidden behind masks of black glass. They speak in low, measured tones, and their words carry the weight of eons. Any outsider who enters is immediately struck by a sense of overwhelming insignificance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Iron Gate",
            "The Slave Pens"
          ]
        },
        "The Slave Pens": {
          "description": "A section of the tower where captured mortals and fiends are held until they are sold, conscripted, or used as raw material for yugoloth weapons. The cells are cold iron cages suspended over a pit of acid. The prisoners are fed sparingly and beaten regularly. A small resistance, the 'Chain Breakers', operates from a hidden alcove, led by a captured paladin named Sir Vael. They have managed to smuggle in a few weapons and plan an escape during the next deployment.",
          "paths": [
            "The Iron Gate",
            "The War Council Chamber"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The General of Gehenna",
        "Ultroloth council",
        "Yugoloth legions",
        "Chain Breakers resistance"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The General of Gehenna is not a single being; it is a title passed between the most powerful ultroloths. The current General has discovered that the Blood War is not eternal—it has a specific purpose: to grind down the fiends of both sides until only the yugoloths remain. The General plans to accelerate the war by deploying a new weapon, the 'Soul Bomb', but needs a fragment of a dead god to complete it."
    },
    "The Blood Field": {
      "name": "The Blood Field",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, flat battlefield in Oinos where the armies of the Abyss and Hell clash in endless, grinding combat. The ground is a churned morass of mud, blood, and the shattered remains of fiends. The sky is lit by the flares of magic and the fires of war engines. The air is thick with the screams of the dying and the clash of weapons. At the edges, yugoloth observers and scavengers pick through the aftermath, collecting souls and salvage. The field is a place of pure, meaningless destruction, where neither side can gain a permanent advantage. A few fortified outposts, built by yugoloth engineers, offer a precarious sanctuary for those caught in the crossfire.",
      "x": -1,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Oinos",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Front Line": {
          "description": "The actual point of contact between the tanar'ri and baatezu forces. Demons and devils tear into each other with fangs, claws, and weapons, their bodies piling up in writhing heaps. The ground is slick with ichor, and the air is filled with the howls of rage and pain. A constant rain of ash falls from the smoke-choked sky. No neutral creature can survive here for long; even the yugoloth observers keep their distance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Scavenger's Camp",
            "The Yugoloth Observation Post"
          ]
        },
        "The Scavenger's Camp": {
          "description": "A makeshift settlement at the edge of the battlefield, where yugoloth scavengers and night hags gather to strip the dead. The camp is a chaotic cluster of tents and lean-tos, filled with the smell of decay and the clink of salvaged weapons. A black market for captured souls operates openly. The camp boss, a canny mezzoloth named Grist, pays well for high-quality salvage and information.",
          "paths": [
            "The Front Line",
            "The Yugoloth Observation Post"
          ]
        },
        "The Yugoloth Observation Post": {
          "description": "A fortified tower of black stone that overlooks the battlefield, where yugoloth strategists monitor the flow of the Blood War. The tower is filled with scrying pools, maps, and communication crystals. The commanding officer, an ultroloth named Xar, is a master tactician who sells intelligence to both sides. The post is heavily guarded, and intruders are executed on sight.",
          "paths": [
            "The Front Line",
            "The Scavenger's Camp"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Tanar'ri hordes",
        "Baatezu legions",
        "Yugoloth observers",
        "Scavengers and night hags"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The yugoloth observation post contains a hidden device, the 'Balance Scale', which measures the exact strength of both sides. If one side ever gained a decisive advantage, the yugoloths would deploy their own forces to restore equilibrium. Xar has been bribing both sides to maintain a stalemate, but a rogue yugoloth commander is planning to tip the balance to provoke a final, cataclysmic battle."
    },
    "The Shores of Mists": {
      "name": "The Shores of Mists",
      "basicInfo": "The arrival point for souls entering Niflheim, a desolate shore of black sand and gray ice where the Styx fades into a sea of freezing fog. The mist is so thick that it clings to the skin and numbs the senses. Souls who arrive here are already half-forgotten, their memories fraying like old cloth. A single, twisted lighthouse, the 'Lantern of the Lost', casts a weak, gray beam into the mist, drawing the newly arrived to the processing grounds. The shore is patrolled by night hags and yugoloth collectors, who harvest the last remnants of identity from the desperate.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Niflheim",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Lantern of the Lost": {
          "description": "A tall, skeletal lighthouse of black stone, its lamp burning a pale, gray flame. The light barely penetrates the mist but is the only guide for miles. The keeper, a silent, hooded figure, tends the flame and records the names of the newly arrived in a book that never seems to fill. A small, cold room at the base offers shelter, but the silence is oppressive. The keeper will answer questions in a whisper, but each answer costs a memory.",
          "paths": [
            "The Processing Grounds",
            "The Frozen Bog"
          ]
        },
        "The Processing Grounds": {
          "description": "A flat area of packed ice where souls are sorted by night hags and yugoloth overseers. The process is quiet and efficient, with no screaming or pleading—the souls here are already too numb to resist. They are divided into groups: those who still have useful memories, those who will be sold as laborers, and those who are simply released to wander the mists. A single, gnarled tree of black ice stands at the center, its branches hung with the faded tokens of past lives.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lantern of the Lost",
            "The Frozen Bog"
          ]
        },
        "The Frozen Bog": {
          "description": "A treacherous expanse of half-frozen marsh, where the mist is thickest and the cold is most biting. Souls who wander here quickly lose their sense of direction and sink into the icy mud. Night hags prowl the bog, collecting the souls of the lost. A few hidden caves on higher ground offer shelter, but they are often occupied by the Frozen Choir, undead voices that sing a mournful dirge.",
          "paths": [
            "The Lantern of the Lost",
            "The Processing Grounds"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Night hags",
        "Yugoloth collectors",
        "The Keeper of the Lantern",
        "Frozen Choir"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Keeper of the Lantern is a former solar who was stripped of his light for disobedience. His punishment is to witness every soul that arrives and to record their names, but he is forbidden to help them. If someone were to offer him a single, genuine act of kindness, he would be freed, and the Lantern would blaze with the light of a thousand suns, dispersing the mist and revealing the path out of Niflheim."
    },
    "The Palace of Memory": {
      "name": "The Palace of Memory",
      "basicInfo": "A grand, decaying palace of white marble and black ice, located at the heart of Niflheim. It is the repository of forgotten memories, a place where the lost identities of Hades's victims are stored, catalogued, and sometimes traded. The palace is ruled by the Memory Keeper, a powerful ultroloth named Mnemosyne, who has spent millennia collecting the memories of the dead. The halls are lined with shelves of crystal vials, each containing a single memory, glowing faintly. The air is still and cold, and the only sound is the faint whispering of the stored memories. Souls who come here seeking to reclaim a lost memory must bargain with the Keeper, who demands a memory of equal value. The palace is also the site of the Vault of Perfect Memory, a chamber where the most precious memories are kept under heavy guard.",
      "x": 1,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Niflheim",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Hall of Vials": {
          "description": "A long, high-ceilinged hall filled with shelves that stretch into darkness. Each shelf holds thousands of crystal vials, their contents glowing with a faint, colored light. The memories within range from the mundane to the profound. A few curator yugoloths float along the shelves, cataloguing and reorganizing. Visitors can view a memory by holding a vial to their forehead, but the experience is brief and often disorienting.",
          "paths": [
            "The Memory Keeper's Study",
            "The Vault of Perfect Memory"
          ]
        },
        "The Memory Keeper's Study": {
          "description": "A small, cluttered room where Mnemosyne receives visitors. The walls are covered in charts and lists of memories acquired, traded, and lost. The Memory Keeper is a tall, gaunt ultroloth, its face hidden behind a mask of polished silver. It speaks in a voice like falling snow and demands a memory of equal or greater value for any it returns. A single, comfortable chair sits before its desk, but the chair is enchanted to extract a minor memory from anyone who sits in it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Vials",
            "The Vault of Perfect Memory"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of Perfect Memory": {
          "description": "A circular chamber at the palace's center, sealed by a door of solid black ice. Inside, a single pedestal holds a crystal sphere the size of a human heart, within which a pure, white light pulses. This is the perfect memory—a moment of selfless love that has never been forgotten. The vault is guarded by two ancient yugoloths and a permanent anti-magic field. The sphere is the most valuable object in Niflheim, and many have tried to steal it; none have succeeded.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Vials",
            "The Memory Keeper's Study"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Mnemosyne (Memory Keeper)",
        "Curator yugoloths",
        "Vault guards"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The perfect memory in the Vault is not a memory of the past; it is a memory of the future—the final moment of hope that will one day restore Hades. Mnemosyne knows this and keeps it hidden not out of greed, but because its release would undo the layer's purpose and possibly destroy the yugoloth race. However, the memory is beginning to fade, and the Keeper is desperate to find a new source of hope to replenish it."
    },
    "The Field of Silence": {
      "name": "The Field of Silence",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, featureless plain in Pluton, covered in a fine, gray dust that muffles all sound. The silence is absolute, and even the crunch of footsteps is swallowed. The field is dotted with the petrified corpses of dead gods, their forms half-buried in the dust. The air is cold and dry, and the only movement is the slow drift of ash from the decaying divine bodies. Souls who walk here lose their voices, then their memories, and finally their identities, becoming part of the silent landscape. A few ancient stones, carved with forgotten names, mark the graves of the dead gods. The field is also the hunting ground of the Silent Watchers, entities of pure stillness that absorb sound and light.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Pluton",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Petrified God": {
          "description": "A colossal, petrified corpse of a forgotten deity, its face frozen in a grimace of despair. The body is over a mile long, and its skin is cracked and grey. Climbing onto the corpse is possible, and from its chest, one can see the entire field. A small shrine at the god's feet, built by unknown hands, holds offerings of ash and silence. The god's heart, a massive crystal of black stone, still beats faintly, causing the ground to tremble.",
          "paths": [
            "The Silent Watchers",
            "The River Lethe"
          ]
        },
        "The Silent Watchers": {
          "description": "A cluster of statues, each depicting a robed figure with its hands over its mouth. They are not statues but the Silent Watchers, creatures that absorb all sound and light. They do not move unless provoked, but when they do, they strike with tendrils of absolute darkness. The Watchers guard the field, ensuring that no sound or memory escapes. A hidden alcove among them contains a small, warm light—a single, undying ember of the dead gods' collective dream.",
          "paths": [
            "The Petrified God",
            "The River Lethe"
          ]
        },
        "The River Lethe": {
          "description": "A slow, black river that flows through the Field of Silence. Its waters are perfectly still, and touching them erases a memory. A single, ancient ferryman, a skeletal figure in a grey cloak, offers passage across the river in exchange for a name—the passenger's own. The far bank is identical to the near bank, but those who cross are forever changed, their pasts left behind. The ferryman's boat is made of petrified wood and does not disturb the water.",
          "paths": [
            "The Petrified God",
            "The Silent Watchers"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Silent Watchers",
        "The Dead Gods (petrified)",
        "The Ferryman of Lethe"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The undying ember among the Silent Watchers is the last spark of divine fire from the dead gods. If it were taken to the Bone Castle and placed on the altar, it could be used to rekindle one of the dead gods, bringing them back to life. The yugoloths would do anything to prevent this, as the return of a god would disrupt the balance of Hades."
    },
    "The Bone Castle of Myrkul": {
      "name": "The Bone Castle of Myrkul",
      "basicInfo": "A fortress of fused bone and black stone in Pluton, once the seat of the dead god Myrkul, now controlled by the ancient yugoloths. The castle is a sprawling complex of towers, walls, and catacombs, all constructed from the remains of dead deities and forgotten heroes. It serves as the administrative center of Pluton and the primary stronghold of the yugoloth leadership. The throne room, known as the 'Throne of the Dead', is where the Lord of the Bone Castle (an ultroloth of immense power) receives visitors and dispenses judgment. The castle also houses the Altar of Unmaking, a black stone slab where the memories of the dead are ritually destroyed to keep the gods asleep. The air is cold and still, and the walls seem to whisper forgotten secrets.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Pluton",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Throne of the Dead": {
          "description": "A vast, echoing hall at the castle's center, where the Lord of the Bone Castle sits on a throne made from the skull of a dead god. The walls are lined with the petrified remains of past yugoloth lords, their eyes still glowing with faint malevolence. The Lord, an ultroloth named Azrael, speaks in a voice that sounds like the grinding of ancient bones. He dispenses justice, assigns tasks, and receives reports from across Pluton. The floor is a mosaic of bone and ash, depicting the fall of the dead gods.",
          "paths": [
            "The Altar of Unmaking",
            "The Catacombs of Memory"
          ]
        },
        "The Altar of Unmaking": {
          "description": "A black stone slab in a side chamber, inscribed with runes of erasure. Here, the memories of the dead are destroyed in ritual sacrifices, their essence fed to the dream of the dead gods to keep them asleep. The altar is attended by a group of silent, hooded yugoloths known as the 'Unmakers'. A single, white candle burns on the altar, but its flame is cold and gives no light. Destroying a memory on the altar is irreversible.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of the Dead",
            "The Catacombs of Memory"
          ]
        },
        "The Catacombs of Memory": {
          "description": "A labyrinth beneath the castle, filled with alcoves containing the stored memories of the most powerful souls. The memories are sealed in crystal vials, glowing faintly. Some are so potent that they leak visions into the corridors. The catacombs are guarded by bone golems and the spirits of dead heroes, who protect the memories of the fallen. A hidden chamber, the 'Vault of the First Dead God', contains the original memory of the first god to die, a memory so old that it predates the multiverse.",
          "paths": [
            "The Throne of the Dead",
            "The Altar of Unmaking"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Azrael (Lord of the Bone Castle)",
        "The Unmakers",
        "Bone golems",
        "Spirits of dead heroes"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Azrael is not the true ruler of the Bone Castle; he is a steward for the General of Gehenna, who considers Pluton a backwater but valuable for its resources. Azrael secretly seeks to free one of the dead gods, not out of mercy, but to use its power to overthrow the General and seize control of Hades. He has been searching for the undying ember from the Field of Silence."
    },
    "The Crawling City": {
      "name": "The Crawling City",
      "basicInfo": "A mobile fortress-city that inches its way up the slopes of Khalas, built on hundreds of iron legs and powered by the still-beating heart of a dead god. The city is a chaotic hive of yugoloth mercenaries, slave traders, and weapon smiths. Its streets are narrow, twisting alleys of black stone and rusted metal, filled with the clang of forges and the screams of the punished. At its center rises the Citadel of Contracts, where the ultroloth overlords hold court and enforce the infernal bargains that bind the city together. The city's movement is slow but relentless, crushing anything in its path. It is a place of opportunity and peril, where fortunes can be made in blood and lost in an instant.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Khalas",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Iron Streets": {
          "description": "The main thoroughfares of the Crawling City, perpetually crowded with yugoloths, slaves, and the occasional desperate mortal. The streets are paved with iron plates that shift as the city moves, causing the unwary to stumble. Stalls and shops line the way, selling weapons, poisons, and captured souls. A permanent haze of smoke and ash hangs in the air. At night, the streets are lit by flickering hellfire lamps.",
          "paths": [
            "The Citadel of Contracts",
            "The Slave Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Citadel of Contracts": {
          "description": "A massive fortress at the city's heart, where the ultroloth overlords rule. The citadel is a blocky structure of black stone and iron, its windows lit by red light. Inside, the halls are filled with clerks and scribes recording endless transactions. The main audience chamber, the Hall of Binding, is where contracts are signed in blood. The overlords sit on thrones of fused skulls, their eyes cold and calculating. Access is restricted, and intruders are executed on sight.",
          "paths": [
            "The Iron Streets",
            "The Slave Market"
          ]
        },
        "The Slave Market": {
          "description": "A grim plaza where slaves are bought and sold. The market is a cacophony of shouted bids, clanking chains, and the moans of the desperate. Slaves of all races are displayed on platforms, their worth estimated by yugoloth appraisers. A separate section handles the sale of souls, stored in black gemstones. The market is overseen by a cruel mezzoloth named Ghar, who takes a percentage of every sale and enforces order with a whip of fire.",
          "paths": [
            "The Iron Streets",
            "The Citadel of Contracts"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Ultroloth overlords",
        "Mezzoloth enforcers",
        "Slave traders",
        "Mercenary captains"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The dead god's heart that powers the city is not merely a source of energy; it is slowly regenerating. If given enough time, the god could reform and burst free, destroying the city and all within. The overlords know this and periodically drain the heart's power into soul gems to keep it weak. A hidden faction of mercenaries seeks to accelerate the heart's regeneration to escape servitude, unaware of the cataclysm it would cause."
    },
    "The Furnace of Perfection": {
      "name": "The Furnace of Perfection",
      "basicInfo": "A massive forge complex on the lower slopes of Khalas, where the yugoloths craft their weapons of war. The Furnace is a series of volcanic calderas and artificial smelters, glowing with intense heat. Here, the raw materials of Gehenna are transformed into swords, armor, and infernal war machines. The chief smith, a powerful nycaloth named Vorgath, is a master of metalworking and a cruel taskmaster. Slaves and captured enemies labor in the heat, many dying from exhaustion or accidents. The Furnace is also a place of innovation, where new weapons are tested on living subjects. The air is thick with the smell of molten metal and burning flesh.",
      "x": 7,
      "y": 5,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Khalas",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Smelting Floor": {
          "description": "A vast, open floor where rivers of molten metal flow into molds. The heat is intense, and the noise is deafening. Slaves push heavy carts of ore, while mezzoloth overseers crack whips. A central cauldron, the 'Mouth of Gehenna', continuously spews lava, which is channeled into the forges. The floor is littered with the remains of those who collapsed.",
          "paths": [
            "Vorgath's Throne",
            "The Testing Grounds"
          ]
        },
        "Vorgath's Throne": {
          "description": "A raised platform overlooking the smelting floor, where the nycaloth smith Vorgath sits on a throne of riveted iron. He is a massive, scarred fiend with four arms, each wielding a different tool of his trade. He barks orders and inspects the work, occasionally descending to beat a lagging slave. A hidden vault behind the throne contains his personal collection of masterwork weapons.",
          "paths": [
            "The Smelting Floor",
            "The Testing Grounds"
          ]
        },
        "The Testing Grounds": {
          "description": "A separate area where new weapons are tested on captured prisoners. The ground is strewn with broken bodies and discarded prototypes. A range of targets, both stationary and moving, are set up for archery and spell testing. Vorgath personally supervises the most important tests, evaluating the effectiveness of each weapon. A small cage holds a particularly tough prisoner, used repeatedly for durability tests.",
          "paths": [
            "The Smelting Floor",
            "Vorgath's Throne"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Vorgath (nycaloth master smith)",
        "Mezzoloth overseers",
        "Slave laborers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "Vorgath is not merely a smith; he is an inventor working on a secret project: the 'Soul Furnace', a device that can forge souls into weapons of pure evil. If he succeeds, the yugoloths will gain a weapon that could tip the Blood War in their favor. He needs a fragment of a dead god to complete the furnace, and he has sent agents to Pluton to obtain one."
    },
    "The Slag Pits": {
      "name": "The Slag Pits",
      "basicInfo": "A region of Chamada where the waste products of Gehenna's industry are dumped. The pits are a sprawling complex of acidic pools, burning slag heaps, and toxic fumes. Here, outcast yugoloths and other desperate fiends scavenge for usable materials, often fighting over scraps. The pits are also a place of punishment, where failed servants are thrown to die slowly. A few hidden warrens, dug into the slag, serve as hideouts for escaped slaves and renegades. The air is acrid, and the ground is unstable, with sinkholes that can swallow the unwary.",
      "x": 2,
      "y": -4,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Chamada",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Acid Lake": {
          "description": "A large, bubbling lake of green acid, fed by waste from the forges above. The surface steams and hisses, and the air is choking. A rickety bridge of iron spans the lake, but it is corroded and unsafe. Scavengers use poles to fish out metal scraps that have not fully dissolved. Occasionally, a body is thrown in by the yugoloth guards, dissolving in seconds.",
          "paths": [
            "The Scrap Warrens",
            "The Punishment Cells"
          ]
        },
        "The Scrap Warrens": {
          "description": "A maze of tunnels and caves dug into the slag, where the outcasts live. The warrens are dark, cramped, and filled with the smell of rust and decay. Fires of burning slag provide heat and light. The inhabitants trade scavenged goods and information, and they are fiercely protective of their territory. The leader, a one-armed mezzoloth named Skar, rules through a mix of brutality and pragmatism.",
          "paths": [
            "The Acid Lake",
            "The Punishment Cells"
          ]
        },
        "The Punishment Cells": {
          "description": "A block of iron cages set near the acid lake, where the yugoloths hold prisoners awaiting execution or disposal. The cells are overcrowded and filthy, and the prisoners are kept in chains. Some are used for experimentation by the arcanaloths of the Tower. A secret escape tunnel, dug by a previous group of slaves, leads from one cell to the Scrap Warrens, but it is collapsed and hidden.",
          "paths": [
            "The Acid Lake",
            "The Scrap Warrens"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Skar (outcast mezzoloth leader)",
        "Scavengers and outcasts",
        "Yugoloth guards"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Slag Pits are not merely waste; they contain rare, crystallized residues from the forging process. These crystals, known as 'Slag Diamonds', are highly valued by alchemists and spellcasters for creating acid-resistant items. Skar has secretly been stockpiling them, hoping to use them to buy his way back into the yugoloth hierarchy."
    },
    "The Tower of the Arcanaloth": {
      "name": "The Tower of the Arcanaloth",
      "basicInfo": "A towering spire of black glass and iron, located on a high ridge in Chamada. The Tower is the center of yugoloth magical research and the home of a powerful arcanaloth named Vharag (no relation to the demodand lord in Carceri; here it's a different individual). The interior is a labyrinth of laboratories, libraries, and summoning chambers, filled with the smell of ozone and old parchment. The arcanaloth Vharag is obsessed with unlocking the secrets of the Far Realm, and he has opened a small, unstable rift in the lowest basement. The Tower is guarded by magical wards, golems, and bound elementals. Only those with an invitation or a writ from an ultroloth may enter.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Chamada",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Observatory": {
          "description": "The topmost chamber, where Vharag studies the stars—or rather, the void beyond. A massive telescope of black crystal points into the darkness. The walls are covered in charts of planar alignments and notes written in shifting script. A permanent scrying pool allows him to observe other planes. The room is cold and silent, except for the hum of arcane instruments.",
          "paths": [
            "The Library of Forbidden Tomes",
            "The Far Realm Rift"
          ]
        },
        "The Library of Forbidden Tomes": {
          "description": "A vast library filled with books of dark magic, forbidden history, and the mad writings of previous arcanaloths. The shelves stretch into the shadows, and some books are chained to the walls. The head librarian, a bound daemon in the form of a floating eye, retrieves tomes on request. It will not speak, but it can answer questions by projecting images into the mind.",
          "paths": [
            "The Observatory",
            "The Far Realm Rift"
          ]
        },
        "The Far Realm Rift": {
          "description": "A small, shimmering tear in reality at the tower's base, leaking an eerie, shifting light. The rift is contained by a ring of silver and obsidian, but it pulses with malevolent energy. Vharag keeps it here to study, but even he is wary of its power. A single, silver key on a nearby pedestal can seal the rift, but Vharag does not know its significance and uses it only as a paperweight. The rift occasionally disgorges minor aberrations.",
          "paths": [
            "The Observatory",
            "The Library of Forbidden Tomes"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Vharag (arcanaloth master)",
        "Bound daemons and golems",
        "Yugoloth guards"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The silver key is the only thing that can permanently seal the Far Realm rift. It was left by a previous arcanaloth who foresaw the danger. Vharag is unaware of its true purpose, and if the rift widens, the entire Tower—and eventually Chamada—could be consumed by madness from beyond. A hidden faction of yugoloth traditionalists wants to steal the key and seal the rift, but they fear Vharag's power."
    },
    "The Frozen Caldera": {
      "name": "The Frozen Caldera",
      "basicInfo": "A fortress of black ice and volcanic rock built into the rim of Mungoth's main crater. The Caldera is the seat of the ultroloth Vhazhnar, who rules this layer with a cold, silent authority. The interior is a maze of ice corridors and chambers, heated by geothermal vents that create a constant mist. The fortress is home to a garrison of ice mephits, white dragonborn, and yugoloth soldiers. The central chamber, the Hall of Stasis, houses Vhazhnar's throne and the petrified god he keeps as a trophy. The air is cold and still, and the only sound is the distant rumble of the volcano beneath.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Mungoth",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Hall of Stasis": {
          "description": "A vast, circular chamber of black ice, lit by a cold blue light. At the center, on a throne of frozen bones, sits the ultroloth Vhazhnar, his form cloaked in frost. Behind him, suspended in a pillar of ice, is the petrified body of an ancient god, its eyes open and aware. The air is frigid, and a faint, psychic pressure pervades the room. Vhazhnar speaks rarely, but his voice echoes like cracking ice.",
          "paths": [
            "The Frost Warrens",
            "The Vent Shrine"
          ]
        },
        "The Frost Warrens": {
          "description": "A network of ice tunnels and chambers where the garrison lives. The walls are smooth and translucent, revealing the dark rock beneath. Ice mephits flit about, and white dragonborn stand guard. The barracks are cold and spartan, but the mess hall has a large hearth fueled by geothermal heat. A hidden armory contains weapons of froststeel, highly prized for their ability to bypass fire resistance.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Stasis",
            "The Vent Shrine"
          ]
        },
        "The Vent Shrine": {
          "description": "A small chamber built around a geothermal vent that releases a warm, silvery gas. The gas is the 'Preservation Mist', capable of putting living creatures into stasis. Vhazhnar uses it to store prisoners and treasures. A shrine to the dead god is carved into the wall, and a single, undying flame flickers on an altar. The gas is mildly addictive, and the garrison often sneaks in to inhale it.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Stasis",
            "The Frost Warrens"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Vhazhnar (ultroloth ruler)",
        "Ice mephits and dragonborn guards",
        "Prisoners in stasis"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The petrified god in the Hall of Stasis is not dead; it is in a state of suspended animation, maintained by the Preservation Mist. If the vent were blocked, the god would begin to awaken, and its first act would be to shatter the Frozen Caldera. Vhazhnar knows this and has placed a failsafe: a spike of black ice driven into the god's heart, which would kill it instantly if removed. A cult of the dead god's former worshipers, hidden among the garrison, seeks to remove the spike."
    },
    "The Chasm of Black Ice": {
      "name": "The Chasm of Black Ice",
      "basicInfo": "A deep, narrow gorge on Krangath's slopes, filled with black ice and utter darkness. The chasm descends into the mountain's heart, and the cold is so intense that even fire freezes. The walls are covered in ancient carvings depicting the history of the yugoloths and their creation from the first yugoloth. At the bottom lies a crystalline chamber, the 'Cradle of Evil', where the essence of the first yugoloth is contained. The chasm is the most sacred site for the yugoloths, though few dare to visit. It is guarded by the Silent Ones, lost souls who have been bound to eternal cold.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Krangath",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Descent": {
          "description": "A narrow, icy path that winds down into the chasm, with sheer drops on either side. The walls are smooth and reflective, showing distorted images of the climbers. The wind is still, but the cold is a physical weight. Carvings along the path tell the story of the first yugoloth and the birth of the race. A single, frozen sentinel—a yugoloth warrior—stands at the entrance, its eyes glowing faintly.",
          "paths": [
            "The Cradle of Evil",
            "The Silent Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Cradle of Evil": {
          "description": "A large, circular chamber at the chasm's bottom, lit by a dim, red glow from a crystal at its center. The crystal, the size of a human, pulses with malevolent energy and seems to whisper. This is the essence of the first yugoloth, the source of the race's fiendish nature. Touching it is fatal, but a fragment can be chipped off with great effort. The chamber is guarded by two ancient ultroloths, frozen in meditation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Descent",
            "The Silent Gallery"
          ]
        },
        "The Silent Gallery": {
          "description": "A side passage lined with the frozen bodies of the Silent Ones—souls bound to guard the chasm. They do not move or speak, but their eyes follow intruders. A few have writing carved into their flesh, warnings in a forgotten language. The gallery is pitch black, and no light, magical or otherwise, can penetrate it. Only sound can be used to navigate, but even that is swallowed by the cold.",
          "paths": [
            "The Descent",
            "The Cradle of Evil"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Silent Ones",
        "Ancient ultroloth guardians",
        "The First Yugoloth (crystal)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The crystal is not merely the source of the yugoloths' evil; it is a prison for a being even older and more dangerous—an entity of pure chaos that predates the gods. If the crystal is shattered, the entity will be freed, and the yugoloths will cease to exist as a race, their souls absorbed into the chaos. The ancient guardians are not protecting the crystal; they are protecting the multiverse from the entity within."
    },
    "Nishrek": {
      "name": "Nishrek",
      "basicInfo": "The fortress of Gruumsh, the one-eyed god of orcs, built on the largest iron cube in Avalas. Nishrek is a brutal, imposing structure of black iron and spiked battlements, designed for war and nothing else. The walls are covered in the skulls of defeated enemies, and the air is thick with the smell of blood and smoke. Inside, the halls are filled with the clamor of forging, the war chants of orcish legions, and the bellowing of Gruumsh himself, who sits on a throne of fused weapons. The fortress is the heart of the orcish war effort in Acheron, and from here, Gruumsh directs his endless war against Maglubiyet and the goblinoid hordes. Those who enter must prove their strength in the Blood Pits before they are allowed to speak with the god.",
      "x": -33,
      "y": -2,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Avalas",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Skull Gate": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Nishrek, a massive gate of black iron flanked by two towers topped with the skulls of giants. The gate is guarded by a pair of ogre sentinels and a squad of orcish archers. To pass, one must present a trophy of a slain enemy or submit to a test of strength: lifting a heavy iron bar. The ground before the gate is littered with the bones of those who failed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Blood Pits",
            "Gruumsh's Throne Room"
          ]
        },
        "The Blood Pits": {
          "description": "A series of gladiatorial arenas where orc warriors prove their worth. The pits are filled with blood-stained sand, and the walls are spattered with gore. Captured enemies, including goblins, hobgoblins, and even demons, are pitted against orcish champions. The crowd roars with bloodlust, and Gruumsh watches from a balcony, occasionally tossing a weapon to a favorite. Victory grants promotion; defeat means death or enslavement.",
          "paths": [
            "The Skull Gate",
            "The Forge of War"
          ]
        },
        "Gruumsh's Throne Room": {
          "description": "The inner sanctum of the fortress, a vast hall of black iron and torches. Gruumsh sits on a throne made from the melted armor of a hundred defeated gods, his single eye burning with rage. The walls are lined with the banners of orcish tribes, and the floor is covered in the skins of his enemies. Gruumsh speaks in a voice like thunder, demanding tribute and obedience. A permanent scrying pool shows the current state of the war.",
          "paths": [
            "The Skull Gate",
            "The Forge of War"
          ]
        },
        "The Forge of War": {
          "description": "A massive forge where orcish weapons and armor are crafted. The heat is intense, and the clang of hammers is deafening. Enslaved fire giants and captured duergar work the bellows, while orcish smiths hammer molten metal into blades. The forge produces weapons of exceptional quality, often imbued with the rage of Gruumsh. A hidden anvil, the 'Anvil of the One-Eye', can create a legendary weapon if fed with a fragment of a god's essence.",
          "paths": [
            "The Blood Pits",
            "Gruumsh's Throne Room"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Gruumsh (orcish god of war)",
        "Orcish legions",
        "Enslaved smiths",
        "Ogre sentinels"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Gruumsh is not merely a god; he is a prisoner of his own rage, bound to Acheron by the same pact that binds his war. His throne is not a seat of power but a cage, and he cannot leave Nishrek. The 'United Horde' has infiltrated the fortress, seeking to free him from his rage and unite the orcs and goblins. A secret chamber beneath the throne contains the original pact, and destroying it would free both Gruumsh and Maglubiyet."
    },
    "Clangor": {
      "name": "Clangor",
      "basicInfo": "The fortress of Maglubiyet, the god of goblins, hobgoblins, and bugbears, located on a neighboring iron cube. Clangor is a sprawling, chaotic structure of scavenged metal and crudely built towers, constantly being expanded and rebuilt. The air is filled with the shrieks of goblins, the barked orders of hobgoblin sergeants, and the low growls of bugbear warriors. Maglubiyet rules from a throne of stolen thrones, surrounded by his most loyal and cunning servants. The fortress is a place of brutal hierarchy, where strength and cunning are the only paths to power. Goblin slaves toil ceaselessly, while hobgoblins plot and bugbears enforce. Those who enter must navigate a maze of traps and rivalries.",
      "x": 32,
      "y": -2,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Avalas",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Rat's Nest": {
          "description": "The chaotic outer slums of Clangor, where goblins live in a warren of tunnels and shacks built from scrap. The air is thick with the smell of refuse and unwashed bodies. Goblins squabble, steal, and trade in stolen goods. A hidden temple to a forgotten goblin hero, the 'Shrine of the Clever Rat', offers a moment of quiet. To proceed deeper, one must either bribe a hobgoblin guard or prove their worth in a goblin brawl.",
          "paths": [
            "The War Halls",
            "Maglubiyet's Throne"
          ]
        },
        "The War Halls": {
          "description": "The military heart of Clangor, where hobgoblin legions drill and plan their endless war against Gruumsh. The halls are a series of large, echoing chambers with maps of the Grinding Plain. Hobgoblin officers bark orders, and bugbear enforcers maintain discipline. A war council, the 'Council of Blood', meets in a side chamber to discuss strategy. The air smells of oiled steel and sweat.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rat's Nest",
            "Maglubiyet's Throne"
          ]
        },
        "Maglubiyet's Throne": {
          "description": "The seat of the goblinoid god, a massive throne made from the conquered thrones of a hundred defeated kings. Maglubiyet, a tall, gaunt figure with a crown of iron, sits surrounded by his most loyal champions. He speaks in a cold, calculating voice, issuing commands and plots. The walls are hung with the banners of goblinoid tribes, and a great war drum beats constantly in the background. To approach, one must crawl on their belly and offer a sacrifice of a defeated enemy.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rat's Nest",
            "The War Halls"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Maglubiyet (goblinoid god of war)",
        "Hobgoblin legions",
        "Bugbear enforcers",
        "Goblin slaves"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "Maglubiyet's throne is not a place of power but a monument to his paranoia. He is convinced that his own followers will eventually betray him, and he has placed a curse on the throne that will kill any goblinoid who sits on it. The 'United Horde' has learned of this curse and seeks to use it to turn the goblins against their god. A hidden chamber beneath the throne contains a mirror that reflects the true nature of any who gaze into it."
    },
    "The Grinding Plain": {
      "name": "The Grinding Plain",
      "basicInfo": "A massive, flat region between Nishrek and Clangor where the armies of Gruumsh and Maglubiyet meet in endless battle. The ground is a churned mass of rusted metal, broken weapons, and the corpses of thousands. The air is filled with the clash of steel, the roar of war engines, and the screams of the dying. The plain is littered with war machines, siege towers, and the wreckage of past offensives. Souls consigned here are immediately pressed into service, given a weapon, and sent to the front lines. Only the strongest survive, and even they are eventually reforged after death to fight again. The plain is a place of pure, grinding war, where no side ever gains a permanent advantage.",
      "x": -1,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Avalas",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Front Line": {
          "description": "The active point of contact between the orcish and goblinoid armies. Orcs and goblins clash in brutal melee, while hobgoblins fire arrows and bugbears tear through the ranks. The ground is slick with blood, and the air is thick with the sounds of death. War machines rumble forward, their wheels crushing the fallen. There is no cover, no mercy, and no retreat. Any living creature that enters is swept into the carnage.",
          "paths": [
            "The Command Post",
            "The Scavenger's Edge"
          ]
        },
        "The Command Post": {
          "description": "A raised platform of iron and bone from which the generals of both sides direct the battle. Orcish warlords and hobgoblin strategists shout orders, their voices lost in the din. A magical map shows the shifting front lines, and messengers run to and fro. The post is heavily guarded, as assassination attempts are common. A neutral yugoloth observer offers intelligence to both sides for a price.",
          "paths": [
            "The Front Line",
            "The Scavenger's Edge"
          ]
        },
        "The Scavenger's Edge": {
          "description": "The edge of the battlefield, where scavengers and salvagers pick through the aftermath. Bladelings, mephits, and the occasional yugoloth search for usable weapons and components. A small, fortified camp offers a place to rest and trade. The scavengers are wary of both armies and often use the chaos to their advantage. A hidden tunnel leads to a neutral area where deserters can buy passage away from Avalas.",
          "paths": [
            "The Front Line",
            "The Command Post"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Orcish legions",
        "Goblinoid armies",
        "Scavenger guilds",
        "Yugoloth arms dealers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The war on the Grinding Plain is not just a physical battle; it is a spiritual one. Every death feeds the pact that binds Gruumsh and Maglubiyet, strengthening Acheron's hold on both gods. If the war were to stop for even a single day, the pact would weaken, and both gods would be freed to leave the plane. A secret society, the 'Peace of the Blade', works to orchestrate a ceasefire, but their efforts are constantly undermined by yugoloth agents."
    },
    "The Great Scrap Field": {
      "name": "The Great Scrap Field",
      "basicInfo": "A vast expanse of Thuldanin covered in mountains of broken weapons, shattered armor, and wrecked war machines. The field is a graveyard of forgotten wars, stretching as far as the eye can see under a dim, gray sky. The air is still and dry, and the only sound is the occasional creak of shifting metal. Scavengers, including bladelings and mephits, pick through the debris, searching for anything of value. Hidden among the junk are ancient relics of immense power, left behind by long-dead generals and forgotten empires. The field is also a haven for outcasts and fugitives, who build shelters from the scrap. It is a place of opportunity and peril, where a single find can change a scavenger's fortune—or end their life.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Thuldanin",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Rust Mounds": {
          "description": "A series of towering piles of rusted metal, each a small mountain of discarded weapons. The mounds are unstable and can collapse at any moment, burying the unwary. The air is filled with the scent of rust and oil. Scavengers dig tunnels into the mounds, searching for hidden caches. Some mounds contain the lairs of rust monsters and other metal-eating creatures. A few ancient, functional war golems lie dormant, waiting to be activated.",
          "paths": [
            "The Scavenger's Camp",
            "The Vault of the Broken Blade"
          ]
        },
        "The Scavenger's Camp": {
          "description": "A cluster of makeshift shelters built from salvaged metal and canvas, where the field's scavengers gather to trade and rest. The camp is ruled by a council of bladeling elders, who enforce a rough code of honor. A blacksmith here can repair or modify weapons, and a small market sells salvage and information. The camp is often raided by yugoloth slavers, so the inhabitants are always armed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rust Mounds",
            "The Vault of the Broken Blade"
          ]
        },
        "The Vault of the Broken Blade": {
          "description": "A hidden entrance deep within the largest rust mound, leading to a sealed chamber of black iron. The door is covered in warning runes, and the air inside is cold and still. The chamber contains the shattered pieces of a primordial war god's weapon, each piece radiating a faint, malevolent aura. The vault is guarded by ancient golems and the ghosts of the god's last servants. Removing any piece triggers a curse that will draw endless conflict to the thief.",
          "paths": [
            "The Rust Mounds",
            "The Scavenger's Camp"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Bladeling scavenger clans",
        "Rust monsters",
        "Yugoloth slavers",
        "Ghosts of the broken weapon"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The weapon in the Vault is not merely a god's sword; it is a fragment of the original 'Blade of War', the first weapon ever created, which was used to slay the first god of peace. The curse it carries is the reason for all war: those who touch it are fated to seek conflict until death. If the pieces were reassembled and destroyed, the curse would be lifted, and all wars across the multiverse would cease for a single, eternal moment."
    },
    "The Silent Citadel": {
      "name": "The Silent Citadel",
      "basicInfo": "A fortress of interlocking iron cubes floating in the void of Tintibulus, home to the Order of the Unspoken, a sect of warrior-monks who have taken vows of eternal silence. The Citadel is a place of absolute discipline and contemplation, where every movement is precise and every thought is focused on the art of war. The interior is a labyrinth of silent halls, training grounds, and meditation chambers. The monks communicate through a complex sign language and written notes. They study the ancient cubes that drift around the layer, deciphering the runes that hold lost strategic knowledge. The Citadel is neutral in Acheron's wars, and its monks serve as impartial tacticians and record-keepers. Entry is granted only to those who can prove their ability to maintain silence for three days.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Tintibulus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Silent Gate": {
          "description": "The entrance to the Citadel, a floating platform of black iron that leads to a single, featureless door. To open the door, one must stand in silence for one hour; any sound resets the timer. The door is guarded by two silent monks who observe newcomers with unblinking eyes. Beyond the gate lies a long corridor where absolute silence is enforced.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hall of Strategy",
            "The Meditation Cubes"
          ]
        },
        "The Hall of Strategy": {
          "description": "A vast chamber where the monks study maps of ancient battles and current conflicts across the planes. The walls are covered in diagrams and notated plans, all in a coded script. At the center, a large, rotating cube displays a three-dimensional map of the multiverse. The hall is filled with the quiet rustle of parchment as monks copy and annotate. Visitors may ask a single question, but the answer will be given in writing and will be cryptic.",
          "paths": [
            "The Silent Gate",
            "The Meditation Cubes"
          ]
        },
        "The Meditation Cubes": {
          "description": "A series of small, isolated chambers where monks meditate on the nature of war and silence. Each cube is a sealed room with no light or sound, forcing the occupant to confront their own thoughts. A few cubes are reserved for visitors who wish to test their mental fortitude. The experience can be enlightening or maddening. A single, larger cube is used for advanced training, where the monk must navigate a maze of shifting walls in complete darkness.",
          "paths": [
            "The Silent Gate",
            "The Hall of Strategy"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Order of the Unspoken",
        "Silent monks"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Order of the Unspoken is not merely a monastic order; it is the keeper of the 'Codex of the Last War', a book that records every battle ever fought and predicts the outcome of any future conflict. The Codex is kept in a hidden chamber beneath the Hall of Strategy, sealed by a spell that can only be broken by the voice of a true pacifist. The monks have been waiting for such a person for millennia."
    },
    "The Storm of Blades": {
      "name": "The Storm of Blades",
      "basicInfo": "The entire surface of Ocanthus is a frozen sea, but above it rages a perpetual storm of razor-sharp ice shards that fly through the air at lethal speed. The storm is the defining feature of the layer, a howling maelstrom of black ice and frozen wind that strips flesh from bone and shatters unprotected armor. No creature can survive exposure for more than a few seconds, and even the hardiest ice fiends use enchanted shelters. The storm is not random; it has a pattern, a rhythm like a heartbeat, and those who listen can navigate its lulls. At the storm's center, the winds are calmest, and there, a single, dark island of fused ice serves as a refuge. The Citadel of the Ice Lords is built on this island, a fortress that has withstood the storm for eons. The storm is also the source of the layer's ice shards, which are harvested by bladeling frost clans for use as weapons.",
      "x": -1,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Ocanthus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Edge of the Storm": {
          "description": "The boundary where the ice sea meets the storm. Here, the wind begins to pick up, and stray shards slice through the air. A few hardy shelters, built from black ice, offer a place to rest before venturing deeper. Bladeling scouts monitor the storm's patterns, marking safe windows for travel. The ground is covered in shattered ice and the remains of those who miscalculated.",
          "paths": [
            "The Calm Eye",
            "The Ice Fortress"
          ]
        },
        "The Calm Eye": {
          "description": "The center of the storm, a circular area of almost still air where the wind drops to a gentle breeze. The ice here is smooth and clear, reflecting the darkness above. A single, black stone obelisk stands at the exact center, carved with warnings in a dead language. The Calm Eye is the only safe place to cross the storm, but reaching it requires precise timing and luck. A hidden cave beneath the obelisk holds the frozen heart of the dead winter god, guarded by ice fiends.",
          "paths": [
            "The Edge of the Storm",
            "The Ice Fortress"
          ]
        },
        "The Ice Fortress": {
          "description": "The Citadel of the Ice Lords, a fortress of fused black ice built at the Calm Eye's edge. It is the only permanent structure on Ocanthus, and its walls are thick enough to withstand the storm. The Ice Lords, ancient ice fiends of immense power, rule from within. They demand tribute from all who seek shelter and are quick to punish disobedience. The fortress contains a great hall, a prison block, and a forge where ice shards are crafted into weapons. A permanent anti-magic field prevents teleportation.",
          "paths": [
            "The Edge of the Storm",
            "The Calm Eye"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Ice Lords",
        "Ice fiends",
        "Bladeling frost clans"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The frozen heart in the cave beneath the obelisk is the source of the storm. If it were melted, the storm would cease, and Ocanthus would become a calm, frozen sea. The Ice Lords know this but keep the heart hidden to preserve their power. A prophecy states that the heart will be melted by the tears of a true pacifist, an impossible condition in Acheron. However, a single tear from the Silent Citadel's awaited pacifist could fulfill it."
    },
    "Regulus": {
      "name": "Regulus",
      "basicInfo": "The capital city of Mechanus, a sprawling metropolis of perfectly ordered towers, bridges, and plazas built atop a colossal gear. Every building is a precise geometric shape, and the streets are laid out in a flawless grid. The city is the seat of Primus, who rules from the Grand Modron Cathedral at its center. Regulus is home to millions of modrons of all ranks, each performing their designated functions with tireless efficiency. Visitors are rare and must submit to a rigorous audit of their purpose and intentions before being allowed entry. The city is a marvel of arcane engineering, where magical energy flows through conduits like blood through veins. Time here is regulated by massive clockwork mechanisms that keep perfect synchronization with the multiverse. The air is clean and cool, and the only sounds are the rhythmic ticking of gears and the soft chimes of order.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 8,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "Mechanus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Gate of Law": {
          "description": "The main entrance to Regulus, a massive archway of polished brass and steel, flanked by two colossal modron statues. To enter, one must present a 'permit of purpose', a crystalline token issued by the modron hierarchy that states the visitor's intent. Those without a permit are assigned a monodrone escort and directed to the visitor's processing center. The gate is monitored by a squad of pentadrones, whose five eyes examine every movement.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Modron Cathedral",
            "The Avenue of Gears",
            "The Visitor's Processing Center"
          ]
        },
        "The Grand Modron Cathedral": {
          "description": "The seat of Primus and the heart of Regulus. A towering structure of white marble and gold, its dome shaped like a perfect sphere. Inside, the walls are covered in mathematical proofs and the history of the modron race. Primus resides in a central chamber, appearing as a radiant humanoid of light and precision, surrounded by a council of secundus modrons. The Cathedral is also the primary factory for creating new modrons, with assembly lines that shape raw elemental matter into the lower ranks. Visitors may be granted an audience with Primus, but only after passing a series of logical tests.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Law",
            "The Avenue of Gears",
            "The Forge of Primus"
          ]
        },
        "The Avenue of Gears": {
          "description": "A broad boulevard that runs through the center of Regulus, paved with interlocking gear teeth that rotate slowly beneath one's feet. The avenue is lined with modron workshops, administrative offices, and barracks. Modrons of all ranks march in ordered columns, performing their duties. The street is lit by glowing orbs of pure law, which cast no shadows. A single, large clock at the avenue's end displays the exact time in every plane simultaneously.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Law",
            "The Grand Modron Cathedral",
            "The Visitor's Processing Center"
          ]
        },
        "The Visitor's Processing Center": {
          "description": "A building near the Gate of Law where non-modrons are registered and assigned a function. The interior is a series of identical rooms with desks staffed by quadrone clerks. Visitors must fill out forms in triplicate, answer a battery of questions about their intentions, and submit a sample of their essence for cataloging. Failure to comply results in immediate expulsion or, in extreme cases, transformation into a monodrone. The center is efficient but unnervingly impersonal.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gate of Law",
            "The Avenue of Gears"
          ]
        },
        "The Forge of Primus": {
          "description": "A massive foundry adjacent to the Cathedral, where the physical forms of modrons are forged. The heat is intense, and the air is filled with the clang of hammers and the hiss of steam. Primus's chief engineer, a decaton modron named Gearmaster Klik, oversees the process. The Forge also creates the Inevitables, constructs of pure law designed to hunt down oathbreakers and cosmic violators. A hidden chamber contains the 'Blueprint of the Perfect Modron', a design that, if realized, would create a modron capable of independent thought—a direct threat to the established order.",
          "paths": [
            "The Grand Modron Cathedral",
            "The Avenue of Gears"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Primus (the One and the Prime)",
        "The Modron Hierarchy",
        "Gearmaster Klik (decaton)",
        "The Inevitables"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Blueprint of the Perfect Modron is not a theoretical design; it was once used to create the Aberrant, who escaped and now hides in the Great Gears. The current Primus knows this and has sealed the blueprint to prevent further aberrations. However, a faction of decaton modrons, believing that the current order is flawed, secretly seeks to recover the blueprint and complete the Perfect Modron, which would trigger a civil war in Mechanus."
    },
    "The Modron Cathedral": {
      "name": "The Modron Cathedral",
      "basicInfo": "The primary creation facility for the modron race, a vast complex of assembly lines, programming chambers, and quality control stations. Located near Regulus, it is the source of all modrons, from the humble monodrone to the mighty secundi. The Cathedral is a marvel of arcane engineering, where raw elemental matter is shaped into perfect geometric forms and imbued with the spark of law. The process is entirely automated, overseen by a council of octon modrons who ensure that every modron meets the exact specifications. The air is filled with the hum of magic and the soft ticking of thousands of small mechanisms. Access is strictly limited to those with the highest security clearance, as the Cathedral represents the very heart of modron society.",
      "x": 8,
      "y": 4,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Mechanus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Assembly Lines": {
          "description": "A series of moving belts that carry raw materials through a sequence of automated shaping, molding, and enchanting stations. Each station is a precise machine that performs a specific function: cutting, polishing, inscribing runes, and infusing the spark of law. The process is mesmerizingly efficient, with hundreds of modrons created every hour. Any deviation from the standard is immediately corrected or discarded. A viewing platform allows authorized observers to witness the process.",
          "paths": [
            "The Programming Chamber",
            "The Quality Control Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Programming Chamber": {
          "description": "A central room where the created modrons are imprinted with their designated functions and the laws of the hierarchy. A series of crystal panels displays the program code in a language of pure mathematics. The programming is performed by a council of octon modrons, who sit in a circle around a central pedestal. A single, floating crystal contains the 'Base Directive', the fundamental law that governs all modrons: obey Primus and maintain order. The chamber is warded against any disruption, and the slightest error in programming could create an Aberrant.",
          "paths": [
            "The Assembly Lines",
            "The Quality Control Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Quality Control Hall": {
          "description": "A large, bright room where newly created modrons are inspected for defects. Each modron is placed on a rotating platform and scanned by a beam of white light. Modrons that meet the standard are stamped with a unique identifier and sent to their assigned posts. Those that fail are melted down and recycled. The hall is overseen by a group of pentadrone inspectors, who are exceptionally thorough. A hidden shelf contains the records of past failed modrons, including several that displayed signs of independent thought before being destroyed.",
          "paths": [
            "The Assembly Lines",
            "The Programming Chamber"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Octon Council",
        "Pentadrone inspectors",
        "Modron assembly workers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Base Directive is not just a program; it is a fragment of the original law that created the multiverse. If it were altered, all modrons would be freed from their obedience, plunging Mechanus into chaos. The Aberrant, hiding in the Great Gears, has been attempting to access the Programming Chamber to rewrite the Base Directive. It has already corrupted several pentadrone inspectors, who will aid it when the time comes."
    },
    "The Great Gears": {
      "name": "The Great Gears",
      "basicInfo": "The vast, interlocking mechanism that forms the landscape of Mechanus. Endless cogs, pistons, and flywheels turn in perfect synchrony, creating a terrain that is both solid and constantly moving. The gears range in size from small enough to fit in a hand to massive enough to hold entire cities. The surface is smooth and metallic, with no natural vegetation or water. Travel across the gears requires careful timing, as gaps open and close with the rotation. The area is patrolled by modron maintenance crews, who oil the joints and repair any damage. Hidden among the gears are the lairs of the plane's few non-modron inhabitants, including the formian hives and the yugoloth observers. The Great Gears are also where the Aberrant hides, using the moving surfaces to evade capture.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -1,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Mechanus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Maintenance Shafts": {
          "description": "A network of narrow tunnels and service passages that run through the interiors of the larger gears. These shafts are used by modron maintenance crews to access the internal mechanisms. The walls are lined with pipes, wires, and spinning shafts, and the air is filled with the smell of oil and metal. Navigating the shafts requires constant attention, as moving parts can easily crush the unwary. A few hidden alcoves offer temporary shelter, and some have been claimed by the Aberrant as hideouts.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gear Surface",
            "The Formian Hive"
          ]
        },
        "The Gear Surface": {
          "description": "The exposed outer surfaces of the gears, where travelers must walk. The ground moves slowly, and gaps between gears open and close. Railing-lined walkways have been installed by the modrons for safe passage, but many areas are unmaintained. The surface is dotted with small maintenance stations and the entrances to the shafts. The sky above is a grid of darkness and light, and the only sound is the constant grinding of metal.",
          "paths": [
            "The Maintenance Shafts",
            "The Formian Hive",
            "The Aberrant's Hideout"
          ]
        },
        "The Formian Hive": {
          "description": "A massive, organic-looking structure attached to the underside of a large gear, home to a colony of formians, ant-like beings of law. The hive is a maze of tunnels, chambers, and breeding pens, all built from a waxy substance that hardens to stone. The formians are allies of the modrons, but they have their own queen and priorities. They trade in raw materials and information. Their queen, the Formian Queen, is a massive, bloated creature who communicates through pheromones and telepathy. The hive is a place of strict hierarchy, with each formian knowing its role.",
          "paths": [
            "The Maintenance Shafts",
            "The Gear Surface"
          ]
        },
        "The Aberrant's Hideout": {
          "description": "A hidden chamber deep within the largest gear, accessible only through a series of false walls and secret passages. Here, the Aberrant modron has made its lair. The chamber is a jumble of scavenged parts, stolen tools, and the remnants of failed experiments. The Aberrant itself is a monodrone with a single, glowing eye that shows signs of true intelligence. It has surrounded itself with a small group of other awakened modrons, and they plot the overthrow of Primus. The hideout is protected by traps and alarms.",
          "paths": [
            "The Gear Surface"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Modron maintenance crews",
        "Formian colony",
        "The Aberrant and its followers"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "4",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Aberrant is not the only awakened modron; it has been secretly creating others, using a hidden programming flaw. These awakened modrons are scattered across Mechanus, waiting for a signal. The Aberrant's ultimate goal is to reach the Programming Chamber and alter the Base Directive, freeing all modrons from their programmed obedience. It believes that true order can only be achieved through free will, a philosophy that is anathema to Primus."
    },
    "The Inevitable Tribunal": {
      "name": "The Inevitable Tribunal",
      "basicInfo": "The seat of the Inevitables, the constructs of pure law who enforce cosmic contracts and punish oathbreakers. The Tribunal is a massive, black stone structure built into a floating gear, its architecture reminiscent of a courthouse. Here, the Inevitables are created in the Forge of Law, and from here they are dispatched across the multiverse to hunt down those who violate the fundamental laws of reality. The Tribunal is presided over by the Grand Inevitable, a marut of immense power, who oversees the adjudication of disputes between powers and the assignment of tasks. The interior is filled with courtrooms, archive halls, and the cells where captured offenders await judgment. The air is cold and still, and the only sound is the slow ticking of the cosmic clock.",
      "x": -7,
      "y": 4,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Mechanus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Court of Cosmic Law": {
          "description": "A vast, circular courtroom where the Inevitables hear cases involving breaches of universal law. The judge is a kolyarut, a construct with a face of shifting runes, who weighs arguments with mathematical precision. The walls are inscribed with the laws of the multiverse, which update themselves as new contracts are signed. Visitors may observe proceedings but must remain silent. A single, empty chair at the center is reserved for the accused, who is brought in chains of force.",
          "paths": [
            "The Forge of Law",
            "The Archive of Oaths"
          ]
        },
        "The Forge of Law": {
          "description": "A chamber where new Inevitables are constructed, using raw materials harvested from the plane's gears and the essence of law itself. The forge is a massive, glowing furnace of white fire, tended by a team of modron engineers and a single marut overseer. The process is slow and meticulous, and each Inevitable is unique, designed for a specific type of enforcement. A hidden vault contains the 'Prime Mandate', the original instruction that created the Inevitables, which grants them their authority. It is guarded by a pair of zelekhut.",
          "paths": [
            "The Court of Cosmic Law",
            "The Archive of Oaths"
          ]
        },
        "The Archive of Oaths": {
          "description": "A library of contracts, oaths, and cosmic laws, stored on crystal tablets that glow with faint light. The archive is maintained by a group of arcane modrons, who catalog and retrieve records. The shelves stretch endlessly into the distance. A single, sealed section contains the 'Forbidden Oaths'—contracts so dangerous that their very existence is a threat to the multiverse. Access requires the approval of the Grand Inevitable. A few rogue inevitables have been known to steal from the archive.",
          "paths": [
            "The Court of Cosmic Law",
            "The Forge of Law"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Grand Inevitable (marut)",
        "Kolyarut judges",
        "Zelekhut guards",
        "Modron archivists"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Prime Mandate is not just a document; it is a direct order from the original power that created the multiverse, granting the Inevitables their right to enforce law. If it were destroyed, the Inevitables would lose their authority, and chaos would spread across the planes. A rogue kolyarut, known as 'the Dissenter', believes that the laws are too rigid and seeks to destroy the Prime Mandate, but it has been hunted for centuries. It may be hiding in the Archive of Oaths."
    },
    "The Golden Village": {
      "name": "The Golden Village",
      "basicInfo": "A quintessential farming village in Abellio, home to about two hundred souls who tend the surrounding fields. The village is a cluster of stone cottages with thatched roofs, arranged around a central green with a large oak tree. A stone well provides fresh water, and a small shrine to Cyrrollalee, goddess of hearth and home, stands at the village's heart. The souls here are content, spending their days in honest labor and their evenings in communal meals and song. The village is a model of cooperation and generosity, where no one goes hungry and all disputes are settled amicably. Visitors are welcomed warmly and offered bread and cider. The village is not a place of adventure, but of rest and simple joys.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Abellio",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Village Green": {
          "description": "A large, grassy area at the center of the village, dominated by a massive, ancient oak tree. The tree's branches provide shade for benches and a small stage used for community gatherings. A maypole stands ready for festivals, and children (the souls of those who died young) play quietly. The green is where the village meets to discuss matters of common interest. A small notice board lists requests for help with harvests or repairs.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shrine of Cyrrollalee",
            "The Well of Plenty",
            "The Community Hall"
          ]
        },
        "The Shrine of Cyrrollalee": {
          "description": "A modest stone shrine dedicated to the goddess of hearth and home, built in the style of a small cottage with a warm, ever-burning fire inside. The shrine's caretaker, a gentle old soul named Mother Anya, offers blessings and counsel. A simple altar holds offerings of bread, flowers, and handmade crafts. The air inside is warm and smells of baking bread. A hidden alcove contains the 'Hearthstone of the Village', a smooth stone that radiates a sense of safety and belonging.",
          "paths": [
            "The Village Green",
            "The Well of Plenty"
          ]
        },
        "The Well of Plenty": {
          "description": "A circular stone well at the edge of the green, its water crystal clear and always cool. The well never runs dry, and its water is said to strengthen the bonds of community. A small cup rests on the rim for travelers. At night, the well glows faintly, reflecting the golden light of the layer. The water also has the property of revealing the true nature of any liquid poured into it, making it useful for detecting poison.",
          "paths": [
            "The Village Green",
            "The Shrine of Cyrrollalee"
          ]
        },
        "The Community Hall": {
          "description": "A large, timber-framed building where the village holds feasts, dances, and meetings. The interior is warm and welcoming, with long tables, a great fireplace, and shelves of well-used cookware. The walls are decorated with paintings of past harvests and portraits of respected elders. A kitchen at the back produces an endless supply of simple, hearty food. The hall is open to all, and visitors are encouraged to share stories of their travels.",
          "paths": [
            "The Village Green"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Village Council",
        "Mother Anya (shrine caretaker)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "1",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Hearthstone in the Shrine of Cyrrollalee is not merely a symbol; it is a fragment of the goddess's own hearth, a gift to the village for its unwavering kindness. If the village were ever threatened, the Hearthstone could summon a protective aura that would repel any evil. The villagers do not know this, as the knowledge would make them proud, and pride would taint the stone's power."
    },
    "The Orchard of Serenity": {
      "name": "The Orchard of Serenity",
      "basicInfo": "A vast orchard of fruit trees in Abellio, where the souls of gardeners and orchard keepers tend to apples, pears, and other fruits that never rot. The trees are arranged in perfect rows, and the grass between them is trimmed by celestial sheep. The air is sweet with the scent of blossoms and ripe fruit. A small, open-air pavilion at the orchard's center offers shade and a place to rest. The orchard is a place of peaceful labor and quiet contemplation, where one can hear the hum of bees and the rustle of leaves. It is also a source of the 'Golden Apples of Arcadia', which are said to grant a day of perfect health when eaten. The keeper, an old soul named Tomas, is a gentle man who shares his knowledge of grafting and pruning with any who ask.",
      "x": 4,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Abellio",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Long Rows": {
          "description": "The main body of the orchard, with trees stretching in precise lines to the horizon. The fruit hangs heavy, and the ground is soft with fallen leaves. A few wooden ladders are set against the trunks for harvesting. The light filters through the branches in a dappled pattern. It is a place where one can walk for hours and always find a new variety of fruit. The trees whisper gently in the breeze, as if sharing secrets.",
          "paths": [
            "The Pavilion of Rest",
            "The Wellspring of Blossoms"
          ]
        },
        "The Pavilion of Rest": {
          "description": "A simple, open-sided structure of white marble and wooden beams, with benches and a small table. A pitcher of fresh cider is always present, kept cool by a minor enchantment. Here, visitors can sit and enjoy the view of the orchard. Tomas often rests here between tasks, and he will offer a cup of cider and a friendly conversation. A guest book contains the names and comments of previous visitors, many expressing gratitude for the peace they found.",
          "paths": [
            "The Long Rows",
            "The Wellspring of Blossoms"
          ]
        },
        "The Wellspring of Blossoms": {
          "description": "A small, bubbling spring at the orchard's edge, surrounded by wildflowers. The water is sweet and cool, and it is said to be the source of the orchard's fertility. A single, perfect blossom floats on the surface at all times. Drinking from the spring is said to renew one's appreciation for simple pleasures. A hidden cache beneath a nearby stone contains a selection of rare seeds from across the planes, left by a planar traveler as a gift to the orchard.",
          "paths": [
            "The Long Rows",
            "The Pavilion of Rest"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Tomas (orchard keeper)",
        "Souls of gardeners"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "1",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Golden Apples of Arcadia, which grow on a single tree at the very center of the orchard (not obviously marked), are not merely fruit; they are the solidified essence of a peaceful day. Eating one grants the consumer a full day of perfect health and mental clarity, and the effects can be extended by sharing the apple with another. The apples are guarded by the celestial bees, who will swarm any who try to take more than one."
    },
    "The Grove of Order": {
      "name": "The Grove of Order",
      "basicInfo": "A section of Buxenus where the forest is at its most orderly: trees grow in exact geometric patterns, streams flow in straight lines, and the animals move with predictable grace. The Grove is a place of intense peace and discipline, where the souls of rangers and foresters practice their stewardship. A large, circular clearing at the center serves as a meeting place for the Keepers of the Wild. At the heart stands the Great Tree of Order, a colossal oak that is the layer's axis. The Grove is a place of quiet power, where one can feel the pulse of the layer's natural laws. Visitors are welcomed but must respect the rules: no harm to any creature, no disruption of the patterns, and no magic that would unbalance the ecosystem.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Buxenus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Great Tree": {
          "description": "A massive oak tree at the center of the Grove, its trunk wide enough to hollow out a small room. The tree's roots spread through the entire layer, anchoring the natural laws. Its leaves are inscribed with tiny runes representing every species. A constant, faint hum of life surrounds it. A circular bench around the trunk offers a place to sit and meditate. The tree's bark is warm to the touch, and its shade is profoundly calming.",
          "paths": [
            "The Council Ring",
            "The Stream of Clarity"
          ]
        },
        "The Council Ring": {
          "description": "A circle of polished stones set into the ground around the Great Tree, where the Keepers of the Wild hold their meetings. Each stone bears a symbol of an animal, and during councils, the spirits of those animals manifest as spectral guides. The ring is a place of decision and balance. A small fire pit at the center is used for ceremonial purposes. Visitors may observe but not speak unless invited.",
          "paths": [
            "The Great Tree",
            "The Stream of Clarity"
          ]
        },
        "The Stream of Clarity": {
          "description": "A perfectly straight stream that flows from the base of the Great Tree, its water so clear it is nearly invisible. The stream is a boundary marker and a source of pure, life-giving water. Animals drink from it in an orderly fashion, each species at its appointed time. A small stone bridge crosses the stream, and from its center, one can see the reflection of the entire Grove in perfect detail. Drinking from the stream grants a temporary insight into the interconnectedness of nature.",
          "paths": [
            "The Great Tree",
            "The Council Ring"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Keepers of the Wild",
        "Treant guardians",
        "Animal spirits"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The withered branch on the Great Tree is the last remnant of the corruption that would later consume Nemausus. It is kept as a reminder of the consequences of overreach. If someone were to prune the branch with a blade of pure silver and burn it in the Council Ring's fire, the corruption in Nemausus would weaken significantly, giving the Resistance a chance to reclaim their layer. The Keepers know this but fear the consequences of interfering with the natural order."
    },
    "The Thorn Maze": {
      "name": "The Thorn Maze",
      "basicInfo": "A vast, living labyrinth of thorny vines, toxic mushrooms, and twisted trees that has overrun much of Nemausus. The Maze is not a constructed structure but the result of the layer's corruption, a chaotic tangle that shifts and grows as if seeking to trap the unwary. The air is thick with spores that cause hallucinations and weakness, and the thorns are coated with a necrotic poison. Within the Maze, the corrupted souls wander, their forms twisted into thorny husks. The Resistance uses hidden paths through the Maze, but even they can become lost. At the center of the Maze lies a glade of relative calm, where the Seed of Original Order is hidden, protected by the last uncorrupted archons. The Maze is a place of despair and danger, but also of potential redemption, for those who can navigate its depths with pure intent.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "Nemausus",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Edge of Corruption": {
          "description": "The boundary between the Maze and the remaining pockets of uncorrupted land. The vegetation here is sickly and twisted, and the air has a faint, acrid taste. A few desperate souls maintain a small camp, hoping to push back the corruption. The camp is led by a former Harmonium member who has renounced his fanaticism and now fights to restore the layer. The camp offers a place to rest and plan before entering the Maze.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shifting Paths",
            "The Hidden Glade"
          ]
        },
        "The Shifting Paths": {
          "description": "The interior of the Maze, a labyrinth of thorny walls that move and reconfigure. The paths are narrow and uneven, and the thorns reach out to snag clothing and flesh. The air is thick with spores, and a constant, low moaning fills the air—the collective despair of the corrupted. Hidden among the thorns are small clearings where one can catch a breath, but these are often guarded by blighted treants. A few Resistance markers, small stones with a rune of hope, can guide the way if one knows where to look.",
          "paths": [
            "The Edge of Corruption",
            "The Hidden Glade"
          ]
        },
        "The Hidden Glade": {
          "description": "A small, circular clearing at the center of the Maze, miraculously free of corruption. The grass is green, the light is soft, and a single, perfect seed rests on a pedestal of white stone—the Seed of Original Order. The glade is guarded by the last three uncorrupted archons, who are weary but resolute. They will allow entry only to those who can answer a riddle about the nature of order: 'What is more fragile than a law, but more enduring than a mountain?' The answer is 'trust'. The Seed can be taken, but doing so without a plan would be folly, as the corrupted forces would sense its absence and attack.",
          "paths": [
            "The Edge of Corruption",
            "The Shifting Paths"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Resistance of the Light",
        "The Corrupted (thorn husks)",
        "The Last Archons"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Seed of Original Order is not merely a seed; it is the crystallized essence of the layer's original purpose. If planted in the pure soil of Abellio and watered with the tears of a reformed Harmonium member (which must be freely given), it will sprout and begin to reverse the corruption. The Resistance has the soil and the seed, but they lack the tears; the former Harmonium member in the camp has not yet fully repented, and his tears are tainted with pride."
    },
    "The Spire of the Outlands": {
      "name": "The Spire of the Outlands",
      "basicInfo": "The infinite needle of stone at the center of the Outlands, around which the entire plane revolves. The Spire is so tall that its peak is lost in the clouds, and no one has ever reached the top. Its base is surrounded by a zone of absolute neutrality, where magic fails, alignment fades, and even the gods are powerless. The rilmani maintain a watchful presence here, ensuring that no force disrupts the balance. Pilgrims and philosophers travel to the Spire to experience the purest form of neutrality, while planar scholars study its unique properties. The Spire is also the anchor for Sigil, which floats above it in the shape of a great ring. The area around the Spire is a desolate plain of grey stone, devoid of life, yet strangely peaceful. It is a place of profound stillness, where the only sound is the wind and the only light is a steady, colorless glow.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 6,
      "region": "The Outlands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Null Zone": {
          "description": "The immediate surroundings of the Spire's base, a flat expanse of grey rock where all magic, psionics, and alignment-based powers are suppressed. No supernatural effect functions here; even a candle's flame burns without heat. The air is still and cold, and the silence is absolute. Rilmani sentinels stand at regular intervals, their metallic skin gleaming faintly. Visitors often feel a sense of profound emptiness, as if their very identity is being muted. A single, ancient tree, twisted and grey, grows at the edge of the zone, the only living thing for miles. It is said that meditating beneath this tree allows one to see the true nature of all alignments.",
          "paths": [
            "The Ascent Path",
            "The Rilmani Outpost"
          ]
        },
        "The Ascent Path": {
          "description": "A narrow, winding staircase carved into the side of the Spire, leading upward from the Null Zone. The stairs are smooth and featureless, and each step seems to require a conscious effort of will. As one climbs, the influence of neutrality weakens, and the effects of the surrounding planes begin to creep back. The path is marked by small, glowing runes that shift between colors representing the alignments. Climbing too high without preparation can cause disorientation and a sense of being pulled in multiple directions. A few small alcoves offer rest, but they are bare and uncomfortable.",
          "paths": [
            "The Null Zone",
            "The Rilmani Outpost"
          ]
        },
        "The Rilmani Outpost": {
          "description": "A low, squat building of grey metal and stone, set into the base of the Spire. It serves as the headquarters for the rilmani who guard the area. Inside, the rooms are spartan and orderly, with maps of the Outlands and reports on the activities of the gate-towns. The commander, an aurumach rilmani named Meridian, oversees the patrols and interviews visitors. The outpost contains a small armory of neutral-aligned weapons and a library of philosophical texts. A hidden door leads to a tunnel that descends into the Spire's core, where the Balance of All is kept.",
          "paths": [
            "The Null Zone",
            "The Ascent Path"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Rilmani",
        "Meridian (aurumach commander)",
        "Pilgrims and scholars"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Balance of All, hidden in the Spire's core, is not a physical object but a conceptual nexus—a point where the four alignment axes intersect. If a being of any alignment were to touch it, that alignment would temporarily dominate the entire Outlands, shifting the gate-towns and causing chaos. The rilmani do not know its exact location; only the Lady of Pain does. They guard the area out of duty, not knowledge."
    },
    "Sigil": {
      "name": "Sigil",
      "basicInfo": "The City of Doors, a torus-shaped metropolis floating above the Spire, ruled by the enigmatic Lady of Pain. Sigil is the crossroads of the multiverse, a city where every door, window, and archway can potentially lead to another plane. Its streets are a labyrinth of stone, metal, and impossible geometry, populated by beings from every corner of existence. The city is neutral ground, enforced by the Lady's absolute power; no deity may enter, and open conflict is met with her terrible justice. Sigil is divided into wards, each with its own character, from the grimy Hive to the aristocratic Lady's Ward. Factions, guilds, and secret societies vie for influence, but all answer to the Lady. The air is filled with the clamor of a thousand languages, the smell of exotic cuisines, and the constant hum of portals opening and closing. It is a place of infinite opportunity and deadly peril, where information is the most valuable currency.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 26,
      "radius": 8,
      "region": "The Outlands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Hive": {
          "description": "The poorest and most dangerous ward of Sigil, a warren of crumbling tenements, narrow alleys, and desperate souls. The air is thick with the smell of refuse and cheap liquor. Here, the desperate, the criminal, and the forgotten scrape out a living. Gangs rule the streets, and the city watch rarely ventures in. Despite the squalor, the Hive is a hub of black market dealings and illicit portal use. A few hidden temples to forgotten gods offer sanctuary to the truly lost. The centerpiece is the Ditch, a massive open sewer that flows through the ward, serving as both garbage dump and makeshift thoroughfare.",
          "paths": [
            "The Market Ward",
            "The Lady's Ward"
          ]
        },
        "The Market Ward": {
          "description": "The commercial heart of Sigil, a bustling district of shops, stalls, and bazaars selling goods from every plane. The air is a cacophony of haggling, music, and the clink of coins. Here, anything can be bought or sold, from spell components to souls. The Great Bazaar is the largest market, a sprawling maze of tents and permanent stalls. The Planar Trade Consortium maintains offices here, controlling much of the legitimate trade. A central plaza, the Forum, is a gathering place for announcements and public debates.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hive",
            "The Lady's Ward",
            "The Clerk's Ward"
          ]
        },
        "The Lady's Ward": {
          "description": "The wealthiest and most powerful district, home to the city's elite: wealthy merchants, retired adventurers, and the embassies of planar powers. The streets are clean and well-patrolled, lined with elegant mansions, parks, and temples. The Hall of Speakers, the city's legislative body, is located here. The Lady's Ward is also the site of the Mortuary, the headquarters of the Dustmen faction, who believe that true life is found in death. The air is one of quiet privilege and hidden intrigue. The Lady of Pain's palace, though rarely seen, is said to manifest somewhere in this ward.",
          "paths": [
            "The Hive",
            "The Market Ward",
            "The Clerk's Ward"
          ]
        },
        "The Clerk's Ward": {
          "description": "The administrative center of Sigil, a district of bureaucrats, scribes, and record-keepers. The streets are lined with government buildings, courthouses, and the offices of the Fated, a faction that believes in the supremacy of the individual will. The City Courts are here, where disputes are settled under the watchful eye of the Lady. The air is filled with the scratching of quills and the murmur of legal arguments. A large library, the Hall of Information, offers access to maps of the planes for a fee.",
          "paths": [
            "The Market Ward",
            "The Lady's Ward"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "The Lady of Pain (silent ruler)",
        "The Planar Trade Consortium",
        "The Dustmen (death faction)",
        "The Fated (individualist faction)",
        "The Harmonium (law faction, reduced presence)"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "5",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Lady of Pain is not a goddess; she is something older and more fundamental, possibly a manifestation of the Outlands' neutrality itself. She never speaks and rarely intervenes directly, but those who cross her are flayed alive or banished to impossible mazes. A hidden faction, the 'Seekers of the Lady', believes that she is the guardian of the Balance of All and that the key to unlocking the Spire's core is hidden somewhere in Sigil. They have been searching for centuries."
    },
    "Glorium": {
      "name": "Glorium",
      "basicInfo": "One of the sixteen gate-towns of the Outlands, Glorium is attuned to the plane of Ysgard. It is a raucous, boisterous settlement of warriors, skalds, and traders, where the spirit of heroic combat and feast is ever-present. The town is built around a central mead hall and a practice field, and its streets are often filled with the clash of friendly duels and the sound of laughter. Glorium is a place where strength and courage are valued above all, but also where hospitality is sacred. The gate to Ysgard opens at the town's edge, a shimmering portal that leads to the Heroic Domains. The town's ruler, a retired planar warrior named Jarl Hrothgar, keeps order with a combination of personal charisma and the threat of his axe. Glorium is a popular destination for adventurers seeking glory, ale, and tales of epic deeds.",
      "x": -1,
      "y": -26,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Outlands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Mead Hall": {
          "description": "The social heart of Glorium, a vast wooden hall with a roaring fire, long tables, and walls hung with shields and trophies. Ale flows freely, and the air is filled with songs, boasts, and the occasional wrestling match. The hall is open to all who can hold their drink and tell a good story. The Jarl's seat is a carved throne at the head of the hall. A bardic contest is held every evening, with prizes of enchanted mead. The kitchen serves hearty fare: roasted boar, fresh bread, and cheese.",
          "paths": [
            "The Practice Field",
            "The Gate of Ysgard"
          ]
        },
        "The Practice Field": {
          "description": "A large, open field where warriors train, spar, and settle disputes. The ground is packed dirt, and the air rings with the clash of blades. All are welcome to test their skill, but the rules are simple: no killing, no magic, and the loser buys the winner a drink. The field is overseen by a retired gladiator named Brunnhilde, who enforces the rules with a motherly ferocity. A small shrine to Tempus stands at the edge, where warriors offer prayers before battle.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mead Hall",
            "The Gate of Ysgard"
          ]
        },
        "The Gate of Ysgard": {
          "description": "A permanent portal at the edge of town, leading directly to Ysgard. The gate is a ring of standing stones, each carved with runes of valor. It glows with a warm, golden light, and the air around it smells of pine and mead. A pair of valkyrie-like guardians, actually erinyes in service to a neutral power, monitor the traffic. Travelers passing through are required to leave a token of their courage—often a weapon or a trophy. The gate is the town's primary source of income and its link to the heroic plane.",
          "paths": [
            "The Mead Hall",
            "The Practice Field"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Jarl Hrothgar (town ruler)",
        "Brunnhilde (field overseer)",
        "The Valkyrie Gatekeepers",
        "Warrior guilds"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "2",
      "hiddenInfo": "The erinyes gatekeepers are not neutral; they are undercover agents of the Harmonium, placed here to monitor and subtly influence the town's alignment toward law. They have been slowly introducing stricter rules and regulations, hoping to shift Glorium's gate-tone from Ysgard to Mechanus. A group of local skalds has noticed the change and is working to preserve the town's chaotic-good spirit. The Jarl is unaware of the plot, but his trusted advisor, an old dwarf named Thrain, suspects the truth."
    },
    "The Hinterlands": {
      "name": "The Hinterlands",
      "basicInfo": "The vast, varied wilderness of the Outlands, stretching between the gate-towns and the Spire. The Hinterlands are a patchwork of environments that shift with the proximity to the outer planes: near the gate-towns, the terrain reflects the aligned plane; further out, it becomes a more generic, neutral landscape of rolling plains, forests, and rivers. Magic is unreliable here, and the laws of nature are flexible. Rilmani patrol the wilderness, ensuring no single alignment gains dominance. It is home to neutral creatures, wandering hermits, and the occasional planar traveler who has lost their way. The Hinterlands are also dotted with ancient ruins, mysterious monoliths, and natural portals that lead to random destinations. The only constant is change.",
      "x": 8,
      "y": -9,
      "radius": 7,
      "region": "The Outlands",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "areas": {
        "The Shifting Fields": {
          "description": "An expanse of grasslands that change color and composition depending on the alignment of the observer. A lawful visitor sees neat, uniform rows; a chaotic visitor sees wild, tangled growth; a neutral visitor sees a pleasant meadow. The fields are home to a herd of neutral-aligned celestial cattle, which graze peacefully. A few rilmani shepherds watch over them. The grass itself is edible but tastes different to each person. A hidden sinkhole leads to a network of caves inhabited by the 'Lost', souls who became trapped in the Outlands and lost their sense of purpose.",
          "paths": [
            "The River of Reflections",
            "The Pillar of the Ancients"
          ]
        },
        "The River of Reflections": {
          "description": "A wide, slow-moving river that winds through the Hinterlands. Its water is perfectly clear, and its surface reflects not the sky but the viewer's inner alignment. Looking into the river can be a profound experience, revealing hidden biases or suppressed desires. Drinking from the river grants a temporary sense of balance, but too much can cause apathy. The banks are lined with willows and reeds. A small ferry, operated by a silent, hooded figure, offers passage across for a single coin of any currency. The ferryman says nothing and never changes expression.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shifting Fields",
            "The Pillar of the Ancients"
          ]
        },
        "The Pillar of the Ancients": {
          "description": "A tall, black monolith standing alone in a rocky clearing. Its surface is covered in carved symbols representing all nine alignments, and it radiates a faint, pulsing energy. The pillar is a focal point for planar magic, and standing near it allows one to cast spells that normally require a specific alignment. However, prolonged exposure causes a gradual shift in the caster's own alignment toward true neutrality. The pillar is guarded by a group of rilmani monks, who meditate around it and will not allow it to be disturbed. A hidden chamber beneath the pillar contains a map of the gate-towns and their current alignments.",
          "paths": [
            "The Shifting Fields",
            "The River of Reflections"
          ]
        }
      },
      "factions": [
        "Rilmani shepherds and monks",
        "The Lost (trapped souls)",
        "The silent ferryman"
      ],
      "difficultyTier": "3",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Pillar of the Ancients is not merely a focal point; it is a fragment of the original pillar that held the entire multiverse in balance before the alignment axes were separated. If the pillar were destroyed, the Outlands would collapse, and the other planes would drift into chaos. The rilmani monks know this and have been preparing for centuries to repair any damage. They possess a crystal that can restore the pillar, but it requires a sacrifice of a soul of pure neutrality."
    }
  },
  "unassignedAreas": {},
  "factions": {
    "The Harpers": {
      "name": "The Harpers",
      "basicInfo": "A loose confederation of spellcasters, scouts, scholars, and spies dedicated to working from the shadows to preserve civilization, balance political power, and prevent any single entity from accumulating dangerous dominance. The Harpers are organised into small cells whose members rarely know more than a handful of fellow agents, communicating through magical sigils, coded letters, and tavern songs that carry hidden meanings. Their members include retired adventurers, gentle bards, mid-ranking nobles, sympathetic merchants, and even a few well-placed clergy. They strike rarely but precisely — toppling a corrupt magistrate here, leaking a tyrant's plans there, sheltering a hunted heir somewhere else. Their iconic silver harp pin is worn only in private; in public, Harpers are anyone and no one.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Decentralized",
      "leader": "The High Harpers Council",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Harpers' decentralised structure was created precisely because earlier incarnations of the order were destroyed by infiltration from within. The current High Harpers Council debates whether to consolidate again now that several major threats are coordinating against them — a debate that has already split veterans into hostile cells. A Zhentarim mole sits on the Council and has been reading their correspondence for nearly a decade.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Twilight Hall",
        "The Moonshae Cell",
        "The Berdusk Spire"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Zhentarim": {
      "name": "The Zhentarim",
      "basicInfo": "Also called the Black Network, the Zhentarim are a sprawling mercenary, smuggling, and information-brokering enterprise that presents a clean public face of legal commerce while running every conceivable illicit operation beneath it. They sell sellsword companies, spies, exotic goods, magical weapons, and protection contracts to anyone with the coin to pay. Members range from bookkeepers and caravan masters to assassins and battle-mages, all loosely bound by ambition and the understanding that the Network rewards results without questions. Their public commercial wing makes them indispensable to half the realm's economies, complicating any attempt to outright outlaw them. The Zhentarim's true creed is that profit and power are the only honest measures of worth.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Darkhold",
      "leader": "The Inner Ring",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Inner Ring is a rotating cabal of perhaps twelve individuals who have never all been in the same room together, and several of whom are not entirely sure who the others are. The faction's recent shift toward 'legitimate' business is a deliberate long-game strategy — they intend to legalise themselves into untouchability before resuming their darker work. A vampire archmage named Manshoon has worn three different faces on the Inner Ring in the last century, and may currently sit on it under a fourth.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Darkhold",
        "The Iron Throne Vaults",
        "The Caravan Coast Stations"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Lords' Alliance": {
      "name": "The Lords' Alliance",
      "basicInfo": "A coalition of allied city-states, monarchs, and powerful merchant houses that pools resources for mutual defence against threats too large for any single member to handle alone. The Alliance is a creature of formal diplomacy rather than ideology, its decisions made by ambassadors and treaty rather than ideal or oath. They share intelligence on monster movements, organise joint military responses to incursions, and occasionally undertake coordinated economic measures against hostile powers. Member cities maintain their independence absolutely, and the Alliance dissolves and reforms itself with each new diplomatic crisis. Despite its bureaucratic nature, the Alliance has prevented at least three would-be empires from rising.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Waterdeep",
      "leader": "Rotating Council of Lords",
      "hiddenInfo": "Several member cities use the Alliance primarily as a vehicle to monitor each other, with intelligence-sharing committees deliberately designed to map the strengths and weaknesses of supposed allies. A breakaway faction of senior ambassadors has begun secret negotiations to convert the Alliance from a defensive league into an aggressive empire-building tool with one of their cities at the apex.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Lord's Vault",
        "The Diplomatic Archives"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Order of the Gauntlet": {
      "name": "The Order of the Gauntlet",
      "basicInfo": "A militant alliance of holy warriors, paladins, righteous clerics, and zealous monks united by the conviction that evil must be confronted and destroyed wherever it is found. The Order takes the field where diplomats fail, marching against undead infestations, demonic incursions, and tyrannical regimes with unwavering moral certainty. Members swear binding oaths of vigilance and service, and their organisation is hierarchical and disciplined in a way most religious bodies are not. They wear distinctive armour bearing a stylised gauntlet sigil, and their reputation for unflinching action precedes them into every theatre. Critics call them inflexible; allies call them the only people who will actually do the hard work.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Helm's Hold",
      "leader": "The High Marshals",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Order's leadership has been quietly compromised by a coven of devil-worshippers who have spent decades placing their agents in key positions, slowly redirecting Order resources toward eliminating the cult's rivals while presenting it as righteous work. A small group of senior knights has noticed the pattern and is preparing a purge from within, but every confidant they bring in might be the one who reports them. The internal investigation has already cost three knights their lives in 'training accidents.'",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Helm's Hold",
        "The Vault of Oaths"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Emerald Enclave": {
      "name": "The Emerald Enclave",
      "basicInfo": "A scattered network of druids, rangers, hermits, and natural philosophers who work to preserve the balance between civilization and the wild lands beyond. The Enclave does not oppose civilization in principle — they recognise the legitimate needs of farms, towns, and trade — but they fight to prevent the unchecked exploitation, despoiling, and aberrant magical corruption of natural places. Their members are extraordinarily self-reliant, often operating alone for years across vast wilderness territories. They communicate through messenger animals, weather omens, and prearranged seasonal gatherings at sacred groves. The Enclave's dedication to balance sometimes places them on both sides of the same conflict, which has earned them confused reputations among more black-and-white factions.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Deep wilderness",
      "leader": "The Grand Druid Council",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Enclave's most senior druids have detected a creeping wrongness in the deep wilderness — something that emerged from beyond the world is slowly poisoning ancient ley lines. They have not yet shared this discovery with civilisation because they suspect at least one major power has been using and possibly causing the corruption deliberately. A schism is emerging between druids who want to warn the world and those who want to handle it without alerting civilisation at all.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The High Forest Grove",
        "The Misty Vale",
        "The Sunset Mountains Sanctuary"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Red Wizards of Thay": {
      "name": "The Red Wizards of Thay",
      "basicInfo": "The ruling caste of a magocratic kingdom built on slavery, necromancy, and the relentless pursuit of arcane supremacy, identifiable by their tattooed bald heads and crimson robes. Red Wizards openly practice forms of magic outlawed across most of the civilised world, including mass enchantment of slaves, large-scale fleshcraft, and the creation of armies of undead. Their kingdom is divided into magical zulkirs, each dominating a single school of magic and waging political war against the others. They export both magical goods and elite mercenary services, making them economically entangled with even kingdoms that despise them. To Red Wizards, mortal life is raw material, and ethical concerns are the affectations of weaker peoples.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Thay",
      "leader": "Szass Tam, Regent of the Council of Zulkirs",
      "hiddenInfo": "Szass Tam is a lich who has ruled in fact if not in name for over a century, having quietly turned the Council of Zulkirs into a rubber-stamp body. His true ambition extends beyond Thay — he is conducting a continent-spanning ritual that will transform the entire material plane into a domain of undeath under his sole rule. The other zulkirs suspect this but are too paralysed by mutual mistrust to coordinate against him. His magical preparations are roughly seven-tenths complete.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Citadel of Conjurers",
        "Thaymount",
        "The Bone Vaults"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of the Dragon": {
      "name": "The Cult of the Dragon",
      "basicInfo": "A doomsday cult founded on the belief that dragons should rule the world, and that the worship of undead dragons in particular will hasten this prophesied dragon-supremacy. Cultists work to elevate ageing chromatic dragons into dracoliches through arcane ritual, then serve those dragon-liches as priest-thralls. They infiltrate noble courts, mercantile guilds, and other organisations to redirect resources toward draconic interests. Members wear dragon-tooth jewellery and are marked with brand-tattoos that connect them to whichever wyrm has accepted their service. The cult has been quietly active for centuries, its long-term campaign hidden behind apparently disconnected acts.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Decentralized",
      "leader": "The Wearers of Purple",
      "hiddenInfo": "Recent leadership shifts have splintered the cult between the traditional dracolich-worshippers and a newer faction that worships the still-living chromatic dragons directly, particularly the Dragon Queen herself. The newer faction is funding a continent-wide ritual to summon their goddess into the mortal realm in physical form. The old guard has begun assassinating these zealots before the ritual completes, but they are losing the internal war.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Skyreach Castle",
        "The Well of Dragons",
        "The Hoarding Vaults"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "Bregan D'aerthe": {
      "name": "Bregan D'aerthe",
      "basicInfo": "An elite mercenary band of male drow exiles and rogues who operate as the most feared assassin company in the Underdark and increasingly on the surface as well. Bregan D'aerthe takes contracts from drow noble houses, surface kingdoms, and even underdark monsters with equal pragmatism, having cultivated a reputation for absolute discretion and lethal efficiency. They are unique among drow organisations in being male-led, escaping the usual matriarchal politics of their race through sheer competence and selective membership. Their agents are master infiltrators, blade dancers, and dark magic specialists, each with reasons to live outside drow noble society. The company answers to no goddess and no city, only to its own contracts.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Skullport",
      "leader": "Jarlaxle Baenre",
      "hiddenInfo": "Jarlaxle's true ambition is not mercantile but political — he is positioning the band to seize control of an entire surface city, transforming Bregan D'aerthe from a contract company into a sovereign power. Several deals he has signed in the last decade have been deliberately calibrated to put him in position to call in massive favours simultaneously. His agents have been infiltrating Waterdeep and other major surface cities for years.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Skullport Hideouts",
        "The Black Corridor",
        "The Surface Embassies"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Shadow Thieves": {
      "name": "The Shadow Thieves",
      "basicInfo": "The most powerful and ruthlessly organised thieves' guild on the Sword Coast, with operations spanning every major port and overland trade route in the region. The Shadow Thieves run protection rackets, smuggling rings, prostitution networks, and a sophisticated information brokerage that rivals legitimate intelligence services. They are organised in a strict hierarchy of cells, each cell knowing only its immediate superiors, with ironclad omertà enforced through swift retribution against informers. Their leadership operates from the open city of Athkatla, where the local authorities have given up trying to root them out and now simply tax them. They have repelled multiple attempts by rival guilds, foreign assassins, and royal task forces to break their grip on the regional underworld.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Athkatla",
      "leader": "The Shadowmaster",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Shadow Thieves' true upper hierarchy is secretly controlled by a small cabal of vampires who have ruled the guild for nearly two centuries through proxy spokespeople. They prevent the guild from growing too large or too noticeable, deliberately keeping operations at a stable level that doesn't draw celestial or paladin attention. Several apparent succession crises in recent decades were actually performances staged to refresh the public-facing leadership while the vampire council remained unchanged.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Five Flagons Inn",
        "The Temple District Vaults",
        "The Coronet Underground"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Xanathar Guild": {
      "name": "The Xanathar Guild",
      "basicInfo": "A sprawling criminal enterprise based in the sewers and underdark fringes beneath Waterdeep, ruled directly by a paranoid beholder who styles itself simply The Xanathar. The guild handles a wide spectrum of illegal activity — drugs, slavery, theft, extortion, smuggling, contract murder — through cells of humanoid lieutenants who report directly to their tyrant master. The Xanathar itself rarely emerges from its lair, preferring to surveil operations through magical eyes and information networks. The guild is a constant war of internal intrigue, as their leader's paranoia means lieutenants who succeed too visibly are as likely to be eaten as promoted. Despite this, the guild remains one of Waterdeep's most powerful criminal forces because The Xanathar's rays make it functionally impossible to assassinate.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Skullport / Undermountain",
      "leader": "The Xanathar (Beholder)",
      "hiddenInfo": "There has not been one Xanathar — there have been at least nine, with the guild's lieutenants quietly replacing each tyrant when they can manage it, hiding the death from the next replacement to prevent existential crisis among the rank and file. The current Xanathar has begun to suspect this and has launched a paranoid purge of senior lieutenants, threatening to collapse the guild from within. A fishbowl-bound goldfish named Sylgar may or may not be the only creature The Xanathar truly trusts.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Xanathar's Lair",
        "The Yawning Portal Tunnels"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Twisted Rune": {
      "name": "The Twisted Rune",
      "basicInfo": "A secretive cabal of liches, vampires, and other intelligent undead who manipulate the politics and economics of the world from the shadows for their own incomprehensible long-term goals. The Rune does not announce itself or accept open members; it operates entirely through human cat's-paws, hired adventurers, and dominated officials who often do not realise who their true patron is. Their interventions are subtle and span generations — a market collapse here, a noble line snuffed out there, a forgotten ruin reclaimed and quietly catalogued elsewhere. They are perhaps the most patient enemies any mortal could acquire, willing to wait fifty years to ruin a single life. Even other evil organisations regard the Rune with caution.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Decentralized",
      "leader": "Council of Eight",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Twisted Rune is engaged in a centuries-spanning project to identify, capture, and consume specific magical artifacts that, when assembled, will allow them to permanently sever a substantial chunk of the material plane from divine oversight. They have completed roughly half the collection. The other half has been deliberately scattered by celestial powers who long ago detected the Rune's plan and are now in a slow-motion arms race to retrieve them first.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Rune Sanctum",
        "The Vault of Thirteen Ages"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Arcane Brotherhood": {
      "name": "The Arcane Brotherhood",
      "basicInfo": "A cabal of dark wizards, necromancers, and ambitious artificers based in the pirate city of Luskan, ruling that lawless port through magical might and barely-restrained brutality. The Brotherhood operates from five towers within Luskan's Host Tower complex, each tower its own faction of competing wizards engaged in semi-formal magical duels for supremacy. They sell magical services, illicit potions, and forbidden knowledge to pirates, smugglers, and any client willing to ignore the human cost. Their members range from young apprentices terrified of their masters to ancient archmages who have not been seen in decades. The Brotherhood wars perpetually with the Red Wizards, the Twisted Rune, and any other mage organisation that threatens their regional monopoly.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Luskan",
      "leader": "The Overwizard",
      "hiddenInfo": "The five towers represent five competing conspiracies, each plotting the others' destruction while presenting unified strength outside. The current Overwizard maintains his position by keeping each tower convinced that the other four are about to attack it. A young apprentice in the smallest tower has accidentally discovered evidence that one of the towers is hollow — the wizards inside it died decades ago, and their magical effects are sustained by something else inhabiting the empty tower in secret.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Host Tower",
        "The Drowning Cellars"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of Vecna": {
      "name": "The Cult of Vecna",
      "basicInfo": "A scattered cult of secret-keepers, mad scholars, and ambitious lich-aspirants who worship the lich-god Vecna, master of forbidden lore and arch-secrets. Cultists work to uncover and hoard knowledge that should remain unknown — names of dead gods, locations of vanished cities, the true mechanics of fundamental magic. They mutilate themselves in imitation of their patron, missing an eye and a hand, and these wounds are healed back into uncanny prosthetic versions through dark blessings. Cells operate in absolute secrecy, individual members often working alone for decades before contacting another initiate. They believe knowledge is power and that ultimate knowledge is divinity, and that Vecna's eventual ascension will reshape reality into a more honest, more terrible form.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Decentralized",
      "leader": "Vecna (deity)",
      "hiddenInfo": "Several of the cult's most senior 'archpriests' are not aware that Vecna himself sometimes responds to their prayers personally, in flesh and presence — a privilege he extends only to those whose research is genuinely useful to him. He has been physically present in the mortal plane on at least three occasions in the past century, walking through cult gatherings unrecognised and carrying away artefacts the cultists thought they were preserving for him. He intends one of these unannounced visits to be his final, full apotheosis.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Citadel Cavitius",
        "The Whispered Library",
        "The Eye Chambers"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of Tiamat": {
      "name": "The Cult of Tiamat",
      "basicInfo": "Worshippers of the five-headed Dragon Queen and goddess of chromatic dragons, organised in temples that mirror their goddess's five aspects — red wrath, blue malice, green deception, black cruelty, white savagery. Cultists work to gather dragons of each chromatic colour into a great hoard-army that, when Tiamat breaks her infernal bonds and returns to the mortal plane, will conquer everything. They infiltrate kingdoms not through subtle politics but through wholesale corruption of leaders, replacing royal lines with dragon-blooded tieflings and dragonborn loyalists. Their high priests can channel raw chromatic dragon-fire and command lesser wyrms with binding rituals. The cult is fanatical and openly violent, making it a frequent target of holy crusades — but they breed faster than any reasonable army could exterminate.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "The Well of Dragons",
      "leader": "The Five Voices",
      "hiddenInfo": "The cult is internally divided over whether their goal of summoning Tiamat to the mortal plane is actually wise — a small but growing faction has begun to suspect that her arrival would consume the cultists themselves first, before turning to their enemies. This faction is quietly sabotaging the summoning rituals while pretending to advance them. If Tiamat ever discovers this betrayal, her retaliation will likely raze the entire faction.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Well of Dragons",
        "The Five Temples"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Order of the Platinum Cadre": {
      "name": "The Order of the Platinum Cadre",
      "basicInfo": "An interfaith order of dragonborn, paladins, and wandering monks who venerate the platinum dragon-god Bahamut, the cosmic counterweight to Tiamat. Members travel constantly, hunting chromatic dragons and dragon-cultists across continents, providing aid to communities suffering under draconic oppression. They fight with curved silver-edged blades and learn breath-techniques that channel divine radiance through their lungs. Their leader is rarely the same individual for more than a decade — Bahamut frequently sends visions designating new champions, and the previous Grand Cadre steps aside without complaint. They are a small force compared to Tiamat's cult, but their effectiveness is disproportionate; a single Cadre paladin can unmake a chromatic conspiracy that took decades to build.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "The Platinum Spire",
      "leader": "The Grand Cadre",
      "hiddenInfo": "Bahamut himself occasionally walks among the Cadre in the form of a humble old human pilgrim, testing the moral fibre of his champions through small encounters. The current Grand Cadre has met him three times without realising, though the old pilgrim's parting smiles have unsettled her each time. Bahamut has begun to suspect a Tiamat-aligned mole at the highest levels of the Cadre and is using these visits to hunt the traitor.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Platinum Spire",
        "The Dragontree Sanctum"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Spider Queen's Faithful": {
      "name": "The Spider Queen's Faithful",
      "basicInfo": "The drow priestesshood of Lolth, demon-goddess of spiders, treachery, and matriarchal cruelty, ruling drow society from the Underdark cities of Menzoberranzan, Ched Nasad, and dozens of other subterranean kingdoms. Lolth's priestesses hold absolute political and religious authority within drow culture, with noble houses competing for her favour through ritualised assassination and spectacular acts of cruelty. They wield potent divine magic alongside trained giant spiders and the ability to summon hellish demonic servants. Outside drow society they are seen as monstrous embodiments of feminine cruelty, but within it they are simply the cosmic order made flesh. Lolth's church survives external pressure largely because they make their own people's lives so unbearable that no outside threat seems worse.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Menzoberranzan",
      "leader": "Matron Mother of the First House",
      "hiddenInfo": "Lolth has been deliberately turning her gaze away from her priestesses for nearly a decade — a phenomenon known as the Silence of Lolth — to test which of them remain loyal without divine reward. Many are failing the test in ways she finds delicious. The Matron Mother of the First House knows this is a test and has instructed her own house to act as if nothing has changed, hoping to be rewarded when Lolth returns. Other drow houses have begun trafficking with rival demon lords, plotting to overthrow Lolth's church entirely.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Menzoberranzan",
        "Ched Nasad",
        "The Spider Temples"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of Asmodeus": {
      "name": "The Cult of Asmodeus",
      "basicInfo": "Worshippers of the King of the Nine Hells, the supreme archdevil whose followers practice tyranny dressed up as enlightened order. Asmodeus's cult does not present itself as evil; cultists are typically polished aristocrats, magistrates, and lawyers who argue persuasively that hierarchy, discipline, and infernal contracts represent the cosmic ideal of civilisation. They infiltrate noble courts, legal institutions, and economic powers, gradually rewriting laws to favour their patron's worldview of dominion through binding agreement. Members typically sign infernal contracts in exchange for magical power, social position, or the destruction of personal enemies. The cult sees demonic chaos and angelic naïveté as equally contemptible — only Asmodeus's order is worthy of worship.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Decentralized",
      "leader": "The Archdevil Asmodeus (deity)",
      "hiddenInfo": "Asmodeus's true purpose for the mortal plane is not conquest but transformation — he is slowly rewriting cosmic law itself through millions of small contracts, each clause precisely worded to advance the project. Once enough mortals have signed, the structure of reality will shift to match his preferred order. Most cultists do not realise they are participating in this reality-rewrite, believing their contracts are simple personal exchanges.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Nessus (extraplanar)",
        "The Iron City of Dis"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of Orcus": {
      "name": "The Cult of Orcus",
      "basicInfo": "A demon-worshipping cult dedicated to the prince of undeath, whose adherents seek to undo all life and reduce the mortal plane to a dust-realm of restless undead and silent tombs. Cultists are necromancers, plague-priests, and gravediggers who view life as a brief, miserable error and unlife as the cosmic correction. They desecrate graveyards, plague villages, and conduct mass-resurrection rites that turn entire populations into wights and ghouls in service to their bone-throned master. Their robes are bone-fetishised, their voices hoarse from breathing the dust of opened tombs. The cult's reach extends primarily through small rural villages and isolated noble holdings where their early infiltration goes unnoticed until graveyards begin to empty.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Thanatos (extraplanar)",
      "leader": "Orcus (Demon Prince)",
      "hiddenInfo": "Orcus has been slain twice in cosmic history and returned both times. His third return is ongoing — his physical form is being rebuilt cell by cell within a hidden tomb on the mortal plane, fed by the souls of every cultist who dies in his name. Several rival demon lords are aware of the rebuilding site but cannot reach it without alerting his followers; they have hired mortal adventurers to penetrate the tomb on their behalf.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Thanatos",
        "The Bone Citadel"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of Demogorgon": {
      "name": "The Cult of Demogorgon",
      "basicInfo": "Worshippers of the two-headed demon prince of madness, whose cult propagates through psychological corruption and the deliberate infliction of madness on populations as offerings to their patron. Cultists are cackling fanatics with split personalities, often cultivating both heads of their dual mind to mirror their master's nature. They commit acts of seemingly senseless violence, leave riddles carved into corpses, and spread plague-dreams through rural communities. Their gatherings involve self-mutilation, gibbering chants, and rituals that leave participants permanently broken. Even other demon cults regard the Cult of Demogorgon with disdainful caution, as their unpredictability makes them unreliable allies even within infernal politics.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Abysm (extraplanar)",
      "leader": "Demogorgon (Demon Prince)",
      "hiddenInfo": "The cult's apparent madness is an actual cognitive technique — what looks like raving incoherence is a precise communication protocol that bypasses divination magic. A cultist whose ravings sound like nonsense to outsiders is actually transmitting detailed orders to other initiates. This information-warfare advantage is the cult's primary value to Demogorgon, and the prince actively rewards followers who develop more sophisticated forms of public lunacy.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Abysm",
        "The Sibilant Asylum"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of Bane": {
      "name": "The Cult of Bane",
      "basicInfo": "Followers of the Black Lord, god of tyranny, fear, and absolute hierarchical dominance, organised in militant cells that infiltrate armies, knightly orders, and city watches. Banite priests preach that strength is the only legitimate authority, that fear is the truest binding of subjects to rulers, and that any kingdom not ruled through dominion will inevitably collapse. The cult attracts ambitious soldiers, secret-police officers, and provincial tyrants who use Bane's blessings to advance their careers and grind their enemies into compliance. They wear black gauntlets bearing the burning hand sigil of their god. Their organisation is rigidly hierarchical, with subordinates expected to overthrow incompetent superiors as proof of fitness.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Mulmaster",
      "leader": "Bane (deity), High Imperceptor",
      "hiddenInfo": "Bane's church regularly orchestrates wars that they appear to lose, deliberately cultivating populations of resentful, traumatised survivors who become more receptive to authoritarian doctrine in subsequent decades. Several major regional conflicts of the past century were Banite operations whose true outcomes were the multi-generational political shifts that followed, not the immediate military results. The current High Imperceptor is preparing the ground for a war he intends to lose spectacularly so that his successors can win the actual prize: the next century.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Mulmaster Citadel",
        "The Black Hand Halls"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of Bhaal": {
      "name": "The Cult of Bhaal",
      "basicInfo": "Murder-cultists of the Lord of Murder, a dead god whose worshippers continue to honour him through ritualistic killing in the absence of his direct guidance. The cult's structure is loose, individual members typically operating alone or in pairs, but they share a common ritual language of murder-tags, signature wounds, and victim selection criteria that allow them to recognise each other's work. Some are professional assassins; others are serial killers who found the cult's framework gave their compulsion meaning. They believe each ritual murder feeds Bhaal's slumbering essence, hastening his eventual return. Bhaalspawn — physically unique individuals born with their god's divine taint — are exalted within the cult but are also frequently sacrificed by ambitious cultists who hope to absorb their power.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Decentralized",
      "leader": "The Knife of the Lord (rotating)",
      "hiddenInfo": "Bhaal's death was not actually permanent — he has been reconstituting himself for centuries, drawing power from the murders performed in his name and from the deaths of his Bhaalspawn offspring. He is roughly two thousand qualifying murders away from full divinity. The cult does not realise their patron's return is imminent; many will not survive his arrival, since he intends to murder his most senior followers as the final ritual to complete his apotheosis.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Throne of Bhaal",
        "The Murder Temples"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Githyanki Empire": {
      "name": "The Githyanki Empire",
      "basicInfo": "A militaristic interdimensional empire of red-skinned, gaunt humanoids who dwell on the Astral Plane and conduct raids across the multiverse on dragon-mounts and silver swords. The Githyanki were once enslaved by mind flayers and freed themselves through warrior-prophet Gith generations ago; they have spent every century since waging an eternal war of vengeance against their former masters. Their cities float in the Astral Sea, where time does not pass, allowing their warriors to live indefinitely without aging. They ride red dragons into battle through ancient pacts and wield silver swords capable of severing souls from bodies. They view all non-githyanki as obstacles, food, or potential slaves.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Tu'narath (Astral Plane)",
      "leader": "Vlaakith CLVII",
      "hiddenInfo": "Vlaakith — the lich-queen who has ruled the empire for over a thousand years — is not what her people believe. The original Vlaakith died centuries ago, and a series of substitutes have worn her identity since, each ritually murdering her chosen champion at the moment they were promised godhood, consuming their soul to extend her own existence. The current Vlaakith is roughly the seventh impersonator. Several senior githyanki have begun to suspect the truth and are quietly recruiting champions who have not yet been called to court.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Tu'narath",
        "The Astral Watchposts",
        "The Susurrus Library"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Githzerai Order": {
      "name": "The Githzerai Order",
      "basicInfo": "The monastic counterpart to the warlike githyanki, a contemplative race of humanoids who dwell in the elemental Chaos of Limbo, where they shape the unstable raw matter of the plane into stable monasteries through pure focused will. Githzerai monks train psionic abilities to extraordinary precision, achieving feats of mental discipline that would shatter weaker minds. They share their distant kinfolk's hatred of mind flayers but reject the githyanki's path of violence, viewing imperial conquest as another form of the slavery they once escaped. Their monasteries are temporary and migratory, rising and falling as their inhabitants' will rises and recedes. They distrust outsiders almost universally, but those who earn their respect gain teachers of profound capability.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Limbo",
      "leader": "Zerthimon's Heirs",
      "hiddenInfo": "The githzerai have detected a fundamental flaw in the prophecies that originally split their race from the githyanki — both factions are missing the same crucial piece of context that would render their thousand-year war meaningless. The senior githzerai have known this for decades but have not shared it because reuniting with the githyanki would require accepting moral compromises they refuse. They are slowly preparing a third path that may render both empires obsolete.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Floating Monasteries",
        "The Will-Anchored Halls"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Illithid Concordance": {
      "name": "The Illithid Concordance",
      "basicInfo": "A loose galactic-scale federation of mind flayer colonies, each ruled by an elder brain in the depths of an underdark city, working together when convenient and warring with each other when not. Illithids consider all other sentient species to be cattle — useful for thralls, food, and breeding hosts. They engage in long-range planning measured in millennia, with current operations sometimes serving goals that will not pay off for thirty generations. Their psionic technology blurs into magic, producing biotechnological devices grown from living tissue. Each colony's elder brain absorbs the personalities of every flayer that dies in its territory, accumulating cumulative wisdom over centuries.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Underdark colonies",
      "leader": "The Elder Brains (council)",
      "hiddenInfo": "The mind flayers are remnants of a far older galactic empire that once ruled across multiple planes through a god-brain network spanning entire universes. That empire collapsed when its enslaved races — including a particular humanoid stock called gith — rose up and shattered the central mind-network. The current illithids have been slowly rebuilding the original network for millennia, and they believe themselves close to reconstituting their lost emperor — the original elder brain, large enough to be considered a true cosmic entity.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Oryndoll",
        "The Embryonic Archives",
        "The Sunken Grottoes"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "House Baenre": {
      "name": "House Baenre",
      "basicInfo": "The First House of Menzoberranzan, the most powerful drow noble house in the most powerful drow city in the Underdark. House Baenre has reigned for thousands of years through a combination of overwhelming military force, ruthless political assassination, and the unwavering favour of the goddess Lolth. Their compound is a fortress of obsidian and mage-fire that has repelled every assault directed at it across centuries. They train more sorceresses, priestesses, weapon-masters, and demon-binders than any other house, and their alliances span the entire underdark. Other drow houses simultaneously hate them and consider their stability the only reason Menzoberranzan has not collapsed into open civil war.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Menzoberranzan",
      "leader": "Matron Mother Quenthel Baenre",
      "hiddenInfo": "Quenthel maintains her position partially through Yochlol-summoned counsel, but the demonic handmaidens have begun feeding her false intelligence at the direction of a rival demon lord who is positioning to replace Lolth as drow patron. Quenthel suspects this but cannot prove it without admitting weakness, which would trigger a fatal succession crisis. She is currently funding a covert investigation through her most disposable house wizards.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Baenre Compound",
        "The Hall of Spider Tapestries"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Seelie Court": {
      "name": "The Seelie Court",
      "basicInfo": "The light-aligned court of the Feywild, ruled by the radiant Queen of the Summer Lands and her circle of archfey nobles, presiding over the bright half of the fey planes. The Seelie maintain a glittering, beautiful, and treacherously alien society where every interaction is a layered game of obligations, gifts, and unwritten codes. Their definition of 'good' does not always match mortal conceptions; they are kind in ways that humans find arbitrary, cruel in ways that pixies find delightful. They wage eternal seasonal war against the Unseelie Court, with skirmishes that play out as elaborate masques and balls in which casualties happen during the dancing. Mortals who attract Seelie favour gain extraordinary boons; mortals who offend them often vanish for a hundred years and return changed.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Senaliesse (Feywild)",
      "leader": "Titania, Queen of the Summer Lands",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Seelie Court's eternal war with the Unseelie is, at the highest levels, a deliberately maintained pageant. Titania and the Queen of Air and Darkness reached a private accord centuries ago and stage their conflicts as theatre, redirecting fey energy that might otherwise turn inward into productive external channels. A growing number of younger archfey have begun to suspect this and are agitating for genuine warfare.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Senaliesse",
        "The Solaris Garden",
        "The Mirror Lakes"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Gloaming Court": {
      "name": "The Gloaming Court",
      "basicInfo": "The dark-aligned counterpart to the Seelie, ruled by the Queen of Air and Darkness from her throne in the night reaches of the Feywild. The Gloaming Court delights in cruelty, predation, and the slow ruination of mortal lives, conducted through the same elaborate fey etiquette that characterises their bright cousins. Their courtiers include hags, redcaps, dark sidhe, and corrupted dryads, all bound by ancient oaths to their queen. They prey on mortals through bargains, dreams, and stolen children, transforming their victims into corrupted fey servants. A mortal who finds themselves at the Gloaming Court is rarely permitted to leave intact, and even rarer to leave at all.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "The Court of Air and Darkness (Feywild)",
      "leader": "The Queen of Air and Darkness",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Queen has grown bored of her secret peace with Titania and has begun deliberately escalating their conflicts beyond the agreed boundaries. She intends to provoke a true Seelie-Unseelie war, using mortal champions on both sides as the actual casualties while her own elite guard remains protected. She is searching for a particular mortal bloodline whose deaths in this war will fulfil an ancient prophecy granting her dominion over both courts.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Black Citadel",
        "The Court of Night",
        "The Whispering Glades"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Modron Hierarchy": {
      "name": "The Modron Hierarchy",
      "basicInfo": "A vast clockwork civilisation of geometric beings dwelling on the lawful-aligned plane of Mechanus, ruled in absolute hierarchy by their unmoving god-monad Primus. Modrons exist in a perfect numeric order: 64 quadrones report to one pentadrone, five pentadrones to one decaton, and so on up through ranks named for their number of sides. Each modron knows its exact place, its assigned tasks, and never deviates from them. Once every 289 years they conduct the Great Modron March, sending battalions across the Outer Planes on inscrutable inspection tours. They are not evil, not exactly good, but profoundly alien — their notion of order is incomprehensible to mortal minds.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Mechanus",
      "leader": "Primus",
      "hiddenInfo": "The current Primus is not the original — the ruling monad has been killed and replaced at least once in cosmic history, the substitution conducted so smoothly that even most modrons did not notice. Some sages suspect the current Primus may be a captured demon prince of chaos, slowly rewriting Mechanus's order from within in ways too subtle for the modron hierarchy to recognise. The Great Modron March's true purpose may be to verify that this reality-corruption has not yet manifested catastrophically.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Spire of Primus",
        "The Cogwork Halls"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Knights of Solamnia": {
      "name": "The Knights of Solamnia",
      "basicInfo": "An ancient and proud order of chivalric knights bound by an iron code of honour, divided into three sub-orders: the Knights of the Crown (loyalty), the Knights of the Sword (heroism), and the Knights of the Rose (justice and wisdom). The order rose to oppose ancient draconic tyranny and has waxed and waned across centuries as the world's needs shifted. Their training is exceptional, combining heavy cavalry warfare with disciplined combat doctrine and unwavering personal honour. They have ruled, fallen from rule, and risen again multiple times in the history of their kingdom. Their relationship with the common people is complicated — admired in heroic times, resented in arrogant ones.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Castle Solamnia",
      "leader": "Grand Master of the Rose",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Knights' honour code contains a secret clause — the Measure of Last Resort — that would permit them to suspend their own laws in defence of the realm if certain conditions were met. The current leadership is debating whether those conditions have been met, with traditionalists arguing absolute adherence and reformers warning that the world will move on without them if they cannot adapt. A renegade Knight of the Sword has already quietly invoked his own personal Last Resort and is operating outside the order's sanction.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The High Clerist's Tower",
        "The Chamber of Honour"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "Wizards of High Sorcery": {
      "name": "Wizards of High Sorcery",
      "basicInfo": "An ancient academic-magical order divided into three sub-orders defined by alignment with the three moons that govern magic: the White Robes (good), the Red Robes (neutrality), and the Black Robes (self-interest). All three sub-orders share a common loyalty to magic itself and to the ancient Test of High Sorcery, which every aspirant must complete to be permitted to wield true arcane power. Failing the Test means death. The Conclave that governs all three orders meets in the Tower of High Sorcery to decide policies that protect magic from misuse — including misuse by themselves. Members of all three colours can be allies in academic and existential threats while remaining individual rivals.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Tower of Wayreth",
      "leader": "Conclave of Wizards",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Test of High Sorcery is not a single exam — it is a magically-shaped ordeal that responds to the candidate's personality, designed to expose their deepest flaw. Many candidates die because they fail to recognise that their flaw is being tested, not their skill. The Conclave has begun to suspect that one of the three moons is subtly corrupting its applicants' Tests, deliberately producing wizards aligned with that moon's hidden agenda.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Tower of Wayreth",
        "Tower of Palanthas"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "Iuz's Empire": {
      "name": "Iuz's Empire",
      "basicInfo": "A nightmarish theocratic empire ruled by the demonic demigod Iuz, a half-fiend tyrant who has personally governed his realm for over six centuries. The empire spans a substantial territory of brutalised provinces, where humanoids of all races serve under fear of the ruler's personal attention. Iuz blends demonic magic with mortal political administration with chilling effectiveness — his realm has functioning bureaucracies, courier services, and tax collection alongside torture pits and demon-summoning altars. His armies include orc legions, gnoll packs, and corrupted humans, all coordinated by a priesthood that worships Iuz directly. He rules through personal terror as much as institutional power, occasionally appearing in person to flay an offending governor before resuming his typical absence.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Dorakaa",
      "leader": "Iuz the Old (demigod)",
      "hiddenInfo": "Iuz is not as autonomous as he appears — his demonic nature places him in indebted service to his demonic mother, the demon queen Iggwilv, who can recall him at any time. He has been searching for a way to break this binding for centuries. Recent excavations of pre-imperial ruins have given him a thread of hope, and he has redirected substantial empire resources toward a research project of which his ministers know nothing. Failure to break the binding could result in Iggwilv reclaiming him, leaving the empire suddenly headless.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Dorakaa",
        "The Boneheart",
        "Iuz's Personal Tower"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Scarlet Brotherhood": {
      "name": "The Scarlet Brotherhood",
      "basicInfo": "A sinister cabal of monk-priests and assassin-philosophers who believe their racial bloodline represents the cosmic ideal of humanity, and that all other races and inferior human ethnicities exist to be ruled by them. The Brotherhood operates from a hidden island monastery, training generations of agents in fanatical discipline, deadly martial arts, and infiltration techniques. They place sleeper agents into the courts and bureaucracies of other nations, sometimes for decades, until coordinated activations can destabilise entire regions. Their long-term goal is the reduction of all 'inferior' peoples to slavery beneath their pure dominion. They are racist in a cosmically codified way, believing their doctrine is supported by metaphysical truth rather than mere prejudice.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Hidden island monastery",
      "leader": "Father of Obedience",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Brotherhood's entire racial doctrine is a fabrication assembled from corrupted historical fragments by the Brotherhood's founders to justify their political ambitions. The genetic 'purity' they claim does not exist; their members are no more or less varied than any other population. The senior Brotherhood leadership has known this for over a century and continues to propagate the doctrine because it is operationally useful, treating their own followers as raw material to be deceived alongside the rest of the world.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Hidden Monastery",
        "The Spider Throne"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Horned Society": {
      "name": "The Horned Society",
      "basicInfo": "A devil-pact theocracy ruled by an oligarchic council of human nobles who have signed binding contracts with archdevils in exchange for personal power and territorial dominance. The Society maintains absolute authoritarian rule over its territory through magical surveillance, sworn-soul enforcers, and devil-bound legions. Their citizens' lives are tightly regimented and constantly monitored, but their territory is also peaceful and orderly in ways that adjacent freer kingdoms struggle to match. The Society's leadership debates frequently about whether to expand or consolidate, with each councillor's position influenced by their particular infernal patron's preferences. They are dangerous diplomatic partners — their treaties hold absolutely, but the reading of any clause must be conducted with infernal lawyer-level care.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "The Horned Citadel",
      "leader": "The Hierarchs (council)",
      "hiddenInfo": "Several Hierarchs have been quietly attempting to renegotiate or escape their infernal contracts, with some success and considerable risk. The current Hierarch leadership is divided between those who view the devils as patrons and those who have come to view them as subtle masters. A faction of younger councillors is conducting research into a binding-reversal ritual that would free the entire Society from infernal servitude — but the same ritual could conceivably break human-side contracts as well, freeing the populace from oligarchic rule.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Horned Citadel",
        "The Inverted Cathedral"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Circle of Eight": {
      "name": "The Circle of Eight",
      "basicInfo": "A small council of the world's most powerful living wizards, dedicated to maintaining magical and political balance through subtle interventions across multiple kingdoms. The Circle's members are individually formidable enough to challenge gods, but they have collectively concluded that direct intervention almost always produces worse outcomes than careful manipulation. They guide events through their apprentices, agents, and adventurers whom they fund without revealing themselves. The Circle convenes only when major cosmic threats demand collective response, otherwise allowing each member to pursue their own interests. Membership rotates as wizards die, retire, or ascend beyond mortal concerns; the Circle is rarely actually full of eight at any given moment.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Greyhawk City",
      "leader": "Mordenkainen",
      "hiddenInfo": "Mordenkainen's commitment to balance has begun to slip toward something more like personal manipulation — he has been adjusting the world toward outcomes that favour his own priorities while presenting them to the Circle as balance maintenance. Several other Circle members have begun to notice and are debating whether to confront him or replace him quietly. The Circle's vote on this question would itself be a major balance-affecting event.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Mordenkainen's Tower",
        "The Eight Sanctums"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Aurum": {
      "name": "The Aurum",
      "basicInfo": "An exclusive society of the world's wealthiest individuals — magnates, merchant princes, and dragonmark-house heirs — who pool resources and political influence to advance shared economic interests. Membership is granted only by invitation and demonstrated personal wealth, and the four ranked tiers (copper, silver, gold, platinum) reflect a hierarchy of investment in the society's collective enterprises. The Aurum funds research, infrastructure, and political campaigns that promote stability conducive to commerce. They are not officially evil — many of their projects benefit the broader population — but their fundamental loyalty is to capital, not to people. They debate openly within their ranks but present a unified face to the public.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Sharn (Eberron)",
      "leader": "The Platinum Concord",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Platinum Concord — the highest tier of the Aurum, comprising the wealthiest dozen members — has begun planning a coordinated economic shock against the major dragonmarked houses, intending to reduce those houses' influence and increase the Aurum's relative power. The plan involves manufactured scarcities, manipulated currencies, and politically-orchestrated regulatory changes that would benefit Aurum holdings. If successful, the consequence would be the worst economic disruption the continent has experienced in two centuries.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Platinum Vaults",
        "The Aurum Headquarters"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Dreaming Dark": {
      "name": "The Dreaming Dark",
      "basicInfo": "A secret society of psionic infiltrators serving the alien quori dream-spirits, working to control mortal nations through dreams, replaceable agents, and long-term political manipulation. The Dreaming Dark's true masters dwell in another plane, projecting their consciousnesses into the minds of sleeping mortals to plant suggestions and recruit servants. Their human and humanoid agents are often unaware of the full hierarchy, believing themselves to be members of a more conventional intelligence service. The Dreaming Dark has been operating across multiple kingdoms for centuries, with strategic patience that no purely-mortal organisation can match. They have plans currently maturing that were begun before the founding of any current government.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Riedra",
      "leader": "The Inspired (Quori-possessed humanoids)",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Dreaming Dark's ultimate goal is not conquest but transformation — they intend to gradually rewrite the dream-archetypes of the mortal races so that the next generation of children will be born already psychologically malleable to quori control. This generational project is roughly halfway complete in their primary territory and has begun to spread to neighbouring nations through controlled migration. Mortal infants in affected regions are subtly different from their grandparents in ways no living mortal can detect.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Dal Quor",
        "The Riedran Towers",
        "The Dream Anchors"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Wayfinder Foundation": {
      "name": "The Wayfinder Foundation",
      "basicInfo": "A society of explorers, adventurers, and academic-adventurers dedicated to the discovery, mapping, and recovery of lost civilisations, ruins, and pre-cataclysm artefacts. The Foundation maintains a vast archive in its central headquarters, funds expeditions across continents, and trains its members in field archaeology, monster combat, and ancient languages. Members include scholars who happen to be capable warriors and warriors who happen to be capable scholars. Their charter requires that significant discoveries be shared with the broader academic community, though this requirement is sometimes honoured in spirit rather than letter. They are seen as romantic adventurers by the public and as competition by less ethical treasure-hunting outfits.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "The Foundation Hall",
      "leader": "President of the Foundation",
      "hiddenInfo": "Foundation expeditions have recently been recovering artefacts that share a common signature — they appear to be pieces of a single pre-history-spanning artefact of cosmic significance. The Foundation's leadership has not yet shared this pattern with the broader membership because they have not decided whether assembling the artefact would be safe. A schism is forming between archivists who want to map and contain the pieces and adventurers who want to assemble them and see what happens.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Foundation Hall",
        "The Pre-Galifar Vaults"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Order of the Emerald Claw": {
      "name": "The Order of the Emerald Claw",
      "basicInfo": "A militant cult of necromancers and undead-revering knights who serve a dethroned undead queen, plotting to restore her to power and elevate her to true godhood. The Emerald Claw operates as both a paramilitary organisation and a religious cult, recruiting from disenfranchised soldiers, ambitious necromancers, and embittered nobles whose families lost status in recent political upheavals. Members wear distinctive emerald armour or sigils, marking them clearly to those who know what to look for. They conduct raids on tombs, libraries, and ancient battlefields to recover artefacts of necromantic significance. They have suffered repeated defeats but recover quickly because their leadership genuinely does not fear death.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Atur",
      "leader": "Queen of the Dead (in exile)",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Queen of the Dead's most senior advisors have begun to suspect that her divine ascension plan, if successful, would not actually elevate her to godhood — it would instead consume her and leave a much darker entity in her place wearing her likeness. They have not shared this concern with her or with the rank-and-file membership. Their internal debate over whether to sabotage their own queen's apotheosis has caused several quiet defections in the past year.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "Atur",
        "The Crypt of the Crimson Hand"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Dragonmarked Houses": {
      "name": "The Dragonmarked Houses",
      "basicInfo": "Twelve aristocratic mercantile dynasties whose members are born with hereditary magical sigils — dragonmarks — that grant them specialised arcane abilities tied to their house's commercial speciality. Each house monopolises a particular service: House Cannith makes magical artifacts, House Lyrandar controls airships and weather, House Orien runs teleportation and overland courier services, House Sivis handles communication and law, and so on. The Houses are chartered as transnational corporations, technically subject to no kingdom's laws when conducting business across borders. They wage commercial wars through subsidiary rates, contract restrictions, and occasional sabotage. Together they hold more practical economic power than most kingdoms.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Various house enclaves",
      "leader": "House Patriarchs (varied)",
      "hiddenInfo": "An ancient prophecy known to only a few highest-ranking patriarchs warns that a Thirteenth House — dragonmarked individuals bearing a forbidden Mark of Death — would emerge and trigger a cascade of conflicts that destroys all twelve houses. Survivors of the original Mark of Death have been hunted and quietly eliminated for centuries. Recent intelligence suggests that someone is rebuilding the bloodline of the lost Mark, and the Twelve are preparing for war against an enemy whose existence none of them officially admits.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Twelve",
        "The Vaults of the Houses"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Pirate Confederation": {
      "name": "The Pirate Confederation",
      "basicInfo": "A loose alliance of pirate captains, smuggler kings, and free-port governors who control a chain of islands and harbours along major trade routes. The Confederation operates by quorum vote among captains, with shares of plunder distributed by complex traditional formulas that prevent any single captain from accumulating disproportionate power. They prey on merchant vessels of major kingdoms while honouring a mutual code that prevents predation on each other. Their port cities are nominally free, hosting markets where stolen goods are laundered into legitimate commerce. Multiple kingdoms have launched naval crusades to suppress the Confederation, and each has retreated, bloodied, after a season of unsuccessful pursuit.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "The Pirate Isles",
      "leader": "The Pirate Council",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Confederation is being slowly infiltrated by a kraken cult whose members have signed eldritch pacts with deep-sea entities for unnatural seamanship. Several captain-seats have already been quietly assumed by kraken-cultists who are repurposing the Confederation's intelligence and shipping networks for cosmic-horror logistics. The original-stock pirates have not yet noticed the shift but their plunder operations have been finding strange artefacts on certain wrecks the new captains specifically targeted.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Pirate Isles",
        "The Black Tortuga"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Frost Giant Ordning": {
      "name": "The Frost Giant Ordning",
      "basicInfo": "The hierarchical society of frost giants in the far north, organised by the Ordning — a cosmic ranking of giant-kind based on personal might, accomplishment, and lineage. Frost giants live in immense longhouses carved from glaciers and ruled by jarls who demonstrate dominance through warfare, raiding, and the slaying of mighty beasts. Their culture is one of warrior-poets who recite saga-poems while drinking mead from drinking horns the size of barrels. They raid coastal kingdoms in longships during the summer months and retreat to their glacier-halls in winter. Their kings are obsessed with the Ordning, constantly seeking deeds that would elevate their position relative to other giant kindreds.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "The Glacier Halls",
      "leader": "King Storvald",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Ordning has been in disarray for nearly a decade — the giant god Annam has fallen silent, leaving the giants without their cosmic referee. Frost giant jarls have been issuing reckless challenges to other giant kinds, leading to escalating skirmishes that no power can adjudicate. Storvald has begun secretly negotiating with hill giant chiefs and even storm giant nobles to forge a unified giant-king position outside the Ordning's traditional rules — an action that would be sacrilege if Annam were still listening.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Glacier Halls",
        "The Throne of Ostoria"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Cult of Bhaalspawn Refugees": {
      "name": "The Cult of Bhaalspawn Refugees",
      "basicInfo": "A small underground network that protects and trains those born with the divine taint of the dead Lord of Murder, helping them resist the murderous compulsions inherent to their nature. The cult was founded by an early Bhaalspawn who refused his bloodline's demand and who taught others to do the same. Members include both Bhaalspawn and supportive non-spawn allies — clerics, monks, and counsellors who understand the affliction. They operate through hidden safehouses and guide their members through years of meditative discipline that channels the murder-impulse into less harmful outlets. They are hunted by the Cult of Bhaal, which views them as thieves stealing the god's rightful inheritance.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "The Dawn Refuge",
      "leader": "First Refusal (a Bhaalspawn elder)",
      "hiddenInfo": "Several of the cult's most disciplined members have begun to suspect that Bhaal himself approves of their organisation — that he wants them as a foil whose existence motivates the loyal Bhaalspawn to commit murders that prove devotion. If true, even the cult's resistance is being woven into Bhaal's broader resurrection plan. The First Refusal has begun investigating this disturbing possibility but has shared his suspicions with no one else in the cult.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Dawn Refuge",
        "The Quiet Houses"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Order of the Cloistered Sun": {
      "name": "The Order of the Cloistered Sun",
      "basicInfo": "A monastic clerical order dedicated to the sun god, devoted to healing, charity, and the slow, patient improvement of mortal lives across generations. The Order operates hospitals, almshouses, schools, and orphanages across multiple kingdoms, with monasteries serving as both shelter and academy. Members take vows of personal poverty but their order itself is very well-funded, drawing endowments from grateful nobles and merchants whose families benefited from the Order's services. Their priests are trained in both classical theology and practical medicine, often serving as itinerant healers in regions too poor to support permanent clergy. They are universally respected and consequently consulted as informal mediators in many local disputes.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "The Sunlit Cloisters",
      "leader": "Most Radiant Father",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Order has been quietly cultivating a network of orphan-recipients raised in their schools who now serve in royal courts, military commands, and merchant houses across multiple kingdoms. None of these alumni receive ongoing instructions from the Order, but they share a deep sentimental loyalty to their childhood benefactors. The Order's leadership has begun debating whether to leverage this sleeping network — and whether doing so would compromise the genuine generosity that built it.",
      "secretLocations": [
        "The Sunlit Cloisters",
        "The Charity Houses"
      ],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Flaming Fist": {
      "name": "The Flaming Fist",
      "basicInfo": "The premier mercenary company of the Sword Coast, functioning as both a private army and the official peacekeeping force for the city of Baldur's Gate. They are highly regimented, well-funded, and known for their distinctive flaming gauntlet insignia and rigid chain of command. While their primary loyalty is to the coin, their contractual obligation to protect Baldur's Gate forces them to maintain a veneer of lawfulness in an otherwise cutthroat region. They possess a heavy infantry tradition and frequently employ magical support, making them one of the most effective conventional military powers in Faerûn. The Flaming Fist is charged with enforcing order in the Lower City and the Outer City of Baldur's Gate, and the highest-ranking officer of the organization is always elected as one of the Dukes.",
      "factionType": "major",
      "baseLocation": "Baldur's Gate",
      "leader": "Ulder Ravengard",
      "hiddenInfo": "The order is struggling to balance its dual role as a public protector and a mercenary corporation, with many officers prioritizing profitable overseas ventures over the safety of the city. A faction of junior commanders have been covertly accepting bribes from The Guild to suppress labor movements within their indirectly controlled enterprises.",
      "secretLocations": [],
      "known": true
    },
    "The Watch": {
      "name": "The Watch",
      "basicInfo": "The Watch is the elite civil constabulary of Baldur's Gate, charged with maintaining law and order exclusively within the affluent Upper City. Clad in plate armour marked with the city's heraldry, they are far better equipped and trained than the Flaming Fist, reflecting the wealth and influence of the patrician families they protect. They serve as the direct arm of the Dukes, enforcing the city's strict codes and ensuring that the Upper City remains a bastion of safety amidst the chaos of the lower districts.",
      "factionType": "minor",
      "baseLocation": "Baldur's Gate",
      "leader": "The two Oversars",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Watch frequently acts as the secret police in the Upper City, detaining suspected criminals and torublemakers without due process. One of the current Oversars is secretly a worshipper of Bane, and has converted a small number of the Watch soldiers to his creed, planning on expanding his influence in Baldur's Gate and becoming a \"grey cardinal\", exercising covert leverage on city's politics and economy.",
      "secretLocations": [],
      "known": true
    }
  },
  "worldVoices": {},
  "npcs": {},
  "worldLore": {
    "Languages of Faerûn": {
      "text": "Common is the most widespread language across Faerûn, serving as the trade tongue spoken by humans and most civilized races in cities and towns. Elvish (including dialects Eladrin and Drow) is spoken by elves and half-elves, commonly heard in forests and ancient settlements. Dwarvish is the language of dwarven clans in mountain strongholds and underground cities. Halfling is spoken by halfling communities, often in rural areas and traveling caravans. Draconic is the ancient language of dragons, also used by dragonborn, kobolds, and as the basis for many arcane incantations. Orcish is spoken by orcs, half-orcs, and many goblinoid tribes. Giant is used by giants and ogres across their territories. Gnomish is the language of gnome communities, particularly in the Sword Coast region. Infernal is spoken by devils and tieflings, while Abyssal is the chaotic tongue of demons. Celestial is the language of angels and other good-aligned planar beings. Primordial (with dialects Aquan, Auran, Ignan, and Terran) is spoken by elementals and genasi. Undercommon serves as the trade language of the Underdark, spoken by drow, duergar, and other subterranean races. Sylvan is used by fey creatures in the Feywild and enchanted forests. Regional human languages include Chondathan (Sword Coast and Dalelands), Illuskan (northern regions), Damaran (Bloodstone Lands), Rashemi (Rashemen), and Thayan (Thay). Thieves' Cant is a secret language of coded phrases used by criminals and rogues across all regions. Druidic is a secret language known only to druids, used to protect nature-related knowledge."
    }
  },
