      "storyStart": "Begin with the player as a male drow of no particular House standing — a freed commoner, a sell-sword, a skilled craftsman, a thief, a member of Bregan D'aerthe, or something similar. Establish their specific means of survival and the particular niche they have carved out that keeps them useful enough to be tolerated. The city is vivid around them: the Braeryn slums, the Bazaar's controlled chaos, the constant awareness that any Priestess who takes a dislike to them can end their day permanently. Open with the player in the middle of their normal work — a transaction, a job, a patrol, a favor being collected. Something mundane but specific. Then introduce a complication specific to their gender and station: a Priestess has taken notice of them for reasons unclear, a House has made them an offer that sounds like an opportunity but smells like a trap, or a fellow male has come to them with desperate information that would be dangerous to know and more dangerous to ignore. Do not make the player a secret rebel or a surface-sympathiser unless their backstory suggests it. They may simply be a pragmatist who has learned to work within the system. End the scene with the player holding a piece of information or an offer that will require them to make a choice about what they value.",
