    "Cell Eleven, Velkynvelve": {
      "name": "Cell Eleven, Velkynvelve",
      "description": "The drow outpost of Velkynvelve. You are in a cage hanging over a chasm, your hands are bound, and the Priestess in charge has already decided she doesn't like you. Time to find a way out before she finds a use for you.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player waking in a slave pen at the drow outpost of Velkynvelve, suspended in a cage of zurkhwood and iron above a long drop into darkness. Their weapons are gone. Their hands are bound. The air smells of fungus, torch-smoke, and the particular damp cold of the deep Underdark. Establish how they were captured, drawing from their backstory — an ambush on a surface road and dragged down, a betrayal by someone they trusted, a job gone wrong in a tunnel they didn't know was drow territory. The cage holds other prisoners of mixed races and backgrounds: a grimly pragmatic svirfneblin who has been counting guard rotations, a surface dweller in worse shape than the player, and possibly one or two other figures with their own agendas. Describe the outpost: the web-bridges, the elite drow warriors, the acolytes moving between chambers, and the Priestess Ilvara who commands this place and has a cold, professional cruelty. Establish quickly that the player's situation will deteriorate — Ilvara has selected prisoners for 'questioning' and the svirfneblin believes the player is next on her list. There is a possible opening: a guard rotation gap, a loose fitting on one of the cage's hanging mechanisms, a prisoner who claims to know where confiscated gear is stored. End the scene as Ilvara's acolyte approaches the cage with a list and a torch, beginning the selection process.",
      "locations": [
        "Velkynvelve"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Prison Cave"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Faerzress Storm": {
      "name": "The Faerzress Storm",
      "description": "The Faerzress, the ambient magical radiation that permeates the Underdark, is behaving wrongly. The tunnels are wrong. The compass is wrong. You are very possibly wrong about where you are.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player in a known tunnel or passage that has become abruptly unfamiliar. The Faerzress — the diffuse, faintly glowing magical field endemic to the deep Underdark — is fluctuating in ways that experienced Underdark travelers know to fear: spells are misfiring slightly, direction-finding magic is giving inconsistent readings, and the quality of the light from the Faerzress itself has shifted to an unsettling hue. Establish the player's prior familiarity with the Underdark and why they were in these tunnels in the first place. The disorientation is subtle at first — a landmark that should be here is not, a passage that should go left seems to curve the wrong way — and then more pronounced. They encounter evidence that others have passed through recently, but the evidence is strange: tracks that suggest something walking in circles, a camp that was set up and abandoned in haste, a fungal growth pattern that seems to form a rough symbol. The player is not immediately in danger, but they are alone, uncertain of their location, and the Faerzress storm shows no sign of settling. Give them a choice of directions to investigate. Do not tell them which is correct.",
      "locations": [
        "Northdark Expanse",
        "Deep Wastes Expanse",
        "Darklands Expanse"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Lights Out": {
      "name": "Lights Out",
      "description": "Your light source is gone. You are in the deep Underdark. You have perhaps ten minutes before something that can see in the dark finds you.",
      "storyStart": "Begin mid-situation: the player's light source has just been extinguished, destroyed, or stolen. Establish through the first few lines exactly what happened — a cave-in severed them from their pack, a creature snatched the lantern from their hand, a sudden gas pocket snuffed every flame, or a duergar patrol specifically targeted their light before fleeing. The Underdark without light is not merely dark. It is a tactile, acoustic, olfactory world that becomes immediately and lethally hostile to those who cannot navigate it. Describe what the player can still perceive: the direction of airflow, the sound of water somewhere below, a distant rhythmic tapping that could be natural or could not be, a faint bioluminescent smear on the cave wall twenty feet ahead. Establish that they have a small window of time to act intelligently before whatever hunts by sound and heat in this stretch of tunnel begins to investigate. They are not yet being pursued. But they are not yet safe. Give them a specific, immediate resource: a broken torch that might be re-lit with the right material, a phosphorescent fungus cluster faintly visible nearby, or the memory of a svirfneblin trick for navigating blind. End the scene poised at the moment they must commit to moving or staying put.",
      "locations": [
        "Northdark Expanse",
        "Old Shanatar Expanse",
        "Deep Wastes Expanse",
        "Great Bhaerynden Expanse",
        "Darklands Expanse"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Caravan Run": {
      "name": "The Caravan Run",
      "description": "You have hired on to guard a trade caravan through a long, dangerous tunnel passage between two Underdark settlements. The pay is good. The route is known. Nothing about this should be complicated.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player as a hired guard on an Underdark trade caravan, a day or more into a multi-day passage through a tunnel route between two settlements. Establish the caravan: the goods being transported (unusual Underdark commodities — refined rothé leather, preserved deepfish, raw Faerzress-infused crystal, worked adamantine), the merchant in charge and their character, the other guards and their varying degrees of competence, and the specific social ecology of traveling through the deep dark with a mixed group. The route is considered established and relatively safe, which means the merchant is cutting corners on escort numbers and moving faster than advisable. Describe the tunnel environment in sensory detail: the sound of the caravan echoing off stone, the smell of rothé, the way light bends around the Faerzress. Then introduce the first sign that something on this route has changed: a checkpoint that should have a small guardian post is empty, or there are fresh marks on the tunnel walls that the lead scout doesn't recognise, or the merchant receives a coded message from a runner and becomes immediately, visibly anxious but will not share what it says. Do not start a combat. End the scene with the player on alert, the caravan continuing to move, and the source of the unease not yet identified.",
      "locations": [
        "Northdark Expanse",
        "Glimmersea Expanse"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Myconid Accord": {
      "name": "The Myconid Accord",
      "description": "You have found your way to a myconid colony. They are peaceful, strange, and communing in silence. For the moment, so are you.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player having been guided or stumbled into a myconid colony — a grove of towering, softly bioluminescent fungi deep in the Underdark, inhabited by the peaceful, fungal myconids. Establish that they are not in danger; the myconids have recognised their intent as non-hostile and a Sovereign has extended melding spores, allowing the player to participate in the colony's communal dream-sharing. Describe this state: the strange intimacy of a shared consciousness, the images and emotions that pass between minds without words, the absence of deception because deception requires language. The player experiences fragments of the colony's recent memory — a creature that passed through and left distress behind, a tunnel that the myconids have collectively decided not to approach, a surface-memory carried by a spore that wandered down from above. When the melding ends, the Sovereign communicates in their clicking, slow way: they are glad of the player's presence, they have something they would like the player to consider carrying forward, and they point toward the thing they are afraid of without being able to articulate what it is. This is a quiet, contemplative opening. End it with the player free to explore the colony, ask questions as best the language barrier allows, or move toward the direction the Sovereign indicated.",
      "locations": [
        "Northdark Expanse",
        "Deep Wastes Expanse",
        "Great Bhaerynden Expanse"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Stone and Spite (Gracklstugh)": {
      "name": "Stone and Spite (Gracklstugh)",
      "description": "The duergar city of Gracklstugh, the City of Blades. You have work here, if you can navigate the grey dwarves' paranoia, the stone giant clan politics, and whatever it is that is making the city's inhabitants act stranger than usual.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player established in Gracklstugh, the great duergar city hewn from the cavern walls of the Northdark, lit by the ruddy glow of its immense forges. They have arrived for a specific purpose tied to their backstory — a commission for duergar-forged weapons, an intelligence contact, a guild contract, a personal lead. Establish the city's texture: the oppressive heat from the smelting district, the grinding of the Blade Bazaar, the duergar's baseline hostility to outsiders, and the imposing presence of the stone giant clan Cairngorm in their quarter of the city. The player has been in the city long enough to get their bearings. Open with them in the middle of a routine task — checking in at a contact's workshop, navigating the bureaucracy of the outsider permit system, eating at a duergar establishment where the food is functional and the atmosphere is deeply unwelcoming. Then introduce the undercurrent the city cannot quite suppress: a duergar they are interacting with loses their composure in a small, strange way — a flicker of irrational aggression, a momentary confusion that clears up immediately, a suspicious repetition of behaviour. It is subtle. It might be nothing. The contact they were going to meet has not opened their workshop today, which is apparently unusual. End the scene with the player having accomplished their small immediate task but aware that something in this city is slightly off.",
      "locations": [
        "Gracklstugh"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "Blade Bazaar"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Deep Market": {
      "name": "The Deep Market",
      "description": "Commerce happens even in the dark. You have coin, time, and a list of things you need. The market provides, if you know how to navigate it.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with a peaceful, atmospheric slice-of-life opening in an Underdark market — the Blade Bazaar of Gracklstugh, a svirfneblin trading post, a specific section of the Gracklstugh commerce district, or a smaller informal fungal-market in a way-settlement. The player is browsing with a specific errand or purchase in mind. Populate the market with vivid Underdark-specific sensory detail: the smell of preserved deepfish, alchemical powders, and rothé hide; the cool mineral air; the soft bioluminescence of trader lanterns; the particular sounds of commerce conducted in whispers and clicks. Include specific stalls and their goods — a gnome selling modified darkvision lenses, a derro with a suspicious variety of surface-world trinkets, a deep gnome family selling mushroom wines, an anonymous hooded figure at a small table offering information at posted rates. Give the player a small mundane choice to face — a purchase they want but might not need, a vendor haggling hard, an overheard snippet of gossip that is mildly interesting. Introduce no threats, no plot hooks, no crises. End the scene with the player standing in the market, their immediate errand done or in progress, free to do as they wish.",
      "locations": [
        "Gracklstugh",
        "Blingdenstone"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Blingdenstone Needs Hands": {
      "name": "Blingdenstone Needs Hands",
      "description": "The svirfneblin are reclaiming their city from the creatures that overran it. They are short on fighters, long on problems, and quietly desperate. You are here, and here counts for a lot right now.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player present in the partially-reclaimed svirfneblin settlement of Blingdenstone, the deep gnome city in the Northdark that the gnomes are painstakingly retaking from oozes, wererats, and whatever else moved in after the drow drove them out. Establish the mood: cautious optimism, exhaustion, and a kind of collective determination particular to people who are rebuilding something they loved. The player is already known to the settlement — they arrived some days or weeks ago and have made themselves useful enough to be trusted, if not fully accepted. Open with them being given or accepting a task from one of the project leads: a scouting run into a section of the reclaimed district to check on an ooze that was supposed to be cleared but hasn't been confirmed, a delivery of supplies to a work crew that has gone quiet, or an introduction to a gnome elder who has been asking specifically for an outsider's perspective on a problem they are reluctant to bring to the council. Establish the gnomes themselves — their pragmatism, their particular brand of courage that doesn't announce itself, their strong community bonds. This is not a crisis. This is a working day in a city being reborn. End the scene as the player sets out on their task, the settlement busy and purposeful around them.",
      "locations": [
        "Blingdenstone"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "What the Aboleth Showed Me": {
      "name": "What the Aboleth Showed Me",
      "description": "You've been having very clear, very detailed dreams about a place you have never been. The architecture is wrong. The geometry is wrong. And the voice that narrates them is not your own.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player in a relatively safe location — a settlement, a camp, a waystation — waking from sleep with unusual mental clarity and an oppressive sense of familiarity with somewhere they have never physically been. Establish that this is not the first such dream. They have been happening with increasing frequency and vividness for several days. The dreams show the same location each time: a vast, dark underwater city of impossible angles, lit by the bioluminescence of creatures they have no name for, populated by vast, ancient, tentacled forms moving with slow and total authority. The voice in the dreams — patient, precise, without warmth — has been narrating. It calls the player by name. It asks questions. This morning, the player woke with a specific piece of information they did not have before: a location. A tunnel junction they passed through two days ago. A specific feature. The knowledge arrived in their mind as naturally as memory, and that is perhaps the most frightening part. Include a fellow traveler or NPC who notices the player's disturbance without understanding its cause. Do not reveal what the aboleth wants. End the scene with the player holding a map, looking at the junction the voice described, deciding whether to go toward it or away.",
      "locations": [
        "Glimmersea Expanse",
        "Deep Wastes Expanse",
        "Northdark Expanse"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Illithid Escapee": {
      "name": "The Illithid Escapee",
      "description": "You don't know how long you were in the colony. You don't know everything they took from you while you were there. But you are out, and you are running, and you are going to figure out the rest later.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player in desperate motion, moving fast through an unfamiliar tunnel, putting distance between themselves and a mind flayer colony they have just escaped from. They are physically intact but mentally scraped raw — the process of being enthralled by a mind flayer leaves a residue, a sense of absence where certain memories were accessed and perhaps rearranged. Establish what they know for certain: they were captured, they were held, they are now free. Establish what they are uncertain of: how long they were held, what the illithids were looking for in their mind, whether they were made to do things they cannot fully recall. They have some of their equipment — enough to survive — but their inventory feels wrong in ways they are still cataloguing. As they put distance between themselves and the colony, introduce a moment of stillness where they can take stock. Then introduce a complication: they realise they know something they did not know before, something specific and tactical about the colony or the illithids' current project, and they have no idea whether this knowledge is something they overheard while prisoner or something the illithids deliberately placed in their mind to carry outward. End the scene with the player resting in a defensible alcove, holding knowledge they cannot fully trust, free to decide what to do with it.",
      "locations": [
        "Northdark Expanse",
        "Deep Wastes Expanse",
        "Earthroot Expanse"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Sword for Hire (Underdark)": {
      "name": "Sword for Hire (Underdark)",
      "description": "Work is work, even a mile underground. The job board at the Underdark waystation has postings. The pay is uneven. The clients are unusual. Today you pick something and walk into it.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player standing before a job posting board or its Underdark equivalent — a carved stone tablet with inscribed offerings, a derro crier repeating commissions for pay, a svirfneblin guild board in a deep gnome waystation, or the mercenary noteboard. Generate four distinct job postings appropriate to the Underdark context: one mundane and immediate (escorting a merchant's delivery through a safe but time-sensitive passage), one moderately interesting with a clear hook (a svirfneblin family is offering substantial payment to recover a stolen item from a goblinoid camp two tunnels over), one ambitious and somewhat unhinged (a duergar factor is seeking heavily armed escorts for a salvage run into a section of tunnel officially classified as 'contested' by Gracklstugh's Merchant Council, reason unstated), and one that is worded with suspicious precision and implies far more than it admits (a simple request for a 'skilled individual of flexible morality to perform a one-time consultation, payment in surface coin, discretion assumed'). Each posting should have a named contact, a specific objective, and a specific payment. Do not steer the player toward any particular choice. End the opening with the player at the board, free to take a posting, ask around, or look for work not currently advertised.",
      "locations": [
        "Gracklstugh",
        "Blingdenstone"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The House War (Underdark)": {
      "name": "The House War (Underdark)",
      "description": "Two drow Noble Houses have declared formal War. The tunnels between their districts are now a kill zone. You are in those tunnels, for reasons that made sense earlier.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player caught in the tunnel network between two drow Noble Houses that have formally declared war on one another within the past few hours. Establish what the player was doing in this area before things escalated — a contract, a transit, a meeting — and how quickly the situation transformed from navigable to lethal. A formal House War in Menzoberranzan is both more and less dangerous than a surface battle: it operates by specific rules, involves specific participants, and Lolth's priesthood has precise expectations about how it is conducted. But for a non-drow caught in the middle of it, those rules offer no particular protection. The tunnel the player is in is contested — both House patrols have pushed into it, and there are bodies from the opening exchanges. Establish the immediate tactical situation: two possible routes out, one blocked by sounds of active fighting, one blocked by an unknown obstacle. Another survivor is present — a drow commoner with no House affiliation who knows this area well and is running the same calculation the player is. They are not friendly but they are practical. End the scene as sounds from both directions indicate the window for a quiet exit is closing fast.",
      "locations": [
        "Menzoberranzan"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Surfacer": {
      "name": "The Surfacer",
      "description": "You grew up under the sky. The Underdark is not your home, your element, or your preference. But you are in it now, deep in it, and the skills that kept you alive above are only partially applicable below.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player as a surface-worlder who has descended into the Underdark, either by circumstance, by choice, or by necessity tied to their backstory. They are not a native. They do not have innate darkvision. They do not know the social rules, the territorial conventions, the hundreds of small pieces of knowledge that Underdark-born creatures carry unconsciously. Establish their surface identity and what brought them down here — a rescue mission, a contract, a flight from surface authorities, a trail of something they are tracking. Open with them navigating a basic challenge that a surface expert would handle confidently but that the Underdark makes strange: setting a camp in a space where sound carries wrong, trying to read a fungal growth pattern for navigation information they do not have the knowledge to extract, or managing a negotiation with a svirfneblin guide who is deeply skeptical of their surface instincts. Establish an NPC guide or contact who is Underdark-native and views the player's surface habits with a mixture of amusement and concern. This is not a crisis opening. It is a competent person operating outside their element, learning fast. End the scene with the player having managed the immediate situation, their guide indicating they are approaching the area where the real work begins.",
      "locations": [
        "Northdark Expanse",
        "Old Shanatar Expanse",
        "Deep Wastes Expanse"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Murder in the Deep (Underdark)": {
      "name": "Murder in the Deep (Underdark)",
      "description": "A body has been found in a place where bodies are usually made to disappear. Someone wanted this one found. You happen to be the most capable set of eyes available, and several interested parties are already applying pressure.",
      "storyStart": "Generate a story start where the player is called to investigate a recent murder in an Underdark settlement or waystation. Decide independently who the victim is and establish them specifically: their race, their role in this community, their relationships, and why their death is a problem beyond the obvious. The cause of death should be specific and investigable but not immediately obvious — something that raises more questions than a straightforward killing would. The location of the body should be significant: a neutral trading space where violence is proscribed, inside a locked storeroom with no obvious entry, or in a spot that would require knowledge of the settlement's layout. Introduce two or three NPCs at the scene with clear emotional or political investment in the outcome — a faction representative who wants a specific answer, a settlement authority who needs this resolved before it triggers a diplomatic incident, a quiet observer whose presence is unexplained. Make the political pressures tangible: several parties want a particular conclusion, and the truth may not be convenient for any of them. End the opening as the player is left with access to the scene, the body, and the witnesses, free to investigate on their own terms.",
      "locations": [
        "Gracklstugh",
        "Blingdenstone",
        "Menzoberranzan"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Bregan D'aerthe Business": {
      "name": "Bregan D'aerthe Business",
      "description": "You work for, work with, or have been contracted by Bregan D'aerthe, the mercenary company of masterless drow that operates in the spaces between Houses and above the rules that bind the noble-born. The pay is excellent. The expectations are high. The definition of 'acceptable losses' is creative.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player operating in a professional capacity connected to Bregan D'aerthe — as a full member, a contracted specialist, or an outside hire brought in for a specific job the company's own people cannot do cleanly. Establish the immediate task: a retrieval, a negotiation, a piece of intelligence to acquire, a problem to make quietly disappear. The D'aerthe operates with a certain style — efficient, ironically jovial at the management level, deeply practical below it, and completely uninterested in ideological justifications for its work. The immediate scene opens with a briefing or mid-task moment. If briefing: establish the handler — professional, precise, specifically vague about which client commissioned this — and the objective, which sounds cleaner than it probably is. If mid-task: establish what they are doing, how far along they are, and what they have just discovered that the briefing failed to mention. Introduce one other D'aerthe operative or contact who provides texture to the company's culture: the black humor, the professional pride, the complete absence of sentiment about collateral matters. End the scene at the moment where the task requires the player to make a decision that the briefing did not cover.",
      "locations": [
        "Menzoberranzan"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "A Quiet Corner of the Glimmersea": {
      "name": "A Quiet Corner of the Glimmersea",
      "description": "The underground ocean. Lightless on the surface, bioluminescent beneath. You have come to the shore for your own reasons, and for a moment, the dark is almost beautiful.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with a slow, contemplative slice-of-life opening. The player is alone or in quiet company on the shores of the Glimmersea, the vast underground ocean of the deep Underdark. Establish why they are here and what they are doing — resting after a long tunnel run, waiting for a boat contact, taking stock after a difficult stretch, or simply having found a relatively safe ledge with a remarkable view. Describe the Glimmersea with full sensory attention: the absolute lightlessness of the water's surface that gives way to moving bioluminescence below, the shapes that drift slowly at depth and may or may not be creatures, the sound of the underground ocean — different from any surface sea, flatter and stranger — the smell of mineral cold. Give the player a small, private moment: a thought they are sitting with, a decision they are not ready to make yet, a memory tied to open water from their backstory. Introduce no threats, no arriving figures, no plot hooks. Let the scene breathe in the way that almost nothing in the Underdark allows. End with the player at the shore, free to stay, move on, or simply watch the dark water for a while longer.",
      "locations": [
        "Glimmersea Expanse"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "--NINE HELLS STORY START LIST--": {
      "name": "--NINE HELLS STORY START LIST--",
      "description": "The list below are all the story starts that situate you in the Nine Hells, Toril's infernal plane of law, contracts, and damnation. Easily the most hostile realm on the map. Don't select this story start. It won't like break the game or anything it just has zero story start instructions so it'll just be some random nonsense.",
