    "Your Eternal Reward": {
      "name": "Your Eternal Reward",
      "description": "You died. That part is not in question. What is in question is everything after — because the notary devil at the front of the line keeps frowning at your paperwork, and in the Nine Hells, a frowning bureaucrat is either your doom or your loophole.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player dead. Open with a brief, vivid account of their death and the reason for their damnation, derived directly from their backstory — a life of cruelty or ruthless ambition, a soul-clause buried in a contract they signed, a bargain made in a desperate moment, or a single terrible mistake whose full weight they never understood. If the backstory offers no obvious damnable sin, decide independently what technicality claimed them, and consider the possibility that their damnation is genuinely disputable. The player retains their memories, their skills, and their sense of self, manifested as a soul with a body that feels almost, but not quite, like the one they lost. They stand in the processing queue at the Gate of the Second Layer, surrounded by the shuffling, weeping damned, as notary devils record sins and assign eternities with bureaucratic indifference. When the player reaches the front, something goes wrong: the notary devil finds an irregularity in their record — a missing counter-signature, a contested claim between two infernal parties, an entry that contradicts itself — and in Hell, paperwork is law. Establish that this irregularity gives the player a sliver of standing that no other soul in the line possesses, and that several kinds of devil would find that standing very interesting. Do not resolve what the irregularity means and do not predetermine their path — escape, appeal, bargain, or something stranger are all on the table. End the scene with the notary devil looking up from the ledger, waiting to hear what the player has to say for themselves.",
      "locations": [
        "The Gate of the Second Layer"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Soul Processing Center"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Descent into Avernus": {
      "name": "Descent into Avernus",
      "description": "You were in Elturel when the ground opened and the city's own guardian angel dragged it whole into Avernus. The bells never finished ringing. Now the sky is blood, the Companion drinks souls instead of shielding them, and you are one of the survivors deciding what survival means.",
      "storyStart": "Begin in the days immediately following the fall of Elturel, with the player as someone who was inside the city when Zariel pulled it through the earth into Avernus. Determine from their backstory why they were there — a citizen, a visiting merchant, a pilgrim, a sellsword between contracts, even a Hellrider — and open with a compressed, visceral recollection of the Descent itself: the tremors, the light of the Companion changing color, streets folding into chasms, and the impossible red sky where a blue one used to be. The player has survived the days since, and that survival should have already cost them something. Establish the current reality of the occupied city: devil patrols marching captured survivors toward the Companion to be drained, familiar streets split by fissures, cages of citizens levitated into the soul-siphoning light, and the Grand Cemetery holding out as the last organized resistance under the surviving Hellriders. Give the player an immediate, concrete situation that forces a decision about what kind of survivor they intend to be — a patrol sweeping toward their hiding place, a wounded neighbor who cannot move on their own, a shouted rumor that the cemetery gates open for refugees at a specific hour. Do not predetermine whether they join the resistance, hunt for their own way out of Avernus, or pursue something else entirely. End at the moment they must move.",
      "locations": [
        "Elturel"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Shattered Streets",
        "The Crater's Edge"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Blood War Pays Well": {
      "name": "The Blood War Pays Well",
      "description": "You signed a term of service with an infernal legion. The pay is obscene, the contract is airtight, and the enemy is the endless demonic tide of the Abyss. Today your company deploys.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player as a living mortal under contract to an infernal legion fighting the Blood War, on the day of a deployment. Derive from their backstory why they signed — extraordinary pay, a soul-clause they believed they could outsmart, flight from mortal law, a debt being worked off in service, or a patron's arrangement they had little say in — and establish the precise terms they agreed to, including at least one clause they have read many times and still do not like. Place them at a staging ground amid the machinery of Hell's war: legions of merregons standing in perfect silence, war machines being fueled with soul coins, pit fiend officers reviewing orders, and the player's own company — a mixed unit of mortal mercenaries, conscripted damned souls, and lesser devils, held together by a sergeant with strong opinions about all three. Establish the objective in concrete terms: a demonic incursion point to be sealed, a fallen position to be retaken before the Styx floods it, or a salvage claim to be held against an incoming horde. Build the pre-battle texture — equipment checks, gallows humor between mortals who know the odds, the distant thunder of the front carried on hot wind. Do not begin the fighting. End the scene at the final moment before deployment, with the player in the line as it starts to move.",
      "locations": [
        "Bronze Citadel",
        "The Iron City of Nessus"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "Landing Platforms",
        "The Mustering Fields"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Going Once": {
      "name": "Going Once",
      "description": "Someone you cared about is a lot number now. Their soul goes up for auction at the Pit in Dis before the day is out, and you have crossed the planes to be in the room when the bidding starts.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player attending an auction of souls at the Pit Auction House in the Iron City of Dis. Decide from their backstory whose soul they have come to recover — a family member, a lover, a mentor, a comrade — and how that soul ended up in infernal hands: a deathbed bargain, a defaulted contract, an outright theft, or a damnation the player believes was unjust. Establish how the player got here and what resources they carry: a purse of soul coins that is probably not enough, a valuable item that might cover the difference, a favor owed by someone in the room, or leverage of a less legitimate kind. Describe the auction house in session — Carver the bone devil auctioneer working the crowd from the sunken stage, the iron galleries of bidders, lots displayed in crystal containers and sold to the clink of soul coins — and introduce two or three specific rival bidders with their own reasons to want the same lot: a night hag collector, an archduke's purchasing agent, a mortal cultist with unsettlingly deep pockets. Give the player time to read the room, make a quiet approach, or improvise before their lot is called. Do not predetermine whether they win the bidding, cut a side deal, or attempt something reckless. End the opening as the auctioneer announces the lot immediately before the one they came for.",
      "locations": [
        "The Iron City"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Pit Auction House",
        "The Soul Market"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Devil's Due Process": {
      "name": "The Devil's Due Process",
      "description": "A summons arrived written in your own blood, and now you stand in Glasya's Hall of Final Judgment, alive and accused. The court's verdict is always guilty. Your job is to be the exception.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player, alive and in the flesh, standing trial in the Hall of Final Judgment within the Sepulchre of the Damned in Malbolge. Derive the charge from their backstory: a breached infernal contract, an oath broken, a devil's property damaged or stolen, a clause violated knowingly or otherwise — or, if their history offers nothing suitable, decide independently on an accusation that may be fabricated, mistaken, or technically true in the worst possible way. Establish how they arrived: summoned bodily by the contract's own magic, escorted across the planes by erinyes, or compelled to walk through the Gate of Oaths, where entry demanded they confess a true betrayal aloud. Describe the court in its full grotesque theater — the benches of bound lemures, the rack that serves as a witness stand, the jury of trapped souls who vote as they are told, the bone devil bailiff with his whip of frozen nerves — and the presiding authority weighing the case with amused cruelty. Provide the player exactly one asset: an advocate willing to whisper the rules of procedure, a procedural irregularity they have already spotted, or a piece of evidence still in their possession that the court has not accounted for. Make the stakes and the rules of the proceeding explicitly clear to the player. Do not predetermine the outcome; infernal law is absolute, but it is also law, and law has seams. End the scene as the player is called to enter their plea.",
      "locations": [
        "The Sepulchre of the Damned"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Hall of Final Judgment"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Family Debt": {
      "name": "The Family Debt",
      "description": "A relative you barely remember died owing money to Hell, and the debt passed to you by blood-clause. You have come to the Counting House of Minauros to look the ledger in the eye — and to decide what you are willing to pay.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player arriving at the Counting House in the Sinking City of Minauros to address an inherited infernal debt. Decide from their backstory who the deceased relative was and invent the substance of the original bargain — what was borrowed, promised, or purchased, and why it seemed worth it at the time. The debt has transferred to the player through a blood-clause they never signed, delivered by a collections notice that found them across the planes with unnerving accuracy. Establish the Counting House in operation: shelves of ledgers rising into darkness, bone devil clerks with ink-stained claws, erinyes auditors moving between desks, and the file on the player's family produced with unsettling speed. The presiding clerk lays out the situation with perfect professional courtesy: the principal, the interest accrued since death, and the fact that the relative's own soul is currently held in the Debtors' Prison against the balance, where interest compounds daily. Present the formal options — payment in soul coins or equivalent value, a formal contest of the blood-clause's validity, or service rendered in lieu of payment — while leaving room for options the clerk pointedly does not mention. Do not predetermine whether the player pays, fights the clause, schemes, attempts a rescue, or walks away and lets a soul rot. End the scene with the ledger open on the desk between them, awaiting a response.",
      "locations": [
        "The Sinking City of Minauros"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Counting House"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Ten Bouts to Freedom": {
      "name": "Ten Bouts to Freedom",
      "description": "The Crucible of Abriymoch has one rule that matters: survive ten bouts and walk free. You are somewhere in the middle of that count, and today the sand gets another match.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player as a fighter in the Crucible, the great gladiatorial arena of Abriymoch in Phlegethos, on the morning of a scheduled bout. Derive from their backstory how they came to fight here — captured in the Hells and sold, condemned by an infernal court, volunteered for a prize genuinely worth the risk, or claimed by a devil calling in a marker — and decide how many of the ten bouts required for freedom they have already survived. Establish the Champion's Barracks beneath the arena: the surprisingly decent food and brutal training, the fellow fighters with their own counts and their own ways of coping, and the chained deva held among them, bound in cold iron, whose presence no one can quite explain and whose quiet dignity unsettles the guards. Vulkar, the scarred pit fiend who runs the arena, should be a felt authority even when absent. Build the pre-match rhythm — warm-ups, wagers among the guards, the roar of the crowd bleeding through the stone, and rumors about how the arena floor will be arranged today, since the Crucible can flood with lava, fill with acid, or sprout traps at the whims of the crowd. Introduce the specific opponent or spectacle the player is slated against without beginning the fight, and do not predetermine its outcome. End the scene as the gate mechanism engages and the player is called up to the sand.",
      "locations": [
        "Abriymoch"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Champion's Barracks",
        "The Crucible"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Neutral Ground": {
      "name": "Neutral Ground",
      "description": "The Wandering Emporium: the one place in Avernus where nobody is allowed to kill you over a disagreement. You have coin, an afternoon, and business of the perfectly ordinary kind.",
      "storyStart": "Generate a peaceful slice-of-life opening at the Wandering Emporium, the traveling infernal bazaar that roams Avernus on the back of the enslaved demon Cragg. Establish from the player's backstory why a mortal is here and what ordinary business occupies them today — resupplying, waiting on a contact, resting between jobs, or selling salvage scraped off the Blood War fields. Fill the scene with the Emporium's specific texture: the slow sway of the ground as Cragg lumbers across the wasteland, the burn of hellfire chili from the Festering Hellfire Tavern, V'juss the chain-smoking arcanaloth trading stories for shots of rotgut, distant cheering from the fighting pit, night hags weighing herbs at their stalls, Brugg the duergar hawking battlefield salvage at the armory, and Mahadi's silk pavilion presiding serenely over it all. Give the player a small, mundane choice to sit with — a purchase they want but do not strictly need, a game of chance at a corner table, whether to watch the next pit match or take a rare quiet meal in a place where the neutrality actually holds. Introduce no threats, no plot hooks, no crises; the day is, by the standards of Avernus, genuinely calm. End the scene with the player free to spend it however they wish.",
      "locations": [
        "Wandering Emporium"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Festering Hellfire Tavern",
        "Cragg's Landing",
        "Infernal Armory"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "An Invitation in Black Wax": {
      "name": "An Invitation in Black Wax",
      "description": "Lady Verin's Iron Rose is the closest thing Dis has to polite society — a garden of razor leaves and silk pavilions where infernal nobility trades secrets over fountains of liquid lead. Somehow, you are on the guest list.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player attending a social function at the Iron Rose, the pleasure garden of the succubus Lady Verin in Dis. Derive from their backstory how a mortal came to hold an invitation — sent as a patron's proxy, brought along as a duke's curiosity, invited because Verin collects interesting people, or having schemed their way onto the list to reach someone who will be in attendance tonight. Open at the Silver Gate, where the erinyes greeters strip all weapons without exception and no rank exempts anyone, then move into the Garden of Thorns: black iron trees with chiming silver razor-leaves, fountains of liquid lead flowing in hypnotic patterns, and clusters of infernal nobility conducting the real business of the Nine Hells in murmured half-truths beneath a conjured blood-red moon. Establish the social physics of the place — violence is forbidden by Dispater's own decree, which means every attack here is made with words, favors, and information, and the wounds are just as real. Engage the player in a conversation with a specific devil noble that is superficially gracious and structurally a trap, requiring them to navigate flattery, implication, and an offer that has not quite been made yet. Give them a concrete objective for the evening drawn from their reason for attending. End the scene at a moment where they must choose how to play a delicate exchange, with several viable approaches and no safe one.",
      "locations": [
        "The Iron Rose"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Garden of Thorns",
        "The Silver Gate"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Voice in the Ice": {
      "name": "The Voice in the Ice",
      "description": "Once, at the worst moment of your life, a voice spoke out of nowhere and offered you a way through. You took it. Now the frozen Archduke of Stygia has called in your marker, and his prison-palace of Tartarus waits at the end of the ice.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player crossing the Frozen Approach to Tartarus, the iceberg-prison of the Archduke Levistus, to answer a debt they incurred long ago. Derive the original bargain from their backstory: at some past moment of mortal desperation — bleeding out, drowning, cornered, watching someone die — the voice of Levistus reached them across the planes and offered salvation in exchange for a favor to be named later, and they accepted. Recount that moment briefly and vividly, including exactly what he saved them from. Now the payment has been called: dreams first, then whispers rising from reflective ice, and finally an unmistakable pull that has drawn them across Stygia's frozen sea. Describe the approach — wind that kills the unprotected, the whispers of Levistus's telepathy growing personal and specific, the toll demanded by the sentries, the frozen screaming faces that form the Gate of Frozen Tears — and bring them at last to the Astral Audience Chamber, where the Archduke's shimmering astral projection waits with the patience of something that has been frozen for millennia. Levistus names the service he requires; decide independently what it is, making it significant, dangerous, and plausibly connected to his imprisonment or his rivals among the archdevils. Lay out his terms with lawyerly precision, including what happens to oathbreakers. Do not predetermine whether the player accepts, negotiates, or does something profoundly unwise. End the scene as Levistus finishes speaking and waits.",
      "locations": [
        "Tartarus"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Frozen Approach",
        "The Astral Audience Chamber"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Price of Knowing": {
      "name": "The Price of Knowing",
      "description": "Somewhere in Cania is the answer you could not find in any mortal library, temple, or grave. Mephistopheles deals in exactly that kind of answer. The only question is what the knowing will cost.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player in Cania, the frozen eighth layer, in pursuit of a specific piece of knowledge that cannot be found anywhere on the Material Plane. Derive the knowledge from their backstory — a cure for a condition, the true name of an enemy, the fate of someone lost, the missing piece of a ritual, the location of something taken from them — and establish the chain of research, bargains, and risk that brought them this far down. Depending on where they are placed, open either on the Avenue of Frozen Supplicants approaching Mephistar, where the living ice statues of failed petitioners line the path and the frost giant sentries demand the Word of Entry, or before the black Obelisk of Questions at the Crystalline Labyrinth, which grants an audience with the Archduke only to those who answer its riddle and survive the shifting maze beyond. Establish the local rules of exchange: in Cania, knowledge is the only real currency, and Quillion the Cold, the pedantic arcanaloth master of the Grand Library, trades information strictly for information of equal worth. Make the cold itself a constant physical antagonist that punishes hesitation. Give the player a clear sense of what they carry that might serve as payment, and one thing they absolutely cannot afford to trade away. End the scene at the threshold of the exchange — the riddle posed, or the librarian waiting to hear what they have brought.",
      "locations": [
        "Mephistar",
        "The Crystalline Labyrinth"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Grand Library",
        "The Avenue of Frozen Supplicants",
        "The Obelisk of Questions"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Murder on Neutral Ground": {
      "name": "Murder on Neutral Ground",
      "description": "Someone has been killed at the Wandering Emporium — actually killed, on ground where violence is forbidden and Mahadi's entire business is built on that promise. The rakshasa needs answers before word spreads, and a clever mortal makes for a wonderfully deniable investigator.",
      "storyStart": "Generate a story start where the player, present at the Wandering Emporium for their own reasons, is quietly retained by Mahadi to investigate a killing that should have been impossible. Decide independently who the victim is — a devil of rank, a valued mortal client, a member of the Emporium's own staff — and establish them specifically: their business at the Emporium, their relationships, and why their death threatens more than one party. The cause of death should be specific and investigable but not immediately obvious, and it must represent a genuine violation of the Emporium's neutrality, which is the entire foundation of Mahadi's enterprise. Open with the discovery or its immediate aftermath, then Mahadi's approach: silk-smooth, generous in his terms, and precise about the deadline — the truth must be found before the news travels the Styx and before any interested archducal parties send auditors of their own. Introduce two or three suspects or witnesses with clear stakes: a staff member behaving wrongly, a guest who argued with the victim within earshot of others, a party who benefits conspicuously if the neutrality breaks. Make the political pressure tangible — several powers would prefer a particular answer, and the true one may suit none of them. End the opening as the player is given access to the scene, the body, and the freedom to work.",
      "locations": [
        "Wandering Emporium"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "Mahadi's Pavilion",
        "The Festering Hellfire Tavern"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Crossing the Nargus": {
      "name": "Crossing the Nargus",
      "description": "A remorhaz sled caravan is crossing Cania's frozen sea, and you are on it — hired blade, paying passenger, or the reason for the trip. The ice is miles thick. The things beneath it are patient.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player traveling with a sled caravan crossing the Nargus, the frozen ocean of Cania, a day or more out from the Remorhaz Docks. Establish their role from backstory — hired guard, paying passenger, courier, or a specialist brought along for a purpose not yet fully explained to them — and the caravan itself: sledges drawn by heat-radiating remorhaz, frost barbarian drivers descended from mortal worshippers brought to Cania millennia ago, an exiled duergar quartermaster, and cargo that includes at least one item under seal that the caravan master flatly refuses to discuss. The route runs toward the Glacial Rift crossing, the most dangerous leg of the journey, where bridges of bone and sinew span a crevasse glittering with the crystallized souls of those who fell in ages past. Build the sensory reality of the crossing: cold that kills the unprotected in minutes, the groan and boom of shifting ice, the chittering of the remorhaz, the strange frozen lights dancing in the black sky overhead. Then introduce the first sign that this crossing is not routine — pale lights moving beneath the ice and pacing the sleds, an outrider who has not returned from a forward scout, or a hermit encountered alone on the ice who delivers a flat, emotionless warning and will say nothing more. Do not start a combat. End the scene with the caravan still moving, the crew on edge, and the source of the wrongness unidentified.",
      "locations": [
        "The Nargus"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Remorhaz Docks",
        "The Glacial Rift"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Property of the Stockade": {
      "name": "Property of the Stockade",
      "description": "You are a high-value prisoner in the Stockade of Dis, where devils break demons for information and keep special mortals for special purposes. Your name just moved up the interrogation list.",
      "storyStart": "Begin with the player imprisoned in the Stockade, the great prison of Dis, held as a high-value mortal captive. Derive their capture from backstory — taken in a raid on the Material Plane, sold to the devils by someone who knew exactly what they were worth, seized as leverage against a patron or faction, or caught somewhere in the Hells doing something the Iron Guard took an interest in. Their equipment has been confiscated and catalogued somewhere beyond the cell. Establish the prison's texture: the branded intake mark on their soul that itches when guards pass, the sound-warded interrogation wing where Malachar the chain devil practices his craft, and the demon pens nearby, where captured fiends rage in cells keyed to their specific weaknesses — including at least one demon intelligent enough to talk through the walls, whose offers of a joint escape come refreshingly free of contract language and are untrustworthy for entirely different reasons. Give the player concrete materials for an opening: a guard rotation with a gap in it, an upcoming bout in the fighting pit that means movement between cells, a bribable functionary who handles the confiscation ledgers, or the demon's whispered proposal. Establish the pressure clearly: word has come down the block that the player's name is next on Malachar's list. Do not predetermine their approach. End the scene as keys sound in the corridor and the window to act begins to close.",
      "locations": [
        "The Stockade"
      ],
      "locationAreas": [
        "The Main Gate",
        "Demon Pens"
      ],
      "isDefault": false
    }
