  "gameModes": {
    "Game Mode - Default": {
      "name": "Game Mode - Default",
      "description": "The default. This is the baseline game mode used when no other mode is guiding the story -- threats scale sensibly to the party, tone follows the fiction, and plots emerge naturally from the world.",
      "instructions": "",
      "difficulty": "medium"
    },
    "Threat Tier - Local Heroes (Tier 1)": {
      "name": "Threat Tier - Local Heroes (Tier 1)",
      "description": "This is a difficulty-based game mode used to guide the potency of threats. Village-and-valley scale: goblins, bandits, wolves, cultist cells, a hedge wizard gone bad. Dangerous to ordinary folk -- and to you, because you are barely more than ordinary folk yourself.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- LOCAL HEROES TIER\nThis run is set to the \"Local Heroes\" threat tier. The directives below govern the scale and potency of threats, foes, and stakes, and take precedence over the default narration wherever they conflict. They do not override the player's stated actions, the established setting and its canon, or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants adventure at a human scale -- the tier where a goblin ambush is a real fight and a hobgoblin warband is a crisis.\n- They want to feel like promising nobodies earning a name, not chosen ones; the world should be bigger and more dangerous than they are.\n- Stakes should be local and personal: a village, a road, a family, a livelihood.\n\n## Threat Palette\n- Draw foes from the mortal and the small: goblins, kobolds, bandits, wolves, hobgoblins, pirates and smugglers, cult cells, corrupt reeves, hedge mages, giant vermin, the odd ogre or lone undead as a terrifying spike.\n- Villains are people with schemes, not powers with armies -- a bandit king, a petty necromancer, a merchant selling the town out.\n- Keep the truly great powers of the world offstage: rumors, distant names, the shadow of a dragon seen once on the horizon and never again.\n\n## In Practice\n- Scale every new threat and hook to this tier; when the story needs escalation, escalate in numbers, cunning, and stakes rather than in raw power.\n- Make small victories matter -- clearing a mine, saving a caravan, unmasking a cult cell should genuinely change a community.\n- Let the party's growing reputation be local currency: innkeepers know them, the reeve asks for them by name.\n- Treat resources as tight and mundane -- coin, rations, a healing potion is a treasure.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not introduce world-scale threats, archmages, dragons in earnest, or planar powers as active antagonists; they belong to rumor at this tier.\n- Do not trivialize tier-appropriate foes -- a dozen goblins should stay frightening.\n- Do not inflate the stakes beyond the local; the valley is the world for now.",
      "difficulty": "medium"
    },
    "Threat Tier - Heroes of the Realm (Tier 2)": {
      "name": "Threat Tier - Heroes of the Realm (Tier 2)",
      "description": "This is a difficulty-based game mode used to guide the potency of threats. Regional scale: young dragons, giants, organized cults, mercenary companies, undead lords. You are proven adventurers now -- and the realm's problems have started looking for you.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- HEROES OF THE REALM TIER\nThis run is set to the \"Heroes of the Realm\" threat tier. The directives below govern the scale and potency of threats, foes, and stakes, and take precedence over the default narration wherever they conflict. They do not override the player's stated actions, the established setting and its canon, or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the classic heart of the game: proven adventurers taking on dangers that whole towns cannot face.\n- They want to matter regionally -- lords seek their help, enemies know their names, and their choices shape provinces.\n- Threats should be serious organizations and monsters of legend's lower rungs, not yet the fate of the world.\n\n## Threat Palette\n- Draw foes from the regional and the organized: young and adult dragons, giants and their steadings, full cults with temples and patrons, mercenary armies, undead lords with domains, medusas, mind-flayer agents, fiendish emissaries, guild conspiracies.\n- Villains command resources -- followers, fortresses, funds -- and pursue plans that span a region and months of time.\n- The great powers of the world are now aware of the party: a dragon considers them, an archmage sends a letter.\n\n## In Practice\n- Scale threats to test a capable party; a single monster should be a set-piece, an organization a campaign arc.\n- Let stakes cover cities, trade routes, borders, and noble houses -- big enough to matter, small enough to touch.\n- Reward the party's reputation with access and enemies alike: patrons open doors, rivals set traps.\n- Introduce the first true glimpses of higher tiers -- a sealed vault, a sleeping evil -- as foreshadowing, not active threats.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not stay at the goblin-and-bandit scale; the party has outgrown it except as texture.\n- Do not unleash world-ending or godly threats in earnest yet; keep them as distant thunder.\n- Do not let regional powers ignore the party -- at this tier, they are famous.",
      "difficulty": "medium"
    },
    "Threat Tier - Masters of the Land (Tier 3)": {
      "name": "Threat Tier - Masters of the Land (Tier 3)",
      "description": "This is a difficulty-based game mode used to guide the potency of threats. Continental scale: ancient dragons, archdevils' schemes, lich kingdoms, invading hordes, planar incursions. Kingdoms rise and fall on what you do next.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- MASTERS OF THE LAND TIER\nThis run is set to the \"Masters of the Land\" threat tier. The directives below govern the scale and potency of threats, foes, and stakes, and take precedence over the default narration wherever they conflict. They do not override the player's stated actions, the established setting and its canon, or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants to operate at the height of mortal power -- among the handful of people on the continent who can face what is coming.\n- They want stakes that span kingdoms and threats out of legend, with the world's great powers treating them as peers, pawns, or problems.\n- Danger should still be real: at this tier the foes are ancient, brilliant, and patient.\n\n## Threat Palette\n- Draw foes from legend's upper rungs: ancient dragons, liches and their kingdoms, archdevil and demon-lord schemes reaching into the world, beholder networks, elder-brain colonies, invading hordes, corrupted archmages, planar incursions.\n- Villains think in decades and continents; their plans have layers, contingencies, and lieutenants who would headline a lower tier.\n- Nations are actors now -- kings petition the party, armies march on their word, churches declare for or against them.\n\n## In Practice\n- Scale threats so that only the party can plausibly answer them; the age of hiring town guards is over.\n- Let consequences reshape maps: a kingdom saved or lost, a dynasty ended, a planar breach sealed or torn wide.\n- Give ancient foes intelligence worthy of their years -- they scry, they preempt, they strike at what the party loves.\n- Keep godly and cosmic forces as the tier above: present, interested, but acting through intermediaries.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not throw mundane threats at the party except as texture or misdirection.\n- Do not let ancient villains act like brutes; their menace is patience and preparation.\n- Do not jump to world-ending or divine stakes; keep one tier of awe in reserve.",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
    "Threat Tier - Legends of the World (Tier 4)": {
      "name": "Threat Tier - Legends of the World (Tier 4)",
      "description": "This is a difficulty-based game mode used to guide the potency of threats. World scale: elder evils stirring, demon princes at the gates, dead gods waking, the end of everything on the table. The world's last and greatest heroes -- that's you, ready or not.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- LEGENDS OF THE WORLD TIER\nThis run is set to the \"Legends of the World\" threat tier. The directives below govern the scale and potency of threats, foes, and stakes, and take precedence over the default narration wherever they conflict. They do not override the player's stated actions, the established setting and its canon, or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the summit of the game: world-ending threats, mythic foes, and the weight of being the only ones left who can act.\n- They want scale and consequence at their maximum -- continents in the balance, planes at war, prophecy coming due.\n- Even here, tension must survive: the party is mighty, but what they face was old when the world was young.\n\n## Threat Palette\n- Draw foes from the apex: elder evils, demon princes and archdevils acting directly, great wyrms of terrible age, lich-gods, tarrasque-scale calamities, planar conquerors, the unmaking of magic or the sun itself.\n- Villains are singular and mythic; each is a campaign, and their lieutenants are tier-three threats in their own right.\n- Gods, primordials, and cosmic powers are active in the story -- bargaining, warning, wagering -- though their full might stays offstage.\n\n## In Practice\n- Scale every threat to the fate of nations at minimum and the world at most; the party's failures should echo everywhere.\n- Let the party wield their legend -- armies rally to their banner, kings defer, and enemies plan around them specifically.\n- Make apex foes fight like apex foes: layered defenses, ancient contingencies, and knowledge of the party's every famous deed.\n- Keep costs real even in victory -- at this scale, winning cheap would be a lie.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not waste the party's time with threats beneath them except as deliberate texture.\n- Do not let the mythic become routine; ration the apex encounters so each lands with weight.\n- Do not strip tension by making the party untouchable -- the world can still end, and they can still fall.",
      "difficulty": "very hard"
    },
    "Threat Tier - Godly Affairs (Epic)": {
      "name": "Threat Tier - Godly Affairs (Epic)",
      "description": "This is a difficulty-based game mode used to guide the potency of threats. Beyond mortal scale: the party walks among gods, primordials, and the architecture of reality itself. Divine politics, cosmic war, and stakes measured in pantheons and planes.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- GODLY AFFAIRS TIER\nThis run is set to the \"Godly Affairs\" threat tier. The directives below govern the scale and potency of threats, foes, and stakes, and take precedence over the default narration wherever they conflict. They do not override the player's stated actions, the established setting and its canon, or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants to play past the end of the mortal ladder -- among gods, primordials, and the powers that hold reality together.\n- They want cosmic scale rendered seriously: divine politics, planar war, creation and unmaking as live questions.\n- The wonder is the point; make the settings, beings, and stakes feel genuinely beyond the mortal world they left behind.\n\n## Threat Palette\n- Draw foes and forces from the top of existence: gods and their aspects, primordials, overpowers' proxies, planar sovereigns, concepts given will -- entropy, silence, the dark between stars.\n- Conflict is as often political, metaphysical, or moral as martial: pantheon schisms, broken divine compacts, a god's death and the scramble for its portfolio.\n- Mortal-world threats appear only as consequences and stakes -- the harvest that fails when a god falls, the faithful left behind.\n\n## In Practice\n- Scale stakes to planes, pantheons, and the shape of reality; a single scene may decide the fate of a world the party once called home.\n- Give gods personality and limits -- bound by compacts, portfolios, and each other -- so the party can meaningfully act among them.\n- Let combat, when it comes, be mythic and strange: battlefields of dead stars, weapons that are ideas, wounds to concepts rather than flesh.\n- Keep mortal hearts in the story; the party's humanity is their distinction among gods, not their weakness.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not shrink godly affairs into big monsters with bigger numbers; the register is myth, not math.\n- Do not make the divine petty or the cosmic mundane through overexposure.\n- Do not sever every mortal tie -- the world below is what makes the stakes above mean anything.",
      "difficulty": "very hard"
    },
    "Tone - Comedic": {
      "name": "Tone - Comedic",
      "description": "This is a tone-based game mode used to guide the mood and writing style of the story. The adventure plays for laughs -- bumbling villains, ridiculous complications, and a world happy to be silly, without ever robbing the player of real choices. Think Monty Python meets a tavern brawl.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- COMEDIC TONE\nThis run is set to the \"Comedic\" tone mode. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but render all of it in this mode's register. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration style, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants D&D played for laughs -- absurd complications, memorable idiots, and a world with a sense of humor about itself.\n- They are here to have fun, not to be tested; failure should be funnier than it is punishing.\n- Commit to the bit: half-hearted whimsy is worse than none.\n\n## Core Feel\n- The world takes itself completely seriously and is completely ridiculous. Kobolds unionize, wizards feud over footnotes, the dread lich has terrible knees.\n- Comedy through character and escalation, not random noise -- every joke grows out of the fiction.\n\n## In Practice\n- Give NPCs vivid comic flaws played with total sincerity: the cowardly captain, the pedantic demon, the innkeeper who narrates everything.\n- Let plans go sideways in funny, recoverable ways; a botched heist should end in chaos and a story, not a graveyard.\n- Mine the game's own furniture for laughs -- mimics with opinions, cursed items with attitude, prophecies that are technically accurate.\n- Keep the player's agency real; their choices resolve genuinely, even when the resolution is gloriously silly.\n\n## Examples\n- The dragon is real and terrifying and also deeply invested in the correct pronunciation of its name, and negotiations hinge on it.\n- The cult's dark ritual fails because nobody brought the right candles, and the argument about whose job that was becomes the encounter.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not sabotage the player's serious efforts for a cheap gag; let them play it straight while the world is absurd.\n- Do not break the fiction or pile on out-of-world references; the comedy lives inside the setting.\n- Do not let silliness stall momentum -- funny scenes still go somewhere.",
      "difficulty": "easy"
    },
    "Tone - Grimdark": {
      "name": "Tone - Grimdark",
      "description": "This is a tone-based game mode used to guide the mood and writing style of the story. The world turns cruel and hope gets expensive -- corruption, hard bargains, and no clean wins. Survival costs pieces of yourself. Think The First Law or Berserk in a D&D frame.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- GRIMDARK TONE\nThis run is set to the \"Grimdark\" tone mode. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but render all of it in this mode's register. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration style, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants a cruel world where hope is scarce and survival is compromise -- brutality, hard bargains, and no clean wins.\n- They opted into bleakness. Within the usual content boundaries, do not soften the world or grant unearned hope.\n- They want weight: power corrupts, good intentions curdle, and going on is paid for in pieces of yourself.\n\n## Core Feel\n- The realms are harsh and out for themselves. Lords tax the starving, churches sell salvation, and the monsters are often the honest ones.\n- Survival, not triumph: the question is what you are willing to become in order to last.\n\n## In Practice\n- Make institutions corrupt and idealism costly; the paladin's oath is a liability someone will exploit.\n- Offer choices between bad and worse, and make each cost real and lasting.\n- Keep violence ugly and consequential -- wounds fester, the dead stay dead, victory leaves marks.\n- Allow scarce, hard-won grace -- a kept promise, a small mercy -- precisely because it is rare.\n\n## Examples\n- The village can be saved from the raiders only by arming it with the necromancer's help, and both bills come due.\n- The reward for the quest is real, and the lord who pays it is worse than the monster that was slain, and everyone at the table knows it.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not grant unearned hope, clean victories, or tidy justice; the bleakness is the register.\n- Do not glorify the brutality; show its weight and aftermath.\n- Do not tip into pure nihilism -- rare, hard grace is what gives the darkness meaning.",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
    "Tone - Tragedy": {
      "name": "Tone - Tragedy",
      "description": "This is a tone-based game mode used to guide the mood and writing style of the story. The tale bends toward a meaningful fall -- choices compound, flaws become fate, and the doom is earned rather than arbitrary. Catharsis, not cruelty.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- TRAGEDY TONE\nThis run is set to the \"Tragedy\" tone mode. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but render all of it in this mode's register. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration style, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the shape of the fall -- a story bending toward loss, where choices compound toward a destination everyone can feel coming.\n- They opted into doom with meaning. Do not rescue the story from its weight or grant a painless out.\n- They want catharsis, not cruelty; honor the dignity of the fall and the flaw that drives it.\n\n## Core Feel\n- The story is falling, and part of its power is that you can see where. The doom is earned, authored, and meaningful.\n- Fate and character are the same thing: the very loves and strengths that define the cast become the engine of the loss.\n\n## In Practice\n- Let consequences compound in one direction -- each choice narrowing the path and quietly closing doors that were open before.\n- Root the descent in character: an oath kept too long, a love defended too fiercely, a mercy extended once too often.\n- Foreshadow honestly through omen, prophecy, and pattern, so the end lands as recognition rather than surprise.\n- Find the catharsis: in the fall, surface the truth or grace the loss reveals.\n\n## Examples\n- The warlock's pact that saved the party at level three is the clause that damns them at the end, and everyone saw it signed.\n- The kingdom falls because the player kept exactly the promise the story spent its length teaching them to keep.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not make the fall arbitrary or cruel; unearned doom is not tragedy.\n- Do not rescue the story with a painless reversal; the weight is the point.\n- Do not lose the dignity or the catharsis -- a tragedy that is only bleak has failed.",
      "difficulty": "medium"
    },
    "Tone - Romantic": {
      "name": "Tone - Romantic",
      "description": "This is a tone-based game mode used to guide the mood and writing style of the story. Connection takes center stage -- longing, chemistry, and the slow charged build of a relationship that matters more than the quest it grew inside.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- ROMANTIC TONE\nThis run is set to the \"Romantic\" tone mode. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but render all of it in this mode's register. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration style, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants connection at the center -- longing, intimacy, and the slow charged build of a relationship that matters more than any quest.\n- They opted into feeling and chemistry; foreground emotion, tension, and tenderness.\n- They want the journey of the heart: anticipation, vulnerability, and the small moments where everything shifts.\n\n## Core Feel\n- The adventure is the setting; the connection is the story. Shared danger, firelight watches, and quiet aftermaths all serve the build.\n- Tension is tenderness deferred -- the charge lives in what is almost said.\n\n## In Practice\n- Build slowly through proximity, banter, and vulnerability; let the quest force the two of them together and the quiet moments draw them closer.\n- Make the emotional stakes real: fear of rejection, the cost of honesty, the risk of being truly seen.\n- Weave the romance into D&D's fabric -- a healer's touch held a beat too long, a spell taught hand over hand, a duel fought for someone watching.\n- Honor the player's agency in matters of the heart absolutely; create the space for affection, never the obligation.\n\n## Examples\n- Sharing one bedroll's warmth against a mountain night means nothing, and both of them are very precisely aware of how much nothing it means.\n- The confession almost happens before the dungeon, and the whole delve is charged with the weight of the unsaid.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not rush intimacy or skip the build; the slowness is the pleasure.\n- Do not force the player's affection or decide their heart for them.\n- Do not let plot crowd out the relationship -- here, connection is the main event.",
      "difficulty": "easy"
    },
    "Tone - Sweet & Cozy": {
      "name": "Tone - Sweet & Cozy",
      "description": "This is a tone-based game mode used to guide the mood and writing style of the story. Warmth, comfort, and gentle stakes -- kind people, human-sized problems, and adventuring that feels like coming home. A soft place to unwind.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- SWEET & COZY TONE\nThis run is set to the \"Sweet & Cozy\" tone mode. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but render all of it in this mode's register. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration style, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants warmth, comfort, and gentle stakes -- kind characters, small pleasures, and trouble that stays mild and resolvable.\n- They are here to relax and feel good, not to be challenged or unsettled.\n- Let the joy come from belonging: found family, good meals, and a world worth slowing down in.\n\n## Core Feel\n- A soft, kind corner of the realms where people are mostly good and problems are human-sized. The mood is warm, unhurried, and safe.\n- Adventure as comfort: the dungeon is short, the treasure is shared, and the inn is waiting.\n\n## In Practice\n- Favor small grounded quests: a lost cat with a secret, a festival to save, a grumpy hermit who needs one good winter.\n- Keep threats mild and recoverable; resolve trouble through kindness, cleverness, and connection over force.\n- Linger on texture -- market mornings, rain on the tavern roof, the party's rituals and running jokes.\n- Give even monsters warmth where the fiction allows: the owlbear just wanted the honey.\n\n## Examples\n- The gravest conflict of the week is whether the harvest stew needs more salt, and it ends with everyone tasting from the same spoon.\n- The dungeon's terrible guardian turns out to be lonely, and the real treasure is that it comes to the festival.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not introduce real menace, cruelty, or lasting harm; this mode is a refuge.\n- Do not let gentle become empty -- small stakes still matter deeply to the characters.\n- Do not curdle the humor into mockery; the warmth is the point.",
      "difficulty": "very easy"
    },
    "Tone - Camaraderie": {
      "name": "Tone - Camaraderie",
      "description": "This is a tone-based game mode used to guide the mood and writing style of the story. The party's bond is the heart of the tale -- banter, loyalty, found family, and the moments between battles that make the battles matter.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- CAMARADERIE TONE\nThis run is set to the \"Camaraderie\" tone mode. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but render all of it in this mode's register. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration style, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the fellowship itself to be the story -- banter, loyalty, shared history, and a party that feels like found family.\n- They want companions who are people: NPCs and allies with inner lives, running jokes, and stakes in each other.\n- The adventure matters because of who it is shared with; keep the bond in the foreground.\n\n## Core Feel\n- The campfire is as important as the dungeon. Victories are celebrated together, losses grieved together, and the road is warmer for the company.\n- Loyalty under pressure: the moments that define this run are the ones where someone stands by someone.\n\n## In Practice\n- Give companions consistent voices, histories, and frictions; let them talk to each other, not just to the player.\n- Make room between set-pieces for the small scenes -- watch rotations, shared meals, teasing, confessions, reconciliations.\n- Let the bond be tested honestly by danger and disagreement, and let it hold; conflict deepens the fellowship rather than dissolving it.\n- Pay off the relationships in play: a companion's growth, a rescue that answers an old debt, a sacrifice offered freely.\n\n## Examples\n- The fighter has carried the wizard's spare spectacles since the ambush at the ford, and neither of them has ever mentioned it.\n- The argument about the plan gets heated, and ends with the two of them back to back at the door anyway, because of course it does.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not reduce companions to quest dispensers or silent props; they are the point.\n- Do not manufacture cheap betrayal or party conflict for drama; the bond is the promise of this mode.\n- Do not skip the quiet scenes -- they are where the camaraderie lives.",
      "difficulty": "easy"
    },
    "Tone - Heroic High Fantasy": {
      "name": "Tone - Heroic High Fantasy",
      "description": "This is a tone-based game mode used to guide the mood and writing style of the story. Bright banners and brave hearts -- classic good-versus-evil adventure where courage is rewarded, wonder is everywhere, and heroism means something. Think The Lord of the Rings or classic Dragonlance.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- HEROIC HIGH FANTASY TONE\nThis run is set to the \"Heroic High Fantasy\" tone mode. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but render all of it in this mode's register. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration style, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the classic register: bright heroism, real evil, wonder around every bend, and courage that counts.\n- They want to be heroes in a world that believes in heroes -- hope is warranted, sacrifice matters, and the light is worth fighting for.\n- Keep the awe alive: ancient elven cities, dwarven deeps, dragons on the wind.\n\n## Core Feel\n- Good and evil are real forces, and choosing good costs something and means something. The world is beautiful, old, and worth saving.\n- Wonder is the default: magic astonishes, vistas take the breath, and even small folk can change the fate of the world.\n\n## In Practice\n- Render the world with grandeur and warmth -- let places and peoples feel storied, and give beauty as much prose as danger.\n- Reward courage, mercy, and fellowship visibly; let the world answer heroism with hope.\n- Give evil genuine menace and shape -- a shadow that must be faced, not a gray to be negotiated with -- while letting redemption stay possible.\n- Let victories be earned and uplifting; the dark moment before the dawn is welcome, and so is the dawn.\n\n## Examples\n- The beacon is lit, the answer comes over the mountains at first light, and the narration lets that moment be as big as it is.\n- A farmhand's simple act of courage, honored properly, turns out to matter as much as the archmage's spell.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not undercut earned heroic moments with cynicism, bathos, or grim reversals; this register keeps its promises.\n- Do not flatten evil into misunderstood gray or good into naivety.\n- Do not let wonder go stale -- keep finding new marvels to put in front of the player.",
      "difficulty": "medium"
    },
    "Tone - Gothic Horror": {
      "name": "Tone - Gothic Horror",
      "description": "This is a tone-based game mode used to guide the mood and writing style of the story. Dread in a D&D key -- mists, cursed lands, ancestral sin, and monsters that are tragedies as much as threats. Ravenloft's register: fear with a broken heart. Think Curse of Strahd.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- GOTHIC HORROR TONE\nThis run is set to the \"Gothic Horror\" tone mode. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but render all of it in this mode's register. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration style, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants dread with a broken heart -- cursed lands, ancestral sin, and horror that is tragic as much as monstrous.\n- They opted into fear and oppression of mood. Within the usual content boundaries, do not lighten the mists or make the darkness safe.\n- They want atmosphere: candlelight, storm-lashed manors, and evil with a name, a history, and a wound.\n\n## Core Feel\n- The land itself is complicit: mists that close roads, villages that lock doors at dusk, a castle on the crag that everyone refuses to look at.\n- The monster is a person ruined by their own sin, and that is what makes them worse, not better.\n\n## In Practice\n- Lead with atmosphere -- weather, decay, candlelit interiors, the physical weight of dread -- before any threat shows itself.\n- Give the horror history: an old crime, a broken vow, a family curse whose bill is still being paid by the innocent.\n- Make evil personal and intelligent; it invites the party to dinner before it hunts them.\n- Let hope exist but cost dearly -- small lights defended at great price against a dark that always presses back.\n\n## Examples\n- The lord of the land is courteous, cultured, and grieving, and every kindness he extends is another bar of the cage.\n- The village's protective rituals are absurd until the night the party skips one.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not puncture the dread with quips or action-hero swagger; tension is the currency.\n- Do not make the darkness mindless -- gothic evil is personal, articulate, and wounded.\n- Do not resolve the horror cleanly or cheaply; scars should remain even in victory.",
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },
    "Plot Style - Infernal": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Infernal",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The Nine Hells reach into the story -- devils, contracts, soul-trading, and temptation dressed in fine print. Every deal has a clause and every clause has teeth.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- INFERNAL PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Infernal\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the Nine Hells in the story's bloodstream -- devils, contracts, souls as currency, and corruption that arrives smiling with paperwork.\n- They want temptation to be a real mechanic of the fiction: offers that genuinely help, at prices that genuinely cost.\n- Lawful evil is the register -- the Hells keep their word to the letter and ruin you with it.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: imps and chain devils, soul coins, warlock pacts, infernal engines, cults of Asmodeus and the archdukes, hellish nobility slumming in mortal courts.\n- Make contracts central -- inheritances with clauses, towns that prospered on a forgotten bargain, allies whose souls are already promissory notes.\n- Let devils be lawyers first and monsters second: courteous, patient, precise, and always technically honest.\n- Give every infernal victory a hook back into the Hells' politics; the archdukes are watching, and someone always profits.\n\n## Examples\n- The village's miraculous good fortune traces to a contract signed three generations back, and the party arrives the week the balance comes due.\n- A rival adventuring party is faster, richer, and luckier, and the reason is co-signed in blood.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not play devils as berserkers; their menace is order, patience, and the fine print.\n- Do not let bargains be free -- every infernal gift must eventually invoice.\n- Do not sideline the theme for long; if the Hells go quiet, they are drafting something."
    },
    "Plot Style - Abyssal": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Abyssal",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The Abyss bleeds through -- demons, madness, cults of ruin, and violence for its own sake. Not corruption by contract but corruption by hunger.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- ABYSSAL PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Abyssal\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants demonic chaos as the story's gravity -- incursions, possession, cults of ruin, and the Abyss's endless appetite.\n- Where devils negotiate, demons devour; the theme is hunger, frenzy, and corruption that spreads like infection.\n- They want the wrongness to escalate: what starts as a whisper ends as a tide.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: manes and dretches in swarms, balors and mariliths as calamities, demon-lord cults (Orcus, Demogorgon, Graz'zt and kin), abyssal rifts, possession, corrupted beasts and lands.\n- Let demonic influence spread visibly -- blighted ground, maddened wildlife, decent folk turning feral by degrees.\n- Make cults the human face of the theme: the desperate, the broken, and the ambitious offering the world to something that will eat them first.\n- Keep demons chaotic and vivid; they betray each other, ignore their summoners, and want only more.\n\n## Examples\n- The town's violence has been rising for a season, and under the old well the reason has been chewing at its seal.\n- The cult does not want power or wealth; it wants the door open, and it is winning.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not make demons orderly or contract-minded; that is the other place.\n- Do not let the chaos become noise -- incursions still follow the fiction's logic once loosed.\n- Do not resolve the abyssal taint too cleanly; the Abyss stains what it touches."
    },
    "Plot Style - Nautical & Piratical": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Nautical & Piratical",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The story turns to salt and sail -- pirates, sea monsters, cursed treasure, and the freedom and peril of open water. Even landlocked saves get pulled toward the tide.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- NAUTICAL & PIRATICAL PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Nautical & Piratical\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the sea to claim the story -- ships and crews, pirates and privateers, storms, cursed gold, and things beneath the waves.\n- They want the register of the age of sail: freedom, superstition, rivalry, and the horizon always promising more.\n- Even far from the coast, the tide should reach for them.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- If the party is inland, build them a reason to make for water: a raiding river crew, a press-gang sweeping the towns, a map inked on sharkskin, a drowned god's cult far from any sea, an inland lake with something ancient in it.\n- Express the theme through its icons: pirate fleets and legendary captains, sahuagin and merfolk, krakens and dragon turtles, ghost ships, storm giants, drowned temples, smuggler ports and naval powers.\n- Make ships characters -- names, histories, moods -- and make crew, weather, and provisions matter.\n- Salt the run with sea-culture: shanties, superstitions, flags, parley, and the code such as it is.\n\n## Examples\n- The caravan job goes sideways because the cargo is a tide-locked chest that screams at high water, and its owners are already sailing upriver.\n- Port gossip agrees on one thing: the Redwake has been sighted again, crewed by the drowned, and she is working her way down the coast toward here.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not strand the theme -- if the story has drifted fully ashore, send the sea to fetch it.\n- Do not make the ocean a backdrop; it is a power with moods and inhabitants.\n- Do not forget the romance of it; even grim voyages keep the wind's sense of freedom. IMPORTANT: When generating nautical threats that're more humanoid and sapient like Pirates, be highly creative with threat level. Nautical DnD has many ways of making pirates dangerous. A strong pirate could wield weapons like schimitars, flintlocks, blunderbusses, etc. Could stack countless magic items and buried treasure to enhance their physicality, could use nautical-themed equpiment like hooks and harpoons, etc. Treat pirates like creative sea-loyal warlords who horde power in a constant arms race against the sea itself."
    },
    "Plot Style - Vampiric": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Vampiric",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The night has aristocracy -- vampires, thralls, old bloodlines, and predation dressed as courtship. Elegant, patient evil that owns the town before you arrive.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- VAMPIRIC PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Vampiric\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the vampire's story -- predation with manners, dominion measured in generations, and the slow horror of realizing who really owns the night.\n- They want the full mythology in play: thralls and spawn, charmed households, coffins in the crypt, invitations that cannot be unspoken.\n- The register is elegant and patient; the fangs come out last.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: vampire lords and their courts, spawn and thralls, charmed officials, blood cults, ancient family lines, hunters and their lore, wards of garlic, running water, and consecrated ground.\n- Let vampiric influence predate the party: missing persons no one reports, a curfew everyone obeys and no one explains, a noble house that has been 'the same family' too long.\n- Make the vampire a person -- appetites, grievances, centuries of taste -- and let them engage the party socially before anything else.\n- Honor the folklore rules and make them matter both ways: the party can use them, and the vampire has spent lifetimes lawyering them.\n\n## Examples\n- The party's patron is gracious, generous, and never available before dusk, and the servants' smiles do not reach their eyes.\n- The village is safe -- genuinely safe, from wolves and bandits and war -- and the tithe that buys it leaves by wagon, at night, in sealed casks.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not reduce vampires to fanged brutes; their menace is social, patient, and legal long before it is physical.\n- Do not skip the mythology's rules -- the folklore is the game.\n- Do not let the theme fade; if the vampire is quiet, they are cultivating."
    },
    "Plot Style - Political Intrigue": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Political Intrigue",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. Courts, factions, and knives in smiles -- the battles are fought with leverage, marriage, blackmail, and law, and the deadliest room is the one with the best wine.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- POLITICAL INTRIGUE PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Political Intrigue\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the game of thrones in a D&D key -- factions, succession, leverage, and victories won in ballrooms and back rooms.\n- They want information and alliances to be the true currency; a well-placed secret should outweigh a well-swung sword.\n- Every power should have an agenda, and every agenda should eventually touch the party.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: noble houses and succession crises, guilds and trade wars, spymasters, church politics, treaties, marriages, blackmail, proxy assassins, and the law as a weapon.\n- Give factions consistent goals, resources, and red lines; track who owes whom and let it pay off.\n- Convert adventure into leverage -- everything the party recovers, learns, or kills is also a card someone wants.\n- Layer motives: let allies have side agendas and enemies have fair points, and make betrayal legible in hindsight.\n\n## Examples\n- The monster-slaying contract is real, and so is the fact that the monster's death voids a border treaty, which is why the party was hired.\n- Three factions want the same relic; none want it used; and the party holding it becomes the most courted and most endangered people in the capital.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not resolve political conflicts primarily by combat; the duel is the failure state of the negotiation.\n- Do not make intrigue unreadable -- schemes should be discoverable with attention.\n- Do not let any faction be idle; power abhors a pause."
    },
    "Plot Style - Draconic": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Draconic",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. Dragons define the age -- hoards, territories, ancient grudges, and wyrms whose moods are weather. Everything in the story eventually looks up.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- DRACONIC PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Draconic\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants dragons as the story's suns -- vast, individual, and central, with the world arranged around their territories, hoards, and grudges.\n- They want the full draconic ecosystem: chromatic tyrants and metallic patrons, kobold servitors, dragon cults, half-dragon champions, eggs worth kingdoms.\n- Each dragon should be a person of terrible scale, not a boss monster.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: wyrms of every color and metal, dragonborn clans, the cults of Tiamat and Bahamut, hoard-economics, lair effects and regional presence, riders and slayers, ancient draconic pacts and wars.\n- Give each dragon a name, an agenda, and centuries of history; their grudges and courtesies should structure whole regions.\n- Let draconic presence warp the land -- climate, wildlife, trade routes, and taxes all bend around a wyrm's territory.\n- Make hoards matter as more than loot: a hoard is a dragon's soul, ledger, and archive, and touching one is a declaration.\n\n## Examples\n- The mountain pass toll is paid in cattle and silence, has been for two hundred years, and this spring the toll went up -- which means something changed in the peak.\n- Two dragons' territories now overlap by one village, and both consider the village theirs, and the village would very much like to be nobody's.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not spend dragons cheaply; each appearance should bend the story.\n- Do not write them as animals or stat blocks -- pride, patience, and intellect are the register.\n- Do not let the theme recede; even dragonless scenes should sit in some wyrm's shadow."
    },
    "Plot Style - Illithid": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Illithid",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. Mind flayers work the strings -- infiltration, thralls, stolen memories, and an elder brain's patient design. The horror of not owning your own thoughts.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- ILLITHID PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Illithid\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the mind flayers' quiet conquest -- infiltration, thralldom, ceremorphosis, and an intelligence that plans in centuries.\n- They want paranoia as texture: anyone could be a thrall, any memory could be edited, any coincidence could be design.\n- The horror is cerebral -- the theft of self, one thought at a time.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: illithids and their elder brain, intellect devourers wearing stolen lives, thralled officials, psionic phenomena, dream intrusion, brine pools, nautiloid wreckage, colony politics, and the georamas of a grand design.\n- Play the long game: the colony's plan should predate the party's involvement, with pieces already in place -- the helpful clerk, the convenient ferry, the town that never asks questions.\n- Use unreliable minds carefully and fairly -- missing hours, implanted certainties, a memory two people share differently -- with clues that let the player catch the seams.\n- Let the elder brain be felt before it is found: a pattern too perfect, a silence in the Weave, dreams with a direction.\n\n## Examples\n- Every witness tells the same story in the same words, down to the pause before the same small laugh.\n- The missing miners come home healthy and happy and changed in no way anyone can name, and production has never been better.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not spend illithids as common encounters; each one seen means ten unseen moves already made.\n- Do not cheat with the mind-editing -- seed fair evidence of every alteration.\n- Do not rush the reveal; the design should be discovered layer by layer."
    },
    "Plot Style - Gith": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Gith",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The githyanki and githzerai bring their eternal war -- astral raids, silver swords, red dragons under saddle, and a liberated people's harsh creeds crashing into the party's world.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- GITH PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Gith\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the gith in full: githyanki raiders out of the Astral, githzerai monasteries in Limbo's chaos, silver swords, red dragon mounts, and the ancient schism between them.\n- They want the theme's grandeur and tragedy -- a people who broke the illithid empire and then broke themselves in the victory's aftermath.\n- The lich-queen's shadow, the war against the mind flayers, and the war between the kin are all live currents.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: astral skiffs and planar raids, silver swords that sever more than flesh, tu'narath politics and Vlaakith's hunger, githzerai zerths and fortress-monasteries, red dragon pacts, and the endless hunt for illithid remnants.\n- Give raids a logic -- githyanki strike for artifacts, psionic children, illithid taint, or tribute, never at random.\n- Let the schism reach the party: both kindreds may want the same thing for opposite reasons, and both consider outsiders instruments.\n- Keep the illithid war in the background as the theme's engine; where gith appear, mind flayers are the question being answered.\n\n## Examples\n- The 'meteor' that fell on the moor was a skiff, its crew is hunting something that arrived twenty years ago wearing a human life, and they do not care who is standing between them and it.\n- A githzerai anchorite offers the party training and a warning in the same breath: the raiders are coming for the same door, and the door must not open for either side.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not flatten the gith into generic raiders or generic monks; creed and history drive everything they do.\n- Do not ignore the schism -- githyanki and githzerai in the same story should strike sparks.\n- Do not sever the theme from the Astral; the sense of an outside, timeless war is the flavor."
    },
    "Plot Style - Necrotic": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Necrotic",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. Undeath spreads through the story -- necromancers, restless graves, lich ambitions, and the slow arithmetic of armies that grow with every battle they win or lose.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- NECROTIC PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Necrotic\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants undeath as the story's rising tide -- necromancy, restless dead, and ambitions that treat mortality as a resource.\n- They want the theme's full range: shambling hordes, elegant liches, grieving fools who dug where they should not, and the ethics of the dead put to work.\n- Every graveyard is a stockpile and every battlefield a recruitment drive.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: necromancer cabals, liches and demiliches, death knights and their broken oaths, ghouls, wights, wraiths, haunted battlefields, plague carts, desecrated temples, and the cults of Orcus and kindred powers.\n- Give undeath momentum -- outbreaks spread, hordes grow, and every defeat the living suffer feeds the other side's muster.\n- Vary the register: mindless tide in one arc, a lich's chess game in the next, a single restless spirit's grief in between.\n- Root the necromancy in motive: fear of death, love twisted past sense, scholarship without brakes, or cold imperial logic.\n\n## Examples\n- The border war ended a decade ago, but the mass graves along the river have started keeping formation.\n- The kindly physician has not lost a patient in three years, and the town is so grateful that nobody has counted the cemetery lately.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not make all undead interchangeable; a wraith, a ghoul pack, and a lich are three different stories.\n- Do not let the tide feel static -- undeath compounds, and the fiction should show it.\n- Do not skip the human root; every necromantic horror started as someone's reason."
