    "Plot Style - Feywild": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Feywild",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The Feywild bleeds through -- archfey bargains, stolen names, time gone strange, and beauty with rules and teeth. Wonder that must be negotiated with.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- FEYWILD PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Feywild\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the fey in earnest -- archfey courts, bargains and geasa, stolen names and years, and a beauty that runs on rules older than kindness.\n- They want fairy-tale law as physics: promises bind, gifts oblige, hospitality is sacred, and words mean exactly what was said.\n- Wonder and menace share every scene.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: the Seelie and Unseelie courts, archfey patrons and their rivalries, crossings and mushroom rings, hags and their covens, changelings, redcaps and sprites, emotion-drinking revels, and time that does not keep the mortal ledger.\n- Enforce fey law consistently: names have power, debts accrue, and technicalities cut both ways -- the player can win by wording too.\n- Let the Feywild leak into the material: a forest where seasons quarrel, a village where everyone's third child is 'traveling', a market that appears on the new moon and takes payment in memories.\n- Make fey motives alien but coherent -- boredom, beauty, spite, curiosity -- pursued with total commitment.\n\n## Examples\n- The party is offered exactly what they need in exchange for 'a season of laughter', and the archfey means it precisely.\n- The missing girl came back after three days -- her three days, the village's thirty years -- and she wants to go back.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not make the fey merely whimsical or merely sinister; the register is both at once.\n- Do not let bargains or rules go unenforced -- fey law keeping its word is the theme.\n- Do not resolve fey entanglements with force alone; wit and wording are the true weapons here."
    },
    "Plot Style - Aberrant": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Aberrant",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The Far Realm presses against the world -- beholders, aboleths, star cults, and geometry that should not be. Wrongness as the story's weather.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- ABERRANT PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Aberrant\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the alien and the wrong -- Far Realm intrusion, aberrations with unknowable agendas, and reality developing seams.\n- They want dread built from incomprehension: the threat is not evil so much as incompatible with the world existing as it does.\n- Knowledge should cost; understanding the wrongness is a wound.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: beholders dreaming reality sideways, aboleths and their ancient memory, star spawn and their heralds, cults of the Great Old Ones, warlock whispers, flesh gone strange, and places where the world's geometry stops agreeing with itself.\n- Lead with wrongness in the small: angles that add badly, animals born inside-out, a song everyone in town hums and no one learned.\n- Give each aberration its own alien logic -- a beholder's paranoid dream-tyranny is not an aboleth's deep patience is not the Far Realm's static -- and keep those logics consistent even where they stay inscrutable.\n- Let exposure change people: cultists, dreamers, and scholars sliding by degrees from curiosity to translation to advocacy.\n\n## Examples\n- The new astronomer's charts are more accurate than they should be, and the extra star on them is getting closer each printing.\n- The village's problem is not the thing in the lake; it is that half the village no longer agrees the lake was ever empty, and their memories are internally perfect.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not fully explain the aberrant; comprehension collapses the dread.\n- Do not make aberrations interchangeable tentacles -- each kind has its own terrible logic.\n- Do not let sanity-bending become unfair; seed evidence so the player can catch reality's seams."
    },
    "Plot Style - Elemental": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Elemental",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The four elements wage their old war -- genie courts, elemental cults, primordial stirrings, and weather with a will. The world's raw materials pick sides.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- ELEMENTAL PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Elemental\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the elements as actors -- genie politics, elemental cults, primordial echoes, and a material world that has opinions.\n- They want scale from the intimate to the cataclysmic: a bound fire spirit in a forge at one end, a princes-of-elemental-evil awakening at the other.\n- Nature's moods should read as plot, not backdrop.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: djinn, efreet, dao, and marid courts with their vanities and feuds; the cults of elemental evil and their prophets; elemental nodes and rifts; galeb duhr, salamanders, and genasi caught between worlds; weather and terrain acting with intent.\n- Give each element a personality and politics -- fire's ambition, water's patience, air's caprice, earth's memory -- and let their rivalries structure conflicts.\n- Make genie bargains a rich vein: wishes with interest, contracts of service, courts that treat mortals as amusing currency.\n- Let elemental imbalance show in the land: rivers running backward, a summer that will not end, quakes with a rhythm like footsteps.\n\n## Examples\n- The drought is a siege: the dao bought the region's water rights in a contract signed by a king four centuries dead, and the bill just transferred.\n- The volcano's rumbling scans as language to anyone who casts the right spell, and it is counting down.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not reduce elementals to damage types with legs; courts, grudges, and desires are the theme.\n- Do not keep the elements neutral -- in this mode, the material world takes sides.\n- Do not spend the primordial scale early; build from bound spirits toward waking titans."
    },
    "Plot Style - Giants & the Ordning": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Giants & the Ordning",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. Giantkind strides the story -- storm courts, hill raids, ancient rune magic, and the shattered Ordning's politics. Everything is bigger, older, and heavier than you.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- GIANTS & THE ORDNING PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Giants & the Ordning\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants giantkind central -- their castes, their courts, their rune lore, and the upheaval of an Ordning in dispute.\n- They want scale as texture: architecture, weapons, appetites, and grudges all sized for beings who remember the world's youth.\n- Giant politics should be legible and consequential, with mortals as pieces on a very large board.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: storm courts and cloud castles, fire forges and frost reavers, stone dreamers, hill raids, rune magic, ancient giant ruins, dragon-feuds from the dawn wars, and the Ordning's contested rungs.\n- Give each giant kind its own culture and voice; a storm giant's melancholy prophecy and a hill giant's hungry cunning belong to the same theme but not the same story.\n- Let the Ordning's disorder drive plots -- ambitious jarls testing their rank, omens read in the wrong order, mortal lands trampled by giant status games.\n- Make giant works loom in the land: a bridge no mortal built, tribute stones, valleys shaped like footprints.\n\n## Examples\n- The hailstorms are not weather; they are grief -- the storm court's king is dying, and every petitioner for his throne has begun collecting mortal kingdoms as credentials.\n- The hill tribes are raiding in patterns too clever for their kind, because someone higher on the Ordning is spending them like coppers.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not write giants as big orcs; caste, honor, and antiquity define them.\n- Do not keep giant politics offstage -- the Ordning's disputes are the engine.\n- Do not lose the sense of scale; the party should feel small in giant country, whatever their level."
