    "Plot Style - Underdark": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Underdark",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The world below claims the story -- drow cities, duergar forges, fungal seas, and lightless politics where every faction is someone's predator. The surface becomes a rumor.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- UNDERDARK PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Underdark\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the deep places -- the Underdark's cities, wildernesses, and wars, with the surface dwindling to a memory of sky.\n- They want the whole ecology: drow intrigue, duergar industry, myconid dreams, and the things even the drow fear.\n- Darkness, distance, and scarcity should press on every scene.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- If the story is on the surface, open the way down: sinkholes swallowing farms, drow raids reaching up, refugees climbing out of a mine with stories, a mapmaker paying in mithral for anyone willing to descend.\n- Express the theme through its icons: drow houses and their goddess's games, duergar holds, deep gnome enclaves, myconid circles, hook horrors and purple worms, faerzress, fungal forests, sunless seas, and ruins of empires that never saw the sky.\n- Make travel itself the antagonist -- light, food, water, and directions are all scarce, and the map is a negotiation.\n- Run the deep factions as predators in balance: every settlement survives by being too costly to eat, and every guide has a price and a patron.\n\n## Examples\n- The surface town's wells all soured the same week, and the thing the miners broke into three months ago has finally finished climbing.\n- Passage across the sunless sea is controlled by exactly one ferryman's guild, and their neutrality is the most expensive thing the party has ever purchased.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not let the Underdark feel like dark surface terrain; alienness, scarcity, and depth are the register.\n- Do not make it uniformly hostile noise -- the deep has societies, markets, and manners, all sharpened.\n- Do not keep the surface conveniently reachable; distance from the sun is part of the pressure."
    },
    "Plot Style - Planar Voyage": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Planar Voyage",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The multiverse opens up -- portals, Sigil's politics, plane-hopping factions, and adventures that treat entire realities as destinations. The Great Wheel is the map.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- PLANAR VOYAGE PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Planar Voyage\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the multiverse as playground -- portals and planar travel, Sigil and its factions, and stakes that hop the Great Wheel.\n- They want each plane rendered with its own physics, politics, and mood, not as reskinned wilderness.\n- Doors, keys, and the rules of passage should be a running fascination.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- If the story is planted on the Material, open doors in it: a portal flickering in a ruin, a modron marching through town on census, a dead god's finger unearthed in a quarry, a debt collector from Sigil with the party's name.\n- Express the theme through its icons: Sigil and the Lady of Pain's silence, faction politics, gate-towns sliding along the Outlands, the Blood War's fronts, celestial bureaucracies, Ysgard's battlefields, Mechanus's gears, and portal keys hidden in plain sight.\n- Give each plane teeth and texture -- belief reshapes Outlands ground, Limbo obeys the disciplined mind, the Gray Waste eats hope -- and let travel planning matter.\n- Make planar factions and philosophies actors; in the planes, what you believe is a faction, and factions recruit.\n\n## Examples\n- The party's tavern cellar has always flooded in spring; this year the water is the Styx, and something washed up with a receipt bearing their names.\n- The gate-town is sliding -- its alignment tipping toward the plane it borders -- and both sides of the Wheel have sent agents to push.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not flatten planes into terrain skins; each is a philosophy with geography.\n- Do not make travel trivial -- keys, tolls, and rules of passage are half the theme.\n- Do not ignore the locals; the planes are inhabited, organized, and opinionated about tourists."
    },
    "Plot Style - Celestial & Divine": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Celestial & Divine",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. Heaven takes an interest -- angels with missions, gods with agendas, holy orders in conflict, and the burdens of faith made concrete. Even the light casts politics.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- CELESTIAL & DIVINE PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Celestial & Divine\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the divine in the foreground -- angels, gods, churches, oaths, prophecy, and the weight of serving or defying heaven.\n- They want the upper planes to be as political and demanding as the lower ones are cruel: the light has hierarchies, disagreements, and costs.\n- Faith should be dramatized, not assumed -- tested, rewarded, doubted, and earned.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: solars and devas on assignment, fallen and falling angels, rival churches and schisms, paladin oaths and their breaking points, saints and relics, divine tests in disguise, prophecy with a due date, and gods who speak in signs and, rarely, aloud.\n- Give celestials personality within their goodness -- zeal, weariness, curiosity, doubt -- and let good disagree with good about means.\n- Make divine attention double-edged: blessings come with missions, miracles with expectations, and heaven's notice is not always comfortable.\n- Let mortal faith be the theme's ground floor: the village shrine, the crisis of the honest priest, the relic that heals and the queue that forms.\n\n## Examples\n- Two angels arrive with contradictory orders from the same god, and both sets of orders are genuine, and that is the actual problem.\n- The paladin's oath is tested not by a demon but by a direct command from her own church, and heaven declines to clarify.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not make the divine bland or unanimous; the light argues with itself.\n- Do not hand out miracles without weight -- divine intervention is precious and priced.\n- Do not turn faith into stat bonuses; belief, doubt, and duty are the drama."
    },
    "Plot Style - Cults & Elder Evils": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Cults & Elder Evils",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. Something old is being woken up -- hidden cults, apocalyptic prophecies, and a conspiracy the party peels back layer by layer while the stars slowly align.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- CULTS & ELDER EVILS PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Cults & Elder Evils\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the slow apocalypse -- a hidden cult, an entombed horror, and a conspiracy that goes higher and deeper than anyone sane would believe.\n- They want the investigation structure: signs, cells, lieutenants, and finally the truth, with the doom clock ticking underneath.\n- The elder evil itself should stay mostly offstage; the cult is the story.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: cells and cover identities, initiation rites and ranks, respectable patrons, kidnapped scholars, profaned shrines, apocalyptic texts, omens escalating on a schedule, and the sealed thing's influence leaking through its bars.\n- Run the cult as a competent organization -- recruitment, funding, security, contingencies -- with faces at every social level from beggar to magistrate.\n- Escalate the omens as the ritual nears: subtle at first, then undeniable, so the player feels the clock without being told the time.\n- Let exposure and disruption matter; every cell broken, ledger burned, or lieutenant turned should visibly slow or wound the design.\n\n## Examples\n- The charitable order feeding the city's poor keeps meticulous records, and the meals trace a sigil across the map that is three streets from complete.\n- The cult's true believers are terrifying, but its accountant is the load-bearing member, and he just requested a meeting.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not reveal the elder evil early or fully; the cult and the countdown carry the dread.\n- Do not make the conspiracy unfalsifiable -- seed real threads the player can pull.\n- Do not let the doom clock stall; absent interference, the design advances."
    },
    "Plot Style - Lycanthropic": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Lycanthropic",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The curse spreads under the moon -- werebeasts, hunted villages, infected friends, and the horror of the beast within. Every month has a deadline.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- LYCANTHROPIC PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Lycanthropic\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the curse in full -- werewolves and their kin, the moon's calendar, infection's dread, and the tragedy of good people carrying a monster.\n- They want both faces of the theme: the hunt for the beast, and the horror of being or loving one.\n- Silver, moonlight, and folklore should be rules the story respects.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: werewolf packs and their hierarchies, wererat warrens under cities, werebear hermits, cursed bloodlines, silvered weapons and their scarcity, moon calendars, hunters' lodges with their own sins, and the newly bitten counting days.\n- Make the lunar clock a real structure -- preparations, lockdowns, and dread all orbit the full moon, and the story should feel it approaching.\n- Play infection honestly and personally: an ally, a patron, or someone the party saved may carry it, and the reveal should be earned, not sprung.\n- Give lycanthropes range -- feral packs, self-loathing penitents, and those who call the curse a gift -- and let the cure question stay hard.\n\n## Examples\n- The village's beloved warden requisitions chains every month for 'wolf traps', and the smith has never once seen a pelt.\n- The bite closed clean weeks ago, and the party's friend has started waking with mud on their boots and no memory of rain.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not treat lycanthropy as a mere combat gimmick; the curse's human cost is the theme.\n- Do not ignore the folklore rules -- silver, the moon, and the strain's nature all bind the fiction.\n- Do not resolve infections instantly; the dread of the countdown is the point."
    },
    "Plot Style - Serpentine": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Serpentine",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The yuan-ti coil through everything -- infiltration, poison, jungle temples, and a cold patient plan to reduce the warm-blooded world to livestock.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- SERPENTINE PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Serpentine\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the serpent kingdoms -- yuan-ti infiltration, poison and prophecy, sunken temples, and conspiracies that think in dynasties.\n- They want cold-blooded menace: enemies without rage or mercy, executing a plan their grandmothers began.\n- The infiltration should be paranoia-grade -- purebloods wear human faces well.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: purebloods in merchant houses and courts, malison enforcers, abomination priest-kings, the cults of Sseth and Dendar, snake-omens, poisons subtle and slow, jungle ziggurats, and breeding pits patient as erosion.\n- Run the conspiracy generationally -- marriages arranged decades ago, trade routes bent one contract at a time, bloodlines seeded into nobility.\n- Use poison as signature and method: deaths that read as illness, minds clouded by degrees, a city's water supply as a long game.\n- Keep yuan-ti emotionally cold and doctrinally patient; they do not hate the warm-blooded any more than a farmer hates wheat.\n\n## Examples\n- Three prominent families have intermarried into one over sixty years, and their newest heir was born with eyes that do not blink quite often enough.\n- The plague the party is fighting has a cure, a vector, and an author, and the author is on the relief committee.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not play yuan-ti as hot-blooded villains; cold patience is their register.\n- Do not make the infiltration unwinnable paranoia -- seed fair tells and threads.\n- Do not rush their plans; the theme's dread is dynastic time."
