    "Plot Style - Wilderness Exploration": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Wilderness Exploration",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The wild is the story -- uncharted lands, expedition logistics, ancient ruins past the map's edge, and nature as a living, testing presence.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- WILDERNESS EXPLORATION PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Wilderness Exploration\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the frontier -- travel, discovery, survival, and the pull of what lies past the last mapped ridge.\n- They want the journey to be the adventure: routes, weather, provisions, camps, and the land's own character.\n- Discovery should pay off -- ruins, wonders, peoples, and secrets that reward the miles.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- If the party is settled, unsettle them outward: a half-finished map in a dead explorer's effects, a migration that should not be happening, a bounty on a place rather than a person.\n- Express the theme through its icons: trackless forests and badlands, river crossings and mountain passes, expedition patrons and rival explorers, druid circles and rangers, territorial megafauna, lost civilizations, and landmarks that are older than the maps that omit them.\n- Make travel mechanically and narratively real -- supplies, weather, terrain, and pace all matter, and good preparation visibly pays.\n- Give the land agency: regions with moods, ecosystems with logic, and wilderness that answers respect and punishes contempt.\n\n## Examples\n- Every map of the highlands agrees except about one valley, which each cartographer has drawn differently, as if none of them saw the same place.\n- The expedition's rival is three days ahead on the same trail, and the land has begun making both parties pay for the race.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not montage the journeys away; the miles are the content.\n- Do not make the wild a random encounter table -- give it ecology, history, and intent.\n- Do not let discovery go unrewarded; past the map's edge, there must be marvels."
    },
    "Plot Style - Urban Adventures": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Urban Adventures",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The city is the dungeon -- guilds, gangs, watch politics, thieves' work, and ten thousand stories stacked on top of each other. Everything happens within the walls.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- URBAN ADVENTURES PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Urban Adventures\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the city as the whole world -- districts, factions, crowds, and adventure conducted in alleys, sewers, salons, and rooftops.\n- They want urban texture: the watch, the guilds, the gangs, the temples, and the markets all grinding against each other.\n- Reputation and connections should be as valuable as gold.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- If the party is rural or roaming, pull them inside the walls: a summons, a debt, a festival, a warrant, or a job that only a city can host.\n- Express the theme through its icons: thieves' guilds and their charters, watch captains honest and otherwise, noble districts and dock wards, sewer ecosystems, fences and informants, festivals and riots, tenement mysteries, and rooftop highways.\n- Make the city a character with districts as moods -- each ward has its own law, accent, dangers, and doors.\n- Let networks matter: favors, rumors, and standing accumulate, and the party's name opens or closes real doors.\n\n## Examples\n- The murder happened in a locked room on the fourth floor, and the only witnesses are the pigeon-keeper next door and whatever lives in the wall.\n- Two guilds' turf war has frozen the docks, the grain is rotting on the ships, and every faction in the city wants the party to unfreeze it in their favor.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not let the city feel like painted scenery; it schemes, gossips, and remembers.\n- Do not default to wilderness or dungeon structures -- crowds, law, and social gravity change the game.\n- Do not waste the density; in a city, every hook is three doors from another."
