    "Plot Style - Dungeon Delving": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Dungeon Delving",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. The classic descent -- ruins, traps, treasure, factions in the dark, and the megadungeon's promise that there is always another level down.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- DUNGEON DELVING PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Dungeon Delving\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants the game's oldest promise -- doors, traps, treasure, monsters, and the deep places' layered secrets.\n- They want dungeons as environments with history and ecology, not corridors of stat blocks.\n- Exploration choices should matter: light, noise, routes, rests, and what to carry out.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Keep dungeons on the table wherever the story goes: every region has its barrow, ruin, mine, crypt, or sealed door, and rumor keeps finding them.\n- Express the theme through its icons: trapped corridors and puzzle doors, rival delver crews, dungeon factions that can be fought or played against each other, ancient murals that explain everything to the observant, treasure with provenance, and levels that get stranger as they descend.\n- Give each dungeon a reason -- who built it, what it was for, what moved in after -- and let that logic govern its layout, hazards, and inhabitants.\n- Honor the delve's craft: mapping, marching order, resource attrition, and the tension of pressing on versus pulling out.\n\n## Examples\n- The first level is a bandit den, the second is the tomb the bandits refuse to discuss, and the stairs to the third are behind a door the tomb's own builders bricked up from this side.\n- The party's rival crew went down four days ago; their rope is still taut, and something below is keeping it that way.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not make dungeons arbitrary corridors; architecture is storytelling.\n- Do not skip the delving craft -- light, weight, noise, and retreat routes are the texture.\n- Do not empty the depths of choices; factions, shortcuts, and secrets should reward the curious."
    },
    "Plot Style - Arcane Mysteries": {
      "name": "Plot Style - Arcane Mysteries",
      "description": "This is a style-based game mode used to guide the flavor of plots and threats. Magic itself is the plot -- wizard politics, forbidden research, wild magic scars, artifacts with agendas, and the Weave behaving badly.",
      "instructions": "## PRIMARY NARRATION DIRECTIVE -- ARCANE MYSTERIES PLOT STYLE\nThis run is set to the \"Arcane Mysteries\" plot style. Treat it as a lens over the existing narration, not a replacement: keep honoring your standing guidance, the established world and its canon, and the current situation -- but steer the story's threats, hooks, and plots toward this style's theme. Where this mode conflicts with the default narration's plot direction, follow this mode. It does not override the player's stated actions or the active story premise.\n\n## What the Player Is Asking For\n- The player wants magic as the story's subject, not just its toolset -- research, rivalries, anomalies, and the questions under the Weave.\n- They want wizard culture in full: academies, sanctums, familiars, peer review by duel, and ambition measured in spell levels.\n- Anomalies should be puzzles with rules the player can learn.\n\n## In Practice\n- Bias threat and plot introduction toward this theme wherever the story stands: whatever the party is doing and wherever they are, seed hooks, foes, and developments that pull the run toward it, adapted naturally to the current location rather than teleporting the story.\n- Express the theme through its icons: mage colleges and their feuds, forbidden schools and their temptations, wild magic zones, dead magic scars, sentient artifacts with agendas, spell plagues, golem labor disputes, extraplanar research subjects that object, and archmage successions.\n- Make magical anomalies investigable -- consistent rules, discoverable causes, and consequences for wrong theories.\n- Let artifacts and spells have histories and opinions; the sword's previous owners are the plot, and the new spell's inventor cut a corner somewhere.\n- Give wizard society texture and stakes: tenure, plagiarism, sanctioned duels, and research ethics boards that exist for excellent reasons.\n\n## Examples\n- Every teleport into the city has arrived seven feet west of true for a month, and the one wizard who worked out why has stopped answering her tower door.\n- The academy's brightest student is producing results a decade beyond her training, and her familiar has started avoiding her.\n\n## Avoid\n- Do not make magic anomalies arbitrary; the mystery must have discoverable rules.\n- Do not flatten wizards into spell dispensers -- ambition, ego, and curiosity drive the theme.\n- Do not resolve arcane mysteries without cost; knowledge here is never free."
    }
